 own, will get you to change that, because, if you don't, you're going to have to pay.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, if you were flying this long holiday weekend, you were not imagining it. Airports are more crowded, in fact, volume-wise, building toward the best levels we have seen since the pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Flock at Chicago's O'Hare with more on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: You know, the only good thing, Neil, I will say, for those of us who flew during the whole pandemic was that, during it, the airports were easy. You didn't see poor guys like this carrying everything by himself. Oh, this poor fellow. Beautiful daughter, though.\par \par \uc2 And the TSA lines were not a problem. I got to put my mask on as we walk inside, because that is the rule.\par \par \uc2 But look at this TSA line now, yes, backing up. And this is not even a busy day.\par \par \uc2 I will show you what a busy day looked like, or at least in terms of the numbers. These are the latest TSA numbers that we got just this morning from yesterday. And then we had the numbers for Friday, which was a record, as you point out, 2.1 nine million people, almost three times what it was last year, and even more than it was in 2019, before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 So what do you get when you get all of those people? You get delays and you get cancellations. Take a look at the numbers for Sunday, 4,000-plus delays across the U.S., 2,000-plus cancellations.\par \par \uc2 And if that wasn't enough for these travelers to deal with, what about unruly passengers? Today, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  announcing new fines of more than $100,000 to nine people who were believed to have been causing a scene and being inappropriate, fighting with people, and all sorts of things on the aircraft.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  today also unveiling a Psaki. That was a public service announcement that employed young children, who are perhaps smarter than we are, about how to behave on an aircraft.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Fighting is not good when you're on a plane.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They will go to jail if they keep doing that stuff.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would be really scared.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would not like that, if someone did that to me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They should know better if they're, like, adults.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Three thousand two hundred and seventy-one complaints since the first of the year about unruly passengers, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has shown that, if you want to play, you're going to pay, to the tune of tens of thousands of dollars -- sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, so maybe, Jeff, when you're flying, you should be calmer and nicer. Maybe that's the problem.\par \par \uc2 But that's wild. That's absolutely--\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Works for me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, my friend, Jeff Flock.\par \par \uc2 That one kid is not going to buy -- that kid behind him is not buying this whole behave thing.\par \par \uc2 All right, you're feeling it on the ground too. In case you didn't check, for a while this morning, we had gasoline prices, certainly oil prices, at a six-year high. The problem seems to be \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and the so-called \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus countries. They can't agree on supply.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, there's more than enough demand to keep prices going still higher. So, don't think you're out of the woods because they can't strike a deal.\par \par \uc2 Patrick De Haan\uc2  knows it, the GasBuddy petroleum analysis head, following all this very closely at the pump. He's been uncannily prescient about these trends, which bothers me, because the trends still don't look good, right, Patrick?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK DE HAAN\uc2 , HEAD OF PETROLEUM ANALYSIS, GASBUDDY: No, they absolutely don't, Neil.\par \par \uc2 It's a little bit of the Hatfields and McCoys, it seems like, with his \uc2 OPEC\uc2  meeting. Everyone wants to increase production. But everyone wants a bigger slice of that increase in production. It came to a boiling point yesterday, with the UAE walking out of the meeting.\par \par \uc2 So here we are. Everyone agrees that more oil production is needed. Then you have bickering amongst \uc2 OPEC\uc2 . And, of course, oil prices, as you mentioned initially, touched nearly $77 a barrel. Of course, we closed down slightly today. But that doesn't end it.\par \par \uc2 I think this the sky's the limit; $80 could be coming here very shortly, maybe beyond that. And we're just starting to get in the just of the peak of the American driving season.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, if you think about it, to fill up a tank, I think the average now is 12 to 15 bucks more than last year at this time.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering, gas wise, what do you see happening.\par \par \uc2 DE HAAN: Well, price-wise, Neil, I think we could go up another 5 to 15 cents a gallon over the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 And there's no telling if that's going to be the peak. I think, if \uc2 OPEC\uc2  does increase oil production, we will see a peak in the next few weeks. But until this economy really slows down and demand slows down, we're not going to see any break at the pump.\par \par \uc2 And that could not -- that may not happen until later this fall, but even then, Neil, I think the question is, do the American commuters start taking back as offices reopen this fall? That could delay a downturn in demand and thus it could delay a decrease in price.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If \uc2 OPEC\uc2  can't do anything -- and you kind of touched on it. They can't agree. All things being equal, they don't budge on supply one way or the other, prices are just going to keep moving higher. Then that is unavoidable, right?\par \par \uc2 DE HAAN: Well, it looks to be that way.\par \par \uc2 In fact, if \uc2 OPEC\uc2  doesn't touch it, look for shale producers and start ramping up and potentially locking in contracts at these lofty valuations. The U.S. rig count is going up. But production in the U.S. really hasn't gone up. But if oil prices continue to advance, this gives a wide open door to us producers to fill that void.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know what is wild, though? And you and I have talk about it before, Pat. It's not stopping consumers. They're well aware of it. It's not that they don't notice what's going on at the pump, but they're still filling up and going out.\par \par \uc2 I just wonder what they will be doing in the fall.\par \par \uc2 DE HAAN: Yes, great question, Neil. I think people will start returning to the office this fall. American commutes will come back.\par \par \uc2 To touch on your point about July 4, a 7 percent increase in gasoline demand, according to GasBuddy data, from Memorial Day, 7 percent over the last holiday, that we're really starting to see demand kick into high gear. The last thing we can afford right now is for \uc2 OPEC\uc2  to be dragging its legs behind on this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I'm glad you said dragging its legs there.\par \par \uc2 Patrick De Haan\uc2 , thank you very much, the GasBuddy petroleum analysis head.\par \par \uc2 We will be monitoring that and some of the pressures there.\par \par \uc2 Also monitoring what is happening on the border. In case you didn't hear -- of course, you heard -- Governor Abbott wants to build a wall. He's got a lot of state governors sending troops there to help him. Here's the problem, though. Legally, can he do it?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, his overall poll numbers have not changed much, but when it comes to dealing with the border, increasingly -- and this is a new poll that shows most Americans disapprove of the way President Biden is handling it, as the number of migrants flooding in continues to rocket.\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin seeing it up front and personal for himself in La Joya, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 It's been thunderstorming out here pretty much all day long. But that hasn't slowed down any of the activity whatsoever. Take a look at this video we shot here right where we're standing this morning. In my four trips to the border during this surge, this was the largest single group I have ever seen apprehended out here.\par \par \uc2 This was at least 100 people all apprehended at one single time, mostly moms and kids. Actually saw some of the moms breast-feeding while they were waiting to be apprehended by Border Patrol. Some of the ones I talked to her from Nicaragua, Honduras, Guatemala. And border agencies have been telling us, in recent weeks, a lot of these folks have been testing positive for COVID.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at these photos right here. We heard Texas Governor Abbott calling for help from other states. And those reinforcements are now showing up. What you're looking at is Del Rio Sector, Florida State Troopers and Nebraska State Troopers working with Texas State Troopers.\par \par \uc2 They are on the ground now helping with operations up in Del Rio. More states are on the way. That's going to be Iowa, Idaho, Arkansas, and South Carolina. But there's a group that's not happy about these other states helping out. That group is called \uc2 LULAC\uc2 , the \uc2 League of United Latin American Citizens\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They sent a letter to President Biden on the Fourth of July, urging him to stop these states from sending their law enforcement here to the border, with their leaders writing in part -- quote -- "We are being invaded by governors of the defeated Confederacy to arm the border against brown women and children. This is an insurrection by recalcitrant and rebellious states 