y, he was completely ostracized all over social media. And they also started picking on his band members, just because he chose to speak his mind on this issue. What do you think about this one, Tammy?\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: Yes, this is another very good example, almost on the same theory of what we've just talked about, which is a mob that is decided that they're going to control the nature of what can be thought, what can be said, and what can happen to people. And this is fascinating to me, because, you know, in the 60s, you had, you know, big social issues regarding war, and, and all of that, this is a young man who actually did effectively a book review, not even a book review, but complementing a book to somebody who was, of course, investigating something. So, this really shows you that totalitarian direction of things, but it also shows you the power of one individual, because if they weren't afraid of the individual, they wouldn't be doing this to him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it's just unreal. If people dig into this, you're going to see that exactly. Like you said, he basically made a comment on a book, and then he said that it caused such trauma to his fellow band members that he had to leave the band. I mean, it's unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Tammy, thank you. Great to see you. We'll see you tonight on "HANNITY."\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: Thank you. Thanks, Martha. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, President Biden seemingly annoyed by reporters who asked him questions about U.S. troops leaving Afghanistan. We're going to have more on this real important story today on leaving Bagram Air Force Base after 20 years when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not going to answer any more question Afghanistan. Look, it's Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So this is the very last thing that they need in Surfside, Florida. It is a hurricane named Elsa, the first hurricane of the Atlantic season. You can see the path as it is determined, at this point, and the storm has already caused damage and flooding, and power outage outages in Barbados. You can see the huge winds coming through Bridgetown, Barbados today. No deaths or injuries that we have learned of thus far.\par \par \uc2 So, those tropical storm force winds could start hitting South Florida as soon as Sunday, according to the local officials there. They say that they're taking steps to protect equipment as the search and rescue operation continues to do the best that they can. They've got a lot of conflicting circumstances down there, structures that they're very concerned about that could topple. They are continuing to go through the wreckage of the condo collapse they're in Surfside. Look at the work that these people are doing, it's just incredible.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says that family members mentioned the threat of hurricanes as a concern. And also, he says they brought up climate change when he met with them yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's interesting to me, I didn't raise it, but how many of the survivors and how many families talked about the impact of global warming, how much -- they know exactly when they talked about sea level is rising. But I don't think there is, at this point, any definitive judgment as to why it collapsed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Today, we learned 20 people are now confirmed dead, 128 missing. There's the wall of remembrance and also the wall of pictures of those who are missing. The latest victims that were recovered, one was especially devastating for these first responders. National Correspondent Matt Finn reporting live from Surfside. Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: And, Martha, the Miami-Dade Mayor now confirms that the body of a seven-year-old girl was found in the rubble. That seven-year-old girl's father is a firefighter who was working as a rescuer on this site. His colleagues had to notify him that his seven-year- old daughter was found dead. Also, this afternoon, Fox News spoke to the father and brother of 26-year-old Nicole Langesfeld and her husband, Louis, who just moved into the tower a few months ago. They're planning to celebrate their recent marriage. Nicole's father and brother tells us, they have hope that Nicole and her husband could defy the conditions and come out alive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PABLO LANGESFELD, DAUGHTER MISSING IN CONDO COLLAPSE: It's a never-ending nightmare we just want to find out. Anyway, she's my baby.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN LANGESFELD, SISTER MISSING IN CONDO COLLAPSE: I know every day it's harder, but my faith and hope have not diminished one bit. It's all there and until I'm told otherwise, I will not lose it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: And right now, rescuers are on the fragile rubble using their hands, cranes, dogs the sound of drills echoing in the air here. They are not letting up. One restaurant told me when they're on the pile, they have to be so extremely careful not to cause any disturbance or more instability. Also, newer cell phone video now shows the moments after the collapse when people discovered the disaster.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh my God (inaudible). Oh my God. Oh my God\par \par \uc2 FINN: And now, the Champlain Towers Condominium Association, which is under heavy fire, is writing in a statement part, the surviving members of the Champlain Tower South Condominium Association board have concluded that in the best interest of all concerned parties, an independent receiver should be appointed to oversee the legal and claims process. The collapse of Champlain Tower South is an unspeakable tragedy that has devastated our community. We are grieving and our hearts ache for those who have been lost and for their families.\par \par \uc2 We expect an evening briefing in a couple hours. We'll update you on the status of that hurricane. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, Matt, thank you very much. Matt Finn in Surfside, Florida. And after nearly 20 years, as of today, all US and NATO forces have left Bagram Air Base in Afghanistan, turning it back over to Afghan Forces. It was built by the US and NATO. It was the epicenter of the mission to oust the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and to hunt down al-Qaeda after 911. None of the recent \uc2 Taliban\uc2  resurgence has swayed President Biden's commitment to leave completely by September the 11th of this year. It leaves a huge void in the region, a place that Iran, China and Russia all seek to influence.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Florida Congressman Michael Waltz former member, former Green Beret and Commander who has served multiple tours in Afghanistan. Congressman Waltz, always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is a big day and I think of background history, with regard to Afghanistan, when the Soviet Union pulled out after many years in Afghanistan, and now, history is repeating itself. Obviously, there are some very different circumstances involved, but what goes through your mind when you see this quiet Bagram Airbase?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, first, Martha, let me just say my heart goes out to the survivors at Surfside and my thanks to the first responders from all over the world. As a Floridian, our hearts are breaking.\par \par \uc2 But on Bagram Air Base, Martha, this policy is just asinine. It doesn't make sense. This one air base if you look at where it's located. From that base, our drones and Special Forces can go after half the world's terrorist organizations that still exist in the Afghan-Pakistani border region. But it's also a sandwich between China, Russia and Iran. In that one base, we can reach out and affect the battlefield for four out of our five major adversaries, that's in the national security strategy. Basically, all of our enemies, except North Korea, out of that one base.\par \par \uc2 If we ever go to some type of conflict in Taiwan, it would be nice to have a base in China's backyard, or in Russia southern flank or Iran's eastern flank. But we just gave it away for free, that was bought with so much blood and treasure. And it just makes absolutely zero sense to me. And the thing is, Martha, the intelligence community and the military have been very clear that al-Qaeda will come roaring back in the wake of a \uc2 Taliban\uc2  takeover that we're seeing as we speak. And when we have to send our soldiers back in with no bases, with local allies that have been decimated because they didn't evacuate them, there will be Afghan and American blood on this administration's hands.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. President Biden has really had a long-standing policy that he wanted to see us leave Afghanistan. Today, he got questions about it from reporters, and he seemed a little bit caught off guard by that he was out there to talk about the jobs numbers. And here's what he said after a couple of questions about Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not going to answer any more questions about Afghanistan. Look, it's Fourth of July. I'm concerned that you guys are asking me questions that I'll answer next week. This is a weekend. I'm going to celebrate it. There's great things happening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think of that answer, Congressman Waltz?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, it was a non-answer because he has no plan. We have no other basis in the region. No other country has agreed to allow us to have our Special Forces or drones, or things that will need to keep a lid on al- Qaeda, ISIS and the other terrorist organizations. And basically, we're going to see, I fear, a repeat of when Obama pulled out of Iraq too soon. And we saw ISIS establish a caliphate the size of Indiana, attacks across Europe and the United States, and untold genocide and massacre across the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 I fear for women in Afghanistan, I fear for our interpreters and our local allies. And I fear for the American soldiers that we may have to send back in without the support that they should have had in that base and otherwise.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. No one wants to go back to the days of the growth of ISIS in that region and all of the horrific things that we saw unfold when they did that. We hope that's not where we're going. Michael Waltz, we appreciate your insight as always. Thank you very much, Congressman. Good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Happy Independence Day.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Thanks, Martha, and have a good Fourth, all right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You too. So, there are new reports out there that connect funding from the Pentagon, potentially to the Wuhan Institute of Neurology. So, we're trying to follow the money here and get the full story and picture for you. That's coming up next, stick around.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the Pentagon reportedly gave millions of tax dollars to a group that then funded in part the coronavirus research at the lab in Wuhan. So, the New York Post has been looking into this, reporting on this, and where the money went. They believe that some of it went to the \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2  for a program called Scientific Research Combating Weapons of Mass Destruction. Now, the Daily Mail reports that EcoHealth, \uc2 Dr. Peter Daszak\uc2  , remember that name, he's involved with that very closely. They got around a government ban on so called gain of function research using a loophole to protect public health or national security. No word on whether they can definitely tie that money back to the Wuhan lab, but they are working on following that trail. The former CDC Director and a former COVID Investigator are among the experts who say that they do believe that COVID leaked from that lab.\par \par \uc2 The Post did not get a response from the Pentagon. Fox News has also asked, so this is a moving story, and we will stay on it as we get more information.\par \par \uc2 All right, everybody. So as Americans get ready for a big weekend to celebrate the Fourth of July, likely to see packed beaches. Look that guy doing a beer funnel in the middle there. Lots of fun is going to be had parades, barbecue. But it's really important that we remember where we were a year ago, back in July of 2020.\par \par \uc2 And the facts are these that we got from there to where we are now in large part due to Operation Warp Speed, which was of course launched by the Trump administration. So I want to bring in Dr. Brett Giroir. He was part of the Trump White House Coronavirus Task Force. Admiral Giroir, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 I was thinking about this at the beginning of the week. And we just -- I think it's really important that we stop and remember where we were a year ago, tiny gatherings, maybe three people to get together on the Fourth of July. And I think it's also really important that people like to give credit where credit is due, and to realize that if it weren't for the work of these pharmaceutical companies and the Trump administration who really did something that most people thought could not be done, to have a huge majority of the country vaccinated by now.\par \par \uc2 BRETT GIROIR, FORMER HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES ASSISTANT SECRETARY: You are exactly right, Martha. And we are in a very, very different place. Just look at January 20th when we left office, we had two vaccines authorized one just around the corner. We had already procured 900 million doses with an option to buy 2 billion more. We had 70,000 vaccine sites. We had bought 1 billion needles, all the dry ice that we needed. And we told the American people that by June, everyone who wanted a vaccine could get one. And that's exactly what you see.\par \par \uc2 The attempts to rewrite history by the Biden administration are going to fail because the facts are so strong. Sure, the Biden administration deserves credit for implementing the plan, but that is what they did. And it is important to remember people like General Perna, Moncef Slaoui, the secretary, the president, the vice president who made this happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, absolutely. And I think back to covering this story, and you and I spoke many times over the course of it. And the feeling was that it was way overly optimistic to think that we were going to have vaccines that would be ready to go in the beginning of 2021. And then, the months that follow that heading into the spring, that that was not going to happen, that it had never been done before.\par \par \uc2 And then, there was also discussion that if we didn't get them done on time, that we couldn't trust them. I remember the vice president, now Vice President Kamala Harris, she was very reluctant to say that she would put confidence in a vaccine that had been rushed through this process. Do you, today, have any reservations about these vaccines at all, as we continue to go into a bigger part of the population? And there are some reports, you know, small reports, but there are some reports of some problems that some people have, what's your take?\par \par \uc2 GIROIR: Well, there always going to be one in a million or one in a half a million type of side effects. But clearly, for those who are over 18, with no other contraindications, these vaccines are greater than 90%, effective, nearly 100% effective at preventing death. And they really are remarkable. They were developed quickly, but there are no shortcuts to safety and efficacy.\par \par \uc2 I do you think we need to look carefully at younger children who are at very, very low risk of the natural disease, and we need to get all the data. But clearly for adults and anyone at risk, these vaccines are a godsend. It was nearly a medical miracle. But as I say, the harder you work, the luckier you get. And it's the result of the president's leadership and incredible people across the whole of society working together to make this happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and including the work that you put in. Admiral Giroir, thank you very much. It was a rocky road and a difficult time, and there are still some things that are being dealt with, with some of these variants and questions, as you say, about some of the vaccines and ages and all of that. But by and large, we're in a very different place now. And thank you, sir. Good to have you on today.\par \par \uc2 GIROIR: Thank you, great.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So some big ego billionaires with some very big toys, rocketing themselves into space to outdo each other. Branson, Bezos and Musk, it looks like a movie, right? Who's going to win this risky, I would remind you, race that begins next week. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD BRANSON, BUSINESS MAGNATE: Astronauts 001, Richard Branson. I'll be evaluating the customer spaceflight experience.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Billionaire bragging rights are on the line as Richard Branson says he is going to space on July the 11th, nine days before fellow billionaire Jeff Bezos is scheduled to blast off in his own Blue Origin spacecraft. Fox Business Correspondence Susan Li joins me now with the story. Hi, Susan.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, CORRESPONDENCE: Hi there. Yes. So we're talking about the billionaires' space battle to get into orbit first, as you mentioned, Richard Branson, Virgin Galactic, going up there nine days before Jeff Bezos heads up on his Blue Origin rocket on July the 20th. Now, we're not talking about going to the moon here, Martha. These are just short trips to the edge of space. And within just a few minutes as zero gravity, alongside bases will be 82-year-old Wally Funk, who trained at the NASA Mercury Program 60 years ago, and despite outperforming some male astronauts in NASA training, Funk never got the chance to take off up until now.\par \par \uc2 Four people will be on board, that Blue Origin rocket, that includes an unnamed $28 million auction winner who will have the fourth seat. Of course, not to be forgotten in all of this is Elon Musk, his \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  , the first private space company to carry US astronauts on board to the International Space Station. And as you know, if you read on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , Martha, there's been a back and forth between Musk and Bezos on space supremacy. But Elon Musk himself has never gone into space. At least not yet, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Susan, thank you very much. Fun to watch that competition.\par \par \uc2 All right, so before we go, we have a tribute to a very dear friend of ours, George Coburn. He was a WORLD WAR II veteran, a good and kind man, father, grandfather, great grandfather and husband, and he passed away on June 25th at the age of 95. George is one of the central characters in my book, "Unknown Valor." He fought alongside my uncle, Harry Gray, when they were on Iwo Jima. They hailed from neighboring towns near Boston, and George kept his great Boston accent until the end. As George put it, the two teenagers were closer than brothers.\par \par \uc2 George was with Harry when he was killed on Iwo Jima at the age of 18. Last year, at the 75th anniversary of Iwo Jima Gala in Washington, DC, I had the pleasure of being with George and his wife, Carlene, and their wonderful, wonderful family. He was so proud of all of them. I will never forget seeing George twirling Carlene, his wife, on the dance floor. Look at these two. It was such a special night and I'm so grateful that we all shared it together.\par \par \uc2 So today, we all remember George Coburn. We celebrate his life as his family lays him to rest at Cape Canaveral National Cemetery. God bless you, George Coburn, a hero, a life well-lived, sir. Rest in peace, we will miss you. Our thanks to all of the Coburn family for their friendship.\par \par \uc2 So that's the story of this Friday, July 2, 2021. Have a great Independence Day weekend, everybody, thanks for being here with us. We'll look forward to seeing you on Monday. Have a great one with your families. Your World with Neil Cavuto starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210703eh7200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Is Las Vegas Back?; Billionaires in Space; Interview With Rep. Kevin Brady (R-TX); Storm May Hamper Condo Rescue Efforts; Interview With U.S. Secretary of Labor \uc2 Eugene Scalia\uc2  ; June Jobs Report Beats Estimates; Holiday Travel Rush\b0\par\par\uc2 Rick Reichmuth, Phil Keating, Peter Doocy, Anita Vogel, Jeff  Flock, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,251 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The great rush is on for the July 4 long weekend.\par \par \uc2 The scene of the Philadelphia Airport, where we're told things are crowded, very crowded, an L.A. highway, where they're even more crowded, and Wildwood, New Jersey, where they're packing them in, and at the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2  , \uc2 Macy's\uc2  helping to ring out the closing bell on what has