 at least 130 airports around the country are going to be super short-staffed.\par \par \uc2 And you know what that means, long lines at security checkpoints. Oh, and don't forget to pack your mask even if you're vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AUGUSTO BERNAL, HOUSTON AIRPORT PUBLIC INFORMATION OFFICER: People are required to use their face mask at the airport. Really, they're not going to be able to go through the TSA without their masks. And our airlines are still requiring it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: Already, we have seen very long lines at airports across the country from Seattle to Texas to Atlanta, especially in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of these people say they are just so happy to travel again, they're willing to put up with all of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBARA, TRAVELER: I'm glad they made up their mind to just get it open. We did our vaccination. I'm sure people were ready for it. And it's just good to see people.\par \par \uc2 DIANA MORSE, TRAVELER: Hopefully, we will see some fireworks somewhere. But just spending quality time together, that's really the key, and being able to travel and do that is awesome.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOGEL: And the other group of folks who are very happy about this increased airline travel, those very smart investors who bought major airline stocks at the height of the pandemic, back in May of 2020, when they were very cheap.\par \par \uc2 Neil, just a few numbers for you, \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  stock 140 percent since May of 2020, \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  stock up over 160 percent since may of 2020, those investors definitely in the green -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. They should get a priority spot online, shouldn't they? But we will see about that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Anita Vogel, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 There is good news. We're all coming out and the demand is there. We're all psyched and all that, until we get to the gas station.\par \par \uc2 And that is something that Jeff Flock is seeing pretty much across every area he covers right now driving aimlessly. He's been doing this all day. I think right now he's actually driven to Canada, but, anyway, the latest on what he's discovered along the way.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: The Tri-State Tollway, Neil. And there's no priority spots out here.\par \par \uc2 Maybe you can see. Take a look at the traffic, backed up here. Tristate would be Indiana, Illinois and Wisconsin, by the way. Yes, traffic everywhere and high gas prices. Take a look at the numbers on this, highest gas prices since 2014, $3.13 the average gallon of regular right now.\par \par \uc2 It's a nickel more than it was just a week ago, almost a dime more than it was a month ago, and almost $1 more than it was a year ago. But, typically, under some circumstances, maybe people wouldn't travel as much, maybe they'd be madder.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, look at these numbers from the AAA. They say we're going to have a record Independence Day on the road; 43.6 million people will take to the roads this year. That's a whole lot more than the 32 million last year in the midst of the pandemic, but even more than the pre-pandemic record.\par \par \uc2 So we're all the way back as far as travel. And the folks at AAA say it's because people have been pent up. They don't mind spending the extra money right now. Maybe at some other time, they would, but not now. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEANETTE MCGEE, AAA: No one likes to pay more at the pump. And when those prices go up, and when they're the most expensive prices we have seen in seven years, that's not good news for a consumer.\par \par \uc2 But it's really not impacting how we're going to travel because we're just excited to get out there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: Excited to be out there. Yes, I guess that kind of goes a long way toward making you not as angry as you might, about $1 increase in the last year in the price of gasoline -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just keep looking at the road, my friend. Just keep looking at the road.\par \par \uc2 You're the best, Jeff. Thank you very much, Jeff Flock driving aimlessly around the country here, just sort of taking a look at what's happening.\par \par \uc2 So you have probably noticed it as well. And you have probably noticed it if you're intending to fly, that it's a little bit tense up in the skies, and they have got all sorts of advice for it. So I could sum it up in one word, patience.\par \par \uc2 Everyone's going to get to where they have to go, but they have to be patient. Easier said than done.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some advice on all of this from Emily Kaufman, TheTravelMom.com, travel expert, calm woman in our own right.\par \par \uc2 So, Emily, we need your help here, because we have seen all these videos of people going nuts on planes, some of them not really keen on the whole mask thing, crying children who the parents get kicked off planes if their kid doesn't put a mask on. So you hear the horror stories, and you hear the horror stories that there aren't enough TSA agents on hand to deal with the crowds, the long lines, at that.\par \par \uc2 Help. What do we do?\par \par \uc2 EMILY KAUFMAN, TRAVEL EXPERT: Neil, it's a really exciting time for travel.\par \par \uc2 And if we focus on all those negative things, we're missing that it's a really wonderful time for us to be returning back to travel. And, yes, things are not quite up to what we expected pre-pandemic. The infrastructure is not there yet. They're restaffing people.\par \par \uc2 The rental car industry sold off more than 700,000 cars during the pandemic to keep their companies afloat. So you got to have your patience everywhere.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things I would suggest to you, Neil, if you're planning to go with a rental car or a hotel or an airline, join that loyalty program before you hit the road or before you get on the plane, because you're going to get some perks and benefits that will help to make that less stressful for you, like a dedicated phone number that you can call, a separate line where you can check in, you can skip the lines at the counters for rental cars, and go right up to the kiosk.\par \par \uc2 So arm yourself with information. It'll help the entire situation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, but I hear that it's hard to get rental cars now, if you're so lucky, whether you're a member of a club or not.\par \par \uc2 I am concerned, though. I had a TSA representative with me earlier on not the FOX Business, which, if you don't get, Emily, you really should demand it. But I digress.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that came up was this idea that you have to get to the airport much earlier than before for domestic flights. Forget the hour to two-hour thing. More like three hours.\par \par \uc2 KAUFMAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I thought to myself, well, I might as well walk.\par \par \uc2 What's going on?\par \par \uc2 KAUFMAN: It's because the TSA is restaffing and getting up to speed. And we're all rusty.\par \par \uc2 Some of the people don't know what they need to do once they're in the TSA line to be prepared, to take out their laptops. Do I have to take my shoes off? Do I not, leave my shoes on?\par \par \uc2 So arm yourself with information from the TSA as well.That will help you through your line as best as you can. And, truthfully, there's excitement and positivity and fun all around travel right now. So let's keep that as the focus.\par \par \uc2 And, no, we're going to hit some speed bumps as we ramp back up. But it's a great time to travel. It's a great time to be back out there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I love your attitude, Emily, but try telling the people in these crowded planes.\par \par \uc2 And there's no liquor being offered in coach, at least. Of course, you travel first class, so you wouldn't know.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things -- I kid. One of the things I notice is that people - - that's a tension convention just waiting in the skies. How do you advise people dealing with that?\par \par \uc2 KAUFMAN: Well, I think you're a little mistaken. I don't only fly first class. I fly coach a lot of the time as well.\par \par \uc2 And I really believe that your attitude and your energy is such an important thing to bring to your travel experience. And people are going to be uptight. They're going to be uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 I try to smile at people, be patient, be kind, and not engage on an aggressive level. I really think that we're all fortunate and blessed to be returning to travel. And, hopefully, that's the attitude everybody's going to bring.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Not always.\par \par \uc2 The guy who sits in front of me invariably puts his seat back full reclined. And I'm so tempted, so tempted, Emily, to kick that dude off the plane, even midair. But you're right. I should calm down. Everyone should calm down.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for your advice, Emily Kaufman, following all of this, with some very good -- we all ought to look at the half-full glass. I just spent all that time, right, looking at the half-empty glass. It is the half-empty glass, just so you know.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, did any of you see this? You're cooking up for a barbecue? Well, the White House put this out earlier. And I know numbers, some numbers. And they're talking about the fact that the cost of a Fourth of July cookout is actually down about 16 cents from last year.\par \par \uc2 And I'm thinking to myself, wow, I should be very grateful, until I started looking under the hood and the barbecue grill. And it's really not all the items that you would have. And we're going to rifle through some of these that might have raised eyebrows with you, like the prices that have gone up markedly over the last year.\par \par \uc2 And I might point out these comparisons the administration has been making was against last year, when prices were already spiking because of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But, anyway, Liz Peek is here.\par \par \uc2 And we're looking at almost virtually every group, the price hikes that go across the board on almost every item you buy. And I'm just wondering, putting out a statement like this, Liz, to make you think it's a bargain this year, I'm not taking anything away from barbecues, but it won't be a bargain, if you're going to notice that things are a lot more expensive.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 LIZ PEEK, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Neil, taking the glass-half-full approach, I think, is great. We have a lot to celebrate, Americans do, this time of year.\par \par \uc2 I think price stability is not one of them. We know that prices overall for consumers are rising about 5 percent. And what's interesting is, the focus has gone from goods, the kind of thing that the White House was uncomfortably tweeting about, to wages.\par \par \uc2 And that is where you get into a really bad cycle. The TSA is not the only group offering $1,000 bonuses to hire people. All kinds of businesses are resorting to higher wages, bonuses, et cetera. We don't have a very good fix right now on what the wage hikes are going to look like for June, July and August.\par \par \uc2 But rumor has it and all the indicators, the PPIs and the NFIB, the small business indicators are that wages are going through the roof. And that means higher prices on pretty much everything. And, by the way, as far as barbecues are concerned--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And we have already seen it. We have already seen it, right?\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Yes, we are. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mean, I'm going to follow up on that, because we're going through some of these.\par \par \uc2 I don't think it's something that I would just pick up on. I notice it when my favorite Italian sausage has doubled in price. But I do notice it when it's across the board. So, for the administration maybe to say, go ahead and celebrate your barbecue, I get that. That's good. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 But some of the items where it's talking about everything is still relatively cheap, it is not. It's not at all.\par \par \uc2 So I'm just wondering, does this just anger people when they hear that? Are they shopping at the same store you are, I am? You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Well, that's a really good question.\par \par \uc2 General Mills\uc2  , I think it was, came out two days ago and said they're seeing inflation of 7 percent. So whatever people are seeing is not being reflected in the data right now. And, by the way, the reason the Biden White House did this is because they're really under pressure about this.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of concern that the big spending bills that already have passed and that the White House wants to pass are driving higher prices. And that's going to be a big talking point as we get close to the elections in 2022. It's a big talking point also now in terms of Fed policy.\par \par \uc2 The Fed has not retrenched in its enormous bond-buying program. Balance sheet is up $4 trillion over last year. Any sensible person has to begin to wonder whether that's not driving these higher prices.\par \par \uc2 So, to your point, Neil, people are seeing it. But -- and on the other thing, by the way, is, it's about to get worse, because rents are accelerating. Rents are a big part of the consumer price index.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: So that's going to lead to, I think, bigger-than-expected numbers this fall.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, on this, everyone else who looks at this in a positive way, a half-full-glass way, I guess, says it's transitory, it's not going to last long. That's why the markets are racing ahead, the Dow and S&P racing ahead, the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  racing head, certainly the S&P.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  hit records today. So, the market seemed to be thinking that this is a short-term issue. Do you think it is?\par \par \uc2 PEEK: I don't think it is.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, the president of the Saint Louis Fed doesn't think it is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: There are a lot of people looking at this stuff.\par \par \uc2 And that's why I mentioned wages and saying, no, we're in a bad cycle right now. It's going to continue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Thank you very, very much, I think. But you kind of gave us a balanced glass there, the half-full and the half-empty side.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, again, if you look at these estimates on price pressures and that they're really small for your barbecue, they're not.\par \par \uc2 But eat up. You know it if you have already gotten your goodies. The Italian sausage thing is sticking in my craw. And I'm not going the asparagus route. That's all I'm saying.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have got a lot coming up here, including the president not only touting how much you're going to pay for, well, your barbecue, but the fact that you're doing OK when it comes to jobs. In fact, things are so OK, that he took time out to say, we're off to the races.\par \par \uc2 But are we?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I told you about records in the market today, the S&P 500, \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  at all-time highs.\par \par \uc2 A lot of it started early this morning. We got word of the June jobs report, much stronger than expected; 850,000 more Americans found jobs last month. That was more than almost anyone thought.\par \par \uc2 The White House wasting very little time taking credit for all of that.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy is there with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 They're taking credit because they see these numbers, 150,000 more jobs created in May than expected, as proof that the president's plans for a post-pandemic economy are panning out already.\par \par \uc2 They're also poring over some other numbers, including those stats that show that the cost of a Fourth of July cookout is 16 cents less.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, does the White House think that 16 cents off of barbecue has more of an impact on people's lives than gas being $1 more at this time, at this Fourth of July?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I would say, if you don't like hot dogs, you may not care of the reduction of costs. You don't have to like hot dogs.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: You can't buy a hot dog for 16 cents.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: A reduction of.\par \par \uc2 I will say that what we are most focused on is the fact that we have created now more than three million jobs since the president took office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Many of the other questions at that briefing and after the president's morning event were about Afghanistan. The president earlier pledged a full withdrawal by 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Now officials are saying that, by the end of August, troops will be home. The president talked a little bit about it today when asked, but then he apparently reached his limit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not going to answer any more quick question on Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Are you concerned--\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, it's Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: On the economy--\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Are you concerned about Fourth of July gatherings--\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm concerned that you guys are asking me questions that I will answer next week. But I'm -- this is a holiday weekend. I'm going to celebrate it. There is great things happening.\par \par \uc2 The economy is growing faster than any time in 40 years. We've got a record number of new jobs. COVID deaths are down 90 percent. Wages are up faster than any time in 15 years. We're bringing out -- bringing our troops home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And it is a holiday weekend. They have got the South Lawn set up -- or they're getting it set up for a celebration tomorrow that 1,000 invitations went out for. There's going to be some fireworks as well.\par \par \uc2 Before the president can start with the holiday weekend, though, he has remarks at the 5:00 hour at a teachers convention here in Washington, D.C. -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Peter, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy.\par \par \uc2 All right, to the former Labor Secretary of the United States under Donald Trump Eugene Scalia.\par \par \uc2 Very good to see you, Secretary.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE SCALIA\uc2  , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF LABOR: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The administration took credit for this, these three million new jobs since taking office. It's all them. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SCALIA: Well, it's a good jobs report. Adding 850,000 jobs in a month is very strong. And so the president is right to celebrate this jobs report.\par \par \uc2 What it reflects is a reopening economy. And that's a result of some really important decisions and investments made more than a year ago in Operation Warp Speed and elsewhere that has led to this very effective vaccination program, which President Biden has faithfully and effectively implemented.\par \par \uc2 He certainly gets credit for that too. But I would not attribute the number to his economic policies. And let me make just a couple quick points about the report, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Again, it's a good report. No report is perfect. One thing that jumped out at me about this report is that we added jobs back in the places where you would add them back when you stop shutdowns. So we reopened our schools, and we added a lot of jobs in education. We opened restaurants and bars, a lot of jobs added in leisure, hospitality, hotels.\par \par \uc2 I noticed that laundry services are hiring again. That's all good. But it's a direct result from reopening.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the policy impact, what we don't see is jobs being added back quickly in manufacturing last month, construction, health care. I do think that the $300-a-week federal unemployment plus-up which the president put in place through September is keeping some people on the sidelines. This number could have been higher, actually, as could the reports for April and May.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it's interesting you mention that too.\par \par \uc2 This idea that much of the improvement might have been coming from states where they dropped those extended federal benefits, and almost forcing people to find work, many have been arguing that that was a big catalyst here. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 SCALIA: Well, Neil, this monthly report is a very important, closely watched report on the national unemployment picture.\par \par \uc2 But my view for nearly a year now has been that, in some ways, the state unemployment report that comes out later in the month is more telling and more interesting, because we have seen huge variance in states' ability to put people back to work.\par \par \uc2 Three states that we watched closely during COVID were New York, California and Florida. And what we saw the last time we saw state job reports was that New York and California's unemployment nearly 8 percent. It's two points higher than the national average, whereas Florida, under Governor DeSantis, is below 5 percent. It's a full point below the national average.\par \par \uc2 So we will learn more about the national -- about the state-by-state picture later in the month. I think we will learn from that whether those states that are eliminating the plus-up are seeing the results of that.\par \par \uc2 And we will also see whether regulatory high-tax states like New York in California are still having a hard time getting their economies going again.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Eugene Scalia\uc2  , thank you very much. Have a safe Fourth of July weekend, Secretary.\par \par \uc2 SCALIA: Have a great Fourth.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Secretary, thank you again.\par \par \uc2 SCALIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, the count and the rescue still goes on in Florida, now complicated by a hurricane that's barreling down.\par \par \uc2 We're on top of that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's a little early, but we have got our first hurricane of the season, Elsa, and barreling toward Florida.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot we don't know, but we do know what's happening with those rescue efforts and why this is such a worry -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, day nine of the rescue effort in Southern Florida after that condo collapse, and complicated by a major storm potentially coming South Florida's way.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest first from Phil Keating in Surfside.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Phil.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 More than three million pounds of concrete has been removed from the scene, allowing search-and-rescuers more access to remove rubble, dig deeper and look for bodies and hopefully survivors.\par \par \uc2 Two more found overnight, bodies, that is. Tragically, one of the two was a 7-year-old girl, the daughter of a firefighter who was on the scene when other firefighters pulled her lifeless body out of the rubble. So he saw her.\par \par \uc2 As firefighters carried her out on a gurney, first responders lined up solidly as they carried her by.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANIELLA LEVINE CAVA\uc2  (D), MAYOR OF MIAMI-DADE COUNTY, FLORIDA: Every night since this last Wednesday has been immensely difficult for everybody and particularly the families that have been impacted.\par \par \uc2 But last night was uniquely different. It was truly different, different and more difficult for our first responders.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEATING: At the end of the president and first lady's day-long meetings yesterday with first responders and families of the missing, they stopped at the memorial wall near the scene and paid their respects.\par \par \uc2 And newly obtained cell phone video shows the big concrete dust cloud, the chaos and the confusion In the minutes right after much of the Surfside condo tower fell to the ground. Survivors rescued people out of the rubble and those standing on their balconies screaming to be saved, those people telling those below that the stairwells they could have gone down were simply gone.\par \par \uc2 So they were rescued by firefighter snorkel trucks. Crews searching this week say they're finding a lot of the victims in their collapsed bedrooms, as most of the residents were sleeping at 1:30 in the morning, when suddenly they and their building plummeted to the ground.\par \par \uc2 Today is day nine of the search-and-rescue operation, not only for survivors, but also for the dead. A lot of the family members who are starting to believe their loved ones are dead, they at least want their bodies to give them proper funerals.\par \par \uc2 And the searching of the pile of rubble could actually have to be paused again. That is because what was Tropical Storm Elsa is now a Cat 1 hurricane, and it is barreling through the Caribbean very soon. And, ultimately, it appears, by the models, will be impacting South Florida, which includes Sunrise, beginning on Sunday -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just incredible. Phil Keating, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 So, what are we looking at with Elsa right now? How dangerous will it be?\par \par \uc2 Rick Reichmuth, FOX's chief meteorologist, on all of that.\par \par \uc2 What are we looking at here, Rick?\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, in general, this is a little bit early certainly to get a major hurricane, a little bit early even to be getting a hurricane, especially in the Eastern Caribbean, where this storm is right now. It's an area that is typically fairly unfavorable for hurricanes this time of the season.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of wind shear that typically breaks these storms apart. That said, take a look at this, an 85-mile-a-hour hurricane, became a hurricane just about 10 hours ago in across parts of Eastern Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 Storm tracking this, pretty good agreement on what happens over about the next two-and-a-half days, very close here to the islands of Cuba, Hispaniola, which is Haiti and Dominican Republic. Those mountains oftentimes really rip storms apart.\par \par \uc2 And if it gets very close here, we will see really the storm weaken quite a bit over some of these mountains. If it stays over water, maybe has a little bit more time to at least stay a little more organized.\par \par \uc2 Now, things are in quite good agreement that it's going to be running either over or parallel to these islands here. And then it makes this right-hand turn. A lot of disagreement still, a big spread in our modeling that we look at here. The official track is that one you see the red, the official track from the National Hurricane Center.\par \par \uc2 That would bring it to the West Coast of Florida, not to the East Coast, where Sunrise is. That said, I'm sure we will get some moisture out of this all across parts of South Florida, say, into Sunday late, maybe Monday and Tuesday from the storm.\par \par \uc2 Neil, the last thing that's really worth pointing out, this is the earliest we have ever gotten to five named storms, so this Elsa now the fifth storm already in this really young hurricane season. It just started on June 1.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this. This is the average of storms. Here we are in the season. You see this very low amount of storms typically. The bulk of the energy that we will see for hurricanes yet to come. And five named storms so far this season certainly doesn't bode well -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Rick, very, very much.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we will be monitoring all these developments live tomorrow morning and the status of Elsa and how it's affecting the recovery, or could, efforts going on in South Florida, tomorrow beginning at 10:00 a.m. on this channel. We will keep you posted on all these developments.\par \par \uc2 We won't leave you waiting on a holiday weekend. We will be here to follow every step of it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, following the move among some progressives to find more money, because, apparently, taxing the rich isn't enough. Why only going after the $400,000-and-over crowd is proving a problem for progressives who want to spend more, and now are considering taxing more -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Remember that old line, the problem with socialism is eventually you run out of other people's money? I'm kind of like conflating things here.\par \par \uc2 But the problem for a lot of progressives right now who want to see still more spending is, they think the president has limited their ability to raise money for that spending by just going after the very rich, the $400,000-and-over crowd.\par \par \uc2 Now, the president so far is not in this camp, but they are -- or at least urging him to look increasingly at may be lowering that threshold.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Brady\uc2  , the Texas Republican, is the ranking member of the House Ways and Means Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of this?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN BRADY\uc2  (R-TX): So, already, though, what's interesting, the president is breaking that pledge, according to the liberal \uc2 Tax Policy Center\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Recently, their analysis shows that about 75 percent of middle-class families will see a tax increase next year. That's going to rise to nearly all middle-class families. That's just with President Biden's crippling tax increases already.\par \par \uc2 But you're right. I think liberals know they can't hope to pay for all this spending, especially if they drive these entitlement costs into the tens of trillions of dollars. They're going to have to search for revenue everywhere.\par \par \uc2 But make no mistake. Even if the president sticks with his current plan, middle-class families and workers are going to pay an awfully steep price here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 As you know, the administration has responded to this study by saying that it is surmising that a wider group of people will be affected by tax hikes because of corporate tax hikes. It disputes that. But your point is well- taken.\par \par \uc2 I guess what I'm asking is whether this is going to be a problem, because, so far, no tax hike has taken effect. And I'm wondering if it's getting pushed back. Is it your sense that a tax hike, whether it comes to pass, doesn't come to pass this year?\par \par \uc2 BRADY: Yes, I don't think -- Neil, the Democrats don't have the votes in the House, certainly.\par \par \uc2 I think, more and more, especially those moderate Democrats who run as pro- business back home, they can't defend these tax increases on Main Street businesses, on local investors who investment in the community. They certainly don't want a tax rate so high around the world we're going to drive U.S. jobs, companies, research overseas, again, which the Biden tax plan will do.\par \par \uc2 And so -- and, ironically, while progressives are searching for more tax revenue, many of them are blocking, of course, a repeal of SALT cap, which is -- predominantly goes to households making a million dollars or more, and as the Tax Policy said, serves as a tax shelter against the Biden tax increases.\par \par \uc2 So, I'll tell you. There's a lot of crosswinds going on in Congress, but these tax increases are not a done deal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the SALT thing, and I do want to raise it with you, because Bernie Sanders hopes to find a middle ground here, Congressman, where the SALT thing goes away for, I believe, the $400,000-and-under crowd, but, over that, it remains.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 BRADY: Well, effectively, in the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, we did that.\par \par \uc2 When we repealed the AMT on families, we raised -- changed the marginal, the rates brackets to accommodate some of those high-tax states. Then we drove the child tax credit up in the families making $400,000, then come from a much lower threshold.\par \par \uc2 So that's why, in most of the states, that SALT cap affects 1 to 2 percent of taxpayers net.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congressman, as I was raising early on with the former labor Secretary \uc2 Eugene Scalia\uc2  , the administration is taking credit for this jobs boom in the latest report, 855,000 new jobs -- 850,000, I should say, better than three million, they say, under their stewardship.\par \par \uc2 They're the reason for that, and I guess, by extension, what they're saying, not you or your tax cuts. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRADY: Yes, they couldn't be more dead wrong.\par \par \uc2 And also, look, let's look at this month. Thank goodness it's improving, because April, May were disasters. Secondly, right now, the president's jobs plan is almost 700,000 jobs short of what he pledged to create. Wages are going up, but prices are going up faster than that.\par \par \uc2 We still have way too many people, workers, on the sidelines. Unemployment long term went up in today's report. Unemployment for all minorities and workers with less skills also worsened this week as well.\par \par \uc2 There are an awful lot -- this month as well. So there are an awful lot of troubling red flags in this report. I would not be taking a victory lap if I were them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congressman, very good seeing you. Have a safe Fourth, and a peaceful one, at that.\par \par \uc2 BRADY: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Kevin Brady\uc2  , the House Ways and Means Committee ranking member.\par \par \uc2 Those tax cuts really were his work. He was slaving on that for a long, long time. Then they all got it done together, working in concert with him.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot more coming up, including billionaires in space. It's not your imagination. Some of the richest guys on the planet are keen on leaving the planet, at least for a little while.\par \par \uc2 Why?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, it's trite to say billionaires and their toys. But these are expensive toys, and these aren't just any billionaires, \uc2 Richard Branson\uc2 , \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  in a race to get into space.\par \par \uc2 And, so far, it's Branson who as the sort of lead here. He plans to blast off, if briefly, into the heavens come July the 11th. Now, the 20th, on the 51st anniversary of the Apollo 11 moon landing, that had been the date that \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  was scheduling to get up into space. But it looks like Branson will beat him.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Mike Massimino, the former NASA astronaut, "2 Funny Astronauts" podcast.\par \par \uc2 I'm particularly proud of Mike, because an Italian-American who made it into the big heavens. And we always hold him up as a great example and a very, very funny guy, which you can be, as an astronaut. Did not know that.\par \par \uc2 Mike, it's very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MASSIMINO, FORMER NASA ASTRONAUT: Pleasure, Neil. Thanks for having me. And thanks for the great introduction.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, you deserve it.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you about this. As an astronaut, you went through hell to become an astronaut, and all the training and everything else. And if you think about what Branson and Bezos want to do is open this up to everybody. You don't have to go through the -- all the nonsense that you did and education and everything else.\par \par \uc2 You have enough money, slap it down, you're going to space. Does any part of that bug you?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: No.\par \par \uc2 As a matter -- I think it's great. I really do.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: I think that it's -- I think it's always been a goal of \uc2 NASA\uc2  to open up the opportunities for people to fly in space.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 NASA\uc2  -- this -- of course, this is being done outside of \uc2 NASA\uc2  by the two entrepreneurs you talked about. But it certainly was a goal of \uc2 NASA\uc2  's when NASA first started and the last couple of years trying to encourage this, so that a government program could lead to economic benefit and experiences and exploration for all.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm really happy about this moment. And I think what I went through was a tough -- was a tough way to do it to become a NASA astronaut, but I really loved it. And I don't know the first thing about trying to become a billionaire.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: So, I think I went the road that worked for me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I just get mad when people walk in and do and get the comforts of everything that you started.\par \par \uc2 It's sort of like when people go into my business, Mike. And I said, I spent years training on the prompter, and you just think you can walk in here?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that's a separate issue.\par \par \uc2 So, let me ask you about getting into space. Both of these flights, particularly Branson's, I think that's 30 minutes, up and down. You're technically into space when you are up 50 miles.\par \par \uc2 What -- can you explain that to me? Because a little different with Bezos' thing. When are you in space?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Well, we -- they set the boundary of space -- it's actually two standards. The American standard is 50 miles. The international, European standard is 100 kilometers, which is like 62 miles.\par \par \uc2 Those are nice round numbers. Really, what they were looking for was, when they started thinking about this decades ago, was where there's no more atmosphere. You're really outside of the atmosphere of the planet, there's really no traces of the atmosphere, just very small traces.\par \par \uc2 And that happens at about 50 miles. So, if you can find a way to get above 50 miles, you're technically an astronaut. There were some astronauts, some pilots who -- test pilots who flew the X-15 who got astronaut wings by flying the X-15 up 50 miles above our planet.\par \par \uc2 So, that's the goal. If you can get yourself above that somehow, then you can consider yourself to be an astronaut.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Who -- forget who is first. It looks like Branson might be first, if everything works out.\par \par \uc2 Does it matter? I mean, is this just the future?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: I think it's the future.\par \par \uc2 I think they're very different. You mentioned there's a little difference in the -- it's pretty almost exactly the same, a few minutes of weightlessness. There's little difference in the way they do it. Branson goes up with a mother ship, and then his spaceship takes off from that and goes to space and lands on a runway.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  is doing something similar.\par \par \uc2 You know what is the name of that spaceship, is New Shepard. \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  is very much a fan of the space program from way back when. He's named that spacecraft, that program after -- after Alan Shepard. He's kind of doing that same suborbital flight that Alan Shepard did.\par \par \uc2 So I don't really think that these are competitive programs. I think they're complementary. And I think it's great, actually, that one's going before the other, because we can get excited about one of them going up and then we can get excited about the Blue Origin spacecraft.\par \par \uc2 And I'm very happy for those teams. A lot of people have worked hard to make this happen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You worked harder to get into space.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, look, I mean, obviously you're not holding grudges, Mike, so that's fine.\par \par \uc2 That's fine.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: No.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will let you go.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: No, I think we want to get as many -- Neil, you need -- Neil, you need to go up, man. You need to get -- find a way, somehow, as reporter, going up there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I might be intrigued, because they don't require any, any physical workout. I might sign up for that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mike Massimino, thank you. So good having you. And thank you for all you have done and some great, great pioneering work in space.\par \par \uc2 Mike Massimino on that.\par \par \uc2 We are at Vegas, baby. And did you hear about this huge casino that's opening up, the first one on the Strip in, what, the better part of a decade? I mean, this thing is ginormous.\par \par \uc2 The guy behind it is here, and next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALES (singing): We love Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, that was the last time I was in Vegas. They love me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The astronaut thing, the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, it really just served as a shameless plug for us, for our next guest.\par \par \uc2 Scott Sibella runs Resorts World Las Vegas. He's the president. And it's the latest casino -- actually, it's been a long time coming, the latest casino to be on the Strip, better part of a decade. And this thing is a monster, not Scott, the casino he runs.\par \par \uc2 Scott, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT SIBELLA, PRESIDENT, RESORTS WORLD LAS VEGAS: Thanks, Neil. Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How's the reception so far?\par \par \uc2 SIBELLA: It's been overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 We opened a week ago. Thursday night, we had over 20,000 people visit. That's the first night. So it exceeded our expectations. And we have been busy ever since. So it's been a great opening.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, it's sort of like a leader for the rest of the Strip and Vegas, which certainly wanted to get back to business.\par \par \uc2 You have a lot of rooms to fill and a lot of -- you have one conference room, I think, that's the size of some stadiums. Tell me a little bit about that.\par \par \uc2 SIBELLA: Yes, we have a lot of conference space here.\par \par \uc2 And we have a 5,000-seat theater, 250,000 square feet of total convention space, over 40 restaurants, a lot of attractions, LED boards everywhere. And, like you said, it's just perfect timing for us to open. Las Vegas is coming back. We wanted to be ready for the Fourth of July. We got it open. And this is going to be a big weekend for us.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I understand that there were already a lot of advance bookings ahead of it. A lot of people heard about that.\par \par \uc2 But it's very clear to me, because we have heard from other casino CEOs, who have been saying, there's no doubt that Vegas is back. You're just waiting, again, on the corporate conferences and that sort of thing to complete the deal, right?\par \par \uc2 SIBELLA: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 The weekends look great for the rest of the year, especially the summer. Last weekend was strong, this weekend. And then we're starting to get the conventions to come back. I think the midweek business in the fourth quarter going into next year will start to pick up.\par \par \uc2 And I hope, by the first, second quarter, Vegas will be close to where it was before this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How are you handling the buffet thing?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SIBELLA: We did it different here. So we don't have a buffet because we have over 40 restaurants. We have an area called Famous Foods Street.\par \par \uc2 We have over 16 stalls, all different types of food from all over the world. And it's just been gangbusters. It's been exceeding our projections and busy every day. So, it's somewhat of a buffet, but a different type of structure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so that's counter to the old casino culture in Vegas. So you're risking a lot there. But I guess, with 40-some-odd restaurants, you can afford to.\par \par \uc2 Who is -- who's coming?\par \par \uc2 SIBELLA: Right now, it's the leisure customer, the casino customer.\par \par \uc2 We're starting to get the cities, the states that surround us, getting a lot of visitors. And, again, we see the convention business coming back later this year into next year. And when we get that back again, I think Vegas will be back on track by second quarter going into middle next year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And how many pools do you have? Seven? Eight? What -- did I hear this right?\par \par \uc2 SIBELLA: Yes, so we want to be best in class in everything.\par \par \uc2 So we have a pool deck that's five-and-a-half acres. You take the elevator down to the fifth floor, so it's easy to get to. There's seven pools there, all different themed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SIBELLA: There's an infinity pool that overlooks the Strip, so a little variety of everything for our visitors.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I wish you well, Scott Sibella.\par \par \uc2 It was a big shot in the arm to Vegas. I know they're pleased. I'm sure you are. The crowds are back. Vegas is back.\par \par \uc2 And who knows? Maybe we will plan -- we should plan a business trip there, right? We should.\par \par \uc2 All right, I will see you tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210702eh720008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Delivery Drivers Say Gas Supply Not An Issue; Jobless Claims Fall But Employers Lack Workers; White House Boast Of Cheaper Cookouts Misses Mark; High Levels of Violence Continue to Afflict Chicago; \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2  Releases Survey of Experts' Rankings of U.S. Presidents which Includes President Lincoln as Number One\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, William La Jeunesse, Peter Doocy, Phil Keating,  Bill Melugin, Mike Tobin \par \uc2 7,815 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Every time I (INAUDIBLE) the show, she says, is your one more thing about Dexter?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: You know, it's so unfair.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So, there you go, mom.\par \par \uc2 EMILY COMPAGNO, FOX NEWS HOST: That's (INAUDIBLE). I love that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That would have been a great celebrity sighting. That was --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't even want to know who you're going to say.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Yes, was --\par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX NEWS HOST: By the way, "GUTFELD!" is tonight. 11:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's right.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: (INAUDIBLE) Friday. That does it for us. Happy Fourth.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Happy Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Happy Fourth!\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Shannon Bream in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", as Americans pile into their vehicles this holiday weekend. Gas prices are surging and inflation fears are worsening. There's mixed news on the employment front as the economy produces more jobs, but the jobless rate creeps up.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Peter Doocy at the White House, he looks at the June job's report. We begin with national correspondent William La Jeunesse in Los Angeles tonight with the soaring gasoline prices. Hello, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Shannon, no matter where you shop at the grocery online or here at the pump, we are paying more.\par \par \uc2 But remember, we're kind of coming off of artificial COVID low. And, in fact, the national price is only about a quarter higher than the 10-year average. But, perception is reality, and the bad news is it's likely to get worse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh my God. This is ridiculous. I can't believe this, over $100 to fill my tank.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice-over): Gas prices reaching a seven-year high as some 48 million Americans hit the road this weekend.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The prices are too high as it is. So, it's not a good situation.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: The national average, $3.12 a gallon, nearly $1 higher than a year ago. Lowest price, Louisiana, at $2.75. The highest, California, where drivers in L.A. pay $4.79 for regular.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We just have to pay for whatever's on the screen, you know, and now just roll with it.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: In most areas, supply is not an issue. But in some cities at some stations, there is no gas. Why? A shortage of truck drivers.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK DEHAAN\uc2 , SENIOR PETROLEUM ANALYST, GAS BUDDY: Well, what's going on is not a gas shortage. There's actually plenty of gasoline, the summer refineries have churning out near all-time record amounts of gasoline. The problem is getting it from the terminal to the station.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Hauling 10,000 gallons of fuel isn't for everyone. It requires certification and training. During COVID, many driving schools shut down, leaving a shortage of tanker drivers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Down here in Denver, I've seen this pretty often lately.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: But supply isn't driving the price, demand is. The U.S. economy is red hot, inflation surge. In May five percent, the highest in 13 years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hotels, air fares, everything is going up.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Over the last year, use cars and trucks up 29.7 percent, clothing, transportation, food, and housing all more expensive.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Now, I don't understand why we have these prices going high. I don't understand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, as for supply, I'm told, don't worry. We have enough for this weekend and beyond. But as worldwide economies recover, you've got the pressure on oil drillers, carbon pricing, opposition oil, many see crude prices going higher, and that could pose a political problem for President Biden. Especially if prices reach about $4 a gallon nationally. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, we're all rooting against that. So, William La Jeunesse, out west. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 It was a big day on Wall Street with record closes for all three major indices. The Dow gained 153. The S&P 500 was up 32. \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  rose 117. For the week, the Dow gained a percentage points. The S&P 500 gained 1-2/3. \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  was up two.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the June unemployment picture, President Biden is choosing to focus on the positive, but there is more to the story.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy looks at the facts and figures for us tonight. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Shannon. The jobs number 850,000 in May beat expectations. And while some of the experts around here say it's just one set of numbers, they are more interested in watching the trends. The president is ready to call his economic plan a historic success.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The last time the economy grew at this rate was in 1984. And \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was telling us, it's morning and America. Well, is getting close to afternoon here.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): But as many states continue offering $300 a week and extra unemployment benefits, 9.3 million job openings remain a record.\par \par \uc2 JOHN CATALDO, RESTAURANT OWNER: There is nobody coming in for a job.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CATALDO: We're in need for -- from bus person, dishwasher, prep people, cook, pizza guy. Just no one comes in for work anymore.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That's OK for now according to the labor secretary.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2 , UNITED STATES LABOR SECRETARY: Yes, there's still job openings to fill. But this report was a good solid report. We're excited, it shows that President Biden's economic plans working.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Republican Congressman Kevin Brady warns, "President Biden has quickly fallen 679,000 jobs short of the projections for his jobs plan, workers are still staying on the sidelines, and while wages are going up, prices are going up faster." But the President says this is post pandemic progress.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Put simply, our economy is on the move. And we have COVID-19 on the run.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The White House's vaccination goal, 70 percent of adults by this weekend will be missed, but celebrations are OK.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You're going to be able to be with your families in backyard barbecues as we hoped.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: On the cheap, according to the White House, where the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account boasts, the cost of a Fourth of July cookout in 2021 is down $0.16 from last year.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, just the White House think that $0.16 off a barbecue has more of an impact on people's lives?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I would say if you don't like hot dogs, you may not care of the reduction of costs. You don't have to like hot dogs.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Yes, you can't buy a hotdog for $0.16 --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: A reduction of. I will say that what we are most focused on is the fact that we've created now more than 3 million jobs since the president took office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, regarding the economy, the White House thinks they're on a roll.\par \par \uc2 And on the subject of hot dogs there, my line of questioning was not intended to be a grilling, I was just trying to be frank. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You did not.\par \par \uc2 OK, listen, maybe we can pull our $0.32 cents together, and then you can have a couple of bites of hotdog to enjoy this weekend.\par \par \uc2 But before we get to that, Peter, what are we learning now about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  possibly meeting with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business partners? We're talking about when the president back when he was vice president.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, have a look. We've got a picture of a 2015 e-mail. And when you start looking at the timestamps on there, this was pulled-off of that infamously left behind laptop. It appears to show \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  trying to arrange a coffee meeting at the Naval Observatory. That is where the vice president lives.\par \par \uc2 And this photo from around the same time that you'll see next shows the former vice president with his son, Hunter, the Mexican billionaire \uc2 Carlos Slim\uc2 , and several other \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  business associates.\par \par \uc2 So, this image could challenge the White House's long-standing assertion that basically has been the same thing for two full years since the president told it to me in Iowa. He insists, and they insist that he has never spoken to his son about his overseas business dealings. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Peter, enjoy that cookout this weekend. Thank you very much from the White House.\par \par \uc2 The death toll at the condo towers collapse in South Florida has risen to 22. We learned just a few moments ago, two more bodies have been found today. However, some of those listed as missing, good news, have turned up safe. So, that now leaves 126 unaccounted for.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, some of the horror experienced by the victims and the responders. Correspondent Phil Keating is in Surfside, Florida again this evening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hey, get the fire truck to kitchen.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Nearly obtained cell phone video taken in the minutes after half the Surfside 12-story condo tower pancaked dramatically shows the chaos, confusion, and the thick concrete dust cloud reminiscent of 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Hours after the search and rescue work resumed Thursday night, a devastating find of another victim, a 7-year-old girl and daughter of a Miami firefighter who was on the scene at the time. Police officers formed a line as her body was carried out on a gurney.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR \uc2 DANIELLA LEVINE CAVA\uc2  (D-FL), MIAMI-DADE COUNTY: Last night was uniquely different. It was truly different. Different and more difficult for our first responders. These men and women are paying an enormous human toll each and every day.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Officials this morning confirming the new death toll stands at 20. And on day nine, where no one's been found alive since the day of the collapse, that numbers expected to rise exponentially.\par \par \uc2 RAYSA RODRIGUEZ, RESIDENT OF COLLAPSE TOWER: Oh, my God! The whole entire building is gone.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: That's resident, Raysa Rodriguez, moments after the collapse, a resident of the tower and plaintive in now one of five lawsuits. She claims she shot these images in 2018, and says the building's condo association did not heed her warnings about water leaks in the garage.\par \par \uc2 Per her attorney, "This is extremely disturbing video that our client recorded in 2018. No one would look at this video and not think this was a serious problem."\par \par \uc2 Anticipating there will be many more lawsuits, the Champlain Towers South Condo Association Board wants an independent receiver to be appointed to oversee the legal and claims process.\par \par \uc2 The board released this statement, "We are grieving and our hearts ache for those who have been lost and for their families. They have our deepest condolences."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEATING: In fact, one member of the condo board is one of the still unaccounted. Now, the reason the number of missing plummeted by 17 today is that officials are realizing now, some of the missing were reported missing multiple times.\par \par \uc2 So, the current number is 126 and we just learned moments ago two more bodies were pulled out of the rubble today. So, now the death toll total is 22.\par \par \uc2 And right now, churning out in the Caribbean is Category 1 Hurricane Elsa with 85 miles an hour winds and South Florida is in the target zone. If it comes close, that could stop the search operation yet again, maybe even for days, at an even more critical time. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, as there are heroes, they are working around the clock. Phil, thank you very much for the update.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Pentagon says the final U.S. troops have left Bagram Airfield in Afghanistan. The defense secretary has approved a plan to transfer command authority over to the mission -- over the mission in Afghanistan, from General Scott Miller to General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 . That is expected later this month.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says he's confident that leaders in that country can sustain the government, as U.S. troops complete their withdrawal. The president says U.S. forces will provide only limited assistance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We have worked out and over to the rise with capacity. And we can be value added, but the Afghans are going to have to be able to do it themselves with the Air Force they have, and what you're helping them maintain.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The President says he's concerned that the Afghan government has to deal with what he calls internal issues in order to generate nationwide support.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has formally approved more than two dozen recommendations for changes in the way the military handles sexual assault cases. That includes removing decisions on prosecuting those cases from military commanders. The President is stopping short of backing a congressional effort to strip commanders of oversight of all major crimes.\par \par \uc2 There are increasing concerns tonight about the threat posed by China. That country's president is threatening broken heads and bloodshed, he says to any nation that tries to bully his, while American officials are calling on Beijing to negotiate over its rapid buildup of nuclear forces.\par \par \uc2 Here is state department correspondent \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): A celebration of a century, 100 years of the Chinese Communist Party complete with a show of force and a warning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 XI JINPING, PRESIDENT OF THE PEOPLE'S REPUBLIC OF CHINA (through translator): The Chinese people will absolutely not allow any foreign force to bully, oppress, or enslave us. Anyone who attempts to do so will end up with broken heads and bloodshed.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: All part of a more aggressive tone from China's government towards the west, and especially the United States.\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE\uc2 , SPOKESPERSON, UNITED STATES DEPARTMENT OF STATE: We're aware of President Xi's remarks, we've taken note on them. But we're not going to comment on the specifics.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The United States under the Trump and now Biden administrations has sanctioned Chinese Communist Party officials, initiated and maintained tariffs, and condemned China for its crackdown in Hong Kong, repression of Uighurs, and stonewalling in investigation into the origins of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In Congress, division over how to deal with China. Democrats and Republicans on the House Foreign Affairs Committee are arguing over a massive China policy bill designed to boost American economic competitiveness with China and give the administration funding and authority to counter China diplomatically.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say the Democrats version needs much stronger language, especially on Taiwan, slave labor, and export controls. There are also disagreements on climate change. Republicans say Democrats will cave on human rights to secure bogus climate promises from China. Democrats say this bill gives the president the authority to address both issues.\par \par \uc2 REP. ANDY BARR\uc2  (R-KY): We have a much more pressing an imminent threat with the rise of the economic and a national security threat that is the CCP.\par \par \uc2 REP. GREGORY MEEKS (D-NY): We need to do both. And I just want to make sure that he is able to lead on climate and human rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Congress has much more ahead on this issue. The House Foreign Affairs Committee resumes work on this bill in a few weeks, the Senate passed a different China competition bill last month. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2  at the State Department. Thanks, Rich.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to talk live with the floral artists from Washington State who refused to provide services for a longtime customer's same sex wedding after today's Supreme Court decision in her case.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: "BREAKING TONIGHT", the Supreme Court will not take up the case of a florist who refuse to provide services for a same sex wedding for one of her longtime customers. The move leaves in place a decision that she broke state anti-discrimination laws.\par \par \uc2 Barronelle Stutzman of Arlene's Flowers in Richland, Washington joins us tonight, along with Attorney Kristen Waggoner of the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 . Thank you both for joining us.\par \par \uc2 BARRONELLE STUTZMAN, WASHINGTON FLORAL ARTIST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WAGGONER, ATTORNEY, GENERAL COUNSEL, DEFENDING FREEDOM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Barronelle, I want to start with this. This is how the Daily Beast is reporting this. The headline says, "The Supreme Court says: Sorry Felicia, you can't turn the gays away." It's one of the things in covering your case for years, I found, is one of the factual inaccuracies. This was actually a longtime customer and friend of yours who had -- you had served. Give us the distinction about why you felt you couldn't then do his ceremony.\par \par \uc2 STUTZMAN: Although I've waited around for 10 years, wait on for another two years that was the fact that my faith teaches me that marriage is between a man and a woman. And when Rob came-in to ask about his wedding, that's something I could not do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, that launched this year's long debate in the legal system. And today, the court has -- you got to get four votes to get on the calendar.\par \par \uc2 Kristen, as you well know, you have argued many a case there. You got three of the justices who publicly said they would have taken on this case. They were Alito, Thomas and Gorsuch. You didn't get the fourth vote.\par \par \uc2 I want to read something from Lambda Legal senior counsel and law and policy director who says this. "The constitutional protections for religious freedom and free speech were never intended as weapons of discrimination for those operating businesses serving the general public." She adds, "We will remain vigilant against all of these efforts to carve out licenses to discriminate."\par \par \uc2 So, this case is over. Where do you go from here?\par \par \uc2 WAGGONER: Shannon, there are a number of cases that continue in the system that stand for the principle that no American should be forced to create art, express a message, or participate in ceremonies that violate their convictions.\par \par \uc2 What this denial does is essentially pave the way for Washington State and the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  to financially ruin Barronelle. But it does not set binding precedent. And so, the question and the request that we have to the Supreme Court is again, to affirm this basic principle no matter what side of the marriage debate we're on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Barronelle, you have always said that you have employed people of all backgrounds, you will serve people of all backgrounds and differences, but what happens to your business now?\par \par \uc2 STUTZMAN: Well, our business is still here, we cannot do weddings, which of course, is a financial burden. But we're still here and our doors are still open.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And what do you hope to see in the future with possibly one of these other cases that Kristen is talking about may find its way to the Supreme Court for an actual decision on the merits, because a lot of people feel like this area is very unresolved right now, given that they won't hear your case.\par \par \uc2 Jack Phillips' the Masterpiece cake, baker. His case wasn't a huge resolution on the merits of these very tricky situations. What do you hope to see?\par \par \uc2 WAGGONER: I think it makes --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 STUTZMAN: (INAUDIBLE). I'm sorry. I didn't hear that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Hey, just -- Barronelle, I'm wondering what you hope to see moving forward, if it's not going to be your case, but another case on this issue of the clash between LGBTQ rights and religious freedom.\par \par \uc2 STUTZMAN: Well, I hope the Supreme Court realizes how important this is to each of us whether you're religious or not. You know what -- the government needs to understand that faith has been part of our constitutional rights. And I live by that, I can't drop that off the steps of the church.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Kristen, quick final question for you. You know, I talked to some folks today who said, you know, a lot of times the justices don't take cases that they think are the best vehicle for deciding these questions. Do you think that could have played a factor in this particular case? And is there one in particular out there that you think would be a better vehicle for them?\par \par \uc2 WAGGONER: It's difficult to speculate on why they didn't take this case. There's a case called three-or-three creative coming up through the 10th Circuit involving a web site designer. There's also a case we won in the Eighth Circuit that is involving other web site designers, graphic designers, as well as filmmakers. There's cases involving those too.\par \par \uc2 But I do think it makes the question, Shannon, of how many years, people like Jack Phillips will have to litigate for their freedom and how many Barronelle it will take for the court and the nation to stop activists from using the law, misusing the law as an arm of cancel culture.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Kristen, and Barronelle, thank you both very much for your time.\par \par \uc2 STUTZMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WAGGONER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 The Boy Scouts of America\uc2  have reached an $850 million agreement with attorneys representing some 60,000 victims of child sex abuse. The move could pave the way for the scouts to emerge from bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 The filing was intended to try to reach a global resolution of abuse claims and to create a compensation fund for victims.\par \par \uc2 Up next we go live to the southern border where local sheriff's frustration with the Biden administration is growing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRAD COE SHERIFF, KINNEY COUNTY, TEXAS: Somebody has to be held responsible in this, I think, is the only way we're going to get somebody's attention. That Biden, -- the Biden administration is not doing their job.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden's calling for bipartisan action on a pathway to citizenship for some migrants as the illegal immigration crisis goes on tonight. The president wants citizenship available to immigrants who were brought to the U.S. illegally as children, foreign born people who have temporary protected status due to strife in their home countries, and for farmworkers.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take you to one of the busiest spots for crossovers. Correspondent Bill Melugin is in La Joya, Texas. Hello, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, good afternoon to you. So, for the past several weeks, Fox News has been reporting live right here in La Joya, Texas. And the reason for that, as you mentioned, is this spot right here is one of the busiest, if not, the busiest spots along the entire U.S.-Mexico border.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): And to that point, take a look at what we got going on right behind us right here. Just about 35 minutes ago, this group of about 30 migrants cross the Rio Grande and illegally walked into the United States here before giving themselves