because the folks coming over, they're not always just going to be these family units who are seeking out Border Patrol and giving themselves up.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Sometimes there are criminals mixed in, Border Patrol reporting here. Just on Wednesday, they arrested three sex offenders, including one man, a Honduran national who had a second degree rape conviction out of the state of New York on a child under the age of 15. All three of those men had been previously deported and tried to come back.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Bill Melugin at the border. Thanks, Bill.\par \par \uc2 It figures to be a deadly and dangerous Fourth of July weekend in Chicago. That city has become one of the epicenters of America's crime crisis, and the carnage is already off to a grizzly start. Senior correspondent Mike Tobin shows us tonight from Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: With the endless cycle of vendettas in gangland Chicago and the careless gunfire that goes with it, little girls now pay the price. Thursday evening a one-month-old was shot in the head when three men climbed out of a vehicle and opened fire in the Englewood neighborhood. The gunman got away. Earlier that afternoon, a drive-by resulted in a nine-year-old shot in the head. The shooter also got away. Thirty additional people were shot Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 RAY LOPEZ, (D) \uc2 CHICAGO CITY COUNCIL\uc2 : They don't see a cohesive strategy. We see that our police officers are being overworked, no days off, 12, 15, 17 hours shifts weeks at a time, all the while not being able to arrest people, not being able to chase people, not being able to do their job.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Chicago Mayor Lori Lightfoot blames everything from gun laws and bulk purchases of weapons out of state.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT, (D) CHICAGO: For far too long in this country, we don't take seriously the flood of illegal firearms that are coming into cities like Chicago.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: To the pandemic closing courts, she says leaving violent criminals out on bail indefinitely.\par \par \uc2 LIGHTFOOT: Our criminal courts have been shut down for 15 months. They need to reopen. Please, Judge Evans, open up the criminal courts to trials.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: The Chief Judge's office said the mayor is wrong at best. In the timeframe referenced, 128,000 cases have moved through Cook County, which included 13,000 guilty pleas, 1,000 bench trials, and since March, 25 jury trials. While there is a backlog, Judge Timothy Evans says "The courts have been open and resolving these matters. It is disingenuous for anyone to say the courts have been closed." At an emergency hearing in city hall the police superintendent also blamed the courts.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROWN, CHICAGO POLICE SUPERINTENDENT: And too little consequences for the behavior in the courts. There is an explosion of violent offenders being released back into our communities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Mayor Lightfoot told Chicago public television that 99 percent of the criticism she faces is because she is a black woman, not her job performance. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mike Tobin in Chicago for us. Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 Up next, who is considered the greatest U.S. president ever? And what and what about the most recent leaders? We're going to see what the so called experts have to say.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 13 in Salt Lake City as reduced snowpacks and high temperatures move the Great Salt Lake closer to reaching its lowest water level in history. The report issued by the \uc2 Utah Department of Natural Resources\uc2  shows 98 percent of Utah is experiencing extreme or exceptional drought conditions.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Kansas City, Missouri, from FOX 4. One of the big stories there tonight, the mayor of Independence, Missouri, jumps from an airplane to commemorate the reopening of the newly renovated Harry S. Truman Library and Museum. Eileen Weir jumped with the Navy Leapfrogs parachute team. The library is reopening after a two year, $29 million renovation.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Who is your favorite president? Tonight, we look at what America's leading historians and presidential experts say. See if you agree or disagree. Here is my colleague, Bret Baier. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Shannon. \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  is still the top-rated U.S. president of all time. That is according to \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2 's latest ranking of presidential leadership. Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  has joined the top 10 now, while former President Trump comes in 41st.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some reaction tonight from historian and author Douglas Brinkley. Douglas, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS BRINKLEY, HISTORIAN: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talk about how this is structured. It's every four years. And who doing this poll? Who is involved in making the choices?\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: They puts together a group this year. We troll, really, for 150 of the top historians, people who write books on the presidents, biographers. And they fill out the evaluation. And it's broken down into a lot of categories. So it's not just a ranking, meaning it's got a lot of internal things going on. It's non-scientific, I better stress, so it's meant to be fun. But you have public persuasion or foreign policy or on race, or on women's rights, or on military leadership. And they are all broken down. And then we add them up and we get the list. And as you mention, Lincoln is always number one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's take a look at the top 10 list. As this survey shows, Lincoln number one, Washington, FDR, Theodore Roosevelt, Eisenhower, \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2 , \uc2 Thomas Jefferson\uc2 , \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2 , \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . You said Lincoln number one. He is and has been, he's also the favorite president of presidents. Talk about why Lincoln gets that honor.\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: First off, a lot of his writings and thinking are now foundational texts, meaning both of his inaugurals, the Gettysburg Address, the Emancipation Proclamation, but also the Civil War is our nation's great trauma. The country divided in two, and here Lincoln was able to pull us together. His funeral was so dramatic. And so whether you are \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , you say Lincoln is my favorite president. If you're \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , Lincoln is your favorite president. He just is that popular, remains so, and in many ways he is in a category all to himself.\par \par \uc2 Washington always comes on a strong second because of all of the reasons we know. What's unusual is there has been criticism of slave owning in recent years in particular, and here Washington is still hanging in there at number two.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and a number of others as well.\par \par \uc2 The biggest gains, what's interest to see from the last time that this was taken. And there you see Ulysses S. Grant, plus 13. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  moving up seven points. Eisenhower up four spots, and Coolidge up three. Grant making a big move in this survey.\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: And I know you're a Grant afficionado, so that's good news for you, Bret. You have a great book coming out soon, which I can't wait to get released. And Grant is getting better because Ron Chernow had written a book about him. People started talking about him more. There used to be a taint of corruption on his administration, but I think now we see what an incredible job he did trying to heal the nation after the Civil War. And he's been an icon forever, but this rise in presidential esteem is noticeable.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . I was a little surprised that he moved up as much as he did. I thought there would still be the lingering effects of the Iraq War and the great recession. But \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  is a beloved ex- president. He scores very high on people trusting him and believing in his moral art of persuasion. I think he has helped himself with his ex- presidency, his books on painting of immigrants and wounded warriors, the way that he is shown to be not full of himself and a great sense of humor. But he just -- two-term presidents, you have a lot more to analyze than you do with the one term. So if I was Bush 43 I would be happy with this rise in the polls.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's interesting. As you mentioned, it's non-scientific, and these are historians making their judgments. But it's fun to look at, and we appreciate your time. Douglas, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Shannon, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bret, thanks so much. That is sure to spark some dinnertime conversation.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday Lightning Round. We're going to talk with the panel about the economy, today's Supreme Court decision, plus their Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We begin to prepare to celebrate Independence Day. Today's job news brought us something else to celebrate. This morning we learned that in June, our economy created 850,000 jobs, 850,000 jobs. The strength of our recovery is helping us flip the script. Instead of workers competing with each other for jobs that are scarce, employers are competing with each other to attract workers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So let's bring in our panel to talk about the economy and other lightning round topics, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," \uc2 Charles Lane\uc2 , opinion writer for "The Washington Post," and Steve Hayes, editor of "The Dispatch." Byron, I'll start with you. You heard what the president said there. Employers are having compete with each other to attract workers. Some would argue they're also have to compete with the federal benefits that have been extended to a lot of those workers.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Exactly. First of all, the bottom line is 850,000 jobs, that's good news. And presidents often brag and take credit for economic developments, maybe if they didn't have a lot to do with them. President Biden took a real victory lap today, maybe two or three victory laps today, and really didn't take note of what we're seeing, is that job competition has led to rising wages in some places. But it's being eaten up by inflation right now. People are quite worried about that. The price of gas obviously this holiday weekend is just terrible all around the country. So there's a lot of anxiety right now that economically just was not captured in this victory lap that the president took, attributing it to himself and this massive stimulus bill the Democrats passed a couple of months ago.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Steve, against the backdrop of what Byron has laid out, the White House did put out a tweet that says we're going to save 16 cents per person at this year's cook out. A lot of people, if you take a look at it, it's being dragged, it's being trolled heavily online. Was that the best decision going into this holiday weekend, when people are struggling with things like getting gas in the car?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HAYES, EDITOR, "THE DISPATCH": Yes, it was a curious decision to tweet that tweet. People know what they see at the gas tank, by what they see at the grocery stores. People understand and fundamentally feel what inflation is affecting by their own reality, by their own existence. They are not going to be persuaded by a White House tweet touting a 16-cent savings.\par \par \uc2 I think Byron is right. I think the federal government, the role of the federal government and the role of state in ending these $300 per week additional unemployment benefits -- you saw more than two dozen states, mostly run by Republicans, move to end these additional unemployment benefits in May, or announce that they're going to be ending in early June. There is a lot more to learn as we dig into the numbers. But I suspect that we will learn that it's no coincidence that we saw this strong jump in jobs at the same time that we've seen these two dozen states cut those off or announce that they were going to cut them off.\par \par \uc2 And it's important to note, too, that nearly 350,000 of those jobs came in the hospitality industry, which had been struggling to find workers I think precisely because, or at least in one respect because of these additional unemployment benefits. So that's at least part of the equation here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And again, to put up the hard data so people can see. Great news that we're talking about adding 850,000 jobs. But it was unexpected that the unemployment rate went from 5.9, that's where it ended up. The estimate was going to be 5.6. Charles, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES LANE\uc2 , OPINION WRITER, "WASHINGTON POST": I think that 5.9 is a little bit of a puzzle. I tend to see it as statistical noise, because the broader unemployment rate that include people who are discouraged from seeking jobs or involuntarily on part time, that number went down. So there was a mixed picture there.\par \par \uc2 I think the big picture on the U.S. economy is positive. I think we went into an incredibly deep hole in 2020, and thanks to a huge amount of federal stimulus and \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  bank stimulus, we are zooming back out of it. And by all accounts, that is going to continue through 2021 and into the first half of 2022.\par \par \uc2 Most of the problems we have actually seem to be the kind of problems you have when you are growing so fast that you can't keep up. Factories can't find enough inputs, especially semiconductors. They can't find enough workers. There are bottlenecks all over the place of people trying to crank their restaurants and hotels back up. And that's showing in a little bit of inflation. I will be very, very curious to see whether this in 2022 is the issue people vote on, or whether people regard this comeback as something that was kind of inevitable once the vaccinations occurred. But we can argue over the wobbles in the data, but the big picture is very positive.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to get a very quick comment from you guys in this lightning round about the Supreme Court wrapping up its term so we can get to your Winners and Losers. But this idea that it has not performed as some of the best and worst predictions were for the court. It hasn't exactly turned into "The Handmaid's Tale." And by the way, I got a list today from the court of all of the clerks that everybody has hired for the fall, so it doesn't look like anyone is going anywhere. Charles, we will start with you, and then we'll go to Steve and Byron.\par \par \uc2 LANE: Just quickly, on the point you were alluding to, the non-retirement, at least as of now, of Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 . If he stays on the court another term, I think some of the people who are practically demanding that he quit ought to do a little soul-searching and think to themselves, was it such a smart ideal to try to humiliate this very proud man in that way?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. It didn't work with Justice Ginsburg. Quick comment from you, Byron, then Steve. Or Steve, then Byron.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think what we've learned from this particular term is that the inclination to divide the court into partisan terms, which has dominated the media over the past decade plus, really doesn't fit in many ways in this particular court where you have institutionalists, conservatives, liberals, what have you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, on this flower shop case we were discussing tonight, I think it's consistent with one thing with the Supreme Court is that they don't want to solve the issue here. In the cake shop case in Colorado, they should a very, very narrow opinion. Now they have completely ducked this one. At some point they are going to have to come out with a ruling on this. But so far, they are ducking it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, got to do it on the merits. Very quickly, we will get through your Winners and Losers. Byron, to you.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Well, my winner is the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  athletes. In the past, amateurism rules kept them from making money off their name or their image or their likeness. The rule has now been changed and they can actually make endorsements, make money. They have been making millions for their colleges for years. Now the can make some for their own.\par \par \uc2 My loser the still unnamed woman who caused that insane crash at the Tour de France. Wanting to show off for a camera, sticks a sign out into the road. A cyclist hits it and there's a terrible chain reaction. She's a big, big loser.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Super quick, Steve, then Charles.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: My loser is Kevin McCarthy for seeming to threaten to punish Republicans who would work with the, albeit admittedly partisan 1/6 Commission. My winner is the \uc2 Milwaukee Brewers\uc2 , a nine game winning streak, two spectacular wins from behind against their rival \uc2 Chicago Cubs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Charles?\par \par \uc2 LANE: My winner is California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  who is going to get a nice, early September 14th recall election and seems to be cruising to victory. They loser, \uc2 Lordstown Motors\uc2 . That the electric truck company that was supposed to save that distressed Ohio town now facing a federal investigation and plunging stock price.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you, panel.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Finally tonight, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: But the hard part, the really hard part is to not know whether they survived or not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There are no words to describe the agony of waiting.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Today innocence came to Mr. Cosby.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He served three years of an unjust sentence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are facing a crisis, and we clearly know that having less police officers is not going to equivalate a safer community.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It is critical we make a distinction between voter suppression and suspension.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a version of COVID-19 that is far more transmissible than any variant we have seen to date.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: If you are vaccinated, you have a high degree of protection. And if you are not, you are at risk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think it's too much when athletes compete to wear the stars and stripes.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Twenty-one, and 22.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, Chuck Grassley, for once again taking it easy on me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don't expect anything to come from the House that is going to be in an bipartisan manner, Bret. We know it's going to be hot as a firecracker. We've got to cool that thing off.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's the Fourth of July. You guys are asking me questions, and I'll answer next week. But on this, the holiday weekend, I'm going to celebrate it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And maybe he will by watching FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Our guests are going to be Texas Congressman Michael McCaul and former CDC director Tom Friedan.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Shannon Bream in Washington. Please join me at midnight eastern for FOX NEWS AT NIGHT. But FOX NEWS PRIMETIME hosted by Jesse Watters will start in just a minute. His final night this week wrapping up, and something tells me he might have something to say about a new book that is going to be out next week that he might know something about. It's time for PRIMETIME with Jesse Watters. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020210703eh7200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Former U.S. Secretary of Labor \uc2 Eugene Scalia\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 613 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 July 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, to the former Labor Secretary of the United States under Donald Trump Eugene Scalia.\par \par \uc2 Very good to see you, Secretary.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE SCALIA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF LABOR: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The administration took credit for this, these three million new jobs since taking office. It`s all them. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SCALIA: Well, it`s a good jobs report. Adding 850,000 jobs in a month is very strong. And so the president is right to celebrate this jobs report.\par \par \uc2 What it reflects is a reopening economy. And that`s a result of some really important decisions and investments made more than a year ago in Operation Warp Speed and elsewhere that has led to this very effective vaccination program, which President Biden has faithfully and effectively implemented.\par \par \uc2 He certainly gets credit for that too. But I would not attribute the number to his economic policies. And let me make just a couple quick points about the report, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Again, it`s a good report. No report is perfect. One thing that jumped out at me about this report is that we added jobs back in the places where you would add them back when you stop shutdowns. So we reopened our schools, and we added a lot of jobs in education. We opened restaurants and bars, a lot of jobs added in leisure, hospitality, hotels.\par \par \uc2 I noticed that laundry services are hiring again. That`s all good. But it`s a direct result from reopening.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the policy impact, what we don`t see is jobs being added back quickly in manufacturing last month, construction, health care. I do think that the $300-a-week federal unemployment plus-up which the president put in place through September is keeping some people on the sidelines. This number could have been higher, actually, as could the reports for April and May.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it`s interesting you mention that too.\par \par \uc2 This idea that much of the improvement might have been coming from states where they dropped those extended federal benefits, and almost forcing people to find work, many have been arguing that that was a big catalyst here. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 SCALIA: Well, Neil, this monthly report is a very important, closely watched report on the national unemployment picture.\par \par \uc2 But my view for nearly a year now has been that, in some ways, the state unemployment report that comes out later in the month is more telling and more interesting, because we have seen huge variance in states` ability to put people back to work.\par \par \uc2 Three states that we watched closely during COVID were New York, California and Florida. And what we saw the last time we saw state job reports was that New York and California`s unemployment nearly 8 percent. It`s two points higher than the national average, whereas Florida, under Governor DeSantis, is below 5 percent. It`s a full point below the national average.\par \par \uc2 So we will learn more about the national -- about the state-by-state picture later in the month. I think we will learn from that whether those states that are eliminating the plus-up are seeing the results of that.\par \par \uc2 And we will also see whether regulatory high-tax states like New York in California are still having a hard time getting their economies going again.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Eugene Scalia\uc2 , thank you very much. Have a safe Fourth of July weekend, Secretary.\par \par \uc2 SCALIA: Have a great Fourth.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Secretary, thank you again.\par \par \uc2 SCALIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210702eh720002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Putin Denies Russian Role In Cyberattacks On U.S.; Biden, Putin Hold Shorter Than Expected Summit; Biden Spoke About Cyberattacks, What's Off- Limits; Watchdog Reviews Grants Amid Wuhan Lab Questions; Former National Security Adviser: Putin A "Ruthless" Leader; Former National Security Adviser On Biden-Putin Summit; US Hiring Crisis Faces New Challenges; Biden Admin Ties COVID Funds To School Equity; Illinois Father Slams Critical Race Theory In Powerful Speech; Navy Brothers Killed At Pearl Harbor Laid To Rest.\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, John Roberts, Mollie Hemingway, Steve Hilton,  Kayleigh McEnany, Mike Emanuel \par \uc2 8,428 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: So, making any progress here with a guy who won't even admit that something's going on, that could be a high bar for President Biden to jump.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Fair to say the tension's still high following these high stakes meetings. John, pack your bags, get on home soon. Great stuff from Geneva.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I'll still have miles to go before I sleep. Thank you, Sandra. Great to be with you. I'm John Roberts in Geneva. Thank you for joining us for our special coverage. I will be with Martha in just a second. "THE STORY" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, I just going say don't pack up quite yet, John. We need you here with us for a little bit longer.\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum in New York. What a fascinating day this has been to watch this play out as Air Force One heads to the skies and brings the president back to the United States.\par \par \uc2 The reviews are starting to come in on Biden versus Putin on the world stage. The meeting was shorter than most people expected it to be. The president said, well, we just got through everything on our agenda a little bit quicker than we had planned. That's all.\par \par \uc2 And then, they went separately before the cameras. President Putin took on the biggest point of contention perhaps that Russia has been launching very crucial cyber-attacks against the United States or at least entities that are based in Russia. He just batted it away.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN, PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): As for American sources, they've said that most of the cyber-attacks in the world are carried out from the cyber realm of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, you see how that worked out. A claim that the former Secretary of State Mike Pompeo said was fantastical.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: To make the assertion that somehow, America is conducting cyber-attacks around the world on a scale that he described is just -- is a -- is a flat out wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, President Biden called out Russia's leaders on human rights abuses and other actions before this testy exchange when a reporter pressed on whether those behaviors will change.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why are you so confident he'll change his behavior, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, I'm not confident (INAUDIBLE). What the hell -- what do you do all the time?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: When did I say I was confident?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You said, in the next six month, you will be able to determine that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I said -- I said -- what I said was -- let's get it straight. I said what will change their behavior is if the rest of the world reacts to them and they diminishes their standing in the world. I'm not confident of anything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Mollie Hemingway is standing by with her reaction to what played out today on the world stage. But first to "AMERICA REPORTS" anchor and former chief White House correspondent John Roberts, who has been covering this throughout the Europe trip, a very big trip for this president. His first overseas. And John joins us once again live in Geneva.\par \par \uc2 John, you know, it's been very interesting watching this play out. One of the things that I noticed was the question about to Vladimir Putin. You know, did you talk about NATO at all? And Ukraine's participation in this.\par \par \uc2 And he just basically rolled right over that question. He said, no we didn't talk about that at all. And I thought to myself, that's interesting, right? I mean, how do you get through this whole conversation and not talk about Ukraine, which very much wants to join NATO and which Russia is standing along the border of.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, it's a very sensitive issue, and it's a question also that President Biden feel that when he was at NATO, Martha, he was asked whether or not the Ukraine should join NATO. And he be -- he sort of sloughed it off too to say, look, there's a lot of things that we have to deal with before we can even entertain that including corruption at the highest levels of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 It's a very, very, very sensitive issue. If Ukraine were to join NATO, then, another big NATO country that Russia has invaded part of would be right there on the doorstep, and so, that would create some more serious tensions.\par \par \uc2 You know, President Biden was saying today that what he -- what he really hopes to do is over time, lower tensions between the United States and Russia. He basically said -- he said he thinks that there is a genuine prospect for better relations between the United States and Russia without giving up a single thing.\par \par \uc2 He said there were no threats to President Putin, and just simple assertions letting him know, where I stood, said President Biden, what we could accomplish and if there were violations of American sovereignty, what we could do? Of course, cyber-attacks were the big part of that.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden -- I mean, the closest he came to a threat, I think Martha, was when he said, look, I let him know what our cyber capability is, and if he doesn't do something to curb these attacks, this is what we could do.\par \par \uc2 And he gave President Putin a list of 16 sectors of the American economy that should be off-limits to cyber-attacks. It raised the question, well, why shouldn't everything be off-limits to cyber-attacks. But these really were kind of red lines.\par \par \uc2 And in his nearly hour-long press conference, President Putin said, look, we got a good idea, where each other's red lines are. He understands where mine are. I understand where his are.\par \par \uc2 But, to Senator Tom Cotton's point a little more than half an hour ago, these were talks about talks, there really were no deliverables. And President Putin came out of this with three big things, he got a five-year extension on the Salt II Treaty, he got the Nord Stream pipeline, and he got a globally transmitted summit with President Biden here in Geneva.\par \par \uc2 I think that Putin came away from this pretty well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and he got the first two things before he even set foot in the room. To have this --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Before we got anywhere near here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- to have this summit. Exactly. I think we have a sound bite from Vladimir Putin where he just flat-out denies. He's like, we don't have -- we don't do any cyber warfare. That's not coming from us. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: Most of the cyber-attacks in the world are carried out from the cyber realm of the United States. In second place is Canada, afterwards, Latin American countries, and then comes Great Britain. Russia on this list -- where the Russia on this list? Well, we are a country whose cyber territory has the most -- the Russia is not on the list.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm going to guess, this was a list created by Russian intelligence, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I could just imagine folks at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the office of the director of national intelligence, and the NSA, and every other cyber agency in the United States, literally rolling on the floor, laughing at what he said. Russia is not even on the list. Russia basically is the list. Well, that in China and North Korea as well.\par \par \uc2 You know, President Biden came back and said, look, we know where all of this is coming from again. We told him what our capabilities are with cyber. There's some idea that the United States may start to go after these bad actors if they start to attack our infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Again, but this idea, 16 sectors of the economy that are red lines, maybe that leads to something tangible. We'll see. You got to, you know, it's like being an alcoholic. You got to admit -- you got a problem before you can do something about it. Well, Putin is not admitting he's got a problem.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I'm guessing if you're an American company that doesn't fall in that list of 16, you're thinking, OK, I guess they're coming for us next.\par \par \uc2 John, thank you. Great coverage. Great to see you over there in Geneva.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: You bet, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And safe home.\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now, Mollie Hemingway, senior editor at The Federalist and a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Mollie, great to have you with us to look back at what we watched this afternoon. What was -- what was really the biggest takeaway for you from all this?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think it's interesting you see how the media talk about what diplomacy is. Where you get the feeling that they think it's all about getting other countries to say nice things about you. That's actually pretty easily accomplished by giving things away.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden administration did give a lot away before these meetings and talks ever happened, including clearing the way for the completion of the Nords Stream pipeline that you know, is a big desire of both Russia and Germany.\par \par \uc2 But diplomacy should really about -- be about a means to an end, and achieving what's in your country's best interest and what's in your people's best interest.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said that his big goal with this summit was to establish guard rails for cyber-attacks in peacetime. And he clearly, by that metric, this was a failure, because at the end of the summit, Putin is still denying that Russia has any role in these cyber-attacks. And so, it's very hard to make progress when someone's not admitting that they are part of this issue.\par \par \uc2 Then, the other thing is that President Biden gave a list of industries that should not be cyber attacked. Really, no American business should be cyber-attacked, and that should be the clear line. So, it's always good to be talking, particularly with nuclear powers like Russia is. There is a lot of room for cooperation in the years ahead, but this was clearly not much of a success.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know you mentioned the pipeline Mollie, and it strikes me that this is really one of the crucial differences between the Trump administration and the Biden administration because we saw during the Trump administration the establishment of energy independence for the United States.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to natural gas that really put us as the number one energy provider in the world. Now, energy is Russia's number one industry. In fact, it's one of their only really prominent industries. So, they're looking at the situation and saying, OK, so President Biden