round the world knows what's happening.\par \par \uc2 And it's important that if this country wants to have the moral high ground, that there are certain behaviors that need to be in place, and we've had some questionable things happening with how we handle rule of law, whether we believe that political opponents should be prosecuted to the fullest extent of the law while political allies are not.\par \par \uc2 But it's also more important just to focus on national interest, that's where diplomacy can really happen, and that's what each country should be thinking of. What's in their interest and how they can work together.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mollie, thank you very much. Great to have you with us today. Mollie Hemingway weighing in.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And still ahead, we've got General H.R. McMaster, served as national security advisor in the Trump administration, on where the United States-Russia relationship goes from here in these very dangerous times.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hilton also joining us as the former CDC chief says about the Wuhan lab, quote, you know, the U.S. funded a lot of that research, leaving the obvious enormous question, why?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, now we're learning of a new federal investigation into the National Institute of Health's grant program run by Dr. Francis Collins, head of the NIH, as the burning question intensifies why was the United States sending money to a lab in an adversarial nation that was doing dangerous gain-of-function research. In other words, making viruses more deadly in the lab.\par \par \uc2 Steve Hilton host of "THE NEXT REVOLUTION", joins me now. Steve, good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HILTON, FOX NEWS HOST: Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'd actually like to start by replaying \uc2 Dr. Robert Redfield\uc2  on this, and how complicit so many nations are in this research. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT REDFIELD\uc2 , FORMER DIRECTOR, CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL AND PREVENTION: The United States funded a lot of that research. So, this is just not China, and I don't think it helps, you know, by pointing fingers at China.\par \par \uc2 Gain-of-function research being pursued in laboratories was not just a China decision. Many European scientists, many American scientists have been pushing gain-of-function research. So, I think there's -- I think, in a sense, there's a broad responsibility.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Which may lead to why this was a theory that was so doused with water so early on, Steve.\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Exactly, right. I mean, when we talk about the lab leak theory, there's actually two parts to it. The second part is did the virus leak from that lab sometime towards the end of 2019? And there's a lot of evidence pointing to that. And, of course, that points to the Chinese regime, their biosafety procedures that allowed the virus to leak. And, of course, the cover-up afterwards.\par \par \uc2 But the first part of the theory is well, what were they working on in that lab? What was there and why? Dr. Redfield is exactly right.\par \par \uc2 But I would go further, it's not just many scientists were doing it, there was one scientist, in particular, who is the world's leading advocate of gain-of-function research, and that is our very own Dr. Fauci.\par \par \uc2 That you have to take my word for it. In 2011, 10 years ago, he wrote an op-ed in The Washington Post defending this kind of research when it was in its infancy. And the words he used, Dr. Fauci in The Washington Post were that engineering a potentially dangerous virus in the laboratory is a risk worth taking.\par \par \uc2 Because in his view, it helps us understand and protect -- prepare for potential future pandemics. Would he still say it's a risk worth taking when we see what potentially has happened?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, and it raises the question, Steve, even if -- even if that's a good argument, right? You have labs in Texas and in North Carolina. They're the only other two places where this kind of lab work is going on.\par \par \uc2 So, even if you say, you know, what, it's a good idea to investigate those viruses. Let's, you know, trick them up and make them really, really lethal and figure out how to fix them in advance just in case we have to confront something like this.\par \par \uc2 But why, why would you give money to a lab that you don't have complete control over in China.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With U.S. tax dollars to do that kind of really, you know, cutting edge dangerous work?\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Exactly. And for that exact reason, first, the Obama administration in 2014 put a stop to that kind of funding. And then the Trump administration, when it lifted the ban put in place a tough regulatory process that said any kind of research like that has to be flagged for review, go through a separate process.\par \par \uc2 And the real question that this I.G. inspection in \uc2 HHS\uc2  has to answer is did Dr. Fauci go around that process in order to push this research of which he has been such a prominent advocate?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You're exactly right. That is the question. There was a ban and there's a suggestion that some American officials might have helped China to get around the ban to keep doing this work.\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We don't know the answer to that question, but it's a question that absolutely must be investigated by journalists and by investigators in our government. So, we're going to stay on it.\par \par \uc2 Steve, thank you very much. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Thanks, Martha, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 So, coming up, Russia's president denies that they are guilty of cyber- attacks on the U.S. and says that BLM's destructive riots in the U.S. are really no different from the opposition that he faces from the now- imprisoned Alexei Navalny and his movement.\par \par \uc2 General H.R. McMaster, former Trump national security adviser is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, he said the U.S. is actually the biggest cyberbully out there, that from the Russian president Vladimir Putin, as the two countries prepare to negotiate on cyber-attacks, like the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2  hack that caused long gas lines and higher prices last month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Responsible countries need to take action against criminals who conduct ransomware activities on their territory. So, we agreed to task experts in both our -- both our countries to work on specific understandings about what's off-limits and to follow up on specific cases.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's bring in retired Lieutenant General H.R. McMaster, who served as national security adviser to former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 General, great to have you back with us. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, Putin says we don't do any cyber-attacks, you know, that's not our thing. In fact, the U.S. is really the one who you have to look to in that realm. And yet, then, you heard the president say, well, we're going to both -- we tasked people on both sides to kind of get together and set parameters for this. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. H.R. MCMASTER (RET), FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: Well, it's going to be a charade on the part of the Russians, right? Russia has been trying to do this for a long time, say, we need to put together a cyber- working group.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, the -- there aren't too many cyber experts left in Russia because they're all hacking our systems and they're involved in cyber- enabled information warfare against us.\par \par \uc2 So, I think what Putin is engaged in is really a propaganda effort, an effort to try to actually reinforce his campaign to subvert the United States, to polarize us, and pit us against each other. And you saw that with the some of the comments he made during the press conference.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, on another topic, I want to ask you about the question of people being held Americans, Marines, in both cases, Paul Whelan and Trevor Reed.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with Trevor Reed's parents here yesterday. I don't know how they're feeling today about the fact that there was no resolution, and there was no news that their son would be coming home.\par \par \uc2 But I would -- I just want to play quickly Putin's response on this, and then, a question that President Biden took as well real quick.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: And we discussed this, there could be some ground for compromise. \uc2 Russian foreign ministry\uc2  and the \uc2 U.S. state department\uc2  will work in this direction.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The families of the detained Americans came up and we discussed them. We're going to follow through with that discussion. I am -- I'm not going to walk away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He said he would follow through with the discussion, he would not walk away -- President Biden. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MCMASTER: Well, I think I'm sure this was top on the list of items to discuss. And that what we need is constant advocacy for the release of what are essentially hostages.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a this is a -- this is a regime under Putin, his Kremlin engages in hostage-taking as an arm of their foreign policy. And he also uses criminal networks routinely. So, it's not just for hacking of our systems and the attack on our infrastructure, but it's also use of organized crime networks broadly to advance Russia's interests.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, we have this image up of Trevor Reed, standing in a cage in a Russian courtroom. And it -- to me, it's gut-wrenching. I mean, just to -- just to see this American Marine, he was on the White House detail as a U.S. Marine. He served protecting Camp David when presidents were there. And we certainly hope that he's going to be able to come home.\par \par \uc2 But it seems that the two people that have been suggested for this swap on the Russian side that are in U.S. custody seems like a pretty difficult negotiation to let those two go.\par \par \uc2 MCMASTER: Right, and this is why we should be under no illusions about the nature of the Kremlin under Putin's leadership. I mean, this is -- this is -- this is a leader who is ruthless, it's a leader who will -- who will take innocent people hostage for coercive purposes. And we see this with China as well.\par \par \uc2 We're in -- the competition, I think, of this century is, is between these closed authoritarian systems and our free and open societies. And remember the two Canadians now in China who are going on their third year of imprisonment without any due process. This is Michael Kovrig and Michael Spavor.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it's time and I hope that President Biden succeeds in rallying the free world to take action. But it doesn't help when Germany completes the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline, and when the United States takes pressure off of Germany for, you know, a better relationship with Germany, but for what purpose?\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think it's time to act and to impose costs on the Kremlin far beyond what Putin factors in. You know when he takes our citizens hostage, when he tries to poison to death his main political opponent, while he does another poison attack in the -- in the U.K., or engages in the sustained campaign of political subversion and disinformation against us in an effort to polarize us and pit our communities, our society -- societies against each other.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what was your sense today? I mean, did you get the feeling that President Biden did any of those things in the manner that you are telling us they would -- they would need to be done to be effective?\par \par \uc2 MCMASTER: I'm sure he raised all these issues in this closed-door session. The question is, where there'll be any progress? Now, a lot of the discussion has been about, hey, well, at least now we know where our interests overlap and everything.\par \par \uc2 But I think we cannot discount the degree to which a motion drives Vladimir Putin. And it's a motion associated with a sense of honor loss during the collapse of the -- of the Soviet Union and hit the drive -- his drive, as he said in his New Year's Day speech in the year 2,000 when he first took power, is that he's driven to restore Russia to national greatness.\par \par \uc2 Now, he knows Martha, he can't compete with us on our own terms, right? His economy is the size of Italy's. And so, what he tries to do is to drag everybody else down. So, he'll be the last man standing.\par \par \uc2 And sadly, you know, we do a lot of damage to ourselves in this connection. You know with the vitriolic partisan divides that we see in our country, the social divides. And Russia exacerbates those plays to them. You know, they support far right organizations, they support far left organizations. And what they want to do is pull Americans to those extremes so we lose our confidence in who we are as a people in our common identity as Americans.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a cagey play. And it's one that he worked on today in terms of sort of poking at BLM and divisions in this country and saying, look, you have the same thing that we do. And you make a great point that it is his deepest desire based on everything we've seen, to put what he saw as the Soviet Union back together in whatever way he possibly can. And oil is his best weapon, because it's their main industry. And he got a big win on that one with \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2 . General McMaster, thank you -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MCMASTER: Martha, what I'd like to ask you, why does it -- how does it make sense to cancel a Canadian pipeline, and then give the chairman's of free pass to put in a Russian pipeline?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, you're 100% right, right? I mean, why do we give up our energy independence to give Russia there's. That's a question I think everybody should be thinking deeply about right now. General McMaster, thank you so much. Come back soon. Good to have you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So on Friday, we have a really important event that we don't want you to miss. We're going to talk to military experts about the future of warfare. The threats America really faces from weapons that look very different now. You've got potential bio weapons, you've got directed energy, and you have cyber attacks with immense potential for undermining the United States of America. We've got really top people to talk about this. So stay tuned for that on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Also next, a story exclusive as Vice President Harris gets called out by some at her own party now, who say it is critical that she go to the border and see it for herself, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not finished. I've said I'm going to the border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So here's the question I think a lot of people are talking about, have Americans become too comfortable working from home? Nearly 4 million Americans quit their jobs in April, that's a 20-year record. As some bosses are beginning to signal that couch time is over.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES GORMAN, CEO, MORGAN STANLEY: You can go to a restaurant in New York City, you can come into the office. We only get paid in New York rates, you work in New York, but Labor Day, I'll be very disappointed if people haven't found their way into the office. And then we'll have a different kind of conversation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that's pretty clear. Joining me now, Kayleigh McEnany, "Outnumbered" Co-Host and former White House Press Secretary. And we're so -- we love having you here when you are able to come by. You had a busy day, Kayleigh, so thank you.\par \par \uc2 But this is something that I think a lot about -- , that this boss had the guts to say that a lot of people don't have the guts to say like, excuse me, are you going to a restaurant? Are you going to the grocery store? Then you can certainly show up in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY, HOST, "OUTNUMBERED": It's exactly right. It's just like the tailwind that President Trump put behind our country a year ago, when he stood at the podium. I stood at the podium and said, the science says go back to school.\par \par \uc2 We need our kids in school, open our churches. If there's following the science, and then there's a necessary advocacy that should follow that it's time to go back to work. It's healthy to have those meetings, it's healthy to get off Zoom and not have your baby crying and your dog walking in the background, and have these kind of adult meetings and interactions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, everyone understands that it has been a really difficult year. It's not a question. But it's, you know, that the structure that used to exist, that allowed people to go to work to go, to the office is something that I think we're not hearing enough about from the White House at this point.\par \par \uc2 And I said to you in the break, I wonder, at this point, with the vaccinations so well underway, which is such an enormous success. What do you think President Trump would be saying right now? Would he be agreeing with this CEO and how hard would he push on this for companies to tell people to get back?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Oh, there's no denying he would be standing at the podium just like he did with school saying go back to work, echoing exactly what you just heard. And by the way, let me emphasize the Morgan Stanley CEO was compassionate. He said, I understand there's childcare issues. He was compassionate. But at the same time, he recognizes that 90% of his company has been vaccinated, that's coming back to work.\par \par \uc2 What's interesting, though, is you see a huge disparity in Texas, for example, where 50% of people are back to work versus New York and San Francisco, where it's 21%. And I think that's fear of COVID. And that's where it's the responsibility of the president, President Biden, to say to some of these more liberal cities, you can feel safe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But is it real fear or not, because that's his point. You saying, wait, are you going out for dinner?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Right, exactly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are you going to the grocery store? Because why -- you know, if you are doing those things, if you're terrified and you're hunkered in your apartment, then that's another issue and maybe, whatever, but if you are doing (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 And I'm wondering when you look at competition with China, for example, I mean, can we -- what happens to growth? What happens to our nation if we don't compete at that level? Because I don't think that other countries are going to, China in particular, are going to be operating from that.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Yes, we have to compete at that level. When you have a 0.001% infection rate in New York City, only 158 cases of COVID, we got to compete with China to your point. That means showing up for work, getting back to life as usual, and relying on the science that were vaccinated and we can have this kind of interaction you and I are having.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Economic competition is going to become more and more stiff as this thing falls to the wayside. Thank God because that does appear to be happening and we need to remain competitive. Kayleigh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Great to join you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we have two big exclusive interviews coming up, including this very passionate father of two.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TY SMITH, HOST, "CANCEL THIS WITH TY SMITH": How do I have two medical degrees if I'm sitting here oppressed? (Inaudible) I have two medical degrees. No mom, no dad and house, work my way through college. I sat there and hustled my butt off to get through college. You going to tell me somebody look like all y'all white folks kept me from doing that? Are you serious?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Biden administration with a big push to make schools more equitable and it is using billions of COVID relief funds as bait. Chief Washington Correspondent Mike Emanuel live in DC where Education Secretary Miguel Cardona was pressed on this issue. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. The Education Secretary spent about 90 minutes in front of a Senate panel going over some budgetary priorities. Secretary Miguel Cardona testifying today as the Biden administration is forcing American schools to pledge taxpayer dollars toward underlying inequities in access and opportunity in the wake of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Some parents worry that it's code for requiring teaching of critical race theory. A big buzzword for Cardona these days is equity.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CARDONA, EDUCATION SECRETARY: Education can be the great equalizer like it was for me and for many of you. But we have to prioritize, replicate and invest in what works for all students, not just some. We must do more to level the playing field.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The Department of Education has $41 billion remaining from the American Rescue Plan COVID relief money to access that funding schools must submit plans for how they intend to use it. And there's a reference in the application process to maintenance of equity.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): We want to make this more diverse and fair education. And it means give everyone equal chance and equal opportunity. I don't think that's unfair.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: A key Republican called on Cardona and educators to focus on teaching the essential skills our children need.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE BRAUN (R-IN), When you get into these other issues that are extraneous, like critical race theory, I think that is not only taking your eye off the ball, I think you're just going to stir up more discussion that is going to divert it from what is important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator Braun suggesting focusing on the skills that potential employers need for our children to one day get hired. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There's an idea. Mike, thank you very much. So my next guest is a father of two teenage boys with a powerful message about teaching children that they are either oppressors or oppressed. Watch this from a school board meeting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Critical race theory, which is pretty much going to be teaching kids how to hate each other, how to dislike each other. That's pretty much all it can say, it's pretty much what it's going to all come down to. You going to deliberately teach kids, this white kid right here got to bear to you because he is white.\par \par \uc2 You're going to personally tell a white kid, oh, the black people are all down to suppress. How do I have two medical degrees if I'm sitting here oppressed? Well, how did I get where I am right now, if some white man kept me down? How am I now directed over folks that look just like you guys in this room right now, how? What kept me down? What oppressed me?\par \par \uc2 I worked for myself in office freeze to where I am right now. You will sit here and tell me there's lack of critical race theory?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joining me now, Ty Smith, Host of "Cancel This with Ty Smith." Ty, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, tell me what that moment was like in there? And what was the response? Were people responding positively to what you had to say or whether there's some pushback?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, no, the ones that were possibly like a part of this whole narrative that make it seem like black folks just can't do anything without getting some help from big brother government. They had nothing to say. So before I spoke, a lot of people were up there saying all of this, black people have this, we need to do more, we need to do this.\par \par \uc2 I'm sitting here thinking to myself, wait, man, the very people that they are talking about, that was me. I was that kid that they're describing right there and I didn't see not one of their faces ever come to my neighborhood trying to help out. But they do all this talking on the outside the margin, they got a fist up and they saying we're with you. When it comes down to being with us personally, none of them were ever there.\par \par \uc2 So I just got really just passionate about it because I'm thinking to myself, no, look, I went beyond this stuff. I took time to sit there and like, wait a minute, something got to be -- something has to be beyond this. I got myself together by saying, OK, what can I do to make this my life better as a kid now, as a kid, as a teenager. And I just started asking questions. And as I asked questions, people pointed me in the right directions. As I kept getting one of the right directions, I end up becoming successful.\par \par \uc2 And the issue that I'm having with this right now is most of the people that helped me was a mixture of black and white people. And I'm the one that decide they want to do something different with my life. I grew up around the crowds. Before I was 15 years old, I've seen at least 15 people get killed. I'm not talking like, you know, just something I hear. No, I'm talking in front of my face. I grew up around the drugs. I grew up around the poverty side.\par \par \uc2 Me and my brother when we were young, before we were even teenagers, we were going around town picking cans to turn it in for money so we can have something to eat. So when I'm hearing this stuff that people are crying about right now, about the blank of being oppressed because of -- that the white people or some white man or some white systemic racism thing I call BS on it.\par \par \uc2 Because I was so naive to that -- what they were saying that I actually was asking people legitimate like hey, this systemic racism, where's that like -- where is the white man? Where's the system that's doing it because I like to go and talk to the people that's behind it.\par \par \uc2 And when I do this, people usually respond like oh, man, it's everywhere. It's mostly sure, so sure that it's everywhere, to point it out to show it to me, but nobody never could show it to me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just want to play one. It's great talking to you. This is another part of what you said the other night that I think people will want to hear. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Martin Luther King say he wanted his kids to grow up in a world where they are judged by the content of their, what?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS (in unison): Character.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Not their skin. If they let this stuff going right now, it is absolutely the complete reverse of what he's doing. So when February comes, don't talk about Martin Luther King. When February comes don't talk about (inaudible) in his grave with this nonsense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm thinking about, you're the dad to two teenage boys, I think, you know, what's your message for them? And is this resonating with them? Or do they hear a lot from their friends that's very different from what you're telling them?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Oh, jeez. Well, my sons, my sons are 19 years old and 17 years old right now. To them, that's dad. What they are seeing right there, that's another walking, you know, that's just another check mark. They see dad do this all the time. And to them, you know, it's like, well, hey, guys, my dad do this all the time, so what's new, because my sons weren't raised with that type of mindset.\par \par \uc2 I never had that -- I've never once said that when my sons go (inaudible), look your sons, you know, these are white people. These are black people. No, my son seeing everybody being male or female as just friends. I never had to have this conversation with them that they're pushing.\par \par \uc2 So it just really enrages me with this because I got nieces and nephews, there's going to be in school and cousins is going to be the school, and I do not want this nonsense being taught to them because it's completely wrong. And that's why I said, when this come around, don't talk about Martin Luther King don't talk about nothing that Martin Luther King said or did because critical race theory is actually doing the reverse of that. It's making what Martin Luther King did when he fought for what he died were meaningless.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't think anybody could say it better than that. Ty Smith, "Cancel This" on Cities 92.9. I really appreciate you coming by. I hope you'll join us again.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Everybody was just so interested in what you had to say at that meeting. And I think he got people thinking Thank you, Ty. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So joining me, once again, she's been sitting here listen to this with us Kayleigh McEnany, "Outnumber" Co-Host and former White House Press Secretary. This is this is the conversation of America right now, this battle.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Oh, it's exactly the conversation in America. And this issue, it's going to have huge political ramifications because parents are sending their kids to school. They're hearing language that you are taught that you are an oppressor or an oppressed based on the color of your skin. And they fundamentally reject that idea that you can or can't succeed based on your race.\par \par \uc2 This issue is one that the GOP is going to seize on, I can tell you that much. If you look at the talking points coming out of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , they reflect this because this is a cultural tipping point. I think it's one of the three most important issues going into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I listened to the Education Secretary there, and he talks about how education was a great equalizer for him. And I couldn't help but think, you know, that's the idea that you go to school and you're taught math and science and history and social studies. And it equalizes everybody because however hard you're willing to work in that classroom, like you just heard from Ty Smith, that's your chance for advancement.\par \par \uc2 But then you hear Mike Emanuel's report. And it's saying know that you're only going to get these COVID grants, $41 billion still left to go to the schools. If you essentially prove to them that you're going to sign on to equity, which is really has become a buzzword for critical race theory, or similar teachings that you say we'll do that, I promise.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Sure. They use these nice sounding words to cover what is really going on. And make no mistakes, it's not just our schools, which are exactly right. It's the great equalizer. This pernicious ideology goes all the way through government.\par \par \uc2 I remember my time in the federal government, President Trump banning critical race theory because we were taught that -- we learned rather that at Sandia Laboratories, a nuclear facility, they were sending off white men to a retreat on the weekend where they had to write letters of apology to their colleagues. Their female colleagues and their colleagues of color snapped --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (Inaudible) that they had done.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: No. And this is going on at a nuclear laboratory. I mean, this is at our school, it's at our federal government, which is why I praise governors like Ron DeSantis and others who are standing up and saying, you will not poison the minds of kids in Florida.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. What you point out in terms of the role that this will play in politics, it's like there's been this divide that has opened up over this issue. And I think also during COVID, people became more and more aware of what was in the history book. And we know, why are you focusing on this? Why do we have to, you know, calling up parents and saying, you got to sign off on these five things if you want to be part of our school communities, like what's going on?\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: That's right. And I think you have very few people as vocal as your previous guests were willing to put their name on it and stand against this. But when you have these schools, these private schools in New York, one parent put his name on a letter and said, not in my school, and he had an anonymous all through \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , anonymous people coming forward saying thank you for being the one to put your name on it. You need more courageous voices like that an amazing man that you just displayed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Kayleigh. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 MCENANY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for sticking around.\par \par \uc2 So two brothers received a proper burial nearly 80 years after they were killed side by side at Pearl Harbor. It's an incredible story. You don't want to miss. Stick around, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is significant development here. Moments ago, Governor Ron DeSantis announced that his state will answer the requests that came from the governor of Arizona and Texas to help send law enforcement agencies to the US-Mexico border to help them out. The governor says that Florida will help in that effort. So we're going to cover that for you tomorrow in more in-depth.\par \par \uc2 And this for you now, almost 80 years later, two \uc2 US Navy\uc2  brothers killed in the attack on Pearl Harbor had been laid to rest. Harold Trapp on the left was 24, his brother William was 23. They were from Laporte, Indiana. Among the 429 crew members killed when Japan bombed the USS Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 For decades, they were buried in graves marked unknown, but the military has now identified successfully their remains last year. And yesterday, Harold and William Trapp were buried at the National Memorial Cemetery of the Pacific in Honolulu, known as the Punchbowl, with full military honors. A Navy chaplain saying that they sacrifice for our country stood tall and made a difference.\par \par \uc2 Their niece says that it means so much to the family that the brothers were laid to rest with grace and dignity. She said she wished so much that her mother could have seen that day and the ceremony that they so richly deserved. We remember them today.\par \par \uc2 That's "The Story" of this Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210617eh6g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Work From Home Debate; Sticker Shock; Interview With Sen. Steve Daines (R-MT); Companies Mandating Vaccine?; Interview With \uc2 Rep. Rob Wittman\uc2  (R-VA); President Biden Set to Meet With President Putin\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne, Lydia Hu, Steve Harrigan, Peter Doocy, Hillary  Vaughn, Bill Melugin, Rich Edson, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 8,360 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Martha, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We're live in Geneva, Switzerland, ahead of President Biden's summit with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  there tomorrow. But, today, the focus is on China, which is facing increasing pressure to cooperate with world leaders, look into the origins of COVID, as the death toll from the illness tops 600,000 in this country today.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Union\uc2  today joining with the \uc2 G7\uc2  in calling for a transparent, evidence-based inquiry. We're going to be speaking with Virginia Republican Congressman \uc2 Rob Wittman\uc2  in moments. He is sponsoring a bill today that would sanction certain Chinese officials until China complies.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First to \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2 . He is live in Geneva, where U.S. and European officials are pressuring China for answers.\par \par \uc2 But the big question, Rich, is, will they get any?\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's a good question, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 And this began this week with the Group of 7 meeting, the \uc2 G7\uc2 , coming out with it statement pushing on the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  to push into this investigation and try to find out how COVID-19 began.\par \par \uc2 That followed on today with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and the United States with this statement coming out earlier today -- quote -- "We call for progress on a transparent, evidence-based and expert-led WHO-convened phase two study on the origins of COVID-19 that is free from interference. And we commit to working together toward development and the use of a swift and independent means for investigating such outbreaks in the future."\par \par \uc2 Now, China's government is largely refusing that. They do not want an independent examination into the origins of the virus in China, they have said, to determine whether this came from the Wuhan Institute of Virology or whether this came from nature. In fact, China's government has essentially said, go look at \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bio labs instead.\par \par \uc2 And so, with all of that, there are some Republican congressional leaders who are calling for another inquiry.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL\uc2  (R-KY): The WHO did a terrible job the first time. There needs to be investigation, but I have been advocating for a congressional investigation in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The whole world has a self-interest in making sure that China behaves, but the only way we will get them to behave is with ostracism by the whole world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: So, the Biden administration also calling for another investigation.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is on this trip with President Biden. He called the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 's first investigation earlier this year highly deficient and that there needs to be accountability for China, though it is still unclear how exactly the administration plans on pushing that accountability or if American allies will join the U.S. if and when it does.\par \par \uc2 All right, the administration now looking right now in the intelligence community investigation. They're the ones looking into the origins, with a report due by the end of August -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. As night falls there in Geneva, Rich, thank you for your reporting there.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is co-sponsoring legislation that would sanction, meanwhile, top Chinese officials until China allows a full investigation into the origins of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 With us now is Virginia Republican Congressman \uc2 Rob Wittman\uc2 . He is a member of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2  and joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Sir, thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, it is your plan to sanction these top Chinese officials until they allow an investigation into that Wuhan lab. How confident are you that that would result in answers, considering there's questions about whether or not there's enough evidence to really come to a conclusion here?\par \par \uc2 REP. ROB WITTMAN\uc2  (R-VA): Well, Sandra, there needs to be an open, transparent and independent evaluation of what happened there at the Wuhan Institute of Virology.\par \par \uc2 And the Chinese have not been willing to do that. I think we need to hold them accountable if they're not willing to do that. I believe the world needs to know. And the only way that we make that happen is to be aggressive, to be direct in what we expect out of China.\par \par \uc2 And what we know is the information that is out there, if they're willing to divulge it. Again, I want to make sure that we are doing everything we can to make sure that we are holding our national security at the highest level. That is to make sure we hold China accountable and to make sure that this never happens again.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, we can obviously see that you and other Republicans are leaning in hard on this issue and acting very aggressively towards China on it.\par \par \uc2 WITTMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: How confident are you, though, that the Biden administration plans to be just as aggressive?\par \par \uc2 WITTMAN: Well, we don't see that by the Democrats here in Congress.\par \par \uc2 We see Democrat chairmen that are saying they're not going to hold hearings on this, which I think is absolutely unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What is their reasoning? Why?\par \par \uc2 WITTMAN: Well, they say that it takes away accountability from President Trump.\par \par \uc2 They're making this about politics. This should be about holding China accountable. Shouldn't we want to get to the truth? They don't seem to want to get to the truth. They seem to want to make this about partisan politics.\par \par \uc2 This is clearly about national security. It's about holding China accountable. It's about making sure this never happens again. Over 600,000 people in the United States have died from this virus. We need to know how this happened, why it happened, hold people accountable, make sure it never happens again.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Why do you think it's only coming to this now? I mean, you obviously heard and saw that interview that just aired here on this network with Dr. Redfield and others who were raising red flags so early on, to the point where some were receiving, in his case, death threats from scientists not to speak out on this.\par \par \uc2 We have heard from Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , who said any suggestions about this originating from a lab where pooh-poohed. It wasn't politically correct, in some cases, to bring this up within the State Department walls.\par \par \uc2 So what is going on here, as the American people -- you are clearly saying with this announcement today, the American people want to know more. So why didn't we want to know more earlier on?\par \par \uc2 WITTMAN: Well, I think this was all couched in politics.\par \par \uc2 I think early on, because it came from Republicans, because it was suggested by President Trump, that the media said, no, this is conspiracy theory. We heard them go after Tom Cotton, saying that this is a marginal thought here. And they actually made fun of many of us who said there's something to this.\par \par \uc2 I'm a biologist by training. I worked in the lab through the years. I said, why aren't we asking these significant questions that must be answered? And the press was unwilling to ask those questions.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WITTMAN: Many media outlets have said -- said this was crazy conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 We see now that that is not the case.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Many of those in the media have had to walk back those comments...\par \par \uc2 WITTMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: ... as this theory that it did leak from a Wuhan lab has obviously grown in popularity, gained steam over time.\par \par \uc2 So, as these leaders, these global leaders do meet now, what are you hearing is the urgency on the part of global leaders -- because, obviously, this is a global pandemic that we're talking about -- to push for answers, and ultimately hold China accountable?\par \par \uc2 WITTMAN: I think more leaders around the world are beginning to see the full picture. They're seeing information that has come from intelligence agencies. They're hearing experts on health and viruses from around the world talk about the higher likelihood of this being an escape from the lab, rather than a natural transference from animals to man.\par \par \uc2 Those are world-renowned experts that are starting to call into question these issues. I think countries around the world are saying, China needs to be held accountable. They need to be transparent. They need to be open and have an independent investigation about truly what happened there at the Wuhan Institute of Virology.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders also called last week for a timely, transparent, expert- led study conducted in China on the virus' origin.\par \par \uc2 You are leading that charge, a couple of Republicans that are pressing for more answers and for the American people to learn more through the World Deserves to Know Act.\par \par \uc2 Sir, I appreciate your time this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 WITTMAN: Thank you so much, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , meanwhile, commemorating the anniversary of DACA. But while she honors those dreamers, border officials say they're still dealing with a nightmare on the ground. The startling discovery from Border Patrol -- next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: In Washington now, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  meeting with dreamers to mark the ninth anniversary of DACA, Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals.\par \par \uc2 But it's at our Southern border that the nightmare is happening.\par \par \uc2 Bill Melugin is in La Joya, Texas, on the latest discovery from Customs and Border Protection.\par \par \uc2 Bill, I know you have got a busy scene playing out behind you, but, first, tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Sandra, we're in the Rio Grande Valley here, and this is by far the busiest section of the U.S.-Mexico border.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol here completely overwhelmed, overstretched and undermanned. And just to highlight that point for you, just as we're getting ready to go live, this large group of migrants just pop out of the brush right behind us here. There are no Border Patrol units here. So they're just sitting down waiting to be apprehended.\par \par \uc2 Some of them look to be in a lot of stress from the heat. It's very hot out here. It's incredibly humid, but, again, Border Patrol units, there's no one here right now to apprehend these folks. And that just kind of tells part of the story there, that they are so stretched thin, we have a group right here just waiting to be captured.\par \par \uc2 But talking about that stash house, take a look at this video that we shot yesterday. This is exclusive video. We were tipped off to. This was a human smuggling stash house that was busted in Alton, Texas, yesterday, about 20 minutes away from where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 It appeared to be an old auto garage. More than 100 migrants being kept there by those human smugglers in some pretty horrible conditions, completely crammed on top of each other in that heat, with no air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 What happens is, these human smugglers will bring them across the border, leave them at the stash house, and hold them there until they're ready to move them elsewhere, deeper into the United States. And actually spray- painted on the wall of that stash house were the letters CDG. Law enforcement sources tell me that stands for Cartel del Golfo, the Gulf Cartel.\par \par \uc2 That's the Mexican drug cartel that runs this area on the opposite side of the border in Mexico. They're big into human trafficking and drug trafficking. And they are profiting greatly from this crisis.\par \par \uc2 More activity. Take a look at this video we shot here from right where we're standing this morning. This is La Joya, Texas. Like clockwork, every single day, every single morning, large group of migrants just come out of the brush and walk down the road and give themselves up to Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 I cannot stress to you how often this happens like clockwork, every single day. These are mostly those family units, those people who are not trying to run away. But we see this every single morning out here.\par \par \uc2 And as we finish up back out here live, taking a look at this again, still no Border Patrol units here. And it's not Border Patrol's fault. They're doing everything they can. They're completely overwhelmed. They can't be everywhere at once.\par \par \uc2 We saw one unit drive by and just leave them there, which suggests potentially they got other action the border that's more important right now. So, despite what the administration says that the border is closed or it's under control or the messaging that migrants shouldn't come right now, that messaging isn't getting through. And the border, as you can see, is not closed.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, thank you for your reporting from there, and those live pictures. Wow.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Customs and Border Protection\uc2  saying situations like that one are becoming much more common. In the case of the stash house, that's a big concern for agents on the ground there.\par \par \uc2 National Border Patrol Council president Brandon Judd joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Sir, thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 Texas, over 100 undocumented immigrants, according to officials. Those U.S. -- in the U.S. illegally were nationals of Mexico, Honduras, El Salvador, Ecuador, Nicaragua.\par \par \uc2 Your reaction to that, and how much more of this is going on right now?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: This is happening everywhere.\par \par \uc2 But what's what's unfortunate is, we're getting examples like this on a very regular basis, if not a weekly basis, on a very regular basis. And from there, from these houses, they're going to put these people into semi- trailers that also do not have air conditioning. And they're going to be even more crammed in there.\par \par \uc2 We have had semi-trailers that have killed people that we found. And this crisis continues, and there's no solutions coming from the White House. Instead, Vice President Harris is meeting with dreamers, instead of meeting with the law enforcement that have to deal with this on a daily basis. We're getting overwhelmed. And if the White House doesn't step in, the numbers are going to continue to rise, and we're going to continue to see people put in inhumane conditions, and they're going to suffer.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Brandon, we will put those live pictures back up that Bill was just showing us of those groups that just continue to pour in.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just at first glance, when I saw that, I quickly noticed that there was a lot of children mixed in there. I mean, I'm seeing some kids that look like they could be 2 or 3 years old on the left-hand side of the screen, kids of various ages there making this dangerous journey.\par \par \uc2 Optics is part of leading, there is no doubt. And all of this is happening while \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the vice president, who has been assigned in charge of this crisis by President Biden, she is currently talking about the 10th anniversary of DACA in Washington. She has not paid a visit to the border. I believe it's 83 days and counting that she's been in charge of this crisis.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the sheer optics of the numbers of migrants pouring in, the story of the stash house, while she's in Washington, and has no plans to visit the border, what is your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: It's disgusting to know that the person that is in charge of the border isn't even going to the border to see exactly what is going on.\par \par \uc2 But the fact remains, she can't. She can't come to the border, because, if she does, she's going to be expected to fix the problem. And then there is no political will to fix the problem.\par \par \uc2 All you have to do is look at the interviews that she's given. When she says that she's an advocate for undocumented aliens in this country, that is clearly inviting these people to come across the border, if they know that the vice president is going to advocate for them?\par \par \uc2 Then she says, don't come because I believe you're going to be sent back. What president or vice president says, I believe something is going to happen? They have to know exactly what the consequences are going to be. Yet she doesn't.\par \par \uc2 She continues to say these things. And then their actions add to this problem.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, a bit of news here. I just want to get your reaction before we let you go, Brandon. This is just out of a meeting today with the U.S. homeland security secretary, Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 The Mexico finance minister, so Mexico saying that it's going to work with the U.S. to modernize Mexican customs. Apparently, it was said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that my Mayorkas and Arturo Herrera, the two agreed to work together to modern modernized Mexican customs offices.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean to you?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: It -- right now, at this exact point, it doesn't mean anything, because, to modernize their agency, it's going to take years to get that done.\par \par \uc2 So this crisis is going to continue while they're trying to modernize customs. Yes, it might work five years down the road. But right now, in the immediacy, it does absolutely nothing for us.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Real quick, do you want \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  -- would it help to have her or the president or both of them visit the border?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Yes, I absolutely want them to come. I want them to see exactly what's going on firsthand.\par \par \uc2 That happened with Vice President Pence when he went to the border. He saw exactly what happened and then they implemented policies to fix the problems. They're not -- this administration isn't doing that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Brandon Judd, I really appreciate your time. We will obviously catch up with you again soon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Our best to you.\par \par \uc2 Got a shot? More companies saying, prove it. But can they legally do it? We will debate that one.\par \par \uc2 And what we learned today about those recent price spikes that could mean sticker shock is here to stay for some time. We report, you might want to hide.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: The clock is ticking to the face-to-face meeting between President Biden and Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What does Montana Senator Steve Daines want to hear? He will join us next.\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Now you will be able to see those smiles at the Happiest Place on Earth, starting today, \uc2 Disney\uc2  lifting its mask mandates for vaccinated guests at its two flagship parks in California and Florida.\par \par \uc2 If you don't have a shot, you will still need to mask up on the ride and other indoor attractions. \uc2 Disney\uc2  also saying that it will not require its employees to show proof of vaccination.\par \par \uc2 But not all companies are taking the same path as the Mouse House. Wall Street firms like \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  pushing workers to prove their vaccination status. Goldman using an internal online portal for proof.\par \par \uc2 But how far can companies legally go with this?\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from attorney Katie Cherkasky.\par \par \uc2 Katie, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Everybody's got big questions about this, because so many folks who have been working from home, they're getting called back into the workplace. They're concerned about whether or not they need to be vaccinated and show proof of that.\par \par \uc2 I believe that they're also concerned about the others around them being vaccinated, because unless you wear a pin on your shoulder that says you are, you just really don't know.\par \par \uc2 So what can companies do to ensure that people are safe?\par \par \uc2 KATIE CHERKASKY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, private employers really have very broad discretion to mandate vaccines.\par \par \uc2 I think that there's a very common misconception that HIPAA requires employers to not ask these questions. But that's just not true, because most employers are not regulated by HIPAA. That's only for very specific health plans and health care clearinghouses.\par \par \uc2 So a normal employer can ask the question, and they can mandate that as a condition of employment, except for in cases of religious exemptions or disability exemptions, which always existed.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: There's -- it's a matter of whether or not you have to show your medical records to your employer.\par \par \uc2 And I'm sure you can tell us all day long about the restrictions there are for doing that. And, certainly, there's medical reasons and, in some cases, religious reasons why people decide not to do. This is going to be -- my guess -- an incredibly difficult path to navigate for these companies who have never been here before.\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: I think that's absolutely true. And I think it's difficult for employers to make the right decision, because, on the one hand, you don't want an outbreak of some sort.\par \par \uc2 But on the other, you don't want to have litigation, even if it doesn't end up going in employees' favor, but you don't want to have to deal with that, on top of disgruntled employees and the morale that might come along with forcing people to do something that they feel is a personal choice.\par \par \uc2 So it seems that a lot of companies are not mandating, even though, legally, I think they have a lot of basis to do that. And they probably could get away with it. But they're not doing that because of those downsides, I believe, at this point.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think it's going to be really interesting if companies do decide to mandate that how many people who haven't been vaccinated will go do so, or if we're going to see lawsuits as a result, people who just don't want to show their medical status.\par \par \uc2 But all of this is incredibly important for getting people back to work, because, in some cases, when we talk about nine million job openings in this country, we are told the reason for that, in some cases, is people don't feel safe returning to the workplace.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of these companies have an obligation to show their staff, show their employees that they're creating a safe work environment.\par \par \uc2 I go to my last question, and that is this. We all are taught what's right, H.R., what is politically correct, what you can and can't say in the workplace. I wonder what it's going to be like if people decide to ask, are you vaccinated, or -- after we all got vaccinated, it seemed like we were all sharing that with each other. But is it going to be OK to ask someone if they have been?\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Well, you could certainly ask somebody that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, obviously, the social awkwardness is one thing to navigate. But there was, in fact, a lawsuit was filed out of Texas, and the federal judge in that case actually dismissed it, saying that the employer could mandate the vaccines even for people that chose not to do so for non-religious or non-disability purposes.\par \par \uc2 So it looks like, on the legal side, they're going to be able to do so. But the question is really, do the employers want to do that? Is that good for business? And that's the bigger question.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: These are big questions that all these corporations are going to have to figure out, some of them on the fly.\par \par \uc2 Katie, appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHERKASKY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, we will have more on this growing tension over returning to in person work later in the show, including a warning that a top CEO is giving his employees, but first to California cashing in on some serious coin from taxpayers across the country, thanks to the return of pork barrel spending in Washington.\par \par \uc2 FOX's Hillary Vaughn is on Capitol Hill with the very latest on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Well, when it comes to pork spending in the House highway bill, California walked away with the prized pig. Lawmakers set aside $5.7 billion in earmarks. And the Golden State gets more of them in this bill than any other state. If passed, California will receive over $900 million in federal taxpayer cash to fund different projects in California's congressional districts.\par \par \uc2 Some of that money fueling Democrats' green energy dreams, $31 million going to clean energy projects alone in the state, including $4 million for electric vehicle charging stations and $19 million to put electric buses on the road.\par \par \uc2 But there are some projects included that may make some do a double-take, including over $6 million for a suicide safety net to be installed at San Francisco's Golden Gate Bridge, $6.5 million for a bike trail in Menlo Park. That's where \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is headquartered -- $3 million for a bike trail through Napa's Wine Country, and another $3 million for new toilets and lights at a San Francisco Bay Area Metro station.\par \par \uc2 California, though, does have the extra cash to pay for this stuff if they wanted to. They have a $76 billion surplus. But instead of dipping into their stash of cash, American taxpayers will be picking up the tab for these things, Sandra, that really only a few Californians even will actually get to benefit from -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Hillary Vaughn live on Capitol Hill for us.\par \par \uc2 Hillary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 From killer to worthy adversary. Ahead of the high-stakes meeting, why is Biden's tune on Putin changing?\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator Steve Daines on that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: President Biden in Switzerland ahead of his high-stakes meeting with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 To FOX News White House correspondent Peter Doocy. He is live on the ground there in Geneva on preparations ahead of this highly anticipated summit.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, good evening from Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 That is right. President Biden is here in Geneva going over his notes ahead of what we are told will be four or five hours of talks with President Putin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Are you ready for tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm always ready.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As the president flew from Belgium to Switzerland, we got some more details aboard Air Force One on what is on the to do-list here.\par \par \uc2 They are the world's two biggest nuclear power, so an administration official says arms control will come up. And so will ransomware attacks launched within Russia that squeezed American meat and fuel supplies this spring.\par \par \uc2 Interference in U.S. elections is something Biden has long complained about. He points to the U.S. intel community's assessment that Russia was involved, which Putin claims isn't true. President Biden also says he wants to talk to Putin about that political rival \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2 , who is in a Russian prison for political reasons,\par \par \uc2 Russian officials have publicly said they don't want to talk about that. U.S. officials have shot back Russia doesn't get to decide what Biden brings up.\par \par \uc2 Back at home in the States, though, critics are concerned about the president's approach.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): I'm less concerned how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stumbles and bumbles his way through describing \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  than I am concerned about how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  behaves towards \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And for the last four months, he has been pathetically weak.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Axios is reporting now that some former Trump White House officials who were involved in the Trump-Putin summit have been advising and briefing President Biden ahead of his meeting tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The president has been sequestered away from cameras and away from public events here in Geneva ever since he touched down this afternoon -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Doocy live on the ground there in Geneva for us. Looking forward to your continuing coverage from there. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, what is our next guest hoping to see out of this meeting?\par \par \uc2 Montana Republican Senator Steve Daines joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, what is behind, do you believe, President Biden sort of reversing his killer tough guy rhetoric on Putin to now calling him a tough and worthy adversary?\par \par \uc2 SEN. STEVE DAINES (R-MT): Well, you can't make it up, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 He's -- he's showing weakness towards Russia,and he's being tough on America. President Biden needs to go in with a position of strength. Remember President Reagan? It was peace through strength. We need that out of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But, unfortunately, rather being tough on Russia, what are we seeing? We're seeing these huge cyberattacks that are going on in America that are crippling our infrastructure. We're seeing President Biden green-lighting a natural gas pipeline from Russia to Germany. At the same time, he killed the Keystone XL pipeline.\par \par \uc2 Here's what I like to see happen. He should move that meeting from Geneva, move it to the Southern border. Bring Putin to the Southern border. We could talk about American energy. We also talk about the out-of-control border crisis we have got right now going on in our own country.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Talk about the balance, though, between that tough talk that you are wishing and hoping for from President Biden, and going too tough, to where there's concern about retaliation from Russia.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, listen, bullies understand one thing. And that's strength.\par \par \uc2 If you go into a meeting with a bully with weakness, guess what? The bully comes out on top. We need President Biden to come in and put a America first agenda forward. We need to hold him accountable. These cyberattacks, these ransomware attacks are a very serious threat to our country, our economic security, our entire national security.\par \par \uc2 We need a forceful president confronting Putin about what's going on here with Russia attacking our country. And we need a president right now who will stand up for America and help our country, instead of being weak towards Russia.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And going to some of the words that he used to describe \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , President Biden called him bright, tough, as he has found, he says, as they say, obviously, that he's a worthy adversary now.\par \par \uc2 You should have seen the headlines that resulted. Biden lavished praise on Putin. If Trump had done this, the media would have had a fit.\par \par \uc2 Mollie Hemingway chimed in, Biden with a huge walk-back from his first comments on Putin. Ted Cruz ripped into Biden for his praise of Putin. Took down the media along the way too, did he.\par \par \uc2 But is there a double standard, do you believe, with the way the media is treating this upcoming summit and sit-down meeting with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , the way they're treating it with Biden vs. the way they did with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, go back and look at the clips from the Main Street media from when Trump met Putin in Helsinki...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAINES: ... compared to the meeting that's going to happen in Geneva. It's -- the contrast is absolutely black and white.\par \par \uc2 And so they're providing this Biden honeymoon here with Putin. And Trump came in to meet with Putin with strength. Biden's going in to meet with Putin with weakness.\par \par \uc2 And I tell you what. That doesn't end very well for our country. You have got to confront Russia head on. He needs to come in, right in, go strong here, confronting Putin about these cyberattacks. They all originate from Russia.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Senator, real quick before I let you go, it did seem that President Biden broke with the norm yesterday, decided to rip into Republicans during this overseas press conference.\par \par \uc2 There was a lot of focus on the opposing party. Why do you believe he chose to do that, finally?\par \par \uc2 DAINES: Well, I tell you what.\par \par \uc2 We talked about -- remember, when President Trump made a few comments, they all went crazy about that. And here's the bottom line, is that President Biden is dividing this country. He's creating division here, where he is driving inflation up. He's driving gas prices up, killing pipelines, an out-of-control Southern border, proposing to raise taxes to cripple our economy, cripple the American people.\par \par \uc2 He needs to come back home here and unite this country, instead of launching bombs, figuratively speaking here, from Europe back at his own country. That's wrong.\par \par \uc2 We need a president that will unite this country, not divide us.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, well, we await the big meeting happening tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Senator Daines from Montana, appreciate it. Thank you. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 All right, Putin is frequently referred to as a killer. So, just how accurate is that label?\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Steve Harrigan takes a look.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, 20 years ago, Russians were really shocked by Putin's tough talk when he talked about rubbing out Chechen fighters, even in outhouses.\par \par \uc2 Now, 20 years later, they are concerned that some of that violence could extend to Putin's political opponents at home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice-over): Before political activist \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2  was poisoned and imprisoned, former Deputy Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Nemtsov\uc2  was shot outside the Kremlin walls.\par \par \uc2 Journalist Anna Politkovskaya was gunned down in the stairwell of her apartment. There was no evidence tied to the regime, but no doubt about the message to surviving colleagues.\par \par \uc2 IRINA BOROGAN, RUSSIAN JOURNALIST: I was shocked. I was scared. I was depressed. And everybody did, because she was so prominent and so important that we believe that nobody -- nobody there to -- touch her.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Calling the president of Russia a killer is debated by Russia analysts, both as a matter of fact and as a tactic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Putin is willing to use murder as a weapon, but we don't need to say that when we're characterizing in public. We need to react to that and to establish strong policies to making sure that such ruthlessness does not hurt American interests.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Other suggests a change in context could help Americans get a sense of Putin's soul.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The best way to understand Mr. Putin and how the Kremlin works is, in the United States, we don't think of criminal organized crime as part of the government. But, in Russia, it is almost the exact same thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: That analogy would posit Putin as the boss of 145 million Russians with a nuclear arsenal -- Sandra\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Steve Harrigan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up: If you can go out to eat, you can come back to work. Morgan Stanley's CEO reportedly saying it. How will workers respond to it?\par \par \uc2 But, first, more sticker shock from store shelves. You might have a cow over our next story.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Wholesale prices up nearly 7 percent from a year ago, and those costs are hitting consumers in the stores. It's another sign inflation is heating up as we head into summer.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Lydia Hu who is on a dairy farm in Easton, Pennsylvania, where local farmers are dealing with these higher prices firsthand.\par \par \uc2 Love it, Lydia. How's it going in there?\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sandra. You're checking in just in time, because the cows are actually getting milked