s. There's a major debate on this, Sandra, whether it's transitory, which means sort of temporary.\par \par \uc2 Ten minutes ago, \uc2 La-Z-Boy\uc2  just reported their earnings. Let me tell you what they just said: "Short term, the company expects a temporary negative impact to our profit margins because, due to a dramatic material price increase, which will only be offset by previously announced price actions," which means they have already announced they're going to raise their prices because the price...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... it will cost that they take, that they need to make their furniture has gone up, in their words, dramatically.\par \par \uc2 So, this is -- this rolls down the hill to everyone watching this show. It's a major question. I think some parts of it will be longer-term, like wages. And I'm just hoping other things, like commodity prices, beef, milk and things like that, start to pull back.\par \par \uc2 We have seen lumber, we have seen still come down dramatically in the last month. Let's hope the rest do as well.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Really important. Producer prices rising at the fastest pace since at least 2009. Obviously, what we have seen happen, a lot of folks stayed home. Savings were built. There is pent-up demand.\par \par \uc2 People are heading out to restaurants. They're vaccinated. They're shopping. They're buying things again. So, obviously, the big bet is whether or not this is transitory or not. It appears investors are not panicking, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Dow did fall about 94 points today, but stocks are still near record highs. So why isn't the U.S. stock market worried about these higher prices, Charles?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sometimes, you got to be careful -- you know this -- with these averages. A few stocks can be up big, and it can mask the under -- all -- overall, internally, the market is very jittery.\par \par \uc2 There have been more losers than winners of late. And there's a lot of anxiety about this. But don't forget, you just heard \uc2 La-Z-Boy\uc2  say that they have been able to pass these costs on. These companies have pricing power. Yesterday, a major firm said, we think Chipotle is a buy, even though they're paying $16 an hour, they're paying for pet insurance, they're paying for all kinds of things to lure workers.\par \par \uc2 They can pass those costs on. So that's another reason for now you see the market up, but how long can they probably pass these prices on? It won't be for forever. And that's why everybody hopes that maybe we can just tap the brakes a little bit.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And, sometimes, you don't know they're getting passed along to you. I always call it sneaky inflation, where they shrink the amount of goods that are in...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Streak-flation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And you just don't even realize, because they don't raise the price. They just put less Cheerios in the box or whatever it is, cereal in general.\par \par \uc2 Charles, we will watch you, 2:00 p.m., "Making Money With Charles Payne," 2:00 p.m. weekdays on FOX Business.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: See you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, if you can go to a restaurant in New York City, you can come into the office. That is what Morgan Stanley's CEO is telling its employees. And it could come with a price if they aren't there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES GORMAN\uc2 , CEO, MORGAN STANLEY: If you can go to a restaurant in New York City, you can come into the office, and we want you in the office.\par \par \uc2 If you want to get paid New York rates, you work in New York. Labor Day, I will be very disappointed if people haven't found their way into the office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That was Morgan Stanley CEO \uc2 James Gorman\uc2  giving a message to his New York employees: Be back in the office full-time by Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 He also says, if they want to get paid New York rates, they need to actually be in New York.\par \par \uc2 So, is this the way companies should handle their return-to-work plans?\par \par \uc2 Joining us, Independent Women's Forum Patrice Lee Onwuka, The New York Post's Kelly Jane Torrance, and Democratic strategist Laura Fink.\par \par \uc2 Patrice, I will start with you first.\par \par \uc2 I mean, obviously, he wants people back in the office. Isn't there -- doesn't he have a point? If you're going out to restaurants, you're grocery shopping, you're back out again, well, shouldn't you get back to the office?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE LEE ONWUKA, INDEPENDENT WOMEN'S FORUM: Well, I think it's a fair point.\par \par \uc2 I mean, a lot of the concerns about health are receding, and especially for parents. Children should hopefully all be back in school full-time this fall. So maybe some of those concerns that kept workers at home are eroding.\par \par \uc2 But I have got to say, this is a big deal. It's going to have a rippling effect in New York, with the competitors, the customers. Any businesses that deal with \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  may start to follow suit. So this can be a really -- a really big deal.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, I can tell you, New York City needs them to return. It would be a good thing for the city to see them back, all those workers.\par \par \uc2 But to go on, Kelly, to his other words, if you want to get paid New York rates, you work in New York. And it's a fair point. If you're going to continue to accept the salary that you had pre-pandemic, and you're living somewhere else, perhaps in a more rural area, or you went to Florida or whatever it may be, then maybe a company shouldn't have to continue to pay you those New York rates.\par \par \uc2 What say you?\par \par \uc2 KELLY JANE TORRANCE, NEW YORK POST: Yes, I think I think he has a point there, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 But there's a reason, I think, that a lot of people haven't come back to New York yet. And that is crime. I can walk to the restaurant down the street from me. I'm not taking the subway right now, honestly, because I don't feel safe. A lot of crimes, including felony assaults, are still up, despite ridership being down.\par \par \uc2 And polls show that only about a quarter of New Yorkers right now feel safe on the subways. Before the epidemic, that was 65 percent. So it's a bigger issue than just, hey, get back to work. There's a lot of work I think the city's leaders need to do to get New Yorkers back to work.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think, Laura, that's a fair point, because we're not just talking about the risks of COVID getting back into the workplace.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, in cases like New York City and some other hard-hit cities as far as crime, workers could be hesitant to go back for that reason, too.\par \par \uc2 LAURA FINK, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Let's look at the data -- $4.1 billion, that's the amount of increase in revenue year over year that \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  made. They could be sharing that wealth with their workers.\par \par \uc2 Instead, they're threatening to cut salaries, when these workers have been increasingly productive, and have provided them and increased their bottom line. Instead of attracting them back to work with the carrot, he comes in hard with the stick.\par \par \uc2 That's pretty typical of sort of top-down banking culture, and different than what we have seen in other tech companies, like \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 . Look, 50 percent of workers want to do some kind of telework. Now, either companies are going to be responsive to that, or they're going to incentivize them to come into the office with rewards, or they're going to face talent and retention problems.\par \par \uc2 That's -- workers have increased power.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Right.\par \par \uc2 FINK: And I think that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, let me dig into that last point with you, Patrice, because \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  just down the street from \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  is saying, we are going to continue to offer a hybrid model.\par \par \uc2 Some of these banks are deciding to give their workers an option to work some days in the office, some days from home. So isn't that going to become a major recruiting point with some of these big corporations, if they're going to offer a hybrid model?\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: Well, I think so.\par \par \uc2 I mean, to Laura's point, when we do look at polling, 86 percent of workers who worked remotely say they want to continue to do so. But only 25 percent want to do it full-time. So, it's an incentive for new employees and to retain current employees.\par \par \uc2 But it is also an incentive to draw those people who are in other far-flung cities and locations that a lot of these companies couldn't necessarily bring to the city, but now can do so through a hybrid model. So I absolutely do think you will see more of this.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Kelly, so wrap this for us, as a New Yorker yourself.\par \par \uc2 He says: "Labor Day, I will be very disappointed people haven't found their way into the office."\par \par \uc2 Do you think people are going to listen to this message and get themselves back?\par \par \uc2 TORRANCE: I think a lot of people want to go back. I'm going back once in a while. I'm not in every day. I'm going in once a while.\par \par \uc2 But the thing is, I have missed my co-workers. I missed that collaboration. So, until everyone is going back, and we're going to see that same kind of teamwork, throwing ideas off each other, I think it's going to be harder to get people in. You have got to get some kind of first wave that brings in the second and third wave.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And, Laura, to your point, while productivity has been up in many cases for these big corporations, there's questions over whether or not that lasts, because if you have a workplace environment that originates outside of the office, will the productivity remain high in some of those cases?\par \par \uc2 All really good points across the board.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for joining us, ladies.\par \par \uc2 TORRANCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ONWUKA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FINK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I liked our ladies panel.\par \par \uc2 It's good to have all of you.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for me today. You can catch me tomorrow at 1:00 p.m. on "America Reports" with \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . He will continue to be live from Geneva, as we await the big summit, the meeting with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 "The Five" starts now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210615eh6f0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Ilhan Omar Under Fire; Biden's Energy Policy; Working From Home Post-Pandemic?; Interview With \uc2 Rep. Darrell Issa\uc2  (R-CA); Interview With Rep. Michael Waltz (R-FL); Migrant Surge; Biden Speaks Out on Russian Threat\b0\par\par\uc2 Jacqui Heinrich, Steve Harrigan, Phil Flynn, Charlie Gasparino,  Mike Gunzelman, Bill Melugin, Jessica Tarlov, Liz Peek, Sandra Smith,  Benjamin Hall \par \uc2 8,093 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  issuing a warning to Russia before taking questions ahead of his Wednesday summit with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , this as another threat of China and the origins of COVID take center stage as global leaders meet. So, how is the president handling each of these crises?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 Benjamin Hall has been standing by. He is live from Brussels, Belgium, with the very latest as the president just wrapped his press conference.\par \par \uc2 Benjamin, a lot to take in there. We will have our panel in just a moment. But what did you hear?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, absolutely, Sandra, quite a free-flowing press conference.\par \par \uc2 And I think we heard from the president much the same as we have been hearing for the last few days, the sense of the \uc2 G7\uc2  and the \uc2 NATO\uc2  meetings were a message that America is back, a warning to both Russia and China that, if they push the boundaries, President Biden will respond. And here's what he said on Russia:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I shared with our allies that I will convey to President -- what I will convey to President Putin, that I'm not looking for conflict with Russia, but that we will respond if Russia continues its harmful activities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HALL: There are still questions, though, about the strength of the messaging coming so far from the meetings he's had.\par \par \uc2 The final \uc2 G7\uc2  communique, for example, made just a fleeting reference to the situation in the East and South China Sea. It is specified China's takeover and militarization of the islands. Meanwhile, on economics and trade, it says with regard to China and competition in the global economy: "We will continue to consult on collective approaches to challenging non-market policies."\par \par \uc2 Well, continue to consult, critics say, is hardly a forceful rejection of China's trade tactics. The fact is numerous European leaders are still reluctant to put their exports at risk to China by taking too forceful of a stance. And, in fact, Germany continues to lead a push for a trade deal with China, while also forging ahead with the Nord Stream II pipeline.\par \par \uc2 On COVID's origins, meanwhile, the \uc2 G7\uc2  said only, "We call for a timely, transparent, expert-led and science-based study in China." Again, many arguing that the time for real investigation has come and gone, with any evidence haven't been scrubbed clean.\par \par \uc2 And all of this comes ahead of President Biden's much anticipated meeting with President Putin on Wednesday, with both sides, frankly, setting pretty low expectations. When it comes to cyberattacks, President Putin already showing what his defense will be, deny, deny, deny.\par \par \uc2 So, as we come into day six of this first foreign trip for President Biden, there's a sense that he's sending some messaging, but he's not really hammering home the point of what could happen, what the repercussions might be. What he says to journalists and what we heard him said again just now is that he won't negotiate in front of the world's press. The people have to wait to see what he will say.\par \par \uc2 That's really between him and President Biden for now. But there are critics who say they would like to hear more forceful rhetoric from the president -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We will get more reaction here in just a moment, Benjamin Hall reporting live from Brussels for us.\par \par \uc2 Benjamin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 How did President Biden handle this press conference?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, FOX News contributor Liz Peek is here, former State Department official Christian Whiton, and FOX News contributor Jessica Tarlov.\par \par \uc2 All right, so let's kick things off.\par \par \uc2 We have first at it,right? He started taking questions at 3:38 local time, ended 3:54. It was 16 minutes in total.\par \par \uc2 Liz, first off, your reaction to what you heard. Was he forceful on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 LIZ PEEK, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think he hasn't really been forceful on either Putin or on China.\par \par \uc2 We keep hearing about there will be severe repercussions and sort of these veiled threats. What's he talking about? We just had the world free -- the free world leaders meet after a year where eight million people have died, Sandra, because China lied and covered up the emergence of this virus and allowed it to spread around the world.\par \par \uc2 And they didn't even address that. They didn't even begin to punish China for the most heinous act and have ever seen in the history of mankind. This is unbelievable to me. Look, these press conferences are pre-scripted. They're safe for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . They don't tell us much and they certainly don't tell us much about what we're actually going to do to confront our enemies.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Jessica, I only mentioned the time because it seemed rather short. There was such a big buildup to this press conference, and it was so highly anticipated. Do you think he struck the right notes?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Let me get to Jessica on that.\par \par \uc2 Jessica.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: That's OK. Hi, Liz.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think he did strike the right notes with what he did get out.\par \par \uc2 I wish that it had gone on for longer. And there was this kerfuffle on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , at least, because everyone was wondering why he was so late. It was over two hours' late for the press conference. And it appears the Ukrainians released a statement that made it seem like they had been allowed into \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and that would be why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was late. And then he got asked the question, which said, we still have to see what kind of commitments they can meet, and they need to clean up the corruption further.\par \par \uc2 I certainly would have loved to hear more about that, and especially the part about what we're going to do to help our allies, someone like Ukraine, against Russian aggression. So there are many unanswered questions left.\par \par \uc2 I did think, though, when it comes to the \uc2 G7\uc2  response to what happens in China, I don't know what they can say at this moment in terms of pushing back beyond we need full access and to be able to do a thorough investigation without the WHO into what actually happened in terms of the origins of COVID-19 out of that Wuhan lab, if that is indeed what happened.\par \par \uc2 But I'm looking forward to Wednesday. I imagine he's not going to stand there next to Putin and say, he told me he didn't meddle in the election, so I believe him. And I think we will have a lot more answers after that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, so you're addressing a multitude of issues. Obviously, he's got a lot of crises on his hand to deal with, Russia, China, the threats that they pose.\par \par \uc2 Christian, what we did hear exact words from President Biden, as he began, I shared with our allies, he said, that I will convey to President Putin -- quote -- "that I'm not looking for conflict with Russia, but that we will respond if Russia continues its harmful activities."\par \par \uc2 Republicans today -- I had Michael McCaul on my show earlier. He said -- he said that Biden's all talk, no action. Was he forceful enough with his messaging to Putin just a few moments ago?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WHITON, FORMER U.S. DEPUTY SPECIAL ENVOY: Well, the point is not to be forceful with your messaging, but with your actions.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Teddy Roosevelt said, speak softly and carry a big stick. Biden is doing the exact opposite of talking a big game and delivering nothing. And if you're \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  or \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , you're looking at this and just seeing a profoundly unserious experience.\par \par \uc2 Let me give you a specific. Earlier in this administration, the national security adviser, Sullivan, said that we were going to retaliate against Russia in cyberspace. He more or less said, that we would do things seen and unseen, if we became confident that Russia was behind the \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  hack.\par \par \uc2 We have become confident that Russia was behind the \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  hack. And yet there was no apparent retaliation. And word of that thing almost always leaks out, word of that type of cyberattack. So this is basically Biden's red line, the equivalent of when Obama said he would retaliate for chemical weapons uses in Syria and then didn't.\par \par \uc2 Biden began his presidency talking a tough game. So he's going to go to Geneva, meet with Putin. The atmospherics are already bad. And Putin's going to come away, I think, as will \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , as will \uc2 Kim Jong-un\uc2  in North Korea, realizing that this guy isn't serious and that these constructs left over from the 20th century, the \uc2 G7\uc2 , the E.U. and \uc2 NATO\uc2 , are just obsolete to the problems we face in this decade.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Liz, those are live pictures there as President Biden looks set to depart. Obviously, this is a big few days for the president there meeting with leaders of the E.U. tomorrow, Putin on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 He's going to hold a solo news conference, we're told, on Wednesday. It will not be a bilat alongside Putin. But he also said today in that press conference that he will make Putin aware of our red lines. He would not broadcast what those are exactly, Liz, because he said he did not want to negotiate on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Yes. They're not going to have a joint press conference, I'm sorry, to say because Putin, I think, would just totally dominate and be totally happy to do so.\par \par \uc2 I think Chris is totally right. What is our red line? If -- and, by the way, going back to this COVID issue that Jessica mentioned, it's not all about whether it came from the lab. It's about the fact that the Chinese knew months before they told anyone that it was viciously transferable, that they allowed people to leave Wuhan even as they were trying to basically ring-fence the city in terms of Chinese transmission and allowed the thing to go global and cause unmitigated damage.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Chinese -- Putin is a problem. I think we can easily confront Putin. I don't think that's such an issue. It's really China that we have to look at as a serious threat to the long-term best interests of Americans.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that the leaders of the free world basically whiffed on that, I think it's appalling. And, by the way, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is much more interested in being the non-Trump, being popular at these meetings, saying America is back.\par \par \uc2 What does it mean? Not much if it doesn't amount to anything.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We will have more on this coming up, as the president just wrapped his remarks.\par \par \uc2 We appreciate our panel. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: All right.\par \par \uc2 WHITON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , meanwhile, telling most employees to get into the office today, while \uc2 Facebook\uc2  says feel free to continue to work from home, folks.\par \par \uc2 So, which company is getting this right?\par \par \uc2 But first: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  saying that she will visit the border at some point, her words, 82 days after being tapped to lead the crisis.\par \par \uc2 Republican Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2  says that point is now. How did he invite her? And did he get a response?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: To the Southern border now, where the migrant surge is still ramping up at this hour.\par \par \uc2 FOX getting an exclusive look at those border crossings today.\par \par \uc2 FOX News' Bill Melugin is live in Mission, Texas, with the very latest from there.\par \par \uc2 Bill, you have been following this so closely. What are you seeing and hearing today?\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Sandra, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 What I can tell you is, it's starting to get to that time of year where it's very dangerous for these migrants to make this journey and cross into the United States. It's brutally hot out here. It's very humid. But that weather, it's just not slowing them down yet. They're still coming. They're still coming in big numbers every single morning like clockwork out here in the Rio Grande Valley.\par \par \uc2 And we will show you -- we will show that to you right now. Take a look at this video we shot in La Joya, Texas this morning about 15 minutes away from us. This is our FOX drone crew showing border agents chasing down a group of men who came across the border and actively tried to get away from Border Patrol. These are the guys who are known as runners. They're not turning themselves in. They are trying to get away. They do not want to be caught.\par \par \uc2 Most of these guys who we have talked to are typically from Guatemala, El Salvador, or Honduras, those Northern Triangle countries. And the reason why they're trying to get away is, they know, if they get caught, they're likely going to be deported pretty quickly under Title 42.\par \par \uc2 But that's not the case with everybody. Take a look at this video we shot also this morning also in La Joya. We mentioned this is like clockwork. This happens literally every single morning out here. These are family units who came through the brush and gave themselves up to Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 These folks are not trying to get away. And you will notice they look different. These are moms with their little kids, moms with their little toddlers. These are people who have been walking for quite some time, once they cross the Rio. And they just give themselves up to Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 And out live now, if you can take a look at our FOX flight team running the drone right now, you take a look at the border wall, there are chunks of that border wall where they're just chunks of it missing, there's holes. So, once they cross the Rio Grande, they're able to walk right through.\par \par \uc2 And then you can kind of see some of the journey they take over in that La Joya area. There are some dirt roads, but there is thick, heavy brush out there. And I can tell you, it is brutally hot out here. It's only getting hotter each day. The humidity is cranking up. It's dangerous for these migrants to do this.\par \par \uc2 Many of them have been asking us for water, asking Border Patrol for water as they come through. We talked to Border Patrol and asked them, do you think this weather, as you get deeper into the summer months, is going to slow down this surge? They said no, but, tragically, we do expect to find more bodies out there.\par \par \uc2 And wrapping this up, we all heard Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  say last week that she has a message for those migrants: Don't come to the border. From what we have been seeing here on the ground and what our crew was seeing in Del Rio last week, that message is either being ignored or just wasn't heard entirely in the first place.\par \par \uc2 We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Vice President Harris traveling to South Carolina today, but still no trip to the Southern border, as pressure continues to mount on her.\par \par \uc2 After saying that she would visit the border at some point, my next guest extended an invitation.\par \par \uc2 With us, now California Republican Congressman \uc2 Darrell Issa\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We also want to mention that we have invited the vice president to join us at her next availability here, and we have not yet heard back.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, great to see you. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA\uc2  (R-CA): Thanks, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Obviously, you have seen the situation at our border unfolding firsthand, and you have for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 So, what is the response you have gotten so far to the invitation you have sent to the vice president?\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Well, so far, I haven't gotten any response.\par \par \uc2 And,oddly enough, I went on this past Friday with four Democrat members of Congress into Mexico. We went and visited with our consul general, discussed the issues along the San Diego border, San Diego and Arizona border. So there's no reluctance by some Democrats, just not by that particular one.\par \par \uc2 It's sort of amazing to me that she's conspicuous in her inability to come to the border, when, in fact, it is clear that we are a draw, and that her message of don't come, it's been rejected.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, let's play what if. What if she does decide to pay a visit to the Southern border, for instance, the portion of the border that she visited as a U.S. senator?\par \par \uc2 What would she see in how the conditions changed?\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Well, what she will see is approximately 100 different countries have -- people from 100 different countries across the border. So it's not just the three countries of the Northern Triangle.\par \par \uc2 She's going to see that the message that's being sent, based on the policies of this administration, are clearly saying to a young man, don't come here looking for a job. Bring a family and stay. And that message is, in fact, much more of a conflict than she might realize.\par \par \uc2 If she hears that from the Border Patrol, hears their frustration, that, in fact, we are going to continue to release people who cannot possibly support themselves, people who are coming as family units, because coming alone as a worker, which is historically what most people do initially, isn't working.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, you covered in the last segment,as the weather gets hot, many young children are not going to make it across the border alive. This really is a dangerous time. And it can be stopped. Returning to the policies that would prevent families from being encouraged to come would be a huge help.\par \par \uc2 It'd be a help to the Border Patrol and their enforcement also.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Congressman, in addition to just showing that she sees it as a serious issue and a serious threat, as far as this being bipartisan, the calls for her to visit the border, your Democratic colleague \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  has spoken out and responded to that new interview that she did where she said she didn't quite understand the question from Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 And he responded she has to understand. And he is making it out that it is very important for her to come and visit the border.\par \par \uc2 How much longer can this go on, her not visiting the border? It's hard to fathom what her reason is for not doing so.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Well, it really is hard to fathom, considering the president gave her the portfolio, considering that she's already made that trip to Central America and to Mexico, and that it really is time for her to begin looking.\par \par \uc2 First of all, if you're -- if you're in South Carolina, you know why we're a draw to people around the world. It's not just what people are running from. It's what they're coming to.\par \par \uc2 But if she goes to the border, she has an opportunity to learn what Henry knows, as a border congressman, I know, as a border congressman, Juan Vargas knows as a border congressman, which is that we have got to support the men and women that we have asked to keep the border safe and secure, to stop drugs from coming over, to stop human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 There's a myriad of issues that are being complicated. So she won't just hear about migration. She will hear about the frustration by the men and women of the Border Patrol in not being able to do the other parts of their job effectively.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And important that she acknowledge the emergency and urgency this is for those local communities there, 180,000-plus border crossings in the month of May, about the same in April and March, but bigger.\par \par \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  says somebody needs to listen to our local communities. So, we will see. We will see what changes. We will see when that is she decides to visit.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, appreciate your time. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ISSA: Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , meanwhile, welcoming all staff back to the office today, as \uc2 Facebook\uc2  tells folks they are welcome to stay at home. So, should workers in this post-pandemic environment have the choice?\par \par \uc2 And later on, White House Democrats -- while House Democrats, I should say, remain divided after controversial comments by Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 , House Republicans moving to censure her and other members of the so-called Squad.\par \par \uc2 Why Republican Congressman Michael Waltz is leading that charge. He is here on that and what President Biden just said on Russia.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: A warning from President Biden ahead of his big meeting with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 What does Florida Republican Congressman Michael Waltz make of what he just said? He's coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: From the couch to the quarter office, most \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  employees back in business today, working in person at the home office in New York City.\par \par \uc2 This coming with \uc2 Facebook\uc2  going the other direction, reportedly allowing more employees to keep working from home.\par \par \uc2 So will more companies be following \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  and Co. or going away of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 The read from attorney Leeza Garber and Internet radio host Mike Gunzelman.\par \par \uc2 Leeza, I will start with you first.\par \par \uc2 I think this is fascinating because so many companies have major decisions to make regarding all of this, because so many people adapted their lifestyles to the work from home. Some like it, some don't. So what do you think? Do you think most companies will follow \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  and return to the office or do a hybrid like \uc2 Facebook\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 LEEZA GARBER, ATTORNEY: Hi, Sandra. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 And I know we're both dog people, right? So it's really been neat to get to work home for many people. You get to be around your dogs, your kids, your spouse, and get to be in a more comfortable environment.\par \par \uc2 In general, I think the pandemic caught many companies off-guard and they didn't have their cybersecurity up to snuff. I have to think about that as a techie and the cybersecurity lawyer. But at the same time, it's really brought up a great opportunity.\par \par \uc2 And Zuckerberg pointed out that it allows companies to take advantage of talent from all over the United States, because they don't have to come to a major city to work in a certain office.\par \par \uc2 What's interesting is it really depends on the company culture. We see Chase, other financial institutions are starting to try to get more people back in because of how they operate. But social media companies, technology giants are excited about remote work.\par \par \uc2 It's just you have to be proactive about cybersecurity.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, Mike, what do you think is going to happen here? Because in the case of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , I think \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  himself leading by example. He says that he too has enjoyed the aspect of his life where he spends more time with his wife and kids.\par \par \uc2 MIKE GUNZELMAN, FOX NEWS HEADLINES ENTERTAINMENT REPORTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: He said in this memo that he expects half of his company's 60,000 employees to work remotely within the next decade. My, how COVID changed the work environment.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Well, you're exactly right.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's no doubt that the workplace environment and the workplace situation has definitely changed, especially in the immediate future. I think that companies are going to now have to take this into consideration when they're trying to recruit new employees.\par \par \uc2 Where perhaps in the past, it was like, hey, you get two weeks paid vacation, now it's we might offer you the ability to work remotely. I think companies definitely have to take that into consideration.\par \par \uc2 Now, as far as what I think we're going to see, beginning in September, we heard this from a lot of companies all across the country. The hybrid schedule is going to be a thing that a lot of companies are doing, where somebody will go on for two or three days a week and work hybrid.\par \par \uc2 I like this idea for two reasons, one, the sheer mental health and the anxiety standpoint, where I don't know if everybody can handle go from zero to 100. I don't know if everybody needs to do zero to 100 immediately. And then also the second reason why a hybrid schedule I support is also there's a lot of time that's wasted waking up early, getting ready, commuting 45 minutes, hour-and-a-half each way.\par \par \uc2 That could be better well spent. And we have all adapted and learned how to make that work. So why do we have to go five days a week, at least in the immediate future?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Someone's -- speaking is from somebody doing this from home right - - we see your bookcases behind you, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I mean, Leeza, it's amazing how so many people's lives changed.\par \par \uc2 Here's my question, though, is, do you easily have people who never worked in the office or never -- never learned the work culture at these places? Like, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  is going to a hybrid model just down the street from Goldman. They're not doing a full return. They're giving people the option.\par \par \uc2 But those were people who were already working in an office environment. They knew their peers. They knew their colleagues. They had team building. And now they say they're very productive at home.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to hiring new people, and not having them come to the office, I think you must run into some challenges.\par \par \uc2 GARBER: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 And this is something Zuckerberg pointed out as well. And many companies have acknowledged this. When you have younger employees that are new to the work force, when you have employees that have recently been hired or transition to a new role, they really need to come in and get a feel for their co-workers and what the M.O. is around the office.\par \par \uc2 That's not to say that can't be done in a hybrid model. And, realistically, work from home is the future. As we have discussed before, it's more efficient. It can be more environmentally friendly and a time saver in terms of commute.\par \par \uc2 But it's also just with the way the world is going, with the way that bandwidth is for the Internet, with our fancy home offices.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARBER: All of these capabilities are here. Why not have people from all over the United States get to work in your office?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, I know New York City would be happy to have \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  employees back, because it's a different feel without those big buildings filled with those workers.\par \par \uc2 But I know they're going to be in shock when it comes to commute times.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Because they're not used to it. And I don't know what it is about traffic around here right now, but it is crazy.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Yes, it's true. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It's great to see both of you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GARBER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUNZELMAN: Awesome. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right.\par \par \uc2 Will new rules coming for corporate boardrooms have investors paying the price? Charlie Gasparino is on it.\par \par \uc2 And House lawmakers set to return from recess at the top of the hour, where a tweet by Democrat \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  has some members of both parties speaking out. But Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  says it's time to move on.\par \par \uc2 Republican Congressman Michael Waltz disagrees. He will tell us what he plans to do.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: SEC Chairman \uc2 Gary Gensler\uc2  unveiling a list of what some are calling woke reforms that the commission is planning to vote on.\par \par \uc2 If passed, the rules would include requiring companies to disclose details on things like board diversity and climate change efforts.\par \par \uc2 So, what all will all of this mean for investors?\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Charlie Gasparino has been following this all very closely for us.\par \par \uc2 So, Charlie, I know that you have got the inside scoop on so many of these companies. Is this a bad thing? Would corporations not look forward to this? Or perhaps it's an excuse to tout all the woke activities they have already engaged in.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes, a little bit of both.\par \par \uc2 Let's back up a little bit. This came out on Friday. FOX Business was first to report that he came out with his 50-item list of proposed rules. Now, what the SEC generally does, it proposes a rule, people comment on it, and then the commission votes. The full commission is five members. Right now, there are three Democrats, including Gensler, right, the Biden appointee, and two Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And all these corporate woke things will likely go down on these party-line votes. And here's the thing I will say. Do investors really need to know board diversity? Maybe some want to know, but do they really need to know? Do investors need to know just how much you are contributing to make the environment a better place? And what are those standards anyway? Who sets those standards?\par \par \uc2 I don't think so. A lot of traditional people in the markets don't believe so, that the profits and losses have nothing to do with these soft issues.\par \par \uc2 But this is -- we're in a different territory right now. We're -- the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is being run by progressives. \uc2 Gary Gensler\uc2  is very close to Elizabeth Warren. And what this does is this. These are disclosure mandates. Disclose to us how well you're helping the environment. Disclose to us your diversity efforts.\par \par \uc2 Do all this, so we can talk about it, so activist investors, who are not the usual activist investors that just want stocks to go up -- these are -- these are -- there any -- plenty of political activist investors in these stocks.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'm trying to think what -- I'm trying to think like what sectors might respond well to this and not.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the technology sector, for the most part, has already been very vocal on climate