this through before that happens. As you know, 2022 elections are coming.\par \par \uc2 And if \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  or somebody else -- I don't know if \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  is running for reelection in 2022. But if the Republicans are -- if they're the people running the Senate Banking Committee, that's the chairman.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: The Senate Banking Committee is chaired by Shelby or someone like that, you can bet that this stuff is going to -- and if he tries this, he's going to be brought up there every three minutes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It's remarkable, because, if you look at the technology industry and how they have already sort of gone in this direction, revealing their interests in climate change.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: They want more. They want...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That's the thing.\par \par \uc2 The employees saw that. And then they wanted more. And so be careful, because...\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: They want -- \uc2 Gary Gensler\uc2  wants...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: ... every company to mimic the leftism of technology, of the tech sector, every company.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It really is fascinating to watch.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: That's what he wants.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, we will see what happens. Charlie, great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You too.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, looks like Vlad is glad when it comes to American energy policy. We will tell you why.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Florida Republican Congressman and former Green Beret Michael Waltz on President Biden's warning for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ahead of Wednesday's summit. So, was it strong enough?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, forget getting tough on Russia.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest says President Biden is giving Russia and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  a big gift, and drivers are paying the price.\par \par \uc2 Oil expert \uc2 Phil Flynn\uc2  joining us now.\par \par \uc2 Phil, as President Biden gets ready for his high-stakes meeting with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , explain what exactly is going on here.\par \par \uc2 And hello to you.\par \par \uc2 PHIL FLYNN\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Hey. Hello, Sandra. It's great to be on with you again.\par \par \uc2 Hey, basically, what we're seeing here is that the U.S. is retreating from the energy stage, and Russia is filling that void. Under President Trump, the United States was competing with Russia and Saudi Arabia, and became the world's biggest energy producer. And that put a lot of pressure on the old foes in the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cartel and our foes in Russia.\par \par \uc2 Now, since President Biden's been in office, we have seen U.S. energy production fall. We have seen drilling moratoriums. We have been killing pipelines. And at the same time, President Biden is approving pipelines in Russia, the Nord Stream pipeline, giving \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  what he really wants, power.\par \par \uc2 And energy production is power. I just don't think that President Biden gets that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Wow. It's really something to look at this crude oil chart on a year-to-date basis, now on a one-year basis. And we see the price continue to go higher.\par \par \uc2 Talk about the real-world impact that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's energy policies are having on prices, not just big picture like crude oil, but gasoline at the pump.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: What is happening is that he is causing prices to go higher, because he's telling people don't invest in oil.\par \par \uc2 And that's one of the things that we're seeing. We're seeing one of the biggest pullbacks in energy investments in history. People are not planning for oil tomorrow, because President Biden says we're going to be off oil in 30 years. I don't think Russia or \uc2 OPEC\uc2  believes that.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they're going to benefit from the U.S. retraction in that. And we are creating the blueprint for an energy shortage and an energy crisis in the future. President Biden hasn't thought this thing out. I think a more smooth transition off of fossil fuels would have been a lot smarter. But to tell everybody to stop investing in oil, that's going to mean that we're going to see shortages and hurt the economy.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Real quick, Phil -- and just some background here, you and I go back to trading floor days in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 And you used to watch for geopolitical risk in the commodities markets, right? It was an everyday concern for any trader that was involved in those markets. But then we became so energy-independent that what was happening abroad wasn't such a concern.\par \par \uc2 So what are markets watching as this summit is about to happen with Putin on Wednesday? What will you be watching?\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: I think we're watching to see if President Biden gets tough on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And I don't think he will.\par \par \uc2 And you're absolutely right. The U.S. energy producers took the global risk premium out of the market.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: Because they could raise production on a dime. But guess what? President Biden pulled back from that.\par \par \uc2 And so when the U.S. producers do not control things, Russia and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  does. That means that the geopolitical risk factor is getting back into those prices. And you and I are going to pay for it at the gas pump.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You always make it so easy for everybody understand, my friend, \uc2 Phil Flynn\uc2 , there in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Best to all the traders there. Say hi when you see them.\par \par \uc2 Phil, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: I will.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: They miss you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. All right, thank you, and me as well.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  approaching two decades as Russia's president meanwhile. And to understand how he amassed so much power, you would have to look at the man he replaced, \uc2 Boris Yeltsin\uc2 , for clues.\par \par \uc2 Steve Harrigan lived in Russia and reported from there for 10 years. Steve joins us now live from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Sandra, there was so much enthusiasm when Putin first came into office, in part because of simply the physical contrast between Putin and \uc2 Boris Yeltsin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice-over): By 1999, most Russians were ready for a change. The hero who stood on the tank in 1991 and broke communist control over freedom of speech, elections and private property was also blamed by those who saw their life savings disappear, as a new class of billionaires scooped up state factories.\par \par \uc2 Yeltsin's lengthy disappearances from public view were blamed on heart attacks or excessive drinking.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To many Russians, President \uc2 Boris Yeltsin\uc2  felt like an embarrassment. He was very famous for being inebriated. He was a known alcoholic and a big drinker. He was very large just in stature.\par \par \uc2 So, the way that he behaved in these high-level public diplomacy meetings with U.S. presidents or other heads of state embarrassed a lot of Russians at the time.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The physical contrast with Putin was stark.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He's fit. He's a martial arts champion. He says he never drinks.\par \par \uc2 And for many Russians, that also gave them a sense of stability. Mr. Putin embodied that physically in a way that President Yeltsin absolutely did not.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Putin also embodied a return to an authoritarian past, shutting down press freedom, vanishing independent oligarchs and consolidating power in his own hands.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: For journalists in Moscow during the 1990s, there was often the sense of panic. Between Yeltsin's four heart attacks and his drinking, he would disappear for weeks at a time.\par \par \uc2 You had to staff the bureau every weekend because there were real concerns Yeltsin could die at any moment -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That is remarkable perspective.\par \par \uc2 Steve Harrigan, thank you for your reporting on that. Appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Some members of both parties saying, oh, my, over a controversial tweet by Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why former Green Beret and Florida Republican Michael Waltz is leading the charge to censure her and other members of the Squad. He will join us live next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: House Democrats returning from recess to a House divided after a controversial tweet by Minnesota Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  sparked backlash from within her own party.\par \par \uc2 FOX News correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has been following all of this and the fallout on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Three House Republicans are moving to censure Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  and her progressive colleagues in the House for -- quote -- "defending foreign terrorist organizations and using rhetoric that contributes to anti-Semitic attacks."\par \par \uc2 Now, the GOP push to punish Omar comes after House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said the Democratic Caucus is moving on after Omar walked back her comments appearing to equate democracies like the U.S. and Israel with terror groups.\par \par \uc2 Appearing separately on the same program, Pelosi and AOC projected a willingness not to let this splinter the party.\par \par \uc2 AOC, for her, part redirected blame at the media. That's an about-face, after she blamed -- or -- excuse me -- her progressive colleagues, rather, slammed her Democratic Jewish colleagues and Democratic leadership for even so much as asking Omar to clarify.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): But what I'm saying is, is end of subject. She clarified. We thanked her.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): This whole hubbub started with right-wing news outlets taking what she said, out of context. And when we feed into that, it adds legitimacy to a lot of this kind of right-wing vitriol.\par \par \uc2 We are putting this behind us, and I believe that we will ultimately come together as a caucus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans, though, are not going to let this go so easily.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Jim Banks said: "Actions speak louder than squishy words. Speaker Pelosi can let members vote on our resolution or she can cover for the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  caucus and their anti-Israel and anti-American rhetoric. We will see."\par \par \uc2 Now, it's unclear if any Democrats support that censure resolution from the House Republicans, although there are some simmering lingering frustrations about the record of comments coming from Congresswoman Omar and the -- what she's said basically over time about the U.S.-Israel relationship and U.S. foreign policy -- Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, Jacqui Heinrich reporting from Capitol Hill for us.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Florida Republican Michael Waltz is one of the congressmen behind that resolution to center the Squad. And he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 We have reached out, by the way, to Democrats that were named in this measure. And we have not heard anything back.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Yes, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: So, start off first with \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  and your decision to censure her. Why?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, we're censuring her and the Squad. We have renamed them the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  caucus, because this isn't just a one-off.\par \par \uc2 This is a series of statements. I will remind everyone that Ms. Omar was formally censured in 2019. That did pass the House. That was voted on for then statements.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is, to equate the United States, Israel with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and -- quote, unquote -- "atrocities" is personally offensive to me. You know I'm a Green Beret that served all over the Middle East, Africa. I have seen women personally attacked. I have seen acid thrown on their faces, girl schools, machine gun, children with suicide vests strapped onto them by these terrorist organizations.\par \par \uc2 And I have visited Israeli homes, all of which, their schools and bus stops included, have to have bunkers to defend their families against \uc2 Hamas\uc2  rockets. So, enough is enough. We need to speak out. This is offensive. And I have received outreach from a number of Jewish Americans who believe that this rhetoric is contributing to the anti-Semitic attacks across the United States that we have seen just in the past few months.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Do you fear that you could be labeled the same for calling them the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  caucus?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, I think that they have shown time and time again they are willing to stand and really turn a blind eye to what these groups are doing.\par \par \uc2 Let's remind everyone that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  is not only launching rockets into cities with the intent to kill civilians, but they're doing so hiding behind women and children, schools, hospitals, and other public buildings.\par \par \uc2 And, as someone who has been shot at by terrorists hiding behind women and children, again, enough is enough. So they can throw out whatever rhetoric they want, but I'm talking about the actions that I have personally seen and they shouldn't apologize for...\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: ... and we need to take a stand for.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I want to circle back on that in just a second.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: But so you're looking to censure not just her, but the Squad. You make that very clear.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  says, you know what, she offered a clarification, \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 . She's OK with that. She says it's time to move on.\par \par \uc2 Why was it not good enough for you?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Because this isn't just a one-time incident.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have Representative Tlaib comparing Israel to apartheid. We have had, again, a series of incidences in 2019 that led to another censure resolution that was voted on. This is a trend. And this is why I think you're seeing my Democratic colleagues of Jewish American background also saying, enough is enough, it's time to speak out, and it's time to stop this.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: As far as division within the Democratic Party, obviously, President Biden just took the opportunity overseas in Brussels to talk about what he called a fractured Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: What about the divisions in the Democratic Party? What do you see?\par \par \uc2 Real quick. I have a few seconds left, 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, just, you know, so much -- so much for politics stopping at the border, right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: I mean, we -- we heard Biden and everybody else criticizing Republican administrations.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: And yet there he is on the world stage criticizing Republicans. It's a shame.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We're out of time.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, I appreciate you joining us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: All right. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That's it for me today.\par \par \uc2 Sorry. I will be on "Gutfeld!" tonight, by the way. You can catch me tomorrow again 1:00 p.m. Eastern for "America Reports" with John Roberts. He will be live from Geneva.\par \par \uc2 "The Five" starts now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210614eh6e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Jackson County, Texas, Sheriff A.J. Louderback; Interview With Fmr. Gov. Scott Walker (R-WI); New COVID Concerns?; Some States Ending Extended Unemployment Benefits; Texas Building Border Wall?; Global Minimum Corporate Tax; House Seeking to Break Up Big Tech Companies\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Jonathan Serrie, Peter Doocy, Scott Martin, Jonathan  Turley, Hillary Vaughn \par \uc2 7,961 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you very much, Martha.\par \par \uc2 On that hockey theme, I think four of the biggest technology companies on Earth have just been, well, iced. A bipartisan effort in the House we're learning about right now to rein them in, that they have gotten too big for their britches, that they should be broken up.\par \par \uc2 Now, this kind of threat has been out there before. Here's what makes it different now. It's done through a variety of bills with bipartisan support. Republicans aren't keen on these guys. Democrats aren't keen on them. And the fact that these guys include the richest on the planet just adds more spice to the drama.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 And FOX on top of a barrelling down on the most successful companies on Earth that they have essentially gotten too big for their britches.\par \par \uc2 Hillary Vaughn on what they're talking about on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, it's a blockbuster day on Capitol Hill preparing for a battle royal between a bevy of bipartisan lawmakers and big tech. It's rare here in Washington for conservatives and progressives to find common ground on anything.\par \par \uc2 But, today, they might have found it with five bills expected to debut in the House targeting companies like Apple, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and \uc2 Google\uc2 . If all five bills pass, they would transform how each company does business.\par \par \uc2 Progressive Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  and Republican Congressman Lance Gooden joining forces on one bill that would end platform monopolies, this one targeted at -- targeted at companies like \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . The bill wants to effectively break up the company by making it illegal for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to own a platform and then offer their own goods and services on it, so that they don't get an extra advantage competing side by side with other sellers.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Gooden tells us this in a quote: "This legislation breaks up big tech's monopoly power to control what Americans see and say online and fosters an online market that encourages innovation and provides American small businesses with a fair playing field. Doing nothing is not an option. We must act now."\par \par \uc2 Another bill would force online platforms to create an interface that lets users move their data to other services, leveling the playing field for emerging platforms like Parler. A different bill would raise the price of filing fees for mergers so that the FTC and DOJ has enough money to actually enforce antitrust laws against these massive companies.\par \par \uc2 But, Neil, this may be too good to be true, because a Republican top aide tells me that, behind the scenes, some Republicans are getting wet feet, as they're getting a peek at the details. And he tells me that, right now, between Republicans and Democrats, it is chaos -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, fascinating, Hillary Vaughn.\par \par \uc2 As Hillary touched on here, there's long been this enmity between tech companies and both parties here. Republicans don't like a lot of these guys, particularly the social media giant \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , because they think it's slanted against Republicans. And Democrats have been concerned that these guys are simply getting too big for their britches, and that they don't really look out for the little guy, as well as rival products on sites like, I don't know, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  comes to mind.\par \par \uc2 But breaking them up and doing all of that, that touches on the antitrust issues that are -- can be potential legal messes.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Jonathan Turley, the G.W. law professor, FOX contributor, joins us on the phone.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, where do you think this is going?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, this is a very complex path that people are choosing, because they're seeking legislatively to break up a company that may not actually satisfy the standard as a monopoly.\par \par \uc2 So, these companies have grounds to challenge any effort to force them to split up. But what I think you're really seeing here, there is consensus in not just the public, but also in the parties, that these companies are becoming something of a menace.\par \par \uc2 The level of power and wealth concentrated in these companies is breathtaking. And I think that most people are looking at this and saying, this is a new type of monopoly. I mean, this is -- the Sherman Act was designed for, like, Standard Oil, where you had someone that was going to just control the market for a particular product.\par \par \uc2 These companies are just massive enterprises that control various aspects of life. And I think that most citizens are realizing that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and even the social media companies touch their lives in so many different way. And I think that makes a lot of people uneasy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Jonathan, I can also be reminded of be careful what you wish for.\par \par \uc2 When you go after giants -- and these are the most profitable and successful companies on the planet -- they're all American, by the way -- you could do more harm than good. And the ills you are addressing at the moment are outdated with technology not to far after the moment, like we wanted -- at the time when they wanted to break up \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , that it sort of owned the technology world.\par \par \uc2 And I could go back in time with a variety of companies where that was the approach, and they rued the day. What do you think of where this could go?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I think these companies could put forward a very strong challenge in court if, legislatively, they order the breakup of the companies.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's ways that you can do it. But it's largely best to be tied to some benefit, right? Congress can do a lot of things in exchange for benefits. So, if you want immunity from lawsuits, for example, Congress can stipulate certain conditions.\par \par \uc2 But that's not what we're talking here. We're talking about King Solomon dividing this company -- these companies in half. And that's not going to be a solution...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: ... these companies are going to accept.\par \par \uc2 And your example is a very good one. You look at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and other companies, those cases went on for years in order to try to make out the case and make out the possible solution to this type of monopolizing conduct. We're talking about companies here which are really a new creature, when we approach them from this type of monopoly standpoint.\par \par \uc2 They don't fit neatly into the monopoly, and yet they have monopolizing aspects. And it is true that the law has to catch up with this new creature. But this may be a little too quick and a little too easy. I think this is a far more complex issue than saying, we just want you to divide it up into parts.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, indeed, the technology industry itself has responded in unison to say that this goes a little too, too far.\par \par \uc2 We are going to explore that with a market panel.\par \par \uc2 But, Jonathan Turley, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, for those of you who just joined us, there's an all-out assault on the four biggest technology -- or among them -- biggest technology companies on the planet. They include \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and Apple, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2 , where the collective read from both parties, because this is a bipartisan approach, is that they have simply got too big and too dangerous, and they leverage off their success to the degree that it harms competition.\par \par \uc2 We have heard that before in American history. It is happening again. We saw it with the breakup of \uc2 AT&T\uc2  in the early 1980s and with the collective assault on \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  certainly in the late 1990s into the 2000s.\par \par \uc2 To Scott Martin now and Larry Glazer and Erin Gibbs following the impact of all of this.\par \par \uc2 Scott, if you're looking for an immediate market response, this story was out there in the past hour, and very little ripple, even in these individual stocks. The S&P for example, today, a collection of widely owned stocks that represent America, hit another record.\par \par \uc2 So, is this the market's way of saying this isn't going anywhere?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MARTIN, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Probably.\par \par \uc2 As a good friend of mine says, Neil, if the market cares, it has a funny way of showing it, doesn't it, when stocks barely move?\par \par \uc2 That's you, by the way.\par \par \uc2 Look, here's the thing.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thought I recognized that.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: The market knows, as you talked about with Jon there, the history.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the history here, Neil, of government either -- kind of on both sides here, as we have seen with this bipartisan effort, going after big tech is a fruitless effort. And I like the aspect of talking about how much \uc2 Google\uc2 , Apple, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  touch our lives every day, but they're doing that because they're good at what they do.\par \par \uc2 I mean, a long time ago, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  was selling books out of his garage, and has grown this company into a behemoth because they're good at what they do. So, if \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and Apple and \uc2 Google\uc2  are to blame for this, for example, and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , too, we're all to blame, because we use these companies as much as we do, because they're so successful at what they do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Erin Gibbs, we're getting a statement out of the International Center for Law and Economics, saying: "Adopting the European regulatory model would make it harder for American tech companies to innovate and compete both here and globally."\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 ERIN GIBBS, GIBBS WEALTH MANAGEMENT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And, in general, one of the reasons that we have a lot of the biggest, most innovative and successful corporations in the U.S. is because we don't have the regulatory infrastructure that the \uc2 European Union\uc2  does. and that's why our GDP grows, and both within tech and other businesses, is so much higher.\par \par \uc2 So, I think following that model is the last thing that the U.S. could possibly want, if they want to maintain truly dominance and growth and also in fighting China.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, it's interesting, the timing, isn't it, guys?\par \par \uc2 You have the president over there in Europe with the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders. They have already agreed that -- a simple minimum corporate tax that would be implemented around the world, that 15 percent is a good idea. They're all on the same page there. The Europeans, of course, have been going after these American technology juggernauts for some time.\par \par \uc2 I'm not sure how it all parcels out, but there's a different vibe, certainly, out of Europe, and now with a new administration in this country, that doesn't necessarily rally around this -- the uber-successful business crowd. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 LARRY GLAZER, MAYFLOWER ADVISORS: Neil, I think you're right that, look, there's a lot of fish and chips going on in Europe, and there's a lot of back-slapping.\par \par \uc2 But, at the end of the day, we all have a love-hate relationship with these big technology firms. And the winner of all this, ironically, will be the Chinese technology firms. They will be laughing all the way to Beijing, that we're going to go after our own tech leaders.\par \par \uc2 Of course, love-hate relationship. We love the fact that our 401(k)s go up. We love the fact that our municipal pensions have gone up to help pay for roads and schools and all the things locally. We love the fact that hospitals have more money in their pensions because of these technology firms. And they have brought our costs down.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, we hate them because we're jealous. They make so much money. They're so successful. There's so innovative. They have done so many things right. They don't pay enough in taxes, and they need more regulation. All those reasons, we hate them.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, it's the law of unintended consequences. And you don't want to kill the S&P 500 because of political theater and grandstanding and wipe out the little guy's 401(k). That's not good long- term business, and it's not good for everybody.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, these companies have a giant bullseye on them in Washington. The bullseye is getting bigger and bigger. And, today, we heard it roar. And the market may need a weekend to absorb it to realize what the long-term implications are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Boy, it's a good thing to have a weekend show to do just that, but enough about me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Back to me and my ideas, Scott.\par \par \uc2 I am wondering, if you think about it, that we are taking advice from politicians, either here or abroad, not too good with handling their constituents' money, but will jump at the chance to judge what these guys do what theirs. That is sort of like me offering you, a thin and fit young man, dietary advice.\par \par \uc2 I would not go there, Scott, but they're going there. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: I would take exercise tips from you any day, Neil, for that matter.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: But here's the thing. You're right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're in bad shape, my friend.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: You know what else is funny about D.C. here, Neil?\par \par \uc2 These are companies that are major donors to both sides, but certainly the Democratic side. And a lot of those companies that they have these bullseyes on, as Larry said, are in Kamala Harris' backyard.\par \par \uc2 So you're right. Not only are they mismanaging a lot of the constituents' money. They're going to even really screw up possible big donations that they're getting in the future. So that's why I think it's right. This is political theater. This is more grandstanding. This is more, hey, look at what we're going to try to do to antitrust laws, and also -- antitrust laws, if I can say that.\par \par \uc2 Also, don't forget, antitrust laws were created to help the consumer with better products and lower prices. How does doing what the government is trying to do today to these four companies actually do that? That's what I want to know.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you know, it's interesting, Erin. I don't know whether all of these companies should reexamine their P.R. and lobbying efforts on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans and Democrats alike hate them. And they want to go after them, albeit for different reasons.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So I don't dismiss this out of hand. I know Republicans are very concerned about weighing into antitrust issues and all of that. I know their inherent DNA leeriness there.\par \par \uc2 But these companies, they better pay attention to this. That's all I'm saying. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 I think this is partially political theater. We're already -- these are the companies that are going to be hit the hardest, potentially, with a minimum global tax, if that did come around, because they are big international companies that leverage other companies' lower rates.\par \par \uc2 So, perhaps it's just a way of scaring them, letting them know that there is a target, and, if they fight too hard on higher taxes, that this is what like that they could also potentially face. So they have been run in front of Congress many times. And I feel like this is political theater.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, they might all entertain the CEOs getting condos in Washington, D.C., if they don't have them already, because I think they're going to be visiting there quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 Guys. I want to thank you all, very, very much.\par \par \uc2 GLAZER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will be exploring this in much greater detail on my Saturday show at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time, the impact of all of this, targeting the most successful companies on the planet and tying it to something else that's going on we're going to be exploring, actually, right after the break this global minimum corporate tax, this notion that companies can run from the tax man, but they can't hide, that it's real, that it's happening, and that this is something that President Biden agrees with and the other \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders want to push, a tax that you can't run away from -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I have been reading a lot of the British press on the \uc2 G7\uc2  meeting and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and what they make of him.\par \par \uc2 They clearly are smitten by him, because he is smitten and on the same page with them when it comes to things like climate change and paying and offering more funds to develop and help the developing world.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is, this is going to be a lot of money. And some of the things they're talking about, it's going to cost companies a lot of money in the process too. Talk of a global minimum corporate tax set at 15 percent, could go higher.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy following it all from Cornwall, England.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good evening from Cornwall.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden team says they believe the key to global economic fairness is this new global corporate minimum tax of 15 percent. A senior Biden administration official explains why they like this tax on a conference call.\par \par \uc2 This: "We see this as a critical step towards ending the race to the bottom on corporate taxes that's come at the expense of workers, at the expense of investments in domestic renewal, and at the expense of growing the middle class."\par \par \uc2 A senior administration official also says a global minimum tax helps with a foreign policy for the middle class, a huge focus of the groups -- the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2 's attempt to try to level the playing field after an uneven recovery from COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 However, just because the \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  endorses a global minimum tax doesn't mean it instantly becomes the law of the land in the U.S. or anywhere else. The world leaders today also talked about COVID-19. They talked -- they got a Zoom from a leader in gender equality in the U.K. And if you add climate change in there, you have got all the host \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  wants to accomplish this week, as he borrows a campaign slogan from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: But building back better together, and building back greener, and building back fairer, and building back more equal, and actually in a more gender-neutral and perhaps a more feminine way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And, right now, most of the world leaders are still having dinner at Eden Place, which is near here, and it is one of the world's largest greenhouses, keeping with the whole theme of the week -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see what they did there.\par \par \uc2 All right, Peter Doocy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Peter in Cornwall, England.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read on this.\par \par \uc2 Claudia Rosett, she predicted this would be the sort of like the falling out there, that they'd all be on the same page, that things have changed.\par \par \uc2 And, Claudia, sure enough, you're right. One of the things that I did notice, besides just the global minimum corporate tax, that the president is pushing and getting the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders, all of them to go along with this. It's an uphill fight, I grant you, but that he is also dealing with something that has bipartisan support in the United States, to break up big, successful technology companies.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering. That's a theme I have heard out of Europe a lot, where, especially when it comes to American companies, they're too big for their britches, and we're going to do something about it. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA ROSETT, FOREIGN POLICY FELLOW, INDEPENDENT WOMEN'S FORUM: Well, breaking up some of the big tech companies makes sense. They're not behaving very well these days.\par \par \uc2 But a global minimum tax is not the way to do it. And, frankly, for British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  to say that these policies are feminine, I find rather insulting to women, who I think are smarter than that.\par \par \uc2 This whole thing that they're producing at the \uc2 G7\uc2  looks to be like somebody at the White House and in the prime minister's office in England read George Orwell's "Animal Farm," that they decided it was full of really great policy ideas.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you know what's changed, if you think about it, they announced as much, that they want to stop the race to the bottom when it comes to taxes.\par \par \uc2 ROSETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This one move, if they get their way, is a signal that now they're racing to the top again.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering how that plays out. You would think they'd work off at advantage with the United States, as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is raising taxes, to keep theirs where they are, lower, to try to woo business from him. That might still happen, but, instead, no, no, they're really kind of on the same higher tax push.\par \par \uc2 ROSETT: We know how this plays out. This plays out where the world's developed democracies, as they talk about building back better, come out of the pandemic, and are hell-bent on saddling themselves with complex and incredibly burdensome regulatory regimes, coordinated in some global way, that's not going to work very well.\par \par \uc2 What we're really setting up here is a high-tax cartel that will promote big government. So, while President Biden has been talking about using the democracies, rallying democracies -- and I love that idea. Freedom, great, let's do it.\par \par \uc2 But what's actually materializing is this vast network of spending and regulation between the climate things, the COVID promises, the rest of it, and this global minimum tax, that's actually going to dramatically burden the world's developed democracies.\par \par \uc2 I think China must love all this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, and China will have a lower tax rate than all of these guys, when all is said and done. Just an aside.\par \par \uc2 Claudia, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 ROSETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Again -- yes, right. It's a weird world, my friend.\par \par \uc2 All right, following all of that, following the trends on this, and then, back in this country, the push for a wall. If Washington won't do it, the governor of Texas just said he will.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, President Biden has stopped building the border wall, so the governor of Texas, who has already declared an emergency because of what's happening there, says, you know what? I will build it myself.\par \par \uc2 Can he do that?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you remember when President Trump was building that wall and moving rapidly to wall off the entire border, or where it could be feasibly done?\par \par \uc2 Well, all of that ended when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office, stopped it immediately. And a lot of people say that's when the problems really began and the run in to our border really started.\par \par \uc2 Now the governor of Texas said, you know what, if you don't do it, Mr. President, I will, that Texas will build a border wall.\par \par \uc2 To the Jackson County, Texas, sheriff who welcomes, A.J. Louderback back with us.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff, very good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So, you support this?\par \par \uc2 A.J. LOUDERBACK, JACKSON COUNTY, TEXAS, SHERIFF: Thank you, Neil. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 We certainly do. I mean, when you -- Neil, when you're faced with the kind of federal administration here that has no regard for Texas or Americans here in the politics that they put in place, we have got to protect ourselves. That's what our governor is doing. That's what Governor Greg Abbott is doing right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Sheriff, one of the things that -- and some of the numbers do, in fact, bear you out, sir, that a lot of these areas where migrants are just flooding into the border are coming in these areas where there are divisions or unfinished parts of this wall.\par \par \uc2 What is your thinking, if he were to finish it just along the Texas border? It doesn't -- other states would have to do what they do or not do. But what do you think it would do?\par \par \uc2 LOUDERBACK: Well, it can help.\par \par \uc2 I mean, walls help. They have helped everywhere, California. They have helped there in Texas, Arizona. They're a necessary part of defining your country from a foreign country here.\par \par \uc2 But that's not all that the governor has done. The governor has a comprehensive plan here, declaration of disaster, compact agreements with Arizona and other states coming, 10,000 beds, ability for us to charge criminal trespass, endangering child charges on illegal aliens for them to cross.\par \par \uc2 If we can't affect the policies that are put in place, which have essentially nullified federal law and his created a system where CBP, the fine men and women of the Customs and Border Patrol and ICE unable to do their job, completely, completely nullified that complete body of law, I mean, what -- what's Texas to do?\par \par \uc2 What are the other states going to do in response to the invasion of your state, the destruction, of the dead bodies, the abuse of women, the ecological issues that nobody complains about? No female rights group complaining about the prostitution, the untold rapes and abuse of women, our chases, our busted fences, our property damage, which Governor Abbott is addressing currently with his new initiative.\par \par \uc2 So these are all things that the United States needs to be aware of that's going on here every day here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Sheriff, you're closer to this than I am.\par \par \uc2 But I -- one thing I have noticed, especially in our Griff Jenkins' reporting along the border, is how brazen this has become and how a smuggler, knowing full well that our Griff was there, his camera was there, even allowed him to talk to him there. They don't care. They are still doing it.\par \par \uc2 And they happily and boldly, brashly, however you want to define it, keep coming. What do you make of that? That's very different.\par \par \uc2 LOUDERBACK: Neil, you're very -- you're very accurate. Total defiance, total arrogance, knowing nothing's going to happen, knowing that federal law enforcement has been completely nullified and taken out of their enforcement area here.\par \par \uc2 The migration patterns -- the empowerment of the cartels is unparalleled in U.S. history in what's happening here today, the money, the millions, hundreds of millions of dollars that they're making every month here. It's a true -- a true crisis, not a laughing matter from this administration.\par \par \uc2 And they still, still today will pay no attention on the policies they intentionally put in place here to do exactly what has happened.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sheriff, we will watch this one very, very closely.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank you for all you're doing and taking the time to talk to us today.\par \par \uc2 A.J. Louderback is the Jackson County, Texas, sheriff. He sees what's going on firsthand, as well as the brazen nature of it that's getting, well, out of hand.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a lot more coming up, including what happens in four states effective tomorrow, those extended federal unemployment benefits, remember, that had been pushed back to September?\par \par \uc2 Well, they pushed them right back out to tomorrow. They end tomorrow. Then what?\par \par \uc2 After this?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have been hearing a lot this week about labor shortages, how difficult it is right now to find workers.\par \par \uc2 Well, a lot of governors, largely Republican ones, are saying they're because of these extended unemployment benefits that last right through September. So, some of them have cut those benefits to expire as soon as tomorrow, four states in that direction. Another dozen are looking at doing so in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 We have got Desiree Tims with us, Democratic strategist, Phil Wegmann of RealClearPolitics to discuss the impact this might have.\par \par \uc2 Desiree, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 DESIREE TIMS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I think this is purely about politics and not about helping working-class people. Even \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , a Wall Street bank, said that this is purely political, and not about economics.\par \par \uc2 Look, the people who are bagging your groceries and delivering your \uc2 Amazon\uc2  packages are not freeloaders, like the billionaires who are paying zero dollars in taxes. We need to make sure we're helping the working class, not cutting their knees from underneath them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I see where you're coming from.\par \par \uc2 But I believe, in this particular case, Phil, the focus was on those businesses that can't find workers, because those same workers to whom Desiree outlined, I mean, they -- they're getting these extended benefits that are more generous than the pay that these businesses can offer them, and therein lies the rub.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEGMANN, REALCLEARPOLITICS: Well, Desiree is right, in that this sets up a really interesting experiment in federalism here.\par \par \uc2 You have got the states that are the laboratories of democracy, and, of those four states that are going to be ending unemployment benefits early, they're Republican states. There are other Republican states who are also thinking of doing something similar.\par \par \uc2 And the argument there is that these unemployment benefits, they have run their course at this point. The argument is that they are subsidizing unemployment, and that they are widening the gap between employers who need labor and employees who need to return to the work force.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, though, you have the White House, who has said that, no, the unemployment numbers are not the result of these benefits. Instead, that comes from pandemic wariness or lack of childcare. And just the other day, we had the president's national economic adviser come out and tout to the fact that unemployment numbers were basically halved.\par \par \uc2 So, what's interesting about this is that, give this a couple months, we might be able to look back at this moment and compare red state vs. blue state and see which economy is doing better.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you're probably right about that.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we're not ending all unemployment benefits, just those extended ones that were going to be pushed into September.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, though, Desiree, I mean, I'm wondering if good intentions go too far. Now, obviously, when these extensions were considered, a lot of Republicans voted for them as well. The notion was that this was going to drag on and be a drag on the economy for much longer than it ultimately has proven to be the case.\par \par \uc2 And now it's the reverse effect. Now it's preventing people from looking for jobs, because they're doing the math. They're not dumb. And they realize that, until these go away, they're better just sticking with what they have than venturing out and