this is that we have had back-to- back jobs reports come out that have been less than stellar.\par \par \uc2 And yet, at the same time, we have seen that COVID numbers are dropping across the board, because there's been a lot of progress in terms of vaccines and other things. These unemployment benefits, they're additional, like you mentioned a second ago, but they weren't expected to last forever.\par \par \uc2 I think that we saw \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  last month, when he said, if you're offered a suitable job, you ought to take it. I think, eventually, that is going to become more of the White House's messaging.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, guys, I want to thank you both, Desiree and Phil, on this very perplexing issue, because both sides continue heatedly arguing their points.\par \par \uc2 We will explore this more tomorrow on "Cavuto Live" at 10:00 a.m., the implications of all of this.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the implications of COVID cases that could be spiking again, and in the weirdest places -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we interrupt these reopenings for a little bit of, shall I say re-worrying, some new COVID concerns that are popping up across the country in some places.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta with more.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan what's going on?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, just some reminders that the pandemic is not yet over as America reopens.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg reports that many of the attendees at last week's Bitcoin conference in Miami have become ill with COVID-19. It's still unclear whether the infections incurred in crowded and largely unmasked official gatherings or at numerous after-parties.\par \par \uc2 The Celebrity Millennium, one of the first cruise ships to resume sailing since the pandemic, reports two passengers sharing the same cabin have tested positive. They show no symptoms, but remain in isolation. For cruises, CDC guidelines require crew members to be fully vaccinated and all passengers 16 and older to show proof they got the shots.\par \par \uc2 Today, the state of Illinois and city of Chicago lifted capacity limits and social distancing restrictions. Residents are still urged to continue taking commonsense precautions.\par \par \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  confirms the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has authorized the release of two batches of ingredients for its COVID vaccine manufactured by its contractor \uc2 Emergent BioSolutions\uc2 . According to the Associated Press, this is enough material to make 10 million doses. However, many more doses must be thrown out because of a manufacturing error -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jonathan Serrie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 To Dr. Mike Varshavski. Always love having Dr. Mike on.\par \par \uc2 Should we be worried, Doctor? I mean, this sort of jars some people when they hear it.\par \par \uc2 rMD-UL_DR. MIKE VARSHAVSKI, FAMILY MEDICINE PHYSICIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, hearing that we're reopening and we have to make changes to our lives can always bring about anxiety. That's part of the human condition, Neil. And what I tell my patients as a family medicine provider is, take it one step at a time. Sort of desensitize yourself to this reopening process.\par \par \uc2 Don't necessarily think you have to go back and return to all your normal activities at once.\par \par \uc2 We actually use this type of desensitization therapy in treating phobias. And if we can implement those steps in our everyday lives, it can make the process a little bit easier. We have to look to the CDC for guidance. We have to expect that the science is going to guide us correctly here.\par \par \uc2 And when I say science, I'm not necessarily talking about scientists, because we scientists follow the evidence. Evidence changes. As evidence grows, as we learn more about the pandemic, as we learn more about the things that are happening with the reopening process, we will continue to update and make these changes.\par \par \uc2 So, expect that. Do it slowly. Do it gradually. And expect that there might be some anxiety. Validate those feelings and continue on with the process.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What are you doing at the White House?\par \par \uc2 VARSHAVSKI: Today, I was participating in a COVID-19 virtual town hall.\par \par \uc2 I got to witness Dr. Fauci, \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2 , the entire crew talking about all the amazing work that they have been doing surrounding the vaccination process, with a very important focus on primary care physicians, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I, myself, am a primary care provider. And I have seen that -- the trust that my patients put in me and the importance of having the conversation with our patients, answering their questions, because there's so much misinformation out there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I just partnered up with Senate majority -- Chuck Schumer the other weekend to talk about the misinformation that's out there and the work that we're doing on my \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel to sort of debunk that, present the facts, and allow people and patients alike to make the best choices for themselves and their families.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you -- you said -- as far Dr. Fauci, he's getting a lot of heat on a lot of subjects. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 VARSHAVSKI: I think that, as a leader in the space that he finds himself in during a very politicized time, I think it's no surprise that is happening.\par \par \uc2 However, he's followed the science. He's made changes where changes needed to be made. And I personally, for -- as far as my opinion goes, I trust him to continue making the right calls.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, these people who are urging him to resign, like \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , you think that's a bit much?\par \par \uc2 VARSHAVSKI: I can't speak to that. That's one person's opinion. I can speak to my own professional medical opinion.\par \par \uc2 And I feel like he's followed the science correctly, made changes when he needed to. And while that may look difficult for some people, because we say one thing, we change it, but the reason why we're changing it is because we're learning new facts.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VARSHAVSKI: And as new facts come out, we have to change our opinions.\par \par \uc2 In fact, if we didn't change our opinions, we wouldn't be following the science and we're not good scientists.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Dr. Mike, great seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Mike Varshavski, board-certified family physician at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Well, la da dee da, right, at the White House.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have Governor Scott Walker with us. He's leading a push right now to get vaccinated, and that, disproportionately, a lot of Republicans, for example, don't. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. SCOTT WALKER (R-WI): I'm just ready to be done with this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 FMR. \uc2 GOV. JIM DOYLE\uc2  (D-WI): I couldn't agree with you more.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: That's what I thought.\par \par \uc2 Here's another thought. Let's do a commercial together reminding people in Wisconsin how important it is to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DOYLE: That may be the best idea you have ever had.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I agree.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I like that. You see a former Democratic governor, a former Republican governor on the same page when it comes to getting vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 Scott Walker joins us right now to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 I like the pitch there, a bipartisan pitch, get the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 But it's interesting, Governor, because there's a stubborn quarter to a third of Americans who are not and might never. And they're disproportionately conservative. I wonder why that is. And does it worry you?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, I mean, the bottom line is, the best way to do this is through persuasion, not through a government mandate, not through vaccination passports, but persuasion.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: You see it in my state and across the country, Republican, particularly rural counties, but you also see it -- and why \uc2 Jim Doyle\uc2 , a Democrat governor, is on there as well -- you see it in heavily urbanized areas, particularly amongst African-American residents.\par \par \uc2 In both cases, there's a certain uncertainty about getting the vaccination. And our response is simple. We're not mandating it, but we're just telling you, hey, it's a good idea. We think it makes sense. I got it months ago. My family got it months ago. We have all been fine in that regard. And we think you're going to be too.\par \par \uc2 And we hope people across this country will get the vaccination. It's good for your health and it's good for the health of the economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A lot of those who have not been vaccinated, Governor, come back and say, well, I think we have kind of hit herd immunity anyway, and it's not a worry for me, and there's been no corresponding spike in cases, even though we have stayed at this level of the population that hasn't been vaccinated, so I don't have to.\par \par \uc2 What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, again, it's not just the flu, a common flu. I get a flu shot every year because I travel a lot. I interact with a lot of people. I'd like to not get the flu.\par \par \uc2 The flu is obviously not nearly as serious as what we have seen from COVID- 19 over the past year. And in my sense is, what's the harm? It's an easy shot. It's easy to get. There's really very minimal side effects. The most, most people got is feeling tired for a day or two. I didn't get anything. I was fortunate, other than a little sore arm for a day.\par \par \uc2 And I think what most people had. So, my sense is, again, I don't want the government mandating it. I think persuasion is a much better way to do it. And that's why I was glad when U.W. Health system asked me and \uc2 Jim Doyle\uc2  to do it. And we said, absolutely. It just makes a ton of sense.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, what if the friendly persuasion doesn't work, Governor, and there's still lots of people that say, no, just not going to do it? Should we force the issue?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No.\par \par \uc2 I think the difference is, unlike other contagious diseases, the bottom line is, the more people that get vaccinated, like I have, like my wife did months ago, my mother, both my adult sons, the rest of my family, the more you get -- the more people get vaccinated, the less worry you have. You're completely safe.\par \par \uc2 In the worst-case scenarios, we see that some people who've gotten the shot who've gotten COVID don't have the horrible circumstances that we have seen for others who have. And so it's just ultimately one of those where I look and say, hey, get out there and do it. If you don't, you're putting yourself at greater risk.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, it's your choice. But it's one more where, why take the risk, when we have vaccines that work and they're effective and can ease your life up? It just, to me, makes a ton of sense. It's like, why I wear the -- take the flu shot.\par \par \uc2 And it's not something, though, that the government needs the force on people. I think, the more of us who get out and make the case, the better off we will be.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If we did, real quickly, Governor, have a spike in cases, do you think the days are gone now where we reimpose restrictions? They seem to be done. But, if it gets to be a spike, should we?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No.\par \par \uc2 I think the lesson learned from the last go-round is, if you put your faith in the people and tell them why things are important and allow people to take reasonable actions, vs. forcing them with what 