 Speaks.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: There you go. That's how you do it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I've been taking lessons from Jesse. So, there you go.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well done, Shannon. All right, Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: I've actually got a book coming out called Broads of the Bible. I can't wait.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I love it.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Actually, it is National Donut Day. And I want to -- I want to show these gorgeous \uc2 Krispy Kreme\uc2  donuts. \uc2 Krispy Kreme\uc2  -- Dunkin has given out free donuts. \uc2 Krispy Kreme\uc2  will give you two if you're vaccinated. So, get a jump on your diabetes.\par \par \uc2 Also, listen to my podcast, FoxNewsPodcast.com, Kennedy Saves the World. You'll love it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You stole that from me.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: You stole that from me so hard. Facial discretion, Jesse. He loves the title so much he couldn't keep his little mitts off it. I'll be your lawyer.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: These mitts aren't that little. I never had a problem in that department.\par \par \uc2 MONTGOMERY: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Richard.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Skittles is ditching the iconic rainbow to celebrate the LGBTQIA community for Pride Month. Skittles is going colorless on both their packaging and their products for the whole month of June. Sadly, we scoured all throughout Times Square and we couldn't find any gray Skittles packages. So, all of my fellow co-hosts have regular Skittle packages. But we want to give Skittles a shout-out. All the proceeds from these little packages go to GLAD, an organization that supports the LGBTQIA community.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Taste to the rainbow, Richard. That's it for us. We'll see you back here on Monday.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020210605eh6400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Western Drought; Biden Set to Meet With Putin; Bitcoin Convention; Disneyland Avengers Campus Opens; Interview With White House Economic Adviser Jared Bernstein; Interview With Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC); 559,000 U.S. Jobs Added in May\b0\par\par\uc2 Susan Li, Christina Coleman, William La Jeunesse, Kat Timpf,  Ashley Dvorkin, Peter Doocy, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,154 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Oh, man, oh, man, Martha, congratulations on that. That's great recognition for a great book on a day we got some great news as well.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at what's happening on the new jobs front. We had 559,000 more of them generated in the month of May, on top of the nearly 280,000 revised job increases in the month of April. So the economy is percolating along.\par \par \uc2 The question is, can this keep going? Well, they say, yes, it can.\par \par \uc2 We will talk to the White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein about all of this, also get the read from the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2 , because, well, a lot of those folks are getting increasingly concerned as to whether this can be sustained. We will talk to Republican Nancy Mace of South Carolina, who wonders that, the more spending we do, will it do more harm than good? She wants to wrap it up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 And what a nice world it is if you're looking for work. There are millions of openings, and not enough people to fill them. It's a nice problem to have, depending on the employer you want to talk to.\par \par \uc2 First to Peter Doocy on the impact of the jobs report at Pennsylvania Avenue -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And part of the president's philosophy is that one way to improve the economy in a time of crisis like this is for the government to spend more money. And he claimed today that it's working.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's testament to the new strategy that is growing this economy, not only growing it, but growing it from the bottom up and the middle out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The unemployment rate went down to 5.8 percent. But there are some experts who say that's just because enough people stopped looking for work and dropped out of the labor force.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have charged that is because the COVID era extra $300 a week in unemployment benefits is just too good to pass up. And now a former Obama economist agrees with part of that. \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  writes about vaccinations increasing in mask mandates dropping in many places, that this: "These factors interact with unemployment insurance, which can allow workers to take longer to rethink their careers, try to find jobs in new industries and negotiate for higher wages, but can also slow employment growth and prolong long-term unemployment."\par \par \uc2 So will President Biden try to quickly pull the plug on the extra benefits? The answer is no.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN DEESE, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: The U.I. program itself provides a critical support network to the American people and the American economy. And those temporary -- the temporary boost is slated to now expire in 90 days. And, as the president said, that's appropriate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president also had a big phone call this afternoon with the lead Republican negotiator for infrastructure, \uc2 Shelley Moore Capito\uc2 . We are told there was no deal. They will speak again on Monday -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So those talks keep going on. Now, there is that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Peter Doocy. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to \uc2 Neil Bradley\uc2  right now, the U.S. Chamber of Commerce executive vice president.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, as I understand it, for the Chamber, this is a mixed blessing, right? The economy is booming. The jobs numbers are booming, but they could be booming even more. The problem is getting enough workers for employers who are having a devil of a time doing that. What's happening?\par \par \uc2 NEIL BRADLEY\uc2 , EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, U.S. CHAMBER OF COMMERCE: Well, you're exactly right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So we have eight million open jobs. And while we were all pleased to see 500,000 jobs gained in the last month, it should have been a lot more. We need to be filling these open jobs much more quickly than what we are right now.\par \par \uc2 We're in the height of the summer season. For a lot of small businesses across this country this is when they make their year. And if they can't open fully, if they can't service the customers that are ready to visit their stores, they're not going to make it.\par \par \uc2 And so this is a work force shortage crisis. And we need to start treating it like one.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What was interesting, Neil, in your Chamber report, is that a lot of your member employers across the country are saying right now that that effort is getting to be so tough right now that it is slowing down the economy in their neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 Could you explain that?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Yes, that's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 So you see it at the restaurant who announces that they can no longer do lunch service because they don't have enough staff to do lunch service. You can go to the construction industry. We surveyed the commercial construction industry, and about one-third of commercial contractors told us they're turning down jobs right now because they don't have enough workers to do the work that they already have.\par \par \uc2 And so that's true in manufacturing. It's true in retail. This is a widespread problem. All these people who are unemployed, we're not getting enough of them back fast enough. We have to start understanding why, why 13 percent of the unemployed have turned down a job offer in the last year, why 30 percent of the currently unemployed say they don't expect to return to work this year or next year.\par \par \uc2 That's a problem.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, even in areas, though, where they taken those extended unemployment benefits away, not as many as you would have thought have returned or ventured into the work force. So this might be something else. What do you think it could be?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Well, we have had 25 states announce an end to the benefits, but, under the federal law, when you announce it, you then have to wait 30 days to actually cancel those benefits.\par \par \uc2 So we actually haven't had any states pull those back yet. But, as I mentioned, we just went out and surveyed the unemployed to ask them why they're not returning to work; 16 percent said because they're getting enough benefits from unemployment and from other stimulus checks that they don't feel the need to return to work. About 24 percent cited access to childcare.\par \par \uc2 That's why we're encouraging governors to increase accessibility to childcare. So there are a lot of reasons going on here. But if we don't start solving some of these problems, employers aren't going to be able to fill those jobs and our recovery is going to stall in its tracks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Switching gears slightly, though, Neil, the Chamber must be encouraged by talk out of the Biden White House that it could scrap the hike in corporate taxes, maybe settle on a 15 percent minimum global tax to entice more support for its infrastructure package.\par \par \uc2 How are you, how's the Chamber with that?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Well, we're glad to see these negotiations are ongoing. Very pleased to hear Peter Doocy report that talks are going to continue on Monday. They should continue.\par \par \uc2 Listen, both sides are going to have to give a little here. We know that an infrastructure package is going to be more expansive than many Republicans want, and not nearly as expansive as many Democrats want. That's probably the sweet spot.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to pay-fors, we got to be willing to put pay-fors on the table, including the business community contributing through increased user fees to help finance the infrastructure that we need.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Neil Bradley\uc2  of the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2 , great catching up with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I want to go right now to Jared Bernstein, Jared, of course, senior White House economic adviser.\par \par \uc2 Very happy to have him here with us.\par \par \uc2 Jared, thank you for coming.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, \uc2 WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS\uc2 : Of course. My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What is your take on the jobs number and this debate that it could be stronger without these extended unemployment benefits?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I think this job report shows that we're just right in the midst of an historic labor market recovery that's delivering real, tangible benefits to the American families and to the American economy.\par \par \uc2 In the first four months since this president took the oath of -- oath of office, there's been over two million jobs created. That's an underlying pace of over half-a-million, 540,000 jobs per month. That is unprecedented at the beginning of any presidential administration. We saw some wage gains in today's report. We saw an over-400,000 decline in long-term unemployment.\par \par \uc2 And I heard the tail end of your conversation with Neil. Look, those two million jobs, they're net new jobs. They're not robots. They're people who've come into the job market and gotten work.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, there are definitely some supply constraints and bottlenecks that are working themselves out throughout the economy. I think the labor market is sending a very clear price/wage signal for workers to get back into the game. And they're responding. So, I think we're clearly in the midst of a strong labor market recovery, one that's very beneficial to the American middle class.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, maybe then, by that yard, we don't need to do as much to spur the economy.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that, that some are saying, for example, in the president $6 trillion budget, there's a lot going out, and we don't need as much going out because the economy is firing up?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, I think you have to be careful kind of lurching from position to position on this.\par \par \uc2 I mean, last month, if you looked at the jobs number, you would have said, oh, we need to do a lot more -- this month, folks, well, maybe we need to do a lot less.\par \par \uc2 Look, the reason we're in the midst of this historic job market recovery is very much because of the measures in the American Rescue Plan, as well as the resiliency and the stick-to-itiveness of the American work force, shots in arms, checks in pockets.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the most important economic variables right now is the rate of vaccination, the vaccine rate, which, of course, is 75 percent for seniors, over 50 percent for adults. If you look at working age adults, that rate has been climbing, climbing sharply.\par \par \uc2 That's very important, and it's one of the reasons, it's one of the barriers between people and the job market. Now, the virus is not vanquished from the land. The efforts of the team here have been tremendously important, kicking up that vax rate. That's one of the -- that's one issue where I think the Rescue Plan's fingerprints are very much on these strong labor market results.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Jared, you mentioned the COVID and the vaccination progress, and that it's proven to be a key negotiating feature in this infrastructure discussion.\par \par \uc2 The president apparently is open to using 70 or so billion dollars of that to pay for this infrastructure package. Republicans want a lot more, I think something closer to $700 billion. But do you see a significant increase in tapping COVID funds to close a potential infrastructure deal?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I think the infrastructure deal needs to be viewed separately from the Rescue Plan, in the sense that the resources in there are, as I have been stressing, very much in play in generating an historic economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 We have been talking about jobs. I could just as easily talk about GDP growth, which is forecast in this quarter to be growing at either high single-digit or double-digit rates. So, those resources are in the economy. We saw state and local governments hire 100,000 teachers -- or educators, I should say, last month. They're still down over a million. So there's much more work to do there. And the state and local funds need to go to help there.\par \par \uc2 The president has elaborated a very detailed set of revenue measures to help pay for the longer-term infrastructure plan, a plan that pays -- that spends out over eight to 10 years. And those negotiations are ongoing, as you well know.\par \par \uc2 But I think they're separate creatures there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Jared Bernstein, very good catching up with you. Thank you very, very much. Enjoy your weekend.\par \par \uc2 Jared Bernstein, the senior economic adviser at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Well, Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace is not convinced, as Jared is, that the recovery has nothing stopping it. She says all this spending could -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, there is a philosophical and a very expensive battle between Democrats and Republicans on how to keep this recovery going.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are of the view of, the more that government spends, the more we will stimulate things and the more recovery will show some traction. Republicans are just of the opposite view, because they think that this is going to do more harm than good, to say nothing of the inflationary pressure it puts on the economy.\par \par \uc2 So, pick your side, well, side.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Mace, the Republican out of South Carolina, already has. She thinks this is a dangerous trend, and that it will hurt the recovery.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, very good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): And thank you, Neil, for having me on today as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's been a rough week for you. And I do want to go into what was happening at your home earlier in the week, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I do want to first off go on to what's happening on the recovery front.\par \par \uc2 You just heard from the senior White House economic adviser, who is convinced that all of this spending is doing some good; now's not the time to curtail it.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, President Biden just had another missed jobs report with the May jobs report, the numbers released today. And he missed that hit, that goal by 111,000 jobs. And last month was no better.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we're seeing the Democratic policy of more spending, greater debt, more expansion of money in the -- by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  printing money. All of this is having a negative back. On top of it, the Biden administration is literally paying people to stay home.\par \par \uc2 And right -- Neil, right before I came on your program, I got a text from a friend of mine in the district who went to a retailer a few hours ago, and that retailer shut its doors a few weeks ago because they couldn't get people back to work. The federal government is paying people to stay home and it's hurting businesses.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, no doubt that that does have an impact, to your point.\par \par \uc2 As far as employment reports that miss their predicted numbers, I mean, not an exact science. I have covered these for many, many years. So, the administration turns around and says, all right, say what you will about the jumps in different months and whether they missed or met estimates, we're averaging better than 400,000 new jobs a month. That's a pretty good trend.\par \par \uc2 You say?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It is a good trend.\par \par \uc2 But we can do better. A state like South Carolina right now, our unemployment rate is hovering just over 4 percent. And I'm grateful to Governor McMaster, who's going to pass on the extra, in additional federal unemployment this month. More people will be able to go back to work.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina, we have 85,000 jobs available and over 100,000 people sitting on unemployment right now. We have got so much opportunity here. And if we can incentivize people to go to work and not stay home, then we can have even greater numbers in the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Congresswoman, it's the first time I have had to talk to you. It's only been a week, as you appeared on my weekend show, and, then just a few days later, you get word your home has been vandalized.\par \par \uc2 And that's a home for you and your daughters. And I'm just wondering, do we know who was behind it? I knew it got pretty scary. But can you update us?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It's -- it's jarring, really, and it doesn't feel good, when I feel like I have got to look over my shoulder.\par \par \uc2 I'm a single mom. I'm raising my two kids here. This is their childhood home. And when I picked them up from school on Tuesday, one of the first things we talked about it -- one of the first questions was, "Mommy, are we safe?"\par \par \uc2 And it's distressing to be in the political environment that we're in today that somebody will show up on your home and vandalize it because they disagree with you.\par \par \uc2 We can have disagreements, but this isn't the way we should go about talking about them. And I have been -- I have been a very strong voice. I go -- I talk against my own party and the Democratic Party for things that I really believe in. I'm passionate.\par \par \uc2 Today -- we had a strong lead two days ago. And, last night, I found out that that lead went dry. I'm feeling very discouraged today. It's past time people that do this kind of political vandalism or harm other people or threaten people, that they're held accountable.\par \par \uc2 I just want the person that did it to be held accountable. And whoever -- whoever did this, if they thought they were going to intimidate me, well, they thought wrong. I'm going to work harder than ever. I'm going to do the job that I was elected to do, and nothing's going to stop that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you and your daughters were home when this occurred, but you discovered all the vandalism that next morning, correct?\par \par \uc2 MACE: No, I was -- no, we were not home.\par \par \uc2 In fact, one of my kids had a sleepover on Sunday night. I had date night, so I wasn't even home when it happened.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MACE: I found out when I was on my way back in the morning.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 MACE: And the police were notified. Someone -- a jogger had come by the house and reported it early in the morning. And I was already on the way home that morning when I found out...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 MACE: ... and walked up on my house. The police were here.\par \par \uc2 It's very unsettling. And if I had footage I could release of it, I would. I'm desperate to find answers and clues, because I want this person to be charged and held accountable to the fullest extent of the law.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, we do know there were reports of three Antifa symbols that were spray-painted on your property.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Did they know anything more about that, about the source of this or a group like that?\par \par \uc2 MACE: They don't.\par \par \uc2 I know that, within Charleston, there are a handful of Antifa and anarchist type of groups that have done graffiti. There was other graffiti after my house was targeted on Monday morning, the early hours of Monday morning, that the individual, it looks like, spray-painted two other locations.\par \par \uc2 And then, about two months ago, there were some spray-painting on the island where I live at a high school. So, the police are investigating whether these things are related or how they're related. I'm desperate for clues and information on this one.\par \par \uc2 It's very -- it's troubling and it's jarring to walk up and be home. I don't feel safe in my own home. And it doesn't matter what your political beliefs are, but everyone should feel safe at home. I don't -- I don't live behind a wall. I don't have a gate.\par \par \uc2 I live in my community with everybody else. I live in the home where my children -- and this is their childhood home. And they're -- I'm raising them here. I have nowhere to go. And I'm not going to stand for it. And I want to stand strong. And I'm going to keep doing the work I was elected to do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Congresswoman, keep us posted on that and whether they ever get to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 But we can disagree on political matters in this country, but, man, oh, man, that is uncalled for.\par \par \uc2 So, be well, be strong. My best to your girls.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You shouldn't have to deal with that. We are better than that, as a people, I suspect.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Mace of the House Oversight and Reform Committee.\par \par \uc2 Much, much more on the fallout of that, but we're also looking at some relatively good news returning with the economy, including Disneyland and a new Marvel theme park. It's pricey, but it proves to be yet again that superheroes are cool.\par \par \uc2 And let's just say, to this financial superhero, I have got some company.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Here's the best way to know that cryptocurrencies are now real and they got some street cred, a big old conference that's packed by the thousands.\par \par \uc2 The latest from what they're telling them in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Disneyland not only open for business, but a whole new Marvel world right now, Disneyland Avengers, more to the point. That opened today as well.\par \par \uc2 But for those who want to be there, I'm going to give you an early indication. Much of it is sold out, ticket prices not exactly cheap, being there not exactly for the financially faint of heart when it comes to this sort of thing. But, man, it is drawing folks.\par \par \uc2 Christina Coleman in Los Angeles with more on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there's a lot of pent-up demand to get out and enjoy a theme park, especially after all the COVID closures here in California. This demand will likely help \uc2 Disney\uc2  cash in on their new Avengers Campus. Tickets for Disneyland and \uc2 Disney\uc2 's California Adventure are sold out for the rest of the month.\par \par \uc2 I just checked to see what it would cost just me to visit one of the parks. Apparently, the next time I could get in would be weeks from now, July 1, and it would cost me $139 for a single ticket. So, expect a boost in overall sales for \uc2 Disney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There's also an upgrade option for the Spider-Man ride that will cost you an extra 30 bucks. And don't forget about all their superhero retail, those high-end Marvel collectibles sold at the park. They're apparently selling a superhero statuette for $685.\par \par \uc2 Now, the new Avengers Campus is built on more than 70 years of Marvel history and 23 movies. Also, fans will get the chance to become a superhero themselves and watch their favorite character in action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have experiences where you are going to get to see Spider-Man climb up a wall, crawl across the rooftops. He's going to soar high above the campus.\par \par \uc2 And then over here at the Avengers headquarters, you're going to see Black Widow and Black Panther square off against bad guys all day long.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: The \uc2 Disney\uc2  theme parks here in California are still at limited capacity and limited to in-state residents until June 15. And that's when the state drops its mask mandate and other COVID restrictions.\par \par \uc2 So, after that, Neil, you got to come out here to \uc2 Disney\uc2  with me. We can watch Spider-Men and share one of their $100 sandwiches.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Man.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: They say they're supposed to be six to eight people.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, you know, it's funny, because you were mentioning the prices involved, Christina, but people apparently are willing to spend the money, right?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 But also, with that $100 sandwich, if it was my husband and my son, they would probably eat half of it. So, six to eight people, it's debatable.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I don't know. I could do that myself.\par \par \uc2 But you're thin and fit. I'm just telling you, I could finish that off myself.\par \par \uc2 All right, Christina Coleman, thank you. Great story.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Kat Timpf on all of this. We have got Ashley Dvorkin with us as well.\par \par \uc2 If you think about it, guys, it doesn't surprise me that people are going to fork over bucks to go to this Marvel-themed event, because it is an event. But would either of you like?\par \par \uc2 Kat, would you? And we're looking at how this adds up, $130 tickets just to get in there, surcharges on certain rides just to enjoy them. I mean, you could be back just one person, whether you go with your husband or not, you're already out close to 1,000 bucks.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY DVORKIN, FOX NEWS ENTERTAINMENT PRODUCER: Yes, it...\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE TIMPF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, probably not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 Probably not, because, honestly, I am not the type of person that this is targeted to. I'm more of the type of person who would pay to not have to go to Disneyland. It's just not really my thing. I'm not a little kid. I know a lot of adults love it. I think that's kind of weird. Same with Marvel movies.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: But I will admit this. I will admit this.\par \par \uc2 The virtual reality aspect of it, where you can move your arm and it looks like you threw something, that is cool, because I will also admit that, a few years ago, I did do a "Ghostbusters" virtual reality thing in Times Square. And I have never seen "Ghostbusters," and I had a wonderful time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Maybe you were drinking, I don't know where, although it was real, going on.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That would be a whole 'nother -- no? All right. All right. I will play along.\par \par \uc2 So, Ashley, apparently to Kat's point, this does seem to be overwhelmingly male-oriented. In other words, to get particularly boys love it more than girls. I don't want to be sexist here. But those are the numbers. And that's the appeal. But say what you will. For families, it's a draw.\par \par \uc2 What did you make of it?\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: Well, I do think there's appeal for the whole entire family and all the Marvel fans in the family.\par \par \uc2 I went. And I think the excitement is twofold because you have \uc2 Disney\uc2 - goers, \uc2 Disney\uc2  park-goers, who are so excited to be back at the park. And then you also have this whole Marvel Cinematic Universe, where they made it so immersive. And you have various experiences around this land. It's only six acres. So then you can go out into the rest of \uc2 Disney\uc2  and have fun.\par \par \uc2 So I think they do incorporate a lot. And \uc2 Disney\uc2  does a lot in the details. So, you can see a lot from your -- all of your favorite movies.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I might point out, ladies -- and you probably don't know this other superhero side of me -- that this interest in superheroes actually started with me more than a decade ago with the financial meltdown...\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Oh.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... when I was FOX's financial superhero.\par \par \uc2 Marvel, of course, was glommed on to that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I'm in negotiations with them as to whether I should be a character there.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is -- and some people are writing this down: You're not a Marvel character!\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Anyway, I'm wondering, Kat, where this goes, though, because, the other day, we were chatting about how much you're willing to pay for higher prices that have now stuck at grocery stores and the rest.\par \par \uc2 If they if they continue like this, is there a limit, and do you start making a choice at what you won't pay more for?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I absolutely think there is.\par \par \uc2 I think, right now, we are in this unique situation, because we were all just locked inside for so long. I am willing to do things that I would have not been wanting to do before just to leave. I don't want to go to the club. But maybe I'd stand outside in the line and just talk to people, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: But how long is that going to last? How long is that going to last?\par \par \uc2 Because I'm anticipating that maybe, in a few months, I'm going to be on my couch again, because that's kind of where -- I spent a decent amount of time there before lockdown at my apartment. I wasn't always going out. I want to go out all the time now because I couldn't. And I would be willing to spend more.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That makes sense.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: But I don't think that that lasts forever.\par \par \uc2 DVORKIN: Yes, I have...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, the danger are the real splurges, right, Ashley?\par \par \uc2 Think about this. United is kicking around the idea that, by the end of this decade, Americans are going to be open to spending thousands of dollars for supersonic air travel, much as they did back in the days of the Concorde. And they're working on buying some jets that will be able to do just that, take you, for example, from New York to London in a little more than three hours, from New York to Tokyo in not much more than six hours.\par \par \uc2 Would you be game for that? Or would there be a price at which you said, no, no, no, I will take my time and pay less?\par \par \