ting. And I don't see any sense at this point.\par \par \uc2 If President Biden wants to talk to him, he can pick up the phone and make that conversation in private.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would you say the same if this were \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and there was an upcoming summit and all of this was happening right before it?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICHS: A hundred percent?\par \par \uc2 What -- a lot of times, what presidents do, they make the mistake of thinking that diplomacy is incompatible with strength. But, really, you want to have economic sanctions, the threat of military pressure, the threat of political cost all going on at the same time. Then you can go have a conversation and give diplomacy a shot.\par \par \uc2 But all we have seen right now from President Biden is that he waived these congressionally mandated sanctions supported by Congress against Nord Stream II, that energy pipeline that Russia wants going into Germany. That would be terrible for the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance.\par \par \uc2 So, not only do we not have a position of strength coming from the United States, but we actually have a blinking. President Biden blinked.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm wondering what Russia is up to, if they are behind, either directly or indirectly, all of these hack attacks. And they have increased in frequency, and something, by the way, you feared when last we chatted, so very good call on your part, well, scary call, but very good one.\par \par \uc2 I'm wondering if we're going to see still more.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICHS: I think we will.\par \par \uc2 And you saw a Biden Department of Justice official warning American CEOs to batten down the hatches and to really tighten up their cybersecurity.\par \par \uc2 I just wish -- that's understandable. American companies should be doing that. But, again, this is a national security concern. And so threats need to be made credibly. I don't need the kind of heated rhetoric that we saw from the Trump administration against certain adversaries. We can do without that. You can speak softly and carry a big stick.\par \par \uc2 But we need some more big stick being waved towards the Russian government. And, Neil, I am worried. President Biden, as a senator, was very focused on arms control, reducing American arms. And I do -- I am afraid that he might be looking for that at this meeting with the Russians, which would be a mistake, when we have the Russian threat, the Chinese threat, North Korean threat.\par \par \uc2 Now is not the time for the United States should be reducing arms.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, but the meeting is still on. To your point, we will see if any of that changes, I guess depends if we have any more incidents in the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 That's entirely likely, given the rapid increase in them.\par \par \uc2 Rebeccah Heinrichs\uc2 , thank you. Good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICHS: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right we just offered on our online site to see if any of you wanted to write me or the show, let us know how you feel. But, man, you're responding in droves.\par \par \uc2 So, some of your more-to-the point points, and something we're going to be expanding now on a regular Friday segment here that will include whatever video offerings you have or messages that you want to send. I should screen out the nasty personal attacks -- and attacks that I have.\par \par \uc2 But, then again, we would have very few e-mails in that event. That may be so. You got to deal with that, but some doozies coming up pretty soon.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it's one thing to do a few days without rain. Quite a different story in much of the West right now, where they're dealing with no rain for quite a while and what has already developed into what they're calling an extreme drought.\par \par \uc2 William La Jeunesse has more from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 William, what's going 