elieve we all have strong opinions. I have strong opinions. On this show, I try to keep them to myself. But we do invariably see things through the prism of whatever those biases are. I just try to keep it in check.\par \par \uc2 Not all of you are convinced I'm doing a good job at that.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, for example isn't having any of it, e-mailing: "Admit it. You hate Republicans. They're always wrong. Biden is always right."\par \par \uc2 Jay sees a very different pattern: "Will you ever let up on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for having the temerity to attack selfish fat -- and I do mean fat" -- always with the fat -- "cats like yourself? Cut him some slack and show some balance, Guido."\par \par \uc2 Guido. Really? All right.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Tim tweets: "Neil Cavuto is compromised milk toast."\par \par \uc2 I can understand being called milk toast, but a compromised milk toast, I won't have it, America.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have got Heather in Baton Rouge: "As a born-again Christian, let me say this in a way that doesn't offend my God. You are a clueless half-wit."\par \par \uc2 But, by the way, further in the e-mail, some nasty words. I didn't want to repeat them. Born-again Christian, you don't talk like that.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, USBornNRaised writes: "I do not watch Neil Cavuto. Is he still having a bromance?" Presumably, we're talking about Dr. Fauci.\par \par \uc2 Keeping the same theme here, Victor e-mails: "I do not watch him. I do not tolerate him, not one bit."\par \par \uc2 Now, I was thinking while I was reading these e-mails, where -- where have I heard that kind of language in writing before?\par \par \uc2 I do not watch Cavuto. I do not like him one bit. I do not like him here or there. I do not like him anywhere. I do not like him in my house. I do not like him here or there. I do not like Cavuto anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, that's just something I added because I thought it was kind of funny. But, well, you don't always agree. That's fine.\par \par \uc2 Jim (sic) tweets: "Used to like Cavuto until he revealed himself as a never-Trumper."\par \par \uc2 Talk to Maria, then, Jeff: "Yet another FOX Trump lapdog who never challenges a thing -- a single thing that Trump says or does. Sad."\par \par \uc2 LetssaveAmerica tweets: "Cavuto is a feckless gnome. His propaganda makes me sick."\par \par \uc2 Smackdaddy writes: "Cavuto is a traitor."\par \par \uc2 I always love those ones who get right to the point. He is a traitor.\par \par \uc2 But I love it when to get to the point like Joe does: "I love Cavuto."\par \par \uc2 I love you too. Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Then there's MainlandJoy, who says: "I like Cavuto to be the most detestable and smug anchor on TV."\par \par \uc2 Well, that's -- think about that maybe. That's quite a crowd to beat out there.\par \par \uc2 Then we got Rack Daddy, who tweets: "Have you ever noticed how Cavuto always says 'Thank you very, very much' to his guests? Annoying as hell."\par \par \uc2 Well, Rack, thank you very much for sharing your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Then we have Burnitin97, who says: "Cavuto is two-day-old fish guts on a hot sidewalk."\par \par \uc2 I guess things could be worse maybe. It could be three-day-old fish guts. So, that is good to know.\par \par \uc2 Charles tweets: "Neil, you look great. Looks like your loss some pounds and your voice is much clearer. Great show."\par \par \uc2 Just when I was feeling happy about that one, I get this one from Duane: "Neil Cavuto kind of looks like the Campbell's Soup kid."\par \par \uc2 Well, you nabbed it dead on, Duane. And that was kind of like a goal of mine growing up.\par \par \uc2 Marlene tweets: "Neil Cavuto most definitely is the next James Bond."\par \par \uc2 There has been a good deal to talk back and forth. Some people thought we doctored a book cover to make it look like that. That was actually my second book. And they didn't have to make a stretch to make me look like Bond.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, they did. They did. They had to make a big stretch.\par \par \uc2 Tony says: "My wife and I think you're the best dressed on the entire network. I'm retired now for seven years, but always wore great-looking ties. You must have quite the collection."\par \par \uc2 Alexandra disagrees: "Clip-on hair, clip-on ties, clip-on brain, when Cavuto remembers to clip it on, of course."\par \par \uc2 Of course, Alexandra. I'm going to put you down as a maybe on being a fan of mine. But it is what it is.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank you all for writing. Thank you very, very, very, very, very, very, very much for writing.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That ticks some people. Just be nice.\par \par \uc2 All right, we will see you tomorrow. We have got a big show lined up for you as we go into the weekend on: Where's infrastructure? Where's this recovery? Where are we going? What's happening at the border?\par \par \uc2 We put it all together in two hours that passes like two minutes, trust me. We have got your back.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you very, very much for having mine.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210604eh640002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,475 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You know, there is a philosophical and a very expensive battle between Democrats and Republicans on how to keep this recovery going.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are of the view of, the more that government spends, the more we will stimulate things and the more recovery will show some traction. Republicans are just of the opposite view, because they think that this is going to do more harm than good, to say nothing of the inflationary pressure it puts on the economy.\par \par \uc2 So, pick your side, well, side.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Mace, the Republican out of South Carolina, already has. She thinks this is a dangerous trend, and that it will hurt the recovery.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, very good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): And thank you, Neil, for having me on today as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It`s been a rough week for you. And I do want to go into what was happening at your home earlier in the week, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I do want to first off go on to what`s happening on the recovery front.\par \par \uc2 You just heard from the senior White House economic adviser, who is convinced that all of this spending is doing some good; now`s not the time to curtail it.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, President Biden just had another missed jobs report with the May jobs report, the numbers released today. And he missed that hit, that goal by 111,000 jobs. And last month was no better.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`re seeing the Democratic policy of more spending, greater debt, more expansion of money in the -- by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  printing money. All of this is having a negative back. On top of it, the Biden administration is literally paying people to stay home.\par \par \uc2 And right -- Neil, right before I came on your program, I got a text from a friend of mine in the district who went to a retailer a few hours ago, and that retailer shut its doors a few weeks ago because they couldn`t get people back to work. The federal government is paying people to stay home and it`s hurting businesses.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, no doubt that that does have an impact, to your point.\par \par \uc2 As far as employment reports that miss their predicted numbers, I mean, not an exact science. I have covered these for many, many years. So, the administration turns around and says, all right, say what you will about the jumps in different months and whether they missed or met estimates, we`re averaging better than 400,000 new jobs a month. That`s a pretty good trend.\par \par \uc2 You say?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It is a good trend.\par \par \uc2 But we can do better. A state like South Carolina right now, our unemployment rate is hovering just over 4 percent. And I`m grateful to Governor McMaster, who`s going to pass on the extra, in additional federal unemployment this month. More people will be able to go back to work.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina, we have 85,000 jobs available and over 100,000 people sitting on unemployment right now. We have got so much opportunity here. And if we can incentivize people to go to work and not stay home, then we can have even greater numbers in the future.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Congresswoman, it`s the first time I have had to talk to you. It`s only been a week, as you appeared on my weekend show, and, then just a few days later, you get word your home has been vandalized.\par \par \uc2 And that`s a home for you and your daughters. And I`m just wondering, do we know who was behind it? I knew it got pretty scary. But can you update us?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It`s -- it`s jarring, really, and it doesn`t feel good, when I feel like I have got to look over my shoulder.\par \par \uc2 I`m a single mom. I`m raising my two kids here. This is their childhood home. And when I picked them up from school on Tuesday, one of the first things we talked about it -- one of the first questions was, "Mommy, are we safe?"\par \par \uc2 And it`s distressing to be in the political environment that we`re in today that somebody will show up on your home and vandalize it because they disagree with you.\par \par \uc2 We can have disagreements, but this isn`t the way we should go about talking about them. And I have been -- I have been a very strong voice. I go -- I talk against my own party and the Democratic Party for things that I really believe in. I`m passionate.\par \par \uc2 Today -- we had a strong lead two days ago. And, last night, I found out that that lead went dry. I`m feeling very discouraged today. It`s past time people that do this kind of political vandalism or harm other people or threaten people, that they`re held accountable.\par \par \uc2 I just want the person that did it to be held accountable. And whoever -- whoever did this, if they thought they were going to intimidate me, well, they thought wrong. I`m going to work harder than ever. I`m going to do the job that I was elected to do, and nothing`s going to stop that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you and your daughters were home when this occurred, but you discovered all the vandalism that next morning, correct?\par \par \uc2 MACE: No, I was -- no, we were not home.\par \par \uc2 In fact, one of my kids had a sleepover on Sunday night. I had date night, so I wasn`t even home when it happened.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MACE: I found out when I was on my way back in the morning.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 MACE: And the police were notified. Someone -- a jogger had come by the house and reported it early in the morning. And I was already on the way home that morning when I found out...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 MACE: ... and walked up on my house. The police were here.\par \par \uc2 It`s very unsettling. And if I had footage I could release of it, I would. I`m desperate to find answers and clues, because I want this person to be charged and held accountable to the fullest extent of the law.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, we do know there were reports of three Antifa symbols that were spray-painted on your property.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Did they know anything more about that, about the source of this or a group like that?\par \par \uc2 MACE: They don`t.\par \par \uc2 I know that, within Charleston, there are a handful of Antifa and anarchist type of groups that have done graffiti. There was other graffiti after my house was targeted on Monday morning, the early hours of Monday morning, that the individual, it looks like, spray-painted two other locations.\par \par \uc2 And then, about two months ago, there were some spray-painting on the island where I live at a high school. So, the police are investigating whether these things are related or how they`re related. I`m desperate for clues and information on this one.\par \par \uc2 It`s very -- it`s troubling and it`s jarring to walk up and be home. I don`t feel safe in my own home. And it doesn`t matter what your political beliefs are, but everyone should feel safe at home. I don`t -- I don`t live behind a wall. I don`t have a gate.\par \par \uc2 I live in my community with everybody else. I live in the home where my children -- and this is their childhood home. And they`re -- I`m raising them here. I have nowhere to go. And I`m not going to stand for it. And I want to stand strong. And I`m going to keep doing the work I was elected to do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Congresswoman, keep us posted on that and whether they ever get to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 But we can disagree on political matters in this country, but, man, oh, man, that is uncalled for.\par \par \uc2 So, be well, be strong. My best to your girls.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You shouldn`t have to deal with that. We are better than that, as a people, I suspect.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Mace of the House Oversight and Reform Committee.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210604eh6400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 News Jobless Claims Fall; Home Prices Surging; \uc2 NASA\uc2  Targets Venus; Vaccine Incentives; Future of COVID-19? Cyber Threats; Interview With Former U.S. Secretary of Defense \uc2 Leon Panetta\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Lawrence, Carley Shimkus, Juan Williams, Charlie Gasparino,  Hillary Vaughn, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,901 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 June 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, thank you, Martha, very much. Look forward to that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, a lot of Americans not looking forward to what has become a routine event, it seems, not only with the meatpacker JBS, but now the MTA, a Martha's Vineyard ferry, \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 . On and on we go.\par \par \uc2 So many businesses hit and attacked and hacked, some of them paying up, but a lot of people wondering what is really going down right now. The administration has sent a clear message to corporate America, harden up, or things could get worse. But is it their responsibility or the government's?\par \par \uc2 We're into all of that, as is Hillary Vaughn on Capitol Hill with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, well, President Biden says he's closely looking at possibly retaliating against Russia for these recent ransomware attacks.\par \par \uc2 But the White House today says it is up to the private sector to protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: ... can't do it alone. Business leaders have a responsibility to strengthen their cyber-defenses to protect the American public and our economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: But the U.S. Chamber of Commerce senior vice president for cyber tells me the status quo isn't working. There does need to be consequences and a credible deterrent from the federal government to hackers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER ROBERTI, \uc2 U.S. CHAMBER OF COMMERCE\uc2 : No company is going to be able to defend itself against a determined nation-state actor or a very sophisticated threat actor that is basically operating either with the tacit or explicit consent of a nation-state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: President Biden says he will be pressing Putin about this latest round of ransomware attacks.\par \par \uc2 But, if Putin does not play ball, the acting director of Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, Brandon Wales, tells me it is really tough for the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to be able to track them down overseas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRANDON WALES, ACTING DIRECTOR, U.S. CYBERSECURITY AND INFRASTRUCTURE SECURITY AGENCY: It is challenging, because, many times, these cyber criminals are operating from countries that are not doing enough to stop them. They're not cooperating with U.S. law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: The White House can -- says they cannot tell private companies that they cannot pay off hackers, but they are working on putting together a comprehensive strategy to figure -- to help companies figure out how to deal with these ransom payments.\par \par \uc2 But acting Director Wales told me today that every time a company pays a ransom, they just make the entire thing more profitable for hackers, and encourage hackers to go after bigger and more important companies -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, some of those businesses often have no choice, Hillary, though, without some help or some assist from the president.\par \par \uc2 We will see.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Hillary Vaughn. Very good reporting on that.\par \par \uc2 Again, whether all of these incidents are tied by the same hacker, there's no way of knowing this early on, but it does keep happening, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 Our Charlie Gasparino taking a look at the private sector and how it is trying to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 A lot easier said than done, right, buddy?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, and, Neil, they are -- as you know, we work for a private sector company. We get e-mails all the time about phishing and watching out for spyware being download -- downloading spyware to your computer.\par \par \uc2 But there's something bigger here. Let's unpack this a little bit. President Biden is spending $6 trillion, or maybe more, on infrastructure. At least that's what he wants. That's his ask. A lot of stuff in that infrastructure is stuff that isn't typically infrastructure. Some of it is, but it's often stuff that's handled by states.\par \par \uc2 For example, New York state builds a bridge to New York state. Taxpayers finance it, not the federal taxpayers, because New York state residents use it. If he really wants to help national infrastructure, which this bill does not do, he should be diverting that money into cybersecurity and these sort of broader, more federally mandated or more federally -- things that have more to do with the federal infrastructure than building a bridge in New York City.\par \par \uc2 We need infrastructure in terms of computers, in terms of cybersecurity. There's not one dime of that in this infrastructure bill. And it's a real shame. And, I mean, the Republicans should seize on this, because businesses -- as the last guy said, it's very difficult for a business to guard against everything.\par \par \uc2 And this is a national issue. We are being attacked. I mean, it's like saying a business needs to find -- finance its own army to stop China from hacking in or Russia from hacking into its software. It's crazy.\par \par \uc2 The government, the national government, the federal government has to have a response. It should be spending some of some of those trillions on this stuff, because this stuff could literally shut down the economy. And guess what? If you spend money on this, they will probably create some jobs as well and stop the economy from shutting down, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well put.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino, thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 To \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2  now, who has been very worried about all this, of course, the former CIA director, former defense secretary, former White House chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 Leon, very good to see you. And thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 To you, this is not a partisan issue. It is a scary national security issue, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 LEON PANETTA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Yes, this is not just criminal activity trying to get ransomware as a result of impacting on our infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 I think this is a national security issue. When you have a cyberattack that can shut down the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2  and create chaos on the East Coast, when you have an attack now on another part of our infrastructure, meat distribution systems, and have the impact of raising prices on meat, when you're impacting on our transportation systems, my concern is that vital infrastructure that is critical to our national security is now at risk.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Leon, I'm getting the sense that it's upping the ante, right, I mean, from the days when we got all unnerved when Target told thousands of -- millions of customers that their security was breached.\par \par \uc2 But now it extends to Mass Transit Authority and trains, extends to Martha's Vineyard and ferries and to meet with JBS and, as you said, the energy concern \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 . I can go on and on.\par \par \uc2 The one thing that they have in common, regardless of who's responsible, whether it's the same group responsible for all of these, is that they're picking up the pace. And I'm wondering if that -- if it is indeed coming from Russia, whether it's countenanced by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  or not, is that a sign of things to come and that more will come ahead of the big summit in a couple of weeks?\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: Oh, I don't think there's any question about that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Look, I have been saying it for a long time, but cyber is the battlefield of the future. And we can see it now happening before our eyes. You have got these sophisticated viruses that can be deployed into our computer systems, and virtually shut those computer systems down.\par \par \uc2 And you have the potential today for a cyberattack that could take down our electric grid system, take down our transportation systems, our chemical systems, our water systems, take down our financial systems. You could virtually paralyze this country with a massive cyberattack.\par \par \uc2 That clearly is something we should be concerned about, because it's our national security that's at risk.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are a lot of folks who are saying, in light of this, President Biden shouldn't meet with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , that it is so brazen, what Putin is up to, or at least countenancing, that it sort of builds his stature. Why do that?\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: Well, I, frankly, think it's important to send Putin a very clear message that there are lines that we will not allow to be crossed.\par \par \uc2 I think, for too long, Putin has assumed weakness on the part of the United States. It's empowered him to go into the Crimea, to go into the Ukraine, to go into Syria, to go into Libya, to attack our own election systems in this country, to engage in a cyberattack, \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2 , which impacted on our industries and corporations and federal agencies.\par \par \uc2 So he has taken advantage of this perception of weakness. And I think it's really important for President Biden to make very clear to him that we consider this a matter of national security, and that it must come to an end. Otherwise, Russia will pay a price.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know what the price could be, with still more sanctions on a country that seems to blow them off.\par \par \uc2 I am curious what you make of how easy it seems to be for people to hack in to our most advanced systems, in a couple of conditions, some defense positions?\par \par \uc2 And I wonder. I thought, after the 2016 experience, with concerns that Russian operatives were trying to intrude in the election, and then with concerns that they were at it again in 2020, that we would have learned a few things.\par \par \uc2 And yet it picks up the pace. What's going on? Why are we still so vulnerable?\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: I think part of my concern is that this is a pretty hit-and-miss operation, in which everybody is pretty much on their own.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: And I really think that what's required now is a national strategy to deal with these cyberattacks that makes very clear that the United States is going to put together a very strong defense and an offense in order to deal with these attacks.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, I think we need to have a stronger bond, frankly, between the public and private sector. I regret this comment that somehow the private sector is on its own.\par \par \uc2 Look, our infrastructure is related to our national security. And a lot of the private sector is running our infrastructure. We have to work with them to make sure that they are properly defended, because it is in our national security interests.\par \par \uc2 And, thirdly, I think we really do need to be on the cutting edge of this technology. We have got to develop better technology to deal with it. We have to develop the kind of expertise, cutting-edge expertise, that is required, if we're going to deal with these adversaries of ours and these criminal operations that are basically attacking the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I was talking to a military operative, Leon, on FOX business.\par \par \uc2 And the issue came up, all right, if they're going to keep hacking us and keep going after us, we can do the same. We can be hacking each other. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: As I said, we need to have a very strong defense.\par \par \uc2 I remember, when I went into the CIA, I was told we were getting 100,000 attacks a day. I'm sure that's multiplied several-fold since I was there. So we're getting a lot of attacks.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's absolutely critical to have a very good defense, but to also have offensive capability, where we can use cyber-capabilities to go after those who are attacking us and show that, when they do this, they have to pay a price.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2 , thank you, sir, the former CIA director, former defense secretary, former White House chief of staff, a bipartisan call to arms here, as he put it, to go ahead and acknowledge the fact that this keeps happening.\par \par \uc2 We have got to do something. The time for sort of pussyfooting around the issue, well, that's over.\par \par \uc2 All right, a lot of you might have heard back and forth in some interviews with Dr. Fauci and what he knew and when he knew it regarding the origin of COVID-19. So I feel all the news networks have done that to death. Some people will find these he was in on the secret, all that. Others say, no, no, no, he's a giant.\par \par \uc2 I'm not interested in that. I'm interested in going forward and a comment he made about where COVID goes from here. We're not out of the woods -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We are just getting worried right now that President Biden does not -- again, does not want to form a presidential commission to look at the January 6 insurrection on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 That's doesn't mean he's opposed to an investigation of this, that it should be in Congress' hands to follow up. On that front, we understand that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is considering making such a move, even though the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  had rejected that as a possibility.\par \par \uc2 But, again, the president for his part, is not going to be appointing any presidential commission to look into how that all came into being and who was responsible, who were the catalysts and the bad actors, and how to prevent it from happening again.\par \par \uc2 So, 9/11-type -- so, no 9/11-type condition coming from the president of the United States, this is, of course, coming from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself just a few minutes ago. We will keep you posted on any further developments.\par \par \uc2 Also keeping you posted on \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2 . There is a cottage industry building, depending on the news network, of what he knew and when he knew it. I felt, looking at all the news networks today and the business networks, that that's been exhausted, that people have their own points of view on this subject, whether he was hiding something at the origins of COVID back in the earliest days, others who say that he was on top of it and handling it all well.\par \par \uc2 I am not here to judge.\par \par \uc2 What caught my attention in some of his comments was what he said about COVID right now, and that maybe, right now, we're not quite out of the woods. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: The one thing we want to make sure is that we don't declare victory prematurely, and feel that, because things are going in the right direction, that we don't have to keep vaccinating people.\par \par \uc2 We're on a really good track now to really crush this outbreak. What I am concerned about are those states in which the level of vaccination is low, that you may continue to see higher levels of cases as we get into the summer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so don't get ahead of our proverbial skis here, that seems to be his message.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Amesh Adalja\uc2  joins right now, Johns Hopkins University infectious disease specialist.\par \par \uc2 What do you think, Doctor, what he's saying there, that there's a concern he has going into the summer and the fall? Do you share that concern?\par \par \uc2 DR. AMESH ADALJA\uc2 , \uc2 INFECTIOUS DISEASES SOCIETY OF AMERICA\uc2 : There definitely are states where vaccination is not where we want it to be, states in the South like Mississippi, for example, states in Arkansas, where doses are going to be expired.\par \par \uc2 And that's going to leave the population susceptible to infection. One thing I think that's different, though, is that, in many of those states, we have got the high-risk individuals highly vaccinated. And that really prevents the cases that are inevitably going to occur, because COVID isn't going to zero, from translating into hospitals in crisis.\par \par \uc2 So, I do think we want to get the vaccination rate much higher in those states, but I don't think we're going to see New York-style outbreaks or what happened in the past, because our seniors are so heavily vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you say, Doctor, to those who do not want to get vaccinated? All sorts of temptations out there. We're going to explore that in the next segment.\par \par \uc2 But there are some who are saying, nope, not going to do it, don't see any reason to do it.\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: You have to really meet people where they are. There's lots of different reasons why people are not vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, someone just wanted me to give them a ride and sit there while they got vaccinated. So I did that.\par \par \uc2 So, it all just depends upon who's being -- who needs to be vaccinated. There are many different reasons. Some of it is just procrastinators. Some people need to be nagged a lot. There are other people that are completely against the vaccine. And they may eventually, as more and more of their friends and family members get vaccinated, as they see nobody is being controlled by a microchip, a couple days down the road, they may then decide to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But it's going to be kind of a one-on-one thing. The people who wanted to get vaccinated, by and large, did. So now you have really just got to kind of take this kind of one step at a time with each person and try and nudge them the best you can.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's going to make -- it's going to be hard, but I think we can still continue to inch up. And the more we inch up, the better we're going to be.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Doctor, man, you are a doctor's doctor. I mean, if you're helping people out through this, and they get nervous, and they want you with them, and you do that, man, you are -- you are a rare breed, sir.\par \par \uc2 So, let me ask you about where we stand on herd immunity. That's been the goal. Now they say, it's not really necessary, but we had -- if we have a stubborn part of the population, particularly young people, resistant to this, and a lot of those same young people returning to college campuses this fall, could we see a spurt in cases as a result?\par \par \uc2 ADALJA: We definitely could see cases occur when people are unvaccinated and they're gathering together and not social distancing.\par \par \uc2 However, the fact is, so many people have been vaccinated, so much -- so much prior immunity from infections in the past is there, that the virus is having a difficult time spreading. So, even unvaccinated people are going to be less likely to come into contact with it. But we will still see cases occur.\par \par \uc2 But they're going to be decoupled from hospitalizations and deaths, because the high-risk people have largely been vaccinated. Universities can make this easier by starting to mandate vaccines for their students, but also for their faculty and employees, which they have really been focused on the students, but it's also important to vaccinate the faculty and employees and have that be a mandate, if they're going to mandate that for students.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Doctor, very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Amesh Adalja\uc2 , the \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2  infectious disease specialists, and, apparently, if we're really nervous about getting the vaccination, he will come with you to the doctor.\par \par \uc2 That's pretty cool right there. That's pretty neat.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, what do you make of all these incentives right now to make sure people get shots? It's -- some have lottery systems going on. Others will give you free concerts. Then there's \uc2 Anheuser-Busch\uc2 , which will give you beer.\par \par \uc2 Would you drink to that? Put your arm out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A million dollars cash or all the groceries you want to buy for a year just if you take that vaccination shot, which way would you go?\par \par \uc2 Psst. Go for the groceries.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CESAR VARGAS, \uc2 ANHEUSER-BUSCH INBEV\uc2 : When we reach this goal of getting 70 percent of Americans vaccinated, we're going to unlock the biggest beer giveaway in our history.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to -- we're going to buy America a round of beer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I was trying to push the \uc2 Anheuser-Busch\uc2  top executive there about this. How much is a round a beer? Do you have to prove the number of people you're giving the round to?\par \par \uc2 I just threw out the number 100. And he said he would actually have to check that.\par \par \uc2 But, be that as it may, this comes in light of all sorts of incentives to make sure you go ahead and get vaccinated, \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , for example, which is offering a year of free groceries. Others are putting up a million dollars.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  would lose money on me with the grocery things, so I think I would go the grocery route. But that's just me.\par \par \uc2 We have got Juan Williams with us right now. Very cool to have him here. Carley Shimkus, very cool to have her here.\par \par \uc2 I think Carley works something like 20 hours a day. But she's here now.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Carley, what do you think of this? What do you think of this idea?\par \par \uc2 Now, the grocery thing, I would go for. But putting it in perspective, what we're seeing is more kind of goodies being dangled at people to get that vaccine. And young people, actually, like you, are not taking them up on it.\par \par \uc2 Why?\par \par \uc2 CARLEY SHIMKUS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, I am of two minds when it comes to vaccine incentives.\par \par \uc2 The first is intense curiosity, because we went from free \uc2 Krispy Kreme\uc2  doughnuts to a million dollars and free groceries for a year in like a month-and-a-half. So I just want to see where this goes. I mean, how much will they offer people? Land? Titles? Royalty?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: I mean, I know it's not in style, but it just seems like we're spitballing here and all options are on the table.\par \par \uc2 The other, more prevailing feeling really is a question. And it is, have we lost our minds? Because these vaccine incentives are extremely expensive, and not altogether necessary, considering the number of people who have natural immunity. Couple that with the folks who voluntarily got vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, they are fun, but a lot of this is taxpayer money. And I would rather pay fewer taxes, less taxes, than pay for somebody's burger and fries in New York.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow. Well, aren't you the party pooper?\par \par \uc2 You know, Juan, I was thinking, the whole -- what if you did all the right things, right? You got vaccinated early. You wanted to look out for your fellow man and woman and make sure that you were safe and they would be safe.\par \par \uc2 There's no reward for them. It's these other Johnny-come-latelies who are getting the free groceries...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: It's so true.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... and the million-dollar -- that doesn't seem right, Juan.\par \par \uc2 I don't know.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, you remember that Bible story, Neil, the prodigal son?\par \par \uc2 The sinner comes home and he gets the big party, while the son that's been there all along doing what was right, he's like, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But here's the thing. And I think this is really important.\par \par \uc2 One, I don't see that it's all that much government money. What we're talking about is \uc2 Kroger\uc2 's, \uc2 Anheuser-Busch\uc2 . This is private companies. And I think they understand that if -- to get the American economy roaring again, we have got to get COVID under control.\par \par \uc2 People don't go back into the bars and hang out and buy lots of beers unless they feel safe. Major League Baseball, they're giving away tickets. Why? People don't go to the game unless they feel safe.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not only a matter of, gee, why is the government giving anybody a million bucks or why are the private companies doing this? Everyone sees that it's in our national self-interest and our economic self-interest to get the economy humming again.\par \par \uc2 I would say they're even going to do this overseas. I mean, if you're selling cars overseas, they're going to do it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 You know, but, Carley, he mentioned the prodigal -- but, Carley, he mentioned the prodigal -- but he mentioned the prodigal son. And the good son, the good son, he doesn't even get the fatted calf. He has a thin little if that he's lucky.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I'm just saying, there's no reward for those who were doing the right thing.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I know there's a biblical story there that says, yes, but, in the long term, it's the good thing.\par \par \uc2 Well, yes, but look who got the fatted calf. Not me.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: I know. I hope you get...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Unless, unless, unless I'm going to \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , where I can find fatted calves.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: That's what I was going to say.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: And, you know, there is some federal money when it comes -- I believe, in Ohio, that $1 million incentive was COVID relief money...\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: ... which really proves that a lot of states were given a little too much.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, I just...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, but if it does some good and protects people, Juan, that's your point, right? It does a lot of good.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: You know what, though, Neil?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Go ahead, Carley.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's my point. Yes, that's my point.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: I just feel like the best incentive was, in the early days of this, if you just told people, you could go back to a normal life, you don't have to wear a mask, that seems like the best incentive of all.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHIMKUS: And the federal government flubbed that one up by saying that fully vaccinated people still needed to wear masks.\par \par \uc2 I don't hate the vaccine incentives. Part of me feels like it's a little bit patronizing. Like, that's what you do for kids that don't eat their vegetables, like offer them yummy things. It's a little bit fun, but, also, I do think it's a bit of a waste of money.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Juan?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't think it's a waste of money at all.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, I think we all want to feel safe. I think we all want to get back to normal. I think we all want to have a really fun Fourth of July. And, to me, this is just not only a matter of public policy, but, as I was saying to Neil earlier, it's a matter of economic goodwill, that -- just good sense.\par \par \uc2 Everybody wants our businesses to thrive, our economy to get back in gear.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And we have got to get coronavirus under control to do it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They really have to redo that prodigal son story. I wonder how they would do it for today, right, guys?\par \par \uc2 Where you're talking to -- you're talking to the wayward son. I will give you a million dollars or the fatted calf. You can also call a friend.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: There you go. Come on home.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's my very poor -- that's my very poor Regis Philbin.\par \par \uc2 All right, guys, thank you all very, very much\par \par \uc2 So, again, to all you stragglers, have at it. The incentives are multiplying as we speak.\par \par \uc2 And so is the quest for space, this time to Venus -- yeah, Venus -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Venus, young man. That seems to be the next call on the part of \uc2 NASA\uc2 . It wants to send to craft up there, as we have been busy visiting Mars.\par \par \uc2 But our actual closest neighbor is Venus. And this is something that's getting attention, because a lot of people think Venus could be Earth in the super future here, that, by looking at the greenhouse gas emissions and all the rest that have essentially, presumably, made its oceans boiling water and virtually uninhabitable, it might be a preview to coming attractions here.\par \par \uc2 That might be a leap, but we will go to Mike Massimino, the former NASA astronaut, "2 Funny Astronauts" podcast host, "Spaceman" author. He's a busy fellow.\par \par \uc2 Mike, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 I always herald Italian Americans who have had great accomplishments, way beyond this planet. And you were that, young man. So, congratulations.\par \par \uc2 But this push...\par \par \uc2 MIKE MASSIMINO, FORMER NASA ASTRONAUT: Thank you. Thank you, Neil. What a great introduction. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There we go. There we go.\par \par \uc2 This push for Venus now, part of me remembers when we were doing this, what, three decades ago. So, can't you say, been there, done that, really didn't get much from that? What do you say?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: No, well, I think we did get an awful lot from that. We learned a lot.\par \par \uc2 But that -- 30 years ago, a lot has happened in technology-wise that's available, and other discoveries have been made. So, the mapping of the Magellan spacecraft 30 years ago that was done was great, but it could not get the resolution to really understand what the planet was like and the surface was like that we can now get with new instruments, with the new technology.\par \par \uc2 So, this mission isn't going to launch tomorrow. It's going to be toward the end of this decade. And, by then, the spacecraft that will go will have the latest technology on board and be able to map it a lot better.\par \par \uc2 Plus, not that long ago, there was some evidence that there might be some biological material in the atmosphere, some organic material that created some of the chemicals that they have seen, that they observed, or what they think they might have observed in the atmosphere -- atmosphere of Venus. So it's worth taking a second look at that as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, a lot of the people have been talking about studying the greenhouse effect there, geology there, that it could be a lesson for Earth, that what happened there and led to the oceans boiling away there -- we assumed they boiled -- we don't know -- could be a reminder and something timely for us to study.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Well, I think, by understanding the planets around us that we think might have formed around -- formed around the same time and might have been similar, for example, even Mars, you know, Earth went in this great direction of supporting life, and with -- a lot of reasons for that, with the resources we have and our magnetic field, and so on.\par \par \uc2 And Venus, very close to the sun -- or closer to the sun, much hotter, inhospitable, as you mentioned. Mars, we haven't been able to find any signs of life, on the surface, at least.\par \par \uc2 But I think by studying those two planets and trying to figure out what happened, why Earth went in one direction, and those two planets that are right next to us went in opposite directions, we might learn a lot about our planet and how to keep it sustainable.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not it's a prediction of what's going to happen, I think that's a bit of a leap.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: But, certainly, by looking at these -- our neighbors, we can learn a lot about our planet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, you have talked about 30 years ago, when we were last visiting Venus and all of that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have got a lot more competition these days, right? I mean, leaving aside just Russia, what China is doing on Mars right now, on the moon right now. India and Japan, I believe those two countries are looking at Venus missions of their own. What do you make of all of this?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Well, I think it's more and more countries now -- space is becoming more accessible for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 It's not just governments, but it's also private -- private companies going into space. It's opening up people's thought process. And with the new technology we can send to these places, we see more opportunity to explore.\par \par \uc2 So I think that we're just seeing a -- just a huge increase in the opportunities for people and experiments and understanding. I would like to see us cooperate with more of these countries. And we are.\par \par \uc2 For example, on the International Space Station, I think it's a great model, where it's the U.S. and Russia, the countries of Europe, Canada, and Japan and other countries all cooperating.\par \par \uc2 I think that these scientific programs, whether it's exploring space or in other areas of science, are great ways for us to find common ground between countries around the world. And, hopefully, we can cooperate and explore together.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mike Massimino, great seeing you again. Thank you so much, and to your service.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Neil, always a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... yourself, young man.\par \par \uc2 Same here.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in the meantime here, as he was wrapping up there, I do want to bring your attention to a notice we got out of the Department of Justice, that it is making ransomware cases a top priority now.\par \par \uc2 This comes at a time we have seen one major business after another that has been hit, and hit hard, not the least of which JBS, the big meat supplier essentially to the world, that said about a quarter of its meat production was sidelined during this whole ransomware attack.\par \par \uc2 Keep you posted on that.\par \par \uc2 Keeping you posted as well on the surge in home prices. They're running up at the highest rate and the biggest increase in well more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 The guy who saw all of this coming and didn't lose faith, particularly in New York, when COVID it, and he has the listings to prove it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you're not imagining it.\par \par \uc2 Home prices are going higher, a lot higher, in fact, the highest they have ever been, and the rate of increase, since way back in 2006. What, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  was president back then.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people thought that those days would never come back, especially when the COVID virus hit and everyone was getting out of New York, and everyone was saying, New York was finished, except one guy I remember, at the time, Ryan Serhant.\par \par \uc2 Of course, you remember him on Bravo, "Million Dollar Listing," much, much more, bestselling author, a couple of books out, "Sell It Like Serhant." But the one that I really like, "Big Money Energy," I think that's still out there right now. But he's a force of nature in himself, very optimistic. I think that's -- that has a lot to do with it.\par \par \uc2 Ryan, very good to see you.\par \par \uc2 RYAN SERHANT, BROKER: Good to see you. Thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, let's talk a little bit about what's going on here now.\par \par \uc2 I mean, last time you and I chatted, it wasn't a matter of prices running up across the board, but we didn't think they would last or go as high as they had since. Now, you did, actually.\par \par \uc2 SERHANT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what do you make of where we are? Because now it's pricing some people out of the entry level market. And that could slow all of this down? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SERHANT: Of course.\par \par \uc2 Listen, there is pent-up demand for housing since 2007. People keep talking about this bubble, it's a bubble, it's a bubble. But what is the definition of a bubble, right? A bubble occurs when buyers rush into a market on speculation that prices are going to continue to rise.\par \par \uc2 Crypto, right, so, the Reddit craze, everything we're seeing. Home prices have been depressed in the United States, if we just keep it national, since 2008. But we don't think about it that way because it's hard for us to think in decades.\par \par \uc2 What we're seeing right now isn't pent-up demand for housing since COVID from the last year. COVID was just like it was for a lot of industries, an accelerant, pushing people back into a housing market to look for homes that they not only just need, because they're having a baby or working or their lifestyle has changed, but homes that they actually also want.\par \par \uc2 And there is a national deficit of housing. It's as high as, I think, three million homes due to multiple years of underbuilding. And as such, any significant chance of extreme price declines is normal, but I think there is multiple years of runway here in what I feel is a very healthy housing market.\par \par \uc2 Sellers put their homes on the market. Buyers want to buy them. And it's amazing to me. When that happens, people say, oh, there must be something wrong. This must be a bubble.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I would agree with you in the larger -- here, that the supply- and-demand equation is certainly favoring still more demand chasing too little supply.\par \par \uc2 But I do get worried, anecdotally, when I hear these people who crowd around a seller's home and offer everything from free concerts to incentives to make them the appointed buyer.\par \par \uc2 SERHANT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's not like it was back in the last housing boom went bust, when people would buy properties sight unseen and flip them.\par \par \uc2 I get that. I don't see that maybe in large enough numbers to be worried. But when you hear stuff like that, does it worry you?\par \par \uc2 SERHANT: Yes. Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 I think people are pushed to follow the crowd. And a person is smart. An individual person can make smart, intelligent decisions. People, as a group, can be very stupid. And they can make irrational, drastic decisions based purely on fear.\par \par \uc2 And the fear now is the fear of missing out, the fear of not buying at the right price, the fear of not getting a home. And that fear is what's driving some of these crazy incentives that you're -- that you're hearing about.\par \par \uc2 But, listen, there are only so many houses. The last boom that we had, if you will, before the Great Recession was really driven by improper lending, right, and soft lending, so that anyone can buy a house who didn't -- who didn't need one...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 SERHANT: ... or couldn't afford one. And we don't see that now.\par \par \uc2 This is people saying, hey, my lifestyle has changed. I want to be able to live and work in a different way. And the world has felt the same way now for the last year. And that's pushed people to want to make moves.\par \par \uc2 And so I just think the housing market has seen depressed prices for the last 12 years. And now we are trying to find that equilibrium, so that we will come down from the peak. And I think that might be towards the fall, possibly this coming winter.\par \par \uc2 But if you're a seller and you're looking to sell, don't wait for the market to get better, better, better, because we have no idea when that will stop.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 SERHANT: Now is an amazing time.\par \par \uc2 But there's a lot of greed out there. And that's what I'm not OK with.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Ryan, great catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Serhant, the Serhant founder, CEO, broker, "Million Dollar Listing," much, much more.\par \par \uc2 And as he was pointing out, the backdrop for a lot of this is still a pent- up demand that is proving itself with every piece of economic data we get, including some incredible numbers we got on jobs today.\par \par \uc2 I will explain after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: we have not seen people filing for jobless claims this low since before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Edward Lawrence on that phenomenon that is apparently picking up steam -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We saw some very good numbers on the initial jobless claims. But if you look under that, you dig deeper into this, that you see about 3.8 million people still sitting on the continuous jobless claim roles. And that means that some say they're being paid not to work here.\par \par \uc2 So, 25 states announced plans to end the federal add-on of $300 a week. The last one to do it was Maryland. All of those governors are Republican. Now, the other half of the states, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  from Michigan is doubling down on this, saying that, if people take a part-time job, they will still get the unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): We want to make sure that we are giving people all of the incentives to get back to work, so that they can take care of themselves and their families, and not forego other opportunities that can help them get back on their feet.\par \par \uc2 So you see a lot of states just kind of taking away help for people who are struggling. We don't think that's -- makes a lot of sense in this environment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And the legislature does have to approve that plan.\par \par \uc2 But talk to any small business owner, they're seeing resumes, report who is applying for a job to the government. Then that person does not show up for the interview, thus keeping their unemployment benefits -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Edward, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 To \uc2 Art Hogan\uc2  right now, \uc2 National Securities Corporation\uc2  genius here.\par \par \uc2 Art, obviously, this has been fuel to the markets, continued fuel to the economy. Just talked to a housing expert who thinks it's going to be very much more extended fuel to housing.\par \par \uc2 Where are you on all of this?\par \par \uc2 ART HOGAN\uc2 , MARKET STRATEGIST: Yes, I think the your last guest that talked about the housing market was spot on.\par \par \uc2 And I think, when you think about household formation in the millennial population, that's just beginning. I think the peak house-buying age is 33 years old. And that peak doesn't come for three years. So I think he's right. I think it's a good long runway there.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the ongoing improvement, the sequential improvement in economic data, especially around the labor force, remember, last month, we had a very disappointing non-farm payroll number. The three-month average is about 500,000 jobs being created. And if we have that pace continuously for the balance of this year, it's going to take us until well into the first quarter of next year before we get back to that pre-pandemic level of full employment.\par \par \uc2 So, to me, I think that we're moving in the right direction. I just don't think it's shockingly good news. I think it's good enough news.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you're right. Tomorrow, we got the employment data, and some people expecting more than 600,000 new jobs added. There's no way of knowing. We will find out then.\par \par \uc2 But what do you tell investors looking at this market, assessing this economy, whether to buy stocks or a house?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Yes, that's a great question.\par \par \uc2 It's actually a great time to be a house seller, if that's a decision you have been putting off, because I agree that things have gotten ahead of themselves when you look at the percentage increase on a year-over-year basis at this point in time, and the average selling price for the house in the United States is about $333,000. It was $286,000 this time last year.\par \par \uc2 But that's a lot -- that's really a demand issue. But it's certainly a supply issue. We're underhoused as a country.\par \par \uc2 I think that, when we think about long-term investing, we would really like to have investors put their money, their asset allocation in a very balanced way. Have some growth on one side of this barbell. Call it 5G, cloud computing, cloud security. On the other side, have some cyclicality.\par \par \uc2 As the economy improves here, you want to see industrials, materials, energy and financials. And if every two months, you rebalance that, and you sell your winners and buy some of your losers, keep that barbell level, I think you're going to outperform this market this year. You did last year. And I think that works again this year.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You were optimistic last year at this time, Art, so I will take that one. We will see where we go.\par \par \uc2 Art Hogan\uc2 , \uc2 National Securities Corporation\uc2 , uncanny read on these markets.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, as Art and I were discussing here, the granddaddy of all the economic numbers we follow, the employment report for the month of May, is due out tomorrow. They're expecting the unemployment rate to dip back under 6 percent to 5.9 percent and see north of 660,000 jobs added to the economy.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the prior month, they were disappointed. These forecasts are never exactly right. But it's the trend they look at. And they say they like the trend.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210603eh630002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hundreds Of Arrests Made Along Border Today; Congress Gets Involved With COVID-19 Investigations; Russian Hackers Suspected In Latest Cyber Intrusion; Californians Could Win Millions For Being Vaccinated; Hundreds Of Arrests Made Along Southern Border Today; Biden Proposes $6 Trillion Budget To Congress\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Bill Melugin, Rich Edson, Mark  Meredith, Chad Pergram, Lucas Tomlinson, Casey Stegall, Steve Harrigan \par \uc2 7,524 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: They're calling it wild. They're calling it magic. Congratulations to Baez, and great win. We're out of time. Bye.\par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX NEWS HOST: That's it.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: That didn't even show the best parts.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: I know.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We'll get -- watch it. It's so good.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: We got a Memorial Day special Monday. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Good evening, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, while President Biden and his team deal with major foreign policy challenges from Russia and China, we begin this evening with a long-standing problem here at home, the southern border crisis.\par \par \uc2 Immigration and border security, the biggest Achilles heel for the president in our latest Fox News poll, along with foreign policy, actually. We have new images tonight from the southern border region as hundreds of illegal immigrants are arrested in just one day. But the effects of that crisis can be seen on the faces of individuals often young children.\par \par \uc2 Correspond and Bill Melugin shows us tonight from La Joya, Texas. Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. This border crisis here really showing no signs of slowing down.\par \par \uc2 Just a short time ago, I had a chance to talk to a Border Patrol source who told me that just here in this Rio Grande Valley sector in the last 24 hours, they've apprehended more than 1,300 illegal immigrants. Remarkable numbers for one single day.\par \par \uc2 And today, we witnessed a lot of that activity ourselves. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): A little girl stands alone on railroad tracks in La Joya, Texas Friday morning, momentarily separated from her family. She runs towards a Border Patrol unit where her parents have been apprehended nearby after crossing into the U.S. illegally.\par \par \uc2 Also, nearby is a group of more than a dozen unaccompanied kids who have all given themselves up, all of them from Guatemala. They include this brother and sister ages four and seven. They came without any guardians. All of the kids in this group, the oldest being young teens traveled without any parents.\par \par \uc2 In Mission, Texas, this driver was arrested and charged with human smuggling after he crashed this Expedition during a traffic stop and tried to run away.\par \par \uc2 Inside that SUV were 17 migrants he was allegedly smuggling into the U.S. Almost all of them single adult men.\par \par \uc2 In Palmview, we came across this traffic stop where migrants had been stuffed into the trunk of this red car allegedly by this man. Border Patrol telling us he will also be charged with human smuggling. One of the passengers agreed to speak to us.\par \par \uc2 Why did you decide to come here now?\par \par \uc2 He told me he can't find a job in his country and it's not safe there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In my country, it's too dangerous. That is no way to live.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Where are you from?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Honduras.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Today, DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas announced plans for quicker and more efficient immigration hearings, while Texas Governor Greg Abbott says the Biden administration's handling of the border crisis continues to endanger Texans.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): It's obvious that there is a crisis that is plaguing farmers and ranchers and residents who live along the border in Texas. Their fences are destroyed, their livestock is lost. Their homes are invaded and their neighborhoods are dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, over the last couple of weeks, both here and in Del Rio, we've had the chance to talk to a lot of law enforcement, whether it's local sheriffs, deputy, state troopers, or border agents themselves, and they all kind of have the exact same reaction when they hear the administration constantly saying that the border here is closed. They'll either roll their eyes, or they'll chuckle and they'll laugh and they say well, if the administration would ever actually physically come here to the border, they will see for themselves that's just not the case. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, you know, you've been there on the border for a while and these numbers are staggering, but they're seeing a flow every day. This day was particularly different?\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes, it was. There was activity all over the sector today and that happens every day here, it's like clockwork. But to have 1,300 apprehensions in one single day, Border Patrol folks we talked to say that is significantly higher than what they're used to.\par \par \uc2 And keep in mind, those are only the ones they caught that they know of. They're those getaways where they just don't know what those numbers are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Bill Melugin along the southern border. Great job, Bill. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the pressure is growing on President Biden to find out if the coronavirus pandemic began with a lab leak in Wuhan, China since natural origins still have not emerged clearly a year and a half later.\par \par \uc2 The theories widely mocked during the Trump administration are now gaining more credibility by the day.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Jacqui Heinrich reports tonight from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): President Biden touting progress fighting coronavirus in Virginia today.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There is light at the end of the tunnel. It's a freight train.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: As the White House punted questions on a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  report saying intelligence officials investigating COVID origins have a batch of unexamined evidence that needs more computer analysis.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE, WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Look, we do not comment on intelligence. As you can imagine, we're going to -- we're going to continue to look at the intelligence. We believe there's more work to do, hence the 90-day review.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: President Biden called for the intelligence community to declassify material and expand their probe for 90 days to include national labs and health and medical experts after China announced it won't cooperate in more investigations.\par \par \uc2 The former lead U.S. investigator says there was no evidence with the virus evolving naturally.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASHER, SENIOR FELLOW, \uc2 HUDSON INSTITUTE\uc2 : We had very definitive information. It's only a bit of which we put out publicly that showed that they were engaged in this classified secret effort to develop these super viruses. And that well, it appeared that it had spilled out of that laboratory.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Congress is also pushing for answers. House Republicans launched an investigation into taxpayer money awarded to the Wuhan lab, expressing concern the recipient \uc2 EcoHealth Alliance\uc2  knew about Chinese attempts to cover up the pandemic and did not inform the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . They also question possible unsanctioned research that could make viruses deadlier.\par \par \uc2 Also, in the House, two new bipartisan bills to investigate COVID and let families of American victims sue Beijing.\par \par \uc2 REP. BRIAN FITZPATRICK\uc2  (R-PA): A lot of people are asking for the smoking gun evidence, that's never going to come. So, we're going to have to rely on proving a murder through circumstantial evidence, but the circumstantial evidence is overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): The first bill would establish a bipartisan commission modeled after the 9/11 Commission focused on \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  response, future preparedness and COVID origins.\par \par \uc2 The second bill would strip China of sovereign immunity, similar action taken against Saudi Arabia after 9/11, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 As Jacqui just mentioned, President Biden was at an event today with Virginia Governor Ralph Northam. The visit comes two years after Biden's call for Northam's resignation following the scandal of the governor's medical school yearbook photo apparently showing him and another person dressed in a KKK outfit and blackface while then-Vice President Biden said Northam should resign immediately, and that he had lost all moral authority.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials believe Russian hackers are behind another major campaign against \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  agencies, nongovernmental organizations and think tanks.\par \par \uc2 State Department correspondent \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2  tells us tonight experts believe it's the same group responsible for a previous high-profile attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Russian hackers have hit the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  again. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  says the same, Russian government backed cyber operatives responsible for last year \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2 ' hack, infiltrated an e-mail system at the State Department's Development Agency and targeted "Government agencies, think tanks, consultants and non- governmental organizations. Approximately 3,000 e-mail accounts at more than 150 different organizations."\par \par \uc2 All this less than three weeks ahead of President Biden's summit with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The ransomware attack by criminal hackers earlier this month, well, that was a warning shot. So, we must defend our nation against these threats.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Some in Congress say the administration needs to respond to this latest attack.\par \par \uc2 House Intelligence Committee Chairman Adam Schiff says, "Russia remains undeterred despite sanctions following the \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  attack. Those sanctions gave the administration flexibility to tighten the economic screws further if necessary, it now appears necessary."\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  says the hackers access the U.S. agency for International Development's marketing service. Then, they used it to send legitimate looking e-mails like this to thousands of accounts. Clicking on the link in the e-mail gives the cyber spies access to computer systems.\par \par \uc2 These e-mails went out as recently as this week and at least a quarter of the targeted organizations working international development, humanitarian aid and human rights.\par \par \uc2 Biden has promised to get tough on Russia. Republicans have recently criticized the president for even agreeing to a summit with the Russian president and for the administration refusing sanctions on the company building the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): The White House says despite the hack, there are no changes to the Biden-Putin summit scheduled for June 16th in Switzerland, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll be there. \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2  live in the State Department. Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We're getting our first look tonight at President Biden's $6 trillion with a T budget plan. It would increase the debt astronomically, it would also include massive tax increases.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith is at the White House tonight with a read on the numbers and what they mean.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): President Biden's new released budget calls on Congress to think big and spin big with an estimated price tag of more than $6 trillion.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN DEESE, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL DIRECTOR: It's just, the president's core economic plan, long term investments that will increase the productive capacity of our economy make us more competitive and will do so in a fiscally responsible manner.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Biden's budget blueprint urges Congress to fund several Democratic priorities from the hotly debated infrastructure bill to the $1.8 trillion American Families Plan which would boost spending on social programs.\par \par \uc2 It also calls for increases in discretionary programs including more than eight billion to boost the CDC, $10 billion to fight the opioid epidemic. Two billion for the Justice Department to address gun violence and more than 800 million to address migration issues in Central America.\par \par \uc2 The White House says to pay for all of this, it needs sweeping tax increases from raising the corporate tax rate to 28 percent, increasing taxes on Americans earning more than $400,000 a year and imposing additional taxes on wealthier Americans who profit off Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 The president himself admits his plans are not cheap. But he says timing matters.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is the right time to invest when we have historically low interest rates. Investing now with a plan to pay for it. That is fiscally responsible thing to do.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Republicans call it hogwash. Alabama Senator \uc2 Richard Shelby\uc2  labeling Biden's first budget a "Blueprint for the higher taxes, excessive spending, and disproportionate funding priorities the American people can expect from his administration over the next four years."\par \par \uc2 With deficits soaring even before the pandemic, others argue the country simply can't afford it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , FORMER NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR: His legacy is going to be -- he is going to be the next Jimmy Carter. Huge inflation, huge deficits, low economic growth, high unemployment. You watch, that's what this kind of budget is going to bring to the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): But Republicans argue the government may end up spending a lot more money than people realize, especially when you account for entitlement programs like Social Security and Medicare, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith in the White House Briefing Room. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today, the Dow gained 65, the S&P 500 added three and the \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  rose 12.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was up about one percentage point the S&P 500 gained around one in the sixth. The \uc2 NASDAQ\uc2  jumped two.\par \par \uc2 Some very contentious issues tonight on Capitol Hill. One involves whether House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  can punish members who do not wear coronavirus masks, another, deals with a long-standing Senate tradition.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Chad Pergram is live on the Hill for us as he always is tonight. Good evening, Chad, late night on the Capitol last night ahead of the holiday weekend. What happened?\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, today could in fact mark the beginning of the end for the filibuster. There was a bipartisan agreement to finish a science bill on Thursday, but a small group of GOP senators held the Senate in session until about three this morning. Their protest means the Senate won't complete that bill until June the eighth.\par \par \uc2 Then, only six GOP senators joined Democrats to break a filibuster to launch debate on the bill to investigate the January 6th riot. They needed 60 yes, they only got 54. This could prime the pump for Democrats to alter the filibuster to pass their agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER (D-NY): The events of the last few days probably made every member of our caucus realize that a lot of our Republican colleagues are not willing to work with us on a whole lot of issues, even issues where we try to be bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now, today's vote likely killed the bill to probe the riot. Schumer will soon bring up the voting rights bill and maybe infrastructure. GOP obstruction could help Democrats make the case to nuke the filibuster and pass bills without GOP support, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad, this debate is also brewing about whether it's constitutional for House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  to find members for not wearing masks on the House floor or passing through those magnetometers to get there.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That's right. Republicans don't think the fines are fair. Article 1, Section 5 of the Constitution gives the House to write and make its own rules and punish its members but enforcement, that's an issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON CHAFFETZ, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Really a speaker or a leader doesn't have much in the way of tools to actually discipline somebody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The 27th Amendment says there can't be a law varying the compensation of members. The House rule is not a law, but it has the effect of law and that's why some members think the fines are unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Still, the GOP did not complain when former House Speaker \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  threatened fines after Democrats conducted a sit in on the floor on guns several years ago, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, back and forth. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, whether the U.S. will get its Afghan translators to safety as U.S. troops leave the site of America's longest war.\par \par \uc2 And later, if you have not been vaccinated, what would it take to get you to sign up for a shot?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You don't have anything to lose and everything to win.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I still can't believe it. It was a crazy night.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Black Lives Matter co-founder Patrisse Cullors is stepping down from her role as executive director of the activist group's central foundation amid questions about her finances.\par \par \uc2 Cullors who has referred to herself as a trained Marxist denies allegations. She enriched herself through donations meant for the organization.\par \par \uc2 Last month, The New York Post reported she had purchased four high-end homes for $3.2 million. The report prompted the head of Black Lives Matter Greater New York City to call for an independent investigation into the foundation's finances. Cullors says her exit has been planned for more than a year.\par \par \uc2 There is new video tonight purporting to show UFOs on radar, harassing a \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  warship he off the California coast in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Filmmaker Jeremy Corbell says the video comes from the USS Omaha. Corbell says the Pentagon has confirmed the authenticity of this video. Next month, as we have been reporting, intelligence and defense officials are expected to present a new report on UFOs to Congress. We'll have that.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we have an update on whether the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  plans to evacuate thousands of Afghan interpreters who may be in serious danger as the U.S. pulls its forces out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Here is Lucas Tomlinson at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS PENTAGON CORRESP