 this Memorial Day weekend is the first major holiday period since coronavirus restrictions have been ease after millions of people were vaccinated. Roads and airports are expect to be very busy this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Casey Stegall shows us tonight from Dallas/Fort International Airport in Texas.\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): By car, some by plane, more than 37 million Americans are expected to travel this holiday weekend according to AAA, 60 percent more than last year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's kind of our first big thing that we're doing after COVID.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: But excited passengers quickly encountered long lines at most of the nation's airports.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A lot more people than I expected.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Heading into summer last year, TSA says it was screening about 344,000 passengers a day. Now, more than 2 million are passing through. To help compensate, thousands of new officers are joining the ranks though it will take time to get everyone on board.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: We have focused on a hiring surge but patience is required.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Tensions in the air have been taking off recently. Just this past week, a Southwest Airlines flight attendant had two teeth knocked out by a passenger, reportedly upset over having to stow her tray table for landing in California.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  says more than 1300 incidents of unruly passengers have been recorded since February.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Violence against a flight attendant on a flight is a -- is a federal offense and those cases are going to be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Masks are still required at most airports and on all domestic flights regardless of vaccination status through at least September. Foreign trips remain up in the air.\par \par \uc2 Vaccine passports have also not been ruled out for travel to some European countries. So says, the U.S. Department of Homeland security secretary.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: One of our principles that has guided us throughout this pandemic is the value of diversity, equity, and inclusion, and making sure that any passport that we provide for vaccinations is accessible to all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL (on camera): Now, the White House contradicted that statement earlier this afternoon, by saying that the president's stance on vaccine passports has not changed.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the airline industry today said the domestic carriers issued close to $13 billion worth of cash refunds to customers last year because of the pandemic. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Casey Stegall live at DFW. Casey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 If you have not been vaccinated yet, governments and private companies are trying to convince you to roll up your sleeve. Many are offering cash and other prizes. Correspondent Steve Harrigan takes a look tonight from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One, two, three.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): When it comes to vaccine lotteries, California is on top with $116.5 million giveaway to those who've gotten at least one shot.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2  (D-CA): $15 million. That night will be distributed one and a half million dollar prizes for 10 individuals.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice-over): The funding comes from the Federal Cares Act, another 2 million Californians will get $50 incentive cards from the government when they get vaccinated. California's move follows the successful launch of Vax-a-Million in Ohio, which just awarded its first million dollars to a 22-year-old engineer who was on her way to buy a used car when she got the call.\par \par \uc2 ABBIGAIL BUGENSKE, VAX-A-MILLION WINNER: It was a Wednesday night that I'm never going to forget.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice-over): Across the U.S. new cases and deaths from COVID-19 are down. But the pace of vaccinations has dropped dramatically. Now at 1.7 million shots per day, roughly one half of the number in early April. Red and blue state governors are racing to introduce lotteries for the simple reason that they work.\par \par \uc2 GOV. MIKE DEWINE\uc2  (R-OH): The 10 counties we're seeing the most increase are all rural counties, and they've seen over 100% increase in vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice-over): Corporations are also stepping up to offer a range of incentives to get the shot. CVS is offering cash giveaways, Norwegian Cruise Lines, free vacations, \uc2 Procter and Gamble\uc2 , Super Bowl tickets, \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  free travel for a year, \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , free groceries for a year. \uc2 Krispy Kreme\uc2 , a free donut. Sixty two percent of Americans aged 18 and over had had at least one shot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The Biden administration's goal is to reach 70% vaccinated by the Fourth of July. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan at Atlanta. Steve, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's considerable foreign policy challenges from Russia and China, plus the domestic issue of immigration.\par \par \uc2 First Beyond Our Borders tonight, the United Nations says around 400,000 people needs a border protection after fleeing a volcano eruption and a wave of aftershocks in eastern Congo. At least 31 people have died there since the volcano erupted last weekend, destroying 3,000 homes and cutting off a major road used to bring aid to that region.\par \par \uc2 Japan is extending its coronavirus state of emergency in Tokyo and other areas for more than for 20 more days. The prime minister says cases remain high and medical systems in Osaka, the hardest hit area in western Japan are still overburdened. This comes just 50 days before the scheduled start of the Summer Olympics. Today, the