 Sandra Smith, Dan Hoffman, Will Cain, William La Jeunesse, Aishah Hasnie \par \uc2 8,189 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: All right, Grady Trimble for us.\par \par \uc2 Sandra, what beautiful weather in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: He was standing in front of castaways. All Chicagoans know the exact location that is North Avenue beach. It's a beautiful spot to enjoy summer.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I like the Winner Circle, that's my favorite.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Great to be with you. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. "THE STORY" with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, both. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum in New York right now. We're following two big breaking stories.\par \par \uc2 A deadly shooting in Northern California at a rail yard next toward of the sheriff's department. We can now report that nine people were killed including the suspect in this case.\par \par \uc2 And also right now, the defense, just moments ago, resting their case after a stunning twist in the Mollie Tibbetts' murder trial. The man accused of killing the Iowa college student now on the stand, claims that armed man showed up at his home and ordered him to hide her body in a cornfield.\par \par \uc2 This was a stunning piece of information that he just came out with moments before they rested this case. And we're going to get to that in just a moment. We're going to go to Matt Finn, he's at the courthouse there, he's been covering this throughout, and we will take you there momentarily.\par \par \uc2 But first, to senior correspondent Claudia Cowan who is on the scene in San Jose with the very latest information on possible motive and suspect in this case. Hi, Claudia.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, as you mentioned, authorities have confirmed that nine people were killed or are now dead, I should say, following this morning's mass shooting at a light rail maintenance facility just a few blocks behind me. That's eight people who were shot and killed, and the gunman who works at the rail yard. Officials have not yet said how he died, but multiple reports say it was suicide.\par \par \uc2 There was a massive law enforcement response this morning when shots rang out at the Valley Transportation Authority maintenance facility just blocks from the county jail and sheriff's headquarters. It services trains and buses that take riders all around \uc2 Santa Clara County\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL DAVIS, DEPUTY, \uc2 SANTA CLARA COUNTY\uc2  SHERRIFF'S OFFICE: There was a rescue operation, so pretty much we formed multiple tactical teams that came into the building to extricate victims out.\par \par \uc2 COWAN (voice-over): Authorities have identified the gunman as Sam Cassidy, a 57-year-old technician at the VTA. And a two-alarm fire at about the same time appears to be connected.\par \par \uc2 Officials are investigating whether he set his house in East San Jose on fire and then went to the VTA facility and began shooting. The House is reportedly filled with hundreds of rounds of ammunition. And at both scenes, authorities have brought in bomb-sniffing dogs and other equipment to check for explosives, booby traps, and other devices that could hurt first responders. But they say there is no immediate threat to public safety.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of this started around 6:34 A.M. local time when police began getting calls of multiple shots fired inside the VTA facility.\par \par \uc2 According to one report, there was a union meeting going on and a possible shift change happening as well. Some 2,000 people work for the Valley Transportation Authority, most of whom are union members, but what if any connection that has to the shooting remains unclear.\par \par \uc2 COWAN (on camera): The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is on the scene helping with what they call a "sizable crime scene". Families are reuniting as we speak, hearing some harrowing accounts of what happened. In the rush to escape, some people fled to nearby rail cars. Police still not releasing critical information in terms of a motive or what kind of weapon was used or how long the gunman had worked for the VTA. So, a lot of questions here.\par \par \uc2 And now, Martha, a second crime scene with this, house that was set on fire. All of this is being investigated, remains a fluid situation. But I can tell you hearts are very heavy here in San Jose today with nine total dead in the nation's latest mass shooting. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Terrible. If you get more details, we'll come right back to you this hour. Claudia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the stunning testimony from the accused killer of Iowa college student Mollie Tibbetts, she was 20 years old. This man now claims that his confession was not the whole truth and that he didn't speak up sooner because armed men had threatened his daughter and her mother.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent Matt Finn live at the courthouse in Iowa where the defense has just rested its case. Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): And Martha, as we are inching closer to the end of the trial portion, it was quite a moment today when Christhian Rivera took to the stand in his own defense. Some members of the jury sat forward into their seat, and we now have some stunning testimony from illegal immigrant, 26-year-old Christhian Rivera.\par \par \uc2 Some of his testimony contradicts what he previously told police in his confession about how 20-year-old Mollie Tibbetts was brutally stabbed to death.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): Rivera claims on the night Tibbetts went missing he took a shower at home. Then, two masked armed men got into his house. They instructed him into his car, and to drive to Mollie Tibbetts town where they circled her on her jog.\par \par \uc2 Rivera claims one of the masked men called the other Jack, which is the last name of Mollie Tibbett's boyfriend. Rivera says he does not know how the two alleged men killed Tibbetts. But afterwards, they all drove to a cornfield, and then those two men walked away.\par \par \uc2 Rivera claims he dumped Tibbetts' body in the cornfield and kept it all a secret because he claims those masked men threatened to harm his daughter and her mother, his ex-girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You drove the two men in your Malibu to the cornfield where you dumped Mollie Tibbetts, correct?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTHIAN RIVERA, SUSPECT, MOLLIE TIBBETTS KILLING CASE (through translator): Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And you're telling us that you drove back to your home alone.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And you happen to find your cell phone and the keys in the trunk of the car.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And these two men just disappeared.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: That's right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN: During interrogation, Rivera was asked at the time if he was covering for anyone, he said no. He now claims that was a lie, saying he was too afraid to admit his version of the truth.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to some of Mollie Tibbetts family on the ground here. And now that the state and the defense has rested, the family tells us that they are satisfied with the prosecution in this case. It could go to the jury tomorrow, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Matt Finn live at the courthouse. Thank you very much, Matt.\par \par \uc2 All right, let's bring in Mark Eiglarsh, criminal defense attorney and former prosecutor. Mark, what do you make of them putting him on the stand and of his stunning story that spilled out today?\par \par \uc2 MARK EIGLARSH, CRIMINAL DEFENSE ATTORNEY, MIAMI: What the defense is doing is what we occasionally see on a football field. We call it a Hail Mary, right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: When your back's against the wall, when you're desperate, when you know defeat is inevitable, you got to do something. So, he took what he could not dispute, scientific evidence proving her blood was in his vehicle, his confession, video evidence showing him at the scene, and he weaved a story that loosely is based upon the facts, but it does not make any sense.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. There's also a video of his car and I would imagine with the technology that we have today, you'd probably be able to figure out whether or not there were two other individuals in that car with him, I would imagine.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 EIGLLARSH: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There's no evidence whatsoever in the course of this case that there were, you know, fingerprints or any other materials in his car that would -- that would prove that there had been anyone else in that car, right?\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: Right. Completely transparent what he's doing here. And with utmost respect, they need to do something, right? The defense lawyers can't twiddle their thumbs, they waive their opening statement, which I thought was ludicrous it's because they really didn't have anything, and they just want to maybe give them some more time to come up with a story.\par \par \uc2 But what also doesn't make sense is, so let's run with it for one second. Two guys who conveniently he can't describe because they're wearing masks. That was great. Somehow target what we learned from the lead detective was a victim who was tantamount to snow white, really, she -- they couldn't find anything negative about her. Her messages were the most kindest messages they've ever seen. It makes no sense, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it doesn't. And then, the detail that he heard one of them call the other one something Jack. And her boyfriend's name is Dalton Jack. I mean, you know, this is like it just feels like it's --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: No, it's perfect. Yes, it's perfectly orchestrated. Except Mr. Rivera, Jack has an alibi because Jack was at work. So, that really doesn't work for you. And you left out the part about stopping the vehicle to help the injured unicorn and nurse him back to health.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's just -- it's incredible. You know, the much had been made of the fact that he came in from Mexico, he talked about the fact that he was brought in by a coyote today. I don't -- you know, why was that relevant at this point for him to be the person saying.\par \par \uc2 He also went back to his 11-hour interrogation, another effort, I would imagine to try to show that he was, you know, mistreated or, you know, there was an issue with reading him his rights and all of those things, anything in there that you think is relevant or useful for the defense.\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: No, I think that these jurors are going to hone in on the evidence. They're going to look at what he said at the time. And also, they're going to see how it doesn't fit. What he's saying does not match. That said, in the interest to being fair, it only takes one juror.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right. That's right.\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: It takes one juror who looks at him and says, I don't know that could happen, I saw that in a cheesy drama once before. I guess it could happen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: And that would be a hung jury, and that would be a victory for the defense. That's what they're hoping for.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that's certainly would. And we'll watch obviously once they begin deliberations to see the nature of any questions that they send back to the judge that might give us some indication about what is on potentially one juror's mind. As we look at this case that looks so clear- cut to all of us following it. We'll see if it goes that way once the jury takes it on.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thank you as always. Good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 EIGLARSH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, also breaking right now, President Biden has changed course on investigating the Chinese origins of COVID-19 after his administration shut down the Pompeo state department probe when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Under a lot of pressure to investigate the origins of COVID-19, President Biden now instructing the Intel community to deliver a report to the White House within 90 days. That gets the United States one step closer to some kind of conclusion potentially on the origins of the global pandemic that claim the lives of 3-1/2 million people around the globe.\par \par \uc2 After it's revealed that his administration had shut down the Pompeo state department's probe which was well underway, including David Asher who we've spoken to here who said that he had concerns that there might be a biowarfare element at work in China and that it could potentially have a link to what happened. And that's what they were trying to get to the bottom of.\par \par \uc2 National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin covering this story for us, joins us now from the Pentagon. Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, the problem is the intelligence community is divided about whether the virus started naturally or in a lab. And that according to the statement issued by President Biden, there is a low to moderate confidence in either a hypothesis.\par \par \uc2 So, President Biden has just issued a statement saying he is unsatisfied with the intelligence he has received so far into the origin of COVID-19. "I have now asked the intelligence community to redouble their efforts to collect and analyze information that could bring us closer to a definitive conclusion, and to report back to me in 90 days."\par \par \uc2 The state department is pushing back on the idea that it shut down the small state department team at the Bureau of Arms Control and Verification that was looking into the origins of the COVID virus.\par \par \uc2 According to state department spokesman Ned Price, "That reporting is incorrect. In February and March of 2021, the team's findings were brief to AVC and Policy Planning policy staff in the new administration. With the report delivered, the work was ended." The National Security Council he says is now taking the lead in the investigation.\par \par \uc2 But members of the disbanded team who I've spoken to, including David Asher suggest otherwise, in a statement to Fox News, Asher defended the investigation and said that at the time, some state department colleagues "were deliberately playing down possible links to China's biological weapons program."\par \par \uc2 Among those at state who Asher says blocked their efforts was Chris Ford, the former acting undersecretary for non-proliferation.\par \par \uc2 He tells me the team's findings were based on "junk science", and he was trying to protect Secretary of State Mike Pompeo and the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Martha, I've known David Asher for years and he had huge successes in exposing the A.Q. Khan network in Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And exposing North Korea's nuclear weapons program. He tells me he welcomed President Biden's statement today.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We've had him here too and interviewed him about this. And we also found, you know, him to be, obviously, has a very extensive background in investigative work. And both Republican and Democrat administrations and, you know, we also found his input to be of great interest.\par \par \uc2 So, Jennifer, thank you very much. Jennifer Griffin, reporting on this story for us.\par \par \uc2 So, K.T. Mcfarland joins us now, former deputy national security adviser for the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 K.T., you know, your thoughts on how this is unfolding. Obviously, there's been a lot of pressure on the Biden White House to look deeper. And I just can't imagine why you wouldn't want to be -- why this wouldn't be a top priority? You've got 3-1/2 million people dead. You've got the prospect that something along these lines could happen again, whether it's intentional or unintentional. Why wouldn't you want this to be one of your top priorities at the White House?\par \par \uc2 K.T. MCFARLAND, FORMER DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Well, I'm sure it is a top priority. Look, I'm not as concerned about shutting down a state department unit as I am.\par \par \uc2 With Jennifer just said about the intelligence community, that there are sort of two versions of events. One that it did come from a lab, one that it didn't come from a lab, and neither of those two hypotheses has a high degree of confidence.\par \par \uc2 So, in other words, they're just guessing. They really don't know. Now, why don't they know? It's because the Chinese never let us in. They never let American scientists in, they stonewalled the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 . The Chinese have had a year to cover up all of the evidence.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not confident or I have a low degree of confidence that the intelligence community is going to find out anything more than they already know. And they already know very little.\par \par \uc2 What we do know is that once the Chinese understood how lethal it was, and how contagious it was because they were seeing it in their own populations in Wuhan that they shut down travel within China, and yet they encouraged travel of international travel from other countries in and out of Wuhan.\par \par \uc2 So, in effect, I think that they were using it and they were taking advantage of it and using it as a biological weapon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND: I don't think they did it intentionally, but there they went.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and you have to wonder when a bad actor or a state actor looks at that situation, you know, there's a learning curve on that, right? When you see the impact of how powerful it is, you know, no battlefield, traveling germ that is moving its way around the world. It's a frightening scenario to consider ever wanting -- you know, we never want to go through that again.\par \par \uc2 In China, they're obviously rattled by the fact that there's a lot more oxygen being put into this story. A piece in the Global Times today which is a state-run newspaper goes after Fauci, says, "U.S. elites denigrate further in morality, and Fauci is one of them. This is a blatant lie, a conspiracy created by U.S. intelligence agencies and the media outlet to slander China, and China has denied it." K.T.?\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND: No, they're getting nervous.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND: But there are a lot of unanswered questions. What was the United States' role? You know, Dr. Fauci has been very careful when he said, well, we didn't fund gain-of-function research. Well, what were we funding in that Wuhan lab? And when by funding something didn't that allow us to have access to other things they were doing. Why were we kept in the dark like everybody else?\par \par \uc2 I just think there are a lot of questions here, not just of China but of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . Just -- and not a Biden thing or a Trump thing, but just the bureaucracy. Why are we spending money in China? Why didn't we use that money in the United States labs?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a great point. And gain-of-function just to remind people is a way that you can make a virus more viral and easier to transmit from person to person. To increase the function of that virus to make it -- make people sicker. It's like something out of a sci-fi movie.\par \par \uc2 Watch this exchange with Senator Kennedy and Dr. Fauci today.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): You gave them money and you said don't do gain- of-function research.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISOR TO PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Correct.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: And they said, we won't.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Correct.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: And you have no way of knowing whether they did or not, except you trust them, is that right?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Well, we generally always trust the grantee to do what they say. And you look at the results --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Have you ever had a grantee and lie to you?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: I cannot guarantee that a grantee has not lied to us because you never know.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Which just makes your point, K.T. Why would you be giving money to a country that you really can't -- you really can't trust? It's not an ally.\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND: No, and why are they doing this kind of research in the first place? I mean, they were turning something like a virus into what a more lethal, a more contagious, a more potent -- a pandemic machine. And why were they doing it? Why were we involved in this? And certainly, why were we involved in it with China? And why did we not have any access to what the Chinese were doing with our money and our research?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: K.T., thank you very much. Great points. Good to see you as always. K.T. McFarland.\par \par \uc2 All right, so, just moments ago, Republican senators sounded the alarm on Capitol Hill on the trillions of dollars in spending that is now igniting inflation across the country. You don't have to hear it from me, you see it every day when you go to the grocery store, or you try to build something with some lumber.\par \par \uc2 "AMERICA REPORTS" co-anchor Sandra Smith is here next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was back in Kansas this weekend and a $100 bill would not fill my ford truck with gasoline.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE, WHITE HOUSE PRINCIPAL DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: President's plan is working. We are growing the economy from the bottom up and the middle out faster than any time in the last 40 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Republicans at odds with the White House's rosy outlook on the economy. They're urging the administration to reign it in or to watch prices on just about everything continue to soar.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has got to stop this reckless spending.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I went through inflation back in the 1970s growing up, and it wasn't a pretty sight.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): It doesn't matter if you're going to the gas pump or the cash register at the grocery store. You are paying more for everything.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): The American people need to understand this is the tip of the iceberg.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joining me now, Sandra Smith, co-anchor of "AMERICA REPORTS", great to have you with us, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, are -- is inflation going to douse this economic recovery?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: BlackRock wrote today that the markets are so fixated on this that they might miss like a major geopolitical event because they're just trying to gauge whether or not this is really going to be the destruction of the consumer and the eventual sell-off in the stock market that so many have been anticipating, considering we've been at record highs for so long.\par \par \uc2 You don't have to look far, you can look at your grocery store, you can look at your local used parking lot -- car store. I mean, car sales are up. The metals that are used to build the cars have gone up. The lumber in the houses, the price of just about everything has gone up, even during the pandemic. And that is real live inflation rearing its ugly head.\par \par \uc2 So, while you've got this free money environment, you've got this high stimulus environment, you've got money billions of dollars getting pumped into the system, you've got a real inflationary environment. But the markets are having a hard time sort of connecting the dots because many think, well, it could just go away.\par \par \uc2 But the Biden administration is standing by. They say it's not a problem, that this is temporary, you know, the price of things some things have gone up, but it won't be here to stay. They are stuck on this spend now tax later sort of environment.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And that's concerning economists on the left and the right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And Jen Psaki said the other day, well, actually, that's not -- it's the Fed's problem if we have inflation. It's not something in our purview, but they're still pushing a $1.9 trillion infrastructure bill, which would bring it to over $6 trillion. I mean, it's impossible to wrap your head around how much money, right?\par \par \uc2 You think about the $1,400 checks that everyone got. Those get eaten up at the gas pump pretty quick. Right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, then you go to the store to buy something and you've got the classic inflate, you know, more money chasing fewer goods, and you've got inflation.\par \par \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  was the economic advisor -- top economic adviser to President Obama. He's a lot -- sending out the alarm bell on this.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: "Now, the primary risk to the U.S. economy is overheating -- and inflation -- to avoid squandering the opportunity, policymakers must accept economic reality."\par \par \uc2 And here is the most important part of this. "The moment has come to move past emergency policies and fight for our country's long-term future." Your thought?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, I mean, he's been sounding the alarm on this for quite some time. To your point, he was the chief economist for the -- for the Obama economic council. He's saying that these are inflationary pressures that we haven't seen in a generation. He used the analogy to your point about it is the fed's job to step in and look at the situation at hand.\par \par \uc2 He said the feds let in the drunks wander around the party. They're supposed to step in and say everybody go home, but they're letting them wander around after having too many cocktails, not putting an end to it.\par \par \uc2 He is warning \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  about more spending from the Biden administration. Democratic administration spend first tax later plan. He says will add more fuel to the inflationary fire.\par \par \uc2 And you don't have to look far for other economists that have been historically on the left that are warning the Biden administration to look closely at this. Jamie Dimon, major corporate CEO. He was a big supporter of Biden in his campaign. He too is warning about the unprecedented amount of spending, what that's going to do to the price of just about everything in our life.\par \par \uc2 Grocery store, head outside, your gas prices. Consumer prices rose at the fastest pace since 2008 in the last report. I mean, who's taking notice of that? Markets seem complacent, though, it's fascinating to see that it's just not budging yet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's like everybody's at the gambling table. They're just going to push few more chips out there, right? Let's see what happens.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A lot of betting going on right now. And \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , no doubt, wants to be the guy who says I told you s, when he sees it fall apart, if indeed it does. But we will see. Thank you, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for sticking around.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see as always, thank you. So government victims suffering from Havana syndrome after mysterious energy attacks call out the Biden administrations what they call disheartening response to what they are going through. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A call for help today from victims of mysterious energy attacks known as Havana syndrome. But it's not just Cuba, two incidents were reported right near the White House grounds. Now, US diplomats and government workers accusing the State Department staffers of denying them medical treatment and rejecting the scientific evidence related to their injuries.\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman joins me now. He's former CIA Chief of Station and a Fox News Contributor. Dan, good to have you with us today. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 So this letter came from 21 individuals. Here's a piece of the letter. It says, we have been disheartened to learn and experience that staff within the department continue to, one, deny employees and injured family members access to proper medical evaluation treatment. Two, reject scientific evidence regarding these injuries. And, three, invalidate our injuries and experiences. What's your take on what's going on here.\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FORMER CIA CHIEF OF STATION: We first detected this Havana syndrome way back in 2017, and we didn't see a whole lot of action that was taken both to treat those who had been subjected to, in some cases, to traumatic brain injury, as well as collect the intelligence to confirm who was behind the attacks.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think what we're seeing now, both from the State Department as well as some of my former CIA colleagues, including Marc Polymeropoulos, who was subjected to an attack in Moscow in December 2017, is that frustration that you rightly highlight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We spoke with Marc about his experience with this. So there's the treating and recognizing the injury to people who are serving their country, and then there's who is doing this and how do we nail down, how to stop it. It's very frightening. It's a silent, silent weapon. How do you think this is traveling around the country, and in China and Russia as well? How is it administered?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Yes. So there are some, I think, more than circumstantial evidence that Russia is behind this. They've been known to be very sophisticated users of microwave technology. The KGB operative in the Kremlin, Vladimir Putin enjoys using asymmetric cloak and dagger espionage. He's used chemical weapons attacks against Sergei Skripal in the UK and his own opposition leader Alexei Navalny in Russia. Let's not forget polonium-210 against an FSB officer in the UK as well.\par \par \uc2 So for those reasons, I think that we have to look at Russia and the potential if they're behind this. And then, the Biden administration is talking about building a stable and predictable relationship with Russia. I'd like to have a little more detail about how we're going to hold them accountable for this and other nefarious acts, like the solar wind cyber attack and the dark side attack as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The President, we now know that he's going to meet with Vladimir Putin in Geneva, in the middle of June. And you rightly point out, they want a more stable relationship with Russia. It's hard to know what that means. I mean, stability is nice but you have to hold people accountable. Do you expect that that President Biden will be tough on Putin in that meeting when it comes to things like these energy attacks?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: So I think this summit is an ideal opportunity to talk about where we find common ground with Russia. There are a few places like arms control and maybe counterterrorism operations, as well as discussion on Afghanistan and North Korea. And, of course, Russia is involved in the negotiations over what looks like another flawed Iran nuclear deal.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, it's important for the President to hold Russia accountable publicly and emphasize that targeting our people, whether it's bounties paid to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , or in this case, microwave attacks on the ellipse, downtown Washington, within view of the of the White House, that's a red line. But if Russia doesn't believe we have the will and the capacity to respond, then we don't deter them. So that's what the President needs to be focused on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you have any level of confidence that we are tracking this down, that we know where these vehicles are, or that we understand the technology of these weapons, or are they just floating around undetected?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: So those are the forensics inside the United States. I think that's certainly happening in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and there's interagency collaboration there. But look at director of CIA, Director Burns, has really focused on this. And this is about collecting the intelligence that will confirm that Russia is behind this, but can't emphasize enough with intelligence. We'll get to a level of confidence, of low, medium or high. And rarely, if ever, do we ever get to even near certainty, and that's going to be the challenge.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a big story. It's very creepy weapon and it's hurting a lot of people more than we know. Thank you very much, Dan. Good to see you, Dan Hoffman.\par \par \uc2 So "Wednesdays with Will" is coming up on a story I think you'll only see here. A high school class called porn literacy an intersectional focus on mainstream porn. Did you take that in high school? I didn't get that one in high school. That's coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESIREE ANDRADE, MURDER VICTIM'S MOTHER: The sad truth is that he has re- victimized me and my family that he has total utter disregard for us victims. You, George Gascon, have put criminals before victims. I truly believed you have confused your role as a district attorney and a public defender.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is Desiree Andrade whose 20-year-old son, Julian, was brutally murdered three years ago, beaten and stabbed and thrown off a cliff. She is helping to kick off the petition campaign to recall Los Angeles District Attorney George Gascon.\par \par \uc2 He has taken the death penalty off the table in most cases and dismissed so called special circumstances charges that can lead to longer sentences. Desiree Andrade says that reduced the possible punishment for her son's killer from life without parole and the death penalty down to 25 years in prison. Gascon says the justice system can't rely "policies that create more victims."\par \par \uc2 And Governor Gavin Newsom will face his recall fight without the support of California's largest state workers union. This is a big deal. The new leader of that union tells the Associated Press, "We're going to run his ass out of office." He will speak with us in just a moment and explain that comment. But first, we're going to go to National Correspondent William La Jeunesse with the very latest from Los Angeles. Hi, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Martha, right. He is angry because Newsome demanded a 9% pay cut from public employees, and the governor thought the state was broke. It's not but some context to those comments. Number one, this is the head of one union where's the president of the statewide SEIU opposes the recall as to most unions, including the big ones like the teachers and the nurses. This union president did not see campaign against Newsome only that he would not support him.\par \par \uc2 And finally, a new poll shows 57% of voters favor keeping Newsom, just 40% want him out. Still Newsome to lose any union support is, as you said, a big deal. Richard Brown told the AP yesterday, "He's going to need support from public sector unions to help fight this recall. When I become president of Local 1000, he can look elsewhere for support. He will not get any from us. He's on his own."\par \par \uc2 What is remarkable, however, is Brown said he would no longer collect dues for political spending. Long a hallmark of the SEIU would spend $75 million last year 99% to Democrat. Here's Brown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LOUIS BROWN, PRESIDENT-ELECT, SEIU LOCAL 1000: On day one, we reduce your dues by 50% and eliminate all political spending by our union. Black Lives Matter fight for 15, open borders immigration and endorsing politicians are not issues for Local 1000 to be involved in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: That's big deal. Timetable for the recall, Martha, we've said it was going to be November, but with these poll numbers, many Democrats now favor moving it up by several months. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: William, thank you very much for laying that out. Let's bring in that labor leader who's refusing to support Governor Newsome, Richard Louis Brown, President-elect of Service Employees International Local 1000, California's largest state workers union. Good to have you with us, sir, today.\par \par \uc2 So, I guess, one of the questions that I have for you is about that pay cut. Are you using this as leverage to get him to give back that money, as he has said that he would? And would that change your opinion -- did?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, thank you for having me. Let me quickly say that I am not speaking on behalf of Local 1000. I haven't been sworn in yet. The official announcement of the results won't happen till May 28. And then, there's a protest period of June 1 to June 10.\par \par \uc2 After I am sworn in, and I assume office is the Local 1000 president, I can officially tell you that -- I'm not going to tell you my whole game plan. But you heard me on video, we will not support Gavin Newsome in this recall effort. You can hold me accountable for that. But again, I am not the president of Local 1000 at the moment.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: understood.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I'm speaking as an American, as a taxpayer, as a public servant in the state of California. And I'm represented by Local 1000. I'm a dues paying member, I'm a job --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you something that William pointed out, you know, that's a very strong statement to say that you will no longer give to any entities, Black Lives Matter. You just reeled off a number of politically- connected organizations. You don't want the union to have any voice in politics, that's refreshing, I think, to some, to a lot of people, probably. Because a lot of people pay their dues, and then they don't like what the union does politically.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: And so you're absolutely right. And as anyone who follows me on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , @rlbrown on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , I have done endless videos. And so, I'm convicted by my words. When I state that politics is dividing our union, Local 1000, that's what I mean. Half of our union are non-members. See, we represent over 96,000 represented employees, but only 53,907 are dues paying members. And the big reason why is, number one, politics.\par \par \uc2 Our union -- half non members are Republicans, libertarians, Green Party or other political parties, and they do not support this use of the Democratic Party. That's dividing our union.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, that's very interesting. We'll see if it has any traction with other unions. It's a bold stand. And if you get sworn in, we'll continue to follow you, Mr. Brown. Thank you very much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we're live in moments at a prep school here in New York City where they're teaching porn literacy and the parents didn't actually know that was happening at school. So we're going to take that on with Will Cain in just a moment after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, here we go again with this stuff. Parents at an elite New York City private school outraged after high school juniors were asked to take a porn literacy course. They brought like 200 kids into the auditorium to have this special presentation that was titled "Pornography Literacy: An Intersectional Focus on Mainstream Porn."\par \par \uc2 And it was held without mentioning to the parents that this was going to happen that day. Some of them complained because they had standardized tests coming up for college and they missed like a practice session to go to this thing. And then, they got in there and they were sort of caught off guard by some of the explicit terms and the pictures they were shown. Will Cain is an expert on this so he's standing by. We're going to talk to him in a moment but, first, to Correspondent Aishah Hasnie live outside at Manhattan's Columbia Prep.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon to you, Martha. You know what, what a story this is. The head of Columbia Grammar & Prep is now apologizing to parents here. But some parents say it's simply not enough. So here's what happened.\par \par \uc2 Some of these students teenager, 16 to 17-year-olds enrolled in a course on health and sexuality. Instead, the kids got a class on porn. Now, these are teaching slides here obtained by the New York Post but not verified by Fox yet. And we just want to warn you some of these images and language that I'm going to be talking about, they are graphics.\par \par \uc2 So according to The Post, the class delve into everything from statistics that show straight women have fewer orgasms, to the most searched porn terms online words like Korean stepmom and massage. One student tells suppose we were all so shocked and mortified. We were like, why are they doing this? Why do they think it's OK? So the class is taught by Justine Ang Fonte, who says on her own website that her teaching method is influenced by Kimberle Crenshaw, as you know, a well-known scholar of critical race theory.\par \par \uc2 Now, Columbia's Head of School is apologizing in a statement shared by The Post. He writes, "In this case, the speaker did not align with our unique CGPS mission. And for this, I apologize." But some parents say it doesn't go far enough, it's just a symptom of a bigger problem.\par \par \uc2 They've allegedly created this anonymous \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account to speak out about these new ideologies. One parent telling The Post, "It's not about this one class, it's about the whole radical direction the school is going into." We've reached out to both the teacher and the school, haven't heard anything back just yet. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Aishah, thank you very much. Here with me now for "Wednesdays with Will," well, can Co-Host of "Fox and Friends Weekend" hosted the Will Cain Podcast. I mean --\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, HOST, "FOX AND FRIENDS WEEKEND": You're going to call me an expert again? This is what you're going to do, you're going to introduce me as the expert on pornography today?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, I meant education, school, students, things like that, clearly