rents that are afraid of "being canceled."\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Parents are going to have to decide to stand up and quit worrying about their career or their social standing, and quit sacrificing their children to this nonsense. You cannot just ride along on a wave of platitudes and serve your children up on the altar of this current radical religion. It is, and as Aishah pointed out, connected to critical race theory and all of the other radical theories that are going on in our society.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, it's worth pointing out, there's a booming business attached to this. That teacher had a private consulting firm, the professor, she supposedly learned this from I'm sure has a nice tenure up at Columbia College, who has best selling books about this stuff. So there are people making big time money on everyone else's desire to not get canceled.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's almost like they're laughing and tempting these parents, like let's see how much we can put in front of them, and if they won't walk. Really quick, play the John Cena soundbite and we'll get Will to react on the other side. He said that Taiwan was a country and then he had to do this.\par \par \uc2 JOHN CENA, ACTOR (THROUGH TRANSLATION): And I made a mistake, I have to say this right now. And it's very, very, very, very important. I love and respect China and the Chinese people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: John Cena, NBA, you know, who else, right? Corporate America, oh, China, I'm so sorry. I said something isn't right.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: China has bought our silence, they've bought us off. They have completely showed us that money doesn't make you strong. Money makes you weak. Either that or we're going to start picking celebrities and people that we idolize, that have strong principles and backbone. They can stand up to somebody else saying, you know what, you're not going to get your money if you don't say what we want you to say.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Will Cain, always good to see, Will. Thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 That's "The Story" of this Wednesday, but as always, "The Story" goes on. I'll see you back here tomorrow. Your World with Neil is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210527eh5q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Administration Facing A Whole Host Of Problems At Once From COVID Crisis To Gas Shortages, Immigration And Concerns On Economy But President Biden Says Everything Is Under Control; People Confused After Last Week's Announcement By CDC Saying Americans Who Are Fully Vaccinated Can Now Ditch Off Their Masks, Yet Some Who Wants To Wear It Can Still Have It; President Biden Weighed In On Israel's Counterattack To \uc2 Hamas\uc2  In Gaza And Said Israel Has The Right To Defend Itself; Squad Members Slam Biden Over Support For Israel; Prince Harry Bashes First Amendment\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Perino, Jesse Watters, Dagen McDowell, Juan Williams, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 7,552 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST (on camera): And thank you for being here. Thanks for being here. I'll be back here again tomorrow for Neil. And you can catch me along with \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  every week day at 1 p.m. Eastern Time for America reports, 1 to 3 p.m. Eastern Time. That is it for us. "THE "FIVE starts now.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST (on camera): Hello, everyone. I'm Dana Perino along with Greg Gutfeld, Jesse Watters, Dagen McDowell, and Juan Williams. It's five o'clock in New York City. This is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 President Biden trying to project confidence while some inside the White House are worried what lies ahead. Reports piling up about concerns over the economy. Political allies are faulting the White House for mixed messaging on the recovery. There are also concerns about growing inflation.\par \par \uc2 And Axios is reporting some Democrats and economists think the president's spending plans could be too big. and the gas crisis the Biden administration told Americans would soon go away is still happening.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, D.C., 71 percent of stations are out of gas and other states are still experiencing shortages. So while warning signs may be flashing, President Biden is telling Americans there's nothing to worry about.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: We've gone from stagnation to an economy that is growing faster than it has in nearly 40 years. We've gone from anemic job creation to record creation for more -- for a new administration. None has ever created these many jobs in this time frame. Progress is undeniable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO (on camera): Greg, then when you hear your allies are complaining about your messaging, that usually actually points to a fact problem, not a messaging one.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS C