thought there would be. We're only at a vaccination rate of 35 percent. OK, listen to what Dr. Fauci said in December, Larry.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, WHITE HOUSE CORONAVIRUS TASK FORCE: The estimate is that you'll need about seven percent, maybe 75 percent of the people in the country vaccinating to get that umbrella of herd immunity that'll get us really on the road. Very close to being normal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That was not the case, Larry. That doesn't appear to be true.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: That's right. I mean, Martha, I didn't mean to interrupt before except to cheerlead Marty Makary, who had the whole story, right. Actually, from the beginning of my new program, back in February or early March, when we first started, he was completely right. And also, because my very good friend Tony Fauci, nonetheless, counted vaccinations, but he didn't count people who had already come through and had COVID.\par \par \uc2 So, this, you know, so called natural immunity process, when you add the two up, which is what Makary said was so important, including the asymptomatic, you're there, your herd immunity for all intents and purposes. So, this is a combination of science. It's a combination of politics, but it's also -- you got to add the economic zone in it. You couldn't have sustained this longer.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think we went way too far with the shutdowns, and that too, is largely a function of the big blue states until, you know, Florida, Texas, and the sunbelt started to rebel and the Rocky Mountain States, and now you see the economy booming. We should have done this many months ago. Look, as you know, former President Trump would have done this, you know, mastery set at least several months ago, OK.\par \par \uc2 But I'm just saying process wise, you have to take a look at the economics, at the science, at the psychology at the schools in education, including, by the way at the women who had to stay home because the kids were in school. So, there are lots of factors, and the CDC should not be the person or the institution to make these final decisions. They shouldn't have the final call, that call should be reserved for the President.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think you're absolutely right. And I just want to remind everybody that there was a study, I think back in January in California, that showed that a year prior in the January of the prior year, a lot of people's blood tests who gave blood showed that they had COVID, right?\par \par \uc2 So, the numbers were much higher than anyone ever knew in the country, of how many people actually had already had it. And that is probably one component of why we seem to have reached this herd immunity much earlier than, than Anthony Fauci had expected. Larry, great to have you here. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Thanks, Martha. Anytime.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to see you. Have a great show. You bet. So, here now a Former Teacher and Civil Rights Attorney Leo Terrell, and Jessica Tarlov, Head of Research at Bustle Digital Group and Fox News Contributor. Great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, I want to talk a little bit about this vaccination question and the enormous pressure that is being put on people to get vaccinated. We know that President Biden said the rule is now simple, get vaccinated or wear a mask until you do, the choice is yours. And yet, back in September of 2020, just last fall, the Vice President said don't trust any vaccine that the Trump administration comes up with. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS, VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He's looking at an election coming up in less than 60 days. And, and he's grasping for whatever he can get to pretend that he has been a leader on this issue when he is not. I would not trust Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Leo Terrell, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 LEO TERRELL, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: You know, it's really sad, Martha, because the bottom line is Donald Trump was absolutely right. Operation Warp Speed is nothing short of a miracle. And Kamala Harris, demonized President Trump, demonize the whole concept of the vaccine, and look where we are now. But for Donald Trump, we wouldn't be in the position right now, where we have a vaccine. And I think it's the mixed message. The Democrats have used the vaccine, the pandemic for political power, political gain.\par \par \uc2 The reason why you saw that announcement yesterday was because the public has gotten aware of the fact that they're sending those wrong messages. And I think now you're seeing the blowback. And I think now the Biden administration is now trying to correct something that was known a while back, this country should have been open. The people got hurt the most are the school kids, the kids who should have been in school in blue states.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jessica, what do you say?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think it's a little bit more complicated than Biden and Kamala Harris, bad Trump good. I've always given credit to Operation Warp Speed where the credit is due definitely got us started on this path.\par \par \uc2 I think the good people at \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna, \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 , \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  are the real heroes here, obviously, and the public health officials and the military who have worked to make sure that we can get a good roll out and that we now have so many millions of people who are vaccinated and can safely return to normal life.\par \par \uc2 But what Kamala Harris I think was talking about and she did clarify a bit more about that, that it wasn't like Donald Trump invented this vaccine and it's bad. It's that Donald Trump spread a massive amount of bad information about this disease including saying that it wasn't a big deal that it was an airborne when we know from a private conversation with a reporter on tape, that he didn't know that it was airborne, and he knew it was a lot more serious. So, that's what she's talking about. But I just wanted to add, we can go back on the timeline on that we can compare it on the timeline --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead. Real quick. Go ahead, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh, I just wanted to say that another reason that we got the mask mandate, the mask mandate lifted is that we want people who are vaccine hesitant to know that there's light at the end of the tunnel, and that if you do get vaccinated, you don't have to wear a mask anymore. People don't want to wear their masks. And now there's a clear line between getting vaccinated and living mask free.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, it also turns out that we knew quite some time ago that you couldn't really get it outside. The chances were incontestable that you needed to wear a mask outside ever, which I think a lot of us felt was a common sense reality that we might have surmised quite some time ago. I want to ask you guys about Randi Weingarten and about the also the decision to reopen schools and the real about face that we saw on that this week.\par \par \uc2 Here's this is from the Wall Street Journal editorial board, sort of moving the dial further about some of what was in her suggestions in her speech about something that many of us believe is a good idea, which is summer school and additional tutoring to help the learning loss that happened over the course of this year.\par \par \uc2 And the journal writes, "No doubt unions will now demand renumeration for the time they have spent tap to spend making up for all the last instruction. The pandemic has been a catastrophe for millions of children. But it has turned out very well from his Weingarten and her union." Leo.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: I'll tell you right now, Randi Weingarten is nothing more than a democratic operative, the millions of dollars they've given to the Democratic Party, her half a million dollar salary. I saw the interview she did with you, she's done an about face. The reason is very simple, they cannot keep the schools closed anymore, the science doesn't dictate it. Her 15 minutes of fame, and Dr. Fauci's 15 minutes fame are over. It's over, open the economy up, open up the schools.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How much damage, Jessica, has been done to the union's because of how long they kept so many students at home.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think some real damage has been done to them. That obviously goes for public schools and private schools are in hot water as well. And people are looking for alternative paths, whether that's creating permanent pods for their kids, looking at charter schools, homeschooling, we've seen a huge surge in that because parents know that they can be relied upon to be there every day for their kids. I don't want to demonize all these teachers, Martha, I know you have a lot of teachers in your family and are very proud of that. And I'm just glad that the decision was reversed and our kids can get back to school because they need it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, there's been a big divide a gulf between the teachers who have worked pretty hard in these conditions. And some of them who didn't want to go back and still don't want to go back for various reasons in the unions. Jessica, thank you. Leo, thank you. Great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we want to turn to the escalating violence that's going on between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  sowing further divisions at home between factions of the Democratic Party, very divided on this issue. And should kids returning to school this fall be taught critical race theory. So, once they get back? What about these curriculums that are becoming more and more pervasive across the country? You need to know what's in these curriculums so that you'll be aware story -- THE STORY continues right after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I don't think we would think we believe that educating the youth and next leaders of the future leaders of the country on systemic racism is indoctrination.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BRAD SCHNEIDER (D-IL): Israel is rightfully responding to these attacks, protecting its citizens and defending its borders.\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): Many members of Congress have instead fallen back on a blanket statement defending Israel's airstrikes against civilians under the guise of self-defense.\par \par \uc2 REP. JOSH GOTTHEIMER (D-NJ): Even though we may not always agree about Israel's policies, we should be very clear that nothing, nothing justifies a terrorist organization, firing rockets at our ally.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): If we're scared to stand up to the incarceration of children in Palestine, it's because maybe it'll force us to confront the incarceration of children here on our border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you see what's going on. Democrats are divided in their response to escalating violence between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . The White House today called the loss of life on both sides incredibly tragic, yet the progressive left seems to be blaming America's key ally in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 While those divisions play out in Washington, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants continue to fire rockets from Gaza, Israel responding to those airstrikes with Iron Dome missiles and preparing for a possible ground invasion of Gaza. Let's go to Correspondent Trey Yingst on the ground with the very latest near the border of Israel and Gaza. Good afternoon, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon, as we speak right now the Israeli military is shelling different positions along the Gaza Strip. We've heard artillery from where we're at right now in front of one of the units that also participated last night in a ground and air assault against Gaza that the Israeli military says more than 450 bombs and shells going into the Gaza Strip and the destruction today that we have seen is significant. From homes to tunnels, thousands of people are homeless.\par \par \uc2 The strikes inside Gaza overnight a significant escalation by Israel. Not surprising though, because the Prime Minister today said his operation is not over. It will continue. The Palestinian death toll tonight, more than 120 people total that number expected to rise. On the Israeli side, these artillery units will continue throughout the night. Sirens blaring throughout the day as rocket fire is incoming. The death toll for Israel stands at nine people, one of them a soldier.\par \par \uc2 And there is a massive coordination underway on both sides of the border, as both sides do expect fighting to continue. But it's not just here that's erupting. We have seen protests today along the Lebanon-Israel border and the Syria, excuse me the Jordanian-Israel border. And additionally, we saw rockets fired from Syria into Israel. So, you have all these moving parts taking place, as well as massive clashes in the West Bank that took place earlier today. Leaving 10 people dead, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey Yingst on an increasingly complicated situation by the hour. Trey, thank you very much. So, critical race theory going beyond the classroom, one organization is criticizing the idea that people of different backgrounds should be treated the same. Senator Tommy Tuberville and Scholar Allen Guelzo, share their thoughts on the ideological teachings behind all of this which you need to become aware of, that is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Big debate in this country going on over teaching critical race theory in America schools. And that debate has reached the White House, of course. So, here's what press secretary Jen Psaki said about educating children about what's known as systemic racism. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It sounds very mysterious and dangerous. Although, I don't think that I don't think we would think we believe that educating the youth and next leaders of the future leaders of the country on systemic racism is indoctrination that's actually responsible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, she says it's a good idea it would be responsible to teach CRT. The spread of this theory in schools has sparked controversies across the country. It has prompted Republicans in Congress to speak out against it. In a moment, we'll speak with GOP Senator Tommy Tuberville of Alabama. He explains why he believes that teachings such as the 1619 Project are fanning the flames in his words of division in our country.\par \par \uc2 First, let's bring in Heritage Foundation Visiting Scholar and Princeton University Professor Allen Guelzo. Allen, great to have you with us today. Let's start with your thoughts on what Jen Psaki had to say at the White House about teaching CRT being the responsible way to train the future leaders of our country.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN GUELZO, HERITAGE FOUNDATION VISITING SCHOLAR: Well, I suspect that the President's spokesperson probably thinks of critical race theory is simply an intelligent way to talk a lot about race. And it is, but Critical Race Theory may also be the most irresponsible way to think about race in America. And I think that's really because critical race theory is a subset of critical theory, which has got long roots in Western philosophy. Back to Emmanuel Kant in the 1790s. Kant lived at the end of a century, known as the enlightenment, the age of reason.\par \par \uc2 But he feared that experience had shown that reason was inadequate to give shape to our lives. There had to be a way of knowing things that went beyond reason. And for him, that meant developing a theory of being critical of reason, hence, critical theory. The problem was that critical theory got away, it instead justified ways of appealing to some very unreasonable things as explanations, things like race, nationality, class, and they gave us Karl Marx and Jim Crow, and every dictatorship in between, that's especially true about race.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I want to put something up on the screen. This is put out today by the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 , as they -- as so many institutions are doing right now try to come up with an equity paper or plan. I think it's about 90 pages long. And in it, this is the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 . And it says, "Seeking to treat everyone the same, ignores the historical legacy of disinvestment and deprivation through historical policy and practice of marginalizing and minoritizing communities. What do you think about that Professor?\par \par \uc2 GUELZO: Well, I think it points in two dangerous directions. One is that the American founder was based upon equality, not equity. Those are two very different things. It was based I think, in the largest sense on reason itself on, on natural law on logical propositions like you find in the Declaration of Independence or the Gettysburg Address.\par \par \uc2 The Constitution itself is a written logical document, built on rules of law and evidence and due process. There have been Americans who have resisted this with the American Confederacy, for instance, the Southern Confederacy was built on a similar kind of critical race theory and equity. But then it was about giving equity to white Southern slaveholders who rejected the declaration of the Constitution and sneered at reason and law.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, modern tyrannies have been founded on (INAUDIBLE) of equity and will and power. And is, is this where we really want to go? Do we really want to go into these non-rational divisions of people based on race and language and ethnicity? Is that really the direction that we want to take the nation? I don't think that it is. At that point, I have to say, you tell me who is being responsible and who isn't?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Professor, thank you. It's worth a close look, and we appreciate you helping us understand it today. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 GUELZO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I'm joined now by Republican Senator Tommy Tuberville of Alabama. He gave a speech earlier this week slamming "progressive efforts" to indoctrinate our children. Thank you very much for being here, Senator. You heard Jen Psaki at the White House podium, she said there's there this is not indoctrination. She said it's elevating our next generation leaders to understand systemic racism in the United States.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOMMY TUBERVILLE (R-AL): Well, we shouldn't be using education to submit the ideas inequality. I've been in teaching and education for 40 years, and that's where we teach kids about this country and about how we got here, and about the future, and not about things such as racism.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, there's racism everywhere, Martha. I've been involved and I've watched it, we work through it. But the thing about this is, we cannot be driving things such as 1619 theory into the heads of kids across this country, we should be teaching them history and civics. Things that they need to understand about how this great country got here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: One of the tenants that goes into critical race theory curriculums, and I spent part of today looking at the recommended curriculums that go along with these programs, put out by the Pulitzer Foundation and others. If you're a teacher and you want to teach 1619, then you go to these websites. And then, I also looked at Kennedy, who's one of the writers who is very central to anti-racism, which is the language that's being used around this.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to put this quote up on the board, because this is what he said in a conversation about how to be an anti racist. He says, "Most white people would say, 'I'm not racist.'" And if they're saying that, and if they're saying and they're swearing that they're not racist, then chances are they're being racist. What do you say to that, sir?\par \par \uc2 TUBERVILLE: Well, the biggest thing is, I mean, you can you can look at that any way you want to. But the biggest thing and the thing that I did as a football coach for all those years, is you try to bring people together as people, not as different color, different races, different religions.\par \par \uc2 The thing that we try to do is we divide people, and then we say we're going to bring them together. We're all Americans, and we if we don't start looking at that, in that frame of mind, we're not ever going to get over this racism. It's not going to happen. We just keep dividing ourselves every day.\par \par \uc2 And it's usually starts in the media. Now, they're trying to get it into education. Now, they've been doing this for years, Martha. This had now started. I've been complaining about it for 10 to 15 years. It's just come to the point now where people are starting to recognize it. Parents are starting to recognize it. And now that that's happened, I think you're going to hear people start coming out saying, we're not going to allow this to happen to our kids.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I remember listening to then Senator Barack Obama at the convention in Boston in 2004. And he gave a speech where he said, we're not a black America, we're not a white America, we're the United States of America. And that notion of pulling us together, as you just discussed, seems to be lost in this current conversation about something that Jen Psaki says, you know, appears dangerous and scary but it's really not.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you. Thank you very much for being here. Good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 TUBERVILLE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So here's the story that you're only going to see here. The Biden administration approving a major offshore wind farm, but the President's latest green energy push maybe playing into the hands of one of our biggest adversaries. What you need to know about this wind farm, coming up. A story exclusive, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The Biden administration moving on is clean energy promises approving the nation's first offshore wind farm. The $2.8 billion project is going to include dozens of turbines located about 14 miles off the coast of Massachusetts. Correspondent Molly Line in Hyannis, Massachusetts with this story. Hey, Molly.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Martha. This is the first commercial scale offshore wind farm approved here in the United States, approved by the Biden administration. It's slated for developing the waters off of Martha's Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 And one of the big concerns here looking at this is that it could potentially cause harm to the region's robust fishing industry and also the environment. The Biden administration bills the Vineyard Wind Project is a major milestone in the country's journey toward a clean energy future, approved to put up to up to 84 turbines in place.\par \par \uc2 Development leaders vow that thousands of jobs will be created and power supply to hundreds of thousands of homes and businesses. The CEO says this approval is about more than a single project.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARS PEDERSEN, CEO, VINEYARD WIND: This is about an industry, an industry that is going to revitalize waterfronts up and down the eastern seaboard, create well paying jobs and delivering clean affordable energy to households.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE: Despite agreements made by the company to provide compensation to fishermen for potential losses, some of them make a living in New England waters fear that Vineyard Wind will impact jobs and fishing grounds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARED FERREIRO, FISHERMAN: You know, the last thing we need to do is mess with the ocean bottom right now and ocean traffic with a ginormous construction zone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE: (Inaudible), the commercial fishing industry, have criticized not only Vineyard Wind, but also plans for the larger offshore wind industry, arguing those in authority care more about multinational business and energy politics than the environment domestic food sources for US citizens.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNIE HAWKINS, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, RODA: It's more a question of going from zero to this huge, huge, huge scale on a technology that for Us waters is really untested what the effects on the ecosystem will be, what the effects to coastal communities and to our fishing businesses will be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE: The base state's Republican Governor Charlie Baker has spoken out and praised Vineyard Wind as a groundbreaking project that will create jobs and provide affordable renewable energy, but former President Donald Trump under his administration, the Vineyard Wind faced some permitting delays, slammed this big news arguing that wind is an incredibly expensive form of energy that kills birds, affects the sea, ruins the landscape and creates disasters for navigation. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Molly, thank you very much. Molly Line in Hyannis today. Texas Republican Congressman and Ranking Member of the Foreign Affairs Committee Michael McCaul joins me now. He was part of a hearing with John Kerry the other day who is the Climate Czar where he questioned him about forced labor in China.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that is brought up by this wind farm plan, congressman, is the amount of materials that are required to build these enormous blades. These blades are the size of football fields in some cases.\par \par \uc2 Craig Rucker says, how many millions of tons steel, copper, lithium, cobalt rare earth elements, concrete, petroleum-based composites and other raw materials could be required for this manufacturer? He goes on to say, much of it would be done in China or in other countries by Chinese owned companies in terms of the manufacturing. What do you think of this project?\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): Well, look, we have wind farms in Texas as well but I think the environmental damage that was talked about in the oceans to go forward with this project. In addition, you know, the American worker is the loser in this energy policy of the Biden administration, which is quite perplexing to me like when he shut down the Keystone pipeline, and yet John Kerry testified that pipe is the best way to deliver energy.\par \par \uc2 And yet, they shut that down. And then the Russians attacked \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2 , at the same time they are allowing the Russians to complete \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  from Russia to Europe. So I am very confused by this policy.\par \par \uc2 What I am most concerned about, Martha, is when we finally became energy independent after so many years, from OPEC in the Middle East, and President Trump helped us usher into that position. My state, the number one exporter of LNG, which is clean burning, and I asked the secretary, why aren't we doing more things like that?\par \par \uc2 What their policy is essentially doing is hurting the American worker, the welders for the Keystone Pipeline, but it's also taking us to a policy being now not energy independent but more dependent now on the Middle East. This is going backwards, not forwards, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it took so many years for us to claim that mantle of energy independence and it changes the geopolitical dynamic with the Middle East. It makes us less dependent on countries that we really don't want to be dependent on.\par \par \uc2 But one thing that shocks me is that, we've removed the head of the EPA, says that we've removed the cost benefit analysis that goes into wind farms and other forms of alternative energy. So it doesn't matter if it doesn't really make financial sense as long as it checks the box of moving in that direction. Does that make sense to you?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, yes. You know, look, the price point is still not there on solar or on these wind farms. It's very good for fossil fuels. This administration is very, very much against fossil fuels. They want to eliminate it. But we have to have a transition here and it has to make sense from a profit standpoint. And as you pointed out as well, a lot of these solar panels are coming out of Xinjiang Province in China, which my bill will declare them guilty of genocide.\par \par \uc2 So the irony is, this administration is importing windmill technology and solar panel technology supply chain out of China, in the very province where they are committing genocide with slave labor of the Uighur Muslims. This is a very paradoxical policy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes, I mean, solar panels, to a lot of people sounds like a great idea. If they're manufactured by slave labor in China, you got to think twice about whether or not that makes sense for America. Thank you very much, congressman. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So it seems like almost every day a state governor is getting rid of the extra unemployment benefits because they're trying to get people in their state to go back to work and fill the jobs that are so needed. Steve Hilton has taken a look at this. He says it's a sign of a healthy republic that these governors are responding this way. He's next\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Two more state is added to the list, Alaska and West Virginia are going to eliminate the extra federal unemployment benefits that have kept a lot of people