over that extra money. And they need to do that because that's the right thing for them to do. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 HILTON: What is this? I've got a centrally planned economy, like in Eastern Europe, where the government is giving the businesses money to pay -- it's crazy. Now that we have such a successful vaccination program, we can open up the economy. We've been saying this for weeks now and all of that, the economy work as it should be.\par \par \uc2 Remember where it was before all this, with record low unemployment, businesses hiring, productivity rising, everything was going great when you let the economy do its thing, things work out well, rather than have the government try and figure it all out from the center.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. This is Jamie Dimon, let's play this, at Wall Street Journal CEO Council. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON, CEO, JP MORGAN: Firemen, police, sanitation, hospitals, retail pharmacies, banks, groceries, it's 40 million of them go to work. And yes, kind of they'd like at home and stuff like that. It doesn't work really well. So, it does not work for younger people. It doesn't work for those who want to hustle. It doesn't work in terms of spontaneous idea generation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Steve, final thought on that.\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Yes. I mean, look, he's got a point. And I think that that interaction between people is often what leads to the kind of creativity, that leads to great breakthroughs and innovation. And also just on a social level, people like to be around other people. So a lot of people are saying this is here with us forever, people working from -- I don't think it's here to stay. I think people want to be with others.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I totally agree. Steve, thank you. Great to see you. Steve Hilton, thanks for coming in today.\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up, we're going to talk a little bit about Prince Harry because he is speaking out again, slamming his family for the way that he was raised and saying that he was miserable, and he wanted to get out of there, all of that. We're going to bet that around with Dominic Green when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Prince Harry is bad mouthing his family yet again. This time, he's comparing his childhood to "The Truman Show" and being in a zoo. He spoke out in a new podcast, Harry says he plans to break the so called cycle of suffering and genetic pain that was passed on to him by his father. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY, DUKE OF SUSSEX: I don't think we should be pointing the finger or blaming anybody, but certainly, when it comes to parenting, if I've experienced some form of pain or suffering because of the pain or suffering that perhaps my father or my parents had suffered, I'm going to make sure that I break that cycle so that I don't pass it on.\par \par \uc2 Basically, there's a lot of genetic pain and suffering that gets passed on anyway. And we as parents, we should be doing the most of what we can to try and say, you know what, that happened to me, I'm going to make sure it doesn't happen to you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Dominic Green, Deputy Editor of the Spectator World Edition. Dominic, what's your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 DOMINIC GREEN, DEPUTY EDITOR, SPECTATOR WORLD EDITION: Well, I'm not a doctor, Martha, but it sounds to me like Prince Harry has a bad case of mumbo jumbo. I'm not sure what the genetic pain part is. Absolutely, Prince Harry had great amounts of suffering in his childhood growing up in "The Truman Show," as he said, and losing his mother at a young age. And it's great that he's trying to overcome these things. But I don't quite understand why he has to do it in public.\par \par \uc2 Everybody has thoughts about how their parents did things. But most people tend to phone them up, rather than announce it to the entire world. And this is the behavior of a sulky teenager, but this guy is 36.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I was struck by the same thing, you know, and I want to play another soundbite by this when he talks about his early 20s, which was definitely kind of a wild period for him based on what we saw in the newspapers. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY: I was in my early 20s, I was thinking of like I don't want this job. I don't want to be here. I didn't want to be doing this. Look what it did to my mom, how am I ever going to settle down and have a wife and a family when I know that it's going to happen again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I love in the first soundbite when he says I don't want to point fingers, you know, but -- and we've also heard how much he respects and admires the Queen and yet, he threw her under the bus as a mother because he basically says she did this to my father and then he did it to me. It doesn't appear that there's any reconciliation of any sort going on here. And if there were, any seeds of that happening, I think this would pretty much dash those, wouldn't it?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: It would. He's being a genetic pain in the neck for the rest of his family. There was this moment of what looked like reconciliation after the funeral of the Duke of Edinburgh a few weeks ago. But getting the dirty laundry out and giving it yet another washing, it's a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 And you have to think, as Harry has realized life is a long business and there are all kinds of second chances. But you don't get second chances, for instance, if you treat your family so badly that they have to distance themselves from you in order to protect themselves. And that is what the royal family in London are doing including, of course, Harry's brother, Prince William.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How's this all playing in London? Whose side are do people seem to be on?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, people in Britain seem generally to be on the side of the queen, but also to be strongly on the side of William and his family. Because William Kate and so on, look to be a fairly sensible hard working family who, despite everything Harry was saying just then, actually have managed to get married, and actually do seem to have made it work, and made kind of private lives for themselves, despite being in the Royal Truman Show.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, if someone said if they wanted to leave the country and have their quiet life in California, they appear to be doing not that at this point. Dominic, thank you very much. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 That's "The Story" for this Friday, May 14th. Happy Friday, everybody. "The story" continues. We'll see you back here on Monday. Have a great weekend. Your World with Charles Payne in for Neil today coming up.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210515eh5e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Gov. Mike DeWine\uc2  (R-OH)\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne \par \uc2 1,198 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Folks, now I want you to take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We don`t see in the data unemployment benefits as a major driver in bumpy, bumpy jobs numbers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, try telling that to all the states that are ditching those extended benefits right in the middle of a labor squeeze, including Ohio, where we`re glad to have that state`s governor with us.\par \par \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  joins us now to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 You have joined now, I think the count is 19 states. You`re going to drop the unemployment benefits, among other things. And I was reading some headlines from the Ohio media. And they seem pretty shocked, because you`re going to lift all the COVID health -- health orders effective June -- in June sometime.\par \par \uc2 What made you come to this decision?\par \par \uc2 GOV. MIKE DEWINE\uc2  (R-OH): Well, two different decisions you just referenced.\par \par \uc2 As far as the unemployment, we just kept hearing from employers that they could not find work. And it seemed to us that one of the reasons is that the government has this additional $300 in there. And this made sense early on. At first, it was $600. Then it was down to $300. We had people who were hurting. They needed the -- needed the money.\par \par \uc2 People could not -- did not have the vaccine. It was not available. But we`re well into this now. We`re coming out of this.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: And we need to come out economically very, very strong.\par \par \uc2 And people have the ability now to get the vaccine. And we set the date 40- some days, I think, ahead, and said, look, you can go get the vaccine, and we will move forward.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Now, the most recent data I have for your state in terms of unemployment benefits for -- with dependent, would be $647. You top that with 300 bucks, and the weekly income of 940, you can see where someone sitting there logically would say, I`m making the same amount of money, but without the hassle that comes along with having to go to work.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not an indictment on people, is it?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: No.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I mean, the media is suggesting that these red state governors are heartless, how dare they, that this is a billion dollars they have taken from people who desperately need it. You must hear this yourself.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Well, look, it`s not the people`s fault.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people are making an economic decision. It`s a rational decision. And, in some cases, they`re better off still economically staying at home. So, why shouldn`t they stay at home?\par \par \uc2 We`re just at a different point now. We`re well into this. As I said, we`re coming out of it. Ohio`s coming out very strong. Our unemployment is low compared to the national average. We have plenty of vaccine now, plenty of opportunity for people. If they`re concerned about their safety and about going back, they can get the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, we just felt it was time to move ahead.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Of course, to that point, of the 19 states, all but three have a lower unemployment rate than the national. Yours is 4.7. The country is 6.1.\par \par \uc2 What`s the next move in terms of trying to help people out to get them out of the house, get them back to work? Because we know that each single day, it`s tough on small businesses, and it`s also tough on folks who sit at home, because their -- their appeal as employee -- employees doesn`t get better.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Well, we have plenty of jobs out there. We have a lot of jobs out there.\par \par \uc2 They can go up to OhioMeansJobs, and over half the jobs are better than $50,000-a-year jobs. There`s a lot of opportunities out there for people, whatever -- whatever skill set they have.\par \par \uc2 We have also put a real emphasis, since I became governor, in upscaling people`s skill sets. I mean, what we believe is that everybody needs to live up to their God-given potential. And what we should stand for, all of us should stand for is just opportunity...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: ... and giving people the opportunity and then giving them the ability to get the tools that they need to move forward. So, that`s what we`re focused on.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK, so, in the beginning, we talked about the introduction.\par \par \uc2 Two things here. Let`s talk now about the mask -- the mask mandate. You`re ditching that. And you`re also -- you have got this lottery to try to help -- or get more folks vaccinated. Tell us about this.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Well, the most important thing we can do now to move our economy forward and to protect human life and to get back to normal is to get more people vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 We`re not going to make anybody get vaccinated. We have got, in Ohio, about 42, 43 percent of the total population that has now been vaccinated. We have also got a group that doesn`t -- is never going to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 But my wife and I have been traveling around. We have been to 40-some sites where people are being vaccinated, and really just talking to people and understand. And it`s clear that there are some people out there who are just kind of ambivalent about it, it`s not a high priority, but they will get it.\par \par \uc2 And so I felt that, if we put forward this -- it`s not technically a lottery under Ohio law. It`s going to be -- we`re calling it a drawing. But we`re going to do it for five straight weeks, and people -- anybody who has been vaccinated, anybody who has been vaccinated will be eligible to win a million dollars. And we will do that five separate times.\par \par \uc2 We`re also doing scholarships for those who are 17 and younger who`ve been vaccinated, and they can get a full scholarship to one of Ohio`s great state colleges, and we will pay room, board, tuition, the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a lot of fun. We`re already starting to see people walk into vaccination sites and mention the fact that: Hey, I`m here to -- I`m here to win the money.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, a million dollars is certainly appealing, for sure.\par \par \uc2 Governor DeWine, thank you very much. Always appreciate when you`re on.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Good to be with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210514eh5e0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Some States Ending Extended Unemployment Benefits; Pipeline Resumes Operations; President Biden Says Fully Vaccinated Americans Don't Need Masks; Interview With Sen. James Lankford (R-OK); Interview With Sen. Ron Johnson (R-WI)\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne, Scott Martin, Peter Doocy, David Lee Miller,  Jonathan Serrie, Gary Kaltbaum \par \uc2 9,192 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In less than four months, we've gone from 5.5 percent to nearly 60 percent of the ad -- adults in America with at least one shot in their arm.\par \par \uc2 We've gone from stagnation to an economy that is growing faster than it has in nearly 40 years. We've gone from an anemic job creation to a record for job creation for a new administration.\par \par \uc2 All of this is the product of prompt action to roll out the vaccine and boost our economy.\par \par \uc2 As I said in my joint address to the Congress, this vaccination effort has been a historic logistical achievement for the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 Over these past 114 days, our vaccination program has led the world. And that's due to the incredible hard work of so many people: the scientists and researchers; the drug companies; the National Guard; the U.S. military; FEMA; the nation's governors, doctors, nurses, pharmacists -- everyone who has moved Heaven and Earth to get as many shots into arms of as many Americans as possible. And it's truly been an all-hands-on deck -- for the country -- effort.\par \par \uc2 You know, some people said we couldn't do this -- that it would not be until the fall that we had this many people vaccinated; that 2021 might be a lost year for our country, as 2020 was. But we proved the doubters wrong.\par \par \uc2 I need to single out one more group to praise: the American people. The American people. For more than a year, you've endured so much and so many lost jobs, so many businesses lost, so many lives upended, and so many months that our kids couldn't be in school. You couldn't see your friends or family. All the moments that mattered so much -- from birthdays to weddings to graduations -- all postponed.\par \par \uc2 And most tragically of all: the lost lives. As the press here knows, I carry a card in my pocket with the number of lives lost to COVID as of the close of business yesterday: 580,073 lost lives. So many empty chairs. So many times a husband or a wife leaned over to touch their spouse, and they're not there. Moms and dads, sons and daughters, sisters, brothers, aunts, uncles, grandparents, your neighbors, your coworkers.\par \par \uc2 Having been there in other circumstances, I know it's of little solace right now, but I promise you: A day will come when the memory of the loved one you lost will bring a smile to your lips before it brings a tear to your eye. That's when you know you're going to get through it. You will get through it. But it's a long haul.\par \par \uc2 You've endured all this. When your country asked you to get vaccinated, you did. The American people stepped up. You did what I consider to be your patriotic duty. That's how we've gotten to this day.\par \par \uc2 As President, I can say I'm pleased, but -- I'm sure the Vice President agrees with me -- we're not surprised. Pleased, but not surprised. Because the simple truth is this: the American people have never, ever, ever, ever let their country down. Never.\par \par \uc2 This country is proving what we've known -- what we've all known for so long. There's nothing we are unable to do when we put our minds, our hearts, and our souls into it, and we do it together.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to be clear about what the CD -- CDC is saying and what the CDC is not saying. The CDC is saying they have concluded that fully vaccinated people are at a very, very low risk of getting COVID-19. Therefore, if you've been fully vaccinated, you no longer need to wear a mask. Let me repeat: If you are fully vaccinated, you no longer need to wear a mask.\par \par \uc2 But if you've not been vaccinated, or if you're getting a two-shot vaccine, and you've not gotten your -- you've only had your first shot but not your second or you haven't waited two full two weeks after your second shot, you still need to wear a mask.\par \par \uc2 Look, we've gotten this far; please protect yourself until you get to the finish line. Because as great as this announcement is today, we don't want to let up. We all know how tough this virus has been. The safest thing for the country is for everyone to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And getting vaccinated is easier than ever. We've worked so hard. We have 80,000 locations. Ninety percent of you live within five miles of one of those locations.\par \par \uc2 You can find a vaccine site near you by texting your zip code to the following number: 438829. Let me repeat: Text your -- your zip code to 438829. And it'll get the places nearest to you, you can go and get vaccinated now. And many places don't require an appointment. It's free. Just walk in, get the shot. Anyone 12 years or above in age is eligible.\par \par \uc2 And for now, after a year of hard work and so much sacrifice, the rule is very simple: Get vaccinated or wear a mask until you do. It's vaxxed or masked. Get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 If you're vaccinated, you can be around the vaccinated or unvaccinated people. But if you're not vaccinated or not fully vaccinated, you should wear a mask for your own protection and the protection of other unvaccinated people. The choice is yours.\par \par \uc2 You all made this possible. Now let's finish the work of beating this virus and getting everything back to normal.\par \par \uc2 Remember, as fast as we've gone, most people under the age of 65 aren't vaccinated yet. We've opened vaccinations nationally to all adults just four weeks ago. It's going to take a little more time for everyone who wants to get vaccinated to get their shots. So, all of us, let's be patient. Be patient with one another.\par \par \uc2 You know, some may say, I just feel more comfortable continuing to wear a mask. They may feel that way. So if you're someone with a mask -- you see them, please treat them with kindness and respect. We've had too much conflict, too much bitterness, too much anger, too much polarization of this issue about wearing masks. Let's put it to rest.\par \par \uc2 Let's remember, we're all Americans. Let's remember that we are all in this together.\par \par \uc2 If you're fully vaccinated and can take your mask off, you've earned the right to do something that Americans are known for all around the world: greeting others with a smile -- with a smile.\par \par \uc2 So, it's a good day for the country. We aren't done yet. We're still losing too many Americans because we still have too many unvaccinated people. We have to get to 70 percent of adults started on vaccinations by July the 4th. And we have to take this pandemic, tackle it not just here, but overseas as well to truly be safe in the long run.\par \par \uc2 There's still more work ahead. But I have said many times: As tough as this pandemic has been, we will get through it. We will rebuild our economy, reclaim our lives, and get back to normal. We'll laugh again. We'll know joy again. And we'll smile again -- you know, and now see one another's smile, look at the smiles on other people's faces. Better days are ahead, I promise you.\par \par \uc2 And the best days for America are still to come. I promise you that as well.\par \par \uc2 Thank you. God bless you. And remember: It's never, ever, ever, ever been a good bet to bet against the American people. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President, how do you enforce unvaccinated Americans to still use their masks?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't -- we don't enforce it. We, in fact, if you don't -- if you haven't been vaccinated, wear your mask for your own protection and the protection of the people who are -- also have not been vaccinated yet.\par \par \uc2 It's not an enforcement thing. We're not going to go out and arrest people. But the fact of the matter is, I still believe the vast majority of the American people care about the safety of their neighbors and care about the safety of their families.\par \par \uc2 And, like I said: If you've been vaccinated, you don't have to wear your mask and you can shake hands. You can even give each other a hug. But if you've not been vaccinated, please wear the mask. Please wear the mask.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Mr. President, is there any update on your meeting with the Republicans, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We had a very, very good meeting. It was great to be back with so many of the colleagues that I had served with in the Senate, and I am very optimistic that we can reach a reasonable agreement. But even if we don't, it's been a good-faith -- there's a good-faith effort that's been started.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Did you compromise on anything other than the corporate tax?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We didn't compromise on anything. What we did was -- I laid out what I thought we should be doing, how it should be paid for. And my colleagues in the Senate came back and said they'll come back to me with a counteroffer of what they are prepared to do and fund, and how to fund it, and then we'll talk again next week.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you all so very much.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, there you have it, the president next to the vice president without a mask, as the CDC says fully vaccinated Americans no longer need to wear one in most cases.\par \par \uc2 It's a day of liberation for many. Are Americans and businesses breathing a little easier this hour?\par \par \uc2 Hello. I'm Charles Payne, in Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 First to Peter Doocy at the White House on the significance of this moment and more on those guidelines -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 As someone who has been in Biden's orbit since the beginning of the pandemic and who traveled with him at the very end of his normal feeling primary campaign, seeing him walk up to a microphone without a mask covering his mouth and nose or without a mask in his hand is something we have not seen in well over a year, before the quarantine started.\par \par \uc2 We were here on January 20, his very first moment stepping onto the grounds of the White House. And he walked through the gate outside right after giving his speech. He had a mask on. Last year, last summer, we were at the beach in Delaware. We saw him riding his bike outside on a bike trail, nobody else around, with a mask on.\par \par \uc2 This is a huge change from this president. He says he always tries to set an example for people to follow. And now the example that he is trying to set is that, if you have got your vaccination, you no longer need to wear your mask outside or inside.\par \par \uc2 He's telling people they only need to wear it if they have not yet been vaccinated. He says they will not enforce that. They're not going to go rounding up and arresting people who are not wearing their masks when they are supposed to.\par \par \uc2 But the feeling around the White House and just in Biden land in the last two hours or so since this guidance came out has been almost -- almost joyous for the people on the complex. White House staff and also the press that cover the White House and who are on the campus all got different e- mails, essentially saying that, if you have been vaccinated, masks are now optional.\par \par \uc2 And so things are already looking different. I was walking around in some of the hallways, seeing faces of people that I had not seen their face in person ever because of distancing measures and because of masking.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: This White House, in addition to being very serious about masking, they have also been very, very stringent about testing and sanitizing and social distancing.\par \par \uc2 So, in the coming days, we will see how the other measures that they have taken to combat COVID-19 evolve or the enforcement of them evolve. But this is a very, very big change felt here on the grounds at the White House -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Peter, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 I want to get the read from Dr. Roshini Raj. She's an associate professor of medicine at NYU Langone Health.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Raj, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 I guess is it hard to overstate just how momentous this moment is?\par \par \uc2 DR. ROSHINI RAJ, NYU LANGONE HEALTH: I don't think so, Charles.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is -- you just heard a moment of joy. And I truly feel that personally, as a physician, as a mom, as a New York City resident. This is a dramatic move today.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that it also really does give an incentive to people who haven't been vaccinated yet to go get that vaccine. Really, your life can dramatically change in a safe way if you are fully vaccinated. So I think it's a very big deal and a wonderful day.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I haven't checked the last couple days. But I know, initially, when they paused one of the vaccines, the numbers started to go -- pull back a little bit. We were at three million a day, got to 2.5 million.\par \par \uc2 Did you see -- have you seen a pronounced slowdown? And is this, maybe to your point, that spark that gets it moving back up?\par \par \uc2 RAJ: Yes, there definitely has been a slowdown recently in the number of vaccines that are being administered every day.\par \par \uc2 And that's an issue. Now, now that we just, just were able to open it up to the 12-to-15-year-old, we're going to see an increase in the numbers soon. But, still, for those adults that are under 65, we still have a ways to go in terms of vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 And another thing I do want to point out is, a lot of people have been saying, well, maybe this should have been done earlier, and it's too late, or it was about time kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 When we look to other parts of the world where things were opened up too quickly, we see the consequences of that. And we see what's going on in India right now.\par \par \uc2 So I think, rather than focus on when this should have been done, we should be celebrating what's going on today, for sure, and really encouraging people as much as we can, this is one step, to get that vaccine, if they haven't already. We're still not at that point of herd immunity, where we can really stop worrying about this virus to a certain extent. We're not there yet, by any means.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 And to that point, and we also saw in Europe last year where, even before the vaccines were ready, where you saw places like Germany sort of take those premature victory laps, and we applauded the fact they were doing so well.\par \par \uc2 What about the folks who still don't want to get the vaccine, particularly those who feel like, well, if we hit 70 percent, I probably don't have to worry anyway, or it's been over a year, going on two years? I would have gotten it. I would have gotten it. Maybe I did get it already. Maybe I beat it already. I don't know.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there's going to be a certain portion, a large chunk, I think, of people who probably will still, not for any other reason other than they just don't think they probably need it.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean for everyone?\par \par \uc2 RAJ: Well, I think what it means is, this virus is going to persist in a meaningful way longer than it has to. And that's -- and that's a problem for everyone.\par \par \uc2 So, I think this idea that, well, I probably had COVID, or maybe I know I had COVID, therefore, I'm protected, we don't really know that that's the case. We don't know how long that natural immunity lasts. And, really, the studies have shown it may not last very long.\par \par \uc2 We also know that, when you get the vaccine, the antibodies you get from the vaccine are far, far higher than what you would get just from a natural COVID infection. So, I think a lot of education has to go into this.\par \par \uc2 And what we're talking about with the herd immunity is to a point where not that COVID is going to go away completely. Unfortunately, many people think it's going to be around sort of like the flu, but in a manageable way. If we're not reaching herd immunity, it still could be a problem, where people are dying and getting sick.\par \par \uc2 So that vaccine is going to be important for individuals. And I also want to point out that, if people still want to wear their masks, as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said, President Biden, in his speech, don't give them a hard time. You have no idea what someone else has gone through, whether they're very sick, they have a depressed immune system, or if they have lost someone to COVID, and they feel very fearful about removing the mask.\par \par \uc2 But to your question about the vaccines and people that are still going to be hesitant, I think we're going to have to do more education in terms of what that really means, and the fact that it is really necessary even when many other people have been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Dr. Raj, thank you very much. Appreciate it very much.\par \par \uc2 So, how are business owners handling this news?\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Erin Bellard. She's owns E's Bar in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Erin, I would imagine this is music to your ears.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BELLARD, OWNER, E'S BAR: It is amazing. We're so, so excited.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's going to give everyone the confidence they need to know that they can come out to eat and eat outside or inside and be safe.\par \par \uc2 The issue is, we need New York state to catch up, because, as it stands right now, they haven't changed the guidance for us. So, inside, we still have to socially distance or have people between partitions.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, we're kind of hoping that this is going to push New York state over the edge to lift those restrictions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, and New York state has been one of those ham-fisted states, in my opinion, that ignored the science and, for whatever reason, went in a direction that destroyed your colleagues.\par \par \uc2 So, first and foremost, congratulations for still being open.\par \par \uc2 BELLARD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But I would think that you must have a lot of people who've been coming there, following the guidance, but also who say, hey, we want to really come in. We want -- how much of an uptick -- if New York says, go ahead, fill the place up, how much of an uptick will you get?\par \par \uc2 Will it be packed?\par \par \uc2 BELLARD: I think that we will be totally packed.\par \par \uc2 Like, right now, we are able to seat 60 people. And that's full all the time. So, I think that the second it's lifted, everyone is going to come rushing back. They're so excited to sit at the bar and to mingle with people, to meet new people, right?\par \par \uc2 So, I think it's going to be amazing once New York gets it together.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Are there any other concerns that you have?\par \par \uc2 We're -- we have been talking to small businesses all week, particularly after that jobs report last week. Are you -- are you having any difficulty getting staffing and things like that? Because that's another issue, unfortunately, that seems to have come out of this whole crisis.\par \par \uc2 BELLARD: Yes, I mean, we have been fortunate that a lot of our staff is coming back to us.\par \par \uc2 But we do have some positions to fill that are tricky. And I'm hoping that, with Broadway coming back and the acting community coming back, that there's going to be a whole sea of people who typically work in hospitality coming back to New York who had previously left.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Erin, did you see me on earlier and then go and dye your hair, so your hair and my tie time matched?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BELLARD: I did not know we matched, but I'm glad we do.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hey, congratulations for being -- for sticking in there, for surviving all of this. And I hope your place is packed. And I will come by and check it out one day myself.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BELLARD: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All righty.\par \par \uc2 Well, the White House is pushing to expand vaccinations for eligible children. Will more parents now get shots for their kids if they can ditch the masks after?\par \par \uc2 David Lee Miller is also in New York City with the latest -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, the new masks guidance from the CDC is yet another reason for children to be vaccinated, but it is too soon to tell if hesitant parents are now going to change their mind and give their children a shot.\par \par \uc2 All kids between the ages of 12 and 15 are now eligible for the COVID-19 vaccine. On Wednesday, a CDC advisory committee gave the green light for adolescent use of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine, just days after the \uc2 FDA\uc2  gave its approval.\par \par \uc2 Now, the shots are voluntary for the kids. The vaccine has only emergency use authorization. And, because of that, many legal experts say it is unlikely school districts or states will try and impose a vaccine mandate. That could change in the future if the vaccine gets full federal approval.\par \par \uc2 Those on both sides of the issue side concerns about their child's well- being.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have some safety concerns as a parent that I don't want this for my daughter. And I don't want this as her passport for an education.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm a health-conscious person. And I think that the benefits outweigh the risks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Now, the overwhelming majority of health experts say the shot is safe.\par \par \uc2 They say that children are known to be COVID spreaders. And for that reason, they should be vaccinated, because it protects not only the child, but it protects others.\par \par \uc2 Also, the more people, regardless of age, who are vaccinated, they say, will move the country closer to herd immunity -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: David Lee Miller, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And now this:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): And this is as close to open borders as we could possibly have. Don't you think that's going to be even more of a pull factor?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, U.S. SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Senator, may I...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes, quick.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: May I have a minute to answer your question?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Again, is...\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Because...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Is that going to be a pull factor or not?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Senator...\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: That's kind of a yes-or-no answer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Fireworks on Capitol Hill at today's Senate Homeland Security hearing, Wisconsin Republican Senator Ron Johnson grilling DHS Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas over the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 The senator, he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 I do want to mention, we did call the secretary's office. We have not heard back just yet.\par \par \uc2 Wow. Senator Johnson, have charts, will travel. You really took it to him, and you had the evidence right there. And it was pretty clear, and, hence, the energy, the sense of urgency from your part. Do you think the message eventually got across that this crisis needs to be dealt with?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I kind of doubt it, Charles.\par \par \uc2 It was surreal being in that hearing room, as we heard the chairman, Peters, and then Secretary Mayorkas talk about how they inherited this crisis, and that the numbers were improving.\par \par \uc2 The only thing that's improving is that they are getting -- and they kept talking about they're getting more efficient. They're getting more efficient at apprehending, processing and dispersing, oftentimes dispersing illegal -- illegal immigrants around the country without even a notice to appear.\par \par \uc2 So, it was actually pretty shocking. I think we were all pretty amazed at the state of denial that the secretary was in. And we obviously have the numbers that show that this is a manmade crisis. This is a crisis produced by President Biden, when he dismantled all the successful policies of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, even -- even Democratic lawmakers in those border states are saying as much.\par \par \uc2 So, while they're focused on the public relations aspect of this, and clearly losing, what can they do? I mean, it seems to me that, during the election period, whether it was the debates or so many other things we heard later on, that a clear message went out to the world, particularly south of the border.\par \par \uc2 Hey, we're open. Come on. No more xenophobia. Come to this country. We're waiting for you. And the world heard the message.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, what they could do is what they won't do. And that's reinstate the policies that worked.\par \par \uc2 During the hearing, they say, well, this crisis really started ramping up starting in April of 2020. Well, yes, numbers started rising because you had Democrat presidential candidates saying that they were not going to deport anybody, they were going to offer illegal immigrants free health care.\par \par \uc2 So that began to draw more people into this country after President Trump had, by and large, stopped the flow of unaccompanied children and family units exploiting our very generous asylum laws.\par \par \uc2 Now, the other thing I had to point out, Charles, is, both President Biden and Vice President Harris know the human deprivations, the inhumanity that their policies are creating, that they are facilitating the multibillion business model, multibillion-dollar business model of the most evil people on the planet, the sex trafficking, human trafficking, the beatings, the kidnappings, the additional ransom.\par \par \uc2 They know this full well. They know that children are being sold, that they're being recycled to be used again for another adult as a family unit. So, they knew all this. And yet they have instituted these policies that are incentivizing this business model. It's shameful.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: No, it really is, when you start to -- you really look at it in a proper perspective.\par \par \uc2 I'm also concerned, and I know the audience is, about the large number of folks who are coming through. They're not -- they're not turning themselves over, like a lot of people are, but just coming through and making it into the country, and no one knows where they go, I mean, what they're doing, what they're going to do, because that feels like that could be the criminal faction, not the ones coming up looking for work or real humanitarian issues, but those with other nefarious things in mind.\par \par \uc2 Does anyone have a plan for that?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No, they don't.\par \par \uc2 We are apprehending, on average, almost 6,000 people per day over the last couple of months, 6,000 people per day. That's a caravan a day, about 1,000 known get-aways. We have no idea how many people now get away without being known, because our border is completely open.\par \par \uc2 The drug trafficking, we have no idea. And, again, they are dispersing people without even a notice to appear. Secretary Mayorkas said, well, we're asking them to voluntarily check in with an ICE office at their final destination.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these people are coming in here illegally. Do you think they're going to check themselves in to be going through an adjudication process where their asylum claim will be denied because they're coming here for economic reasons? Of course they won't.\par \par \uc2 So, they -- these folks are in a fantasyland. They're in a state of denial. And it is definitely affecting our national and homeland security. It's unfortunate. In 2006, you had 26 Senate Democrats vote for the Secure Fence Act, including President Obama, President Biden, Secretary -- Secretary of State Clinton.\par \par \uc2 Now it's -- they have turned into a partisan issue, as every Democrat on my committee voted against my amendment to just complete the wall with the money that's already been spent. We're going to be -- we're going to be wasting $2 billion of American taxpayer money...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: ... because we won't complete that wall, and we will get nothing for it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Senator, let me switch gears here, because the CDC updating its guidance today, saying fully vaccinated people can now go without masks indoors. That's in most places.\par \par \uc2 I'm sure you think this is a move in the right direction. But do you wonder why it took so long?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes, this action is well overdo.\par \par \uc2 The vaccine is highly effective. By the way, so is natural immunity. I just consulted with a doctor, had an antibody test. My antibody level seven months after I had asymptomatic COVID is at a level as high as the doctor's is, who had two shots of the Moderna vaccine.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we should certainly include people that have natural immunity as well. And I would dispute that doctor you had an earlier that natural immunity is not as strong or that it's not as long-lasting.\par \par \uc2 We still have people from original SARS 17 years later that have immunity to that. So, it's true of almost every other virus that, once you have the virus, you're immune from it, even long-term.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Why are we automatically assuming that's not true?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And, of course, you have taken a fair amount of heat for your position on this.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I have.\par \par \uc2 But let's face it. I'm not in a position of either encouraging or discouraging. I celebrated Operation Warp Speed. I have had every flu vaccine for the last 30, 40 years. I'm up to date with all my other vaccines.\par \par \uc2 But I do believe this is a right to choose. I'm a big supporter of right to try. I'm the champion of right to try. But the corollary -- corollary to that is right to choose.\par \par \uc2 What about health care privacy? You know, we're losing all these liberties during COVID. We ought to reclaim these liberties.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Senator Ron Johnson, thank you very much. I really appreciate it. Your passion on these things, it's infectious.\par \par \uc2 We will talk to you again real soon.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, folks, the East Coast pipeline may be back online, but so are drivers. Is there any relief in sight?\par \par \uc2 And that -- extra jobless benefits, take that away and Americans, well, will they take jobs? A lot of states are doing it. Now these Republican senators are actually pushing all states to do the same thing. Will it work?\par \par \uc2 And a shot in the arm, a mask off the face. President Biden taking it all off, right? He goes maskless for the first time.\par \par \uc2 I want to get reaction from Republican Oklahoma Senator \uc2 James Lankford\uc2 . He's coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It was a mixed day for the Mouse House, \uc2 Disney\uc2  beating earnings estimates for the first three months of the year, but missing on revenue estimates on a slowdown in Disney+ subscriptions.\par \par \uc2 The stock getting hit a little bit in after-hours trading.\par \par \uc2 We're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, that East Coast pipeline is back online, but drivers are still seeing long lines here.\par \par \uc2 Here was the latest reaction, President Biden telling Americans not to panic and also saying that the fuel shortages are only going to be temporary.\par \par \uc2 Now, this with the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2  expecting partial service to resume to all of its markets at some point today, but it's still no comfort for drivers dealing with gas prices north of $3 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Serrie in Sandy Springs, Atlanta, and Jonathan has more -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Actually, we moved to another station. We're now in Smyrna, just outside of Atlanta, because the station we were at in Sandy Springs ran out of gas. This is the first station we have visited during this crisis that has not run out of gas.\par \par \uc2 In fact, a fuel truck showed up a couple hours ago, so perhaps a good sign.\par \par \uc2 I want to show you now a live drone shot, some aerials that we have over yet another Atlanta suburb of Doraville. It's flying near a fuel terminal, where tanker trucks are off-loading gas that has arrived from the pipeline system.\par \par \uc2 The trucks then make deliveries to gas stations. Federal officials predict many communities will see fuel supplies return to normal over the weekend and in more remote places early next week.\par \par \uc2 According to GasBuddy, roughly half of all gas stations here in Georgia, South Carolina and Virginia have run out of gas. And, in North Carolina, two out of three stations are without gas. Now, state and federal officials continue to urge motorists not to panic-buy. They say that this problem is temporary and things will be fully restored in just a matter of days -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, the pipeline is back up and running. That's good. But with prices now over $3 a gallon and gas lines still forming, my next guest thinks this situation for drivers this weekend could be really bad.\par \par \uc2 Want to bring in energy analyst Tom Kloza.\par \par \uc2 Tom, so, what's the deal here?\par \par \uc2 TOM KLOZA, \uc2 OIL PRICE INFORMATION SERVICE\uc2 : Well, I think I have got good news, actually. I think that it's getting better.\par \par \uc2 In the business, it's what you call trying to keep your stations wet, making sure that have fuel. And they're getting wetter now that the pipelines running again.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the pipeline shutdown was a pretty serious problem. But the reaction of the maddening crowd reminded me of sort of 1978 and 1979 with the Iranian oil crisis. Everybody's hoarding. Everybody was topping off the tank. People in the Peninsula of Florida, which is supplied by water and not by pipeline, were filling up with gasoline.\par \par \uc2 The good news is, that's calming down. I was an abysmal failure at being the gas whisperer early in the week.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KLOZA: But I can tell you, talking to marketers, talking in stations, they're getting the gas back now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Now, even before, though, the colonial ransomware attack, gas prices were trending higher. And we saw crude oil breaking out. Is there a sense that we could still see a very expensive summer driving season?\par \par \uc2 Also, let's add into the fact that we're reopened. I mean, people are going to hit the road. You got a choice. You're going to staycation, not go away from this country.\par \par \uc2 KLOZA: Well, we actually predicted that there could be some problems this summer, not because of a lack of supply, but because of lack of truck drivers.\par \par \uc2 When COVID hit and demand got knocked down to where it was in the Kennedy administration for a while, probably 15 or 20 percent of all the drivers of those transport trucks left and went to work elsewhere, Amazon FedEx, you name it.\par \par \uc2 So, when you have that pent-up demand, and I think probably July and August, maybe a little bit Memorial Day weekend, some of the destinations - - I live by the Jersey Shore here. If you're by national parks, you might see that.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, when you tell people to stay calm, and not to load up with gasoline, it's like telling people in March of 2020 not to buy toilet paper. You see your neighbors and everybody else getting it, and you start to hoard.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, Tom, you will always be our oil whisperer and gas whisperer. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 KLOZA: Thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The CDC, meanwhile, folks saying, ditch the mask if you're fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 So what does this mean for our return to normal? Reaction from a top Republican senator.\par \par \uc2 Also, will taking extra jobless benefits away from Americans get them to take jobs? These states are doing it, and we're going to debate it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: As President Biden was meeting today with a group of Republican senators on infrastructure. Florida Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  leading another group of Republicans and saying, forget spending, it's time to get Americans working.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO\uc2  (R-FL): Enhanced unemployment benefits are creating an incentive for people not to return to work until they expire.\par \par \uc2 It's not because people are lazy. I'm not accusing anyone of being lazy. It's because people are logical, because it's logic that, if you're going to make close to what -- or as much, and in some cases more than what you do in your work, you will go back to work when that expires.\par \par \uc2 We have a labor crisis in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, so who's right here?\par \par \uc2 I want to go FOX Business contributors Scott Martin and Gary Kaltbaum, along with Optimal Capital's director of strategy Frances Newton Stacy.\par \par \uc2 Gary K., I got a feeling I know what you're going to say. But where should we be going, more spending or more workers?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, look, it's unfortunate that the president said there's no data. He can come to Lake Mary, Florida, and I will walk them into some restaurants, and they will tell him exactly what's going on.\par \par \uc2 The theme parks are offering bonuses. And all you have to do is just go print out what all the states are giving before this extra on unemployment. There are some states that are paying out $900 with the extra per week. So, people are definitively staying home. I'm pretty sure the president knows this.\par \par \uc2 I wish he would roll this back sooner, rather than later. Why? Because there are a lot of businesses just getting off their back that can't go 100 percent because they can't find people to show up at this juncture.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 To be clear, the administration says they haven't seen the evidence, because they know it's out there. They're just saying they haven't seen it. Yes, real cute.\par \par \uc2 You know, Frances Newton Stacy, right toward around 3:00, the biggest trend on social media was: "We are closed." In other words, the left now saying, we're not going to go back to work, even with the CDC guidelines, until we get more money. We feel like we have got big business on the ropes. \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  raised their prices.\par \par \uc2 So we could still see a worker crisis.\par \par \uc2 FRANCES NEWTON STACY, OPTIMAL CAPITAL: Yes, we definitely could.\par \par \uc2 I think what's kind of weird is that we -- taxes are going to go up, right? And these companies that made it through COVID, and God bless the ones that didn't, the companies that made it through COVID have a record amount of debt service, probably, on their books.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that this sort of demand and supply in the hiring is going to kind of even out toward the end of the year. Also, I think that there are some other sort of headwinds coming in the system that might kind of even out the record amount of pent-up demand that we're seeing play out now.\par \par \uc2 So this hiring thing could even out. But I do agree that it should be handled on a localized basis, because if you just cut it off at the federal level, you really do risk leaving some people behind. And, locally, you can analyze that a bit better.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, at this point, though, Scott, we're talking about small businesses, to Gary's point.\par \par \uc2 It's gone on a long time. It was a noble thing to do. This has been -- this has been extended now two or three times. So, the question is, do we need it right now?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MARTIN, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: No.\par \par \uc2 And I love how \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  use the word logic. Like, you don't need evidence. I mean, it's easy to ignore the evidence if you close your eyes, but, like, logic. I mean, I get it too. If you're getting paid to stay home or go out and party, go to the beach, whatever you want to do after you have been cooped up, by the way, thanks to the government, for a year, yes, I would do that as well.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I will say that right now. So the fact that we want to get this great reopening going, we want the markets and economy to enjoy a normalized environment, yet we're going to keep this stimulus going, is just crazy to me.\par \par \uc2 And it's because the government wants to control you. They still want to have control. They still want to have you dependent and under your thumb for funds. And so, as long as we are in this position, I'm now concerned, Charles, about this great reopening and the pent-up demand that Frances was talking about, because there's no way that demand is going to be satisfied, if you're a small business, a big business, if you can't find the workers to service people.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Gary K., we just heard from President Biden at the top of this show. He came out. He took a victory lap. And he's been in an awkward position, because I think a big part of this is that they have to sell a certain amount of fear, a certain amount of urgency. There's no way you can push through another $4 trillion in spending and at the same time say things are great.\par \par \uc2 So, they're walking this line and they're not walking it very well, not that it can be. And so what do you make of the notion that the administration can't admit that things are going well, and they have to keep certain things in place to pass the rest of their agenda?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Well, the problem is, they want to grow the government big time, and they want to just rip on the taxpayer, especially the supposed privileged few, and it's a shame.\par \par \uc2 And, really, the big shame is, all they really had to do was come in and do nothing. The economy's ready to get going. The economy's ready to soar. The pent-up demand that's out there is gargantuan. And once the airlines get going and international flights get going and the offices open in the big cities, it's look out upwards.\par \par \uc2 But they are so determined -- and this is what I believe -- to put all these headwinds in front of the economy. We're not talking about a $500 billion tax hike. We're talking about four, five, six -- we still don't even have the outline of it -- but I counted up a total of seven trillion bucks.\par \par \uc2 And to take seven trillion bucks out an economy and put it in their hands just coming out of pandemic is a sin.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: And it does nothing to help the economy, and it hurts everybody in the lower income and middle class that are trying to become wealthy. Crazy stuff.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I think the old saying is, a trillion here, a trillion there. Soon, we're talking about real money.\par \par \uc2 You three are the best. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Hey, did President Biden just take a big step to get more shots in arms by taking the mask off his face?\par \par \uc2 I'm going to get reaction from a top Republican senator on that next.\par \par \uc2 And the folks behind the \uc2 Colonial Pipeline\uc2  just releasing a new update. We will have it for you right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: 