mployees.\par \par \uc2 And now, we're seeing some states, Martha, around the country that are rolling back on these benefits hoping that that's going to encourage more workers to return to the workforce. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a strange situation. Thank you very much, Lydia Hu.\par \par \uc2 My next guest is the owner of Ohana Grill in Lavallette, New Jersey at down the Jersey Shore. He's run the place for 12 years with his wife, James Costello, he's here now.\par \par \uc2 You listen to this James, from the president this afternoon. What goes through your mind?\par \par \uc2 JAMES COSTELLO, OWNER & CHEF, OHANA GRILL: Well, the first thing that comes to my mind is who's going to be checking that because we have ads up and people apply for jobs and set up interviews and then just don't show up. And I can tell you, every restaurant owner I know is in the same boat.\par \par \uc2 So, people -- you know, they're -- when he says you're not going to game the system, they are gaming the system. But no one's -- no one -- no one's calling me and asking if somebody applied for a job, so.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Really? So, no one has ever called your establishment and said, you know, can you give us the names of people who you offer jobs to?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: No.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No? Shocker.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Not once.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, here's what else he said about help coming to presidents to -- here's what the president said about help coming to restaurants, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're delivering assistance to tens of thousands of restaurants and bars across the country. Today, we're sending out the first relief checks to 16,000 hard hit restaurants with help for many more to follow. A lot more people are going to be employed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What about that?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: That's -- I'm assuming he's talking about the Restaurant Revitalization Program.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: We applied for it, but we're on a waiting period. I guess the first three weeks for only certain groups that were going to be getting funded. And then after that, everybody else will get a chance at that money.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I guess my question is, is more checks what you need at this point? Or would you feel that you would be better off if Governor Murphy had allowed you to open up full capacity? And you were able to hire people? Because they were not getting checks all the way through September. I know, you've got a summer -- mostly summer restaurant that you want to pack?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Yes, that federal extension to September really wasn't -- I don't think that was exciting for anybody in the Jersey Shore. Since this is, you know, the season from May till, you know, September, October. And we need people to come to work.\par \par \uc2 I mean, as it is right now, we were sort of dialing back our operation because we wouldn't be able to handle the business if it did come with the employees that we have.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know your wife's a teacher, and she's helping you keep the business open because you can't get anyone to work. It's honestly, I heard this from people all over. I was traveling a little bit over the weekend. And everywhere I went, people said, well, it would be great, but I can't get anyone to work because of these extended benefits.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you very much, James. We wish you good luck. I hope you have a great summer down there in Lavallette. And I hope that you can pack them in, and that some folks show up for work. Thank you, sir. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: All right, thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, David Asman is with me here, Fox Business Network host. David, you know, what would you say?\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX BUSINESS HOST: Well, I say, first of all, it's incredible that the mixed messages keep coming from this administration.\par \par \uc2 You get the sense that this administration is filled with bureaucrats and academics who don't really know how businesses operate, and they need a game plan. And you have to follow that gameplay, whether you're talking about masks, whether you're talking about protocol for the -- for the pandemic, or whether you're talking about spending trillions of dollars, some of which goes to keep people at home, rather than sending them to (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 It's great that the president said what he did an hour ago. But just yesterday, you had the Commerce Secretary on saying that these unemployment benefits had nothing to do with it lousy jobs number that we saw on Friday. We were expecting a million jobs, we got a quarter of that, 260,000. It was -- it was the biggest upset that I've ever covered in following these jobs numbers.\par \par \uc2 So, this administration, you know, they may have the best intentions, the academic theories may work out in the classroom, but they don't work out on the ground, and people like that restaurant owner knows it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, they put this out, because they have to convince people that the economy is still struggling, right? And they're sending more money to California now. And California has a $75 billion surplus Gavin Newsom just mentioned.\par \par \uc2 So, they're going to start sending checks to people too. So, we're sending to struggling cities more money from the federal government.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: By the way, can I just say about the California surplus, that's coming from Silicon Valley, that's coming from these huge mega corporations that have -- that have been making trillions of dollars during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the small guys, I had a business owner from California on a restaurant tour from California earlier, he said the same thing that this guy did that were getting