 they were available. So, I mean, that, that's the way it happened. But the fact of the matter is, the transmission was very low even in the fall. There were plenty of colleges and independent schools that were back all fall, that were in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: So the bottom line became, they were back in the fall. And then, they went in and out. We had this conversation about the 3% in New York City. The bottom line, Martha, is it's time for our kids to be back in school. And I am grateful that the Biden administration is listening to parents and to teachers and administrators, and to even Fox, to try to figure out how we get people vaccinated, how we get our kids back to school, and how we get our country --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you about vaccinations -- absolutely. Everyone agrees on that score. I mean, we can discuss why it didn't happen a long time ago. But God forbid, you know, I mean, now it's spring, right? School is almost over and a lot of these kids are not back yet. What will it take? You said it's time to go back, so are teachers going to require that all the students are vaccinated, and when is that going to be? Are you going to have everyone back in September, 100% in class five days a week?\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And will you require that every student who comes into the school is vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: No, no.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely not?\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: But that's why you have to still have some mitigation, because it's only emergency use vaccination right now. But I would hope that Fox would do what everybody else in public health is doing, which is we have to get our kids vaccinated, once the vaccination is OK.\par \par \uc2 I would hope Fox says to all these people that don't want the vaccines, that think there's a problem, why don't you and I do things together to get our kids back to school? This is what we need to do. We have to stop the misinformation. We have to stop the disinformation. We have to stop --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: As you know, many of those students were back without being vaccinated. And the CDC said it wasn't required for teachers or students to be vaccinated to go back to school. So children are a separate issue. Every family across America is going to make that decision about their children. We know that these are going to be widely available for them, which is great, so people have that option. And we certainly hope that they take advantage of that option.\par \par \uc2 But we're going to talk to you -- I want to hold you over the break, because we've got more to ask you, Randi. We're very glad you're here. So we'll take a quick break. And we'll come right back.\par \par \uc2 So as you know, folks at home, there are several states that have now taken action to ban what's called critical race theory from being taught in their schools. \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  says that opponents are trying to ban a "factual version of oppression," so we're going to circle back with her on all of that. A hot topic it is, right after this. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Two states, Idaho and Oklahoma, have banned the teaching of critical race theory in some form in public schools. And now at least nine other states are considering taking similar measures. The bill in Oklahoma reads in part, "No teacher shall require or make part of a course that one race or sex is inherently superior to another race or sex." The governor of that state joins us exclusively in moments, Governor Stitt. But, first, back to AFT President \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  once again.\par \par \uc2 Randi, really thank you very much for sticking around with us. I want to ask you about this because it's become such a hot topic. And you have said that, we quoted you on the way to the break that there's -- that this is a factual finding the, 1619 report that was put together in the New York Times that now has been converted to learning materials that are in classrooms, and are parts of curriculum. Do you believe that 1619 is a factual program?\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: So, Martha, I believe that -- I'm a history teacher and I'm a social study teacher, and I believe we should teach history. And from everything I can see and understand from the data I see. 1619 was the year that the first slave boat came from Africa to the United States. So that's a point in history that I think we should be teaching.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But that's, I mean, that's very simplistic take on it. I mean, that 1619 Project teaches that that's the true beginning and the founding of our nation, not 1776. And that the reason for the revolution and the colonization was because people wanted to preserve, colonists wanted to preserve slavery, that the country was founded on the basis of wanting to preserve slavery. But that is not factual. That is not true.\par \par \uc2 In fact, scholars in this area say there's no evidence that colonists were motivated by that in coming to the United States. So it would be wrong as a historian to want to teach them something that is not true, because that's the basis that sets up all these other tenants that lead to teaching kids that we live in a systemically racist country.\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: So I would actually say that I've had several conversations with Nikole Hannah-Jones, and I have not arrived at the same conclusion from her work as you have. But let's put it this way.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's not my conclusion, that's directly from their work. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: I would hope that Fox would be just as focused on let's get rid of the misinformation about what happened in this election. This election was --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Come on, Randi, come on. This is not the topic that we're here to talk about. I'm not going to talk about that. We've talked about that before. But that's a dodge, OK? Because children in this country, we rank 36 in math, OK? That's about midway of all the developed nations in this country. So I am asking you, you say your social studies teacher, do you favor teaching students that 1619 is more important than 1776? Do you favor that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I favor us teaching about 1776, which I have often done. I favor us teaching about 1619. I favor us teaching about the Holocaust, and the genocide in terms of Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: Absolutely. I mean, I learned about all of those things in school that you just mentioned. But do you think that it's wrong to suggest to children that if they're white, they belong to an oppressor class, if they're black they belong to a victim class? Do you have any problem with that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think we should be lifting up all ethnicities. I don't think we should say that one is an oppressor class and one is a non-oppressor class. I am a big believer in celebrating diversity and actually looking at and helping look at people's lived experience.\par \par \uc2 But if you're really talking about misinformation, now, Martha, and I hope you are, I really would hope that Fox would really look at what happened in this election and how we can because every social studies teacher is wrestling with this discern fact from fiction. We have to do that\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We have -- well, we have a president, President Biden, was elected in 2020. I think that all of that is quite clear. So I'm not sure why you are so concerned with that part, with that particular moment in history, every election is significant. Nobody is hiding anything under any rocks here.\par \par \uc2 But I do want, you know, the fact that you have two people who just made it onto the school board in Texas, right? They won by 40 points, because they said, you know what, this stuff, parents, you better become aware of it. Because if you think that it's just, oh, the first ship came with slaves on it in 1619, you better dig in a little bit deeper, because there's more to it than that.\par \par \uc2 And then you watched Tim Scott, right, who stood up in front of the country and said, America is not a racist nation. There are racism incidents. There is racism, we all recognize that. But if you raise children in this country, believing that it's a bad country that was founded in wanting to preserve slavery, which is simply not true, then we've got a problem in our school system. And apparently people noticed it in that district in Texas, and they voted in people that they think are going to make sure that that is clear to all the parents.\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: Martha, I know you love having me on to tell me all these things.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I like discussing it with you. I think it's -- yes. I mean, I respect you and I like discussing this with you because it's important.\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: I love America. My grandparents came from the Ukraine. My grandfather was persecuted, almost killed on his way to the United States. I love America. And I am so grateful that I live in a nation that can actually see how to make itself better. And that's what we try to do in education all the time.\par \par \uc2 And so, look, I think we should be teaching about 1776. I think we should be teaching about all the civil rights laws. I think we should actually be doing more with civics and science in school. And I do think that we should actually be talking about when the slave trade started in a place we now call America. All of those --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I don't think anybody has any problem with all the things that you just listed. Those things are absolutely not the issue that we're discussing. And I agree with you on all of those. And I want to thank you, because I do, I think it's a good debate. It's a good discussion. And as you know, I come from a long line of very proud teachers, and I'm very proud of the teachers in my family. I think some of them would be a little disturbed by some of what they see happening, so that's why I think it's so important that we all get it out in the open and we discussed it.\par \par \uc2 But I'm really heartened to hear that you think we're going to be at 100%, you think we'll be at 100% back in class in September, Randi.\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I hope so. Alright, I thank you very much for coming on. Good to see you, Randi. \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , President of the AFT.\par \par \uc2 So, Oklahoma is becoming the second state after Idaho to sign a bill preventing schools from teaching the programs that we just talked about, teaching that one race is more superior than another, victors and oppressors. Oklahoma's House Bill 1775 bans public schools from teaching those topics. It also prohibits sexual diversity training at their universities and colleges. And I want to ask him about that, Governor Stitt joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you here, sir. Why was this bill so important to you? You just heard our discussion. I think everybody could be on board with learning about the slave ships in 1619, the beginning of that history, which is a dark moment in United States history. But there's more to that than this, is there?\par \par \uc2 GOV. KEVIN STITT (R-OK): Well, absolutely. Well, first off, I want to say that Oklahoma schools, we've been 100% open. We had two school districts this entire year that weren't open. And now, as of about two months ago, we've got 100% of our schools open. But it's so important to us to teach history, like she said, but I'm not going to allow taxpayer dollars to be used to teach that one race is inherently superior to another race. It's so simple.\par \par \uc2 And I encourage Ms. Weingarten and everybody in America just to read this bill. Yes, to read this bill, it's so important because there's nothing controversial in this. I don't understand the big issue. We're not going to teach that one race is superior to another race, or that there's an oppressor class. And I believe that Oklahomans and really American stand with us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I would encourage everybody to dig into reading about this, because I think that it does get sort of, you know, a broad brush. But it is foundational in terms of understanding the country and what it's built on. But I want to ask you about why you've decided to eliminate training for sexual diversity at colleges and universities. What was that training and why do you not want it anymore?\par \par \uc2 STITT: Well, we're not limiting the training in college and universities. We want free speech. We want to be able to teach that in our universities. We just aren't going to mandate it. So as long as it's available on a volunteer basis, you can enroll in that class, but we just didn't want to mandate diversity training for racial or sexual purposes, and so, but it certainly is available voluntarily.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So at your colleges and universities, state colleges and universities, in order to work there, you don't have to have diversity training, correct? But you can have it if you choose.\par \par \uc2 STITT: That's exactly right. We said as long as it's available on a volunteer basis, that's perfectly fine. But we weren't going to mandate that type of training in our universities.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting, all right. Governor Stitt, thank you. Very good to have you here today. Thanks, sir.\par \par \uc2 STITT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up tomorrow, we have yet another exclusive for you. I will speak with Betsy DeVos, former Education Secretary under President Trump. You can see why we want to talk to her because there's all of these issues around school reopening and critical race theory. She has not spoken out since she left the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 She will do that with us tomorrow in Washington, DC. And also coming up right here right now, did sexual predator Jeffrey Epstein drive a wedge in the marriage of Bill and Melinda Gates? The bombshell claims on that and "Fox Nation" Host Nancy Grace when "The Story" continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Bob Baffert did it again. Medina Spirit has won the Kentucky Derby.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, but Kentucky Derby winner Medina Spirit's chances of a triple crown are now in limbo after testing positive for an excessive amount of an anti-inflammatory steroid. If a second result comes back positive, the Colts win it, Churchill Downs will be nullified. So today on "America's Newsroom," which I'm sure you are watching, legendary trainer Bob Baffert said he did nothing wrong. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOB BAFFERT, LEGENDARY HORSE TRAINER: That horse has never been treated with that, Actually, it's a legal therapeutic medicine and the amount that was in it wouldn't have any effect on the horse anyway, but we don't -- that horse was never treated with that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So they got to see how the second test comes back. That's going to be extremely important here. There's no decision yet on the Preakness, which is Saturday. Baffert's lawyer says he is ready to file a restraining order if Medina Spirit is not allowed to run. Very intriguing, so we'll keep you posted on that.\par \par \uc2 And it turns out that the breakup of one of the country's wealthiest couples, Bill and Melinda Gates, was years in the making. The Wall Street Journal reports Melinda Gates met with divorce lawyers back in 2019. That, perhaps coincidentally, was right around the time that \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 ' ties to convicted sex offender Jeffrey Epstein became public. There has been some reporting that there was some strife over that. National Correspondent William La Jeunesse has the story for us. Hi, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Martha, so the question is right, did those ties play any role in the divorce. \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  claims that he did not have a business relationship, nor a friendship with pedophile Jeffrey Epstein. Maybe acquaintance is more accurate.\par \par \uc2 Well now, some would argue your \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , why would you even hang out with a guy knowing he got 18 months in jail after seeking sex with a minor? On the other hand, you're a billionaire raising money for a foundation. Other billionaires, that's a good place to start. Here's what Gates said when that relationship became public.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES\uc2 , CO-FOUNDER, \uc2 MICROSOFT\uc2 : Any people with a reputation spending time with him probably gave him an undeserved sense of, you know, being back in the mainstream or not being a pariah, so my doing that was a mistake in that case. I made a mistake in judgment that those discussions would lead literally to billions of dollars going to global health, turned out that was a bad judgment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Gates first met Epstein in 2011, two years after Epstein went to prison on that sex charge. Gates did once fly on his plane and visited his townhouse in Manhattan. In an email, Gates told friends "his lifestyle is very different and kind of intriguing although would not work for me." Now, Gates spokesperson says that email refers only to Epstein's unique decor, and his habit of spontaneously bringing friends to meet \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Melinda met Epstein in 2013. According to The Wall Street Journal, she worried about her husband's relationship with Epstein, who committed suicide two years ago after being arrested for trafficking underage women. They were married 27 years, Martha. A mediator is going to divide their $145 billion fortune, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That should be interesting. William, thank you very much. Joining me now, Nancy Grace, Host of "Crime Stories with Nancy Grace," and "America's Most Wanted Overtime" on Fox Nation. Nancy, great to have you back on the program. What's your reaction to this whole Bill and Melinda Gates situation?\par \par \uc2 NANCY GRACE, FOX NATION HOST: I find it very intriguing myself that people are somehow trying to link Gates with Epstein, who is a known child sex offender along with his girlfriend Ghislaine Maxwell. The fact that they met is a very tenuous connection. And to suggest that his wife, Melinda Gates, sought the divorce because of a relationship with Epstein is very far-fetched. I'll be very wary of trying to loot Gates in on some type of child molestation ring.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. He makes I think a good point in that admission, in that interview, you know, that anybody who gave him the aura of being back in the mainstream made a mistake, Nancy, a big one.\par \par \uc2 GRACE: A mistake, possibly. But when I look at cases or fact scenarios, I look for evidence, hard evidence, not salacious gossip. So yes, mating with Epstein, trying to raise money for charity, I don't know that I would not that myself. I don't know that that will be a mistake. What Epstein did was Epstein's fault. And I don't think that that can be cast on anyone else.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, very good point. So let's talk a little bit about in the valley of sin. And first of all, we'll just play this trailer to set it up, Nancy. They don't want to hear about this very intriguing, awful story. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Surprises even me how traumatizing it is. I have a flashback experience.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Over two years, 43 people were charged with 29,726 counts of child sex abuse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If it's true, it's one of the worst sex crime certainly I've ever heard of.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If I could tell my 11 year old self one thing, it would be run.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nancy, tell us about this.\par \par \uc2 GRACE: Well, it's really disturbing. Always thinking that I'm on the right side of the law and doing the right thing to find out. Another prosecutor investigator went forward on nearly 60,000 child molestation charges against 43 adults revolving around 60 children, all of them a lie with an investigator drunk on power and arrogance.\par \par \uc2 This has ruined the lives of these children who were forced to make statements, putting their moms and dads behind bars for false charges. They've become alcoholics, drug addicts, all sorts of life problems because of this to this day. It's a cautionary tale.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It what was the motivation, and real quick I got 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 GRACE: Again, I really believe the detective nearly in the child molestation station unit was drunk on power. And he took down so many families that can never recover from this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's incredible. It's called the "In the Valley of Sin." It is a fascinating and awful story on many levels. Nancy Grace, always good to see you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GRACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that is "The Story" of this Monday, May 10, 2021. But as always, "The Story" continues, it goes on and on, so we will see you tomorrow live from Washington. I'm going to sit down with Senator Lindsey Graham, talk about what's going on the Hill right now, and a lot more. We'll dig in from there with Betsy DeVos as well tomorrow. So Your World with Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto, get Started in just a few seconds from now. Have a great day, everybody. Thanks for being here. See you then.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210511eh5a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Rep. Barry Loudermilk\uc2  (R-GA)\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne \par \uc2 852 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: With employers struggling to find workers, and on the heels of that sharp miss in the April jobs report, my next guest is introducing a bill to cut extended federal unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 Georgia Republican Congressman \uc2 Barry Loudermilk\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, tell us about your bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. BARRY LOUDERMILK\uc2  (R-GA): Well, Charles, this is simple.\par \par \uc2 It`s insanity that the government is paying people to be idle, instead of taking the millions of jobs that are available right now. I spent the last two weeks really integrating in the community, even working side by side with some folks in the construction industry.\par \par \uc2 And they are desperate to get people, not only to work for them, but to work for the suppliers. I mean, look at lumber prices, 200 percent higher than they were this time last year. And a lot of that is attributed just to supply chain issue of getting products to people.\par \par \uc2 And for the government to actually pay people more to stay at home than employers can afford to pay them to come to work is insanity. And how does a business compete with the big government like that? We can`t.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And here`s the thing, though. The federal government, President Biden in a jam.\par \par \uc2 On one hand, he`s trying to take credit for the most jobs created in the first 60 days of an administration, even though very few of his policies, certainly his tax policies, haven`t kicked in. But, on the other, he needs this urgency, he needs to sell the notion of fear, because he`s got another $4 trillion in federal spending on the drawing board.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, they`re walking this line, this thin line. And, in doing so, they keep disrespecting businesses, particularly small businesses, that are saying, you`re destroying us. They are not -- they`re completely ignoring them.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what do you say to your Democratic colleagues about this?\par \par \uc2 LOUDERMILK: Well, I`m actually hearing this from some Democrats back home who are small business owners that they get to stop this insanity.\par \par \uc2 Look, they want to do this multiple-trillion-dollar job stimulus package. We don`t need that job stimulus, other than just the federal government to get out of the way and get people back to work.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just the business owners. I talked to one guy in the construction industry who is very frustrated because he gets up before dawn, and so he can be at the job site by sunup, works until sundown, and then goes to another project their company has to work in the evening.\par \par \uc2 And when he does get to come home, he sees his neighbor two doors down sitting on the front porch drinking coffee because he knows that he doesn`t have to go back to work yet because the federal government will continue to pay him to stay at home.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 LOUDERMILK: It`s frustrating to the worker as well.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Wait until they get together over the weekend to play video games and you see sharp his neighbor`s skills have gotten.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hey, before I let you go, you mentioned reopening the economy; 165,000 women had to leave the labor force last month.\par \par \uc2 And I`m sure most of that`s tied to the fact that these schools would not be reopened in these blue states, again, an avoidable mistake. Can we get this -- do you think we`re finally going to get past that hurdle?\par \par \uc2 LOUDERMILK: Well, I sure hope so, if we can let the local school administrators to do what they -- I think what they feel is the right thing is get kids back in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is so important. Look, we can do it safely. We have proven it. Look at Georgia. We opened up back in May a year ago and started rolling things back out. We opened the schools again fully this year.\par \par \uc2 Everything is OK. It`s time to get over the fear. It`s time to get back to work and let Americans do the hard work that has made this country the -- what it is now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LOUDERMILK: But to get the Democrats to give up a big government program, well, you have to pry it from their cold, dead fingers, as they talk about our Bibles and our guns, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, congratulations on your bill. Let`s see how far it gets. Certainly, it`s needed.\par \par \uc2 LOUDERMILK: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Thank you very much, sir.\par \par \uc2 LOUDERMILK: All right.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210510eh5a0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Some Businesses Begging for Workers; Texas Bill would Ban Gender Reassignment Surgery for Children; Disney Employees Forced into Woke Trainings; More Trouble for Hunter Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Matt Finn \par \uc2 7,453 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: And that, my friends, is what I mean.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for watching "FOX News Primetime" all this week. It's been an honor to share it with you. I'll see you this weekend on "FOX and Friends."\par \par \uc2 Tucker Carlson, next.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 So what would happen if you gave a group of angry ignorant ideologues, the same people who tell you that all lives don't matter? What would happen if you gave them the keys to the U.S. economy? We don't need to guess about that, we are finding out now because all around us, there are signs that under this White House, things are getting dangerously flaky in our economy.\par \par \uc2 Here's one example.\par \par \uc2 The official government jobs numbers came out today. Unemployment is up, far higher than expected. Close to 14 million Americans say they want to work but cannot find a job. That's an old story. Here's the new twist.\par \par \uc2 At the very moment that unemployment is rising, fewer people are working, American businesses say they can't find employees and it is obvious they can't, go to the nearest strip mall and count the "Help Wanted" signs on the doors. They're everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Restaurants are closing because they can't find anyone to hire or manufacturing plants. That means American now has far too many workers, but simultaneously, far too few workers. How can that be?\par \par \uc2 Another question: what happened to all the consumer products? Many of the things that you buy and need to buy are more expensive than they've ever been. Inflation is real, it's galloping. Yet at the same time, a lot of products seem to have gone missing completely.\par \par \uc2 The United States is currently experiencing shortages of chicken, generators, roofing materials, lumber, ketchup, bicycles, diapers, ammunition, automobiles, plastics, appliances, chlorine, pork, propane tanks, among many other things.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, this is peacetime. We haven't mobilized for a foreign war. Yet, we're now getting reports that we should brace for a gas shortage this summer. Why is this all happening? Well, because things are so great in the economy. That's what they're telling us on television.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This week, there were more signs that the U.S. economy is roaring back to life and they come as President Biden is proposing trillions of dollars in new government spending.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER, CBS NEWS BUSINESS ANALYST: It's really good news, this week, we found out that in the first quarter of this year, the economy expanded at a 6.4 percent annualized rate. That is huge.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Could we see a great economy and a lousy return for the stock market?\par \par \uc2 KATIE KOCH, \uc2 GOLDMAN SACHS\uc2  ASSET MANAGEMENT: I think the good news for everybody is that we're actually probably going to see both, that we're going to continue to see a strong economy and we also are set up actually for some great equity market returns here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: There's an awful lot of lying in financial journalism. No one ever says that, but it is self-evident.\par \par \uc2 But they are telling us things are gr