s, great seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 Sits on the Finance Committee, Republican from Montana.\par \par \uc2 Fair and balanced, a view from a Democrat who says: Forget about spending too much. We're not spending enough.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you just heard from a Republican senator who says, right now, Democrats are spending too much. You have Bernie Sanders leading a host of others saying they're not spending enough.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  joins us right now, the California congressman, who is nice enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, I know you are joining those lawmakers who are urging an expansion of Medicare eligibility and all of that. But a lot of this stuff costs. And something like that could be hundreds of billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering, how do we pay for all this?\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): Well, it's going to save money, expanding Medicare.\par \par \uc2 If you talk to businesses and CEOs, they will tell you 20, 30 percent of their payroll is health care costs. They don't want to be in the health care business. That's why manufacturing is going offshore. It's why working-class wages have stagnated. So, I think it's going to free up small business and entrepreneurship.\par \par \uc2 But the way we pay for it, Neil, is what President Biden is proposing, which is commonsense measures. Three specifics. He's saying let's eliminate the step-up basis when you die. Right now, if I buy a million dollars of stock in \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , sell it for $10 million -- or hold on to it until I die, it becomes $10 million, then I give it to one of my kids, they don't pay capital gains tax on it.\par \par \uc2 That's absurd. He wants to close that. He wants corporations to pay some tax, not zero percent. And he wants for people who are over a million dollars -- making over a million dollars to equalize the capital gains and ordinary income tax rate.\par \par \uc2 And you know who did that? President Reagan in 1986. So, these are commonsense proposals.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Of course, we had a much lower capital gains tax rate, right?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I guess what I'm asking, though, is that I see the standpoint on the part of you and many others in your party to come up with creative ways to raise money, to raise taxes. Not nearly so creative when it comes to just pairing all that money that's going out.\par \par \uc2 Why is that?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Actually, we have proposed cuts. Our military budget is 53 percent of federal discretionary spending.\par \par \uc2 And I have gone -- I even went on Laura Ingraham's show, and she said that we need to cut some of the bloated defense budget. Pulling out of Afghanistan, which has been bipartisan, that's going to save us money, we shouldn't have been deployed overseas.\par \par \uc2 And I do think we need to make sure that money going out is effectively spent. And if Republicans want to work with us to make sure that's happening, I'm all for that.\par \par \uc2 But the key is -- and, look, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , who has warned about inflation, he said that we can spend up to $5 trillion -- that's \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  -- if it's productive investment, if we're building our roads, our bridges, providing Internet to rural America, competing with China.\par \par \uc2 Why aren't we talking about what China is spending? Look at what they're doing. We keep talking about the Chinese competitive threat. Look at what they're spending. Do we want to be left behind?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, they don't run deficits, and they don't have debt, right?\par \par \uc2 So, I guess what I'm asking you, Congressman, is, if this keeps going at this pace, we won't be able to afford this. With all the tax increases and everything else the president's proposed on the rich and those with $400,000 income and the capital gains thing and all that, for all of that, it is still little more than half the monies that are being appropriated to spend down the road.\par \par \uc2 Is it fair to say, sir, that other people are going to have to start kicking in dough, not just the rich?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: No, I don't think so, because I think the productive investments are going to lead to an extraordinary amount of growth.\par \par \uc2 And when you have a low interest rate, low inflation environment, and if you're investing in things that are going to grow the economy 3, 4, 5 percent, then that's going to expand the tax base and will allow us to pay down our debt.\par \par \uc2 And so I guess it depends on, do you believe in the possibility of American economic growth? I believe in it. I believe that you can grow the economy, just like President Reagan.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you would have to have lot of growth. But you would have to have a lot of growth, right, Congressman? For it to pay for all of this spending, you would have to hope for something like an Internet boom that the Clinton administration enjoyed to get anything like that, right?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: You would have to hope for the type of growth we saw in the 1980s and 1990s.\par \par \uc2 But I believe we can do it. I believe, with A.I....\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: ... with quantum computing, with synthetic biology, if we get -- if we have the Internet go to rural America, think of all the millions of talented Americans who are not participating in the economy right now and don't have the opportunities who would have those chances.\par \par \uc2 I believe we can have 4 percent, 5 percent growth. And I guess -- I guess, if you don't believe that, if you believe in secular stagnation, and you don't think America is going to grow, then you would be more concerned.\par \par \uc2 But I believe we're going to boom as a country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will see.\par \par \uc2 We didn't get that kind of growth during the Obama years. We didn't get that kind of growth, a little, maybe a little more during the Trump years. So we'd have to go quite far back, right? But it's possible. I guess it's possible.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , thank you very, very much. Enjoyed having you on.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. We will see -- we will see how all that sorts out.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we're getting word now that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is indeed looking into of a probe of that police shooting in Maryland, but it's not done. It is not done by a long shot -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I just want to correct something I said before, that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is opening a civil rights probe into that police shooting in North Carolina. I might have said Maryland. I apologize for that.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, we have got Griff Jenkins in Elizabeth, North Carolina, with the very, very latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We are less than four hours from a curfew kicking in at 8:00 p.m. here in Elizabeth City, which, after six nights of peaceful protests, may cause some clashes with the police, as we have seen in other cities when they enforce these curfews.\par \par \uc2 And what they're likely going to be upset about was the Brown family attorneys today in a press conference releasing the results of an independent autopsy that showed Brown was shot five times, with the fatal shot being to the head.\par \par \uc2 Lead attorney \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2  described that shot this way. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP\uc2 , ATTORNEY FOR FAMILY OF ANDREW BROWN JR.: Now, you all know from the death certificate that it was a penetrating gunshot wound to the head.\par \par \uc2 But, attorney Sellers, is what they did not know was that it was a kill shot to the back of the head.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Now, Pasquotank County Sheriff Tommy Wooten, who has been under increasing pressure to release video and resign, putting the statement out saying: "I want answers about what happened as much as the public does. The private autopsy released by the family is important and I continue to pray for them during this difficult time. However, a private autopsy is just one piece of the puzzle.\par \par \uc2 "The independent investigation being performed by the SBI" -- that's the State Bureau of Investigation -- "is crucial. And the interviews, forensics and other evidence they gather will help ensure that justice is accomplished."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, last night, the protests were demanding, of course, Wooten release that video. We may get that eventually tomorrow. But it comes as FOX News' new poll shows that only 33 percent of Americans favor reducing funding for police, as opposed to moving into another area; 62 percent favor -- oppose that -- excuse me -- 33 percent favor, 62 percent oppose.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil, the big question is whether or not we will all see that video. Tomorrow at 10:00 a.m., a hearing to see if a judge will sign off, after the sheriff filed a motion yesterday to get it out to the public -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Griff Jenkins, Elizabeth City, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Griff, very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Want to bring you up to date on this development in the Persian Gulf right now, where three Iranian fast boats were approaching U.S. Naval vessels. It got pretty messy, got pretty heated. And it got pretty dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Our Jennifer Griffin will give you an update right after this.\par \par \uc2 You're watching "Your World."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Iran getting provocative again.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Griffin, our national security correspondent, on some, shall say, unsavory behavior? That's putting it mildly.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, we're getting some new details about an incident that occurred late last night in the North Arabian Sea in the Persian Gulf.\par \par \uc2 There were two U.S. vessels, a Navy vessel, the USS Firebolt, and a Coast Guard patrol boat, that were approached by three Iranian Revolutionary Guard navy boats. They're small gunboats. They were armed. And they started getting within 68 yards of the U.S. vessels.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. vessels tried to try to issue a warning, bridge-to-bridge radio warning, as well as through loudspeakers. Eventually, the U.S. vessel the Firebolt had to fire warning shots. That is when the Iranian boats disappeared.\par \par \uc2 But, again, this was a tense moment. It's something that hasn't happened in the Persian Gulf, with Iranian fast boats approaching U.S. vessels, for the past three years -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Jennifer, I want to continue pursuing this, because this is a big deal here.\par \par \uc2 Now, we are looking like we're going to be reengaging in those nuclear talks with Iran. Surely, it must be cognizant of that backdrop for an action like this. So it makes you wonder who is sort of firing the orders on this.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, I think it's very clear that there are certain forces in the region, as well as within the Iranian Revolutionary Guard Corps, that probably do not want negotiations to start again for the -- with regards to Iran's nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 And so you're seeing the Iranian Revolutionary Guard flexing its muscles. They don't like diplomats like \uc2 Mohammad Zarif\uc2  talking to the U.S. and other European negotiators. And so you're seeing a tension play out within Iran between the Revolutionary Guard Corps, who are trying to assert itself and provoke the U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 This was a tense moment just last night. And, again, we haven't seen something like this in the past three years.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I am wondering as well, then, what our response was then. It looks like we were taken aback by this. What did we do specifically? What are we promising since?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, I think, right now, you saw these -- this naval vessel, the USS Firebolt, show extreme -- very professional behavior, fired the warning shots.\par \par \uc2 And that's when the Iranian Revolutionary Guard naval vessels sped off. This used to be something that happened quite frequently, and they -- they're really more of an annoyance, a testing for the U.S. -- the Fifth Fleet, which is based out in Bahrain. There was a Coast Guard vessel with the USS Firebolt this time.\par \par \uc2 But, again, the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2 , very professional, fired the warning shots, and the Iranians bolted, if you will.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I did want to get into very quickly, if I could, Jennifer, these charges against \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , the former secretary of state, as a private citizen, was talking, lobbying with the Iranian higher-ups, some even said sharing secrets, Israeli secrets.\par \par \uc2 Do we know anything about that? He's denied it all, but what do we know?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, he's denied it.\par \par \uc2 And, remember, former Secretary of State \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  had a relationship with \uc2 Mohammad Zarif\uc2  back when they did negotiate the nuclear accord.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: So, that relationship proceeded.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  denies that he revealed any secrets. Those Israeli airstrikes in Syria had been in open press reports. If we remember back to that time period, Israel was targeting inside Syria, and what -- and it had been reported from -- through the Israeli press that they had done.\par \par \uc2 So, Kerry's denying it. But, again, all of this is serving as a backup drop. Israel and certain elements of I