ty and this is "The Five."\par \par \uc2 It's yet another rush to judgment over a deadly police shooting. This is a tragedy. There is no denying that. The Democrats and the media racing to politicize an officer killing 16-year-old Michaela Bryant who had a knife in her hand and was lunging at another girl. Instead of waiting for all the facts, the White House said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: She was a child. We are thinking of her friends and families and the communities that are hurting and grieving her loss. We know that police violence disproportionally impacts black and Latino people and communities and that black women and girls like black men and boys experience higher rates of police violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And now liberals in the media are trying to downplay the dangers of knife fights. Former Obama White House advisor Valerie Jarrett tweeting "A police officer immediately decided to shoot her multiple times in order to break up a knife fight." And a CNN guest said this --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: And the question we have to ask yourself, what if it were your daughter? What if it were your child? What if it were a member of your family, your neighbor in an essentially teenage fight, a schoolyard fight?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But maybe they should listen to the girl wearing pink who could have been stabbed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: She (BLEEP) came at me with a knife.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: She came out -- at you with a knife earlier?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: No, she just -- that's why the police did it because she came at - -\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: That lady on the floor?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: -- she came after me.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: With a knife?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yes. So he got her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, well, first of all, it's not a knife fight when only one person has a knife. That's a butcher shop. All right. So, Juan Williams, you're an officer, you get out of the vehicle and you see mayhem. There is a guy that's kicking a girl down to the ground and another woman lunging at a defenseless woman like this with a sharp blade, you have seconds to react. She doesn't put the knife down when you warn her. What does Juan Williams, Officer Juan Williams do to save the ladies life in pink?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, I guess I would shoot the gun and not necessarily at somebody, but may be shoot the gun and maybe, you know, run at the person and try to disarm them, like, I don't know. I mean -- saving someone's life --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Wait, wait, you would shoot the gun in the air like a warning shot?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, hopefully to distract her, to try to stall or something so I could get, or my partner could get the knife away I see is what I would - - well, I don't know. I mean, taking someone's life is pretty strong.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't know if that would work, but okay.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't either -- I don't either, Jesse. I mean, Jesse, policing is tough work, but all I'm saying is --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- you know, I think that that woman with a knife is a danger to society and certainly a danger to the other person and we want her to stop and be disarmed. I just also think that killing a human is pretty radical. I don't think that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 But what I see overall here, you know, you asked me what I see. I see people now using this Ohio case which is messy as you and I just discussed, to somehow really try to change the subject, you know, from what happened in Minnesota with the Chauvin guilty verdict with the George Floyd murder on tape, which is right now prompting a lot of eyes to open in America to the idea that really, there is a problem with policing, that what black people have been saying about police brutality and abuse and lack of accountability for police to engage in that kind of behavior is a real problem in America.\par \par \uc2 So I think a lot of people just want to change that conversation by looking at this case where there it's not clear who is in the right and wrong. All that we know with is, you know, with some certainty that deadly force had to be used on a lot of people and saying, gosh, you know, why do we have to do that every time?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't know, Greg, if people are trying to change the conversation. This conversation was thrust upon us by really disgusting headlines that made it look like this was a racial execution. And NBC actually got caught deceptively editing the video to make it look like the girl didn't have a knife. And they edited the 911 call.\par \par \uc2 Again, we are back to cleaning up another racially charged story that has been foisted upon us in order to divide us.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: The only time the media is interested in looking at the complexities of this stuff is when they are caught out on it, right? No one change the subject. No one change the subject. The media embraced the story and they pushed it to the forefront because they thought it was going a certain way.\par \par \uc2 And then suddenly when it went another way, people started deleting their tweets, right? But the real -- and the fact is Juan says these stories are messy. We've been saying that every time we have eight or 10 of these a year. We go these are each -- one of these cases is so different, but it's the media that is trying to make them sound exactly the same and it's not.\par \par \uc2 It is messy, but here is the real outrage. We have lowered our expectations so much that we deem a knife fight as an acceptable part of childhood. If that is true, right, we need to examine these childhoods instead of making disgusting excuses. We saw two narratives collide last night.\par \par \uc2 Police shooting a suspect which the media obsesses over. Black on black violence, which the media ignores. But you can't have the former without the latter. Police end up at the end of these people's lives because they were called there due to the violence that the media ignores.\par \par \uc2 Cops end up in situations caused by the problems that are dismissed. So they end up there because it's their job to be there at the end. And no, it's not Juan or me that has to rush in and not make this decision. It's that guy. He can't ignore it. He has to show up and risk everything to. Imagine if we honestly tackle the problems at its root, the piss poor education, the violence, mental wellness, family structure.\par \par \uc2 What if we examined the variables that end up with the police there at the end of a person's life rather than dismiss it as a -- dismiss it as a harmless knife fight or what Jen Psaki said. Those are the variables that contribute to this because the more you dismiss, the variables that lead to this horrible tragedy, the more it keeps coming.\par \par \uc2 This is not about a police officer. This is about everything, everything that led to that moment. And if you deny that, you are propagating this problem. You are part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And so, Dagen, Greg makes an excellent point which he does once in a while, and that is that if you reduce the number of 911 calls coming from these communities, you're going to reduce the chances of these horrific, very messy, officer involved shootings. Isn't that right?\par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX NEWS HOST: Right, and people will die if the police are not there for them. I -- you can look at that video one of two ways. That was a police officer saving the life of a young, black woman.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: And you know what, when the police aren't there, people die. In another part of Ohio earlier this week, in Cincinnati, a 13-year-old was stabbed to death by another 13-year-old. The girl who died, Nyaira Givens, she was stabbed by another who's been charged with murder. I'll leave it at that.\par \par \uc2 But what's happened with this -- the left-wing liberal framing of all of this, and this is their modus operandi, that they get ahead of the actual facts and that you -- or ignore them or lied to make sure that you shape the story to their narrative at all cost.\par \par \uc2 So the narrative is cops are evil, this country is racist, down with America and then the preferred outcome is that we destroy the nation as it exist right now to rebuild it. It's a craved (ph) power grab. Ridiculous if it wasn't so insidious.\par \par \uc2 But I'll point to the verdict in the Derek Chauvin trial, guilty on all counts. Justice for George Floyd's family certainly. But see, that pointed to the fact that the justice system is not systemically racist, that a cop who oversteps his power and kills another individual will be brought to justice and will be sent to prison for the rest of his life.\par \par \uc2 But see, that didn't fit the left's narrative. So they're trying to -- they tried to change the subject with this shooting, this police shooting in Ohio. So what does the left do? The tensions are cooling, you got to bust out that canister of kerosene. You got to keep the lie alive, yell louder.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, Martha, one of the things that I found pretty disturbing was watching a lot of African Americans pundits look at this video and basically come to the conclusion that we should just let them stab each other. Don't get involved. You know, don't try to break it up. You know, knife fights happen. Let's just let them slash each other to death. That was pretty shocking to hear.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, unfortunately, you know, I mean, as Dagen just brought up the other case that she talked about. I looked up the number today of young people under the age of 18 who have been killed in violent events like this in 2021. There is 441 children that you have -- you never hear about.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  does not show up for their funerals. The White House never talks about them. And this police officer intervened in a situation that might have ended like that did. You know, this is a tragedy. Obviously, this young person lost their life.\par \par \uc2 And the bottom line is that we don't know what happened here yet. So this is why I don't think anybody should walk to the podium at the White House and make judgments about what happened.\par \par \uc2 I'd like to see the kind of responsibility that we used to see when situations like this were dealt with where you say, look, we see what's on this video and there is an investigation underway and we are keeping an eye on it and we're going to find out, we're going to see where it leads. We're going to allow them to do this.\par \par \uc2 I'm watching to see what happens to a man named Ned Pettus. Ned Pettus is the Columbia safety officer, Columbus, Ohio safety officer. And he urged everyone not to rush to judgment. And we all saw what happened to the city manager in Minneapolis who said the same thing, who said hold on, everybody, let's just go through the process. Let's see what happened in this situation. Let's go through it play by play. And he lost his job.\par \par \uc2 So, I hope Ned Pettus doesn't lose his job for urging a little bit of circumspect, a little bit of caution, a little bit of maturity and responsibility when we look at these situations, to not rush to judgment. Everybody that you just showed who's already been talking on TV, they all know what happened I guess.\par \par \uc2 Well, they weren't there, and they don't know exactly what happened. All they know is what they see in that video and there is a lot more to the story to be told.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, and we'll follow that as it develops. Coming up next, hold the hot dogs and the hamburgers.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Hey, Jesse --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: President Biden is threatening to cancel your Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: President Biden is warning Americans they may have to cancel their Fourth of July celebrations. Even ones outdoors if not enough people get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: To celebrate our independence from this virus on July 4th with family and friends in small groups, we still have more to do in the months of May and June. We all need to mask up until the number of cases goes down until everyone has a chance to get their shot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: And while the fully vaccinated president insists on wearing mask outside, multiple liberal publications are now starting to question outdoor mask mandates. One even calling it meaningless political theater. But the CDC director is still unsure about the science.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS HOST: Do you think that if you are outside and not close to people you still need to wear a mask?\par \par \uc2 DR. ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , CDC DIRECTOR: You know, this is a question that we are looking at. We will be looking at the outdoor masking question, but it's also on the context of the fact that we still have people who are dying of COVID.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Greg, you're outside, buck naked, communing with nature all by yourself, you better have a mask on.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I always of. That's my typical Sunday. Look, Joe is more out of step on COVID than then going up to the Air Force One steps. The idea of remaining inside when the weather is good will contribute to more deaths, right? There is science to that. The science says it's extremely rare to contract the disease when you are outside.\par \par \uc2 It's like almost impossible. It's hard to even document the cases. And still, we see people walking around outside. If you send people, old, young, sick, healthy to their apartments or to their homes, you are only spreading the disease.\par \par \uc2 This is an indoor illness, not an outdoor one. And I will say this and then I'll shut up. We are hypocrites when we are giving our own advice on this because all of us, I believe have been vaccinated. Some of us have gotten COVID and been vaccinated which essentially makes you superhuman.\par \par \uc2 Lecturing people on the science as we are sitting in our isolated boxes, is it really correct? I think that -- it's like we have to -- we had to send a message. I mean, what message are we sending by being separate right now? I think it's time to return to the studio.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There we go.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And start seeing each other, looking at each other in the eye when we're talking so we know we're not interrupting each other. So we can actually -- I feel like every day we are in a batting cage and I would rather like to be passing the ball around. We weigh the benefits and the risks. So let's get back in the studio. There is no science.\par \par \uc2 We have the vaccines, and we have the rapid testing. There is no reason for us to be doing this all the time unless it's legal B.S., which is probably the case for everything in life. We're controlled by lawyers. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think I was going to say, I think most of us are here so maybe we should just like, in a commercial break --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You're next to --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- come down. My gosh.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You're like 10 yards away from me, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm going to come over. We'll finish this show over there.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Martha, keep going, keep going.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I totally agree. Yes, I had \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  on yesterday and he said that if