n what we've heard.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And would smell like would. You know, Martha, one way to reduce the emissions is to keep us locked down, you know. How interesting is that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Except \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  because he's very important. And he can't get to his meetings unless he has a private jet, but you all understand. What I think is fascinating here is China, right? So, China watches this, and they're trying to compete with us militarily, economically, in every way possible.\par \par \uc2 So, they say -- they chimed in there -- and one of the deputies in the Chinese government said, you know, we have 1.5 billion people here. I just don't think it's realistic for us to try to do this. And I thought, well, that's that, you know, I mean, they're not even going to attempt this. They said, we're going to keep going up, up, up in our emissions until 2030. And then we're going to max out, we're going to peak in 2030, and then after that, we'll come back and we'll chat with you about maybe cutting back.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, it's just -- they love it. It's great for their economy, and they're just going to watch us close all these industries, and they just, it works perfectly for them.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. Juan, you know, I'm really hopeful that China will take inspiration from us and close all those coal plants that they're building right now, because I think they're going to see the magic of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's ideas in the Green New Deal. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, there's no way that we can enforce it, but that's the idea. And that's what you just heard from \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  today. He said they were going to have strict limits on use of coal in China. I mean, you know, he might sell you a bridge or something, but let's see what happens. But we got to try to get people on board.\par \par \uc2 And today it was inspiring to see that you had not only the Chinese leader, you had the Russian leader. But yo