 about that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Sorry, Dagen.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That is it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hello, Bret.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020210426eh4m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Sen. Bill Cassidy\uc2  (R-LA)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,111 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Now the implications of this with \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , the Republican Louisiana senator.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of all of this, Senator?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (R-LA): Boy, have you ever heard of tax-and-spend Democrats?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: What yours is mine and what`s mine is mine?\par \par \uc2 Clearly, Republicans lowered taxes, and we had incredible economic growth. Before COVID hit, we had record rates of employment among every group, including the disabled, women, veterans, African-Americans, Hispanics, you name it.\par \par \uc2 And we had record -- we had wage growth disproportionally in the lower income. That`s what happens when you let people keep their own money, invest it as they see fit.\par \par \uc2 Now they wish to take this money, filter it through the government agencies, of course, public employees do pretty well, and then give it to the favored few, hoping that we do well.\par \par \uc2 It is a difference in philosophy. Unfortunately, I think the average working person will be the person who suffers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, there`s that old line, Senator, that elections have consequences. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was elected. He telegraphed a lot of this. We didn`t quite see the 43.4 percent thing coming.\par \par \uc2 But now it`s playing out. Do you think they have a chance to get this through, even if they vote it along just party lines?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: If they vote it along party lines, clearly, they could.\par \par \uc2 I think -- I`d like to think they won`t, because the economic consequences of creating such uncertainty in investment is going to just send signals that jobs are going to be killed.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the totality of what they`re doing today, let`s move our oil and gas industry to other countries, and so all those oil and gas jobs are dead in Louisiana, Texas, Oklahoma, you name it. We`re going to tax people who invest in our country, encouraging them not to invest.\par \par \uc2 Sooner or later, all this is going to chill job growth. And there`s only so much that large government programs can do. Sooner or later, it has to be the private sector. It`s like they`re intent on killing the private sector.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Democrats turn around, Senator, and say, of Republicans, well, they`re fine ones to talk about excessive spending and not paying for things. At least we have proposals out there that have highlighted ideas how we pay for things. We`re going to go after the rich. We`re not really very sympathetic to the rich. They can afford it. They should do it.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Yes, so a couple things.\par \par \uc2 Recall \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , right when Trump took office, and there just was an immediate upsurge in investment. And \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  said the animal spirits are back, now -- alluding to John Maynard Keynes.\par \par \uc2 What happened under Obama, there was such uncertainty, that people who had wealth chose not to invest. So, Trump created certainty. A Republican Congress created uncertainty, and investment took off, and so did jobs.\par \par \uc2 Your Charles Payne was talking about all the uncertainty. That trickles down to less investment. You may think it`s taking it to the rich. The rich are going to do OK. It is the person whose livelihood depends upon a new construction job because somebody invests, it is she or he who will not -- who will bear the price of this.\par \par \uc2 Folks will feel great: Oh, we stuck out to the rich.\par \par \uc2 No, it`s the working family that gets stuck. And we have seen that over and over. It is their big lie.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, just to be fair, during the Obama years, we did come off the financial meltdown. And the markets did do quite well under his eight years, when all was said and done.\par \par \uc2 But, having said that, you raise a good point about the creativity in raising taxes, not so much in addressing spending. So, we`re very great at coming up with ways to get more money from folks, including raising capital gains taxes and all, but no one, no one seems to be reining in the spending part.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Well, first, regarding the Obama years, yes, the market did well.\par \par \uc2 Look at those working families. They did not do as well. And it wasn`t until the fracking boom came along, ironically enough, that you begin to have job growth again in those folks who are considered working. The markets will take care of themselves. Rich people will do OK.\par \par \uc2 It is the working family I`m concerned about.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I get a feeling, though, Senator, that this administration might not care about the markets at all. Never quotes them. Never addresses them.\par \par \uc2 So, their view might be, who cares? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: It might be, who cares, except, as Charles pointed out, as you did, wait a second. The person who depends upon that pension fund or upon their 401(k) doing well, they`re caught in the downdraft as people exit the stock market.\par \par \uc2 They are going to care. Let`s put it this way. They should care when they think about the impact upon middle-income families getting caught in the downdraft of a market sell-off.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Senator, I want to thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 But there`s one final closer on the cost of all of this and what you think about the impact and where we`re going here is just to go ahead and crunch the numbers.\par \par \uc2 The $370 billion -- I want to thank the senator again for joining us.\par \par \uc2 The $370 billion they hope to make on this in the next 10 years is dwarfed by the trillions more in planned spending. So, even raising these taxes, even to 43.4 percent, a top individual rate of 39.6 percent on the wealthy, the surtaxes to pay for Obamacare that would remain in effect, the 3.8 percent there, even allowing for that, you have raised but a third of the money you`re going to need to pay for everything else.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210422eh4m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Worker Shortage?; Immigration Battle; DOJ Launches Probe Into Minneapolis Police Department; Interview With Former Housing and Urban Development Secretary Ben Carson\b0\par\par\uc2 Gianno Caldwell, Ashley Webster, Kristin Fisher, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,366 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: After the verdict, the backlash\par \par \uc2 Right now, Derek Chauvin looking at what could be quite a few years in prison. We don't know the details yet and what the judge will administer. What we do know is, there has been fast and serious fallout in Washington, with the attorney general of the United States, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , announcing a probe into policing practices in the city of Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the president was bemoaning systemic racism that he wants corrected, and corrected now.\par \par \uc2 We will be speaking with Ben Carson on the implications of all of this, what the former HUD secretary thinks about potential overreach here.\par \par \uc2 But, in the meantime, let's get the latest from Kristin Fisher on all of these measures to respond and take advantage of, critics say, a very sore situation -- Kristin.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN FISHER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, President Biden says that yesterday's verdict is definitely a step in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 But he also says that there is still much more work to be done to address what he describes as the systemic racism embedded in U.S. institutions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It was a murder in the full light of day, and it ripped the blinders off for the whole world to see the systemic racism the vice president just referred to, the systemic racism that's a stain on our nation's soul, the knee on the neck of justice for black Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FISHER: So, President Biden is really using this moment to try to pass the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Justice in Policing Act. And all eyes are now on Capitol Hill, where the bill has passed the House, but is still stuck in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The bill would be in choke holds and no-knock warrants. It would also eliminate qualified immunity for police officers. That's the biggest sticking point for many Republicans. But it appears that a compromise could be close.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator Tim Scott, who put forward his own police reform proposal back in June, said today -- and I quote -- "I think we are on the verge of wrapping this up in the next week or two, depending on how quickly they respond to our suggestions."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, over at the Justice Department, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has just announced a sweeping investigation into the entire Minneapolis Police Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Yesterday's verdict in the state criminal trial does not address potentially systemic policing issues in Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Now, remember, the Justice Department was already investigating the officers involved in \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 's death to see if they had committed some sort of civil rights violation against him.\par \par \uc2 But now what this new investigation is going to do is look at the entire Minneapolis Police Department to see if there is a pattern or practice of unlawful or unconstitutional policing -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Kristin Fisher, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Kristin at the White House on all of that.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to \uc2 John Yoo\uc2  right now, the former deputy assistant attorney general, \uc2 University of Berkeley\uc2  law professor.\par \par \uc2 John, this probe into police practices, it seems open-ended. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO\uc2 , FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, Neil, it is -- it has to be limited in this sense. It's not about isolated wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 If Derek Chauvin is an example of someone who was a rogue cop -- and there was testimony by Minnesota Police Department -- Minneapolis Police Department officers saying he had violated practice and procedure of the department -- then a pattern and practice investigation is not called for.\par \par \uc2 This is an investigation into the structure and the way that the whole department does its job. And this is another example also that elections have consequences. As I think your reporter mentioned, the Bush -- I'm sorry -- the Trump administration had pulled back from these kinds of investigations.\par \par \uc2 There was concern that the federal government was becoming too intrusive in the way that state and local police departments were doing their job. The Biden administration has made clear they are going to start doing those investigations again, they're going to start taking intrusive looks into way police departments do their jobs, and they're going to go to court and try to create rules that police departments are going to have to follow.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, indeed, this \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Act that they want to push through Congress would essentially expand this obviously way beyond the Minneapolis area.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering where all of this legally goes.\par \par \uc2 YOO: No, it's a good question, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Even if Congress doesn't pass any new legislation, this pattern and practice lawsuit or investigation does give Washington, D.C., does give the Justice Department the ability to review the way police departments all around the country do their work.\par \par \uc2 A lot of these never get to court. Basically, what the Justice Department is trying to do is to get these local and state departments -- police departments to enter what are called consent decrees with the federal government, where they agree to change the way they use force, to change their policing strategies and so on.\par \par \uc2 And then the federal government and the states create a whole new system of policing. And it never goes through, say, Congress. It doesn't really get to court.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, John, this is more a legal question, even though it seems like I'm asking like a dictionary-related question.\par \par \uc2 Systemic racism, systemic slighting, disfavoring targeting minorities, what is the legal definition of that? And if that is what you are addressing, what -- where does it go?\par \par \uc2 YOO: You're actually making an excellent point, because systemic racism is not in the federal law here. It's a code provision called 14141.\par \par \uc2 It talks about systematically violating someone's constitutional rights. And it has to be -- again, it can't just be Derek Chauvin and the other officers he was -- were with made mistakes, or actually intentionally tried to kill \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It has to be something in the way the department as a whole does its job. For example, if the department as a whole in Minneapolis was using choke holds in a way that was really unsafe, and they were being trained to do it, that's the kind of thing that would be a systematic violation of constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 But systematic racism is a rhetorical term. That's a political term that the president is using and the vice president is using. It has no real counterpart in the way the Justice Department is supposed to carry out its job here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 John Yoo\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The former deputy assistant attorney general, \uc2 John Yoo\uc2 , on that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, it was the video that did him in.\par \par \uc2 How many times did you hear that about what ultimately doomed Derek Chauvin's chances? The fact of the matter is, videos have played a key part in key cases across the board, especially lately, whether it was the Chauvin with his knee on \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 's neck, or the Daunte Wright case, also in Minnesota, where an officer says she missed took a handgun for a Taser, or a black and Latino army officer pepper-sprayed back in December during a traffic stop in Virginia, a 16-year-old girl believed to be brandishing a knife shot dead by police in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 A pattern here that is built on powerful video.\par \par \uc2 Pat Brosnan, the former New York Police Department detective, with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Pat, I was just thinking of you that we didn't have this kind of access to video way back when. It was a big deal, certainly in the 1990s, with the Rodney King arrest or the Rodney King beating filmed by a bystander with a video camera that changed this entire matter.\par \par \uc2 But what do you make of it now and the impact these videos have?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK BROSNAN, FORMER NYPD DETECTIVE: Well, I got to tell you, Neil, camera phones and bystander videoing is really the ultimate double-edged sword, because the captured video and corresponding audio can either affirm or refute alleged inappropriate or criminal behavior.\par \par \uc2 So, the video has the ability to be a very powerful and compelling eye and ear witness on both sides of the equation, refute or affirm.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It also -- in the Chauvin case, we had many such videos, right, from virtually every angle, dozens of them that were played in court, and, certainly, in the one that we came famous or infamous, the entire nine minutes and 20 -- 29 seconds caught on camera here.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm just wondering where this kind of technology takes us, because that's a fairly black-and-white issue. We see what seems to be something senseless. Other times, it's kind of in the eye of the beholder, like this girl brandishing a knife, and she's -- ended up shot by a policeman.\par \par \uc2 What are the limitations with this?\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: So, the -- there are limitations. And, like anything, there's pros and cons.\par \par \uc2 A couple of the central problems with the capture either to refute or affirm on a criminal behavior allegation is that video, first of all, can be manipulated. Video can be -- audio can be muffled. Audio can be deleted. There's a lot of manipulation that can happen.\par \par \uc2 But one of the central problems, Neil, is that the video may not provide the beginning, the middle and the end of the story. And it's absolutely critical that all elements of the story are presented.\par \par \uc2 When they're bifurcated or deleted, you have only a partial story. And what you don't have then is factual context. And without factual context, there may be central elements of the story -- of the story that may deliberately not have been provided, or indeliberately, but either way, say, for instance, a threatening crowd or the existence of a weapon that's outside the focus of the camera.\par \par \uc2 That's a real problem. So, bifurcated video, video that does not provide the entire story, or the story in its totality can only be partially informing in many cases.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, now it's standard procedure. Police have these bodycams on them. Their cars, vehicles are -- have them as well.\par \par \uc2 I believe part of the strategy here in the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Act is to expand that even more. How would you go about doing that?\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: Well, better technology, number one, 360-degree video, as well as video that's aligned with other videos. So, the bodycam might align with a dashboard cam. Dashboard cam might align with a vehicle cam that's strategically positioned in the front or the sides of the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 And, in totality, you would be able to cobble together the separate pieces of video and be able to tell, as a factual matter, the exact story of what transpired from the very beginning to the very end, with no omissions, no deletions, no ambiguity.\par \par \uc2 And that's the way to go. That's the way to go. It really is.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: So, it's going to be enhanced technology.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that they're all in -- that they're all in sync.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: And it's coming.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That they're digitally in sync.\par \par \uc2 BROSNAN: Correct. Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 Pat Brosnan, thank you very, very much, a former NYPD detective, Pat Brosnan.\par \par \uc2 Well, businesses, in fact, the entire business community, increasingly speaking out on this, but it can boomerang as well.\par \par \uc2 We will explore that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Corporate America responding to the Chauvin verdict yesterday.\par \par \uc2 And pretty much to a company, the theme was fairly consistent, welcoming the verdict as a sign that maybe society can move forward. As the Twitter CEO put it, that this might provide some justice in the long -- in the long road and that, while it can't bring back \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 , it can avoid future George Floyds.\par \par \uc2 Gianno Caldwell has been looking through all of these tweets and all of this corporate reaction and weighs the significance of all of that.\par \par \uc2 They're all weighing in with kind of the same message, Gianno. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GIANNO CALDWELL, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 Well, I agree with the verdict that happened yesterday. I thought that Derek Chauvin did, in fact, kill \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 . So I can agree with that.\par \par \uc2 But the thing about companies weighing in on the Georgia law, as an example, or this case, once you get started in that and you begin to take sides, it becomes problematic. For one, your customer base, people may disagree with you.\par \par \uc2 And, two, when a lot of companies are jumping on the diversity and inclusion bandwagon, which nothing wrong with them being about diversity and inclusion, but that's when people begin to look into their homes, if you will, their corporate structures, and notice that a lot of it is all talk.\par \par \uc2 So, with that, it becomes very problematic for a lot of these corporations, because, again, they're using these instances to boost sales or look better for their branding and their investors. And that, I think, is disingenuous.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, should they comment at all? What do you think their responsibility should be?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Well, honestly, honestly, I think, with matters of politics or even a criminal case, if it doesn't impact your business, or if it's not something related to you, I think they should stay out of it.\par \par \uc2 And that's why we have seen backlashes when it comes to companies that do this, whether it be they make -- they support a policy that's for the right or the left. You see that level of backlash that occurs.\par \par \uc2 And that's why they should just, simply put, stay out of it, Neil. That's what I do believe.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so you found out what this is like when you do put your opinion out.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It was an opinion shared by a majority of whites and blacks and everyone in between...\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... who thought that this officer's use of force was excessive.\par \par \uc2 But you got an earful, and this is a family program, but a lot more, from people who just didn't -- didn't agree with you.\par \par \uc2 Could you explain?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: You know what?\par \par \uc2 And I was very shocked. If people want to see the comments, you can go to my \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  @GiannoCaldwell. I encourage people to follow me there.\par \par \uc2 But especially on my \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , I got almost 500 comments for simply putting, "Thank God justice was served, Derek Chauvin guilty on all charges."\par \par \uc2 And I was gaslighted. I couldn't believe it. People are not operating in facts. We're talking about people who are part of the conservative base here, people who are friends of mine that I have seen time and time again, who say that we are a party of law and order.\par \par \uc2 If we're a party of law and order, then the facts state that the jury has declared that Derek Chauvin was guilty on all charges. He is the reason that \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  is dead today.\par \par \uc2 Why is it that people are amplifying these conspiracy theories, saying that...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, a lot of them thought -- and a lot of them thought, Gianno, the ones that I was reading, a lot of them thought that he had nothing to do with it. And I thought that that was a bit of a stretch.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: I'm seeing comments on my \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , again, @GiannoCaldwell, where people say that \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  actually killed himself.\par \par \uc2 What is this? Who comes out with this irrational garbage? We should not be in this place right now. This is the reason why people are making fun of some of this conservative speech, because people are literally operating in conspiracy theories that are not based in any facts.\par \par \uc2 If you believe that George -- the Derek Chauvin trial was not fair, then provide evidence. We live in a country where we have seen juries convict mobsters, terrorists, gang members, all kinds of folks who can literally target their families.\par \par \uc2 We have not heard any reporting about that being done in the Derek Chauvin case. And if that -- if that happened, then show the evidence. We have to be a party of law and order. And that should be consistent across the board.\par \par \uc2 I shouldn't see this kind of garbage from conservatives. I expect to see things like this on the other side, but not with my own party. Therefore, I have to speak out against this kind of stuff, because this is a very serious moment in our country, one where we should be uniting as Americans, not being divided over something that simply isn't true.\par \par \uc2 Facts are facts. You can't change that. So, don't try to gaslight me on social media because you disagree with simple facts. It's inappropriate. And it's not what our movement should be about.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Were you offended by a couple of them who said you harbored that view because you're an African-American?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 And I'm an American. I'm a human. I'm a Christian. I am an African- American. And I think logically about the -- and I come to logical conclusions.\par \par \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  may be alive today if it wasn't for Derek Chauvin putting his knee on his neck for nine minutes. He had his knee on his neck for three minutes past after he died. We know that. There was 38 witnesses that came up that the prosecution called. They all said the very same thing.\par \par \uc2 So, why are we arguing with facts? We should be beyond that at this moment. We should be talking about police reform. We saw Tim Scott's statement. We have seen a number of other Republican leaders' statements, saying, it was the right verdict.\par \par \uc2 We should move on. Let's talk about police reform and ensure that something like this never happens again. And I'm calling on all of my conservatives to speak out loudly against this kind of foolishness. We shouldn't be a party of that. We are not conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 And we shouldn't be gaslighting individuals for having opinions which mainstream agrees with.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: God bless you, Gianno. Good or ill, you speak your mind, young man.\par \par \uc2 Gianno Caldwell, FOX News political analyst, very popular podcast...\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... so much, much more.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot coming up here, including what's happening on the vaccine front, some promising developments, but a plea from the president: Please get your vaccine, like, like now?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You have heard about all these Republicans who are not running for president if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  decides to run for his old job. Ben Carson might not be among them, but not for the reasons you think.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Ben Carson on that.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The time is now to open up a new phase of this historic vaccination effort.\par \par \uc2 To put it simply, if you have been waiting for your turn, wait no longer. Now's the time for everyone over 16 years of age to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that's the goal, at least, the president lining the fact, celebrating the fact that 200 million Americans have gotten at least one dose of a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 He just wants to see more people take advantage of that, including every adult 16 or older. But some people are fighting back. Some people have some concerns about the J&J vaccine, the blood clotting issue. That vaccine has been resumed in Europe, albeit with a warning, but not so here.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bob Lahita back with us, the St. Joseph Health Institute For Autoimmune and Rheumatic Diseases director.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, what did you make of the president's push today that we have kind of hit like a block or a bump in the road, and he wants to encourage particularly young people to get this vaccine?\par \par \uc2 DR. BOB LAHITA, ST. JOSEPH UNIVERSITY HOSPITAL: Yes, I agree, Neil. This is a big problem.\par \par \uc2 The demand has gone down. And, obviously, our approaching herd immunity is not going to be reached if we don't get a critical number of people, roughly 70 to 80 percent of the population, immunized. So we need to increase willingness.\par \par \uc2 About a fifth of people refuse, unless required. And I don't advocate requiring that people get mandated to get the vaccine. But it seems to be we have hit a bump in the road, we have hit a big pothole right now. And a lot of that is fueled by the skepticism from the J&J problem.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Doctor, the J&J thing, the \uc2 European Union\uc2  open to giving it another shot, so to speak, but with warnings, and you can decide for yourself whether you want that.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that approach?\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: Well, I think that's a legal approach. That's a good approach.\par \par \uc2 The blood clots in the brains of these patients is exceedingly -- and I say exceedingly with a capital E -- it's very rare. So, it's analogous to getting hit by lightning, but you're more likely to get hit by lightning than to have these blood clots form in your brain.\par \par \uc2 So -- but that has fueled skepticism. There's a lot of people out there that were just on the edge and waiting to hear what was going to happen. And I still hear from patients and everyone saying, well, we will give it a year, we will give it two years, we will see what happens to everybody.\par \par \uc2 That's not the approach we need in order to stop this virus from infecting everybody.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Doctor, what do you tell your younger patients, many of whom, if you're a certain age -- I can remember this -- it was like, actually, about a century ago, when I was their age -- where you feel indestructible, right?\par \par \uc2 You don't think it's for you. You know that young people are in pretty good shape with this. I mean, there have been isolated cases. I don't minimize it, but that they feel they don't need to.\par \par \uc2 Now, the president took a different tack with that crowd today by saying, well, if you care about your mom or your dad or your grandma or your grandpa, then maybe you should rethink that. What did you think of that approach?\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: I think that's a good approach.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of people over the age of 16 are skeptical. But believe it or not, it's not that population that is a difficult population to vaccinate. It's the people between the ages of, say, 25 to 40, the early -- I'm not going to say middle age, but the early group of people who are very skeptical.\par \par \uc2 They have had enough time to think about. They have -- they have had enough years to experience the flu vaccine and other vaccines. So, I think young people are not going to be that bad a group to convince.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, because if you're calling them middle-aged, Doctor, then I'm Noah.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, I just was a little worried there.\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: I was very careful.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Doctor, how do you think we're doing?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know that there are problems certainly abroad, in a place like India and Brazil, much of Europe, but we're doing pretty well here.\par \par \uc2 Some states have these bumps, and they're reimposing limited restrictions and the rest. But, by and large, I don't want to jinx it, we seem to be doing OK.\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: Yes, I would say we're doing very well; 200 million people vaccinated or having had one shot in a population of 350 million people, that's not bad, but we still have a ways to go before we can reach herd immunity.\par \par \uc2 Now, in a lot of the Southern states, in a lot of the Southwestern states, the numbers have decreased and the demand has gone down. And vaccine hesitancy is not what we need at the present time. We're on a roll. We need to get this done.\par \par \uc2 And people just need to get out there and get their shots.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Doctor, good advice, Dr. Bob Lahita.\par \par \uc2 Good seeing you again, my friend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAHITA: Good to see you, Neil. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you probably heard of the Marco Rubios -- thank you, Doctor -- the Marco Rubios and Nikki Haleys, who said, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs for president, they will not.\par \par \uc2 Now, not that Ben Carson is interested in running for president, doesn't seem like he is, but he doesn't think that should be a factor for any Republican.\par \par \uc2 He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, to hear President Biden tell it, systemic racism is a problem in this country and remains a stain on our nation.\par \par \uc2 I took that up with Ben Carson, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's former HUD secretary, what he made of that.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BEN CARSON, FORMER HOUSING AND URBAN DEVELOPMENT SECRETARY: No, not at all.\par \par \uc2 I think the United States of America is one of the least racist countries in the world. And, again, it's because we have made such amazing progress.\par \par \uc2 Remember, now, even though we did have slavery, this was a country that went to civil war over this issue. At a time when we only had 30 million people, we lost 600,000 people in a war over this issue. And half of those people were on the side of getting rid of slavery.\par \par \uc2 So, as you go down the annals of history from that time until now, you have to realize there are a lot of people in this country who think the right way, who've always thought the right way, have tried to do the right thing, have had love in their heart for their fellow man, not hatred, not a desire to cancel them, but a desire to help them and to lend a helping hand.\par \par \uc2 That's one of the reasons that America was able to flourish, even in the early days, when communities were separated by 50 miles, 100 miles. Why were they able to flourish? Because people with different skill levels, different talents were able to work together to build their community.\par \par \uc2 And if you don't have that, if you -- on the other hand, you just have people who just hate each other, it can never possibly lead to a good place.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A number of supporters of the former president are among those, even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  acknowledged in an interview with \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 , might be reluctant to take the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Now, he has consistently pushed people to take the vaccine. Maybe the issue has come up whether he should make an announcement, a Psaki, to say take the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that would be a good idea?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Well, President Trump has encouraged people to take the vaccine. You know, President Biden has encouraged people to take it.\par \par \uc2 I think people -- the problem is that they're getting inconsistent messages. And we have injected politics clearly into it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You mentioned politics.\par \par \uc2 I can't ignore of what's been happening as people look to 2024, Doctor, where, already, Nikki Haley, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  have indicated that, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs, they won't.\par \par \uc2 You were a presidential candidate. Do you feel the same way? If you ever entertained running again, whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is running or not, would that influence your decision?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Well, I have no interest in running.\par \par \uc2 I have a supreme interest in helping America be successful, which is why American Cornerstone Institute was developed. And that, we will be working with whoever is running, as long as they understand the principles, the cornerstone principles of faith, and liberty, and community, and life.\par \par \uc2 And we will push those things. And whoever's pushing those, that's who I'm going to support.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you're open? You're agnostic on the '24 race?\par \par \uc2 I mean, the reason why I ask is, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  apparently is gearing up a presidential run, whether the president does or not. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: There is no crown that goes automatically to anybody.\par \par \uc2 I think, hopefully, people will be objective. They will realize that there were a lot of amazing things that were done in the last four years. But people have a right to decide on their own, based on what they see, and an objective dissemination of information.\par \par \uc2 And there are some platforms that are being worked on right now that will allow objectivity, as opposed to censorship of information. And I think that will be very helpful for the 2022 and the 2024 elections.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You stayed throughout the entire term of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in your HUD position.\par \par \uc2 You never attacked fellow Republicans. You didn't go on political diatribes. You avoided that. You seemed to listen a lot more than talk, which makes you unique, certainly watching in Washington circles.\par \par \uc2 But I'm wondering, in this environment, where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has attacked some fellow Republicans, including \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , those who voted for his impeachment, et cetera, does that bother you on any level? Is that productive, in your eyes?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: No, I don't think it's useful.\par \par \uc2 I would encourage people to look at the big picture. We're not really talking right now so much about Republicans and Democrat. We're talking about people who understand what America is. It's not so much a place. It's an idea.\par \par \uc2 And we're talking about another group of people who don't like that idea and who think that it should be fundamentally changed to something else. And that's what people need to focus on, not on their little pet peeves, because those things are irrelevant, compared to what we're talking about now.\par \par \uc2 This is a major inflection point, in terms of which way our nation is going to go and what we're going to leave for our children and our grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Finally, Secretary, there's a strong plurality of Republicans, led by no less than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself, that thought the wrong person won the presidential election, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was robbed of that victory.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Well, I think it's certainly worthy of investigation.\par \par \uc2 What disturbs me is when people say, you can't even talk about that. It's like you stole the cookies from the cookie jar and then you said, no one can talk about the cookie jar or even go near the cookie jar. Well, that does raise your suspicion.\par \par \uc2 I would like to see any credible things investigated, so we can put it to rest once and for all.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You don't think there were enough investigations and enough calls to look into things, that just Republican- and Democratic-appointed judges alike examined and, in more than three dozen cases, shot each and every one down?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: That's what they would like you to believe, but they didn't really examine the evidence.\par \par \uc2 There were only two cases in which the evidence was actually examined by the judicial system. And both of those are still active. So, don't buy the narrative that they went and actually investigated it. It's not true. And that's what I'm saying.\par \par \uc2 We need to do a real open investigation, not try to hide things. If there's nothing to hide, there should be nothing to worry about.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, in your gut, Doctor, is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a legitimate president of the United States?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Well, it doesn't really matter what my gut says.\par \par \uc2 What matters is, are we going to be open and fair about this and about elections in general? We need to make sure that we have a system that half the people aren't skeptical of. And the only way to do that is to be open and fair. We have got to be transparent.\par \par \uc2 And if we keep hiding things, and we keep trying to punish people who want to know answers, all that does is increase the suspicion.\par \par \uc2 You know, these are things that we can learn from history. There are so many people who want to obliterate history. History gives you your identity. And your identity gives you your beliefs.\par \par \uc2 And we need to have strong beliefs to weather the storm that's coming by people who want to fundamentally change this nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK, Ben Carson.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot coming up, including what's happening in Arizona right now, the governor there just declaring a state of emergency because of what's happening at the border.\par \par \uc2 But, remember, it takes two to tango on a state of emergency. The president has not signed off on that. What that could mean -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. DOUG DUCEY\uc2  (R-AZ): President Biden, if you want to stop the disaster that's unfolding here and will only get worse in the coming months, President Biden, you should declare a national emergency and deploy the vast powers of your administration to stop what's happening here.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, I declared a state of emergency at the border. And I announced we're deploying the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, the governor of Arizona declares a national emergency at the border, and he is dealing with the best and the furthest he can as governor by bringing in National Guard troops.\par \par \uc2 But the president doing the same for Arizona would obviously double that impact, if not triple it. That has not happened as of yet.\par \par \uc2 Art Del Cueto joins us right now, the Border Patrol Union vice president.\par \par \uc2 Art, let me ask you, then, what would -- if the president did act in concert with the governor, declare what's happening at the border that something that warrants an emergency in Arizona, how would that affect what you do? What would it provide?\par \par \uc2 ART DEL CUETO, VICE PRESIDENT, BORDER PATROL UNION: Well, I mean, it would definitely affect it, where you can use some of these National Guardsmen in positions that they could relieve some of the agents, so the agents can get back to their line activities.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you, one of the issues is, I know the press conference was down there in Yuma, where the majority of these individuals are turning themselves in.\par \par \uc2 What a lot of people are failing to talk about, and I think it's a huge deal, is the got-aways across the country, specifically in Tucson sector.\par \par \uc2 So, Tucson sector right now, year to date, they can account for 55,000 got- aways. And that's just a guesstimate. So, when you're when you're going to deploy individuals down in Yuma, you have to start thinking, where is it that they're funneling in through, these got-aways? And it is through the Tohono O'odham Reservation in Tucson.\par \par \uc2 They just committed $35 million to a soft-sided facility in Tucson. And I can't think of any other best way to use this is have some of the National Guardsmen perhaps work in this soft-sided facility, so the agents can actually get back and do -- and start doing the line work.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know what I have noticed? I don't know what applies to the border along Arizona, but that many are coming through these holes or gaps in the fence or the wall that was under construction until \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came into office and stopped it, wouldn't provide any additional funds.\par \par \uc2 Do you have a rough idea how at many are coming in via that, these holes in the wall?\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: Well, when you're looking at the numbers, you're looking at over 150,000 year to date in -- that are got-aways across the United States. So that's a humongous number.\par \par \uc2 Those are the got-aways. Those are the individuals that have serious criminal backgrounds, and they're doing everything they can not to be apprehended. When you are seeing individuals turn themselves in and pretty much get released with no consequences, then you would have to know that the people that are desperate enough to come across and not want to be detected, those are your hardened criminals.\par \par \uc2 And no one seems to be talking about that. You have a lot of individuals that will down to the border, and the first thing they ask is, can I get pictures or can I see that some of these unaccompanied juveniles?\par \par \uc2 Well, what's really going to hurt us in the long run are the got-aways, the drugs that are coming in, the heroin, the meth, the fentanyl. And all that is happening because you have the drug cartels knowing that the agencies are preoccupied with some of these family units, unaccompanied juveniles.\par \par \uc2 And then they're just bringing their product and bringing the criminals right behind, when they know the agents are somewhere else. And no one wants to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How many of those drug cartel figures are getting in themselves?\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: I mean, that's a good question.\par \par \uc2 And when you're looking at the individuals that are crossing, it's not just people from Central America. It's not just people from Mexico. It's people from all over the world.\par \par \uc2 Just recently, you -- everyone saw the headlines when they were talking about individuals that were apprehended in the Southern border that were on the terrorist watch list. And that's what got apprehended. So, when you start looking at the numbers, and you see that it's 150,000 got-aways, 150,000 got-aways, how many of those individuals are coming in here with actual motive to do something bad to our country and American citizens?\par \par \uc2 At this point, it's not a political theater. It's -- they need to stop with, hey, we just want to take pictures at the border, we want to see what's going on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: And people need to sit down and fix the problem. And it takes both sides of the aisle to fix that problem right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 And, as you say, it only has to be a very, very, very small percentage of that 150,000, and you have got problems. You have got big problems, as you know better than I.\par \par \uc2 Art Del Cueto, the Border Patrol Union vice president.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Art. Hang in there.\par \par \uc2 DEL CUETO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When we come back, businesses trying to hang in there. Here's the good news. Their business is booming. Their restaurants are opening.\par \par \uc2 Here's the bad part. They can't find workers -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, this could be a case of good government intentions gone very, very bad.\par \par \uc2 All these extended unemployment and related benefits now has tightened the number of workers who would otherwise be available for restaurants that are opening up at a brisk pace to keep up with that brisk pace.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Webster in Ybor City, Florida, right now with how that is falling out.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Ashley.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY WEBSTER, FOX NEWS BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I'm at Bernini's in Ybor city, just outside Tampa. It's an Italian restaurant, very popular. Guess what? They have nine openings here, waitstaff, kitchen staff, bartenders, but they can't find anyone to fill those jobs. They have offered cash. They're offering $100 to come in and, if you get hired, you get the 100 bucks. And if you stay another 90 days, you get another $300.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't matter. It's not working. If you quickly look at the number of jobs that are still missing from the restaurant and bar industry, comparing February of last year, just before the pandemic, to last month, you will see the number of jobs that are still gone from this industry.\par \par \uc2 California almost up to 400,000 jobs still have not been replaced, New York, Illinois, Florida and Texas, as you can see, many, many thousands.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to the owner here, Jason Fernandez, who said to me: Look, I can't compete with free money and the benefits that potential workers are getting. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON FERNANDEZ, OWNER, BERNINI: It is one of the biggest challenges that we face right now in operations. We run ads. We do referral fees. Very difficult to compete with the free money.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Well, you know what? If you're looking for a job, there are a lot of pickings.\par \par \uc2 There's a -- well, he's moved now, but a bartender over here, Bob Burton, moved here from Nashville six months ago just wanting to find work. He didn't have a lot of trouble doing that. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOB BURTON, BARTENDER: No, it's -- I mean, I feel like a lot of people were still maybe getting money from an employment and they just don't want to go back to work. I mean, I personally work seven days a week. So...\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: You didn't want to do that?\par \par \uc2 BURTON: Work seven days a week?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: No, live off of benefits.\par \par \uc2 BURTON: Oh, no. I like to work. I feel good working.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Bob appears to be the exception, rather than the rule, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Those -- of course, those benefits extend through Labor Day. So, restaurants say they still expect to find a very difficult job finding people to actually work here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just incredible.\par \par \uc2 Ashley, I did want to remind you, though, that there are restaurants in New Jersey dealing with the same thing.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, for some reason, you gravitate to Florida.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I guess it was just the luck of the draw, I guess, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Luck of the draw.\par \par \uc2 But you know what? Someone has to do it, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: And it's got my name written all over it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Thank you, Clark Kent Webster doing his best to get these stories out to you.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Webster, great reporting, my friend.\par \par \uc2 But he's absolutely right. The longer these benefits continue, whatever you make of them, they are preventing these restaurants from having enough people to deal with the improvement that's very real and going on.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210421eh4l0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Should Workers Return to Office?; Homeland Security Secretary Visits Border; Examining MLB's Decision to Move All-Star Game; U.K. Nearing Herd Immunity?; Interview With Sen. Roger Wicker (R-MS); Interview With Gov. Brad Little (R-ID)\b0\par\par\uc2 Liz Peek, Gary Kaltbaum, Marty Makary, Benjamin Hall, Charlie  Gasparino, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,291 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Herd immunity, it's coming. But what if I told you it's coming Monday? The bad news is, it's not coming here. It's coming to Britain Monday.\par \par \uc2 New modeling that shows right now the United Kingdom will reach that historic event, again, next Monday. As to here, hard to say.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest right now from Benjamin Hall in London on the significance of this and what it really means -- Benjamin.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN HALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Look, herd immunity is what every country has been striving for. It is the goal. And if indeed it is true that the U.K. has reached it, well, it's happened a lot faster than predicted.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 University College London\uc2 , who has done this modeling, 50 percent of the population, of the adult population, has been vaccinated. Around 42 percent have been exposed to the virus and have antibodies, and about 10 percent have preexisting immunity.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to their modeling, that suggests taking into consideration overlap and those who no longer have the antibodies, 74 percent are now immune, and herd immunity is thought to be 75 percent.\par \par \uc2 However, other scientists are skeptical, the most pessimistic among them in the U.K. saying that current immunity levels are only 35 percent. The main disagreement there being over how many people have already had COVID.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we seem to have some problems out of there.\par \par \uc2 The significance of this can't be lost. A lot of people have been waiting for herd immunity here, a successful percentage or percentage enough of the population seems to have developed a resistance to this, an immunity to this, that everyone benefits. Could that happen here?\par \par \uc2 The significance of this right now with Karl Friston of the University College London professor, senior scientist behind this model.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, thank you very much for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 Could you explain the significance certainly of this and the magic of Monday? What's going to happen?\par \par \uc2 KARL FRISTON, \uc2 UNIVERSITY COLLEGE LONDON\uc2 : I think the magic of Monday, as you nicely phrase it, is, it probably slightly overstated.\par \par \uc2 I think the quantitative modeling that we're talking about can be read as a milestone, a milestone that is a testament to the -- both the efficacy and the efficiency of the rollout of vaccinations, certainly in the U.K., but from what I hear also, now in the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, really, it's really a marker or a reflection of how well we have done and the state of affairs. Clearly, a crisp threshold crossing on Monday is a little bit ridiculous. But it does reflect some of the issues that have just been mentioned in terms of how many people have now acquired immunity and the utility of being able to vaccinate very large numbers to put us in a much safer position.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is there such a thing as a percentage of the population that gets vaccinated that allows you -- we can time it to a day or an event -- I get that -- but to safely say we are at this point?\par \par \uc2 FRISTON: Yes, you can say that, but I think it needs to be very carefully qualified.\par \par \uc2 The kind of herd immunity threshold that we're talking about here is only relevant for the current transmission risk, the probability that, if you're infected, and I have -- I'm in close contact with you, what's the probability that you will transmit the virus to me?\par \par \uc2 And that clearly depends upon lots of fluctuating time-sensitive factors, the transmissibility of the virus that itself could reflect viral mutations or variants of concern. And I know that a lot of people in America will be very worried about the Brazilian variant at the moment.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, there's another time-sensitive determinant of that transmission risk, which is the seasons and where I'm socializing. Where am I in close social contact with you? And am I doing social distancing?\par \par \uc2 So, there are lots of factors, both purely epidemiological, but also sociobehavioral, that actually determine the transmission risk that itself than determines the herd immunity threshold.\par \par \uc2 So, that's why I refer to this as a milestone, which is a useful measure of how we are doing in terms of a population or a community. It should not really be read as job done. The job is ongoing--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 FRISTON: -- and requires lots of different -- interactions of very many different factors, a lot of which depends and rests upon our communal and also personal responses to the levels of infection.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Feel free to correct me if I'm wrong on this, Mr. Friston, but we were told that, in Britain, the emphasis was at least getting one dose out of a given vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 's a little different, because it's a one-dose vaccine anyway, but that the priority was get as much and as many of these vaccines, doses out to people without putting the priority on the two- vaccine doses first.\par \par \uc2 I mean, could you explain that?\par \par \uc2 FRISTON: Yes, so that was a delicate and possibly brave decision by the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  ,on the basis of scientific advice, that the benefits in terms of reducing morbidity and specifically mortality death rates were greater if one invested or spread the first dose over twice the number of people, than focusing the available resources to follow the original protocol of the two dose with a certain set of separation between the two doses.\par \par \uc2 That rested on the assumption that there was going to be at least a 50 percent efficacy, and that efficacy could pertain to providing sterilizing immunity, which means that you can't catch the virus and, therefore, you can't pass it on. Or it could mean an efficacy of a clinical sort, that if you got the vaccine, then you're unlikely to develop a serious systemic illness and die.\par \par \uc2 Happily, it is now almost self-evident that the efficacy of all the vaccines is well above 50 percent and that that move was fully justified.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, Karl Friston, University College London professor, senior scientist behind this fascinating study here.\par \par \uc2 And, again, it could come here. Maybe not.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read up from \uc2 Dr. Marty Makary\uc2 , FOX News contributor, \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2 , bestselling author,\par \par \uc2 Doctor, what do you think? Now, their approach was the one dose, get as many of those first doses out to as many Brits as possible, then worry about the follow-up dose. The strategy appears to have worked.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARTY MAKARY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It worked, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Look, if you have got two life preservers, and there's a lot of people swimming in the ocean, why give both to one person? We know the first dose is 92 percent efficacious at four weeks. That's in "The New England Journal of Medicine." Initially, it was one report. It was dismissed. It's been validated in many countries, in many studies.\par \par \uc2 And the 2,000 people in the Moderna trial that never got their second dose, they were followed, and from Israel. So the data are now clear. We have got to swallow our pride and say we need to move to this strategy in area where -- in places where we're supply-constrained and we're seeing outbreaks, like with young people in Michigan, in the New York tristate area, and in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 Right now, they're resulting in a more persistent infection epidemic in the United States.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, the way the press has generally covered it in Britain, Doctor, is that there was some sort of, I don't know, magic or attachment to Monday, or that a percentage would be reached, that the scientists there just super dismiss, that they're essentially there now.\par \par \uc2 How far are we from being essentially there?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: Well, thanks for mentioning that, because I think it's important for people to understand that herd immunity is not binary. It's not all or nothing. It's not like we cross a finish line and then, all of a sudden, it's a different world.\par \par \uc2 Herd immunity is gradual. It's exponentially increasing slowing of the spread. And we're seeing that in some parts of the country now in the U.S.. And it's something that we're slowly going to be able to track and be more precise about.\par \par \uc2 Early on, Neil, the models were all over the place. They were wrong by 15- fold. Now you can actually be really accurate, because you're adding 1 percent of the population getting vaccinated immunity almost every day in the U.K. In the U.S., it's about every 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 And so it allows you to click calculate a very linear trajectory to when you will get across the threshold. And in the U.K., they calculated, by next week, on Monday in particular, they will have 74 percent of their population with immunity, both natural and vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we should stress, the good scientist here dismissed the Monday connection. I have no idea. I defer to you, the doctor.\par \par \uc2 But, Doctor, while I have you here last week, I had \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  on, and he took exception to your taking exception to the idea that you have to hit a certain threshold of vaccinations before you get to that point.\par \par \uc2 I'm overly simplifying this. This is exactly what he said on this subject. This is a \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  on this show. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: Let me respond to one thing that that person is saying that he's just completely misunderstanding.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Dr. Makary, right.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Yes, Dr. Makary.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: When I'm talking about -- he says, when I talk about herd immunity, I'm only considering people who are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And that's completely incorrect. When you talk about herd immunity, which is a bit of an elusive concept, because we don't know exactly what that percentage is, it's a combination of the people who've already been infected and very likely are protected, plus the people who get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Those are a group of people, which, together, are individuals which are generally immune or protected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What do you think, Doctor?\par \par \uc2 MAKARY: My question to Dr. Fauci is, how many Americans have natural immunity from prior infection?\par \par \uc2 And, if so, put that into the formula. The studies show it's somewhere between a third to 55 percent of Americans. So it was nice to see that he's acknowledging natural immunity. I hope he changes the description of the formula and the path to normalcy based on that, as we're hearing from the U.K.\par \par \uc2 But I reached out to Dr. Fauci on Easter. I felt bad for being critical. And I wanted to let him know this is just a different medical opinion in the spirit of an open discussion. It's nothing personal. I respect him. And he actually suggested that we're a lot closer on this than others -- than some may think or that some of the newspapers have pitted us out to fight about.\par \par \uc2 I think it's good that he's talking about natural immunity from prior infection now, because, in the past, he clearly did not. I know he told you that he has. But look at the quotes. We have to vaccinate 70 to 85 percent in order to reach herd immunity. He told NPR that, The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , CNBC. He's been saying it repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm glad he's now changing his tune.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And he was complimentary of you, I should stress, as well, as you were with him, two classy guys.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you very much, \uc2 Dr. Marty Makary\uc2 , on that.\par \par \uc2 Again, the attention that's getting noted here, whether it's happening this week or next Monday, the notion that herd immunity, something you hear so much about, maybe has come to Britain and could come here. It's all in how the progress goes, not only with vaccinations, but the body's own inherent ability to fight things off on its own, regardless of those vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 So, we are on top of that.\par \par \uc2 We're also on top of what was behind Major League Baseball's decision to boycott Atlanta and move Atlanta out of the consideration for the All-Star Game. What if I told you it wasn't all of Major League Baseball; it was just one guy?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we're told that Major League Baseball made its decision essentially collectively to boycott Georgia because of the new voting law there, and, in so doing, the Atlanta All-Star Game scheduled for July.\par \par \uc2 It's not quite that black and white.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino has been digging into this and discovered some actually fascinating behind-the-scenes developments. He joins us right now -- Charlie.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: It is. It is fascinating story, with the commissioner, Bob Manfred, Neil, on the hot seat.\par \par \uc2 Let's put it all into context. Everybody does business in Atlanta. The MLB is doing a major piece of business in Atlanta, scheduled for July, where they were going to have the All-Star Game. The All-Star Game was going to be a celebration not just of fans returning to the stadium, but of the passing of the great African-American ballplayer, probably the greatest ball player to ever live, Hank Aaron, who died this year.\par \par \uc2 Amid all that, you have this controversy over the -- over the Georgia voting law, which has taken immense political proportions, where you have people like President Biden calling it a new Jim Crow. You have \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , the gubernatorial candidate, also a voting rights activist herself, credited with turning the Peach State blue in the last election, coming out against this law.\par \par \uc2 LeBron James, the basketball star, also a voting rights activist, against the law. Put all that together, and the MLB commissioner, Manfred, feared that the these activists would turn the All-Star Game into some sort of a circus and political debate over this law, take it away -- take it away from what Major League Baseball is, a game, a pastime, a relief from all the pressures of day-to-day life.\par \par \uc2 Here's what we know. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and LeBron James -- \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  herself and reps for LeBron James voting rights group did meet with senior MLB officials to advocate that the league denounce the new voting laws, and that the commissioner believed the least political solution, as this -- as this pressure was mounting, would be to just get out of town, say something marginally positive -- or negative about the law itself, because Major League Baseball believed that this -- the days leading up to the All-Star Game and the game itself would have been a huge political debate, not just about the Georgia voting law, but even H.R.1, that new legislation passed by the House that's designed to nationalize elections.\par \par \uc2 I should point out that James did not return a call for -- a representative of James did not return a call for comment. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 ' P.R. person has pushed back pretty hard on this. She's saying -- he is saying, listen, we didn't want them to move out of Atlanta. We wanted them to support our efforts to overturn the Georgia voting law.\par \par \uc2 Here's a statement from her spokesman: "In a single one-on-one conversation with an MLB senior adviser, she urged the league to keep the All-Star Game in Georgia and speak out against the law when they do."\par \par \uc2 I will tell you that people close that Manfred said that that pressure, with other pressure to make this game so political, is what's behind him moving out. He believed the least political decision was to get out of town and to try to focus back on the game.\par \par \uc2 He made a few missteps, if you talk to Major League officials, along the way. He could have left town without endorsing \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 ' efforts to overturn the Georgia voting law. I mean, this is -- this is baseball getting involved in one of the most thorny political issues we have seen in a long time that's going on in Georgia with this voting law, which has taken national -- has national implications with -- given it's coming on the heels of the passage of H.R.1 in the House.\par \par \uc2 And baseball's right in the middle of it. But if you listen to people that are close to Bob Manfred, he had the best -- he just wanted to depoliticize the situation, even if he may have increased the politicization, and maybe led to a boycott by his fans.\par \par \uc2 I hear the MLB is getting bombarded with fan complaints about this whole issue -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Charlie Gasparino, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, you have heard evidence right now that things are worsening at the border. I want you to meet the sheriff who has the photos to prove it, here and only here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Think they're easing up at the border? These images show right now migrants are doubling down and finding creative ways to get across.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff who witnessed all of this and wants to share all of this -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the highest-ranking administration officials to date visiting the border right now, the homeland security secretary, Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 We have got Alex Hogan there in Hidalgo, Texas, on what he discovered -- Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2  sat down with Mayorkas to talk about the pandemic and also address the challenges here at the border. They say that it was a positive discussion. They called it, more than anything, a listening session.\par \par \uc2 Activists thanked the congresswoman for organizing the event, so that they could share their experiences of what they're seeing here every single day. More than 20,000 children are currently in U.S. facilities.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR\uc2  (D-TX): But this is an unsustainable situation. There's no doubt about it. It's unsustainable for the migrants. It's unsustainable for the agency. But we can do this so much better.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's going to take an entire re-envisioning of how we address vulnerable souls who arrive at our front door.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: New numbers show that border agents stopped about 19,000 kids in March alone. That is the largest tally ever. They also stopped 53,000 family units, the most since June of 2019.\par \par \uc2 Altogether, CBP stopped 172,000 people, confirming their projections about that from last week. Meanwhile, Texas Governor Greg Abbott is calling for the closure of a federally funded migrant facility in San Antonio after reports of neglect and sexual abuse.\par \par \uc2 Well, Congresswoman Escobar today said that these claims need to be taken seriously. But she fired back at the governor, saying that he has zero credibility on this issue or any other issue related to protecting human life.\par \par \uc2 So, some very divided opinions that we're seeing about how to handle this border situation and this humanitarian crisis. Meanwhile, one thing that they both did agree on is the need for more resources and support for these children and the agencies caring for them right now -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Alex Hogan.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Alex.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you're talking to Sheriff Mark Lamb of Pinal County, Arizona, the sheriff there, you got to do something, because he deals with it every day, taking the liberty to take some of these images you're seeing right now of creative ways in which migrants try to sneak in here.\par \par \uc2 And the good sheriff was kind enough to say that, hate to break it to you, it's getting worse, they're getting more creative, and there are more of them, period. The sheriff joins me right now.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff, thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 Some of these images -- and we were looking at them here -- do not show a crisis that's easing. That's pretty clear. How would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 MARK LAMB, PINAL COUNTY, ARIZONA, SHERIFF: No, they don't.\par \par \uc2 We're used to it. But, obviously, this has increased 100, 200 percent for us. But this is definitely a crisis. And this is what we're trying to get our politicians in Washington, and, frankly, get America to understand.\par \par \uc2 Those images you're seeing, we're getting people running from us on a daily basis. And then they do what's called a bailout. Well, they run from a vehicle. That particular vehicle you saw pictures of, they had seven people bail out of the cab of that truck. We found eight more undocumented aliens in the back of that truck.\par \par \uc2 And this is happening to us every day. We just finished dealing with one a couple hours ago, where we had you stop sticks. And then they come in. You see they're wearing camouflage clothes. They use things like. This is a carpet shoe.\par \par \uc2 These are folks that are not here to claim asylum. They are dressed in camouflage shoes -- or camouflage clothes, carpet shoes. They are trying to come into this country undetected. We have no idea what kind of criminal history they have, what gangs they represent. Honestly, it's a disaster for us, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And I appreciate you bringing light to it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I appreciate you at least providing the proof. We hear this talk all the time. You decided -- there must have been a point, Sheriff, where you just said, this is ridiculous. I'm actually going to show the world this.\par \par \uc2 And that's why I'm very happy you did, just to bring it to people's attention.\par \par \uc2 Now, the homeland security secretary at the border today. I don't know if you had a chance to talk to anyone in the administration about this. But what would you tell them?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: I would tell them we got to get back to securing the border. That's first and foremost.\par \par \uc2 With all these children coming in -- and you're right. We do want to take care of these children. But we can't keep taking children and women every day. You have to stop the bleeding. You have got to secure the border. They need to go back to the policies that President Trump had implemented, put the pride aside, and get back to those policies, because what they're doing is a disservice to the American people right now.\par \par \uc2 They are putting our communities at risk. We just had to release a letter on behalf of -- one of the sheriffs out of Massachusetts wrote a letter. Over 275 sheriffs signed on to it from 39 different states. That should tell the American people how bad this issue is for us.\par \par \uc2 It is affecting the communities across this country, and we want it to stop. So my message would be, let's secure the border and we will help you fix this problem.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think the numbers -- now, these are people and individuals you caught, Sheriff. But many more even beyond your jurisdiction are getting through. What do you think that percentage is?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: Oh, the percentages, I mean, been talking to some of the agents from Border Patrol. In one weekend, 10,000 people come through. They caught 5,000 of those people, so at least half.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 LAMB: And of those 5,000, 2,500 of them were other -- were not from Mexico. These are staggering numbers.\par \par \uc2 We cannot sustain this in our communities in these states, nor can you sustain them throughout these communities. Now, remember, there's always the element of the human trafficking and drug trafficking that comes with it. We're standing up as sheriffs. I started an organization called Protect America Now. You can check it out at ProtectAmericaNow.com, sheriffs coming together to stand against this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Sheriff, we appreciate just the images and getting to know what you're dealing with every day, because, sometimes, we talk in all vague generalities about what's happening at the border, to see this, and to see that crush of individuals trying to get in here of all types of backgrounds, when our focus is on migrant minors.\par \par \uc2 We're missing the far, far bigger and more worrisome picture.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff Lamb, thank you very much. Be safe. And thanks for all you're doing.\par \par \uc2 All right, we're going to be following that.\par \par \uc2 We're also going to be following the controversy over vaccine passports. Meet the governor who has issued an executive order to say, not in my state -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, there are four states in this country right now that ban these so-called password vaccines, or vaccine passports, if you will, proof that you have been treated and given the vaccination to deal with the virus.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Texas and Florida are chief among them, but you might want to add Idaho to that list.\par \par \uc2 That state's governor, \uc2 Brad Little\uc2 , kind enough to join us right now to explain why he said this isn't going to fly in his state.\par \par \uc2 Governor, why not?\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRAD LITTLE\uc2  (R-ID): Well, we believe in personal freedoms and choice in Idaho. It's just built into our DNA.\par \par \uc2 We believe in patient privacy. We believe in individual freedoms. And we don't think we ought to be creating different classes of citizens. And we want to facilitate the free flow of commerce. So, it just doesn't fit into what we do in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 We think it's the right thing to do. We don't mandate vaccines in Idaho. That's -- everybody's well aware of that. We have got about 30 percent of our population that's eligible that's already had at least one dose. We're getting there. And I, frankly, think that a passport, a mandatory passport, just creates friction and distrust.\par \par \uc2 We want people to choose to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, if businesses in your state or even schools in your state, Governor, still would feel safer getting some sort of proof -- it doesn't have to be a passport, per se -- that people have been vaccinated, how do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 Is this beyond your reach? How would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: Well, we -- Idaho is famous for being the least regulated state in the United States.\par \par \uc2 And we want to -- every day, we work on efforts to maintain that position as the least regulated state. And we're going to continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 But the state of Idaho is not going to be involved in it. I, frankly, doubt that there will be much in the way of mandates. There might be some in the specialized health care area that look at this, but it's not going to be a mandate by the state of Idaho.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood.\par \par \uc2 But there are a lot of businesses that say they would be safer or feel better -- restaurants come to mind, Governor -- knowing that, if someone is coming in, have they been vaccinated, are they OK?\par \par \uc2 You would not weigh in or force that issue with them? They could do what they think is necessary for their business? Or could you update me?\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: Well, we always work with businesses to facilitate commerce in every way, shape we can.\par \par \uc2 We were one of the least restrictive states all during the last year. And we want to continue that. As you well know, if you have been vaccinated, as I have, you get a paper card that isn't the most secure document in the world.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: And I think there's a high probability of fraud. It's happened in other areas.\par \par \uc2 And I just believe that, if people choose to do the right thing and businesses operate that way, it'll be the best for everybody, and we will maintain our position as one of the least regulated states and the states where we lead the nation right now in economic momentum.\par \par \uc2 We want to do all we can to maintain that position.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How are things going on the virus front in your state, Governor?\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: We have actually -- I have got about four counties that have an incident rate of over 25, maybe five this morning.\par \par \uc2 But we got a lot of counties that are down to neither zero incident or single-digit incident. We're getting ahead of it. We're getting a lot of vaccine out. We're doing all we can to promote vaccination, as I have.\par \par \uc2 But we want to allow people to make that choice. I believe it's the best way to get to a position to where we will have everything back to normal. And, in fact, for the most part, in Idaho, every business that wants to be open today can be open.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood.\par \par \uc2 Governor Little, thank you very, very much. Very good seeing you again -- again, in Idaho, joining now three other states that are saying this idea of a vaccine passport not going to fly in their respective states.\par \par \uc2 Others, by the way, I think a half-a-dozen, are considering just that. We will keep on top of that for you.\par \par \uc2 Also keeping on top of what is happening right now with the slow return to work and things as normal, whatever normal is these days, and getting the work force back.\par \par \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , who runs a big investment firm, is saying, if he had his druthers, i.e., a warning to you workers of this brokerage firm, get back to work, all of you -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, by the fall, we're told, next fall, it is very likely that things will be back as close to normal, whatever normal can be, and most will be able to go back to work if they choose to.\par \par \uc2 So many do not choose to, and they like an arrangement where they can work from home at least a couple of days a week. Count J.P. Morgan Chase chief executive \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  not too keen on that idea. He is among a number of the investment honchos who says the better part of valor is to get pretty much anyone and everyone back in their office chairs, because you miss something when a good chunk of them are at home or telecommuting.\par \par \uc2 So, let's get to read on that with Liz Peek, FOX News contributor, Gary Kaltbaum, a contributor as well.\par \par \uc2 Liz, his message echoes what I have heard out of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , to a lesser degree, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , investment houses all, that seem to value in person relationships and in person culture that's unique in that industry, and that can't be substituted the way it's been thus far. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 LIZ PEEK, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think what's interesting is, yes, that in person culture is important, but it's really more important to these banks in terms of training their employees.\par \par \uc2 The big problem last summer, Neil, is that the whole class of analysts that come in every summer had no interaction with senior people at all. They were mostly at home, some living with their parents--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 PEEK: -- some in far-flung places.\par \par \uc2 And there was no opportunity to train them, because, when they're in a meeting, when they're looking over their boss' shoulder as they're working on a model or something, or if they're listening in on a phone call, that's when they learn the business. It's an apprenticeship industry.\par \par \uc2 So the real big problem here, not clients. Interestingly, so far, clients are not that keen to meet with their bankers. They can do that over Zoom. This whole idea of getting on a plane and flying four hours to go for an hour meeting, that's really kind of dead right now.\par \par \uc2 It's really the training that is so important to this industry. And that's why they want people back.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, a lot of people tell me, we're delighted, Neil, if we never see you in person. Virtual is fine with us.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PEEK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that's my bosses.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Gary, I'm wondering about where this goes, because there's certain industries -- and I think Liz is right -- where this lends itself to in person communication, particularly in an industry, such as both of yours, where that person-to-person contact supposedly is valued somewhat.\par \par \uc2 But it depends, obviously, for whom. Where do you think this goes and whether we're going to have -- still, regardless what these guys do, we will still have a hybrid work environment next fall, and maybe indefinitely?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, where it goes, I think it's \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  is going to be 50 percent real soon. \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  is capping at 30 percent. And \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  says nobody basically until November.\par \par \uc2 So, where it goes, I think there's going to be a lot of different decisions made--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: When you're referring to 50 percent, 30 percent, the number of people in physical offices in a city or wherever?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: In physical offices in, correct.\par \par \uc2 And, look, this is about big decision-making based on expenses vs. personal interactions and camaraderie, as well as productivity. And I think each individual business, big or small, it's going to be on a case-by-case basis.\par \par \uc2 My big worry longer-term is that you save a heck of a lot of money with less office space. And if you believe you're able to do the same amount or more business with a certain percentage of people from their homes, you may tend to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there will be a percentage that will never, ever go back. But I think time heals everything. And, as we get farther and farther away from this virus, I suspect the numbers are going to spike big time.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's in all businesses. But I do believe Liz is right as far as business travel. I'm not so sure there's going to be any hour flights anymore. I think the word Zoom and Teams and Skype are going to be big words going forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I'm -- I will still do imaginary shows. I mean, people will think that they're real, but they're just -- they're not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's all fiction.\par \par \uc2 Guys, thank you both very, very much, Liz Peek, Gary Kaltbaum on that.\par \par \uc2 You would want to personally meet them, though, right? Wouldn't you want to meet Liz and Gary? I mean, they're fine brains, so you got -- you want to be exposed to that.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, Republicans who are saying, look, infrastructure is fine, Mr. President, as long as it is just infrastructure, Senator Roger Wicker among them.\par \par \uc2 The reaction he's getting for saying just that, keep it to that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, maybe the first sign things were changing, when we heard that Senator \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2  had ventured the possibility of an alternative to the infrastructure plans the administration is considering with something about a third as expensive, focused only on infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  also looking forward to a path that can go ahead and get Republicans behind fixing our roads and bridges, but focusing only on roads and bridges and things like that.\par \par \uc2 Senator Roger Wicker along with them, the Republican Senate Commerce Committee ranking member, senator from Mississippi, with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 That's been your argument all along. In prior interviews, you have said: I'm all for and open to infrastructure, but it better be just infrastructure, right?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER WICKER (R-MS): Well, we have been -- we have been advocating infrastructure for four years as members of the EPW Committee and the Commerce Committee and the Republican Conference.\par \par \uc2 Senator Gillibrand got a little ridiculed yesterday for tweeting that paid leave is infrastructure, child care is infrastructure. Actually, it's -- it's President Biden that really deserves a good bit of the raspberries on that, because it's -- actually, his bill is $2 trillion, maybe $2.3 trillion, and only about 30 percent of it, by any stretch of the imagination, can be called infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 So, I think -- I heard what \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2  said on one of the Sunday shows. And I like it very much.\par \par \uc2 We have a problem with -- with the president when we ask him to compromise. On the rescue bill, he offered $1.9 trillion. We offered $600 billion. And then he said it was up to us to make yet another counteroffer. That's usually not the way compromise works.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, where is this going, Senator?\par \par \uc2 Because I know other concerns you had was, leaving aside the price tag, the taxes that go along with it, now we're hearing, because of concern on the part of Joe Manchin, the Democrat of West Virginia, who seems to be a very influential power broker in all of this, he is not keen on a 28 percent corporate tax rate from the 21 percent we're at now, maybe more like 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 The president quickly adding: I'm open to negotiate this.\par \par \uc2 Where do you think this is going?\par \par \uc2 WICKER: Well, I just hope we can have a real negotiation.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin likes 25 percent corporate rate. I really think the 21 percent is what got us a lot of job creation and a lot of economic growth and GDP growth until the pandemic hit last March.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WICKER: So, boy, I'm not at all keen on moving past 21 percent corporate rate.\par \par \uc2 But we did have some good news from Joe Manchin today. He--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, even under your plan, Senator, even under your plan, to look at something maybe decidedly smaller and focused on infrastructure, whether it's the $600 billion/$700 billion plan that folks and colleagues like Senator Blunt are looking at, how would you pay for that, or would you?\par \par \uc2 I mean, would you expect it to be just the growth presumably spurred on by this spending?\par \par \uc2 WICKER: Generally -- generally, Neil, we have looked for user fees for infrastructure. That's been the way we have paid for this for decades.\par \par \uc2 And states have looked at this. And my state of Mississippi several decades back went to a 5 cent increase in gas tax per gallon. But states all around my state, Tennessee, Arkansas, Mississippi, they have come up with a -- with a combination of this, and a lot of this is three P, public-private partnerships.\par \par \uc2 We can -- we can devise a way to pay for this. And I think a lot of it needs to be the people that actually use the roads, the trucks, and the people who drive the roads. I think it -- I think the public--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what about a mileage tax, then, Senator?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WICKER: -- like the idea of--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What about a mileage tax, when a lot of people have these electric vehicles, right, and a gas tax would be pointless for them.\par \par \uc2 WICKER: That -- yes, we would have to -- we would have to allow for that. We would have to--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The transportation secretary kicked around the idea of a mileage tax. Are you open to that?\par \par \uc2 WICKER: I'm open to talking about a tax that users pay for.\par \par \uc2 And, actually, I tell you what. Our trucking friends are open to this, the people that make a living up and down the highways. They're used to that too.\par \par \uc2 Two good things, Neil, if I can say this, about what Senator Manchin did today. He came out emphatically against abolishing the filibuster. I think that's very helpful, and it will help move negotiations along, because it says that the Democrats are not going to be able to do something with a 51- 50 vote.\par \par \uc2 And, also, he said something I think most senators agree with. Reconciliation doesn't need to become the norm in legislation.\par \par \uc2 On this bill, this infrastructure bill, senators, Republicans and Democrats, can get together and work out the details of about a $500 billion to $650 billion infrastructure plan. We can do it because we have done it down through the decades.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, leaving infrastructure aside right now and the economy, the way it's percolating right now, the argument we hear out of the White House is that, if things are booming, why are Republicans against a modest -- they call it a modest increase in taxes on corporations and individuals who could easily afford it, keep the boom times going?\par \par \uc2 People like \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  are open to paying higher taxes if it will get the infrastructure ball rolling. What do you say on that?\par \par \uc2 WICKER: I say, the lower taxes on job creators, including corporate small businesses and the larger companies that create jobs in the United States of America, that lower tax rate is what has led to a booming economy, and it's what led to great economic growth before the pandemic hit last March.\par \par \uc2 And so my argument would be keep the tax rate that has caused more -- more people to invest in jobs, rather than having to send more money to Uncle Sam.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Looking at the environment right now on the virus, we're told we're very close to herd immunity, Senator. And I don't know how things are specifically going in Mississippi, but are you optimistic that the worst is behind us?\par \par \uc2 WICKER: Infection rates are way down in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 And we heard on the news today that, actually, contrary to what the mainstream media expected, in those states that have opened up, like Texas and Mississippi and Arkansas, rates are way down. For some reason, in the more restrictive states, like New York and Michigan, rates are up.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the idea is stay the course, get the vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 WICKER: But -- but, at the same time, we can balance a healthy economy with being careful.\par \par \uc2 And it's time to get this economy open again.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 WICKER: And I think that's what Governor Reeves and Governor Abbott in Texas are all about. I think they're being vindicated by the statistics.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All righty.\par \par \uc2 Senator Wicker, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210409eh4800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Rep. Tim Ryan\uc2  (D-OH)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,630 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN\uc2  (D-OH): Our friends on the other side running around with their hair on fire.\par \par \uc2 Heaven forbid we pass something that`s going to help the damn workers in the United States of America! Heaven forbid we tilt the balance that has been going in the wrong direction for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 We talk about pensions, you complain. We talk about the minimum wage increase, you complain. We talk about giving them the right to organize, you complain.\par \par \uc2 But if we were passing a tax cut here, you would be all getting in line to vote yes for it.\par \par \uc2 Now, stop talking about Dr. Seuss and start working with us on behalf of the American workers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, well, maybe, as evidence of just the divisiveness on Capitol Hill, not a single Republican voting for that $1.9 trillion stimulus, only one Democrat today, in that 220-to-211 vote.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , the fellow you just saw there, from Ohio with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it`s not a shock that this went down the way it did, right? But, I mean, it`s not as if Republicans weren`t trying to get some of their views in on the things that didn`t have to do with COVID. Half in this package has nothing to do with COVID, and certainly nothing to do right now.\par \par \uc2 So, why were you portraying them as just only interested in tax cuts, and not in scaling down and focusing the size of this package?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, Neil, I think they`re completely out of touch with where the American people are.\par \par \uc2 Well over 70 percent of the American people support this package. And when you look at even the internals on Republican voters...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they didn`t know what was in it. They didn`t know what was in it, right?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, they know...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They didn`t know the half wasn`t even happening this year. They didn`t know about the features that had nothing to do with COVID, right?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Neil, they know they need help. They know they need help and that this bill is intended to help them and will help them. That`s why they`re supportive of it. They`re not worried about the details of it.\par \par \uc2 They know they need help. And if you`re unemployed, you need the $1,400 check. You need help.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But they`re paying for those details, Congressman. They`re paying for those details.\par \par \uc2 Now, some are getting it. And you`re quite right, that a lot of these benefits and stimulus for some of the people affected, an extension of jobless benefits, very real, very palpable, very needed.\par \par \uc2 But I think what the Republicans were saying, and I think that others were advocating, is this focused enough, or did we actually waste a lot of money here or put it back, in the hopes of trying to sell it under the COVID umbrella, when, in fact, a lot of that money is delayed and a lot of that money isn`t now, and a lot of that money doesn`t address COVID at all?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: It addresses the economic problems that have stemmed from COVID, the economic collapse.\par \par \uc2 And, look, the Republicans can wrap themselves up in a million different ways and get wrapped around the axle about this. The bottom line is, the American people support this. Vast majority of Republicans support this. And they haven`t even seen the benefits of it yet. Wait until their unemployment gets extended, and they can continue to safely and calmly pay their rent and their utilities and not destroy their credit.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, we haven`t even seen the benefits of a trillion dollars of unspent money from prior stimulus, right?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Hold on.\par \par \uc2 Neil, you can go on and on all you want. I`m a congressman in Northeast Ohio, that has been devastated by this. And I cannot have enough staff in my office to handle the -- facilitating unemployment claims, seeing if people can get their $1,400 checks.\par \par \uc2 Some people haven`t even gotten their past checks. People are struggling, and they want help. And they want a federal government that acts on their behalf, and not a federal government that you guys want to wrap up and shrink and put in a bathtub and drown it.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is, the American people need a federal government that steps up to bat...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don`t know what you mean by you guys. I`m not -- I`m not carrying anyone`s laundry on this, Congressman. I`m just trying to ask you a couple of simple questions...\par \par \uc2 RYAN: And I`m answering them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... on hearkening back to the days that you challenged \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  to be House leader. And back in those days, you were pretty moderate when it came to liberals going too far, spending too much, not really doing the math on this stuff.\par \par \uc2 You just seem to have changed an awful lot from those days.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Yes, well, I tell you this, Neil.\par \par \uc2 When times change, when you have a pandemic and an economic collapse, you probably shouldn`t keep the same mind-set that you had when things were going a little bit better.\par \par \uc2 But, look, here`s the bottom line. The middle class has been destroyed in this country over the last 30 or 40 years, OK? If the minimum wage was tagged for inflation starting in 1968, it would be at $22 an hour.\par \par \uc2 And you wonder why everybody in this country, vast majority of people are so pissed off. It`s because they can`t survive economically. And now here we are, in the middle of a pandemic, in the middle of an economic collapse, and you have a political party that couldn`t -- not one of them could cast a vote for a rescue package for the American people who are going to lose their unemployment?\par \par \uc2 And they -- Neil...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think we`re in the middle of an economic collapse right now, Congressman? Do you think we`re in a collapse right now, right now?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Neil -- Neil, they want to be the party -- they want to be the party of workers? I mean, are you kidding me? Like, that is insane.\par \par \uc2 And these people are going to get thrown out of their homes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, do you think we`re in a collapse right now? Do you think we are -- we are so badly off right now that this is still an economic collapse? Do you -- do you really see it that way?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: No question, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s a -- it`s a recession when your neighbor loses their job, and it`s a depression when you lose yours, the old \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  adage. The reality is there are millions of people, 10 million-plus, who still can`t find a job.\par \par \uc2 There are waitresses and waiters who are working the same amount of time they worked pre-pandemic, and they`re making half the wage. What do you call that? That is a problem.\par \par \uc2 And the Republicans are positioning themselves on the other side of helping these working-class people, who haven`t done anything wrong. And we`re trying to support them, so that they can pay their rent, pay their mortgage, pay -- make their car payments, not absolutely destroy their credit, which will take a working-class family 10 years to climb out of.\par \par \uc2 And we want them positioned well when the pandemic recedes and they get the vaccine. That`s what the Biden administration is doing. That`s what this package does.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: And it helps...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... in the process.\par \par \uc2 So, let me ask -- let me ask you, then, Congressman. Your name has come up -- understood, sir.\par \par \uc2 Your name has come up as a possible candidate to run for the seat that Rob Portman will be leaving. Are you interested in that? Are you interested in running for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Yes, I am. I am interested in it. We`re looking very closely.\par \par \uc2 I think we need strong leadership on behalf of these middle-class people. Clearly, there are a lot of people in this country who don`t think there`s a problem, don`t think they struggle, don`t think their needs are real.\par \par \uc2 And someone who grew up in Niles, Ohio, just outside of Youngstown, I understand that. And we need a strong voice in the Senate that can make these kind of decisive decisions, that can stick up for people like that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: In the COVID bill, Neil, we had a pension reform in there that saved millions of people their pension, \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , who worked hard, played by the rules, drove a truck.\par \par \uc2 It took us years to get that, the Butch Lewis Act, into some legislative vehicle that could pass. And now we rescued millions of families.\par \par \uc2 That`s what you need in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . And that`s why I`m interested and looking closely at it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: You need that kind of strong leadership on behalf of the forgotten people and the forgotten communities.\par \par \uc2 And I have done that my whole career. And we`re very interested in potentially doing that in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 All right, keep us posted, Congressman. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: You got it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , the Ohio congressman.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Maybe a future senator. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210310eh3a0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Sends Team To Borders Amid Surge; White House Says No Talk Of Reversing Policy; \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  Aides Reportedly Altered Health Department Report; Homelessness Problem in San Francisco Increases Due to COVID-19; Former President Trump Criticizes Immigration Policy of Biden Administration; Reporting Indicates Data Hidden by Governor`s Office Concerning Nursing Home Deaths in New York Due to COVID-19\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Kristin Fisher, Peter Doocy, Aishah Hasnie, Casey  Stegall, Phil Keating; Lauren Green, Chad Pergram, Jonathan Hunt \par \uc2 7,594 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS HOST: If you have the virus, the machine can detect it from the particles spray coming out of your mouth while you shout sing. Dutch entrepreneurs trying it in airports and offices but still in the A.Z. (PH) stages of testing.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: They should put those down at the border.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening I`m Shannon Bream in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Biden is sending a high-level team to the southern border to get ideas on how to handle the first major crisis of his administration, an unrelenting surge of illegal immigration coming from Mexico. The President is taking criticism for opening the floodgates despite the consequences.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage. White House correspondent Kristin Fisher with the administration under fire and digging in, but we begin with Casey Stegall. He is in Eagle Pass, Texas tonight with a look at the situation on the ground. Good evening, Casey.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The wave of migrants currently flooding America`s southern border runs the gamut from entire families to children all alone. And now, once again, larger packs traveling together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Huge groups are coming in, because they know that they`re going to be let go.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: U.S. border patrol agents say it`s not at all uncommon to encounter clusters of 20, 30, 50. Sometimes even groups of more than a hundred migrants wandering the desert and surrendering as the overall apprehension numbers keep ticking up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re between 700 and thousand daily.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Which translates into a shortage of detention beds and space, even as additional temporary overflow facilities are brought online. The Washington Post reports some facilities will be converted into large rapid style processing centers capable of moving people faster through the system.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The backlog is humongous. So, there`s nowhere to put them.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Meantime, the CEO of Greyhound bus lines asking the federal government for emergency funding to help cover migrant transportation costs. They`re also requesting a "100 percent assurance" from the feds that passengers who test positive for COVID will not be allowed to board their buses.\par \par \uc2 After reports surfaced this week out of Brownsville, Texas, that more than six percent of migrants screened there had the virus, yet we`re still allowed to travel within the U.S.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have the governor sending DPS troopers to help us out. If that`s not a border crisis, I don`t know what is.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: With government resources running low, more private groups and relief agencies have stepped in to help.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We know the borderline best. We know how to provide care. We know how to serve.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Hope Border Institute assists migrants with everything from a hot shower to a change of clothes to immigration lawyer referrals. The deputy director, however, says humanitarian groups can`t shoulder all of the burden.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We just need that additional investment from the federal government to be able to make sure that we do this in the most safe, dignified and humane way possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Officials are now even considering the use of local hotel rooms if and when space runs out at all of the detention centers, something that could happen as soon as the end of this month if the projections hold, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Casey Stegall in Texas. Thank you, Casey.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go now to White House correspondent Kristin Fisher and the administration under fire and digging in. All right, good evening, Kristin. There`s been a lot made about the terminology being used here. What`s the latest from the White House?\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN FISHER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, the White House is continuing to call the situation at the border a challenge, but not a crisis. Whatever you call it, the situation has gotten so bad that President Biden is now instructing that a team of his senior advisors should be sent to the border to brief him on the situation about what`s happening there and see what else could be done. But the fundamental underlying policy that Republicans say is causing this problem at the border is not up for a debate.\par \par \uc2 I asked the White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  today, if based on all of these data points, based on all of the numbers about this surge of unaccompanied minors at the border now reaching potentially record levels. I asked her if the administration was at all considering reversing its policy of potentially allowing all unaccompanied minors that come to the border to enter the United States, and she said definitively no.\par \par \uc2 So, I then asked her, you know, is there a limit? Is there a cap in terms of how many unaccompanied children are allowed into the border? And she didn`t really answer the question. She said, I think this issue requires us taking a step back as human beings and as mothers, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and Kristin, I know that was something that you both connected on. Of course, as mothers, you have great compassion for what is happening there, It is a terrible situation.\par \par \uc2 We actually have some of your exchange with her, so let`s play that and then we`ll get a little bit more explanation from you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Don`t the American people deserve to get the data straight from you and straight from the White House?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Data of projected numbers and internal documents for policy discussions?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Data about how many unaccompanied children and migrants are crossing the border on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It is made public by the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and the officials who oversee the entire process.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Can you confirm how many illegal border crossings there are on average everyday right now?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I would send you to the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and CBP, who, of course, would be overseeing that process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And as part of that conversation today, Kristin, you learned a little bit more about -- and you pressed her on this issue of all these children who are involved too. We`re learning a little bit more about some other plans that they may have -- things they may have to trigger to use to actually house some of those children. What did you learn?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Yes, so the spokesperson for the Pentagon confirmed today that Health and Human Services officials did a site survey yesterday at Fort Lee in Virginia. And so, they are seriously considering converting some military bases into temporary housing for these migrants. And there`s also been a lot of reporting in both the Washington Post and the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  that the Biden administration is now converting immigrant facilities in South Texas along the border there into rapid processing centers.\par \par \uc2 And so, the whole idea is to get them in and out in 72 hours, which is what they are technically required to do.\par \par \uc2 You may remember in the Trump administration, a lot of those children, of course, were held in those facilities for longer periods of times. And so, the administration is going to great lengths to avoid that.\par \par \uc2 But in the process, you have former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  putting out this lengthy statement today, really blasting the Biden administration over completely reversing so many of his immigration policies. And so, that`s what we`re hearing over and over from Republicans, again, is, you know, what did -- what did the Biden administration expect if you roll back all of these stricter immigration policies, especially in the middle of a pandemic when you have all of these coronavirus related spacing restrictions. What did you expect?\par \par \uc2 And we`re hearing a lot from both Texas Republicans and Democrats on the border who are very concerned that this is only the beginning of the problem, and that it`s going to of course get much worse, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. As you know, bipartisan pushback and a number of Democrats increasingly out of Texas raising the alarm, because this is their border, and they`re there and these are their constituents seeing it every day.\par \par \uc2 Kristin Fisher on the White House there for us tonight. Thank you very much, Kristin.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There`s another damaging report tonight in New York Governor Andrew Cuomo`s nursing home scandal. Top aides reportedly altered a state health department report or pressured it to be altered to remove the true number of residents who died from the coronavirus. Correspondent Aishah Hasnie has the latest tonight from New York.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDREW CUOMO\uc2  (D-NY): I have not hidden anything from the people of this state.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Nearly a year ago, New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  promised to lead the state through the pandemic with transparency. Tonight, he faces a shocking claim he did anything but that.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal reports the governor`s top advisors convinced state health officials to alter their report on nursing home deaths to only count deaths that occurred inside the facilities, not patients who died after being taken to the hospital. The governor`s office says it did so because he could not verify those out of facility numbers.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal writes: the changes revealed that the state possessed a fuller accounting of out of facility nursing home deaths as early as the summer of 2020.\par \par \uc2 It wasn`t until January of this year, New York`s Attorney General revealed the state had underreported nursing home deaths by as much as 50 percent. 15,000 New Yorkers died in nursing homes, their families unsurprised by the latest blow.\par \par \uc2 VIVIAN ZAYAS, PATIENT`S DAUGHTER: It`s bittersweet when we get information that we knew all along was -- you know, this is what it was.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The new details are also raising eyebrows about the timeline of events. The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports just four days after the release of that altered report, Cuomo was publicly commenting about writing his book, preparing to cash in on his pandemic achievements. White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  today push to comment.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We`ve certainly seen those reports. Obviously, they`re troubling and we certainly would support any outside investigation.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Pressure is mounting for Cuomo to step down.\par \par \uc2 RON KIM, NEW YORK STATE ASSEMBLYMAN: \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  doesn`t have the self dignity to resign. He must be impeached.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: As new details emerge over sexual harassment claims, former aide Charlotte Bennett tells \uc2 CBS\uc2 , the governor propositioned her for sex.\par \par \uc2 CHARLOTTE BENNETT, FORMER AIDE OF \uc2 GOV. ANDREW CUOMO\uc2 : Without explicitly saying it, he implied to me that I was old enough for him and he was lonely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And Shannon, tonight, state Democrats are calling for an oversight hearing into that Wall Street Journal report, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Aishah Hasnie in New York. We`ll see you again at 11:00 tonight. Thanks, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up. The Dow surged 572, the S&P 500 gained 73. NASDAQ jumped to 197.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow gained 1-8/10 percentage points, the S&P 500 rose eight tenths, NASDAQ lost two.\par \par \uc2 The administration is touting the February jobs report which came in at just under 380,000 positions filled and with a drop in the unemployment rates. That is giving the GOP though new ammunition in the fight against the president`s stimulus package. Here`s White House correspondent Peter Doocy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Obviously, today is jobs day.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): And the just released February jobs report was strong enough. Republicans are ready to shell that $1.9 trillion stimulus.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): The jobs report just came in 379,000 new jobs. The economy`s just ready to take off.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden doesn`t think so.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our economy still has 9.5 million fewer jobs than it had this time last year.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So, despite pressure to let reopenings recharge the economy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): Clearly, the economy is getting better.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The administration is moving on with the nearly $2 trillion American Rescue Plan. Chief of Staff Ron Klain tweets: If you think today`s jobs report is good enough, then know that at this pace 379,000 jobs a month, it would take until April 2023 to get back to where we were in February 2020.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Some of last month`s job growth is a result of the December relief package. But without a rescue plan, these gains are going to slow.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And the rest of the Biden agenda may bring new burdens to business owners, including regulations to rein in polluters or a $15.00 an hour minimum wage, which didn`t make it into the stimulus.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): This reconciliation bill does not include an increase in the minimum wage to $15.00 an hour. In my view, it should have.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That could come soon. And Bernie Sanders has an ally in the Oval.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We agree with Senator Sanders and the president is going to be standing right alongside him fighting for an increase in the minimum wage to $15.00 an hour.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president says his American Rescue Plan will create millions of jobs. After that, he says his climate change plan will create millions of jobs. But so far, 44 days into his term, he has not signed any major job creating legislation into law, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Peter Doocy at the White House. Thank you, Peter.\par \par \uc2 Up next, it is spring break in Florida. We`re going to show you how the adults are trying to keep students from starting another coronavirus outbreak.\par \par \uc2 First, here`s what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 4 in Dallas as the Public Utility Commission in Texas rejects proposals to lower electricity prices in the wake of the recent power outages due to a severe winter storm. A firm that markets those prices says the electric reliability council made a $16 billion error in billing during the week of that storm.\par \par \uc2 Fox 40 in Sacramento as California lawmakers approve a $6.6 billion plan aimed at pressuring school districts to return students to the classroom. The bill does not order districts to resume in-person instruction or parents to send their kids to the classroom. Instead, the state will offer money to school boards that provide in-person instruction by the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Miami from WSVN. One of the big stories there tonight, a snorkeler finds almost 70 pounds of cocaine floating off the coast of the Florida Keys. Authorities say the bail contain 25 bricks of cocaine. They say the drugs are valued at more than $1.5 million.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Connecticut Governor \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2  says the state will reopen restaurants, places of worship, and other facilities at full capacity, two weeks from today. But he says the mask mandate will remain in effect.\par \par \uc2 The NBA All-Star Game will be played in Atlanta this weekend after being canceled in Indianapolis. Atlanta`s mayor has pleaded with fans to stay home and not come to her city.\par \par \uc2 Florida health officials say COVID precautions worked well during the Super Bowl activities. They report only 57 total new infections from the more than 20,000 fans who attended the game in Tampa.\par \par \uc2 Spring break is underway in Florida, officials there are trying to rein in students to avoid a new coronavirus outbreak. Correspondent Phil Keating shows us how tonight from Fort Lauderdale.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Are you going to do spring break?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes!\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Spring break 2021 in South Florida is on beginning three peak weeks. And Washington University`s Hamza Tahir says young people are letting loose.\par \par \uc2 HAMZA TAHIR, STUDENT, WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY: Here at this spot, especially, like on the streets and stuff, people get wasted, it`s pretty wild.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Last year spring break got effectively killed when the beach is closed to the public. But this year, the sand, surf, and sunshine are wide open; and that`s a big concern for city leaders who`ve suggested wild and crazy kids go to Vegas instead, fearing that a year of pandemic will fuel pent-up demand to party and spread COVID.\par \par \uc2 DAN GELBER, MAYOR OF MIAMI BEACH, FLORIDA: If you`re coming here to go nuts, if you`re coming here to let loose, if you think anything goes is what we want, you`re very mistaken. We have lots of rules.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Florida`s $90 billion a year tourism economy feeds on Florida`s lack of snow and spring break is a major boost to hotels, bars, and restaurants, which all lost a lot of money last year when the crowds disappeared.\par \par \uc2 But some spring breakers may be surprised at tighter rules. In South Beach, there`s a midnight curfew, a mask mandate, social distancing rules, 400 cops patrolling day and night, no coolers allowed, no open containers, and arrests and citations will be made.\par \par \uc2 JAZMIN TARANZO, STUDENT: Yes, I want to go out. I`m dying to go out. But obviously, I know I can`t. So, I`m going to be in the house.\par \par \uc2 EVA PEREZ, STUDENT: We can be responsible for now, and then like in a couple of months we could party it up.\par \par \uc2 TARANZO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: In Fort Lauderdale, it`s more lax, there is no curfew.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Last year, the CDC did some post-spring break COVID analysis, and found there definitely was some spread, which made its way all the way back to the hometowns across the country. Exactly how much? Difficult to pin down. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Phil Keating on the beach. Thank you, Phil.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s "FOCUS ON FAITH", \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is honoring Christian martyrs during the first-ever papal visit to Iraq. Today, he visited the site of a brutal massacre 11 years ago. Religion correspondent Lauren Green has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GREEN, FOX EWS CHIEF RELIGION CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  arrived in Baghdad this morning, defying COVID and escalating violence in the region, becoming the first bishop of Rome to visit Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Thousands waved flags along the route, greeting the pontiff`s high-security motorcade. Francis met with Iraqi Prime Minister Mustafa Al-Kadhimi and also the Iraqi President \uc2 Barham Salih\uc2 . It is the pope`s most perilous trip yet as the COVID pandemic continues to be an increasing threat. The pope addressing the crisis in his first speech.\par \par \uc2 POPE FRANCIS, SOVEREIGN OF THE VATICAN CITY STATE (through translator): This crisis is above all a summons to rethink our styles of life and the meaning of our existence. It has to do with coming out of this time of trial better than we were before, and with shaping a future based more on what unites us than what divides us.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Francis making the trip despite rocket attacks earlier this week by militias aligned with Iran. But Iraq is deploying thousands of additional security personnel and the pontiff is riding in a bulletproof car.\par \par \uc2 The papal visit is a show of solidarity in the birthplace of the Prophet Abraham, the father of the three monotheistic religions: Judaism, Christianity, and Islam. He`s also bringing hope to the remnant of Christians, now starting to rebuild from years of ISIS attacks that destroyed homes, churches, and whole communities.\par \par \uc2 DAVIDE BERNOCCHI, COUNTRY REPRESENTATIVE, CATHOLIC RELIEF SERVICES: The message of the pope and the visit of the pope is extremely important to let Iraqis feel that they are not left behind, that they are not forgotten.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREEN: In another gesture of brotherhood, tomorrow the pope will meet with the very reclusive Muslim cleric, the Grand Ayatollah Ali al-Sistani. He`ll then conclude his journey on Sunday with an open-air Mass in Erbil. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A momentous journey. All right, Lauren Green, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The remains of a Kansas priest who was awarded the Medal of Honor by then- President \uc2 Obama\uc2  in 2013 have been identified. Reverend Emil Kapaun died in 1951 as a prisoner of war in Korea. He was captured by communist forces while tending to soldiers and continued to minister to the troops until he died of pneumonia.\par \par \uc2 The Defense Department says his remains were identified Tuesday. Kapaun is being considered for sainthood by the Roman Catholic Church.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`ll get a live date -- update on the Senate debate over the massive coronavirus relief package as it continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is still slow going for the nearly $2 trillion coronavirus relief package. Congressional correspondent Chad Pergram has an update live on Capitol Hill. Hello, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Shannon. Well, the Senate`s vote-a-rama has become a vote-a-drama. The reason, senators working behind the scenes to decrease additional unemployment benefits from $400 to $300 each week.\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrats are trying to get Joe Manchin of West Virginia on board to extend unemployment aid through September instead of August. Now, Democrats are wary of changing the bill because that could impact the House next week. Democrats can only pass the bill if they stick together and Republicans know it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): There is some bipartisanship we believe to change the bill. But apparently, that`s an unpardonable sin on the other side. And the result is we`ve done nothing for four hours and 20 minutes to break somebody`s political arm.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): The Senate began the first vote in the voter-a-rama at 11:03 this morning. That vote is still open. The Senate has accomplished nothing all day, the Senate could face more than 40 votes tonight.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (D-VT): Senator Graham said it`s going to be a long night, bring it on. We`re ready. We`re going to pass the American rescue plan.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: There is focus on GOP Alaska Senator Lisa Murkowski to see if she would vote yes. Now, GOP Alaska Senator Dan Sullivan had to leave because of a family issue. So, Democrats may not need Vice President Harris to break a tie.\par \par \uc2 The Senate basically lost a day trying to finish the bill, Wisconsin Senator Ron Johnson required Senate clerks to read the new version of the bill out loud until 2:00 this morning. Johnson said he wanted senators to know what was in the bill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): I sat on the floor of the Senate. I listened to the entire 11 hours. What we would have been doing is we would have gone to vote-a-rama last night, no time to read the bill, no time to really prepare --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now, although the vote is technically still open, the Senate has rejected an effort by Bernie Sanders to override the parliamentarian and impose a new national wage standard. He need 60 votes to do so, but Sanders only garnered 42 votes. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Chad, we will follow along with you. Again, that tonight at 11:00 to see where we are then.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: See you then.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you so much. See you, then.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will seek a new agreement with Congress concerning the commander-in-chief`s authority to make war. The White House press secretary says the president will work with lawmakers to produce what she calls a narrow and specific framework that will ensure we can protect America from terrorist threats while ending the forever wars.\par \par \uc2 A bipartisan group of senators is currently pushing for an end to the use of force authorizations that govern the two Persian Gulf Wars.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco spending huge money to shelter homeless people while people with homes are scrambling to leave the city. Chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt takes a look tonight. Hello, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Homelessness in San Francisco was a problem pre-COVID, and it certainly has worsened in the last 12 months. And one way the city is trying to tackle it is with what officials call safe sleeping villages. But those villages are costly, $16.1 million total for 262 tents at a rate of $61,000 per tent per year. That`s more than $5,000 per tent per month, around twice the cost of the median rent for a one-bedroom apartment in San Francisco. And that`s in a city that`s projecting a $650 million budget deficit over the next two years.\par \par \uc2 And because the encampments are considered group housing, they are not eligible for federal reimbursement so no one seems happy about it. City Supervisor Ahsha Safai says, for instance, quote, "I understand the motivation to create sleeping space during this COVID 19 crisis, but we really need to dive deep to see if this is a sustainable model." And the executive director of San Francisco`s Coalition on Homelessness says, quote, "We do not think that a tent is a permanent solution to homelessness."\par \par \uc2 Meantime, tens of thousands of people are packing up and leaving San Francisco. A new study by the nonpartisan California Policy Lab says while the numbers of people leaving the state of California are largely unchanged, there is a genuine exodus from San Francisco. The net number of people leaving the city in the last nine months of 2020 was almost 39,000. That is up a staggering 649 percent on the same period in 2019. And with so many San Francisco based tech companies sticking with remote working for the foreseeable future, the study says the trend of city residents heading for the exits is likely to continue. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Jonathan Hunt out west for us tonight. Thank you, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the panel on President Biden`s moves to address the massive surge of illegal immigration at our southern border.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The former president just released a statement saying that the Biden administration must act immediately to end the border nightmare that they have unleashed on --\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: We don`t take our advice or counsel from Former President Trump on immigration policy, which was not only inhumane, but ineffective over the last four years. We`re going to chart our own path forward.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CORNYN\uc2 , (R-TX): I don`t know whether it`s the politics or just the incompetence of this administration. It seems to be the rule of thumb that whatever Trump`s policies were, you do the opposite, whether it makes sense or not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let`s bring in our panel, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman and CEO of Empowerment and Inclusion Capital, Mollie Hemingway, senior editor at "The Federalist," and Byron York, chief political correspondent for the "Washington Examiner." Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 Mollie, I`ll start with you. Is there something to that, that there could be some policies in managing the border from previous administrations that might be helpful now? This is what now president, then candidate Biden said about what he would do in 2019.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I would in fact make sure that there is, we immediate surge the border. All those people are seeking asylum. They deserve to be heard. That`s who we are. We`re a nation that says if you want to flee and you`re fleeing oppression, you should come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mollie, he told us what he was going to do.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, SENIOR EDITOR, "THE FEDERALIST": Exactly. President Biden said in the months leading up to his inauguration that he was going to rescind everything that the Trump administration had done to secure the border. He set about doing it with his executive orders as soon as he took office. He`s introduced an amnesty bill.\par \par \uc2 The message is loud and clear. You are encouraged to come to the country illegally, and if you make it over the border, you will be completely allowed to stay. This is a big difference from the Trump administration policy. It took a lot of work to secure the border, to protect these border crossings for humanitarian crises. They involve smuggling operations, cartels. They are inhumane, actually, contrary to what \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said about why you need to control the border. But this is a willful deterioration of everything that was accomplished in recent years when there were previous crises.\par \par \uc2 And it`s one thing to not prioritize border security, or to say you have these other things that you care about more. That`s not what the Biden administration is doing. They`re actually going out of their way to tear down any and all border security. They`re being fairly open about it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They are saying, though, now, that the messaging they want to get across to people trying to come to the U.S. is don`t come now, or it is not the open border total freedom policy you think it`s going to be when you get here. But in response to that, here is what the Texas senator, Republican \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , a little bit more from him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN CORNYN\uc2 , (R-TX): I think the Biden administration has miscalculated. If they think a radio ad in Guatemala or some rhetoric from the podium in Washington, D.C., is going to keep people from trying to enter the United States, they are just fooling themselves.\par \par \uc2 The human smugglers, sometimes called transnational criminal organizations or cartels, this is a money-making proposition for them. They`re going to move as many people as they can, exploiting the gaps in U.S. law, the gaps we need to fill and fix.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Harold, it`s not just Republicans who are speaking out on this. We`re hearing from a number of Democrats down in Texas, Congressman Cuellar comes to mind, saying this is a real problem and that pronouncements from the White House or from the president have real-world impact that he is saying communities there are not equipped to handle.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR. (D) FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: So I would say the frustration in this matter is bipartisan. I would agree largely with some of the things that Mollie, expressed, but I would add to it. This is not a humanitarian crisis for us. It`s a national security challenge on the border.\par \par \uc2 The southern border has to be protected. It has to be guarded. We have to ensure that we don`t just let people in. I would argue that a wall alone is not going to solve this problem. Fundamentally, there are two other issues here. First is, and I`ve tried to say it before, is an economic instability issue in parts of Latin America and Mexico that have to be addressed, which has begat a worse drug problem, a worse drug cartel program, and a worse violence program.\par \par \uc2 If people are willing to send their children to walk tens if not hundreds of miles to a border with the hope, the possibility that the dream of getting in and letting your children alone do this, it says a lot about the threats you feel. I`m not saying that we should let people in, but we have to think about this more deeply and more seriously than I think the Trump administration did. And honestly, I believe the Biden administration is coming to understand that this is not just an issue there. This is not just a problem with cages. This is a more serious problem than I think they anticipated.\par \par \uc2 So all of us have to take a step back and ask the question, how would we address a national security issue? That`s how we should focus and address this issue.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Byron, there are Republican lawmakers asking the White House to declare this is a national emergency, but this is what we see every four or eight years. It can be that the White House and both houses of Congress are controlled by the same party, and we can hear conversations about getting immigration done, and we`ve talked about this before, yet no one seems to be able to get this done. What has happened so far is through executive orders, that was much of what was done in the Trump administration as well. But why can`t Congress get its act together on this?\par \par \uc2 BRYON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Harold said that this is perhaps turning out to be more serious than anticipated. It was very much anticipated. It was very predictable. The White House is in a mess over this. Publicly they are in a state of denial. They denied that there is a crisis on the border. They say instead that it is a challenge, but clearly behind the scenes, they`re very concerned about it or they wouldn`t be sending this fact-finding mission down to the border.\par \par \uc2 But the thing that they can`t admit is that the magnet that is drawing more people here, which is the message that if you come here, you can stay, is what`s causing the problem, and they will not change that. They are absolutely dead set against changing that.\par \par \uc2 So as far as any prospects for any kind of comprehensive immigration deal on Capitol Hill are concerned, it is now 