ter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS HOST: Carley, thank you so much. And thank you all for watching "FOX NEWS PRIMETIME." We'll do it again tomorrow. I'm Lawrence Jones.\par \par \uc2 Tucker Carlson is up next.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening, and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT, just 36 days into a new administration, and already, Joe Biden has fulfilled a major campaign promise.\par \par \uc2 If you didn't vote for him, it might be hard to admit this, but don't worry, this is nothing that's going to improve your life or the life of the United States. It's nothing that's going to make you freer or happier, help you drop 20 pounds or bring you closer to your family.\par \par \uc2 It's nothing you can use to buy dinner. You're not getting a stimulus check this week.\par \par \uc2 Your kids are not going back to school. You're not getting a raise. You thought you might be, back on the campaign trail, Joe Biden promised low wage workers will bump to 15 bucks an hour. Remember that? Sorry, not happening, not because hiking the minimum wage might eliminate jobs. That is a real argument, but it's not relevant to what just happened.\par \par \uc2 The minimum wage isn't going to 15 bucks because the Senate Parliamentarian wouldn't allow it. Raising the minimum wage to that level would violate established procedure, the Parliamentarian said, and of course the White House wanted no point, no part of violating procedure.\par \par \uc2 As Biden's Chief of Staff Ron Klain put it, quote: "We're going to honor the rules of the Senate and work within the system."\par \par \uc2 Okay. Keep that in mind when they try to eliminate the filibuster or pack the Supreme Court or making D.C. a state.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, though, no living wage for you. That's \uc2 one campaign\uc2  promise that Joe Biden appears to have forgotten.\par \par \uc2 But the promise he remembered wasn't made to you in the first place or to the country. It was made to defense contractors and neocon think tanks in Washington. Joe Biden promised them that once he took power, the United States would immediately bring war back to the Middle East, after a four year respite. That really agitated people in D.C.\par \par \uc2 And when he did that, Biden said, he wouldn't consult Congress or even the Senate Parliamentarian before he did it. He wouldn't even pretend to quote, "work within the system," he'd just do it, and he wasn't kidding. On Thursday, he did it.\par \par \uc2 Biden sent F-15s to kill people in Syria. Now, no one in this country benefited from that, not even in the smallest, most theoretical way, and that in itself was deeply reassuring to the political class in Washington. They consider any attempt to improve this country a terrifying form of nationalism. Ooh, it scares them.\par \par \uc2 But the idea of killing people in a faraway place -- thrilling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): I do think it was important for the Biden administration to send a message, a very clear message early on that these attacks on our forces will not be tolerated.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISSA SLOTKIN (D-MI): And we're going to take a proportionate response that we're going to respond, and we hit back at them. And I think it's just sort of resetting expectations that if you hit at us, we're going to punch back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No one likes to talk about the fact that we have to protect ourselves, but that's the national security reality and I think the Biden ministration understands it, and they're acting accordingly.\par \par \uc2 MAX BOOT, CNN GLOBAL AFFAIRS ANALYST: I think that President Biden is pursuing a tough, but smart approach.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is it a mistake to target bases in Syria right now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Actually, it was pretty smart. This is counterterrorism. So I