the wake of new accusations by another former employee.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is breaking her silence on the matter. All of this as the investigation into the governor's orders affecting nursing homes in New York continues.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas, leads us off tonight from Brooklyn. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, New York Attorney General Letitia James has officially been given the green light by Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  to launch an independent investigation into sexual harassment allegations made against him, as Cuomo's latest sexual harassment accuser blasts the governor for refusing to "take responsibility" for his predatory behavior.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voice-over): 25-year-old Charlotte Bennett is not accepting Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 's apology. Saying in a statement released on Monday, "It took the governor 24 hours in significant backlash to allow for a truly independent investigation. These are not the actions of someone who simply feels misunderstood; they are the actions of an individual who wields his power to avoid justice.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday night, Cuomo issued a rare and lengthy apology to anyone who may have been offended, including Bennett, who accused the governor of making inappropriate sexual remarks that she said made her feel horribly uncomfortable and scared\par \par \uc2 Cuomo writing in part, "At work sometimes I think I'm being playful and make jokes that I think are funny. I acknowledge some of the things I have said have been misinterpreted as an unwanted flirtation."\par \par \uc2 The apology received bipartisan condemnation, including from Democratic New York City Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO\uc2 , MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY, NEW YORK: That's not an apology. He seemed to be saying, oh, I was just kidding around. You know, sexual harassment is not funny. It's serious, it has to be taken seriously.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Bennett, a sexual assault survivor told the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  over the weekend, Cuomo asked her about her sex life, whether she had ever had sex with older men, and says she understood the governor wanted to sleep with her. Cuomo also said he never inappropriately touched anybody.\par \par \uc2 Last week, another former aide Lindsey Boylan accused him of unwanted kissing and touching. Cuomo initially tried to control who would investigate him. He first recommended a federal judge with ties to his closest advisors.\par \par \uc2 Then, he proposed a joint investigation with the chief judge, who Cuomo, himself appointed. Cuomo relented only after Attorney General Letitia James publicly spoke out. Writing in part, "It is my responsibility to carry out the investigation."\par \par \uc2 In a "FOX NEWS FIRST", \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is publicly commenting about the accusations, writing, "These stories are difficult to read, and the allegations brought forth raise serious questions at the women who have come forward and all New Yorkers deserve answers to."\par \par \uc2 House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  called the allegations serious and credible. New York Senator Kirsten Gillibrand, Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, as well as the White House also support an independent investigation.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We will leave it to the attorney general and others to conclude that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Tonight, the governor has hired a veteran white-collar criminal defense attorney to represent the administration's executive chamber, not over the sexual harassment allegations, but rather over an ongoing \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation into the Cuomo administration's handling of COVID-19 death data in the state's nursing homes. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Important to remember that. Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In just a few hours, the first injections of the \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  single dose-coronavirus vaccine will be going to Americans. Nearly 4 million doses of the formula are being delivered around the country following the approval over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jonathan Serrie has the latest tonight from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  start shipping the first doses of its newly approved COVID vaccine today. Employees at the company's Kentucky distribution center used a sharpie pen to write positive notes on boxes containing the life-saving vials.\par \par \uc2 Although the vaccine is 66 percent effective in preventing mild to moderate cases of COVID-19, many of the clinical trials were conducted in South Africa, where a resistant variant is widespread. Even there, the vaccine was 85 percent effective in preventing severe disease.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , DIRECTOR, NATIONAL INSTITUTE OF ALLERGY AND INFECTIOUS DISEASES: Even though the vaccine itself was not specifically directed against those variants, it did extremely well when it came to preventing severe critical disease. And as we've heard many times now, there were no hospitalizations or deaths in any of those studies.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: J&J plans to distribute 4 million doses to states this week but expects that figure to increase to 20 million by the end of March, and 100 million by June.\par \par \uc2 ALEX GORSKY\uc2 , CHIEF EXECUTIVE OFFICER, JOHNSON AND JOHNSON: I really can't think of another time in our history where we've been able to ramp up with this kind of pace.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: In a 60 minutes interview, President Biden's coronavirus coordinator said the Trump administration dropped the ball on distribution.\par \par \uc2 JEFF ZIENTS, WHITE HOUSE COVID COORDINATOR: Is there really was no plan to ramp up the supply of those vaccines. There wasn't enough vaccine. There were not enough vaccinators, people actually take vaccines and turn it into vaccinations by putting needles into arms.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: President Trump's former assistant health secretary \uc2 Brett Giroir\uc2 , disputed the "60 Minutes" interview in a tweet. Saying, "Fact is, current supply and ramp up are 99 percent due to Trump's Operation Warp Speed, including the pharmacy and health center rollouts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): Although credit and blame fall along partisan lines, both sides have nothing but praise for the researchers or volunteers who continue to bring safe and effective vaccines to fruition, including the one being deliver today. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Serrie in Atlanta. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden met with his Mexican counterpart virtually. A short time ago, immigration which is turning out to be a bigger hassle than the administration seem to predict was at the top of the agenda\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Kristin Fisher has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Kristin.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN FISHER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Evening, Bret. Well, there really wasn't any discussion about the current situation down at the border during the 15 minutes that cameras were allowed into this meeting. Instead, President Biden focused on the relationship between the two countries and said that his administration use Mexico as equal to the United States.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Right now, there are roughly 4,000 illegal border crossings every day, according to a former trump administration official and the numbers are even more concerning when it comes to unaccompanied minors.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 400 were taken into custody on Friday alone and 97 percent of the beds available for them to the office of refugee resettlement are full.\par \par \uc2 But the new secretary of Homeland Security, Alejandro Mayorkas, does not think it constitutes a crisis.\par \par \uc2 FISHER (on camera): Do you believe that right now this is a crisis at the border?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: I think that the answer is no. I think there is a challenge at the border that we are managing and we have our resources dedicated to managing it.\par \par \uc2 FISHER (voice-over): Secretary Mayorkas's predecessor in the Trump administration, \uc2 Chad Wolf\uc2 , strongly disagrees. Citing the new 10 facilities to handle the surge in migrants popping up in Texas.\par \par \uc2 CHAD WOLF\uc2 , FORMER ACTING SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Everyone knows there is a crisis going on and I think that the leader of the department needs to be honest with the American people.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Secretary Mayorkas says his department is working around the clock to rebuild a humane immigration system that he claimed was dismantled by the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: It takes time to build out of the depths of cruelty that the administration before us established. We are not saying don't come. We are saying don't come now.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: But a Democratic Congressman from Texas told Axios, "The bad guys know how to market" that kind of message. Adding, "You just can't say, yes, yes, let everybody in because then we're affected down there at the border."\par \par \uc2 Cooperation on migration top the agenda of President Biden's virtual meeting with the president of Mexico. President Obrador had signaled that he was planning to ask President Biden to consider sharing part of the United States' coronavirus vaccine supply with his country. But the White House press secretary said the answer is no.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The administration's focus is on ensuring that every American is vaccinated. And once we accomplish that objective, we're happy to discuss further steps beyond that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FISHER (on camera): But just moments ago, President Biden was asked if the U. S. would send vaccines to Mexico, and President Biden responded, "We are going to talk about that."\par \par \uc2 So, we'll see but White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , certainly gave a definitive no just a few hours ago. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Kristin Fisher, live on the North Lawn. Kristin, thank you. Stocks surged today. The Dow gained 603. The S&P 500 jumped 91, its biggest day since last April. The NASDAQ was up 396.\par \par \uc2 Any reports of former President Trump's political demise may have been greatly exaggerated, especially, if you saw this weekend. Mr. Trump giving a rousing 90-minute address at a conservative conference yesterday. He is still considered by many a front-runner for the Republican nomination in 2024. Correspondent Mark Meredith reports tonight from Orlando.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Hello, CPAC. Do you miss me yet? Do you miss me?\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Former President Trump jump back into the political spotlight Sunday night, telling conservatives at CPAC, he's far from retired.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: For our movement, for our party, and for our country, our brightest days are just ahead.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Trump dismissed the idea of starting a new party, but insisted Republicans can only win back control of Congress next year if they embrace his America First agenda.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: We will take back the House. We will win the Senate. And then, a Republican president will make a triumphant return to the White House and I wonder who that will be.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: In his speech, Trump repeatedly questioned the legitimacy of the 2020 election, while also slamming Republicans, by name, who voted to impeach him following the Capitol Hill riots.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The Democrats don't have brand standards like \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , little \uc2 Ben Sasse\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But he saved his sharpest criticism for his successor, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has triggered a massive flood of illegal immigration into our country, the likes of which we have never seen before. Millions of American children are having their futures destroyed by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's anti- science school closures.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Well, the CPAC crowd appeared thrilled with this speech.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People love \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  loves America, and he needs to run again.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Other Republicans warned, keeping Trump front and center could doom the party's future.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (R-LA): CPAC is not the entirety of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 If we idolize one person, we will lose. And that's kind of clear from the last election.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Former U. N. Ambassador Nikki Haley, a potential 2024 candidate praise Trump's speech and tweeted, "The liberal media wants the GOP civil war. Not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): CPAC attendees were polled on who they'd like to see as the Republican nominee in 2024. President Trump came in first place in the survey. In second, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who generated a lot of buzz over the weekend as a potential candidate down the road. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith in Orlando. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, candidate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  promised to hold Saudi Arabia's crown prince directly responsible for the death of a journalist. Now, things have changed.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox Richmond as Loudon County, Virginia public schools reportedly dropped their annual Dr. Seuss celebration. The author's birthday has been observed in schools for more than two decades as Read Across America Day. But activists say Seuss's books are covered with what they call orientalism anti-blackness and white supremacy.\par \par \uc2 Fox 4 in Dallas as the state's largest and oldest power cooperative files for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection. Brazos Electric Power Cooperative is citing the recent winter storm that left millions without power. Much of Texas's power grid collapse followed by its water systems.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the state's top utility's director has resigned just hours ago in the fallout from the outages.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Los Angeles from our affiliate Fox 11. One of the big stores there tonight, the man who was shot while walking dogs belonging to entertainer Lady Gaga, says he is feeling the support and love he has received. Ryan Fischer is said to be recovering. The two dogs were returned to Lady Gaga over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden is facing questions tonight about why he's not directly punishing the Saudi Crown Prince, as he said he would, following a report linking the Crown Prince to the death of a U.S. based journalist. White House correspondent Peter Doocy has tonight's update.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has had years to think about how he'd respond to the murder of journalist Jamal Khashoggi, this was in 2019.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Trump has not punished senior Saudi leaders, would you?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But that punishment he promised doesn't include new sanctions for MBS, \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 . \uc2 Obama\uc2  era Spy Chief John Brennan had been hoping to see something for the Crown Prince. Tweeting, no meetings with senior U.S. officials and no visits to the U.S. would be a good start.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the U.S. is placing new visa restrictions on 76 Saudis the State Department won't name. Making a request for the Saudis to disband a rapid intervention force and pledging to review future arms sales.\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESMAN: In terms of our partnership with Saudi Arabia as a recalibration, it's not a rupture.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Now, a United Nations expert is warning, there's a risk in telling the public MBS approved the Khashoggi killing and then doing nothing about it.\par \par \uc2 AGNES CALLAMARD\uc2 , UNITED NATIONS HUMAN RIGHTS EXPERT: It is extremely, in my view, problematic if not dangerous to acknowledge someone's culpability and then to tell that someone: but we won't do anything.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But there's bipartisan agreement, it's tough to untangle ties with a strategic partner in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: You just can't whip it aside and say this is a black and white issue. It is not.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: It sounded like something bigger was coming on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There will be an announcement on Monday as to what we are going to be doing with Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But then, the White House backtracked.\par \par \uc2 KATE BEDINGFIELD\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: This is not a new policy announcement, but it will be an elaboration on some of the steps that the administration took on Friday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): So, the White House made some changes but they are not shaking things up too much with Saudi Arabia. Even though it sounded like that could be coming during the campaign when candidate Biden said he thought there was very little social redeeming value in the present government in Saudi Arabia, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy in the White House Briefing Room. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 China is said to be hacking in to the power grid in India. That's according to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , multiple sources. The story stating last year, Chinese malware may have been responsible for a major power outage in Mumbai that affected 20 million people.\par \par \uc2 It says China is responding to tension with India over conflicting territorial claims in the Himalayas along both countries' borders.\par \par \uc2 Up next, congressional Democrats fighting amongst themselves over the Biden agenda.\par \par \uc2 First, more stories Beyond Our Borders tonight. Saudi Arabia says it intercepted a missile attack over its capital and bomb laden drones targeting a southern province. The government is blaming the assaults on Yemen's rebel Houthis. No casualties or damage initially reported there.\par \par \uc2 Prince Philip is in a London heart hospital tonight. The 99 year old husband of Queen Elizabeth II was transferred there to undergo testing and observation for a pre-existing heart condition as he continues to be treated for an unspecified infection.\par \par \uc2 And former French President \uc2 Nicolas Sarkozy\uc2  is found guilty of corruption and sentenced to three years in prison.\par \par \uc2 A Paris court says Sarkozy tried to bribe a judge after leaving office. He was also convicted of peddling influence in exchange for confidential information about an investigation into his 2007 campaign finances.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond Our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer says his colleagues will begin debating coronavirus relief that bill sometime this week. The House passed its measure early Saturday.\par \par \uc2 There are growing questions tonight however, about what happened to the unspent funds the last time from the earlier coronavirus relief packages.\par \par \uc2 Politico reports, Republicans are insisting Congress should not spend more money when a trillion dollars in aid still has not been spent. The story says most of that money is assigned to programs designed to distribute it over time.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Associated Press reports states are being hammered with a new wave of fraud as they fight scammers who have siphoned billions of dollars from pandemic related unemployment programs.\par \par \uc2 Massive shams springs from prior identity theft from banks, credit rating agencies, health care systems and retailers. Perpetrators buy stolen personal information on the dark web, and then they use it to flood state unemployment systems with bogus claims.\par \par \uc2 The battle over the stimulus is exposing the growing rift between Democratic progressives and more moderates in Congress. Here's Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): After Senate parliamentarian Elizabeth MacDonough ruled $15.00 minimum wage didn't fit Senate rules to be included in the Democrats' COVID relief bill under reconciliation, progressive Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  told reporters she should be fired.\par \par \uc2 "I certainly do think that there should be either an override or replacement." Their Senate whip however, says it's time to move on.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): I think that that is unlikely. And I would recommend that we look for another venue. I support increasing the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: But progressive Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez says not so fast, telling reporters moderate Senators Kyrsten Sinema and Joe Manchin don't run the show.\par \par \uc2 "We can't just roll over and allow, you know, a tiny group, maybe two members of the Senate on the Democratic side, two conservative senators to drop all democratic policy, including Biden's policy."\par \par \uc2 While progressives are making their voices heard, a prominent name is trying to downplay any rift in the party.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I know that everyone likes to talk about the divisions within our party. But what brings us to Washington as Democrats is that we're trying to make this government work for working people.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And while Democrats tussle over the nearly $2 trillion COVID relief bill, Republicans say there isn't much they can do about it.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any hope of making substantive changes to the bill in the Senate, Sir?\par \par \uc2 SEN. STEVE DAINES (R-MT): Unfortunately, with this package, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , Chuck Schumer and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says, it's their way or the highway.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): Without Republicans, that means all Senate Democrats must be on board and that's why Senators Manchin and Sinema are under a great deal of pressure, if they also have significant clout, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Mike, thank you. Let's talk about the Democratic infighting and next steps on Capitol Hill. Senior political analyst Brit Hume is here tonight. Good evening, Brit.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST (on camera): Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, this stimulus bill, there are a lot of questions about it, what's inside it and whether Democrats are all going to come together and pass this and reconciliation in the Senate, 51 votes?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, there are two points about that, Bret. One is that, obviously, there's a good question whether it's needed really, because we now have a vaccine, the cases of coronavirus are down, businesses are reopening. And that of course, I think, you know, the biggest stimulus you could imagine would be get the economy reopen and the schools open, so that people who depend upon the school to look after their children can get back to work. That would be this biggest single benefit to the economy one can imagine.\par \par \uc2 And we also know, Bret, and I know you've reported this a number of times, that the money left over from previous bills of this kind has -- is nowhere near spent. So, there's a lot of money in the pipeline. It would seem prudent to wait for that to flow out there to see what effect it had before they go on.\par \par \uc2 But the Democrats are determined to do this. And, you know, they'll have to hang together in the Senate 100 percent to do it. But it looks like they've got a good shot of pushing it through.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, for all the talk as a candidate, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  saying that he was going to work across party lines. It doesn't seem at least initially, that that's happening a lot.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, that's for sure. I mean, think of those Republicans who went down with an offer in hand as a starting point for negotiations. They went down to the White House to see him and he met with him and was polite to them and that was the absolute end of it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they never got a response from him. They never got a counteroffer. They never got anything. It went absolutely nowhere, which bespeaks the determination of the Democrats to shove this thing through in the very form that they -- that they want with the only exception of course, being that it doesn't look like they're going to be able to include the $15.00 minimum wage in there -- the $15.00 an hour minimum wage in there.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, there's a hazard in this Bret, and you see this are two things at work here. One of them is that when you have a narrow majority, as the Democrats do in the Senate and even in the House now, having lost seats in the House, it's dangerous at times to use your majority, to cobble together a majority and ram something really big and ambitious through because there's a good chance that it won't be that popular.\par \par \uc2 And you may get into trouble the next election and watch it begin to crumble. That's certainly what happened with Obamacare. Look how much of Obamacare has been eaten away over time because Republicans got control of one house or both and cut pieces out of it. And pieces -- so Obamacare is a shadow of its former self. That risk is happening with a lot of what the Democrats are trying to do, not so much with this because they will never be able to grab back that spending.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing, of course, is, Bret, when you have a narrow majority, it just bedevils the president, because the hardcore ideologues in your parties, and this is true of both parties, think you can do whatever you want. And of course, with narrow majorities you cannot. So you have a lot of very unrealistic expectations that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to have to contend with on his left in the House, and perhaps to some extent in the Senate as well, and that's part of the reason why you're seeing the divisions you are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll talk about the divisions in Democrats that gives a lot more power to the Joe Manchins and Kyrsten Sinemas in the Senate. But on the Republican side, after watching CPAC, what's your take about the GOP?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I was struck by a number of things. People have asked me what Trump said, and I said he said everything. He talked forever, and he said about everything you've heard him say including, regrettably, in my view, that rehashed the old business about how he won the election in a landslide and it was stolen from him and all that.\par \par \uc2 But when the straw poll was taken among CPAC participants to see how many thought he ought to be a candidate, now, this is a pretty Trumpy audience, Bret, as I'm sure you know, and only about 55 percent said that they wanted him to run again. I think that's a significant number and bears watching, because it may mean that if he does decide to run again, despite all the excitement, as you can see there on that floor when he suggested he might, he might have trouble. And Republicans might want to look for someone else with his agenda. I think that in fact may be probable.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Brit, as always, thanks.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When we come back, tensions grow in the twin cities ahead of the trial of the police officer accused in the death of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The main focus is prevention - prevention of property damage, arson, and looting.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They are more afraid of the people than they are afraid of police violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thousands of Chicago public school students were back in class today for the first time in almost a year. That's despite the protests of many teachers who are working with in-person and remote students at the same time. Some students will attend classes Mondays and Tuesdays while others go on Thursdays and Fridays. Schools will be closed on Wednesdays for cleaning.\par \par \uc2 California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and state legislative leaders there have reached an agreement aimed at getting most public school children back in the classrooms by the end of March. School districts could receive up to $6.6 billion if they reopen class by March 31st.\par \par \uc2 And the head of the public school teachers union in Berkeley, California, is being called a hypocrite for sending his daughter to an in-person private school while insisting his colleagues not be forced back to work. Matt Meyer tells FOX News video of him and his daughter is an invasion of privacy. He says private schools are a completely different universe than public schools.\par \par \uc2 And the Senate has just confirmed Miguel Cardona as President Biden's education secretary on the education front.\par \par \uc2 We are just a week away from the start of jury selection in the murder trial of a Minneapolis police officer blamed for the death of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 . The twin cities have been on edge since that death, and the tension is rising once again. Senior correspondent Mike Tobin shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: The defense for former Minneapolis police officer Derek Chauvin hinted they will argue that drugs killed \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 . In a brief filed Friday, they cited the autopsy, claiming "Fentanyl was present at 11 nanograms per milliliter, higher than chronic pain patients. If Mr. Floyd had been found dead at home and no other apparent causes, this would be acceptable to call an overdose."\par \par \uc2 The brief was part of an appeal brought by the prosecution asking to reapply a third-degree murder charge ahead of the trial.\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL\uc2 , SPECIAL PROSECUTOR: This is our only chance today to reinstate that third-degree murder charge. We can't fix it later on appeal.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Derek Chauvin is facing second-degree murder and manslaughter charges. The third-degree charge was dropped in October.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, barricades and razor wires surround the Hennepin County courthouse where jury selection for the first trial following \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 's death will start next week. A security plan called operation safety net will involve National Guard and multiple police agencies, plans to protect critical infrastructure and police precincts, and secure fire crews if the city burns again. Council members are critical.\par \par \uc2 LISA BENDER\uc2 , (D) MINNEAPOLIS CITY COUNCIL PRESIDENT: Too many city leaders think the most grown-up response to a problem is always with force and always to begin by sending people with guns.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: One plan was ditched in the face of public blowback and the appearance of propaganda. That was to pay social media influencers to combat misinformation and distribute messages from the city.\par \par \uc2 DAVID RUBEDOR, MINNEAPOLIS ASSISTANT CITY COORDINATOR: This was never about trying to persuade or change public opinion about any particular message.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Police Chief Medaria Arradondo says there is no specific information for violence following the trial. This time around he says the city has had time to prepare. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin in Chicago. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Controlling immigration at the southern border is turning out to be a lot harder than it looked for President Biden. We'll talk about that with the panel when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: We are working around the clock seven days a week to make that time frame as short as possible, but they need to wait. But they need to wait with a particular goal in mind. We are not saying don't come. We are saying don't come now, because we will be able to deliver a safe and orderly process to them as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you believe that right now there's a crisis at the border?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS I think that the answer is no. I think there is a challenge at the border that we are managing, and we have our resources dedicated to managing it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Homeland Security secretary there in the White House briefing room. This as a former Trump administration official talking about border crossings, says "The 21 day average is around 3,600 illegal border crossings per day. However, this past week it has been north of 4,000 and is trending upwards." And whether that's a crisis or not, it's a determination that was asked today and a briefing room.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Ben Domenech, publisher of "The Federalist," Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, CEO of Empowerment and Inclusion Capital, and former White House press secretary, \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ari, let me start with you. You heard the Homeland Security secretary there saying don't come now. Come eventually, but don't come now. And you just wonder whether that message is effectively translated to all of the immigrants were coming across the border.\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It is by the smugglers. The smugglers translate and say come on, give me the money, let's go.\par \par \uc2 There's only one message. The message should be don't come illegally in any way, shape, or form. If you come illegally, we will stop you. You will not enter America. We are a nation of immigrants, and we should have been to come here. It enriches us. We're a better country. But one of the first things you do when you want to become an American is you land legally. You began building your new life legally.\par \par \uc2 So the Biden administration has created this crisis, and it is a crisis. There's a story that just broke before we came on the air in the "Washington Examiner," and it says the border patrol released 350 migrants onto the streets of Yuma over the past two weeks, and that number is expected to grow into the thousands. They are just releasing people into the streets. This is a crisis.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ben, even some Democrats along those border states are raising alarms. Moments ago, in this virtual meeting with the Mexican president, here is what President Biden said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We look at Mexico as an equal, not a somebody who is south of our border. You are equal. And what you do in Mexico and how you succeed impacts dramatically on what the rest of the hemisphere will look like.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There wasn't any public talk about what's happening on the border of there. We assume that that happened behind, when the cameras left. One of the things that worked before is when Mexico did enforce its southern border, preventing some of those caravans from coming through.\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, "THE FEDERALIST": And that enforcement happened under pressure from President Trump and his administration on a lot of different fronts. One of the interesting things about the dynamic in that situation was that the Mexican government felt that the Trump administration would be a fair partner when it came to this because the president was not that interested in mucking about in anything other than security for that border. That was his number one priority.\par \par \uc2 And I think that what you saw over the course of the past couple of months is a changing and that relationship in a number of different ways. Back in December Mexican lawmakers, for instance, reversed something that has been traditionally the legal respect that they have for U.S. officials such as DEA agents, eliminating the diplomatic immunity that they typically enjoyed. We are seeing an increased tension with our relationships with Mexico in a number of different fronts, and things that I think are only going to continue and lead us towards -- if it's not a crisis now on the border, it's going to become an explosive one over the coming weeks and months, not because of something in the abstract but because of the Biden rollback of the various border policies that did work on the President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR. (D) FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: So thanks for having me on. I've listened to this conversation when Ari's boss, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , was president who tried to enact a bigger immigration reform package, to presidents since. We need to stop treating issue at our border as simply an immigration issue. Both sides have scored political points over the years from it. This is a hemispheric challenge and a national security issue at its core. If we don't realize that everything from weather to economic instability, which can be compounded by it, to the violence from the drug cartels and gangs, that's what's causing this crisis.\par \par \uc2 I would disagree, with all due respect, to the secretary of DHS, Department of Homeland Security, he sees a lot more than any of us do, but it is hard to imagine how this is not -- if not a crisis, to Ben's wind, is emerging one. We need to think about this very differently. I know President Biden talked about providing economic support to some of these nations. That may not be a popular thing, but we have to think about how we want our borders to be managed, because it's not clear to me, I'm not convinced that a wall alone will solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 We need a comprehensive reform package -- and Bret, I know you've raised concerns, rightly so, about the use of that word -- combined with how we think about the instability that seems pervasive across Mexico, parts of Mexico, and Latin America. That's the motivator here, that's the source, and that's what we have to address and attack if we are going to deal with this issue in the long term.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The other issue that's coming up again and again and again is getting kids back to school. President Trump brought this up in context with the border situation over the weekend at CPAC.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The Biden administration is actually bragging about the classroom education they are providing to migrant children on the border, while at the same time millions of American children are having their futures destroyed by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's anti-science school closures.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We would like kids to be able is to be in school five days a week.\par \par \uc2 I think we're going to spend more of a time focused on communicating about our agenda for the American people than responding to criticism from the former president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ari, there's all kinds of takes about former President Trump's speech, 90 minutes, and all the things that were in there. But there were some lines in there that really hit at policy issues that are a vulnerability for the Biden administration now, and school openings is one of them.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Absolutely, which was a crucial issue in the suburbs where Republicans really did lose in the presidential race, particularly with college-educated suburban voters. And school and getting your children educated and having parents were able to work and enjoy their lives is a massive issue in the suburbs.\par \par \uc2 But it's so hypocritical, the Biden administration doesn't hesitate to criticize \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You heard it today where they are still going after them for Operation Warp Speed and saying that they were left with nothing on the shelf. They continue to say that. And then \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  today says but we are not going to respond to President Trump's criticisms of us. So they just want to have a one way, not very unifying, but certainly very hypocritical.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A little bit of an audible here, Ben. Just in the past minute or so we confirmed that the administration is going to move forward with sanctions against people tied to the poisoning of Russian activist \uc2 Alexey Navalny\uc2 , and they are going to move forward with sanctions against Russia. That comes in the context, however, of deciding not to sanction the Crown Prince of Saudi Arabia for the killing of Jamal Khashoggi, the journalist. Thoughts about that and the contrast?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Well, I think -- I certainly support sanctions against those who targeted \uc2 Alexey Navalny\uc2 , and did so very blatantly, by the way. In the context of the conversation around the Crown Prince, this is a story that's obviously terrible, but it's also been used by a lot of people to try to drive a wedge between the normalization of relations between the Arab states and Israel, something that we definitely believe is in the interest of the United States. And a reorienting of our foreign policy, I think, to a point where a lot of those issues that are within the Middle East are solved by the nations that are there is something that I think a lot of Americans are in favor of. We look at the strike at Syria, for instance, last week as something that could be viewed as a brushback, but could also be a prelude to potentially getting more involved in the area. I think a lot of Americans want to see that end. It's something obviously candidate Biden called for himself. But I think it still is an open question about the kind of foreign policy that he's going to have and how different perhaps it is from President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, I want to head back to Capitol Hill, and that is this COVID relief bill. This is \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2  about what's inside and what's not.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATE BEDINGFIELD\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE COMMUNICATION DIRECTOR: Do you want to cut the money to get schools reopened? Do you want to cut the money to get vaccines in the arms of Americans around the country? So he believes that this package is tailored to meet the critical needs that we are facing right now in his moment of crisis in this country.\par \par \uc2 SEN. STEVE DAINES, (R-MT): Ninety-five percent of the dollars will be spent in 22 through 28. In other words, not this year, but in the years out to 2028. So it has nothing to do with the COVID relief. It's a liberal wish list is what it is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, when Democrats pushed back, they say the polls are great for this COVID relief bill, but the question is do people really know what's inside it?\par \par \uc2 FORD: They may or may not. I watched last week in one of your great interviews, Bret, with the governor of West Virginia who comments to this question, saying that the country needs this. He ran businesses. Who cares if you overshoot a bit?\par \par \uc2 The asymmetry of the recovery over the last year where the richest and the biggest did far better than they ever could have imagined, and those who are smaller and even smaller didn't, I'm OK with a little overshooting. And if we take care of main street America and provide a renaissance for middle-class families, I'd remind all of us was, there a candidate for president who four years ago argued this was the thing to do for the country. That was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ari, I've 15 seconds here.\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: I really -- I think that's exactly right. I don't know any other way to look at it and I --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: It's a lot of money, it's not needed, it won't be spent, and the Democrats are going to pass it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, when we come back, sneak peek at tomorrow's headlines. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, a look at tomorrow's headlines tonight. Harold, first to you.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Seventy percent of the U.S. supports a $1.9 trillion COVID relief, but McCarthy and McConnell still oppose it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supported it during the campaign for the specials in Atlanta and Georgia. We'll see what Republicans do in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not that bill, but good point. I'd told you he'd point to the polls eventually. Ben, you're up.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Cuomo-sexuals consider conversion therapy. The number of people who fell in love with \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  last year because of his demeanor on COVID issues I think are now questioning their blatant support given all the allegations and the questions about his own policies.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ari?\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Mine's not as sexy as Ben's, but Cuomo resigns. I don't know that that's tomorrow's headline, but it is an inevitable headline. He cannot survive this. Cuomo resigns.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, gentlemen, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "FOX NEWS PRIMETIME," have you seen the show? This week it is hosted by Lawrence Jones. It starts right now. Lawrence?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020210302eh3100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Gov. Mike DeWine\uc2  (R-OH)\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne \par \uc2 1,358 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 March 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The Buckeye State of Ohio bucking the trend and getting most of its school districts back to in person learning today, part of a deal made by Ohio school districts and Governor DeWine allowing teachers to get vaccinated for the reopenings.\par \par \uc2 And we are pleased to have the governor with us.\par \par \uc2 Governor DeWine, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 GOV. MIKE DEWINE\uc2  (R-OH): Hey, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And congratulations.\par \par \uc2 This has been a huge obstacle for many states. Walk us through the process and how we got here.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 Well, January 1, we had half of our kids who were totally remote.\par \par \uc2 And, basically, what I told all the schools -- and we have 600-and-some school districts in the state -- said we`d love to have everybody back. Our goal was to have everybody back March 1. And if a school is willing to do that, then we will vaccinate everybody, all the adults in the school.\par \par \uc2 And we actually had them sign a piece of paper where they pledged to come back on March 1. And today, out of 600-and-some school districts, we only have eight that are not back.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Seven of those eight will be back in the next several weeks.\par \par \uc2 So, we went from 50 percent now today better than 90 percent. And it`s -- it was just imperative that we did it. It was a tough decision. But we really need to get these kids back in school, particularly kids in the urban districts.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: We have many children in our big city districts that have not been in school since March. So, we`re approaching one year.\par \par \uc2 And we were just hearing from parents. We were hearing from educators, mayors. The mayors of the big cities of the state said, look, we have got to get these kids back in school.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, the so-called achievement gap, it`s already up to two to three years. It`s going to be -- who knows what kind of long-lasting damage there will be.\par \par \uc2 With this new vaccine that was announced by \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 , how much of that is your state going to get? And how much will that help to continue the reopening process?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Yes, we`re going to get over 90,000 doses this week in the next several days. We`re putting those out to independent pharmacies, but also to health departments and to hospitals.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re spreading it out around the state. It`s very exciting. Frankly, that, plus the additional doses that we`re getting because of the uptick from both Moderna and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , over 400,000 doses this week. It`s enabled us to lower the age to 60. It`s enabled us to add police officers, members of fire department, as well as some other groups.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re very excited about this.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The stimulus package is -- it`s on its way. The Senate will vote on it.\par \par \uc2 There`s a sort of -- I guess you can call it a formula for what states get part of that big $350 billion pile of cash. Your feelings on this? I mean, as a governor, maybe you want any kind of money you can get, but it seems like overkill at this particular point, considering where the overall economy is.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Well, the formula that the House came out with, I don`t think is good. A number of the governors wrote letters, wrote a letter, and just basically said, look, this is not fair.\par \par \uc2 What they`re doing is, they`re basing it on unemployment, instead of population. And we think they should stick to population. That, historically, is what Congress has done.\par \par \uc2 And Ohio, our unemployment is pretty decent. We`re about 5.5 percent. And we have tried to balance between keeping things open, but, at the same time, keeping -- keeping people safe.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: And we brought the business community in very early to kind of design the system and the protocols that would be followed by business.\par \par \uc2 So, we have been able to do this. And we don`t think that Ohio and other states, frankly, should be punished for that. We think we should just stay with the normal population formula.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Yes, it feels...\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Whatever the Senate ends up doing, we hope that they flip back to that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, it feels like the more irresponsible or at least ham-fisted states were, the more they`re going to be rewarded.\par \par \uc2 I want to switch gears a little bit, Former President Trump at CPAC yesterday talking about schools, and heaping a whole lot of blame on President Biden. Let`s take a watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There`s no reason whatsoever why the vast majority of young Americans should not be back in school immediately.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: The only reason that most parents do not have that choice is because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  sold out America`s children to the teachers unions.\par \par \uc2 His position is morally inexcusable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Governor, once we learned the science was not necessarily there, I know, and -- to keep these schools closed, I mean, you just cut a deal. I`m sure the teachers union was a major obstacle.\par \par \uc2 Is there a lesson to be learned here?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Well, I think one thing that you and I have not talked about was very important, and I didn`t mention it.\par \par \uc2 But we started hearing from teachers. We had a lot of schools who stayed in all year. And then the question was, if Sally in class comes down with the virus, then who gets quarantined around her? And we followed the CDC. We said anybody within six feet would have to be quarantined.\par \par \uc2 Teachers kept coming back to us and said, look, you`re -- we`re quarantining them for a few weeks, and no one`s getting sick. So, what we did is, we actually went out into our schools, seven different school districts. And we did -- we did a test. We started testing kids that were close to somebody who came down with it.\par \par \uc2 And we also did a control group. And what we found was that it was about 3 percent for the control group was positive, about 3 percent for those who had actually been -- quote -- "been close to someone" and were able to be exposed.\par \par \uc2 And so what it showed us was that there -- when everyone`s wearing a mask in classroom -- and kids were very good about it, teachers have been great about it -- when that was happening, we were, frankly, not seeing much spread at all in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 So, that was another piece of evidence that, when we talked -- when teachers would say, is it safe to go back, a legitimate question, our answer is, look...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: ... we tested, and we showed that, in the classroom at least, there`s no spread.\par \par \uc2 Plus, now we`re going to vaccinate the teachers and everybody else in the school that wants the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I`m out of time, but if you could give me a one-word answer, is there a civil war in the GOP, or are things getting better? Just a yes or no?\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: It`ll work out.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: We will be OK.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It`ll work out. I like that.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: We will be OK. We will be OK.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That`s good. We will leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Governor DeWine, all right.\par \par \uc2 DEWINE: Thanks, Charles. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210301eh3100002\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  is Interviewed about Immigration and Neera Tanden; \uc2 Sen. Mark Warner\uc2  (D-VA) is Interviewed about Capitol Protection, Khashoggi Repot, Iran and the Minimum Wage Fight; Biden Administration Reopens Migrant Child Facilities; Air Strikes on Iran-Backed Militia; Power Player of the Week Caleb Anderson.\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Wallace, Ben Domenech \par \uc2 8,811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Chris Wallace.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump set to make his first public appearance since leaving office as the Republican Party tries to figure out its future.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice-over): The GOP facing an identity crisis at the Conservative Political Action Conference in Orlando.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): In the face of this mortal threat to our nation, our very existence, some preferred to fan the flames of a civil war on our side. That's foolish, it's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: We'll ask Florida Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who is in charge of trying to win back the Senate for Republicans, about his call for party unity.\par \par \uc2 Then, President Biden's COVID relief package passes the House.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH R. BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If we act now, decisively, quickly and boldly, we can finally get ahead of this virus. We can finally get our economy moving again.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But Democrats face a big setback in their bid to boost the minimum wage. We'll discuss the critical week ahead with the White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, the president takes his first military action as commander-in-chief, ordering an airstrike on Iranian-backed militia. We are joined by Senate Intelligence Committee chair \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, Mr. Biden's moves on immigration face sharp push back as he stamps out Trump era policies. We'll ask our Sunday panel about the new crisis on the border.\par \par \uc2 And our "Power Player of the Week," a 12-year-old whiz kid on his way to college.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): And hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The center of the political universe today is Orlando, Florida, and the annual conservative gathering at CPAC. While a parade of 2024 hopefuls has addressed the crowd, the main event is former President Trump's first public appearance since he left office more than a month ago.\par \par \uc2 Insiders will be watching closely not only for what Mr. Trump says about his political future, but also where he sees the Republican Party headed over the next few years. Perhaps the biggest immediate issue, whether he intends to work with or against the GOP establishment.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll speak with Florida Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , head of the National Republican Senatorial Committee, charged with winning back the Senate for the GOP in 2022.\par \par \uc2 But first, Mark Meredith reports from Orlando, where conservatives are eagerly awaiting Mr. Trump's big speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Former President Trump is expected to get a hero's welcome when he addresses conservatives at CPAC this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're not going anywhere. We are Trump nation and we are here to stay.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: All weekend, some of Trump's staunchest supporters have delivered speeches calling for the 45th president's agenda to remain the Republican Party's platform.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: And let me tell you this right now: \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  ain't going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 RIC GRENELL, FORMER ACTING DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: America First is now positioned to guide our country through the trials and tribulations of the next generation.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Today, Trump is not expected to announce plans for a run in 2024, but other potential presidential hopefuls appear to be testing the waters, from rallying behind lifting lockdowns.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: Florida got it right and the lockdown states got it wrong.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R), SOUTH DAKOTA: I don't know if you agree with me, but Dr. Fauci is wrong a lot.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: To pushing back against the Democratic agenda.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: I hear President Biden say, America is back. Back to what?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): They are more focused on renaming Abraham Lincoln High School than they are opening up so kids can go back and learn there.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Some high-profile Republicans avoided the conference, including former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who declined an invitation to speak.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Minutes before Trump speaks today, CPAC is due to announce the results of its straw poll. Attendees have been asked who they'd like to see the 2024 Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 Chris, given the crowd that we've seen all weekend long, Trump should do very well -- Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Mark Meredith reporting from CPAC in Orlando, Mark, thanks for that.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now from Florida, Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who as we said has the job of trying to win back a GOP Senate majority next year.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): Chris, it's nice to be back.\par \par \uc2 The CPAC was fun. I got to go there on Friday. I talked about the Republican civil war is canceled.\par \par \uc2 If you get out of D.C. and talk to people, they are focused on what the Biden administration is doing and what Republican stand for, and that's what I'm going to do --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Let me pick up -- let me pick up on that. You said Republican civil war is now canceled, but let's look at the divide just this weekend about CPAC.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the Republican leaders who were -- either weren't invited or chose not to attend: Vice President Pence, Senate Leader McConnell, Governor Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 And listen to this split among House leaders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you believe former President Trump should be speaking at CPAC this weekend?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Yes, he should.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY\uc2  (R-WY): I don't believe that he should be playing a role in the future of the party or the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Senator, that is not a united party.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, Chris, if you -- if you look at -- if you look at what's on TV, you'd say, yeah, right, there's a division. But if you get out and talk to voters -- I talked to voters, you know, especially in Florida, you know, almost every day, and they're focused on what the Biden administration is doing. They do like open borders. They don't like killing the Keystone pipeline. They don't like men-women sporting events.\par \par \uc2 Those are things that bother people and then all the Biden administration is not going to be good for jobs. So I'm going to make sure that in '22, when we have our elections, next elections, we're going to vote on job growth, we're going to vote on secure borders, we're going to vote on supporting our law enforcement, school choice.\par \par \uc2 That's why we're going to win. That's how I won make my elections. My -- you know, each of my elections was an issue election and that's you win. And that's what we're going to do. It's not going to be about --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Let me --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: -- what TV is staying about some infighting.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But this isn't just TV, Senator.\par \par \uc2 President Trump is expected to stake his claim as the continuing leader of the Republican Party in a speech today. But you have top Republican leaders like \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , like \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  who say the president forfeited that because of his actions after the November election.\par \par \uc2 So let me ask you, is this still \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  party?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: It's the voters party. I mean, it always has been the voters party. Let's go back to 2010 and my primary. Every Republican I think in the country endorsed my opponent and I won.\par \par \uc2 I talked to President Trump about a week ago and I told him, this is my job. My job is to help Republican senators win all across the country, and he made a commitment to me to help me do that. I believe he's going to be helpful, but I think other Republicans are going to be helpful.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, it is your election and when you're running, you've got to do all the things. You've got to talk about the issues important to your state. You've got to do your grassroots. You've got to do all these things to make sure you win. You've got to get prepared for your debates.\par \par \uc2 That's where we are going to win in '22, we're on the right side of the issues and the Democrats are on the wrong side of the issues.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But -- and we'll get to that, I promise, but you know, let's talk about Trump and his continuing hold on the party. This is kind of a loyalty oath for the Trump party.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you a straightforward question, Senator. Did \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  win this election fair and square?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Absolutely. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the president. We went through the constitutional process. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the election.\par \par \uc2 Now, do -- are there people that believe we've got to focus on making sure people feel comfortable that elections are fair? Yeah.\par \par \uc2 I've done two bills. We need to have voter ID. You need to prove citizenship. You need to show your Social Security. You can't have, you know, unmonitored ballot box.\par \par \uc2 You've got to do the things that make all Americans feel comfortable that we should have 100 percent participation and zero percent fraud.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I want to pick up on that because there are a number of Republican state legislatures which are talking about new voting restrictions, and I want to ask you about at least one of them. In Georgia, the Republican-controlled House has a bill that would limit early voting, mostly to weekdays from 9:00 to 5:00. It would reduce the number of absentee ballot drop boxes and it would restrict early voting on Sundays when black churches hold Get Out the Vote events.\par \par \uc2 Do support making it harder for people to vote?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Of course not. We need to have -- we need to create a process that people can get out the vote, but we've also got to create a process where there's no fraud.\par \par \uc2 People should not feel that their vote is ever diluted, so should you have ballot boxes that are unmonitored? No. Should you have your vote in by election night? Yes. Should you keep voting -- keep counting the votes once you start seeing -- we get the results quickly? Yes. Should you signature match? Yes.\par \par \uc2 There are some basic things we should do, but we should create a process where people get to vote, but make sure no vote is ever diluted.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But -- but let's pick up, because this specific -- the one that struck in the Georgia bill, you know, I know that a lot of black churches hold Get Out the Vote events. You go to church, then you go for early voting.\par \par \uc2 If you restrict voting on Sundays, what's -- what reason is that other than trying to suppress the black vote?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I want people to -- I want people to vote. We have -- we have voting on Sunday in Florida. You know, I think we ought to have some national standards of what we expect to make sure we get -- so there's no fraud.\par \par \uc2 You know, it worked in Florida. We have mail-in ballot systems that works. I want these things.\par \par \uc2 I want people to vote. I would -- you know, I tell people what they are going to vote for me or not, get out and vote and participate in the process.\par \par \uc2 But I don't want people -- I don't want people to feel like their vote was wasted because there was fraud. I mean, come on, unmonitored ballot boxes, no signature match, no voter ID, those things don't make any sense. That's what I'm focused on.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Let me talk about another issue that you're going to face, which is primary battles inside the Republican Party in 2022, which could make it harder, your job, to take back the Senate for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has talked openly about endorsing primary candidates at all levels to run against GOP incumbents. Let me put up a few of those. He's going after Georgia Governor Brian Kemp. He just backed a challenger to Republican Congressman Anthony Gonzalez, who voted to impeach him, and this one affects you directly for the Senate, he says he'll oppose Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , who is running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 So will you support incumbents running for the Senate or would you support or Trump challenger to that incumbent in the Senate in 2022?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I am supporting every Republican incumbent in all the Senate releases. So I believe all of our incumbents are going to win. We have some open Republican seats, open Democrats seats. I think we're going to get -- I trust the voters. I think we're going to get great candidates come out.\par \par \uc2 And look at -- look at Ohio, we have, what, five or six great candidates that are -- that looks like they're going to be in. We're going to have a great primary. I think we'll have great primaries across the country.\par \par \uc2 And once these individuals get to the primary, I'm going to a very aggressive to make sure we win all these seats and we get back the majority.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Finally, the $1.9 billion -- $1.9 trillion COVID relief plan from President Biden just passed the House. It's headed your way to the Senate and President Biden has taken to asking this question. Take a look, Senator.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH R. BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNTIED STATES: Critics say the plan is too big. Let me ask them a rhetorical question. What would you have me cut? What would you leave out?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Senate -- Senator, what's your answer? What would you cut?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That's pretty easy, Chris. That's pretty easy. Oh, boy, that's pretty easy.\par \par \uc2 Do we need to pay for bridges? Does it have anything to do with COVID? Do we need to pay for tunnels for Silicon Valley? Does that pay -- does that do anything?\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you look -- if you look at what they're doing -- listen, if we're focused on COVID, let's take care of the people of lost their job, let's help our businesses, let's make sure that we have plenty of tests and make sure we have plenty of vaccines, get out quickly.\par \par \uc2 But this radical left stuff -- first off, giving money to states when their revenues have not even gone down and we've already given them $400 billion and we don't even know how much -- how much has not been spent.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have $27 trillion worth of debt in this country. Let's spend the money where we need to spend the money but stop wasting money. Interest rates are going up. Inflation is starting to kick in. We're going to hurt the poorest families in the country if we're not careful.\par \par \uc2 So I want to help people, Republicans want to help people. This is -- this is just a radical left-wing agenda that they're trying to cram through because they think -- they think they can tie it to COVID.\par \par \uc2 If they like the tunnels so much in California, do a separate bill, see if it passes. If they like the bridge for Schumer in New York, do a separate bill.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: All right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: See if it passes. It wouldn't pass.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Senator Scott, I had a feeling you'd have an answer to that question. Thank you. Thanks for your time this morning and we'll be watching what President Trump has to say later today. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: See you, Chris. Have a good day. Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Up next, now it's the turn of Senate Democrats to pass President Biden's COVID relief package. But what will they do about the $15 minimum wage?\par \par \uc2 We'll ask White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  about that and more when we come right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: President Biden's sweeping $1.9 trillion COVID relief bill has passed the House and is now on to the Senate, but there is still plenty of hurdles there before Vice President Harris can cast the tie-breaking vote.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll speak with White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 . But first, let's bring in David Spunt, traveling with the president in Wilmington, Delaware -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Chris, President Biden is moving full steam ahead with his recovery plan, and he's doing it without Republican support.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Now the bill moves to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  where I hope it will receive quick action. We have no time to waste.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice-over): The eyes of anxious Americans are now in 100 senators poised to debate, then vote on the president's $1.9 trillion relief package. The White House hoped for bipartisan support, but in a House, it was nonexistent.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Democrats are so embarrassed by all the non-COVID waste in this bill. They are jamming it through in the dead of night.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The Democrats bill would increase the minimum wage to $15 an hour, a major progressive priority.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: The $7.29 minimum wage that exists now in many instances an exploitation of the American worker.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: But the Senate parliamentarian rolled, including the minimum wage in the relief package would break Senate rules.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB CASEY (D-PA): I think it will pass if it doesn't have it in it, but I'll tell you, that's a big loss for the country.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Senate Democrats are already working on plan B to force large companies to pay at least $15 an hour or pay a 5 percent payroll tax penalty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Democrats want the entire plan signed into law before March 14th. That's when unemployment benefits run out -- Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: David Spunt reporting from Wilmington -- David, thanks for that.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now, White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jen, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Thank you, Chris. Thanks for having me on this morning.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: President Biden has taken to asking critics of his COVID relief bill what would you cut. The nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  says that more than one-third of the total bill, $700 billion, would not be spent this year but would be spent between 2022 and 2031.\par \par \uc2 So how does that qualify as COVID relief?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first, Chris, the focus of the package is getting direct checks to the American people, getting vaccines in the arms of Americans, and ensuring that people who need help at this very difficult time can get it.\par \par \uc2 But people also have to plan. Schools have to plan to reopen. They need to do budgetary planning over the course of years. Businesses need to do that as well.\par \par \uc2 So this package is meant to, of course, provide immediate, direct relief, but also provide a bridge to help us all get through this crisis.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But let's take one specific area that you just mentioned, schools. The bill would give $170 billion to schools from elementary through college, but the CBO projects that 95 percent of that money, again, won't be spent this year, but will be spent from 2022 to 2028.\par \par \uc2 And let me drill down even further, $480 million of the COVID relief bill, according to the CBO, would go to the arts, humanities, museums, libraries. Again, what does that have to do with emergency COVID relief and getting kids back into schools this year?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first of all, the -- 90 percent of this package goes to address the twin crises we're facing right now, Chris, which is getting the pandemic under control and helping the American people go back to work, helping them get the relief they need to get through this period of time.\par \par \uc2 Schools are like businesses, Chris. They need to budgetary plan over a period of time. Some have to frontload changes whether it's to their facilities or insuring that if they have to hire additional teachers or bus drivers at this point of time --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But we're talking about -- but we're talking about a decade from now, Jen.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: -- social distancing.\par \par \uc2 Well, they have to plan over the period of time. I'm sure.\par \par \uc2 I don't want my kids to be going to school where they have to fire teachers next year, or won't have to be able the facilities up -- facility upgrades I should say, necessary, in order to insure they're going to school safely. That's exactly what is included in this package.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Let me turn to another subject about the bill. Now that the Senate parliamentarian has ruled that the $15 minimum wage does not qualify under the budget rules, some Senate Democrats are talking about either using tax incentives or tax penalties to get corporations and small businesses to go there as part of the COVID relief bill.\par \par \uc2 Does the president support that?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I think you're referring to Senator Sanders' proposal, Chris, and we have not yet reviewed that proposal. But the Senate -- the president supports exactly what Senator Sanders does, which is increasing the minimum wage for the American people, for workers who are just trying to make ends meet, and he thinks that's long overdue.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have to spend the next several days or even weeks figuring what the best path forward is, but he's committed to doing that.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You also had a busy week in foreign policy and I want to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 During the campaign, candidate Biden said that -- he promised to crackdown on Saudi Arabia for the murder of Jamal Khashoggi. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're going to, in fact, make them pay the price and make them, in fact, the pariah that they are. There's very little social redeeming value of the -- in the present government in Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: And on Friday, U.S. intelligence agencies released this report: We assessed that Saudi Arabia's crown prince, \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , approved an operation in Istanbul, Turkey, to capture or kill Saudi journalist, Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 Couple of questions, Jen. Does the president still believe as he did back in November of 2019 that the crown prince was personally responsible, personally ordered the murder of Jamal Khashoggi? And if he does, why didn't he move to act against MBS personally?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, the president believes this was a horrific crime, and what we saw outlined in the unclassified report released, as you referenced, Chris, is confirmation of the details of this crime, some that we've known for sometime. And president put -- was an advocate for releasing that and abiding by our legal obligations.\par \par \uc2 Let me be very clear, though. We took specific actions on Friday. Our objective is to recalibrate our relationship. We've already taken steps to do that, make sure it's not a continuation of the last four years, and prevent the horrific acts that happened to journalist Jamal Khashoggi from ever happening again.\par \par \uc2 That's why we put sanctions through the Treasury Department, on the deputy head of the intelligence department, why we set -- why we -- under the Magnitsky Act designated the Saudi intelligence forces, why we've taken very specific steps, Chris, to make sure we're sending a clear message to the world.\par \par \uc2 And that's what we announced on Friday.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But it isn't a clear message.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the president in a Democratic debate said that he believed that MBS, the crown prince, personally ordered the murder of Khashoggi. That's what his intelligence report says.\par \par \uc2 I'm asking you now, does he personally believe that MBS ordered the assassination of Khashoggi? And if he does, why not penalize him? He sanctions a bunch of other people. He doesn't sanction the crown prince.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first, let me be clear that behind the scenes there are a range of diplomatic conversations that make absolutely clear to the Saudis and to others around the world, that this is going to be a different kind of relationship. And our actions make that absolutely clear as well, Chris.\par \par \uc2 Historically though, as you all know, you've been covering these issues for some time, the United States has not historically sanctioned the leaders of countries where we have diplomatic relations or even some where we don't have diplomatic relations. We understand that's a bar some are holding this to, but our objective here from the government, from the Biden administration, is preventing this from ever happening again.\par \par \uc2 I should also note, we also announced the Khashoggi Rule, which gives power to the secretary of state to be able to prevent foreigners who have shown that they are going to threaten dissidents or journalists from traveling on a visa to the United States. This is a global issue and one we're going to be clear in sending a message about, both privately and publicly, Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I'm running out of time but I got a couple of questions I really need to ask you.\par \par \uc2 The president also ordered a military strike in Eastern Syria this week against Iranian-backed militias. Is he sending a message that there is a link between an Iran nuclear deal and Iran's activities in the region, that there can't be an agreement, there can't be successful diplomacy at the same time that Iranian-backed forces are attacking U.S. personnel?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Chris, he's sending an ambiguous message that he is going to protect our men and women serving and our coalition forces. And that's exactly what he did with this strike.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the Iranian nuclear deal, we believe that the best path is a diplomatic path forward. We want to prevent Iran from acquiring a nuclear weapon. That's in our national interest, that's in the global and national interest. Where that sits now is we're waiting, the Europeans are waiting to hear back from the Iranians on whether they will sit at the table and have that discussion.\par \par \uc2 But the strike this week was specifically about responding to threats and attacks that were threatening our troops and our men and women serving overseas.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: The president -- or rather the Biden administration this week reopened a migrant facility for children that was used under President Trump and that unleashed a storm of criticism, as you know.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez tweeted: This is not OK, never has been OK, never will be OK -- no matter the administration or party.\par \par \uc2 Now, you have distinguished between what you admit you are doing and what the Trump administration is doing. I'm asking you a different question though.\par \par \uc2 Isn't the Biden administration contributing to this crisis by reversing the Trump policy which called for immediately returning migrant children who crossed the country -- unaccompanied children who crossed the border by themselves?\par \par \uc2 There -- Border Patrol officials are saying that you are creating a surge and that by May we could see a bigger surge than we saw during the worst moments of the 2019 crisis on the border.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, Chris, the vast majority of families and adults are turned away at the border. This is not the time to come and we have been very clear about that.\par \par \uc2 But our approach from the Biden administration is that we think the most humane step we can take here is to have these children, unaccompanied, kids under 18, not send them back to take a treacherous path forward.\par \par \uc2 This is a difficult choice but that's one that we felt was the right one, the most humane one, because these are kids, they're fleeing prosecution, they're fleeing difficult circumstances in their country. And our objective is to treat them humanely and to keep them safe.\par \par \uc2 And the reason, I should say, that we opened this facility is because of COVID, Chris, as you know. And that's why it's open because we want to keep these kids safe, including at a time where we're facing a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But aren't you, by changing the facility, aren't you encouraging some of these young people to come? Which means you're going to have a bigger crisis on the border.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, Chris, we've been unambiguous and very -- being clear in the region, this is not the time to come. And this is the difficult choice for everyone.\par \par \uc2 But as a mother, as a parent, I'm not going to support, nor was the president, sending kids under 18, some at the age of 11, 12, 13, back on a treacherous trip across the border, to face persecution and difficult circumstances in their country.\par \par \uc2 These kids are coming alone and this is -- this is the choice we've made because we feel it's humane and we feel safe. That vast majority of any family that's coming together, any adults that's coming are turned away at the border --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: -- because we have not had time to put in place comprehensive immigration policies yet at this point in time.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Finally, when will the White House pull the nomination of Neera Tanden to be budget director?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, Chris, we remain committed to fighting our hearts out for Neera Tanden. We believe she is the right person. She's qualified, she's experienced, to lead the budget department. She brings unique experience as somebody whose lived through benefiting from a number of these programs and she's worked on these issues for decades across the aisle. So we're going to keep fighting for her.\par \par \uc2 We knew that some of these fights would be difficult and this is certainly one of them. But she's going to have a number of continued engagements this week and we are certainly hopeful we can get it across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Jen, there was lots to talk to about. You guys made a lot of news this week.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I Know. We tried.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Thank you. Thanks for joining us today. Always good to talk with you.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thanks, Chris. You too.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Up next, President Biden takes his first action as commander in chief in Syria and releases that report on the Saudi crown prince. We'll talk with the chair of the Senate Intelligence Committee about the emerging Biden foreign policy, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Coming up, the White House releases a report on the murder of Jamal Khashoggi, but doesn't sanction the Saudi crown prince.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY BLINKIN, SECRETARY OF STATE: What we've done by the actions that we've taken is really not to rupture the relationship, but to recalibrate it.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: We'll ask Senator \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2  about the president's decision, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: This week, President Biden was staking out new U.S. policy in the Middle East, but at the same time, Congress was still grappling with a shocking attack on the Capitol last month.\par \par \uc2 Here to discuss it all, the chair of the Senate Intelligence Committee, Senator \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, the acting chief of the \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  said this week that they intend to keep up enhanced security at the Capitol at least through the president's speech to a joint session of Congress sometime next month.\par \par \uc2 From all the intelligence you see, is there a credible threat of another attack against the Capitol?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK WARNER\uc2  (D-VA.): Well, Chris, in a public reporting this past week, there were indications that some of the insurrectionist groups that stormed the Capitol on January 6th potentially had a -- have interest in seeing if they can cause more destruction when President Biden gives his State of the Union. That's coming just from the public reports. So I think it is appropriate that we keep that security in place.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I want -- I want there to be a new regime. I'm -- I'm -- I'm the local guy. I live in northern Virginia. Capitol Hill is, in a sense, my extended neighborhood. I want those fences to come down as soon as possible so that the American people can have access to their elected representatives to see the halls of Congress.\par \par \uc2 But I was there January 6th when those insurrectionists were willing to trash our Capitol. And if there is still valid intelligence that that threat continues, we've got to get this balance right. It's one of the reasons why I think it's extraordinarily important that my committee, the intelligence committee, not with a backward look, but on a forward-looking, look at these domestic, violent extremists. They're oftentimes ties to similar groups in Europe. Oftentimes the fact that they are amplified by Russia. The truth is, as director of the FBI, \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2 , has said a number of times, this is a long-term national security threat to our country.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Senator, let's turn to the intelligence community's assessment released this week that the Saudi crown prince, \uc2 Mohammad bin Salman\uc2 , was responsible, that he ordered the assassination of the journalist Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of the Biden administration's decision not to impose sanctions on MBS and, I don't know if you noticed, but in the interview I just had with \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , she declined to even repeat the president's contention during the campaign that he believed that MBS had ordered the assassination.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, Chris, first of all, let's step back.\par \par \uc2 You know, Jamal Khashoggi was a journalist. He tried to hold powerful people accountable, and that cost him his life. And I think if we look back, particularly during the Trump administration, Mr. Trump took his first trip to Saudi Arabia. He said business is open. Matter of fact, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  even complemented MBS, the Saudi leader, for the kind of crackdowns that took place during \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's tenure.\par \par \uc2 And this is a very messy neighborhood, Saudi Arabia. Its record toward dissidents, towards women have been pretty dreadful for decades. They are a key ally in terms of our challenges against -- against the Iranians.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But -- but, sir, I just have to ask you, because we've got limited time. Senator, do --\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Let me -- no, let me -- let me finish -- sure, let me finish, though.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Well, do you think that the -- that the president should have imposed sanctions on MBS?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: And -- and the question is this, we -- it was our intelligence committee -- it was our intelligence committee that passed the law that made this report public. And I think what we've seen out of the Biden administration s they've taken the first step of sanctioning those individuals around MBS. I think they need to keep open additional sanctions against MBS if we don't see a change in behavior.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Then, there was the U.S. air strike in eastern Syria this week against Iranian-backed militias. It seemed that in the Biden -- in the Obama administration they felt that the nuclear deal was so important that they didn't link it to Ian's malign activities in the region.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that as the Biden administration pursues diplomacy, perhaps another nuclear deal, that that should be linked to what Iran does in the region and specifically that you can't have diplomacy at the same time that Iran is sponsoring attacks on U.S. personnel?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Well, I think the Iranians are always going to test a new regime. And when Iranian proxies are putting our troops in Iraq in harm's way, there needs to be punitive actions taken. And I think the Biden missile launch was appropriate pushback against the Iranian proxies. There needs to be a guarantee that we're going to protect our troops. I think you can create a dual track where you guard our troops, but also potentially reopen conversations with the Iranians, but they will continue to press any opportunity they see and I think the Biden action was appropriate pushback.\par \par \uc2 Now, I wish the Biden team would have given Congress greater knowledge and greater warning. We got a heads up about 15 minutes before the attack took place. I think it brings into question a whole new debate around the authorization of use of military force, something my friend Tim Kaine has been advocating for almost a decade now. We need to have that debate in Congress.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Senator -- Senator, I've got a little over a minute left, I need a quick answer here.\par \par \uc2 As we said, the COVID relief bill is coming to the Senate this week. Were you concerned at all about the questions I asked \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  about billions of dollars, hundreds of billions of dollars that wouldn't be spent for years, and where are you on the idea of trying to use the tax code to get around the parliamentary issue and, in effect, either impose tax penalties or tax incentives to get companies to raise the minimum wage to $15?\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Actually, some of the things you referenced were investments in mass transit systems that around the country are about 90 percent down in terms of user ridership. I don't think we ought to completely destroy mass transit during this nonuse during COVID.\par \par \uc2 In terms of -- in terms of the $15 for corporations penalty, I'd be concerned there about those corporations then outsourcing that work to small enterprises that wouldn't be a penalty. I'm hoping in this package we can narrow some of the checks to those most in need and potentially put some broadband investment here. Broadband, I think, is absolutely linked to COVID.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But not using the tax code for the $15.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Listen, I want to see that proposal. I've seen the written reports. I do -- I do want to make sure that a company would not simply move their work from the large corporate enterprise to a smaller enterprise that might not fall under that penalty and somehow have the work outsourced in a way that may not be appropriate.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Senator Warner, thank you. Thanks for coming in. Please come back, sir.\par \par \uc2 WARNER: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Up next, we'll bring in our Sunday group to discuss the growing crisis at the southern border and what it means for President Biden's overhaul of U.S. immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This is not kids being kept in cages. This is -- this is kids --\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: This is a facility that was opened that's going to follow the same standards as other \uc2 HHS\uc2  facilities. It is not a replication. Certainly not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  defending the Biden administration's immigration policies against critics who say he's dealing with migrant children the same way President Trump did.\par \par \uc2 And it's time now for our Sunday group. Co-founder of "The Federalist," Ben Domenech, Susan Page of "\uc2 USA Today\uc2 ," and former Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Jane Harman\uc2 , director of the Wilson Center and author of the new book "Insanity Defense: Why Our Failure to Confront Hard National Security Problems Makes us Less Safe."\par \par \uc2 Ben, what do you make of the White House contention that what they're doing, putting these kids in migrant child facilities, is different from what the Trump administration did, and are they, in effect, as I discussed with \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , are they creating a magnet for these unaccompanied minors to come over by saying that they can stay in the country?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, "THE FEDERALIST" AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: They absolutely are, Chris. And I want to correct something that the White House keeps saying here about just reopening this facility, declaring essentially that this is a limited action. They also have opened, you know, a new facility in Donna (ph), Texas, and they're going to be opening another one in Del Rio area because -- within the coming days because of the increases of numbers that we're seeing, to levels that traditionally we don't see until later in the spring.\par \par \uc2 This is a situation where the Biden administration is dismantling the Trump approach to the border, and that is having consequences, serving as a magnet, as you've said, for more people to come over and we're only going to see this crisis continue to grow in the coming weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yes, Susan, I want to pick up on that, that there is an impact from the Biden decision to reverse the trump policy, which was, if you had unaccompanied -- this isn't kids that are being separated from families, these are unaccompanied minors, 12 to 18, 19 years old, and it -- where before they would turn them around and send them home, now they're saying you can stay. Some would argue that by saying they can stay, more of them are going to come. And, in fact, there's an alarming report this week from "Axios" that border officials now project that 13,000 unaccompanied minors will cross the border in May, which would be worse than at any point in the crisis in 2019.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": So this is a good example of how governing is so much more different than politicing. So the Biden administration has made it clear they've got a moral stance when it comes to immigration. But it has all these real-life consequences. And while \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  talked about the difficult choices involved for the Biden administration in making some of these calls on immigration, you can imagine the difficult choices that desperate parents are making in Central America. And they've got to look at these policies and say, she says this isn't the right time to come to the border. Maybe it is the right time to send our unaccompanied minors, our kids, to the border with the hopes that they get into a facility, get into -- perhaps get vetted, get moved to live with relatives that are in the United States.\par \par \uc2 This is not -- this is a problem now. It's going to be -- I agree with Ben, this is going to be a much worse problem in the next couple months, especially as pro-immigration groups in the United States pressure the Biden administration to do even more to implement the policies he talked about when he was a candidate.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: All right, let's turn to the U.S. air strike on those Iranian- backed militias in eastern Syria this week.\par \par \uc2 Here was a comment from Defense Secretary General Lloyd Austin. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN, DEFENSE SECRETARY: We're confident in the target that we went after. We know what we did. We're confident that that target was being used by the same Shia militia that -- that conducted the -- the strikes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Congresswoman Harman, was this a good move by President Biden, perhaps his first military action at least that we know about as commander in chief, and what about the argument that as he moves to get back into a nuclear deal with Iran, that any progress on diplomacy should be contingent on Iran's actions in the region, and at the very least that they're not attacking U.S. personnel?\par \par \uc2 JANE HARMAN\uc2 , FORMER CONGRESSWOMAN (D-CA) AND WILSON CENTER DIRECTOR: Well, a couple of points, Chris, and -- and thank you for mentioning my book.\par \par \uc2 I think this was a proportionate response and the right response.\par \par \uc2 What I think was not adequate was the notice to Congress. \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2  is right, both the Gang of Eight, which I used to be part of as ranking member on the Intelligence Committee, and the Foreign Policy Committees of Congress, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would be screaming if you were chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, should have gotten fuller notice. It's also true that Congress should do more to replace those authorizations to use military force enacted right after 9/11 that are completely irrelevant to what's going on now. We have done 40 military actions in 19 countries using the 2001 AUMF, which just applied --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HARMAN: Anyway, I think it was the right thing. And on them malign activity of Iran, I never believed that the agreement that was struck by the Obama administration prevented us from calling Iran on its malign activity.\par \par \uc2 We're doing the right thing. We should still be talking to Iran. They should expect that we can walk and chew gum at the same time.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HARMAN: Hopefully they can too, and they have an election coming up and I think this is smart action.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Ben, what do you think we learned this week about President Biden and particularly his foreign policy, both from the air strike on those Iranian militia in Syria, and also from what he did and didn't do with regard to the Saudi crown prince and the assassination, the brutal murder of Jamal Khashoggi?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Well, t\\o circle back to what Susan said, governing is harder than politicking. And I think that on the -- his reaction to the Khashoggi killing, I think that that's a good example of that.\par \par \uc2 The killing has been used, obviously, to create a big wedge in -- or attempt to create a wedge between this normalization of Arab states with Israel that we saw under President Trump. I think that's going to continue to be a nagging issue.\par \par \uc2 As it relates to the strike, I think the real question we should be asking is, is this a brushback or is it a prelude to more involvement in the region? The American people, I think, have largely become convinced that we ought to have most of the solutions to those problems solved by the nations that are in that region, and at America's foreign policy interests should shift to look a little bit more at the rising China and other threats around the world that go directly to our national interest.\par \par \uc2 But I think that that's something that we don't really know yet to -- in terms of what to expect from this Biden administration and it's a question that remains open, I think, after this week's action.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You know, Susan, it's so interesting, with Obama, with Trump, now we hear with the Biden administration they keep saying we're going to get out of the Middle East and we're going to focus on the Pacific and take on China. They never seem to be able to get out of the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes. Well, you know, it's hard. Foreign policy is tough. I think what we've learned this week about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and foreign policy is pretty pragmatic. I mean I think that's true with the situation with the Saudis, unwilling to blow up our relationship with the Saudis because of this horrific killing of a journalist working for an American newspaper. That is to the disappointment of some of his supporters, but it's to the relief of some in the foreign policy community.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Thank you, panel. See you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our "Power Player of the Week." A remarkable whiz kid racing through school and proving the sky's the limit.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: At age one, he could read. At age three, he qualified for Mensa. And now at age 12, he's heading for his dream. Here's our "Power Player of the Week."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CALEB ANDERSON, 12-YEAR-OLD COLLEGE STUDENT: My gift is just, you know, I learn a lot quicker than any -- you know, a lot of other children.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Today is decision day.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: To say Caleb Anderson learns quickly is an understatement. At age 12, he's a college student.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are thrilled to offer you --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: And he recently found out he's going to \uc2 Georgia Tech\uc2  next fall where he'll study aerospace engineering.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): You say that you're not that smart. Really?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: No, no, I don't think I'm smart. Number one, there are a lot of people who are smarter than me and, you know, there are a lot of people who work harder than me. So, compared to them, you know, I'm definitely not smart.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Caleb, what does this say.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: Book.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice over): Caleb's parents beg to differ. At age six months, he was reading.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Dog (ph). Yes, that's right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He's always had this like hunger and thirst for knowledge.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: They nurtured his abilities, teaching him sign language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can you sign juice?\par \par \uc2 Juice.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: By age two, he was a first grader.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How many hats were there?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: Three.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Good job.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: I definitely remember, you know, all of these kids, you know, twice my age and twice my height towering over me. I think that's when I first realized that, you know, I'm a bit different.\par \par \uc2 So, six minus three is three.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Being different wasn't always easy.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: Middle School was pretty bad to me. They're not old enough to, you know, understand and they're not young enough to, you know, not care.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: These days Caleb is wrapping up high school while taking courses at a technical college.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: Currently I'm just studying really basic things that, you know, I'm going to need for aerospace engineering. I'm studying physics one, chemistry one, and I'm taking world and American literature.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): How to the other students treat you?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: Well, every once in a while I get a double take, but they really look at me as, you know, a little brother.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: If in your academic life you're a college student, where are you outside of school in terms of your social life?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: I'm a 12-year-old seventh grader. I like to swim. I love Marvel. I love "Star Wars."\par \par \uc2 What's the most extensive computer.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (voice over): But then there's his remarkable gift. Comedian Steve Harvey gave Caleb a laptop and is paying his full college tuition.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: Thank you!\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Plenty for the family to celebrate, including younger siblings who are also in gifted programs.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE (on camera): Do you feel pressured to live up to your potential?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: Yes, yes I do. I'm a pretty big risk-taker. I'm taking the fast track, but I -- you know, it's going to be very hard. That's what drives me because I know that if I don't do this now, this chance is never going to happen again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: What a remarkable young man.\par \par \uc2 Caleb also hopes to be a role model for young students of color, encouraging them to consider careers in science.\par \par \uc2 And that's it for today, have a great week and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020210228eh2s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Hackensack Meridian Health CEO \uc2 Bob Garrett\uc2 ; Party Infighting; Minimum Wage Fight; Republicans Focus on Party Future at CPAC; Interview with Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby; Interview with Rep. Roger Marshall (R-KS)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Adam Lashinsky, Hillary Vaughn, Blake Burman, Gillian  Turner \par \uc2 8,798 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: The Will Cain Show\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, Martha, very much.\par \par \uc2 Do you ever get the idea the parties are not on the same page when it comes -- I'm not talking about with each other, each party with themselves?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. So glad to have you. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World," and making sense of the two parties that are showing more intra- playing division than we have seen in quite some time.\par \par \uc2 For Democrats, a lot of it has to do with hiking the minimum wage. Progressives are very concerned the president dropped the ball. They don't want it to happen. They're going to continue leading the fight. Meanwhile, among Republicans, division over whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is their future.\par \par \uc2 And the comments of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  indicate that maybe, just maybe not everyone's on the same page about that future. Depends on who you talk to. Fair and balanced, we will talk to both sides, beginning with Hillary Vaughn in Orlando right now at CPAC on the fallout from all of this for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, here at CPAC, you're right, it is all about getting Republicans on the same page. President Trump, former President Trump, will be here on Sunday to chart out what he thinks will be a winning strategy for Republicans in 2022 to try to flip the House and win back the Senate after they lost it in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Do you blame the former president for losing the two Georgia Senate seats?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I don't have any further observations to make about that.\par \par \uc2 We're looking forward...\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Well, let me ask you this.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: ... dealing with the new administration and trying to hold the Senate in '22.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me -- it's ahead of this big speech this weekend at CPAC.\par \par \uc2 Should the former president be speaking at CPAC this weekend?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: I don't have any advice to give the former president about where he should speak or what he should say.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK.\par \par \uc2 Is there a civil war in the GOP currently?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: No.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: But, Neil, we have seen some friendly fire on stage here today.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  called out his colleague \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  , saying, if she were here, she would get booed off the stage.\par \par \uc2 I also caught up with Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  on his way into CPAC. He's the head of the campaign arm for the Senate Republicans. He told me -- quote -- "The Republican civil war is canceled" and the way to win in '22 and '24, no matter who the nominee is, to focus on the issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): Of course. President Trump has flaws. I have flaws. We all have flaws.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Sources tell FOX News that President Trump is at least warm to the idea of a 2024 run. And conservatives here at CPAC are more than warm but fired up about the idea of another term for Trump.\par \par \uc2 McConnell told own Bret Baier that he thinks, even if Trump is in the mix for 2024, he hinted that he will have some competition from some of McConnell's colleagues in the Senate. One of those suspected to be a 2024 presidential hopeful is Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  , who told me just moments ago he thinks, if Trump wants to try for a second term, he will be the party nominee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): I would expect he would clear the field.\par \par \uc2 I mean, listen, I don't know. I'm not a prognosticator. But, I mean, my bet would be that, if he says he wants to run again, that he will be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: So, you're not thinking about 2024?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Well, I'm thinking about 2024. But I'm up for reelection in the state of Missouri in 2024. I hope the people of Missouri keep me around.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: What about 2028?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: I don't have any plans to run for president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And, Neil, that was a question in response to what we heard from McConnell. He said there are four members in the Senate who are thinking about running in 2024.\par \par \uc2 But as you heard just there, Hawley says he's not one of them -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for that, Hillary Vaughn.\par \par \uc2 Now to the Democratic side. They have their problems too, particularly when it comes to hiking the minimum wage. Now, the Senate parliamentarian in charge of these matters said, for a reconciliation measure, you really can't be forcing something like this that will not have any effect on budgets or revenue into this particular measure, so you're going to have to find another way.\par \par \uc2 Some of them are looking for that other way right now.\par \par \uc2 Blake Burman in Washington with more on that front.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Blake.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE BURMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 As we know, Democrats have control of power here in Washington. But, already, we have seen a hiccup in the Senate. And, later tonight, we will be watching a vote as it relates to that $1.9 trillion rescue plan that President Biden has been pushing.\par \par \uc2 Democrats ahead of this are confident that that vote can pass in the House. However, they can only lose a handful or so votes for that to happen. Now, if it does pass, it would then go to the Senate. However, the Senate parliamentarian has already ruled that the part of the package that includes raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour cannot be a part of the legislation in that chamber.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats, though, are keeping the $15 minimum -- $15-an-hour minimum wage increase in the package that will be voted on tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, the top Republican in the House, Kevin McCarthy, says that is only because the House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  , wants to appease the left.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): She knows it will not survive in the Senate because the parliamentarian already told her that. So, is she keeping it in the bill to keep her payoffs exactly what I named the bill? This is for a progressive wing of the party who says they will not vote for the bill unless they have that in there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURMAN: Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  has already saying, if the Senate gets the House bill, he will try to compensate by adding an amendment that would call on large corporations to pay their employees at least $15 an hour, or else risk losing tax breaks.\par \par \uc2 Aboard Air Force One earlier today, the White House press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  , was asked if President Biden would support that idea.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We have not reviewed, and we don't have a final conclusion on that proposal. I will tell you that we are committed to finding the best path forward to increasing the minimum wage, and that will require a number of conversations with leaders in Congress and members who are committed to this issue moving forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURMAN: Neil, over here at the White House, they say that President Biden is -- quote, unquote -- "disappointed" in the Senate parliamentarian's ruling. However, they say they are going to respect that decision -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Blake Burman at the White House, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 So, there you have it, a little bit of consternation within both major parties in this country. Is it as big a deal as it appears to be? Or are these the kind of things that happen a lot, sometimes at the same time, and no need to worry, if you're a Democrat looking at the Democrat infighting or Republican looking at the, well, Grand Old Party infighting?\par \par \uc2 Let's go to \uc2 Reid Wilson\uc2  right now, The Hill correspondent.\par \par \uc2 What do you think, Reid? Who is more divided for the time being?\par \par \uc2 REID WILSON\uc2  , THE HILL: I don't see either party terribly divided here, and sort of for the reasons that both your reporters just talked about.\par \par \uc2 Look, on the Democratic side, they're trying to appease a few members in the House Progressive Caucus on the far left and people like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  in the Senate, and the members on the right side of the Democratic Party, the Joe Manchins, Kyrsten Sinemas of the world, who still have tremendous power, because they are the 49th and 50th vote in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Republicans aren't really divided at all. I mean, the war that people were talking about between the never-Trumpers and President Trump, you heard from Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  there, who said the war has been canceled.\par \par \uc2 It's been canceled because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's already won it. He is as much a future of the party as he wants to be for the foreseeable future, because those folks down at CPAC and activists across the country are still simply enamored of him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I think you hit on a key point, Reid, that that base, if you will, or -- you talk about progressives within the Democratic Party who might have to just swallow their concern about this wage thing not being included in the stimulus bill. Where else are they going to go?\par \par \uc2 The faction that is the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  faction that has prompted disinvites to the likes of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  , \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  isn't going to be there, \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 's not there, \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  , \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  , on and on, there's a huge difference between this so-called subset in the party that, as far as I can see -- and you cover this far more adeptly than I do -- that it is substantial in the case of Republicans, the so-called Trump wing, whereas, with Democrats, this progressive wing that wants to push still further into areas like a higher wage a smaller part?\par \par \uc2 WILSON: Yes, one of the things that I think we have noticed over the last couple of years is that there's a disconnect between Republican leaders in Washington, I mean, the people that we think of -- or thought of as the leaders of the Republican Party prior to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , and the actual activist base of the party.\par \par \uc2 And in a lot of senses, I mean, those Republican leaders in Washington are afraid of their own voters. I'd point to a place like Virginia, where the state Republican Party just decided to have a convention to nominate their gubernatorial candidates. And there was a lot of hand-wringing among what you would think of as sort of traditional Republican candidates and the Trump faction of the party over whether or not to hold a primary or a convention or some event that -- basically, a lot of Republicans were trying to figure out, how could they best control the voters who are at the moment willing to kick the bums out?\par \par \uc2 I mean, there is a level of anger within the Republican base that is aimed just as much at the Republican Party as it is as Democrats. And I think that's been the case basically since the Bush administration.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that was the genesis of the Tea Party movement, the genesis of President Trump himself.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, but they're shooting themselves, aren't they?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WILSON: You know, there's a long time to go until 2022.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're right.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  is an interesting guy to watch. He is the head of the Senate Republican Committee. He's got a line to both \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and to President Trump himself.\par \par \uc2 And he knew Trump before either of them got into politics. My sources tell me they see each other as sort of peers more than Trump does -- than Trump sees other politicians as peers.\par \par \uc2 So, I would watch someone like \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  and how he acts and how he talks about this Republican infighting going forward.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you might be right, going back, obviously, to \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  's Columbia Health days, when he was running that health care conglomerate.\par \par \uc2 Reid Wilson\uc2  of The Hill, thank you, my friend very, very much.\par \par \uc2 So, if there's not a civil war, let's say, going on in the Republican Party, is it just an uncivil type of conduct in the Republican Party, when so many of the speakers that we have heard at CPAC are routinely trashing other Republicans?\par \par \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  comes to mind. \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  comes to mind, and, yes, some of these other top names that are also being touted as potential candidates down the road.\par \par \uc2 Roger Marshall is the Kansas Republican senator, joins me right now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, I will put it to you flat, then. Do you think or are you worried about the tone coming out of CPAC right now among some, not all, where you're going after \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  , going after \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  ? There's a reason why they're not there. And the powers that be don't want them there.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER MARSHALL (R-KS): Well, Neil, I think, first of all, President Reagan, and he said something like, thou shall not speak ill of your fellow Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: So, I might remind everybody of that.\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, we're Republicans. We're allowed to disagree amongst ourselves.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Senator, they're not -- Senator, they're not doing that.\par \par \uc2 That rule for \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  , that's long gone, right?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Well, I think, regardless, I certainly -- we're allowed to disagree with each other. I believe in this concept of iron sharpening iron.\par \par \uc2 But over the course of the next two years, you're going to see the radical liberal policy of the Democrats unite Republicans. So, there are sort of -- we disagree on some things as Republicans, but we certainly disagree with the Democrat policy that we're seeing going forward right now, policy that's killing jobs, open borders, taking away our freedoms.\par \par \uc2 And that's what's going to unite all the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. So, let's get into that a little bit here, because much has been made of some prominent Republicans who were talking about the possibility of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  running for the presidency again.\par \par \uc2 I want to go first to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  , who might surprise some folks by tipping his hand where he would go if that were the case. This is from \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Well, there's a lot to happen between now and '24.\par \par \uc2 I have got at least four members that I think are planning on running for president, plus some -- some governors and others. There's no incumbent. Should be a wide-open race and fun for you all to cover.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: If the president was the party's nominee, would you support him?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: The nominee of the party? Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. So, he's the nominee, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is going to support.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  was asked his very question by my colleague \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2  on FOX Business, which, Senator, if you don't get, you should demand. But I digress.\par \par \uc2 And the issue came up again. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were running for president in 2024, he were the nominee, would you support him? This was a little bit more cagey answer.\par \par \uc2 This is \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-