rump we clear the field. He will raise all the money. And it'll be a -- he will make pretty short work of it.\par \par \uc2 But it's a long time. It is a long, long time, dog years in time, between now, when that primary really kicks off.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it certainly is.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to indulge this too much. And you have been very patient, Senator. But there are a lot of people who feel \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might have a good shot at winning the Republican nomination and influencing a lot of Republicans, but in the general electric , he -- electorate, I should say, he doesn't command the same poll attention and support.\par \par \uc2 Some other of those Republicans I mentioned are concerned, if he were the nominee, and it was a divided Republican Party -- we see hints of that today between the pro-Trump crowd and the anti-Trump crowd -- that Republicans are doomed. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: You make a great point, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And, gain, I'm going to circle back. It's a long time between now and then, I think, after four years of Democrat policies, I think a lot of those people that kind of voted for President Trump in '16, did not in '20, they may be ready to come back. Once they get a -- gets a big spoonful of these liberal policies of the Democrats, it may be a different day between now and then, because, look, I think most people, the supermajority of Americans love President Trump's policies.\par \par \uc2 I understood that they don't always like some of the things he did or tweet -- tweeted or the way he said it. But I think they certainly liked his policy. So, four years of liberal policy may swing a bunch of those people back his way.\par \par \uc2 But we will see.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You don't think they will forget January 6, what happened, or attach any of that to him?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Well, I think that's all -- that's all part of it. I can't make a good argument against it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: 