as well.\par \par \uc2 Adam, that's the fear, right? That is the fear, that we have hit this seminal inflection point that might drift people out of stocks and into Treasuries, not in a crazy way, but maybe starting the process. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 ADAM LASHINSKY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's absolutely the fear for stock investors, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And we have been through a period of time in recent months, certainly for all of the pandemic, where one of those periods in history where it looked like it was easy to invest in stocks, because they all went up. And you just laid out very clearly for people why they all went up, because investors had to get -- had to risk, had to get their yield somewhere.\par \par \uc2 They simply weren't going to get it from bonds of any kind. So, they were looking for it in any kind of stocks, any kind of equities. And just judging from the past couple days, as you said, it appears like that time may be ending, because it doesn't take much. When equities are vastly overvalued, the way they have been, it doesn't take much to turn that around.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We have been so used to, Adam, interest rates near zero. I always talk about the shorter end here, but people who know, that applied for mortgages, they benefited from that.\par \par \uc2 It's been the only reason why people rushed into stocks. Where the heck are they going to go? Now, lately, a lot of them go into real estate. I understand that. But if this it takes hold, where will investors go? Will it be a healthy development? Will it be something that people sort of spread their riches and alternatives?\par \par \uc2 How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 LASHINSKY: Well, generally, it feels like a healthy development to me, starting because you know that -- just takes sort of \uc2 GameStop\uc2  as a metaphor. You know that wasn't healthy.\par \par \uc2 You just sort of -- everyone understands that's not healthy, when valuations on all sorts of things go kablooey. That's a term of art on Wall Street, by the way, Neil. And so that's one healthy development.\par \par \uc2 And then being able to get some return on your savings in a pretty safe place, including government bonds, which are almost riskless, is also kind of, I think, a healthy development, as opposed to not having anywhere to get a pretty darn safe return.\par \par \uc2 So, I see all of this as generally healthy, but, as you point out, forces people to make some decisions.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A quick thought on something, though, Adam.\par \par \uc2 I'm old enough to remember what inflation really was like, and I'm old enough to remember -- when I pass this story to young people, they always said, OK, here goes gramps talking about his first mortgage.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LASHINSKY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But when I got my first mortgage with my wife, we paid 13.5 percent, and we thought we were financial geniuses. People are just saying, well, you're an idiot. I mean, it's over 2, 3 percent, I'm not in.\par \par \uc2 Could we get back to those kinds of days? Is that -- sometimes, you don't know inflation until beyond seeing the whites of its eyes. But what do you think?\par \par \uc2 LASHINSKY: Well, I didn't have a mortgage at rates like that. But I did have savings accounts that I remember were higher than 10 percent, which I thought was normal as a...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 LASHINSKY: I was a teenager or a kid or whatnot. But that was kind of cool to be making 10 percent. And that's how I started to understand that.\par \par \uc2 Look, the fact that we're debating it so aggressively, the fact that so many people like \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  and economists in the Biden administration are saying, oh, don't worry about inflation, I find that somewhat comforting, right?\par \par \uc2 It's a debate. It's not an, oh, yes, you should really be worried about runaway inflation. So I'm not worried about runaway inflation. I am worried about inflation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. Well, you're young and clueless, Adam, so that -- I don't know.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LASHINSKY: It's true.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mean, seriously, very good to have you back my friend, Adam Lashinsky, Business Insider columnist, great writer on these developments over these many decades. So, he just looks young, but he's not really young.\par \par \uc2 Dan Bongino speaking at this big event right now going on, CPAC. You can catch him on FOX Nation. We are streaming all of that. So, if that's your cup of tea -- and Dan is everyone's cup of tea, right? You can go there.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we are going to go to the Pentagon spokesman right after this on this first attack into Syria on the part of the Biden administration of this rite of passage that has a number of critics, many in the president's own party -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the first strike as commander in chief, but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is getting criticism for his move against Iranian-backed militias in Syria from the darndest of places, like fellow Democrats -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we're getting a few more details here on something that was kind of long known, but we didn't know exactly just what the culpability was in the murder of that \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  columnist Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 What we're learning as well is that this was released to the world. And the Saudis have had, I guess the word would be a curious response to it all.\par \par \uc2 Gillian Turner right now in Washington with more on that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, the U.S. intelligence community has now released its first official assessment of the murder of Jamal Khashoggi. And the verdict is crystal clear. They blame Saudi Arabia's crown prince, also the country's de facto leader.\par \par \uc2 Now, the director of national intelligence writes in an unclassified three- page report dressed out now -- take a look -- quote -- "Saudi Arabia's Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  approved an operation in Istanbul, Turkey, to capture or kill Saudi journalist Jamal Khashoggi."\par \par \uc2 They also write: "We base this assessment on the crown prince's control of decision-making in the kingdom, the direct involvement of a key adviser and members of \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  's protective detail in the operation and the crown prince's support for using violent measures."\par \par \uc2 We're now, Neil, three years out from Khashoggi's death. This report is a huge victory for critics of the Saudi government and the royal family. While the intelligence community has long confirmed that bin Salman is responsible in private, this is really the first opportunity the U.S. public is getting to see this assessment in writing. It makes it a lot more real.\par \par \uc2 Now, this report lays bare the extent to which senior members of the Saudi administration went to cover up on behalf of the royal family. The Saudi foreign minister explicitly denied charges on behalf of the crown prince on FOX News back in 2018. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADEL AL-JUBEIR, SAUDI ARABIAN FOREIGN MINISTER: The conference has denied this. The conference is not aware of this.\par \par \uc2 Even the senior leadership of our intelligence service was not aware of this. This was an operation that was a rogue operation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: That is Saudi Foreign Minister Al-Jubeir. He even went so far as to blame unknown perpetrators. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AL-JUBEIR: We will never engage in such behavior. This is an aberration. This is a mistake. This is a criminal act. And those responsible for it will be punished.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Now, this is still developing, Neil, but in response to all of this, the State Department is announcing that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  's actually going to do very little.\par \par \uc2 They say they are taking steps to restrict the visas of 76 Saudi nationals, including the crown prince, maybe. So it's really not a whole lot -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 Gillian, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 The read right now with John Kirby, the Pentagon press secretary.\par \par \uc2 John, is there not then going to be any administration response to this?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Well, I won't get ahead of decisions that the president may or may not make on this, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I mean, from the Pentagon's perspective -- and the secretary talked about this last week after he called the crown prince. I mean, we have a military-to-military relationship with Saudi Arabia. There are security commitments we have to help them defend themselves.\par \par \uc2 But, clearly -- and we are, as a government, unafraid to deal with issues, concerns overrule of law, civil rights, human rights. But I won't get ahead of -- I won't get ahead of the White House on this. They have made it clear that our relationship with Saudi Arabia going forward is going to be different than it was in the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 But, again, that's really -- that's really for the White House to speak to.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, the president did have a phone call with the royal family.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just concerned, is there any push on our part to say, all right, you got to switch guys, you got to do something, because this is not palatable?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Again, I think we all share the concerns over what happened to Jamal Khashoggi, and for his family.\par \par \uc2 And as we have made clear, the relationship, the bilateral relationship with Saudi Arabia will have to be different. It's going to have to change going forward. The question is, exactly how do you balance those concerns over, from our perspective at the Pentagon, security commitments that we have in the region?\par \par \uc2 And, again, I wouldn't prejudge anything at this point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. I will switch gears. That was only just news because it just happened today.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I understand.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But also happening overnight, of course, yesterday, at this time, John, was this surgical strike, apparently very swift, very clear, very precise, on these positions, Iranian-backed militia positions within Syria.\par \par \uc2 Can you tell us the results of that yet, that -- they have been few and far between? Who was taken out, if anyone was taken out, was can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, Neil, our battle damage assessment is ongoing for these strikes. But what we can say is that two F-15 Strike Eagle aircraft launched seven precise guided munitions on a set of structures in one compound, destroying basically nine buildings, nine facilities that were at this compound.\par \par \uc2 This compound, the Abu Kamal, basically, an entry point compound on the Syrian-Iraqi border, on the Syrian side of the Syria-Iraq border, was used by two groups that we know were linked to the recent attacks on our troops and Iraqi troops inside Iraq, rocket attacks, and including the one in Irbil, which killed a contractor from the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 We know these two groups were involved in those attacks. We know that those two groups used those facilities, that compound on that structure to help facilitate their activities inside Iraq.\par \par \uc2 And so, again, airstrikes by two manned aircraft basically demolished the structures on that site.\par \par \uc2 I don't have an update for you, Neil, on casualties on site. Again, we're still processing the information from that. And when and if we get more information that we can share, we will do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Were you surprised at the criticism you got from Democratic Senator Tim Kaine on this, that it wasn't justified, you didn't -- essentially, what he's saying, you didn't consult Congress on this.\par \par \uc2 He has been consistent on that type of policy, but a number of other Democratic congressmen and certainly a few Republicans saying the same thing, that this was a cavalier approach. What did you think?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I can tell you, from the Pentagon's perspective, Neil, there was nothing cavalier about doing this kind of mission.\par \par \uc2 It was planned carefully, deliberately, methodically. Now, we wanted to give our Iraqi partners time to investigate and to come up with their view of attribution in terms of the groups responsible. They were very valuable in that regard.\par \par \uc2 Nothing cavalier about this. And Congress, proper notifications were made on Capitol Hill. And we have made it clear that we -- we have been in consultations today. And we are prepared to give a classified briefing on this operation later this week. We fully understand the need to keep members of Congress informed.\par \par \uc2 This was a perfectly legal authorization by President Biden, both under Article 2, his responsibilities as commander in chief in the U.S. Constitution, and Article 51 of the U.N . Charter, which gives nations the right of self-defense.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: John, the Russians are complaining that they got only a couple of minutes' notice. I don't know whether that is significant in the scheme of things, though they were given notice regardless, but what did you think of their criticism?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, I'm grateful that the Russians acknowledged that we actually complied with the deconfliction channel and did inform them of air operations, kinetic air operations that were happening inside Syria, that we did use the notification, the deconfliction channel, if you will, to do this properly.\par \par \uc2 And, look, as for time, I won't get into the specific timing of that. It was used. It was used prior to the strike happening. And I think you and your viewers would perfectly understand why we would want to be relatively careful with the speed with which information was provided outside U.S. and coalition channels, for operational security purposes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Does this have -- I know this -- I can almost predict a non-answer here, and it would be understandable.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But we are looking at reopening nuclear talks with the Iranians, right? And then something like this happens. I know you can work on a couple of different tracks. I get that.\par \par \uc2 But have you gotten any word, or has the defense secretary gotten any word that this complicates that, or does it help that? Does it send a strong signal to the Iranians?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think, Neil, it's really important to remember that this strike was done to protect U.S. forces on the ground in Iraq particularly, but also our Iraqi partners.\par \par \uc2 These two groups were the ones responsible for rocket attacks that actually did injure one U.S. service member and threatened our security at various facilities. And it was important for us to remove from these groups' hands a facility that we knew they were using to facilitate their operations inside Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Also, it was certainly going to send, and we hope it sends, a deterrent signal to these groups and to their backers in the region and other adversaries in the region.\par \par \uc2 This was about protecting American troops on the ground in Iraq. And we certainly hope that the message of our resolve and our determination and our ability to do that has been sent wide and clear throughout the region.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Once again, though, that point, John, has been disputed by a number of progressives, who were not fans prior to this of the president freezing all troop withdrawals that were in effect throughout much of the world, forget the Middle East, places like Germany, and Japan, that he wants to keep the troop presence as it is.\par \par \uc2 Others, by the way, welcome that, that it shows a resolve not to go too far with this sort of thing. And many argue that the Trump administration might have been going too far, particularly in places like Germany and Japan.\par \par \uc2 But I'm curious as to the criticism from those Democratic senators and the like, who fear that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be another Middle East-engaged president, whether he likes it or not.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think you have heard the president speak quite a bit about ending the forever wars, and particularly when we're talking about Afghanistan, which I know we aren't today, ending this in a responsible and sustainable way.\par \par \uc2 We also have significant security commitments in the region.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because that's -- well, that's what President Trump said, right, John, on ending these never-ending wars.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: That's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you drew a line at removing all troops, right?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Well, what we're allied with is ending these long wars, and doing it in a way that is sustainable.\par \par \uc2 But it's important, Neil, to remember why we're in Iraq and why we have a small footprint in Syria, and that's about going after \uc2 ISIS\uc2 , which is a greatly diminished terrorist threat and network, but they are still there. And they still do pose threats.\par \par \uc2 And we only have 2,500 troops on the ground in Iraq and less than 1,000 in Syria. But it's very focused on the counter-\uc2 ISIS\uc2  threat. And we have to be nimble. And so that -- I'm not going to prejudge any decisions that the president may or may not make about troop force presence there.\par \par \uc2 But it's important to remember that it's limited to a significant mission there. And we're there at the invitation of the Iraqi government.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have in the department -- and I think you and I have talked about this -- Secretary Austin has ordered a global posture review to take a look over the next couple...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: ... of months about where we have troops everywhere around the world and what missions they are doing and whether it's tied to the appropriate strategy. And we will work through that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 John Kirby, thank you. I know I threw a lot out there.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon press secretary on these fast-moving developments. A lot going on just on that field, right?\par \par \uc2 All right, the good news is that the vaccines are coming up fast and furious. The bad news is, so are the long, long lines to get them.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the good news is vaccines are coming out fast and furiously. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  panel on the verge of officially approving \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  's one-shot dose that will obviously expand the pool of available treatments that are out there.\par \par \uc2 The problem is getting your hands on them. And they have these mega-sites set up all over the country, including one in East Rutherford, New Jersey, that could ostensibly handle that traffic. But it is still an uphill battle.\par \par \uc2 Bob Garrett\uc2  joins us right now, the Hackensack Meridian Health CEO.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good to have you on.\par \par \uc2 These facilities are very large. They aim to be very efficient, but, invariably, crowds form, and they don't know the procedure or who gets what and when and how appointments are honored.\par \par \uc2 How is that being handled?\par \par \uc2 BOB GARRETT\uc2 , CEO, HACKENSACK MERIDIAN HEALTH: So, Neil, first of all, thanks for having me back on your show. It's always a pleasure to see you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Same here.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT: I think we have to kind of level set and put it in perspective.\par \par \uc2 If you think about it, just six months, nine months ago, who would have thought that 70 million Americans would have already had a vaccine? They were talking about developing a vaccine that would take years. Distribution would probably take another year after the vaccines were developed.\par \par \uc2 So, we have to put it in a little bit of perspective. I do really understand people's frustrations. We are really literally working around the clock to try to deliver vaccines. The mega-sites are ramping up still. We started a couple of weeks ago doing about 1,500 vaccines a day there.\par \par \uc2 Next week, we're going to be up to 4,000 a day. We are getting more and more supply in as the weeks go on. It's been a little bit slow. But I think it is improving by the day. And, as you mentioned, with the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine hopefully being approved over the next couple of days, we're actually expecting the shipments to come as early as next week to places like New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, on the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine, the fact that it's a one-dose vaccine, apparently proven to be quite effective, how is it sorted out who gets what vaccine?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT: Yes. So, what each state has a separate distribution plan.\par \par \uc2 In New Jersey, those sites that invested in the ultra-cold freezers are delivering the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT: Other sites that don't have that ultra-cold storage have been delivering \uc2 Moderna\uc2 . \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  only needs refrigeration, so it's going to be a much easier distribution plan.\par \par \uc2 So, I can speak for Hackensack Meridian. And what we're planning to do is, if we do get the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  (INAUDIBLE) the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccine next week, we're going to be delivering that vaccine in places like our emergency department, because it is a one-dose vaccine. So it's easier to administer to those patients. They don't have to come back.\par \par \uc2 We're going to try to even administer it to those patients who are eligible who are being discharged from the hospitals themselves. We're going to try to get them out to people's homes through our home care program, and also to some of the underserved communities through some of our clinics and some of our primary care physician offices.\par \par \uc2 So, the J&J product will be much more portable, if you will, and much more deliverable, because it doesn't have to be stored in those -- those ultra- cold temperatures.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Bob Garrett\uc2 , Hackensack Meridian Health CEO.\par \par \uc2 Again, to his point, there's no shortage of possible vaccines and treatments out there. The problem is that proverbial final mile. They're trying to address it in some of these large distribution centers.\par \par \uc2 You see similar ones popping up all around the country, with the hope being that all those vaccines getting in a lot more arms and a lot faster.\par \par \uc2 All right stay with us on that front.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we're learning right now that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is getting ready for the big vote on the stimulus plan in the House. In the Senate, it's going to be a dicey call to get that all done, especially with the battle over the higher wage.\par \par \uc2 But she is going to address the press soon to talk about a vote sometime tonight on that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we are waiting to hear from \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  . She's going to spell out the plans tonight to vote on that $1.9 trillion stimulus measure in the House.\par \par \uc2 Remember, they had a devil of a time getting the hike in the minimum wage approved as part of the reconciliation vote they're going to have in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 She is still trying to come up with ways around this. It's anyone's guess how she can do that. In the House, it's one thing, in the Senate, quite another. And it's the Senate that will finish this recipe, won't it?\par \par \uc2 Erin Gibbs, Gibbs Wealth Management president and CEO.\par \par \uc2 They're trying to find ways to save this. So let me take the leap that they find a parliamentarian way to do it in the Senate, Erin. How are the markets going to like that?\par \par \uc2 ERIN GIBBS, GIBBS WEALTH MANAGEMENT: I think, for the most part, they're going to respond positively. Though we know that there's going to be some difficulties with small businesses trying to meet those new minimum wages, at least they are giving them it looks like three years to adapt.\par \par \uc2 And so businesses have the opportunity to move more towards automation and increasing productivity, so that they can adjust to these higher wage prices. So I think, overall, we understand that those higher wages ultimately lead to hundreds of more billions of dollars within the economy.\par \par \uc2 And the people at the lower end of the wage scale, they spend their money, and it's been proven that that money immediately gets spent, vs. some of the growth in the higher income brackets. And so I think, overall, Wall Street will see it as a positive.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wall Street seems to be expecting it. But there's a lot in this package that, first of all, isn't immediate. More than half of it is in future outgoing years. We have got a trillion unspent, as you have heard and reported on -- when we have talked on this in the past, Erin.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm wondering, we need, obviously, the focus on the vaccines and all of that, that stuff, and to deal with what's happening in nursing homes and schools. I get it. But that covers about a third of the cost of this. Does anyone care about that?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes, I think, when you look at the package, there are so many different components of it that you may not personally agree with or agree with.\par \par \uc2 I think, in general, Wall Street just wants it, getting it done, so that we don't have that uncertainty. As you know, Wall Street doesn't like uncertainty. So, if the package just gets done, it can at least get passed, and figure out how to do business the new way.\par \par \uc2 So that's really more the concern vs. certainly picking apart certain pieces. I'm sure everybody has a problem with some of the spending within the bill.\par \par \uc2 But I think the bigger concern is, with a $15 minimum wage, the fears about inflation are going to be -- rear their heads a little more, vs. if they had gone for that $10 or $12 minimum wage, because we know that the national average is already $11.80.\par \par \uc2 And so anything below that, the $10 or $12, probably would have been much more easily absorbed by Wall Street. I think the concerns over inflation, particularly in the farther out, two to three years out, might be the one negative that Wall Street will see from this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're going to get more stimulus down the road.\par \par \uc2 We only have less than a minute here, but I'm -- we're going to have infrastructure. We're probably going to have another type of a trillion- dollar package to address a whole host of things. I mean, does all this spending worry you?\par \par \uc2 I mean, we're on the cusp of going to two back-to-back record deficit years. Where do we go?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 And the bigger problem, one of the bigger concerns is that we're spending all this money during a period of super low interest rates.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: And, as interest rates get higher, being able to make those payments is becoming harder.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, it is a concern, definitely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Erin, thank you very much, Erin Gibbs, Gibbs Wealth Advantage.\par \par \uc2 She's been talking about this, the fear of spending crazy.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will outline that. We will have reaction to that, the impact of all of this on stimulus and where the economy goes from here, tomorrow, 10:00 a.m.\par \par \uc2 We will see you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210227eh2q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Visits FEMA Facility In Texas; Syria Condemns U.S. Military Action; U.S. Airstrikes Signal Muscular Approach In Iran; Some Americans Waiting Outside Vaccine Distributions Centers in Hopes of Getting Vaccines that would Otherwise be Discarded; Deepfake Technology Causing Concerns over Identify Theft and National Security; Biden Administration Launches Airstrike against Iranian-Backed Militias in Syrian; Senate Parliamentarian Rules $15 Minimum Wage Increase Not Eligible for Reconciliation\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Chad Pergram, Peter Doocy, Trey Yingst, Chad Pergram,  Mark Meredith, Jonathan Hunt, Casey Stegall, Gillian Turner \par \uc2 7,686 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: They say we love animals that most people don't necessarily love. So, when they find them and they need help, they nursed them back to help. And then they let them go again. But look at how cute but they're not that cute when they're big. They get to be 37 inches long.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's it for us, have a great --\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: The House is hours away from voting on a relief bill as lawmakers refuse to strip that bill, the $15.00 minimum wage, even though the Senate parliamentarian says it can't be in there.\par \par \uc2 Plus, President Biden's surveys the damage in Texas after destroying targets in Syria.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans host their first meeting since the election and decry Biden policies and cancel culture.\par \par \uc2 Plus, is it or isn't it how an online Tom Cruise is blowing people's minds while also presenting a problem with separating fact from fiction?\par \par \uc2 This is SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is in Houston right now. He's delivering remarks at this hour to coronavirus vaccination facility run by the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2 . You see him there. We are monitoring these remarks and we'll turn around any news.\par \par \uc2 First tonight, White House correspondent Peter Doocy on the president's domestic trip, when many of the questions he's facing are about foreign policy surrounding the order for airstrikes in Syria that we first told you about at the end of SPECIAL REPORT last night. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Within the last hour on his way to those remarks, President Biden told reporters those airstrikes were to send a message to Iran. "You can't act with impunity, be careful." And the day after hurting Iranian back forces, he is down there trying to heal Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You're saving peoples' lives. As my mother would say, you're doing God's work.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): On the way to Texas, the White House explained the rationale behind the first airstrikes Biden ordered as commander-in-chief destroying facilities used by Iranian backed groups who targeted American interests in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president sending an unambiguous message that he's going to act to protect Americans. And when threats are posed, he has the right to take an action at the time and in the manner of his choosing.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And every peaceful response must have been exhausted already. According to foreign policy rules, Biden himself explained at the Pentagon two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The central indispensable mission of the Department of Defense is the -- is to deter aggression from our enemies, and if required to fight and win wars to keep America safe. But I believe force should be a tool of last resort, not first.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But some Democrats wish Biden would have asked them for an OK. Senator Tim Kaine protests, offensive military action without congressional approval is not constitutional absent extraordinary circumstances. Congress must be fully briefed on this matter expeditiously.\par \par \uc2 When locations in Syria were targeted in 2017 by Trump, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  had questions. "Also, what is the legal authority for strikes? Assad is a brutal dictator, but Syria is a sovereign country." Now, she argues that was different because Trump was interfering in a Syrian matter.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: 2017 was an attack on the Syrian -- on Syrian military installations in response to a chemical weapons attack. Last night was a strike on militant groups in defense of U.S. personnel under attack in Iraq. There's a massive difference in both policy and law.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There's also a massive difference between House and Senate rules. The House is allowed to pass a COVID-19 relief bill with a $15.00 minimum wage attached, the Senate is not.\par \par \uc2 According to the Senate parliamentarian, progressives are now pushing Vice President Harris to overrule.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): There are progressives who believe that the vice president should do the same thing that Vice President Rockefeller did, Vice President Humphrey did in not accepting the advice of the parliamentarian.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Not happening according to the White House.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN DEESE, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL DIRECTOR: Well, the president and the vice president respect the Senate process and will not overrule the parliamentarian's decision in this case.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: In a statement last night, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said President Biden is disappointed in this outcome, but he couldn't have been surprised because this aired three weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But that may not be in your American Rescue Plan?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, I put it in, but I don't think it's going to survive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He was right. But as of right now, even without the minimum wage increase in there, this package has zero Republican support. So, even though \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  told me yesterday that the White House was not resigned to trying to pass this with Democratic votes only, it looks like that's what's happening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Syria is condemning Thursday's military action, calling it a cowardly attack and urging President Biden not to follow what he calls the law of the jungle. Correspondent Trey Yingst has the latest from our Middle East newsroom.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Fox News has learned the threat level for American service members in Iraq was raised today, as the U.S. military fears retaliation for airstrikes overnight in eastern Syria.\par \par \uc2 According to U.S. officials, seven 500-pound laser-guided bombs were dropped near the Iraqi border, killing a reported 22 Iranian backed fighters.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and coalition personnel in Iraq were targeted three separate times with rockets this month by Iranian backed Shia militias. One attack happened 11 days ago in the city of Erbil, killing a contractor and injuring five Americans, including one U.S. service member.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , DEFENSE SECRETARY: We're confident in the target that we went after, we know what we hit. And we're confident that that target was being used by the same Shia militia that conducted the strikes.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The Pentagon quickly released a statement calling the strikes proportionate and done in consultation with coalition partners.\par \par \uc2 It was the first major American military action abroad since President Biden took office and the Department of Defense was clear, these targets were meant to send a message.\par \par \uc2 The ongoing provocations from Iranian proxies come amid a backdrop of significant tension regarding the 2015 nuclear deal. The stalemate has only increased regional provocations against the U.S. and its close ally, Israel.\par \par \uc2 A high-ranking Israeli official told Fox News on Friday that the Jewish state was notified ahead of the U.S. airstrikes in Syria. Reports last month indicate Israel is preparing plans to strike Iranian nuclear sites if needed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The United States, Iran and Israel are all making ongoing calculations. We do know the region is waiting to see what happens next. But a single misstep could lead to a much larger conflict in the Middle East, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, there in Israel, how much concern is there that the Biden administration despite this action against Iranian militias, is really working hard to reestablish this Iran nuclear deal?\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The major concern for Israel, Bret, according to officials we've spoken with here in Jerusalem is that the Biden administration is going to cut a deal of their own with the other partners to the JCPOA agreement that would ultimately leave the proxy threats off the table.\par \par \uc2 Israel faces threats from places like Gaza, with groups like \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and Islamic Jihad and also Lebanon with groups like \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst live in our Middle East newsroom. Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A declassified U.S. intelligence report released today states that Saudi Arabia's Crown Prince was aware of and likely approved the killing of U.S. based journalist Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 The Washington Post writer was murdered inside the Saudi consulate in Istanbul in 2018. Saudi Arabia says the report has unjustified an inaccurate conclusion. Khashoggi was a critic of Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 's authoritarian consolidation of power.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about the Khashoggi report, the U.S. airstrikes in Syria and the next steps with Iran and this administration with Norman Roule. He is a former National Intelligence manager for Iran, and a former senior CIA Middle East expert. Norm, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 NORMAN ROULE, NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE MANAGER FOR IRAN: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's start with the strikes and the administration saying it's essentially a warning shot. Your thoughts on the strategic importance of it or the criticism they're taking for it. Where -- what about this strike?\par \par \uc2 ROULE: So, let me explain what's happened here. In this case, the United States has either been attacked or received information of an attack and has responded with public and private warnings to Iran, not to undertake those attack, Iran failed to do so.\par \par \uc2 And we then responded with an action that demonstrates that we're not going to allow Iran to destabilize Iraq, but we're demonstrating our power and our intelligence and our capacity to strike those who are behind the attack.\par \par \uc2 Here's the question, will Iran respond by getting the message or just taking it up another step on the escalatory ladder to test our will in the long run?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, meantime, as this is happening, as I just mentioned with Trey, the administration's clearly making moves to get back into some sort of nuclear deal. There were reports that some members of the administration were kind of talking to the Iranians, including Secretary Kerry, as the Trump administration was in office. Thoughts about that and what it means for the strategic Middle East geopolitically?\par \par \uc2 ROULE: Well, first, diplomacy is always a good thing. This said, engagement thus far with Iran has not been producing I believe what the administration seeks. In addition to arriving with the -- with the idea that they would engage with Iran and return to the nuclear deal, if possible, the administration has also been sympathetic to Iranian requests for funding through the \uc2 IMF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look what we've seen in response, attacks on U.S. troops in Iraq, an attack today on an Israeli vessel in the Gulf of Oman that looks like the IRGC. Iran is refusing to cooperate with the \uc2 IAEA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thus far, Iran is testing our fortitude and seeing how far it can push the Biden administration and the international community for additional concessions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, I want to read from this intelligence report about Saudi, the assessing the Saudi government's role in the killing of Jamal Khashoggi. Saudi Arabia's Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  approved an operation in Istanbul, Turkey to capture or kill Saudi journalist Jamal Khashoggi. The Crown Prince viewed Khashoggi as a threat to the Kingdom and broadly supported using violent measures if necessary, to silence him.\par \par \uc2 You know, I asked the foreign minister about this back in October of 2018. Here's an exchange ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're an authoritarian government. You're saying that the Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  did not know about this at all, even though there are members of this 15-person team that are closely aligned with him?\par \par \uc2 ADEL AL-JUBEIR, FORMER MINISTER OF FOREIGN AFFAIRS OF SAUDI ARABIA: Well, Bret, first of all, we're not an authoritarian government, we're a monarchy. We have our checks and balances. We have our systems. The individuals who did this did this outside the scope of their authority. This is an aberration. This is a mistake. This is a criminal act and those responsible for it will be punished.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To the original question, did the Crown Prince know anything about it if there were people closely tied to him involved in this operation?\par \par \uc2 AL-JUBEIR: There weren't people closely tied to him were involved in this operation. There were pictures of some security officers who may have been part of the security detail from time to time, but this is normal. This was an operation that was a rogue operation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, this administration choosing to declassify this report, not seeing any strict punishments for MBS, the Crown Prince. Your thoughts on this and the fallout in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 ROULE: Well, the administration has achieved today several things. First, this is another step in its evolution of resetting relations with Saudi Arabia. In recent weeks, we've seen the president denied the opportunity to speak with the Crown Prince himself, they ended an arms deal. They've also cut back on support of with Saudi Arabia or regarding Yemen.\par \par \uc2 But this report makes it very clear, the U.S. intelligence community believes or assesses that the Crown Prince played a decision-making role in this action.\par \par \uc2 I should note, this report provides no evidence. It appears to be based on intelligence community's understanding of how Saudi Arabia works less than the actual information.\par \par \uc2 I should also note that despite this report, there have been routine discussions between the United States' leadership and Saudi Arabia on multiple security issues within the last few weeks. The Secretary of State, the Secretary of Defense, the president himself have all engaged their counterparts. And the very influential Saudi ambassador to the United States has been in routine contact I understand with the State Department and the National Security Council.\par \par \uc2 So, the reset continues, but the broader relations between the United States and Riyadh on issues of importance to each of our countries does go on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And just quickly, obviously, the last administration used the Gulf states to kind of do a different way to Middle East peace and we're having some success, obviously, significant success. Does this change that? Does this administration take an opposite view with not only Iran, but the Gulf states?\par \par \uc2 ROULE: Not regarding the Gulf States and Israel. This administration will attempt to further the success of the Trump administration, which was significant and historic. But I think that success will take some time to develop.\par \par \uc2 I also believe the administration will push the Gulf States to somehow engage Iran and they're going to be reluctant to do so since they're under a routine attack by Iran's Kurds' force.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Norman Roule, we really appreciate your perspective always. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROULE: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're expecting a vote sometime tonight in the House of Representatives on the president's coronavirus stimulus package, we mentioned that earlier. But there is still uncertainty over one of the major items in the legislation, an increase in the federal minimum wage. Congressional correspondent Chad Pergram tells us where things stand tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Tonight's vote is the first test for President Biden's legislative agenda and a test for Democrats to see if they can stick together in a divided Congress.\par \par \uc2 The Senate's procedure umpire disqualified inclusion of a minimum wage increase in this COVID package. The ruling rock liberals. Some argue Democrats should ignore Senate parliamentarian Elizabeth MacDonough.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: 32 million Americans are being denied a raise because of a decision of the non-elected parliamentarian. She has an incredible integrity. She's not to blame.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Dropping the wage increase from the bill could actually grease the skids to pass it in the Senate. Democrats Kyrsten Sinema and Joe Manchin opposed including the wage requirement in this bill. Democrats need their votes.\par \par \uc2 But the exclusion of the minimum wage increase reveals the political realities of the progressive agenda. This could rip at the seams of the Democratic coalition as the party struggles to pass liberal priorities like immigration reform and climate change policy in a split Congress.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY): I think if you're looking for a real Civil War, look at the Democrats in the House.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republicans are in near lockstep opposing the bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): We already know what is the best stimulus plan out there. It is to fully reopen our economy. To do