r \par \uc2 First, Beyond Our Borders tonight. Unidentified gunmen seized more than 300 girls in a nighttime raid on a school in Nigeria. Police say they believe some of the girls are being held in a forest nearby. It's the second such kidnapping and a little over a week in a region increasingly targeted by militants and criminal gangs.\par \par \uc2 A dozen Palestinian protesters clashed with Israeli security forces in the West Bank city of Hebron. The violence started after Friday prayers when Palestinians marched through the Israeli controlled city center holding banners demanding the removal of Jewish settlers. Troops responded with stun grenades and tear gas.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's prime minister says her government is backing a new project using drone technology to understand and protect endangered Maui dolphins. The animals live in a small stretch of the ocean off of the west coast of New Zealand's north island. Current estimates suggest only 63 dolphins older than one year remain, raising concerns that they may soon be extinct.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond Our Borders tonight. We will be right back here in D.C.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now to a story about how technology and the celebrity culture are fooling a lot of people and causing quite a fuss over a one of the world's top movie stars.\par \par \uc2 It involves something called a "deepfake." Actor Tom Cruise in a kind of deception that could potentially turn out to be, in some cases, extremely dangerous. Here's correspondent Gillian Turner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Deepfake videos of Hollywood megastar Tom Cruise doing magic tricks, cracking jokes, tripping, and golfing, are going viral on social media at TikTok. They are pretty realistic looking, or as some users are comments, scary good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hey listen up sports and TikTok fans, if you like what you're seeing, just wait until what's coming next.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The last celebrity deepfake to go viral was a video of Queen Elizabeth's Christmas Day speech. She gives it annually from Buckingham Palace.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Which brings me back to that question of trust, of whether what we see and hear is always as it seems.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The deepfake version included jokes about Prince Harry and \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2 's move to L.A. But deepfakes are no laughing matter. A \uc2 University College London\uc2  report calls them the most dangerous form of crime through artificial intelligence. And cybersecurity experts warn Americans should beware.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN WRIGHT, CYBERSECURITY EXPERT: What if a president four years from now, somebody has video of a president, man or woman, saying something inflammatory. The mere perception that this got out and that you have to spend all this time walking it back, that is too much time between the time it's said in the time somebody perceives that's what you said. That becomes their reality and they take action on that.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Deepfakes use what's called deep learning, a type of artificial intelligence, to take existing videos, like this well-known one that appears to show President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , and replaces the people in them with other people's faces and even bodies, making it appear they are saying and doing things they never did.\par \par \uc2 People live more and more of their lives online where their content can be captured and exploited by pretty much anyone with an Internet connection. So experts insist if you are looking to avoid becoming the next deepfake victim, protection is key.\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: As technology progresses, so does crime, and so does the ability to create these deceptions, which at some point it will get to be, is it the real thing or is it a fake?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Congress has taken very little action to regulate the use of deepfakes online. But they did recently direct the Secretary of Homeland Security to produce an annual report outlining the use of deepfakes over the next five years. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian, thank you. Next election could be interesting.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the panel with reaction to the U.S. airstrikes in Syria, the latest on Capitol Hill, CPAC, and winners and losers, if we can squeeze it all in.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It was a proportionate military response. The president is sending an unambiguous message that he is going to act to protect Americans, and when threats are posed, he has the right to take an action at the time and in the manner of his choosing.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: I hope it just wasn't bombs in the desert. I hope it really went after something that threatened the United States of America. If not, it's useless.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, what message were you sending to Iran with your first military action?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You can't act with impunity. Be careful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The air strikes in Syria killing one, wounding three, apparently, getting some criticism from both sides of the aisle, although \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  just put out a statement saying he supports the president's authority to do what he did. But Tim Kaine, senator from Virginia, "The American people deserve to hear the administration's rationale for these strikes and its legal justification for acting without coming to Congress. Offensive military action without Congressional approval is not constitutional absent extraordinary circumstances. Congress must be fully briefed on this matter expeditiously.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, CEO of Empowerment and Inclusion Capital, and Steve Hayes, editor of "The Dispatch." Steve, with what we know about this strike, what was behind it, striking out at Iranian militias, what do you think of the action and what it means for the Middle East?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HAYES, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Look, I think this is a pretty big deal. How many times, Bret, over the Obama administration did we talk about President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's efforts to decouple talks with Iran about its nuclear program and pursuit of the Iranian nuclear deal with all of the other actions, malign actions, of the Iranian regime.\par \par \uc2 And basically, the Obama administration's approach was nothing is going to get in the way of us executing this deal, us coming to a deal. Ben Rhodes announced that early. It was what the Obama administration prioritized throughout the second term.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is sending a message pretty early that he won't decouple these things, that he will consider Iran's trouble making in the region as he looks to get back into the nuclear deal. I don't think we should be surprised that he still prioritizes getting back into the nuclear deal, but this was, I think, a welcome expression of some distance between his policy and President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and, Trey, Tim Kaine, to his credit, has had this stance for a long time, saying congressional action, congressional approval is needed, or at least briefing. They want a new authorization of use of military force. Don't know if that is going to happen in Congress.\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , (R) FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: Yes, I applaud him for being consistent, even if he is wrong. He mentioned extraordinary circumstances. The extraordinary circumstances, Bret, are that no one in Congress can keep their mouth shut. So if you notify them ahead of time, they are going to leak it. And if you debate it on the floor of the House and the Senate, then you lose the element of surprise.\par \par \uc2 And thirdly, and arguably most importantly, there is never a consequence when the executive branch outpaces its branch authority. The legislative branch never does anything to the executive branch. So, if you are the president, you are not going before you do it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Now, as far as what is happening on Capitol Hill, Harold, take a listen to the debate about the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you're making less than $15 an hour, you are living below the poverty wage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But that may not be in your American rescue plan --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No. I put it in, but I don't think it is going to survive.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: This is for a progressive wing of the party who says they will not vote for the bill unless they have that in there. All the studies show you are actually going to lose more jobs.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN DEESE, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: We were disappointed by the parliamentarian's ruling. We believe it's a justified, in fact urgently needed step forward. Passing the minimum wage would give a raise to 27 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So the Senate parliamentarian essentially says it doesn't fit in budget reconciliation, but the House says, you know what, we don't care. We're going to put it in there anyway, and they're going to vote on it tonight.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR. (D) FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: Thanks for having me on. I'd first say the reset of our foreign policy is welcome, and I agree with everything that was said prior.\par \par \uc2 With regard to minimum wage, the parliamentarian is following the rules, and I think President Biden would be wise to follow those rules and to follow through on the other thing that he promised most during this last campaign, the vaccine distribution and the COVID relief package would find their way to states, localities, and everyday households and main street businesses, which is what it will do.\par \par \uc2 As we well know, Florida passed a minimum wage increase bill that was a referendum on the Florida ballot. What I can imagine happening, Bret, in the weeks and months is that Democrats rally much like Republicans have over the years to perhaps get minimum wage increase referendums on state ballots all across the country in 2022. Let's let states make this decision. So many of my old Republican colleagues, former by that when I say "old," has suggested this should be a state by state, region by region issue. Let's put it on the ballot and let voters make that determination.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, pass the COVID-19 relief package. Let's get schools reopened, our economy reopened, and people back to working again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve, there is just concern about what is in there and whether it is actually going to hit the COVID relief that they say it is going to hit.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I think people are right to have concerns about that. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  asked rhetorically the other day what should he cut, and people came forward with any number of programs, many of which were not at all related to COVID-19 and not at all related to relief.\par \par \uc2 You have this $1.9 trillion package, and on the heels of it you are hearing rumblings of the Biden administration with an additional multitrillion dollar infrastructure deal. At some point people have to care about the debt. They have to care about the spending, and if they pass this $1.9 trillion tonight and it becomes, it eventually becomes law, we are going to be in a much, much deeper hole.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to head down to CPAC, take a listen to a lot of Florida talk down there about COVID. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS\uc2 , (R) FLORIDA: In Florida, schools are open. Every Floridian has a right to earn a living and all businesses have a right to operate. Florida got it right, and the lockdown states got it wrong.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): We will not win the future by trying to go back to where the Republican Party used to be. If we do, we will lose the working base that President Trump so animated. We're going to lose elections across the country, and ultimately, we're going to lose our nation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): Let me tell you this right now, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  ain't going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just a little flavor, former president speaking Sunday. That will be one to watch. All right, quickly, Winners and Losers around the horn. Trey, first to you, winner then loser.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Winner, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . Anytime the Senate parliamentarian gets more press than you do, you have had a great confirmation week. The loser, women who accuse prominent Democrats, because the standard is completely different if you accuse a prominent Democrat than if you accuse, say, I don't know, \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Harold, winner and loser.\par \par \uc2 FORD: The winner is President Biden resetting our policy, prioritizing our intelligence over that of adversaries. Congratulations to him.\par \par \uc2 Losers, I congratulate CPAC for talking about cancel culture, but those there, the losers are people who continue to say that President Trump had the election stolen from him. Move on, focus on ideas. The country needs it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve, winner and loser.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: My winner is Lucia DeClerck, New Jersey nursing home resident, 105 years old who survived COVID, she says by eating gin-soaked raisins. Not sure that has been proven, but kudos to her anyway.\par \par \uc2 My loser is \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 . He has been investigated for hiding nursing home deaths, he is bottom 10 in vaccine administration, and now he has got the sexual harassment charges that Trey mentioned earlier.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen, enjoy some gin-soaked raisins this weekend. You guys have a good one. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally tonight, it is Friday. That means "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I thought they would probably take better care of us since when you don't pay your electric bill they're going to cut it off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mayday, mayday.\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , DIRECTOR, NATIONAL INSTITUTE OF ALLERGY AND INFECTIOUS DISEASES: We want to make sure that people continue to wear masks despite the fact that they are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: I think these lockdowns that we've had, Katie, and the closing of the schools are the worst public policy decisions of my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These children are political hostages. They want to go back to school. We are so fed up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When a person comes forward, they deserve to be treated with dignity and respect.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There is no evidence of any impairment whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He's going to be back. I have no doubt about it. He's going to be back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY\uc2 , (R-LA): I'm not saying she's a smoked turkey, but the smoker is warming up.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): I have got to say, Orlando is awesome. It's not as nice as Cancun.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are confident in the target that we went after. We know what we hit.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't remember "The Muppets" ever being offensive, but here we are again, cancel culture.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I want you to get \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's answer on Mr. Potato Head going genderless.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If the president was the party's nominee, would you support him?\par \par \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This nation will smile again. This nation will know sunny days again.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA) HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: On that high note.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One week.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. FOX NEWS PRIMETIME hosted by Katie Pavlich, and I took a couple seconds, Katie, starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020210227eh2q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Photos from Mars; Manhattan District Attorney Gets President Trump's Tax Returns; Biden Administration to Issue Report on Jamal Khashoggi Killing; Biden Touts Vaccine Rollout; Minimum Wage Fight; Interview with Former OMB Director \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2 ; Stocks Retreat as Interest Rates Spike\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Dan Hoffman, Blake Burman, Chad Pergram, Rich Edson,  Gary Kaltbaum, Charlie Gasparino \par \uc2 8,948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, we are chronicling a big sell-off at the corner of Wall and Broad. And I know this sounds a little weird. The bad news there, the good news here in the economy. It's simply too good to be true.\par \par \uc2 That had interest rates backing up today. The fact of the matter is, we were pelted with one good piece of economic news after another, that jobless claims registered 730,000. Most thought that they would be at least 100,000 more, that we were seeing durable goods orders that you order ahead of time and then make a big commitment continuing to move higher, six-month high.\par \par \uc2 The fourth quarter GDP ended up being a lot stronger than originally reported. Then we got news on the virus front. I mean, that is really dramatically improving across the board. The number of cases is what is startling people, because they're declining.\par \par \uc2 But I'm ending here showing you a little chart. If we can go back to that, guys, because, well, a lot of people know that I'm sort of like the doctor of dull. And I like to bring the boring stuff.\par \par \uc2 This legitimizes it for me, because I want to point your attention to a chart. It's not sexy. It's not riveting. But what it tells you is that there is a narrowing going on, what you get investing in treasuries and the rate you get on those -- they have been backing up -- and what you get dividends on the S&P 500, a popular market average.\par \par \uc2 It's been narrowing to the point right now that, if we just took a look at that last year, they're touching each other. Here's the big deal with that. The good news was prompting interest rates to back up. Very few people saw the economy churning to the degree it has and is.\par \par \uc2 That led interest rates to go back, back, back, back, back. At the same time, we're really not seeing much in terms of dividends, what you get for buying stock in the S&P 500. That's been kind of like a constant.\par \par \uc2 And you always got more bang for your buck investing in stocks, because interest rates were near zero. They're not near zero anymore. Market rates are backing up because of the improving economy, as I said.\par \par \uc2 And now, all of a sudden, there is an alternative to stocks, at least for people just leery of losing their money. You have the full faith of Uncle Sam backing up your investment. That's the fear. Good news was bad news. It was an inflection point today that bears watching.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know I'm giving you a lot of gobbledygook and a lot of charts. Sometimes, they legitimize my existence here, but I do it for another reason. This was an important day because we have not seen something like this since way back in 2016.\par \par \uc2 What's going on here? Why is good news bad news and people feel all of a sudden that maybe we don't need as big a stimulus package. Maybe, if we get one, it will be good money after bad money. Maybe, maybe, maybe things are improving enough on their own that we don't need any more out of Washington.\par \par \uc2 Hard to say. This much is very easy to say. Right now, the news is so good that Wall Street really wonders, well, is it worth taking a pass on stocks now, when you can invest in things like Treasury notes and bonds, and get as much, if not more, for your money. That's the fear. But that's also the hope. It's weird, I know.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is your money world.\par \par \uc2 Today, I want to, first of all, get right to it with Ted Weisberg of \uc2 Seaport Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ted, when we look at that, I know we were looking at a lot of years, but that development in interest rates is interesting, because, all of a sudden, there is potential competition for stocks. And that prompted a good deal of this selling. Is that competition legitimate to you? Is that a genuine worry?\par \par \uc2 I guess what I'm asking, should be we worried about good news?\par \par \uc2 TED WEISBERG, PRESIDENT, \uc2 SEAPORT SECURITIES CORPORATION\uc2 : Well, it's a bit of a dilemma, Neil, because the age-old adage in Wall Street is, don't fight the Fed.\par \par \uc2 And the Fed as recently as today and yesterday, are basically telling us that they're on hold, and nothing is going to change in the short term. But the complex and the puzzling issue is that the stock market is basically sending a different message. And which is the message that you want to pay attention to?\par \par \uc2 I think it's a mistake to not pay attention to Mr. Market, but it's probably also was a mistake not to pay attention to the Fed. So, this is a real dilemma.\par \par \uc2 Now, this happens to come with all the popular averages at all-time highs. So, if you were a technician, Neil, you could probably we say, yes, certainly, the 10-year is sending a nervous signal to the markets about interest rates moving higher, but the economy doing better has been predicted for many, many months.\par \par \uc2 So, this shouldn't come as a big surprise. And you counter this with the Fed's position that they really intend not to make any changes in the near term. And so, we have seen interest rates moving higher. I mean, yesterday, we had a terrific day, plus day in the market, and today, of course, a complete reversal. Yesterday, the market was ignoring that 10-year bond. Today, it's not.\par \par \uc2 I mean, have we gotten to a point which is a tipping point, or have what we have seen is a technical reaction from an overbought market that's had a big dramatic move? And one day sell-off, even though it's 500-some-odd points, doesn't make a bear market.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I want to do something here, because I don't think that chart showed it.\par \par \uc2 And, Robert, I don't know if you can see this. I'm throwing this at you right now. This is, guys -- I'm sorry -- our chart that we showed goes over many years, and it doesn't illustrate my point.\par \par \uc2 I do want to point this out, though, not to get nerdy and wonky with you. This is the gap that you have between the yield on a 10-year Treasury now. Mortgages are attached to that. A whole lot of borrowing is attached to that. And what you get right now is a dividend average on the S&P 500.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know that looks like gobbledygook, and I'm probably doing a free commercial for Apple. I apologize for that. But do you notice something, what's going on here? That it's narrowing, that they're closing together? In the beginning, you got a lot more bang for your buck simply keeping your money in stocks.\par \par \uc2 Now there's some competition. They're touching each other. And that has not happened in a long, long time. Now, I apologize for getting all highfalutin with you and all, but that's what's going on. That's the very big deal. And that's something that bears watching.\par \par \uc2 So, we're going to look at that in a little bit more detail after Ted with Gary Kaltbaum.\par \par \uc2 But before I leave you, Ted, that is the multi-gazillion-dollar question, right, whether this trend continues and gives people a chance to decide, all right, I have got huge gains in this market. I don't want to risk it. So, maybe, maybe I will slowly retreat, lock in what I have got.\par \par \uc2 I guess what I'm asking, does this have staying power?\par \par \uc2 WEISBERG: Well, the answer is -- the answer is, as the economy improves, and as we have more vaccines and less negative issues from COVID, I think the knee-jerk reaction is going to be quite positive for the economy.\par \par \uc2 And it would only be natural that we would see interest rates start to move higher. But we have to weigh that with what the Fed is seeing. I suspect the market is probably telling us a better story here than perhaps the Fed is. But we have learned year after year, decade after decade is, don't fight the Fed.\par \par \uc2 And as long as the Fed stays in an accommodative mode, and if, fundamentally, nothing really changes, Neil, I think, at least in the very short term, yes, we're having a negative reaction in the market to the 10- year bond moving higher, but it's also coming with a market that's overbought, at all-time highs. This could be nothing more than a technical pullback.\par \par \uc2 And let's keep in mind, stocks always go down faster than they go up. And if they go down fast and sharp here and hard, it could, in fact, end up being more of a buying opportunity than a selling opportunity.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you, my friend, Ted Weisberg.\par \par \uc2 To Gary Kaltbaum right now.\par \par \uc2 And I know I'm getting into the weeds here. Some of you watch this. But you're used to dull stuff from me, right? I mean, I invented the stuff. I am the chief nerd here.\par \par \uc2 Gary is almost as nerdy as me.\par \par \uc2 But, Gary, I want to play this out a little bit more, so folks can appreciate why good news can be bad news, the improving economy, the rapid rush of vaccinations getting to market, the fact that the more that do reach the market, the sooner we get out of this COVID pandemic disaster, and the economy can brighten up.\par \par \uc2 We have already seen signs of that, with a number of big retailers pointing to optimistic developments on their front, seeing things improve, \uc2 Costco\uc2  raising the $16 minimum wage, that this is a trend that's picking up.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering now how that affects stimulus in Washington, because, remember, that nearly $2 trillion in stimulus was meant to address a serious economic drawback. Now we might not need it, right?\par \par \uc2 And then are they at loggerheads deciding, is that good or bad news?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: First off, Neil, there is absolutely nothing dull about you.\par \par \uc2 The first few minutes of the show have been a master class on things a lot of people don't know, so very good stuff.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Look, the stimulus is coming no matter what.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Could we book him again? Get Gary Kaltbaum back.\par \par \uc2 But go ahead.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: The Dems have the upper hand here.\par \par \uc2 We will get a big stimulus. It's just more and more spending. I wish it was more direct to the things that need it. There's a few too much stuff that's what they call pork. But it's coming. And it helps the economy. It goes towards GDP.\par \par \uc2 And you're seeing it in some things in the markets right now. The market is coming down, I would suggest, because we have been on a sea of easy money. But the market itself right now is tightening, while the Fed sits back and keeps printing.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's a little bit confusion there, and the market is doing the bidding. And just remember, when interest rates go up, growth stocks tend to come down. They are leading down right now. A whole host of Nasdaq names, including the big ones, have broken support. I saw a ton more today.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we may have a little bit of trend to the downside here on things. The best places to be will be the nerdy places right now. The opening up trade will hold up better than most as the economy gets stronger. But the worry is, as interest rates go up, yes, as you say, the competition for money is vital and important in this market right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gary, thank you very much, my friend. You are so coming back after saying those nice things. So thank you, again, Gary Kaltbaum.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Take care.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I have known the man for decades, as I knew Ted, as I have known this guy, from afar originally, but now we're working together.\par \par \uc2 So it's all the better, Charlie Gasparino.\par \par \uc2 And, Charlie, you and I were kicking this around on FOX Business, which, if you don't get, you should demand. And one of the things that came up was, what is bad about good news? And the part that's bad, if you call it that, is that interest rates back up a tad. They're still very low. You and I can remember when they were a little bit higher, but the trend is up.\par \par \uc2 And while that's good to get a little inflation, we want to see a little inflation percolate, it's bad if it keeps accelerating and keeps offering a potential alternative to stocks and keeps people thinking, well, wait a minute, maybe we don't need all this stimulus out of Washington.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of things it raises that we haven't had to think about in the better part of a generation, right?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Right, inflation, and massive, massive deficits and whether the deficit will finally at some point matter.\par \par \uc2 Remember, I think let we're talking about a trillion to $4 trillion, right? I mean, that's a big -- a big jump in terms of in terms of the deficit. And that's pandemic-related and all the spending and you name it and the economy slowing down.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do we even need it right, Charlie? Did this raise a question?\par \par \uc2 Because I think there's a battle between those on Wall Street. They love stimulus. They love it, no matter how expensive or how much it plows us deeper into debt and all.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Some do, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They're all in.\par \par \uc2 But you're quite right, not all.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There are others who worry, why throw good money after bad when we have got signs that things are looking pretty good, right?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Listen, nobody knows right now. And it's -- that's the scary part. I mean, we're here to give answers.\par \par \uc2 I can just tell you, there's a debate going on in the market. There's a debate that is starting to percolate up that inflation might matter. Why is that debate starting to percolate? Doesn't mean it's going to happen. Teddy Weisberg might be totally right. This is a buying opportunity.\par \par \uc2 But here's why it's percolating up. We have a vaccine. We have -- even though we had a pandemic, we had -- we had problems in the economy that were very narrow. Like, most people were working from home and doing OK.\par \par \uc2 There was a segment and I would say a minority segment of the population, that was devastated by this, hotel, hospital workers, hotel and -- hotel and hospitality workers. There's a massive amounts of stimulus being thrown at the problem, at the issue, giving money to people that probably don't need it, Neil, on top of the fact that we have vaccines and the pandemic looks to be almost over. It looks. Who knows?\par \par \uc2 I'm just saying that's part of -- if you put all that together...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Absolutely. Anything can happen.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Anything can happen.\par \par \uc2 If you put all that together, you do have a scenario where there could be massive inflation, where people are going to spend a lot of money, people that are sitting on savings, they didn't really go out and spend it, will spend it. And you have on top of all that record low interest rates. And you have lots of -- lots of issues involving record low interest rates.\par \par \uc2 You have massive stock speculation. As you saw, \uc2 GameStop\uc2  was all -- been over the place. It's back up again miraculously, after falling down to levels that may be where it should be trading. This is a company that's not doing very good, almost traded at $500 a share, came down to $40. It's almost back up to $200 today, although it came off its highs.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is all the sort of stuff that goes on in one of these crazy markets. And I can just tell the viewer this. We don't know.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: We do know that there's a debate going on. And the debate now centers on inflation, whether we're going to get it or not. There's some good...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bingo