e able to do it.\par \par \uc2 He has succeeded at that. But I'm wondering here whether the equation is changing. We have been reporting on this now for a long time, this uptick in activity, commodity prices, anything from food ingredients...\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You might be seeing it at the store. I know you have a SWAT team of people, Charlie, who shop for you, but that they might be seeing it, and it's real. That's real. It's a good thing if it's contained. But it's a bad thing if that chart gets out of whack, right?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes, I mean, we should point out, the Fed can control the long- term rates and has been trying to do that through bond purchases. It actually goes in market and does bond purchases.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. There's a limit, though. There's a limit.\par \par \uc2 They committed to doing that.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And now, even in the face of that promise, the markets seem to be testing it.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Rebelling against it and saying, no, we don't agree with that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And so, listen, it's -- there is no magic bullet here. It'll be interesting to see what goes on.\par \par \uc2 I think the smartest thing to do from a public policy standpoint, if you're worried about inflation, is to pare back this $1.9 trillion thing, stimulus package, direct it to get needles in people's arms, and giving money to people that need it, like the people that are long term out of work, like your restaurant worker, your bartender, not computer programmers that are working.\par \par \uc2 If they did that, that might go a long way into sort of calming the markets about inflation, because this is a lot of money being thrown out at a problem that could be over in a month. And that's what's priced in.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, it might not be over in a month. I don't know.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. We don't know.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: If this vaccine works, it would be.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... because we have not seen this in the better part of five years.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's why we're watching.\par \par \uc2 But, Charlie, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 There's one final element to this today. And you will be quizzed later.\par \par \uc2 But, Charlie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, good news is bad news. I don't know how I can't stress that enough. But I have got a little fancy chart update for you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: The economy is good. That's a smiley face. The markets are bummed out and sad because the economy's good, so they have a sad face.\par \par \uc2 That is where we stand. And that is what's causing a bit of a conundrum. And that is what could be holding up stimulus, because a lot of people are saying, hey, we're doing OK. Do we need to spend $2 trillion?\par \par \uc2 Wait until I draw the next suspenseful chart.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Could this get to be a nasty back-and-forth over whether you can or can't include a wage hike in a reconciliation bill?\par \par \uc2 SEN. SHELLEY MOORE CAPITO (R-WV): Well, it's a technical question that the parliamentarian's going to answer here whenever, I mean, any minute now.\par \par \uc2 I think it is expected to drop out. The vice president, when we were talking with him -- I mean, the president, when we were talking with him, expects it to drop out.\par \par \uc2 We need to have a broader discussion on minimum wage. I think it's time to relook at it. We haven't looked at it since 2007, where I voted for it then.\par \par \uc2 But do I think it's going to get down to the point where you bring Vice President Harris in to break a tie, and you raise the minimum wage, and you go against the parliamentarian? I don't think we're going to