cans for them, all Democrats against them?\par \par \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2  with us now, the former Office of Management Budget director under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Director, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 If it goes that way again, it will follow a pattern in Washington on major pieces of legislation, where it's one party or the other, but not both. Does that concern you?\par \par \uc2 RUSSELL VOUGHT\uc2 , FORMER OMB DIRECTOR: It does.\par \par \uc2 It makes each and everything that we try to do in this town to improve the lives of the American people harder. And I think it's going to make the Biden administration's life a lot harder as a result of a party-line vote, because they want to turn and unfortunately move towards another big spending bill either on infrastructure or climate spending.\par \par \uc2 And so this is the first of many spending bills. And we can't afford it as it is right now. But it -- this is going to be a crucial moment in the Senate to see whether this is going to be party-line or not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Still, there was a lot of spending under the Trump administration. You were there for that. I'm not blaming, as much as saying it's sort of like a bipartisan buying binge pattern.\par \par \uc2 You eventually have to pay this money back, right?\par \par \uc2 VOUGHT: We do.\par \par \uc2 And under the Trump administration, we were putting forth many budgets that proposed a lot of trillions of dollars in spending savings and reforms. Unfortunately, we dealt with a pandemic and an economic shock in which it was a bipartisan view that we needed to throw everything we could at the problem.\par \par \uc2 But the challenge that we have right now is, we're on the way out of it. And why would we spend $1.9 trillion when there's a trillion dollars that has not been spent? Why would we have a liberal boondoggle for blue states on -- at a time when we have -- it's only 9 percent COVID-related, and we can't afford it any longer as a country?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Still, to be fair, Director, even before the pandemic, roughly a year ago, you guys were spending a lot of money. And even with the economic boom, we were seeing deficits worsen.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering whether there is any way the two parties can find a way to address this systemic problem that has gripped the capital. We're running out of capital.\par \par \uc2 VOUGHT: I hope that there will be a moment when we have a bipartisan view that spending is important to address, that deficits are important.\par \par \uc2 Our approach, the president's approach was always to take the issues as they were most important to the country at the time. We had to get the economy growing with a big tax cut. We had to rebuild the military. Then we had to deal with the reality of the economic shock that we were faced with.\par \par \uc2 But I think you're seeing interest rates ticking up. I think that will get people's attention. That has a historic way of getting people's attention in this town to focus on what we can and cannot afford and ending silly season in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Director, while I have you, I know you will be speaking at CPAC, I believe that not too long before President Trump. There's -- we don't know what the president will be saying, but that Karl Rove and others have recommended now might be a good time to stop relitigating the election.\par \par \uc2 Do you share that view, that this is a chance for the president to look forward, but not focus back on the campaign?\par \par \uc2 VOUGHT: My sense is, the president is always looking forward and that he wants to make sure that, when we have voter fraud reforms, that these are not new. Conservatives have been pushing for voter fraud for years.\par \par \uc2 There was a Baker-Carter Commission that identified mail-in voting as an especially prevalent form of systemic voter fraud. And so, when we're looking forward to reforms...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know that.\par \par \uc2 But his thesis, as you know, Director, is say that he was cheated, he was robbed, that he actually won the election. I'm just curious, do you share that your view?\par \par \uc2 VOUGHT: Neil, I think it's important that we account for the widespread lack of confidence that people have with regard to this election.\par \par \uc2 And it's an important conversation. I'm willing to have that conversation. I think we need to do you look forward to the reforms.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you believe he was cheated, Director? Do you believe the president, if he brings that up -- he's saying that it's -- he was cheated, he was robbed.\par \par \uc2 Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was robbed?\par \par \uc2 VOUGHT: I understand, Neil, where all of the concern is coming for across the country.\par \par \uc2 The president increased his vote by 10 million voters across this country. And he did better in every single state, except where the -- the states that where there was significant concern. And you look at why we weren't able to win in court. It comes from the fact that you don't have a lot of time to prepare these cases.\par \par \uc2 And there was a bipartisan reluctance to look at the evidence. And so looking forward is what we're trying to do. My guess is that's what the president is going to be talking about.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you don't think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the duly elected -- it doesn't matter now, but you don't think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the duly elected winner of that election? Because he got more votes in the end than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ..\par \par \uc2 VOUGHT: I didn't say that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I said that we hadn't -- didn't have an opportunity to really study the evidence and have it presented in courts in a way to give...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, just you personally. I'm sorry, Director. Just you personally.\par \par \uc2 You have worked with him. Do you think that he actually won the election, as he says he has, and he did and he might say again this weekend?\par \par \uc2 VOUGHT: I think the president did very well in the election.\par \par \uc2 I'm not willing to state, to say one way or the other, because we had significant...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 VOUGHT: ... assertions about voter fraud that I believe are very credible.\par \par \uc2 We're trying to move forward to give the American people confidence. And I think that's where we need to be moving towards.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Russell Vought\uc2 , thank you very, very much, the former OMB director under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 To \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2  now, following a push in suddenly back in vogue for earmarks. Remember them?\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson\uc2 , I thought Republicans had buried those. Are they back?\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It appears, Neil.\par \par \uc2 It's been a decade, but it appears as though the earmarks are coming back, or, as some call them, congressionally directed funding. And they may be making an appearance this spring in the House transportation bill. That's according to Chairman \uc2 Peter DeFazio\uc2 , who recently told Punchbowl News that earmarks are likely going to be part of that massive transportation bill.\par \par \uc2 That's on top of earmarks for the regular government funding bills. Many Republicans say they oppose resurrecting the practice.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): It says something as to the priorities of congressional Democrats that, as soon as they get majorities, what they want to do is bring back the corrupt process of earmarks. Earmarks have contributed to the out-of-control spending in Washington. They contribute to the corruption of the Washington swamp.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Earmarks supporters say, by banning earmarks, Congress is actually ceding its congressional and constitutional spending power to the executive branch.\par 