group, we just heard from, New York's \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 . The reason we bring it up, the press secretary, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , asked today if the president will talk to the governor about his handling of nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 You just saw some of that exchange there, Neil. Psaki said that she expects the president will focus instead -- instead, his focus, she said, will center on the pandemic -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you, Blake, very much, Blake Burman at the White House.\par \par \uc2 The White House has been making news of right and left here on a number of other issues that we thought were resolved, but following up on a report that blames the Saudi crown prince in the 2018 killing of Jamal Khashoggi of The Washington Post.\par \par \uc2 And that could mightily change our relationship with the Saudis and what punishment might be meted out on the part of the new administration, if there's punishment at all.\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman is a former CIA chief of station, FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Dan, we seem to have finger the Saudi crown prince, that he was behind this. Now what are we going to do with this? What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 DAN HOFFMAN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I think that's the penultimate question, frankly.\par \par \uc2 We already know that the CIA concluded with a high level of confidence that \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , the crown prince, ordered that gruesome dismembering assassination of Jamal Khashoggi back in 2018. And the Senate voted unanimously. They passed a resolution in 2019 condemning the crown prince for that act.\par \par \uc2 The question, I think, before the Biden administration is how they handle Saudi Arabia's human rights abuses, while, at the same time, recognizing that Saudi Arabia is an ally of ours in the counterterrorism fight, is a bulwark against Iran, as well as Chinese and Russian influence in the region.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so we don't want to go too far, I guess, is the gist of what you're saying, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, they're an important business partner. They have been very, very instrumental in these breakthroughs we have made with a number of countries that either recognize or want to do business with Israel. So, how far do you go?\par \par \uc2 If this guy was fingered in the killing of a U.S. journalist, and you know about it, and it looks like it's true, how far do you push it?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: I mean, that's the diplomatic challenge.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: We want to expand the Trump administration's success, the Abraham Accords. We also want to support Saudi Arabia's Saudi vision 2030, which is meant to diversify their economy, and while, at the same time, we need to hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 Look, they reportedly launched assassination hit teams against a dissident in Norway, as well as Dr. Saad al-Jabri in Canada. He was a Minister of Interior, highly valued counterterrorism partner of the United States. So we kind of have to do both.\par \par \uc2 I'd like to hear from Secretary of State Blinken what the plan is. We need to do both, hold them accountable, but, at the same time, hold them close and be good allies. And that's a challenge, but that's what I think the administration needs to do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Dan, very good catching up with you, my friend, I'm sorry, under these circumstances.\par \par \uc2 But you're right. This is really like a live grenade here. Who tosses it and how?\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman, a former CIA chief at station, of course, FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot more coming up. As you know, the president lost his fight in the Supreme Court to keep his tax returns private. Right now, the New York district attorney has them, millions of pages of documents in their hands. Now what?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now the Southern District Court of New York has them. Right now, all of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's tax returns from roughly 2011 to 2019 in \uc2 Cyrus Vance Jr\uc2 's hands. He's the Manhattan DA who is conducting a wide- ranging probe of the Trump family business.\par \par \uc2 So, Tom Dupree was kind enough to join us to discuss what happens next.\par \par \uc2 Tom, what do you think? Obviously, there's a lot to go over there. What's the procedure now?\par \par \uc2 TOM DUPREE, FORMER JUSTICE DEPARTMENT OFFICIAL: Well, the procedure, now that the district attorney has got these documents that he has sought for so long, he and his team are going to plunge into them, they're going to be looking at the former president's tax returns.\par \par \uc2 They will probably be looking at his communications with his accountants. And it appears that they're focused on whether or not he committed either tax fraud or some sort of fraud maybe in connection with obtaining loans.\par \par \uc2 But it's going to be a slow process, Neil. There are millions of pages of documents spanning almost a decade. And, as we know, the former president has an extremely complicated financial business, to say the least.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, do you expect -- this is more opinion than a legal type of analysis here, Tom -- that the president will want to get ahead of it? He knows what's in those documents.\par \par \uc2 If there's anything questionable, not that they're necessarily is, should he get ahead of it and say something?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: Yes, he might do that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 But my guess is, at this point, he probably would do better keeping his cards close to his vest, because, look, in a way, it's not like they're looking for a needle in a haystack, but they're not going to understand a lot of this material that they get. It's going to take them time to wade through it, to figure out the details of all these complicated financial transactions.\par \par \uc2 And so I don't think the president or former president would want to say, hey ,look at this deal. Here's what I was trying to do here, or here's why I made this decision.\par \par \uc2 I think he's probably going to sit back. I suspect he will continue being on the offensive against the DA and the -- what he perceives as the political nature of this investigation. But I kind of doubt he's going to want to get into the financial details at this point.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Tom, these kind of documents aren't supposed to leak out. Invariably, they do. There's been a great temptation.\par \par \uc2 I cannot imagine those around Vance Jr.'s office who are trying to get their hot little hands on them. Obviously, there's severe punishments for anyone who even thinks of doing that, but it happens.\par \par \uc2 What do you think here?\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 Look, these are grand jury materials. And so they are treated extremely seriously, extremely confidentially by the law. And you're absolutely right. They cannot and should not be leaked. Are there are people who might have political motives who would want to leak them? Perhaps.\par \par \uc2 But, my gosh, they would be taking an enormous risk if they tried to score political points by unlawfully leaking the president's financial documents. My guess is, if we see these documents, if they see them at the light of day sometime, it will come as part of a court filing by the district attorney, rather than by way of an illegal leak.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Tom Dupree, thank you very much, my friend.\par \par \uc2 And the investigation rolls on now, armed with all of those millions of pages of the president's tax documents from 2011 to 2019.\par \par \uc2 All right, I told you that in the beginning of the show about all the red on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 Much better pictures, all the red on Mars, crystal-clear 4K images that are coming to us from the Red Planet that has a former astronaut saying we have only just begun -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you one of those who goes on a trip, you take a lot of pictures? My family hates it when I do that, because I must take hundreds of them. And they eventually have to be whittled down to a respectable number to show to family and friends.\par \par \uc2 But I'd be right at home on Perseverance here, because it has already set out tens of thousands of images from the Martian surface. Some of them will just blow you away here in their crystal clarity and everything else.\par \par \uc2 Mike Massimino joins us right now, the former NASA astronaut, the author of "Spaceman," \uc2 Intrepid Museum\uc2  space adviser. This guy is the real deal.\par \par \uc2 Mike, an honor to have you. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 These are incredible images, Mike. I cannot believe we're getting them from half-a-galaxy away. Incredible.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MASSIMINO, FORMER NASA ASTRONAUT: Yes, Neil. Thanks a lot for having me, Neil. It's great to be on with you and about such a great topic.\par \par \uc2 Yes, a lot of this was out of the need for science and exploration to be able to get good images, to know what to look into a little bit further, to transfer information back to Earth. And as a result of that science and exploration need, we get a real treat of feeling like we're there with the rover, we're along for the ride because of those images, I feel like.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: As an astronaut yourself, do you say to yourself, gosh, I wish I were there, I wish I were there right now?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Actually, yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: It would be a wonderful place to explore. To be a part of that is -- but this is the next best thing.\par \par \uc2 The truth is, we're not able to get there yet with people, but this is a really big step, not only with the images that we're seeing, the science that's going to be able to take place, but also exploring such an interesting area, a dried-up river delta, a lake bed, where there may be signs of life, maybe water that we could use when we do send people there, and also doing things like taking the oxygen out of the CO2 in the atmosphere, so we could have breathable air when we get there as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's incredible.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: So, yes, I'd love to be there, but we need to take care of those other things before we can do that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You might be surprised to learn, Mike, that a lot of my viewers have volunteered to put me there.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 But can I ask you this? I mean, we're waiting. Obviously, there's a rover and it gets around the surface. There's a little mini helicopter that's going to take wide aerial views. They're going to be able to dig up sediment and soil. That will eventually be carted off to another waiting rocket that will eventually make its way back to Earth.\par \par \uc2 So, this is just staggering in terms of what it could mean. We have never seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: No, we haven't.\par \par \uc2 And it shows the advances in technology, even since the Curiosity rover a few years ago to where we are now...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: ... not only with the images that we have seen, but also with the science capabilities, being able to land where they did.\par \par \uc2 Because they want it to go to an interesting area, it made it a very challenging place to land. And it's -- as you said, it's a long way away. So it had to be done purely autonomously. And they pulled it off.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Really did.\par \par \uc2 But, Mike, I'm curious.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: So, we have really come a long way even in just a few years.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I'm sorry to jump on you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 But I did want to ask you, because circling Mars right now are this spacecraft from United Arab Emirates and China, both very big entrants in space program. In fact, the Chinese are spending at a dramatically higher rate than we are. They're already on the moon. And they are going to have the dark side of the moon, building a lunar station, what have you.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of all the competition?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Well, I hope it turns into cooperation.\par \par \uc2 What we found with the International Space Station program in my years as an astronaut, we worked very closely with the Canadians, with the Japanese, with the countries of Europe, and with the Russians as a major partner.\par \par \uc2 And when you have a shared goal, all of your differences kind of fade away, and as you're trying to explore. And that's what we found with the space station. So, I hope the same as with the moon and with Mars, is that it's a way that countries can cooperate.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's one of the great advantages, one of the great benefits of our space program, is the international cooperation that it brings. And that's for science in general, but I think particularly in the space program. We have seen examples of that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , with his program, is looking for a volunteer -- an all- volunteer crew. Some are paying top dollar for that.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: Well, I think, if -- you asked me if I wanted to go, and my answer is, I'd love to.\par \par \uc2 But I want to come back.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: And I think that's what -- that's what people should be wanting to do.\par \par \uc2 I -- it's one thing -- the one-way trip, I'm not a fan of that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. I hear you.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: I have always liked -- when I got sent to space, I got travel orders, and it said from Kennedy Space Center to low Earth orbit and return.\par \par \uc2 And I was glad to see the return.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And return. And return.\par \par \uc2 MASSIMINO: So, I think having volunteers that want to come back is a good idea.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Mike, thank you very much, Mike Massimino.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, a lot of you are saying, Neil, you needn't return.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210226eh2p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Rep. Lance Gooden (R-TX)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,039 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, HOST: In the meantime, fair and balanced now, we have Lance Gooden joining us. The Texas Republican serves on \uc2 the House Financial Services Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know whether you had a chance to hear some of that with your colleague \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 REP. LANCE GOODEN (R-TX): Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... about finding a way to offer credits and the like to help small businesses absorb that higher minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 GOODEN: Well, I mean, I think it`s a nice gesture.\par \par \uc2 But, at the end of the day, this $15 minimum wage number is too high. I don`t believe it`s going to get bipartisan support. It doesn`t even have all of the support of the Democrats in the Senate. And I think Democrats in the House know that. And so they`re trying to come up with these carrots to get a -- get a little more support on the side of their policy.\par \par \uc2 But what the issue is, I think, is that we have seen this COVID relief bill come forward. Only 9 percent, less than 10 percent of what`s in this bill will actually go to coronavirus relief.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the frustration in the Republican Conference on both sides of the chamber, that so much of this bill is waste. I saw it very interesting proposal from Mr. Romney and Mr. Cotton today with respect to the minimum wage settling on a $10 figure, but also tying it to E-Verify.\par \par \uc2 I would be interested in something like that if we are absolutely set on raising the minimum wage over time, if we tie it to E-Verify and actually have a disincentive for cheaper labor, which is the whole reason we`re talking about this minimum wage in the first place.\par \par \uc2 And I think that might be something that would get some Republican support.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Congressman, you mentioned \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, as you know, he has a column in The Wall Street Journal talking about this package being a clunker. He`s referring to the whole stimulus plan, because so much of it is spending that you don`t need, and a lot of that, despite the trumped-up headlines to get it out there right away, is $700 billion, $800 billion worth that can`t be touched until next year.\par \par \uc2 So, given that, are you a no, as things stand now in the House?\par \par \uc2 GOODEN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 If Senator \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  thinks this is a bad bill, and it`s too liberal for him, you can bet that the rest of the Republican Conference in both chambers is against this bill.\par \par \uc2 And it`s a real shame, because prior coronavirus relief packages in the last year of President Trump`s term were bipartisan. Republicans and Democrats were willing to work together, President Trump was willing to accept a compromise. President Biden has not gone down that path.\par \par \uc2 He wants what he wants. And he wants this high figure. And it`s so bad that not even all Democrats are yet on board. And I think that, as the days progress, back to your bird comment, as people flock toward the Senate, I think that we will see less -- less support in a bipartisan manner, unless we cut some of this pork out of this bill, unless we get away from this $15 minimum wage talk that`s just too high.\par \par \uc2 And it doesn`t do anything to combat the wage earners or -- excuse me -- the low salaries and the low wages that are the reason we`re talking about this increase in the first place.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Congressman, I see your point there, but, I mean, we talk about big pieces of legislation. Oftentimes, there isn`t bipartisan support. In the beginning, for the Trump tax cuts, it was all Republican votes, not a single Democratic vote, for Obamacare, all Democratic votes, not a single Republican vote.\par \par \uc2 And this history exists that, on big pieces of legislation, it`s all or none. Does that disturb you? Does that make you wonder whether bipartisanship will ever return on this and other matters, infrastructure, for which we`re told there is bipartisan support, but it never materializes?\par \par \uc2 GOODEN: No, it doesn`t disturb me, because everything you just mentioned are examples of big policy proposals that were instrumental in the campaigns of the people that got elected.\par \par \uc2 What you`re talking about, though, and what I have been talking about is something in response to a national emergency, a national crisis. And that`s what we`re in right now. We`re trying to defeat the coronavirus. This is not just a tax cut. This is not Obamacare. This is a national crisis.\par \par \uc2 And in the last year, this country has come together. Congress has come together. In the last year of the Trump presidency, we came together. And I think Congress is ready to come together now. And we need President Biden to lead and put forward a proposal that we can come across the aisle and support in a bipartisan manner.\par \par \uc2 This is very different than past give it -- take-it-or-leave-it policies, where there was no bipartisan support. The coronavirus legislation over the last seven to eight months has historically been more or less bipartisan. And I think this should be as well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Congressman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 That one bird analogy was very good, but, maybe to your point, this whole process is for the birds. So, I will try to have the last word on that.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, seriously, very good having you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GOODEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you very...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very, very good.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210224eh2o0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With \uc2 Rep. Ro Khanna\uc2  (D-CA)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 865 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: We have got \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 . We`re putting him under enormous pressure, the Democratic California congressman, how all this passes.\par \par \uc2 Very good to have you, sir. And thank you for taking the time.\par \par \uc2 Any bird analogies are welcome.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But let me ask you, first off.\par \par \uc2 I know you have your own plan to try to find a middle ground on this. But if this is not ultimately part of the president`s COVID stimulus plan, will you be disappointed?\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): Yes, I will be disappointed.\par \par \uc2 The minimum wage is $7.25. I don`t know anyone in this country in any region who can live on $7.25 We ought to raise it. And $15 seems reasonable to me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, you know a number of Republicans are concerned, maybe even some in your own party, that`s going to chase away jobs, a CBO report saying that, to the same effect, 1.4 million of them, if this were to go through.\par \par \uc2 But you are looking at a way to help a lot of the small businesses to absorb that. Could you explain?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So, for the big companies, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , Target, they can pay $15, and they have done it nationally. And there`s no reason that someone in my district should be getting 15 bucks from \uc2 Amazon\uc2  or \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , and someone in rural America shouldn`t be getting $15. So, we -- for the big companies, it makes sense to have a national standard.\par \par \uc2 For small businesses, I understand. Some of them have difficulty, especially with the pandemic. They have higher expenses. And it is a challenge. And that`s why we need three things.\par \par \uc2 One, we need to make sure that there`s a gradual implementation. Two, we should look at tax credits to help them if they really can`t deal with the costs. And, three, one of the things you have to do is give brick-and- mortar businesses tax credits on the sales tax.\par \par \uc2 That`s how \uc2 Amazon\uc2  got as big as it is. They got preferential treatment on sales tax. Why can`t we do that for our mainstream businesses?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, you`re looking for others to join you in this. What`s the latest on that?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, I just reached out to Leader McCarthy. I`m hopeful, on common sense and supporting small businesses, that we can do that, because it is true that small businesses have been hard-hit, especially with the pandemic, and we should find ways to help them.\par \par \uc2 But we shouldn`t allow \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , Target, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  off the hook, and not pay a $15 wage. And the people who say let`s do it regionally, what I don`t understand is, why would you let \uc2 Amazon\uc2  pay lower wages in certain parts of the country and only pay higher wages in California? That doesn`t make sense to me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, is it your sense that, one way or the other, this stimulus measure passes the House, goes on to the Senate, with or without this feature, it`s a done deal, but it might be pretty much along party lines? Is that your gut?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: That is.\par \par \uc2 And it`s unfortunate, because I think the country is desperate for coming together, doing things that can find common ground. And, here, I think it`s going to be on a House party-line vote and then on a Senate party-line vote.\par \par \uc2 After this, whether it`s infrastructure, whether it`s small business tax credits, I hope we can find things that we can work together on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, while I have you, Congressman, if you don`t mind, of this big recall that for going on with \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  in California, it seems to be gathering steam. What do you think of it all? Is the governor in trouble? Should he be in trouble?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: It`s a concern.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think he will survive it. I`m supporting him. I don`t think recalling a governor in the midst of a crisis is the right way to go. And it`s tough. Look, there isn`t a governor in this country with COVID who`s had an easy time of it. Every time it looks like we`re turning a corner -- I heard your earlier segment. I hope that they`re right, that we`re getting past it.\par \par \uc2 But variants come in. And it`s just a tough time to govern. And people -- you can`t -- anything you do is going to upset both sides. And so I think the governor is doing his best. It`s a tough circumstance. He will survive it. But it is a serious challenge.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will see, ultimately, what happens.\par \par \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , California congressman, very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 Please keep us alerted, sir, to what kind of progress you`re making on this move to try to find a way to salvage a higher minimum wage and to help small businesses in the process.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210224eh2o0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Lawmakers On The House Are Now About To Vote On Biden's Whopping $1.9 Trillion Stimulus Package But Republicans Are Pushing Back And Calling It A Pork Filled Wish List By Liberals; New York Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  Could Face Some Criminal Charges For Trying To Hide The Real Numbers From COVID-Related Death Toll In The State's Nursing Homes Back In October; After All the Controversy That Surrounds China, The Wuhan Institute Of Virology Is Expected To Receive Funding From American Taxpayer Dollars For The Next Three Years To Fund Its In-Depth Research On Animals; Media Accuses FL Gov. DeSantis Of Playing Politics With COVID Vaccine; Feds Investigate Engine Explosion; Disney Slaps Content Warning On "The Muppet Show"\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Dagen McDowell, Jesse Watters, Juan Williams, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 7,307 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS HOST: We've got to leave it here. We will keep working with you. We'll keep going to you, but right now, I've got to go to --\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST (on camera): Hello, everyone. I'm Dana Perino along with Greg Gutfeld, Jesse Watters, Dagen McDowell, and Juan Williams. It's five o'clock in New York City, and this THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Democrats ready to go it alone on President Biden's massive nearly $2 trillion COVID relief bill, but Republicans are blasting it as pork filled liberal wish list. Here are some of the proposals and spending the GOP has taken issue with.\par \par \uc2 Raising the minimum wage of $15 an hour over one billion for socially disadvantaged farmers, 270 million for arts and humanities endowments, and 200 million for museums and libraries. The bill also contains $350 billion to bail out states, the money for Obamacare and almost 130 billion for schools whether they reopen or not. Republican lawmakers are sounding off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO