ute\uc2 , or even a university that may be more down the middle. The CBO generally comes out with left leaning studies. It was a surprise to see this.\par \par \uc2 The pushback they're going to get from Democrats is, well, there might be places in Chicago and San Francisco that can absolutely bear this kind of burden. Is West Virginia, and Senator Joe Manchin, whose vote they're going to need, can they bear that kind of burden? Can places in Minnesota. The cost of living is so much lower, I kind of doubt it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Biden also spoke out about his two hour phone call with China's President Xi.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I pointed out to him, no American president can be sustained as a president if he doesn't reflect the values of the United States. And so the idea, I'm not going to speak out against what he's doing in Hong Kong, what he's doing with the Uighurs in western mountains of China, and Taiwan, trying to end the one China policy by making it forceful, I said -- he gets it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So I guess Biden admits that Xi knows that he's just paying lip service to Biden's raising the issue of human rights. So he said basically that Xi gets it that he has to make a nod toward human rights, Chris. Xi just must be laughing at us at this point.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: I think Xi was very, very, very excited to see Donald Trump and Pompeo and the Republicans have to take the backseat to Joe Biden, someone who he has actually already potentially compromised members of Joe Biden's family on this. They don't expect a harsh response. It's going to be interesting to see who Biden nominates in the end to fill some of these positions, if he goes for liberal people here. But exactly, Xi is watching that.\par \par \uc2 And some of these answers just over the hour that we had tonight of questions from the audience from Joe Biden were so rambling that it almost made me a little relieved that he is playing Mario Kart at camp while Kamala Harris is apparently taking the calls from world leaders these days.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Chris, at one point, someone asked him a question and he said if you give me your address, we're going to get our plan for small businesses. The poor guy owns brewery. Well, if you give me your address. Like what, what is that? All right, Chris --\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: I was hoping for more on that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, a little bit more on that for the small business owners. Chris, thanks so much. We tried to warn them.\par \par \uc2 There are voices on the left who genuinely believe that anger over the Texas blackouts is just about stoking a culture war. Our message to them and the real root of the issue in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's about coming to the moment to deal with this maximum threat that is now facing us, called climate change, with the greatest sense of urgency.\par \par \uc2 I'm signing today an executive order to supercharge our administration's ambitious plan to confront the existential threat of climate change. It is an existential threat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Did someone have to wake him up to deliver that? I wonder what Joe would call being without heat and electricity in the middle of the deep-freeze? That's the existential threat millions of Texans are facing right now tonight as rolling blackouts, unprecedented cold grip the state, and a number of people have already died trying to stay warm. So what's the ultimate cause of all of this?\par \par \uc2 Here now is Chuck DeVore, Vice President of National Initiatives at the Texas Public Policy Foundation. Chuck, you say this is, what, largely rooted in Obama-Biden administration policies? Explain that.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK DEVORE, \uc2 TEXAS PUBLIC POLICY FOUNDATION\uc2 : Laura, there's two things going on here. First of all, Texas's independent system operator called ERCOT made some really poor calls a couple of days ago, and in fact Governor Greg Abbott has called for their resignation. He wants a clean sweep. So there were some bad short-term decisions.\par \par \uc2 But underlying all of this is some really poor long-term policies that have caused an overbuilding of wind and solar in Texas and a decommissioning of reliable coal-fired power plants and some older natural gas plants. And the more you do that, Laura, the more the grid becomes unsustainable, unreliable. The resiliency declines, and when you get these extreme weather events, or whether it's hot weather in the summer or this cold wave in the winter, problems are going to happen.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Earlier tonight, MSNBC's Chris Hayes says that the GOP is lying about the cause of all of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: It is just a lie that wind turbines, green energy, are the root causes of the problems in Texas right now. They want to turn it into some culture war, it's about the libs don't want you to have.\par \par \uc2 We have a choice collectively as a country. We don't have to have politics about where we get our energy divide along the lines of abortion or policing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Chuck, your response?\par \par \uc2 DEVORE: Well, I think he needs to understand a little bit more about physics and how electricity works. It needs to be generated the moment it is consumed. And you can't store wind power without massive and expensive batteries. There is some battery capacity in Texas, but with the cold snap, their capabilities were cut by 60 percent because batteries don't work all that well when it gets cold. And these are enormously costly facilities that need to be created.\par \par \uc2 What is happening right now, Laura, is that wind and solar producers in Texas and across the country are not responsible to produce power when it's need. That is not valued in America. We take it for granted. And the more wind and solar that you have on the grid, unless it's backed up with natural gas plants that just sit around waiting to be used or these huge battery farms, what's going to happen is California style blackouts are going to become increasingly common throughout the entire country. Texas just experienced a little bit of California, and it's not good. People are dying.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wait until that happens in the hot, hot summer months. We saw a lot of unrest last summer for other reasons, but you mix that in with no air conditioning, my God, it's going to be horrific. Chuck, thank you for explaining this.\par \par \uc2 A quote from Joe Biden's town hall made our ANGLE look prophetic, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Just as our ANGLE laid out, Joe Biden has his administration focused on the things that Americans really, truly care about.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we have to deal with systemic racism that exists throughout society. And one of the things I'm going to recommend there is that we look at an entire panoply of things that affect whether or not people of color primarily are treated differently.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Panoply. Ooh, impressive word usage.\par \par \uc2 That's all the time we have tonight. Shannon Bream and the "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" team take it all from here. Shannon, take it away.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020210217eh2g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Ex-President's Team Calls Impeachment Trial Political Theater; Top Union Leaders Slam Biden's Job-Killing Order; Jen Psaki Mocks FOX's Peter Doocy When Asked About \uc2 Keystone\uc2  Job Losses; Alabama \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Employees Pushing For Union; Blue States Struggle With COVID Vaccine Distribution; Chicago Mayor Announces Tentative Deal With Teachers Union To Reopen Schools; Antifa Crowd Marches Through D.C. Streets Threatening To Burn It Down; Biden's Border Crisis; Rep. Waters Denies Encouraging Violence Against Republicans; Biden Administration Disavows \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . Director's Comments In Favor Of School Reopenings\b0\par\par\uc2 Jackie DeAngelis, Hillary Vaughn, Edward Lawrence, Jason Chaffetz,  Joe Concha, Mike Emanuel, James Freeman \par \uc2 8,153 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: Anyway, thanks for watching us today and every day as we report the news to you "After the Bell." I am Connell McShane. We'll see you back here at the same time tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK FINANCIAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, everybody. I'm Jackie DeAngelis, and this is FOX BUSINESS TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 First up, Senate Democrats today announcing they have reached an agreement with attorney for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on how to proceed with their second impeachment trial.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's legal team responding moments ago in a statement saying this: "This process will provide us with an opportunity to explain to senators why it is absurd and unconstitutional to hold an impeachment trial against a private citizen.\par \par \uc2 Now, a host of Republican senators have also stated that the trial is unconstitutional and say it'll only further the divide of the Republic. Here is Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): I think if we're going to criminalize speech and somehow impeach everybody who says, "Oh, go fight to hear your voices heard." I mean, really, we ought to impeach \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  then.\par \par \uc2 People want to call President Trump accountable for language. There has to be a consistent standard. And to my mind, it's a partisan farce because they're not doing anything to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , not doing anything to Representative Omar, not doing anything to \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  . It's just not fair.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Also ahead tonight, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's backers seem to have some buyer's remorse. The head of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  , a top union leader is now criticizing President Biden's Executive Order to halt the Keystone Pipeline. That's a move that will cut 11,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD TRUMKA\uc2  , \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  PRESIDENT: I wish he hadn't done that on the first day because the Laborers International was right. It did and will cost us jobs in the process.\par \par \uc2 I wish he had pared that more carefully with the thing that he did second by saying here's where we're creating jobs. We can do mine reclamation, we can fix leaks, and we can fix seeps and create hundreds of thousands of jobs in doing all of that stuff.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You think Biden realizes that that was a mistake? That announcement?\par \par \uc2 TRUMKA: I think so, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Plus, President Biden says the \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . will release guidance on reopening schools this week, but the President failed to mention that the \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . already released that guidance in mid-September.\par \par \uc2 So why are so many students still out of the classroom? We're going to be taking all of this up and more with a great lineup of guests tonight. We are joined by former Congressman Jason Chaffetz, "The Wall Street Journal's" James Freeman, media columnist for "The Hill," Joe Concha. And Dr. Qanta Ahmed.\par \par \uc2 But we're going to begin this evening with the Biden administration's growing job crisis. Thousands of American energy workers were laid off because of President Biden's Executive Order canceling the Keystone Pipeline, a decision that's been blasted by the head of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 President Biden, of course and his team have promised high paying green jobs for those currently unemployed workers, but pressed for when those jobs would be available. White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  shared this tense exchange with Fox's Peter Doocy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, I'd certainly welcome you to present your data of all the thousands and thousands of people who won't be getting a green job. Maybe next time you're here, you can present that.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: There are people living paycheck to paycheck. There are now people out of jobs once the Keystone Pipeline -- out of jobs once the Keystone Pipeline stop construction.\par \par \uc2 It's been 12 days since Gina McCarthy and \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  were here and it's been 19 days since that EO. So what do these people who need money now, when do they get their green jobs?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, the President and many Democrats and Republicans in Congress believe that investment in infrastructure, building infrastructure that's in our national interests and the boost to the U.S. economy creates good paying union jobs here in America and advances our climate and clean energy goals are something that we can certainly work on doing together and he has every plan to share more about his -- details of that plan in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Now, this all follows previous comments from Special Climate Envoy \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  , who told oil workers to go make solar panels and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  , who said that coal miners could reconsider reclaiming abandoned land mines.\par \par \uc2 A historic union vote is underway in the suburbs of Birmingham, Alabama. Roughly 6,000 ballots have been sent to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse workers. They could decide if a warehouse there becomes the company's first unionized facility in the United States.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business correspondent, Hillary Vaughn is in Washington. She has got the latest for us -- Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Jackie. Well, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  actually wanted their workers to have to show up in person to vote, but the vote is happening by mail and the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  , an agency representative with them tells me, they denied \uc2 Amazon\uc2  's request to delay this vote so that it could happen in-person and instead the ballots have been sent out today by mail.\par \par \uc2 Workers have seven weeks to get their ballot in and opt in or out of unionizing. But if they do opt in, they will be the first \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse in the U.S. to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, pro-union laborers in Alabama also got support from Senator Bernie Sanders who sent pizza to the people at the rally and signaled that what \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers decide in Alabama could make other \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers around the country want a piece of the pie tweeting this. "It cannot be overstated how powerful it will be if \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers in Alabama vote to form a union. They are taking on powerful anti-union forces in a strong anti-union state, but their victory will benefit every worker in America."\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has gone on an aggressive counter campaign discouraging their workers from voting to unionize. They even placed fliers for workers to see in their bathroom stalls. An \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokesperson gives us this statement saying, quote: We don't believe the RWDSU represents the majority of our employees' views. Our employees choose to work at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  because we offer some of the best jobs available everywhere we hire."\par \par \uc2 Now normally, workers considering unionizing at or advocating for higher pay or better benefits. But that's not the main issue here. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers at this facility make over 15 bucks an hour. That's twice the Federal minimum wage right now, and 30 cents more than what President Biden wants to raise it to. They also get healthcare, vision and dental benefits and a retirement plan as well.\par \par \uc2 The union that wants to represent these \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers said they did brief the White House on their campaign and their efforts to unionize. So not only is Senator Bernie Sanders keeping an eye on what's happening in Alabama, but it seems that the President is, too -- Jackie.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Hillary Vaughn reporting for us in Washington. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the race to roll out the coronavirus vaccine is in full force as major retailers like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  , Sam's Club and Winn Dixie begin distributing the vaccine today that's in the southeast.\par \par \uc2 But Democrat run New York's vaccine hubs, well, they look like virtual ghost towns. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " is now asking how progressive leaders are struggling to distribute vaccines quickly and efficiently writing quote, "Some blue states have created intricate rules about who qualifies for a vaccine and then made a big effort to keep anybody else from getting a shot," end quote.\par \par \uc2 For more in the blue states struggling with the distribution of coronavirus vaccines, let's go to FOX Business correspondent, Edward Lawrence -- Edward.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE FOX BUSINESS NETWORK GENERAL ASSIGNMENT REPORTER: Hey, Jackie. Yes, that has been the rub. You know, the gap between the number of doses actually going into arms and the number of doses the government has actually delivered, and that gap is shrinking, but it has taken all that time to get here.\par \par \uc2 Now as of today, 59 million doses have been delivered and 41 million doses have gone into arms. That's about 70 percent. But the numbers did not look like that a week ago.\par \par \uc2 The main problem has been state rules in some areas, states like New York, California, Maryland and Illinois set strict tier rules that frontline workers in long term nursing homes get the vaccination first, but only about 50 percent of medical professionals wanted the vaccines, so the number of doses supplied to hospitals and nursing homes sat in freezers while the White House COVID response adviser acknowledged the slow ramp up. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDY SLAVITT\uc2  , SENIOR ADVISER TO WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE TEAM: We understand that the process moves more slowly than anyone would like. But each day, we are putting forth efforts to increase vaccine supply, including by you through the Defense Production Act to create more places to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And compounding the issue, the \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . says, the number of the doses going into nursing homes was miscounted. They count it by the number of beds, not the number of people. So the South Carolina Governor tweeting that he is reallocating some of those doses, the Governors of California and Texas did the same thing.\par \par \uc2 Now, Bill Galston with the Brookings Institute researched this and found the best state in the nation at distributing was not buying into that Federal program for the nursing homes, but allocating all the doses with cooperation in mind.\par \par \uc2 Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM ARTHUR GALSTON, EZRA K. ZILKHA CHAIR IN GOVERNANCE STUDIES, SENIOR FELLOW, BROOKINGS INSTITUTION: West Virginia, by contrast, decided to deal directly with local mom and pop pharmacies. They created a network of about 250 of them. That turned out to be a good move because those mom and pop pharmacies have personal relationships with local institutions and even local residents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And West Virginia opened up who can get the vaccine among the fastest in the country. So 90.2 percent of doses that they got are in the arms. Now by contrast, California set that strict rules, and it only has 64 percent of doses that are in arms, the rest in freezers waiting for those bodies. Back to you, Jackie.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Edward Lawrence in D.C. Thank you so much for that report. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Vaccines, of course are also at the center of a major battle between cities and powerful teachers unions. In Chicago, a tentative agreement could see schools gradually start to reopen for in-person learning this week.\par \par \uc2 FOX News senior correspondent, \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in Chicago this evening with the latest for us -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: And Jackie, we could finally be getting to that end of that long and winding road back into the classroom with the Chief of the Schools here in Chicago as well as the mayor announcing what they called a breakthrough a tentative agreement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT (D), CHICAGO, ILLINOIS: These past 11 months had been a whirlwind for our city, and that's putting it mildly. But with our school reopening, we have taken a momentous step forward in our journey of renewal and recovery. The schools are the spine for our city.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: The Chicago teachers union emphasized "tentative," called it a framework and said any agreement must first be approved by the House of Delegates within the union, then the rank and file teachers with a vote.\par \par \uc2 The framework involves thresholds to halt in-person learning like three positive coronavirus cases in a 14-day period. It also mandates that no \uc2 CTU\uc2  teacher can be forced into the classroom without access to a vaccination.\par \par \uc2 But it was the Director of the \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . who said vaccinations are not necessary for a safe return to the schools, and the data doesn't show that the virus spreads in a controlled environment like a school.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2  , C.D.C. DIRECTOR: The data from schools suggest that there's very little transmission that is happening within the schools, especially when there's masking and distancing occurring.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: In Chicago, pre-K and Special Ed classes could resume on Thursday. A phase-in all the way up to eighth grade could happen during the month of March. There is no schedule yet in Chicago for a turn of high school students. Jackie, back to you\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: And \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in a very cold Chicago tonight. Thank you so much for that report. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to Wall Street where stocks finished at record highs today and the Dow up 237 points, the S&P up 29 and the NASDAQ gaining 131. Volume on the big board, 4.7 billion shares.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, President Biden handcuffing immigration and customs agents from enforcing the law. We're going to talk that and more with former Congressman Jason Chaffetz. You're watching FOX BUSINESS TONIGHT. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Breaking news, Antifa activists marched through the streets of our nation's capital threatening to quote, "burn it down." The crowd clashed with police officers this weekend, shining lights in officers' eyes and pushing back against the police line. No arrests were made.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking, Immigrations and Customs Enforcement will soon arrest and deport fewer illegal immigrants due to guidance coming from the Biden White House. I.C.E. officers will no longer seek deportations for illegal immigrants convicted of drunk driving. The Biden administration policy also weakens the ability to arrest wanted immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Now one I.C.E. official telling "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " quote: "They've abolished I.C.E. without abolishing I.C.E. It literally feels like we've gone from the ability to fully enforce our immigration laws to now being told to enforce nothing."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Jason Chaffetz, former Congressman and former Chairman of the House Oversight Committee, bestselling author and FOX Business contributor.\par \par \uc2 Jason, great to see you. Good evening.\par \par \uc2 JASON CHAFFETZ, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CONTRIBUTOR: Thanks for having me. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Look, let's first start about -- start off talking about what we saw in Washington, D.C. over the weekend. One of the messages coming out from President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself on Inauguration Day was we are going to unify this country, and as we get set to kick off a Senate impeachment trial tomorrow, and we're talking about violence and bringing people together, why are the Dems being so silent about what happened here?\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Well, I guess that's what's so concerning, their silence on this. I think Republicans have been very consistent from everything that happened on January 6, which was absolutely awful. But all the violence that was happening, particularly in these big major cities in the Pacific Northwest, but Democrats have been absolutely silent.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  spent her summer out there raising money to bail people out of jail as fast as they could to get them back into these protests and riots and this violence.\par \par \uc2 Look, there is nothing more American than protesting your government, but when it turns violent and you're advocating violence and you're attacking police officers, why can that not be the uniting moment? The Republicans, Democrats, everybody should be united on that one.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: It's an opportunity, right? It's an opportunity to step in and say we condemn all violence.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yes, and they give a little bit of lip service, you see a tweet here and there. But when it's happening in real time, and they're not pro- Trump people doing it, you don't hear a word from the Democrats, and everybody should say, we support our law enforcement, they should not have to put up with this.\par \par \uc2 These are our friends. They're our neighbors. They're our loved ones. And they put on a uniform to protect us.\par \par \uc2 But then they have these political leaders and mayors and governors, they're just silent, and it's so demoralizing and it's frustrating as an American who just wants safe cities.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Let's switch gears for a second and talk about immigration and some of these new policies for I.C.E. We've seen different administrations really swing wildly with respect to their policies on the pendulum, right? So we are used to that.\par \par \uc2 You've got Biden saying that he wants to reorient, but what you really are talking about here is a dramatic loosening of the policies, and you've got about half the country that voted for President Trump, who liked a more stringent policy when it came to deportations, illegal immigration and what was going on at the border. Your thoughts about the approach here?\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: I've spent a lot of time with I.C.E. officers, the men and women who have one of the most difficult jobs out there, they are not out there just arresting somebody who is here illegally. They are looking at the criminal element within the illegal immigration population.\par \par \uc2 And for them to have their political leaders say, well look, driving under the influence is really not that serious, so we don't want you to detain them and we don't want you to deport them.\par \par \uc2 In my mind, if you're here illegally, and you commit a crime, again, why can we not agree that the solution to that, and the way you actually solve this problem is to deport the person. Instead, we allow them to stay here and commit more crimes.\par \par \uc2 Under the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration, more than 60,000 times -- 60,000 -- a whole football stadium, they had somebody who was here illegally, and instead of deporting them, they just released him out of the streets.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Yes. And it's a privilege to be in this country, especially for those who have come through the system and gone through the system in the proper way, through the proper channels.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you have people here who are sort of coming over illegally -- not sort of - and in some cases and when they are breaking the law, we're basically just saying, "That's okay."\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Yes, and it's not -- the people we are failing, the ones I feel closer affinity to are the people that are trying to do this in the legal and lawful way.\par \par \uc2 They are standing in line. They are going through the visa process. We process and bring in more than a million people every year. But the people who bypass all of those, they get here illegally and commit a crime. Remember, this is the criminal element of the illegal immigration population.\par \par \uc2 There has to be restitution. Part of restitution is go back to where you came from, because you didn't come in the front door, the right, proper and legal way.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: All right, let's talk about President Biden's Super Bowl interview. I want to play soundbite for you here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You know, I'll bet you, there is not a family you know that doesn't have somebody in a family that had a drug problem or an alcohol problem.\par \par \uc2 But the honesty with which he stepped forward and talked about the problem and the hope that it gave me hope reading it. I mean, it was like, "My boy is back," you know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: So Jason, he is, you know, talking about his son very lovingly there. He is promoting his book as well. The one thing he is not talking about is \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's business dealings in China, and a lot of people want to know about it.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: On a human level, any parent, any loved one that you're dealing with that is going through tough times, I understand. But as the President of the United States doing it in a so-called the interview from a news organization has to ask about these difficult questions about the proximity of not only \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's brothers and the businesses and how they have profited from their relationship as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as a senator, as a Vice President, and what does that look like as a President?\par \par \uc2 None of those hard questions have ever come to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , and that's why a lot of us look at it and say, you know what, if you had a chance with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , you wouldn't treat him that way. It's the duplicity and the fact that they missed an opportunity to ask a probing question, instead, it's just a fawning, you know, interview that really doesn't illuminate anything.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Well, they definitely would have asked \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can come on this show and we'll ask him.\par \par \uc2 Jason Chaffetz, it's great to see you. Take care.\par \par \uc2 CHAFFETZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: All right, up next new evidence that President Biden's agenda will kill more jobs than it actually creates. We're going to take it up with "Wall Street Journal's" James Freeman. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is determined to shift to renewable energy like wind and solar by 2035, but that's going to come at the expense of Americans, both rich and poor.\par \par \uc2 Germany shifted its energy to similar renewable sources in the year 2000, and since then, electricity prices have doubled, while just 33 percent of its energy comes from solar and wind.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  says that the Democrats plan to increase the minimum wage to $15.00 an hour will kill more jobs than it actually helps people, 1.4 million jobs would be lost compared to the 900,000 who would no longer be below the poverty line.\par \par \uc2 The Biden White House today called the CBO's estimates, quote "overstated."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, James Freeman, assistant editor at "The Wall Street Journal" and Fox Business contributor. James, it's great to see you. Let's start there and talk about the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 You know, there's no doubt in anybody's mind that we need to work on wage growth in this country, but that's not to say that you need to jump to a standard $15.00 minimum wage that could crush businesses. The estimates there that the administration is saying, you know, are overstated at this point. That has potential to really have an impact, and you have to look at the math there. It doesn't add up.\par \par \uc2 JAMES FREEMAN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I