oing to change emissions around the world. Because we have a vibrant technology, friendly economy, we've actually in the United States been the leader in recent years in reducing global emissions, our shale revolution, fracking has replaced coal with gas and that's been a good thing.\par \par \uc2 This is going to cost a lot of jobs in the U.S., and I think it's going to be a big issue going forward if the President continues on this path. So you're going to see a split between union leadership and industrial workers who are members of the Union, obviously, a lot of them voted for Trump, and probably more of them are going to be upset with our new President if he continues in this manner.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: And I'll make the point that we brought our emissions down not being part of the Paris Agreement, when many of the other countries that are a part of it aren't really doing their share, yet we go and reverse that, we get right back into the agreement with folks that are really not holding up to being accountable for their actions and their contributions, which is one of the criticisms there.\par \par \uc2 But you know, when a lot of people look at what's happening with what President Biden is doing with the climate change, you know, the sort of the climate actions that he is positing, if you will, he said that he was going to be a moderate President. He said that he was not going to support a Green New Deal.\par \par \uc2 He said that he was going to do things over time and phase out and that he wasn't going to ban fracking. Remember all of that? He said it. You know, and some are saying no, he had to come into office, and he had to sort of go to that more extreme left position because he has to answer to his party.\par \par \uc2 And that, you know, this may be a case, we have to see how the policies play out. But this may be a case to a certain extent of a bait and switch. You take this President. He is moderate, right? Bernie Sanders, Elizabeth Warren, they scare people. Take him, but then he's going to kind of just do it -- do it anyway.\par \par \uc2 FREEMAN: Yes, that was the theory in the hope that this was the more moderate path. Certainly, a lot of voters and a lot of Democratic voters thought they were saying no to the extreme big government agenda of Bernie Sanders when they voted in primaries last year.\par \par \uc2 I think, unfortunately, not just the flurry of Executive Orders, we were just talking about the \uc2 Keystone\uc2  decision to kill that pipeline, there were others reviewing all of Trump's environmental policies. But I think the big negative signal of the week is that decision to press ahead with his stimulus plan on a straight Democratic vote.\par \par \uc2 You had a bunch of Republicans making huge offers. Their opening bid was over $600 billion. Personally, I think the bid should have been zero, but he probably could have had a bipartisan deal to spend a trillion dollars. He is demanding $1.9 trillion.\par \par \uc2 And you have a lot of \uc2 Obama\uc2  -Biden, economic leaders saying it's really not the moment for that. It doesn't make sense. It doesn't fit our current situation.\par \par \uc2 The economy is ready to thrive this year, we don't need it. So I think unfortunately, yes, all of the signals are a move to the left, and I don't know if he has to, to please his party. I would hope there's some leadership to say, no, we're going to chart a moderate course. But we're not seeing it.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: James Freeman, thank you so much for your insight. Always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 FREEMAN: You too. Thanks, Jackie.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: All right. Coming up next, Corporate America anoints Bruce Springsteen as the moderate unifier of the country. "The Hills" media columnist, Joe Concha joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Breaking news: Senate leaders reach an agreement on the rules for Tuesday's impeachment trial of former President