ying violence against Republicans, particularly this comment from June 2018. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXINE WATERS (D-CA): Let's make sure we show up wherever we have to show up and if you see anybody from that Cabinet in a restaurant, in a department store, at a gasoline station, you get out and you create a crowd and you push back on them and you tell them, they are not welcome anymore anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Remember that? Here's what she said about that comment yesterday on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: As a matter of fact, if you look at the words that I used, the strongest thing I said was, "Tell them they're not welcome. Talk to them. Tell them they're not welcome." I didn't say go and fight. I didn't say anybody was going to have any violence and so they can't make that stick.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: All right, interesting interpretation. And did you see this during the Super Bowl last? Night automaker \uc2 Jeep\uc2  using Bruce Springsteen in an ad designed to be about unifying the country. The two minute ad ends with a message: "To the ReUnited States of America." Now Springsteen has always supported Democrats, including President Biden, even threatening to move to Australia if Trump was re-elected.\par \par \uc2 Springsteen, however, never made a similar call for unity while President Trump was in office.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to talk more about that and everything else, Joe Concha, media and politics columnist for "The Hill" and FOX Business contributor. Joe, great to see you tonight. Thank you so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with the Bruce Springsteen stuff because I was glued to the game last night for other reasons having to do with Tom Brady, but I did see that ad and it's interesting because when it comes to Corporate America becoming so political, you know, it seems like CEOs and companies may be a little tone deaf with respect to the overall population.\par \par \uc2 And when they send out messages like this in a partisan way, that they're alienating people and those people have money to spend.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CONTRIBUTOR: Well, let's tackle that first statement you made, Jackie. So you're watching because of Tom Brady, so you're probably in the same boat as my wife. That's her Hall Pass.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: So yes, very interested in seeing Tom with his helmet off in the sidelines for some reason, but conversation for another time. Yes, Springsteen, Jersey Shore guy, I believe we are Jersey people as well, probably saw you at the Parker House, I'll pray, a younger Jackie, anyway way back when down at the Jersey Shore.\par \par \uc2 But who were the ad wizards that came up with the -- you know what, let's take Bruce Springsteen, all right, from I believe, Red Bank, New Jersey, and let's stick him in Kansas, throw them in a Jeep , make him look like a 21st Century John Wayne, and then he could preach to the country about unity, despite the fact that you know, he campaigned for \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  , \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  , \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Yes, that's the nonpartisan guy that we want talking about bringing the country together, the one who obviously is probably one of the most partisan performers that we will see out of Hollywood or the music industry, and that that's a tough man of the hole.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: And Joe, I want to go back to the comments from \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  . We played the old soundbite of her and her sort of re-explaining herself. As this impeachment trial kicks off, part of the issue is going to be actually going through the transcript of what the President said, and trying to make the case that he did in fact, incite violence.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the timeline of when people started going over to the Capitol, he wasn't even done speaking yet. There is a line in there where he says, you know, we're going to go to the Capitol today peacefully. And so some would argue that he didn't incite violence.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes protests have a way of getting out of control, and this one did. Nobody condones what happened that day. But how could she stand up there and say, you know, I didn't do that. I didn't say that.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: It's right there on tape. Right? I mean, and the key part is the worst part of what she said was "push back on them." Now you could say, well, she meant that verbally. How do we know that? And then we saw what happened to some Republican lawmakers that were just horrible during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, Rand Paul . Remember? He was beaten in his own front yard, had his ribs broken. He had a collapsed lung. He was in the hospital for weeks.\par \par \uc2 Then remember Steve Scalise at that softball field, right outside of D.C. where he was actually shot, nearly killed, along with some other lawmakers and people that were at that field that were shot as well. So that kind of rhetoric, right, it has no place, no place in our dialogue these days.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  , absolutely wrong there. What happened at the Capitol was one of the darkest days of we've seen in modern history, quite frankly, when a police officer gets killed, and as you said, no one condones that.\par \par \uc2 So yes, the fact that PolitiFact fact checks \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  said no, no, she didn't mean that. Well, now you're getting subjective and now it's getting interpreted when clearly the words are right there to listen to and read.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: And Joe, we're going to start an impeachment trial in the Senate at a time where the country is still in crisis, the COVID crisis, and we're trying to get the economy back up and running and get people vaccinated and get back to our normal lives, not