ANGELIS: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says it's necessary for children to return to school, but that it must be done safely, of course. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It is a national emergency. It genuinely is a national emergency.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you think it's time for schools to reopen?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think it's time for schools to reopen safely -- safely. You have to have fewer people in the classroom. You have to have ventilation systems that have been reworked.\par \par \uc2 Our \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . Commissioner was going to be coming out with science-based judgment within, I think as early as Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: The President not mentioning the guidelines the \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . has had in place in September that consider the number of new cases within a given community and basic mitigation efforts like wearing a mask, social distancing, hygiene and disinfecting things, of course.\par \par \uc2 Now, Americans are largely split on receiving the COVID-19 vaccine as well. According to a new \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll, half of Americans plan to get the vaccine at the first chance, while another quarter say they don't intend to ever be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 New York pulmonologist Dr. Qanta Ahmed is imploring medical professionals to build confidence in the vaccine, writing quote, "Most fears can be allayed by good communication and doctors have a disproportionate role in leading the way -- we are the most trusted when it comes to health information. When we fear vaccination, fears are magnified in the public," and Dr. Ahmed joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Wonderful to see you doctor. You're a pulmonologist at NYU Langone Hospital and a Senior Fellow at the Independent Women's Forum.\par \par \uc2 The first question that I want to ask you is with respect to President Biden and what he said about reopening schools, you know, he talked about new ventilation systems. This is going to cost a lot of money to re-outfit every kind of school when simple measures from the \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 .: wearing a mask, washing your hands, staying six-feet apart, could work for people who are under a certain age and also don't have a preexisting condition. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 DR. QANTA AHMED, PULMONOLOGIST, NYU LANGONE HOSPITAL: First of all, it's very refreshing to hear President Biden refer to our schools and the need to open them as an emergency. There is nothing short of a public health crisis developing with all these closed schools.\par \par \uc2 When children are out of school, they are becoming anxious. Anxiety and depression is being diagnosed in children as young as five. Mental health admissions from children are skyrocketing. We've heard of the terrible incidences of I think 24 suicides coming out of one county in Nevada in children. So there is a huge cost to be keeping schools closed.\par \par \uc2 We don't have to reinvent the wheel. We know if we have good ventilation, open the windows, dress the children a little bit warmly, space them out, wear a mask, children can be very safe, and we know that because countries like Sweden, South Korea and China did not close their schools.\par \par \uc2 In Sweden, they have two million children in schools while the pandemic was raging free without lockdown, and less than 50 children became ill enough to require hospitalization.\par \par \uc2 It is extremely rare and children under 10 almost never, ever become ill. And closing the schools are much more damaging to the children than opening them immediately.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: And Doctor, the \uc2 C.D.C\uc2 . Director essentially set out these guidelines that we can all follow that we've all been following in our daily lives and the teachers have been pushing back and saying, well, it's about having the proper PPE in the classrooms, sanitizers. Did you not have soap and water at public schools -- at the 98,000 public schools before the pandemic started?\par \par \uc2 And do the teachers not, in the way I do when I go to the grocery store, wear a mask or have a mask? You don't even need a fancy mask, any kind of face covering would do to help prevent the spread.\par \par \uc2 AHMED: I think it's very important that we say there are many teachers who are making huge sacrifices to go in and teach children. And also, some of my patients are teachers who are explaining how difficult it is to teach remotely because not everybody in America is on broadband, not everybody has multiple laptops at home. There are digital deserts in America, so children can't even access the remote services.\par \par \uc2 But the unions, particularly the developments in Chicago are so troubling the one questions whether the union really has the child at priority. The child in this pandemic in the world is the most marginalized patient and our children, we are sacrificing an entire year in America's public schools while children in South Korea, China and Europe continue their education is a devastating generational blow to this country.\par \par \uc2 It's not just for children, the parents are desperate for their children to get back in and learn and have the social interaction, play and the work of children and they need that for mental health as well as educational health.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Doctor, you brought up the point of having the broadband capabilities, not every household has them and so remote learning can be quite challenging for some families, but there's also the issue of some of those kids needing to go to school for those meals. They really rely on that.\par \par \uc2 AHMED: It's not just -- you bring an excellent point up it's not just meals. The schools are more than just a place for education. They are a place of safety and sanctuary, a place where we do immunization, a place where we give children health education, sex education.\par \par \uc2 When children are not in school, they are more vulnerable to sexual violence, exploitation, child abuse, domestic violence. They have many other hazards. Children are missing so many routine immunizations.\par \par \uc2 It's tragic that we've get to come out of this pandemic and find many children are not vaccinated against completely preventable illnesses like measles and mumps because they were not in school.\par \par \uc2 And yes, I am in a very nice area of Long Island where my husband lives, but even in New York City, there are digital deserts where children are so deprived and so forth. They can do no learning if they're not in the classroom, let alone no eating because they're poor, and they depend on school meals.\par \par \uc2 This is something that has to happen immediately, and I'm ready to support the administration in any way to get this done advocating for our nation's children.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Dr. Ahmed, thank you so much for your insight tonight. Great to talk to you. And hopefully some of that information will resonate.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: The office of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today writing about tomorrow's Senate impeachment trial, quote: "This process will provide us with an opportunity to explain to Senators why it is absurd and unconstitutional to hold an impeachment trial against a private citizen," end quote.\par \par \uc2 It is a point of view echoed by Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: I think if we're going to criminalize speech and somehow impeach everybody who says, "Oh, go fight to hear your voices heard." I mean, really, we ought to impeach \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  then.\par \par \uc2 If people want to hold President Trump accountable for language, there has to be a consistent standard and to my mind, it's a partisan farce because they're not doing anything to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , not doing anything to Representative Omar and not doing anything to \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  . It's just not fair.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Be sure to join us here again tomorrow night and thanks for watching FOX BUSINESS TONIGHT. Have a wonderful evening.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020210209eh2800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Gov. Brian Kemp (R-GA); Limits On ICE Arrests?; Keystone Fallout; Winter Blast; Will Democrats Raise Minimum Wage?; Interview With Former U.S. Acting Immigration And Customs Enforcement Director \uc2 Ron Vitiello\b0\par\par\uc2 Charles Payne, Mark Mancuso, Chad Pergram, Jonathan Turley,  Hillary Vaughn, Gary B. Smith, Kristina Partsinevelos \par \uc2 7,669 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: If the minimum wage is hiked, will jobs take a hike?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  issuing its report today, the CBO warning that, if the minimum wage is hiked to $15 an hour, it would kiss 1.4 million jobs goodbye and that the federal deficit would skyrocket. The report also stating that it would lift 900,000 out of poverty.\par \par \uc2 Senate Budget Committee Chairman \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  saying today Democrats are ready to pass it without any GOP support, if necessary. But is it time to put the push on ice?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 To Hillary Vaughn with the very latest -- Hillary\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Biden's promise to raise the minimum wage to $15 an hour might be slightly delayed. And now he may have a hard time getting moderate Democrats to back that, after the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  is out with a new report today that shows that raising the minimum wage to $15 an hour would put more people out of a job than those it would help pull out of poverty.\par \par \uc2 The CBO says 1.4 million would lose their job from it. Only 900,000 would be lifted out of poverty because of it. It would also add another $54 billion to the deficit over 10 years. So, some Senate Republicans say it's going to make stuff more expensive for consumers. It would also hurt people they are trying to help.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  tweeting this: "Including this harmful policy in COVID relief bill would devastate same biz people already hurt by the pandemic."\par \par \uc2 But others, like Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , are not letting that news burst their bubble. Sanders says they should do it anyway and says the news from the CBO proves his point that they could pass it through budget reconciliation with no Republican support and just 51 votes, saying this today: "The good news is that from a Byrd Rule perspective, the CBO has demonstrated that increasing the minimum wage would have a direct and substantial impact on the federal budget. What that means is, we can clearly raise the minimum wage to $15 an hour under the rules of reconciliation."\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Biden threw cold water on the idea that the minimum wage hike would be included in the stimulus package. And, today, White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki would not say what they would do if they couldn't get it through \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 ' way -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hillary, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now, with the economy already reeling from the pandemic, is this any time to be hiking the minimum wage and risk the loss of at least 1.4 million jobs?\par \par \uc2 With us now, Republican strategist Alexandra Wilkes, Democratic strategist Jason Nichols, and market watcher Gary B. Smith.\par \par \uc2 Alexandra, let me start with you.\par \par \uc2 It's one heck of a tradeoff. How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRA WILKES, GOP STRATEGIST: Look, I think that this is such a slap in the face to small businesses through proposing a minimum wage hike at this time.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, in July of 2019, the nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  estimated that this would result in the loss of 1.3 million jobs. Now, in a post-pandemic era, businesses are suffering more than ever, and all of us can see it in our general lives, when we see the increasing automation and the services that we use.\par \par \uc2 Businesses are already trending in this direction. And a $15 minimum wage hike is only going to push them over the edge.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, of course, Jason, that was in 2019 version; 1.3 million was on the low end, the high end at 3.7 million. I haven't had a chance to go through the entire CBO report today.\par \par \uc2 But there's obviously some tradeoffs, And, to Alexandra's point, I don't know. They didn't even talk too much about the small businesses and places where it's obvious they couldn't afford it.\par \par \uc2 So, is this a time to sort of try to change the rules of reconciliation and to force this down the throats of small businesses?\par \par \uc2 JASON NICHOLS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, I think that we're looking at this the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 First of all, if the minimum wage had grown at the same level as American productivity, it would be $19.33, $19.33. Raising the minimum wage would raise pay for 32 million Americans; 31 percent of African Americans would get a wage increase. And it would benefit the economy as a whole, as more people would have more money to spend.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that, when we look at that job loss, and your previous guests just mentioned automation, automation is just driven by technology. That's not going to be driven by this. And we look at the results in Seattle. They have not caused this massive job loss that people are predicting.\par \par \uc2 Economists are not necessarily all on the same page. But I think that just choosing this one study, when there are many other studies by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  and other organizations that say that this will be positive for our economy as a whole, I think is something that we should look at.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Although Seattle, home to some pretty large corporations, probably can handle it.\par \par \uc2 Gary B., I'm not so sure, though, Birmingham can handle it. I'm not so sure Jackson, Mississippi, can handle it. And there are small businesses throughout the country, even in prosperous cities, who probably couldn't handle it. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 GARY B. SMITH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 Let's take the case of some city in the middle of the country, Des Moines, for example. The typical case would be, if they have a restaurant, Charles, with, say, 10 on the waitstaff, the choice comes down to this. The manager has to walk out and they go, OK, well, you six are now going to make 20 percent more.\par \par \uc2 You other four are now going to make zero. That's what it comes down to. And as far as automation, look, I have even seen it here in Jacksonville, which is relatively low cost of living, for the local \uc2 Publix\uc2  and amongst other supermarkets. They are adding automation. They're replacing cashiers, checkout people.\par \par \uc2 And now it's self-checkout. Look, businesses will adapt. This is not some money that, oh, Daddy Warbucks will just keep paying up. Businesses adapt. They add automation, or what they don't do is, they don't expand. They don't say, instead of the second Mamma Mia's Pizzeria, I'm just going to stick with the one, because I couldn't afford the waitstaff for two.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Jason, here's the thing. Often, households that have one person earning minimum wage may actually have two or three. And my concern is, if you have two people making $10 an hour, and that's $20 an hour, then the wages go up to $15, and one of those people lose a job, that household actually has lost income.\par \par \uc2 Why isn't the focus on going further, far beyond minimum wage? Why isn't the focus on, hey, this is the land of opportunity, where we import people from other countries to take six-figure jobs? Why isn't the Democratic focus on that element, as opposed as always being so focused on the minimum?\par \par \uc2 The poverty line is $12,000. Can we aim higher?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Well, I think the focus should be on the people who are most needy and most desperate, the people who, by the way, you and I end up paying for with social services.\par \par \uc2 So, I think one of the things that the Democratic Party wants to do is have a rising tide that lifts more boats. That includes people in the middle, but that also includes people at the bottom. And as I have said with -- another one of your guests mentioned automation. I think automation is coming.\par \par \uc2 There's no way to avoid that. That's capitalism and technology. That is not rising the minimum wage. As we have seen, the minimum wage is still $7,50 nationwide, and we're still getting their people replaced at the local grocery store with machines.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't think that that's what's driving this.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Alexandra, on Friday, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that he would not try to force us into this whole reconciliation thing.\par \par \uc2 Already, they have got something of a mess on their hands. So I'm not sure -- listen, we know that the progressives are trying to force his hand. Whether this comes up or not, what would the Republican side be? What do you say to people who are struggling at that level in terms of them being able to just grab themselves by the bootstraps?\par \par \uc2 WILKES: Look, I think what the Republicans are saying is that we need to open up, we need to get our schools open, we need to get our businesses open. This is going to be the best way to rev up the economy.\par \par \uc2 And I think that what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is running into here is the -- sort of the fanciful campaigning running up against the realities of governing. It's hard. Not everything can be done by fiat with an executive order and the stroke of a pen.\par \par \uc2 And in this case, when the going got tough, he seems to have just abandoned his plan, I think showcasing the weakness of the Democrats' argument here. They know that this that this strategy doesn't work across 50 states. They know that this would be a damper on the economy.\par \par \uc2 And I think that it's another example of where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to face problems from the progressive wing of his party.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, I want to take -- sort of shifts gears a little bit, folks, take a corner -- look at the corner of Wall and Broad, another record start to the week.\par \par \uc2 In fact, we close at the highs of the session, Dow, \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2 , and the S&P 500, in fact, all closing an all-time highs, boosted in part by hopes of an economic recovery as we get rid of these COVID lockdowns, to Alexandra's point. Also, oil, those prices rising to the highest level in a year.\par \par \uc2 Now, next question is, should teachers move to the front of the vaccination line? This is highly controversial, and a group of Georgia teachers are now demanding it. So, why is the state's Republican governor denying it?\par \par \uc2 Well, Brian Kemp is here.\par \par \uc2 And, later, it's not just Republicans ripping President Biden's move to kill the Keystone pipeline. Find out what this big labor boss is saying and why Mike Rowe says he's right. Mike Rowe is coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , NIAID DIRECTOR: You can have children going back to school as we have had in the past without necessarily having everyone vaccinated, all the teachers or all the students vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  telling Neil those kids can get back to school without everyone being vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 This as a bitter fight between states and teachers union rages on and a White House waits for official CDC guidance on safe reopenings later this week.\par \par \uc2 We will get reaction from Georgia's Governor Brian Kemp in a moment, but first to Kristina Partsinevelos on this ongoing national drama -- Kristina.\par \par \uc2 KRISTINA PARTSINEVELOS, FOX NEWS BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, you have school closures across the country and a lack of in person learning that has become -- quote -- "a national emergency," according to President Biden in an interview yesterday before the Super Bowl. But the problem that we're seeing across the country is that it varies so widely in regards to how schools are reopening.\par \par \uc2 Take, for example, this map that we have. We can see that there's four states right now that have mandatory school reopenings. Then you have got six states that have partial closure orders, and then the rest of the states are leaving it up to the local districts to decide on the reopening process.\par \par \uc2 And the nation's most powerful teachers union leader told The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  that teachers need more money and more time, saying -- quote -- "I'm confident that we will overcome the fear, but it's not going to happen in two-and-a-half nanoseconds."\par \par \uc2 And this statement comes even though the Biden administration did make a promise to reopen schools within 100 days of his term. But we know that process is a little bit tough.\par \par \uc2 In Chicago, for example, the teachers union did agreed to a tentative agreement to reopen schools, but K-8 students will not return until at least early March.\par \par \uc2 Then you have, in Philadelphia, the local district over there threatened not to reenter schools this week. Here in New York, Mayor \uc2 de Blasio\uc2  did make a big statement that middle schools could reopen on February 25, an important step for over 60,000 city students.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO\uc2  (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK: Our kids really benefit emotionally, intellectually, and even in terms of their physical health, getting out to school, being in the school community, being somewhere where there's caring adults who can help them out in so many ways.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of kids have not done well with the isolation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PARTSINEVELOS: That plan, though, is for middle schools. There's still no plan yet for high school student students here in New York.\par \par \uc2 And so that means there are a lot of children and students left in limbo. One nonprofit study from October said that roughly three million students may not be receiving a formal education, which means either virtually or in person.\par \par \uc2 So, as students fall behind, the renewed urgency continues to improve the state of education here in the United States and get students back to classes safely -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Kristina, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Atlanta Board of Education requesting teachers be moved to the front of the vaccination line.\par \par \uc2 But the state's Republican governor is saying, while teachers are a priority, for now, they will have to wait. How come?\par \par \uc2 Well, Brian Kemp joins us now with the answer.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Just what is the answer? And, apparently, obviously, you must have stated it before, but for the audience who are not aware of this, it's a major donnybrook in your state. What are you telling these teachers?\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): Well, I wouldn't necessarily agree with you about a major donnybrook in the state.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, we'd love to be vaccinating teachers right now. We need more supply from the federal government. We're thankful for the little bit of good news we got over the last seven days to get some more vaccine.\par \par \uc2 But we have two million seniors that -- and first responders and other folks that meet the current qualifications here in Georgia, so we still have a long way to go to get those vaccinated. Obviously, if there's 65- year-old individuals that are in our school systems, they're eligible to get vaccinating -- vaccinated now.\par \par \uc2 And we'd love to do teachers. But we're really following the data and the science and trying to protect the most vulnerable to the virus. And that's what Dr. Toomey...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: That's the advice that she's given me as well.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, with all due respect, I see that's what you call phase one 1- A+, the two million folks; 1.3 million are seniors. But the teachers are saying, hey, we're nervous, we're very anxious.\par \par \uc2 And there is a big fuss, particularly after this apparent raid in Elbert County, where 470 shots were confiscated from a medical center that had been giving them out to teachers. So, there's something going on in your state with respect to the teachers.\par \par \uc2 Why -- why don't you believe they are accepting in your answer to why they have to wait in line?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, there's certainly something going on in Georgia, Charles.\par \par \uc2 We have had teachers and students in person in the classroom since August and the first part of September in all parts of our state. So, the data says that that's a safe place to be if you do it the right way. We have supported our schools in doing that. We have given out 8.3 million pieces of PPE.\par \par \uc2 We have given out cleaning supplies and other things to help our schools safely reopen. But we left it up to them, complete local control. So, there's a lot of teachers that have been in the classroom for months and months now. Much like we did our reopening plan in Georgia, we followed the guidance, we followed the data.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: We put in good regulations to be able to do that. And it's worked for our economy and it's working for our schools.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What about the caregivers for the elderly? Where do they fall in? Are they in this 1-A or the 1-B part of this program?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, certainly, the caregivers in nursing homes, skilled nursing facilities, long-term care facilities and other places, they're being taken care of through Operation Warp Speed.\par \par \uc2 It is a little bit disappointing...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I'm just saying, I'm taking care of my grandmother. Someone's taking care of their grandmother. What does that -- where do they fall in the process?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, caregivers and those are eligible in that process.\par \par \uc2 Look, we want to expand. I would love to do that now. But we have got to have more vaccine to do that. We're running 10 points above the national average for doses given. Hopefully, we will be able to expand soon, when we get \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  board, because, look, that's what we want to do.\par \par \uc2 We want to get everybody vaccinated. But we're following the guidance and the public health data right now. I mean, the concern for me, Charles, is making sure that we have a hospital bed for everyone in this state that has a COVID situation or non-COVID.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: And we know that 65-year-olds are more adversely affected by COVID than those that are 45 or 50 or the teaching age.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: It's not that I don't want to do it, but we're really following the advice of public health officials.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Governor Kemp, appreciate your time. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, ahead of the impeachment trial tomorrow, we know Democrats are against former President Trump. But Jonathan Turley says one of their arguments goes against every American's legal rights. And he's going to explain.\par \par \uc2 Plus, big labor now worried about big job losses from President Biden's pipeline decision. Mike Rowe is here on that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Jonathan Turley on tomorrow's impeachment trial, Mike Rowe on big labor's beef with President Biden, and the polar vortex that's about to hit us all with the coldest air in years.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Moments ago, Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer laying out the rules for the Trump impeachment trial which kicks off tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill with the very latest -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Well, here is the bipartisan agreement. This is worked out by Senate Democrats and Republicans, the House impeachment managers -- they're the prosecutors in this case -- and also the president's defense counsel.\par \par \uc2 It all starts around 1:00 Eastern time Tuesday afternoon. And this is the framework, 16 hours for House managers to make their case, 16 hours for the president's defense counsel. And this is spread out over two days. It is then up to the managers to request witnesses.\par \par \uc2 Later, there will be equal time for senators questions. They are submitted in writing, then closing arguments and actual Senate deliberations. Remember, it takes two-thirds to convict in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, the Senate travel go dark for the Jewish Sabbath. That's at sundown Friday night, at the request of the president's counsel, David Schoen. He is an Orthodox Jew. The trial would resume on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 What we don't know right now is whether or not there would be a motion to dismiss. That's something we have heard a little bit of chatter about. That would take just 51 votes. But this is the framework, and it starts tomorrow afternoon at 1:00 Eastern time -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Chad, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now, ahead of the impeachment trial's start tomorrow, to preview today, lead impeachment manager, Maryland Democrat \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , says Trump's refusal to testify -- quote -- "supports his guilt."\par \par \uc2 Well, my next guest says that statement trashes a core constitutional principle.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Turley is a George Washington University law professor and FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 And he's here to explain.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we did reach out to Congressman Raskin, and we are still awaiting a response.\par \par \uc2 Professor Turley, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, I would not count Congressman Raskin's silence on this issue against him, because that's an important value.\par \par \uc2 And putting that aside, this is a constitutional process. And our constitutional values should not be a stranger to this trial. One of those core values is that you not only have -- you're not only allowed to decline testimony, but, generally, that is not used against you.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's true this isn't a constitutional -- I'm sorry -- this isn't a criminal case. But these are important values that define us. It's also, by the way, grossly unfair. Presidents historically have not testified. There's various reasons for that.\par \par \uc2 But, in addition to those reasons, this president contests the constitutionality of this trial. So, all those reasons are rather obvious for not appearing for testimony. So what the House is saying is that we're just going to ignore all that and argue that this is -- can be used as evidence of your guilt.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  didn't personally testify at his Senate trial. It's a really obnoxious argument to make, particularly in these circumstances. And, Charles, one of the really curious aspects of this is that the House is making this argument, when it has done nothing to lock in testimony that might show the president's intent or give us more information.\par \par \uc2 After holding a snap impeachment, which I have been very critical of, they have allowed weeks to go by. They haven't locked in the testimony of a dozen people who've spoken publicly who witnessed and spoke to the president during this period.\par \par \uc2 They could have just locked in that testimony in the House. They could have created the record that's missing over the last few weeks. But, instead, they're saying that they're going to use the this silence of the president against him.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Now, you also wrote that the House Dems, the most grievous constitutional crime that they're suggesting, that they're saying this is the most grievous constitutional crime ever committed by a president, and yet there's no declaration of an incitement.\par \par \uc2 There's no deliberate deliberation or meaning behind this. I mean, they're making these declarative statements. They're bold. Certainly, they're incendiary and provocative, but they're not any -- they're not adding any substance to it. I mean, is this how we're going to go through this, just with the sort of lobbing these sort of grenades, and nothing behind it?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, Charles, this is part of the inherent conflict in the House position.\par \par \uc2 They are insisting and many experts are insisting that the president should not be arguing free speech or the First Amendment or those cases dealing with incitement. And yet the House managers want to call this a constitutional crime. They want to call it incitement to insurrection.\par \par \uc2 They just don't want to talk about the underlying case law. Well, there is case law there.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: It's relevant. It's not controlling.\par \par \uc2 But we often refer to these cases and the crimes that are referenced, at least in part, in an article of impeachment. Those cases do not support the House position that this is actual criminal incitement. Some members have suggested it is. Many experts have.\par \par \uc2 I expect that case would likely collapse ultimately in federal court, if it was actually brought against the president.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Jonathan, I got 30 seconds, but you also wrote about our addiction to rage as a nation, citing where one neighbor was pretty upset that her Trump neighbors shoveled out her driveway.\par \par \uc2 Is all of this connected in your mind, and the impeachment, just to what's happened with this country and our addiction to rage?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: I think it is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the problem that the Democrats will face is that many of the past reckless comments on the other side will be played. And everyone's been an accessory to this -- these -- this politics of rage and division. But people don't want to admit that they're actually addicted to it. They like it.\par \par \uc2 And I think that column in The L.A. Times reflects that addiction. And that may be the saddest thing of all that will come out of this trial.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Professor Turley, thank you very much. Always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you're not already digging out, get ready for bitter cold temperatures setting in. And that battle over President Biden's action on the Keystone pipeline heating up, as big labor boss \uc2 Richard Trumka\uc2  blasts it as a job killer.\par \par \uc2 Is it a decision that the president should consider reversing?\par \par \uc2 Mike Rowe is up. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Get ready for another winter blast, as some folks are still digging out. A polar vortex is moving in and bringing in, in the coldest air and years to millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 AccuWeather\uc2 's Mark Mancuso has the latest on where the deep freeze is heading and just how cold can it get -- Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MANCUSO, ACCUWEATHER METEOROLOGIST: That's right.\par \par \uc2 So far, the polar vortex has been well-behaved, but no longer. Check this out, life below zero. These are the consecutive hours that we have been below zero in these cities. Minneapolis, we finally climbed up to zero. So we stopped the streak at 46 hours. So, what is that, anywhere from one to three days of subzero air.\par \par \uc2 There it is, the big chunk of cold air. We call this the polar vortex. That's now dropped the farthest south we have seen it this winter in actually, as you said, the past couple of years. We haven't seen cold like this.\par \par \uc2 So, here it is. This big area of purple is the arctic air. And notice right here in West Central Canada, the coldest of the year swings down through the Upper Midwest here at the end of the week. So, temperatures will relax just a little bit tomorrow, certainly no heat wave, but not as cold. This will probably be the warmest day, and then temperatures will start to drop again.\par \par \uc2 Look at this. In Chicago, we're in the teens, but at the end of the week, it gets colder. And look at Kansas City. The average is 41. We're in the teens. And by the end of the week, with that second shot of cold, highs will be down closer to zero.\par \par \uc2 And with all this weight of cold air coming down from the north, the storm track will be suppressed farther south. So that will pave the way to some light snows the Midwest to the Mid-Atlantic. Farther South, we will see some ice and rain. And for the end of the week, the next storm a little farther itself, so we could have some problems with the ICE farther south, off to the north.\par \par \uc2 It's pretty chilly. And that colder air will swipe through the Northeast before it moves out over the weekend. Looks like we will have to wait until after Valentine's Day to warm things up -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, so those Kansas City tears will turn into ice cubes.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, big labor pushing back against President Biden's cancellation of the Keystone XL pipeline.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD TRUMKA\uc2 , PRESIDENT, AFL-CIO: I wish he hadn't done that on the first day, because the Laborers' International was right. It did and will cost us jobs in the process.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you think Biden realizes that that was a mistake, that announcement?\par \par \uc2 TRUMKA: I think so, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: AFL-CIO President \uc2 Richard Trumka\uc2  speaking out against the plan to cut thousands of Americans' jobs.\par \par \uc2 The move also drawing criticism from my next guest, host of "Six Degrees With Mike Rowe" on Discovery+.\par \par \uc2 Mike Rowe, Mike, I'm sort of surprised that \uc2 Richard Trumka\uc2  is surprised. Nevertheless, he says what you have been saying for a long time. And this is painful stuff.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ROWE, "SIX DEGREES WITH MIKE ROWE": Yes, the surprise is surprising.\par \par \uc2 Far be it for me to criticize, but it is kind of interesting to see the candidate that was supported by big labor do something that is so clearly not in the interest of big labor. I guess the cognitive dissonance is interesting to ruminate upon.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I don't think -- I don't think this story, from where I'm sitting anyway, is just about the loss of jobs, which is a big deal, nor is it just about the potential loss of energy independence, which is an even bigger deal. It's about both of those things.\par \par \uc2 But, from my perspective, it's also about the incredible degree to which so many millions of Americans have become disconnected from the role of fossil fuels in their personal daily lives, not just at the pump, and not just at the thermostat inside of the house, but with everything from yoga pants, to the plastic keys on my keyboard right now.\par \par \uc2 This is -- we have affirmatively embarked, it seems, upon a larger plan, where it's OK to make energy the enemy. And I think a lot of what's playing out right now around this story are the logical symptoms of what a reasonable person might expect to happen when a country decides that there's truth in the claim that the world is going to end in 12 years.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I think they actually moved that up to nine years.\par \par \uc2 But that being said...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. You may have to change some of your vacation plans.\par \par \uc2 But, that being said, there's also an interesting twist in this, because now oil prices are starting to skyrocket. And they have gone up a lot since that executive order on Keystone. And this essential ingredient to our economic freedom that gets us to take ambulances to take people to the hospital, to provide the plastic partitions, so that teachers can go to school and teach kids, is actually going to become more expensive.\par \par \uc2 Isn't that the sort of crazy irony of all this? The transition period to when we can really get off of fossil fuels is so far away, that this is -- seems suicidal.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Well, the irony is undeniable, and it's rich.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that a lot of people I don't think really understand about the nature of energy is that there is no bigger investor, my understanding, in alternative fuels than oil and natural gas.\par \par \uc2 So, you can't make those guys the enemy, if they are our best hope of facilitating the transition that most reasonable people want. I don't know of anybody who wants to live on a dirty planet or an unclean -- in an unclean country.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: But this idea that we can flip a switch and suddenly get off of one teat and onto another, that's harebrained.\par \par \uc2 And the shows that I work on over the years, I didn't really intend to do this, but when I look back at "Dirty Jobs" and "Somebody's Gotta Do It" do it and now "Six Degrees," they're all attempts to challenge this idea that we're not connected in every imaginable way, because we are.\par \par \uc2 And we have become disconnected, unfortunately, in my view, from some of the most critical things that make us a united country, from our history, from our food, and of course, from our energy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: All of these things still matter hugely.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Mike -- Mike, I got 30 seconds, but you hit upon your new show, "Six Degrees."\par \par \uc2 Tell us how it's different from what you have done and what we should expect.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Well, it's very different from "Dirty Jobs." This is a show that does attempt to prove that everything is in fact connected.\par \par \uc2 Ironically, the lead sponsor is the energy industry. So, I'm not on your show for that reason, but it is very, very timely that this idea that we are still connected can be proven through history in a really lighthearted way. Each episode starts with a ridiculous question, like how a horseshoe can help you find your soul mate.\par \par \uc2 I get an hour to prove that it's possible. And what we learn along the way is that none of it would happen without energy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Love it.\par \par \uc2 Mike, I can't wait to watch. You have always been a favorite of everyone, including myself. So, congratulations, and we will see you again real soon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: Take care.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, will the new administration's rules mean fewer restraints on the illegal immigrations and actually more restraints on agents?\par \par \uc2 Why one guy who used to run ICE is worried about America's safety.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Abolish ICE! Abolish ICE! Abolish ICE! Abolish ICE!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Remember the calls to abolish ICE?\par \par \uc2 Well, one official telling The Washington Post that's what the Biden administration is essentially dealing with new rules that are coming. Now, they reportedly stop agents from pursuing deportations of some convicted drug criminals, DUI drivers, and those involved in low-level assaults.\par \par \uc2 Former acting ICE Director \uc2 Ron Vitiello\uc2  is worried about these changes. And he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Ron, lay them all out for us, because it just -- it's fascinating, but worrisome at the same time.\par \par \uc2 RON VITIELLO\uc2 , FORMER ACTING U.S. IMMIGRATION AND CUSTOMS ENFORCEMENT DIRECTOR: It is, Charles.\par \par \uc2 My heart goes out to the work force, because we just went through four years of a president who wanted to try and fix the border and bring integrity back to the immigration system. And now this administration is sending a signal that we will travel the globe, that we're going to go soft on border security and soft on immigration enforcement.\par \par \uc2 And so they put this memo out on the -- I guess it was on the 20th. They made exceptions for somebody who's a national security threat. They made exceptions for people who are convicted of aggravated felonies and if they just crossed the border.\par \par \uc2 So, that leaves millions of people in the United States who are criminally involved. They're in gangs. They could be accused of crimes like murder and pedophilia, rape, arson, robbery, human trafficking. Those people would still -- if they're not convicted of those crimes, even though they committed them, they wouldn't be subject to deportation under the guidelines that are be putting out.\par \par \uc2 So, we're definitely going soft on interior immigration enforcement. And let me just say, last year, ICE arrested, in the administrative sense, 103,000-plus people; 90 percent of them had criminal arrests or convictions.\par \par \uc2 And so they have already prioritized how they use their resources to protect us.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I mean, obviously, you would argue coming into the country unauthorized is illegal, in of itself, and then to be able to compound that and still not be forced or not be deported.\par \par \uc2 But I suspect that you're going to have then major cities who are going to glom onto this as reasons not to cooperate with ICE, which we saw before was an unmitigated disaster.\par \par \uc2 So, again, you started out by saying you feel badly for your former colleagues. What's going to become of their jobs, I mean, the ability to do their jobs?\par \par \uc2 VITIELLO: It's going to be very difficult.\par \par \uc2 This memo authorizes those narrow exceptions. And if they want to make an arrest outside of those exceptions, they have to get permission from the director in Washington. That's going to deteriorate the chain of command.\par \par \uc2 And so what -- the other signal that's going out here is that they don't trust the work force. You know, it's very rare for someone to get arrested in the interior United States just for being here illegally. Most of them are found by ICE because they have done something else, they're fugitives from immigration court, or they're fugitives from local jurisdictions where they have been arrested.\par \par \uc2 And that's how ICE finds some most of the people that they remove.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 VITIELLO: And now they're taking those tools away from agents. And it's not going to be good for the work force.\par \par \uc2 You have these people in the House that got elected on the abolish ICE call.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VITIELLO: And it's just -- it's not boding well for the country's security.\par \par \uc2 This is going to put more pressure on the border. People are going to recognize that we're not serious about this.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I wanted to ask you about that.\par \par \uc2 Ron, I have got less than a minute.\par \par \uc2 VITIELLO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: But I was a security policeman in the Air Force.\par \par \uc2 And the first thing I learned about anything is that deterring folks is the main mission. You can deter women and young kids from making an arduous multithousand -- trek or being victimized by mafias and coyotes. It seems to me now that all of that sort deterrent is gone. And it's going to put more people in danger.\par \par \uc2 Just your thoughts on that? And, again, I'm sorry. I have only got 30 seconds left.\par \par \uc2 VITIELLO: No, it'll be -- it will put more pressure on the border.\par \par \uc2 It'll be more difficult for the agents down there to do that job, because, like you said, the smugglers and the cartels are going to profit from the flow that comes up this pipeline. And the people who are in the pipeline, the smuggled aliens, they're not in good shape either.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VITIELLO: These smugglers leave them out all over the terrain in terrible weather. It's dangerous for them as well.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, we have heard the stories.\par \par \uc2 Ron Vitiello\uc2 , thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we called \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  for an interview. And we are still right now waiting to hear back.\par \par \uc2 Up next: There's a new push and Texas to crack down on social media companies from cracking down on free speech. How will it work and do other states need to do the same?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Bitcoin is definitely not a bit player, surging again today on news that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's Tesla bought $1.5 billion worth of the cryptocurrency. The electric carmaker planning to accept Bitcoin for future payments.\par \par \uc2 We will, of course, continue to watch the story as it rapidly develops.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the free speech battle taking center stage in Texas. Republican Governor Greg Abbott pushing for a bill that would prevent social media platforms like \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  from canceling conservative speech.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from attorney Leeza Garber. By the way, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  had no comment for us on this story. And we're still waiting to hear back from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Leeza, what do you make of it? We're seeing these states now take a stand.\par \par \uc2 LEEZA GARBER, ATTORNEY: Hi, Charles. So good to see you. It's been too long.\par \par \uc2 And Texas' approach right now is a creative one. It's certainly something that other states are looking into, this kind of proactive legislation to look into how \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and other social media platforms are moderating their users and moderating content.\par \par \uc2 It all comes back to Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act, which is a piece of legislation that's now almost 30 years old. And it provides this broad shield against liability for having the social media platform to be able to moderate as they see fit and not be held liable for what users post.\par \par \uc2 The problem is -- and this is something we see often in technology -- the law simply doesn't move as fast as the technology does. So it's lagging behind. And that's why states are looking to pick up what it's leaving behind. And it's also President Biden is also saying there needs to be changes. Pelosi has said there needs to be changes. So it's coming from many, many areas.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, and, obviously, 30 years ago, these laws could not anticipate the sort of reach and power. These have -- companies have become de facto the public square. And it's -- everyone agrees it's time to do something.\par \par \uc2 And yet isn't it ironic that, more recently, it feels like they have actually flaunted their disdain from conservatives? No longer trying to hide it, no longer is saying the right things in press releases. They have taken a lot of people off the platform. \uc2 Google\uc2  last week removed 100,000 people who had negative reviews of Robinhood.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they're taking it upon themselves to actively police their platforms, and not they're policing it in favor of conservatives.\par \par \uc2 GARBER: The problem is inherently, with the legislation that exists, these platforms can do as they see fit. They are allowed to create their own rules and they govern themselves.\par \par \uc2 What's interesting is that Zuckerberg of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  said as recently as early this month that they want rules, they want better legislation, they want to have less responsibility in terms of dictating what they're supposed to do in terms of moderation.\par \par \uc2 But, so far, we're just seeing piecemeal steps. We have seen broad claims that Section 230 helped create the Internet almost 30 years ago, with 26 little words, because it provided this freedom.\par \par \uc2 But now we see major political figures really pushing for better legislation, more specific legislation to take into account the billions of users that are on these platforms.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, but I think we all have to be careful. After Congress said they would take steps against banks on Wall Street, what we saw were no banks. We went years without any new banks. Same thing with oil. The only ones who are going to win are big oil.\par \par \uc2 Whenever they take these steps, they, ironically, build the moat against these powerful companies, and they have no true competition.\par \par \uc2 What -- is there a legal argument that says, OK, Parler can't compete, certainly doesn't have the bandwidth to compete? There's no true alternatives. Should that create a greater sense of urgency for lawmakers to take some action here?\par \par \uc2 GARBER: There should be a greater sense of urgency because of all these issues that have come up all at once.\par \par \uc2 You see the deplatforming of major figures. You see questions of free speech, even though free speech doesn't really apply, because these are private platforms, private companies. But, in general, definitely, there should be a push for a review of this legislation, because we have to remember there are simply mere mortals behind the moderation of these platforms.\par \par \uc2 We have lawyers. We have H.R. executives. But why are they the ones that are allowed to say who's allowed to do what on these platforms? So, we need better legislation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Well, we say the plot tickets. We will see, Leeza.\par \par \uc2 By the way, folks, Neil is back tomorrow. You can catch me on FOX Business.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210208eh2800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden's Job-Killing Agenda Takes Aim at American Oil and Gas Workers; Democrats Launch Another Round of Impeachment Theater; U.S. House Strips \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  of Committee Assignments; Interview with Rep. Jim Jordan (R-OH) and \uc2 Rep. Matt Gaetz\uc2  (R-FL); Interview with \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 ; Interview with Rep. Nancy Mace (R-SC); Interview with \uc2 Sen. Josh Hawley\uc2  (R- MO)\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Dan Bongino \par \uc2 8,180 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST, "TUCKER CLARKSON TONIGHT": -- in this moment. I hope that moment doesn't last long, but I guess that is what we are.\par \par \uc2 We'll be back, 8:00 p.m. every weeknight. The show that is the sworn enemy of lying, pomposity, smugness, and groupthink.\par \par \uc2 And so, at 9:00 p.m. Eastern, the great \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  takes over from New York.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: All right. Tucker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And welcome to "Hannity."\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Democrats -- they've been in charge of Congress, the White House, for just 16 days and as predicted, their own vindictive radical extreme agenda is now taking center stage in that swamp.\par \par \uc2 As we speak, the Biden the administration now is quickly implementing AOC's insane Green New Deal agenda, destroying tens of thousands of high-paying career jobs in the process, all with the stroke of a pen, signing now 47 executive orders and actions, which is over two and a half times more than Obama, Bush, and Clinton combined.\par \par \uc2 Now, meanwhile, next week, the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  is set to hold another pointless, vicious, unconstitutional impeachment Schiff show. It serves no purpose. The outcome is predetermined other than to vent their rage, and to smear President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in exile one last time.\par \par \uc2 And in the House, Democrats are waging a campaign against an obscure Republican freshman lawmaker who has been on the job for one month and one day.\par \par \uc2 Joe, by the way, if you're still awake, if you're still with us now, you are leading a country with great unity. Got to give you applause for that.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, during a House-wide vote, Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  was stripped of all committee assignments because of things that she has said, some conspiracy theories we talked about last night that she floated before getting elected.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats went ahead with their punitive measures even though Congresswoman Taylor Greene has now disavowed about her previous beliefs and conduct during an unscripted speech on the House floor today. Let's take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): When I started finding misinformation, lies, things that were not true in these QAnon posts, I stopped believing it. And I want to tell you, any source -- and I say this to everyone -- any source of information that is a mix of truth and a mix of lies is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 You see school shootings are absolutely real. And every child that has lost, those families mourn it.\par \par \uc2 I also want to tell you that 9/11 absolutely happened. I remember that day crying all day long watching it on the news. And it's a tragedy for anyone to say it didn't happen. And so, that, I definitely want to tell you that I do not believe that it's fake.\par \par \uc2 These were words of the past and these things do not represent me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm glad the congresswoman delivered that speech. But ultimately for Democrats, this is not at all about \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , a new congresswoman. Instead, it's a mere political ploy. The idea, they want to smear all conservatives, all Republicans as extreme conspiracy theorists. And almost every single sanctimonious Democrat that voted today, they are all guilty of the biggest hoax, the biggest conspiracy, the lies that they have told for three straight years.\par \par \uc2 That's right, the big Trump Russia collusion lie. They have put this country through -- for three years and everything they told the American people was false.\par \par \uc2 Now working in support of the big lie, they have their allies, their press office. We referred to them as the media mob. And listening to these lying Trump Russia collusion truther's running nonstop wall-to-wall coverage of the \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , it's pretty sickening.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , she apologized. She said that her beliefs were wrong, misguided. She doesn't have them today. So, my question is when the Democrats going to follow suit? When will the media mob apologized and correct the record on so many stories they all got wrong?\par \par \uc2 Here is my prediction. Hell, you know, down there will freeze over. First, don't hold your breath. They are feigning their phony outrage as usual and playacting, like this is the most important story of our time.\par \par \uc2 It's not. Congresswoman Greene, she doesn't set the agenda for the Republican Party, the way the radical squad led by the real speaker of the house, Congresswoman \uc2 Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  does for the 