raumatic and harrowing day. It was a dark day in our nation's history.\par \par \uc2 But we have to be truthful when we share our stories and experiences because we want everyone to understand what we went through and believe us when we talk about it. But when you exaggerate it and are dishonest about how you represent what actually happened, it waters down everyone's trauma.\par \par \uc2 I've been very vocal about the experiences of that day.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, it's a big difference if -- if they were outside your door and basically saying they were coming for her, you know?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And they are out there shouting, where is she? Where issue?\par \par \uc2 And if they are not in the building and they're not in the hall and the police are in the hall, and I'm sure the police were calmly guiding everybody and protecting everybody in that process. Not anything anyone wants to go through.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACE: No.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But certainly, the story she is telling then, you're basically saying is absolutely not true.\par \par \uc2 MACE: It's not true. And I simply made that statement of fact earlier today. And she literally lost it on her favorite social media top platform, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so, I know I'm doing the right thing, doing the right job when I've got members of the Squad coming after me all day long on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And, you know, as the old adage goes, Sean, judge a woman by the enemy she keeps. And I'm totally fine with that.\par \par \uc2 This was a terrible and awful experience, but we have to separate truth and facts from fiction when we -- when we share our experiences about this. It's very important to make that very clear in this instance.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I want -- you know, like every American, I look at what happened, the breach. We knew hundreds of thousands of people were in town. We since learned the capitol police requested troops even before the events. Six separate occasions they asked for troops.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, we are finding out that there was intelligence and investigators are now finding out that there were people that preplanned a lot of -- a lot of this and I don't see anybody really uniting behind the idea of a 9/11-style commission to make sure this never happens again to our country.\par \par \uc2 MACE: There should be an investigation. There should be hearings. We should know what happened and when it happened.\par \par \uc2 I sent my children home. I'm a single working mom with two kids in middle school. I flew them up for my swearing in. I was excited to have them there that week, but someone threatened to shoot me, a Republican threatened to shoot me on social media in the days leading up to the events of January 6th.\par \par \uc2 And I send my kids home early. I got home the first flight home Monday morning because I was worried and if I was worried without having a lot to go other than the rhetoric I was seeing on social media and online and coming out of different voices online, then, you know, I would hope that our law enforcement monitoring the situation might have better, clearer information.\par \par \uc2 And I remember at one point, I was stuck in the tunnels under the capitol and a red two weeks ago that there were rioters that knew that people were stuck in the tunnel under the capitol at one point during the riots, and they were trying to find a way to get down there and find us.\par \par \uc2 And I remember during all of this, I was standing right next to Congressman Dan Crenshaw and thinking, if anything happens, I'm going to be safe because I had this military war hero who I admire and respect. He's going to save everybody in that tunnel.\par \par \uc2 But this was a traumatic experience and it's not okay to exaggerate or politicize it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me just say this. I agree with you about politicizing something but I will say this, too. We have to protect our institutions.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: How this can happen in a post-9/11 United States is inexplicable to me. We've got to fix it and we've got to protect all elected officials. This isn't about Republican, Democrat, conservative, liberal. We've got to protect elected officials, period.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Thank you for sharing your story.\par \par \uc2 MACE: And we've got to protect our police. AOC disparaged our Capitol Hill police earlier this week. And if folks want to learn more, it's nancymace.org.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for having me on tonight, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, thank you, Nancy, and you're welcome back anytime. And I assume you'll be attacked by the mob and the media any second now.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up, zero experience Hunter Biden, well, he continues to profit off of his father's name. He just found a new way to do it. There are no oligarchs involved, no Chinese nationals, no Bank of China, no \uc2 Burisma\uc2  -- but he figured out a way. We'll explain.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Believe it or not, Hunter Biden has an upcoming memoir. It's called -- coming out. It's called "Beautiful Things." The book expected to focus on Hunter's relationship with his father, as well as chronicle his struggles with substance abuse.\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, the book is being published by Gallery Books, which is part of a publishing giant, \uc2 Simon and Schuster\uc2 . Full disclosure, they published my last book, only one published book of mine, the same publisher that axed their deal with Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  amid his post to restore election integrity.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now with reaction, author of "The Tyranny of Big Tech," Missouri Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I hope they gave you a big -- I hope they gave you a big advance and you don't give it back. I'm not sure if that's part of the equation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): Well, you know, Sean, this is just a classic, left-wing hypocrisy. What we are seeing here is woke capital. They have an agenda. They want to push their cronies. They want to push their ideological agenda and they want to silence and punish conservatives.\par \par \uc2 And they tried to do it with me although I'm not going to be silenced. But you and I both know they are trying to do it across the board, on social media. They're trying to go after small business people who donated to Republicans. It's unbelievable and we just have to stand up and say no.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They're going to be putting on this big loud show next week with a predetermined outcome. First, do you agree it's predetermined?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: It is predetermined. Yeah, impeachment -- I mean, listen, I can't imagine that there's any way that the Democrats get to the numbers that they need.\par \par \uc2 It is totally unconstitutional. It is a sham trial and it's a disgrace to have the Senate even going through with this.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay. Now, I'm glad you did get another publishing deal. I believe you got it with Regnery, and I'm glad to hear that. Marji Ross I knew for a long time. She has since left the company but was there a long time.\par \par \uc2 So, what bothers me in all of this is they are choosing sides here. And that's a problem for me. And if big tech, for example, they never censor. They never fact-check the Russia collusion we know to be lies and conspiracy theories, but they are silencing conservatives left and right.\par \par \uc2 And then big tech companies join forces to shut down a group like Parler and shut down the president of the United States and silence the -- the story of "The New York Post" about Hunter Biden and the laptop. And there is now a full on criminal investigation into him.\par \par \uc2 All I know, Senator, is there any criminal investigation into you right now going on? Are you a target of an investigation?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Not that I know of, Sean, and this goes back to the double standard. I mean, the woke capitalists, the corporate media, they will push their ideological line. You mentioned the Russia hoax, pushed it for years. It turns out that there was no collusion. There were no crimes committed. It's just absolutely unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 And now, with Hunter Biden, the guy who is under investigation for numerous potential crimes, including money laundering, potentially connected to his father. And yet, the woke media celebrates him with what they are trying to punish conservatives.\par \par \uc2 I'll just go back to the fact this will only succeed if conservatives agreed to go along with it, if we agree to be silenced, and I for one I'm not going to be silenced.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So, the person that is leading the case in the -- from the House is a guy that actually challenged the 2016 election results. Doesn't that seem a little -- a little bit of irony there?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Well, it's pretty amazing, Sean, that these Democrats who objected to states after Republicans won the election in 2001, and 2005, and 2017, now they said that if Republicans do that, if Republicans do the same thing, they raise specific objections. Oh, then, they're seditious. Then, they're traitors. Then, they are criminals.\par \par \uc2 It's total hypocrisy. It's a total power grab. They know it's a power grab. That's why they are doing it.\par \par \uc2 And, again, we've just got to stand up and expose that life for what it is.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Has there been any other push back? I know they were talking about, oh, we've got to remove Senator Cruz and Senator Hawley.\par \par \uc2 You were asking for an audit. It's all it was, an election audit, something that you have the right to do. Any other consequences that you have faced as a result beyond that which we know?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Well, the Democrats, as you say, they wanted us to be stripped of this or that, expelled some of them said. But I can tell you, Sean, I'm not going anywhere. I'm not going to bow down to the woke mob.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And those that speculated you were doing this to run for president in 2024. You said, no, you're not?\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: I'm not -- I'm not doing this to do anything other than my job, which is represent the people of Missouri. And I was representing their voice on January 6th, Sean, and I'm going to keep doing it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know what? If we have more senators like yourself that take strong stance and fight for their beliefs, and also stand up for this very important principle of liberty, freedom, freedom of speech, we'll be better off.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Good luck. We're looking forward to seeing the book.\par \par \uc2 Now, coming up, Democrats are coming for your Second Amendment rights and it's serious this time. We'll get reaction. \uc2 Dana Loesch\uc2  speaks out, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We're also learning tonight that the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's January gun background checks statistics show people buying firearms in what experts are calling a blistering pace where Americans apparently very wary of Biden's lack of reverence of the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 For example, a new Democratic bill reportedly would force all gun owners to go through psychological evaluations. Let me examine you from the government I'm here. Along with your family members and furthermore, the legislation introduced by Congresswoman Sheila Jackson would dramatically expand the Justice Department's ability to surveil legal gun owners and raise the age to owning a firearm to 21 years old.\par \par \uc2 Here with the reaction, syndicated radio talk show host \uc2 Dana Loesch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dana, I also read that they will talk to and ask for a psychological evaluation of the person's ex if they have a divorced spouse?\par \par \uc2 DANA LOESCH\uc2 , NATIONALLY SYNDICATED TALK RADIO HOST: Yeah, Sean, good to see you again. Thanks for having me. You're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm sure the spouse will have wonderful things -- yeah.\par \par \uc2 LOESCH: I know, right? And not just the spouse, because the way that HR-127 is written, Sean, is it even includes associates. So, not just your family members but associates also, which I'm sure you know in this era of our parents (ph), it's going to go so well when people call the cops over somebody not wearing a mask in their backyard.\par \par \uc2 But I think, honestly, the people who should have the psych evals are the people in Congress who proposed this type of legislation. Those are the people who need any kind of psychiatric evaluation before they do anything, or members of congress. Not law-abiding Americans.\par \par \uc2 Now, if we're going to apply this precedent to the Second Amendment, are we also now going to establish a pattern to apply it to every single right? The Second Amendment is not the second class right, and this bill is bonkers.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Are you or have you ever been a supporter of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Is that going to be one of the psychological profile questions by your government psychologist?\par \par \uc2 LOESCH: Right. Yeah, I can only imagine some of the questions they will ask.\par \par \uc2 And the other troubling part of this is that this is going to -- I mean, in terms of how this is going to be determined, what is the criteria to determine whether or not someone I guess mental health renders them ineligible to purchase or carry is entirely left up to the A.G. It's not actually medical professional, Sean, that are determining the criteria, at least according to the language of the bill.\par \par \uc2 But it's actually a bureaucrat -- it's actually a lawmaker, a politician. It's the A.G. that's going to be determining that. So, that's kind of scary. I mean, it kind of reminds me under the Obama-Biden administration when they were determining that anyone who is a social security beneficiary, and this includes veterans too, that simply needed help with their finances, was suddenly ineligible to carry a firearm because they needed the assistance.\par \par \uc2 And they decided this. It was bureaucrats who determined it without any aid of any mental health professional. So, this is -- this just compromises so many rights.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. By the way, you know, I always ask people, Dana, OK, God forbid, a bad person breaks into your house, business, wherever, what are you going to do? I know what I will do. What will they do?\par \par \uc2 All right. I got to make a comment. I think you're the guest that gets the best score ever in the history of the show for their own background. Is that your own studio? It is?\par \par \uc2 LOESCH: It's my own studio in-house, God bless America. Yeah, and my husband --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No, look straight. No --\par \par \uc2 LOESCH: -- and friends actually help to did this. So, hat tip to Chris.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Everybody's doing this kind of zoom appearances and, you know, that's a great background. So, congratulations. Dana, great to have you back.\par \par \uc2 LOESCH: Well, thank you. I want to feel like I'm -- I want to feel like I'm surrounded by patriotism.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. All right.\par \par \uc2 LOESCH: I just need the eagle.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. More "Hannity" after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Unfortunately, that is all the time we have left this evening. We hope you'll set your DVR so you never miss an episode. We hope you'll join us. Get ready for next week, because they're going to go nuts. And we'll be here to chronicle all of it and we will fight back as they say they fight back.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, let not your heart be troubled. We're always be independent. We're not the media mob.\par \par \uc2 And Laura Ingraham is standing by to take over right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may  not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of  the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020210205eh2400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Republican Infighting; Biden Holding Firm on COVID-19 Relief Bill; Minimum Wage Debate; Interview With \uc2 Rep. Henry Cuellar\uc2  (D-TX); Interview With Walgreens Vice President Rina Shah\b0\par\par\uc2 Adam Lashinsky, David Spunt, Karl Rove, John Layfield, Chad  Pergram, Gary Kaltbaum, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,085 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: What did you say? You will get some Republican support?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we'll get some Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, the president of the United States is confident he is going to get some Republican support.\par \par \uc2 He's not so sure or hinting who that support might be. But it could explain what some have been saying is a pretty cavalier attitude on the part of the White House of getting $1.9 trillion stimulus through with bipartisan support. They must be sensing they have at least one or two Republican senators behind them, even in the face of one Democratic senator who might not be with them.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. This is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 Those are the numbers, $1.9 trillion. That is what Democrats want in that COVID stimulus measure. It is more than double of what is being planned on the part of Republicans, who want to leave out some things, like a hike in the minimum wage and help to cities and states that now have a lot of people wondering, all right, how is this going to eventually get done?\par \par \uc2 We do know as well that talks have been going on behind the scenes to get at least a couple of Republicans on board. But the fact that the president of the United States seems to be saying he could have that and might already have that, again, does explain why they are sticking to that $1.9 trillion number.\par \par \uc2 We're going to explore that, because, separately, Republicans are meeting amongst themselves to discuss what's going to happen to \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , as far as her future in leadership among the Republicans in Congress, and \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  on some of the conspiracy theories that could land her out of some committee assignments that she has had.\par \par \uc2 So, let's get right to this right now with the stimulus measure and where things stand at this point and that $1.9 trillion figure you hear so much about.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt following it closely from the White House.\par \par \uc2 Hey, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 A lot of deal-making going on here at the White House and on Capitol Hill. After all, the president is a creature of the Senate. He spent 36 years walking the halls of Congress. He has always touted his relationships with not only Democrats, but Republicans. The White House says he is eager to work with Republicans. He wants to work across the aisle.\par \par \uc2 But the clock is ticking, and it's ticking loudly. This afternoon, he met with Democrats in the Oval Office -- you see him right there -- to discuss aspects of that nearly $2 trillion -- with a T. -- package.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Senate Democrats voted to move forward with crafting the bill with a vote of 50 to 49.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHARLES SCHUMER (D-NY): We cannot dawdle, we cannot delay, we cannot dilute, because the troubles that this nation has and the opportunities that we can bring them are so large. So, we're going to all work together with this president. We are united as one for a big, bold package, working with our Republican friends when we can.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: But Republicans say it's not that easy. They are upset because Democrats moved forward without them during what's called budget reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 Monday night, the president met with Republican senators in the Oval Office. Now, while both sides admit that they had a productive conversation Monday evening, they did not reach a deal, which, Neil, is to be expected. After all, you're talking about $2 trillion, nearly, and hashing out a package in few hours is difficult to do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY): Senate Democrats plowed ahead with a party- line vote to set the table for a partisan jam. The new president talks a lot about unity, but his White House staff and congressional leadership are working with a different playbook.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Republicans, Neil, are proposing $600 billion, nearly a third of the Democrats' package.\par \par \uc2 A close aide to President Biden, a confidant, Senator Chris Coons from Delaware, he was at the White House. He came out to the cameras. He said it's not a matter of months, but a matter of weeks to get a package done -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, David, thank you very much, my friend David Spunt at the White House.\par \par \uc2 So, what was behind that meeting that President Biden did have with those prominent Republicans? Was it all for show? Well, you wouldn't be excused here if you thought, wait a minute, I think I have seen this before, a little case of deja vu, back in early 2010, when then President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  met with prominent Republicans, at the time the effort to find a bipartisan agreement on health care.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove knows how that ended and suspects it might be playing out here as well.\par \par \uc2 Karl, what do you think? And is this idea of reaching out to Republicans not happening? I'm just beginning to wonder if it's really not happening, and this is all for show.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I was taken, in the aftermath of the Monday meeting, by two things.\par \par \uc2 First, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  came out and said, while there was interesting conversations, our plan has been carefully drawn and to meet the stakes of the moment, she said, and that we're not going to accept anything that doesn't meet these pressing needs, which is like saying, basically, we listened to the Republicans, but go away, we have already decided what we're going to do.\par \par \uc2 And then a senior administration official, an unnamed White House official, talked to the press and said -- and criticized the Republicans for not being in favor of a big expensive program on stimulus checks.\par \par \uc2 You may remember that the Democrats call for $465 billion in checks. You can make $300,000 a year per couple and have a bunch of kids and still get a stimulus check. The Republicans wanted $245 billion. And if you make more than $50,000, an individual, $100,000, a couple, you don't get the check.\par \par \uc2 And what the -- what the Biden senior official said was that, well they're not going to get credit when we pass -- any credit when we pass these big checks, which is sort of like bread and circuses. This is not about meeting the needs of people who are truly needy. This is about getting credit by sending people a check.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's a -- that's a faulty rationale. And if you look at the rest of the things, there are big disagreements. You could reach a middle ground. But it doesn't seem to be that the White House has any interest in doing that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There does seem to be quite a chasm between what the president says on reaching out to Republicans and his actions and executive orders that indicate quite the opposite.\par \par \uc2 But it did remind me a lot, Karl -- I know you're quite the historian yourself -- back in 2010, when his old boss \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was working on what was an attempt at bipartisanship to get Republicans on board with a number of his initiatives, including health care for all.\par \par \uc2 I want to play this bite for you and then get your reaction. This is going back about 11 years now. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN MCCAIN\uc2  (R-AZ): Treat all Americans the same under provisions of the law, so that they will know that geography does not dictate what kind of health care they would receive.\par \par \uc2 I thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let me just make this point, John, because we're not campaigning anymore. The election is over.\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: I'm reminded of that every day.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, the -- we can spend the remainder of the time with our respective talking points going back and forth. We were supposed to be talking about insurance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I remember that vividly. And I thought, say a lot about \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , but that was a snippy and undeserved comment. And it set the stage for what would be zero Republican cooperation.\par \par \uc2 And I'm wondering if we're replanting the same seeds.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Oh, yes, sounds like it.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this was the Obama/Biden administration. And that's the second time he pulled that stunt. Remember, in -- a year earlier, in February of 2009, the House Republicans come down to make recommendations to the president about the scope of a stimulus bill, and he cut off \uc2 Eric Cantor\uc2 , the Republican leader, one of the Republican leaders, by saying -- quote -- "I won," in other words, shut up.\par \par \uc2 What was interesting to me, in that picture, you saw a guy sitting next to McCain, \uc2 Mike Enzi\uc2  of Wyoming, who was one of the leaders of the Republicans on the Senate Finance Committee, and sat down and, with Chuck Grassley and others worked through the health care bill with the Democratic colleagues led by Max Baucus.\par \par \uc2 And then, when it looked like they were coming to some agreements that might require some changes in the legislation that might be supported by Democrats and Republicans, Harry Reid, at the White House direction, made a public announcement that he would not be bound by any agreements arrived at by the Republican and Democratic members of the Senate Finance Committee and killed it.\par \par \uc2 So, back then, agreement with the administration was more critical than cooperation with the administration and compromise. If you had -- compromise was defined as you compromising with your principles of voting for anything that I tell you to vote for.\par \par \uc2 And I think, unfortunately, it sounds like that's infecting the West Wing today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, back then, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  had the run of the table, with the House, the Senate and the White House under Democratic control. So does, albeit by smaller margins, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We will see. But they're pretty confident they're going to get this whole thing through their way or the highway.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, the margins matter a lot, because -- you bet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I agree. Finish that thought.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, margins matter a lot. It was 60 to 40 in the Senate. Now it's 50/50. You're better off compromising and bringing more people aboard to make it more durable and to gain the support of the American people. A straight party-line vote is not going to serve them well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, my friend, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 At the Karl's point here, Democrats are trying to see if they can get this done through a procedure called reconciliation that requires a simple majority to get their way. But, again, they do hint that they have got Republican support here. We just don't know who and how many that might be.\par \par \uc2 We will keep an eye on it.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram keeping an eye on a number of crucial meetings going on, on Capitol Hill today, particularly among Republicans, but not having anything to do with this particular matter, something else, I hear, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Internal family fights are always rough. House Republicans meeting this hour to hear from the number three in their leadership, Conference Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . Many Republicans want her stripped from her leadership post. Cheney voted to impeach former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Also, the \uc2 House Rules Committee\uc2  is preparing a resolution to kick \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  off the Education Committee. Republicans are wary of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM COLE\uc2  (R-OK): The action the majority is taking today raises questions that have nothing to do with Congresswoman Greene, but concern the institution as a whole, which is why I feel that this hearing is premature and should instead first be adjudicated by the Ethics Committee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republican Florida Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  says Greene suggesting that the 2018 Parkland shooting is a hoax is -- quote -- "disgusting."\par \par \uc2 Other Republicans question Greene too.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. KEVIN CRAMER\uc2  (R-ND): The anti-Semitism, school shootings weren't real. I mean, she runs the gamut, the idea that forest fires are started by Jewish -- Jewish lasers. I mean, it's just so profound, so far out there, that it does make me wonder how she got elected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The House will vote to oust Greene from the Education panel tomorrow. Republicans are returning the favor. They have an amendment to remove Democratic Minnesota Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  from her committee assignments.\par \par \uc2 Republicans will be on the record tomorrow voting to support Greene or throw her overboard -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Chad, see if I got this right. It takes a while to penetrate my skull.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  could be punished for voting her conscience on this impeachment thing, but \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  not on some of the crazy stuff she said. There's something upside-down about this.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That's the problem that some Republicans, when you talk to them privately, express, that exactly that very scenario will come to play here.\par \par \uc2 This is why \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , the House majority leader, was trying to get an agreement with Kevin McCarthy, the minority leader, to work something out. We're told that the suggestion was that they would try to move Greene to another committee. The Democrats' main beef here is that Greene was on the Education Committee and questioned the veracity of the school shootings at Sandy Hook and also at Parkland.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow. We live in interesting times, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Chad Pergram, thank you very, very much for that.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, no sooner does she get approved as the Treasury secretary of the United States than \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  is looking into \uc2 GameStop\uc2  and a host of other players. And she hopes to rally around all the top economic officials. When all is said and done, that will include chairman of the Federal Reserve, the head of the SEC, all these other various committees with him she's been chatting to find out, all right, what do we do about this?\par \par \uc2 Better question might be, why do anything about this?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: She believes the integrity of markets is important and has asked for a discussion of recent volatility in financial markets and whether recent activities are consistent with investor protection and fair and efficient markets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that is the administration's way of saying we have got our eye on the ball here.\par \par \uc2 But what you don't know is whether Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , when she does talk to heads of the various money agencies, all the way from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 , the \uc2 Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2 , you name it, will they come up with a plan to beat down what has been an epic battle royal between those who want to short stocks, in other words, pound them lower, and those who counter their moves by buying them like crazy to undo what the guys who were shorting the stocks were doing.\par \par \uc2 A lot of this is all perfectly legal and fine.\par \par \uc2 What worries Gary Kaltbaum is whether the government could make things worse by sticking its nose into things.\par \par \uc2 Gary, that's your biggest concern, right?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 The first concern is the people that have taken rates down to zero percent distorting price and yield are the ones that are talking about how they need to have efficient markets. That's, for me, a little bit of a comedy act.\par \par \uc2 But I just think, if they do too much, that would not be good for markets. At this point in time, the markets are already in the trees. And I hate to put it this way, but it almost has worked out the way it should. People who decided to buy \uc2 GameStop\uc2  at 480 bucks, guess what, when it was 20 bucks, you're going to lose money.\par \par \uc2 The people that decided to short a stock that was 150 percent short of the float, any type of movement up will cause short squeezes. And you saw it not just in \uc2 GameStop\uc2 . It's others. So, it's actually worked itself out. A lot of things are coming down to earth again. Hopefully, people learn a very big lesson from what just happened and keep their feet on the ground.\par \par \uc2 I just hope they're on the outside looking in. If there was collusion, if they find chicanery, do something about it, but, absent that, I think let be and let the markets be.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and to be fair to the Treasury secretary, that's what she's trying to find out.\par \par \uc2 But, having said that, I'm just wondering, there's a new cottage industry evolving, Gary, where people are saying, all right, I missed out of the Teslas, I missed out on the Amazons and the Apples, let me scour this new list of undervalued possibilities, like all of these stocks that are shorted, and that that's what got the attention on the \uc2 GameStop\uc2  and the AMCs, even \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , that they had been heavily shorted.\par \par \uc2 They were heavily discounted. And these guys tried to counter that. And I keep seeing it apply to other areas. There's a whole sphere looking at the biotech and drug industry and battered-down issues there.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering if this devil may care, squeeze the lemon for all the juice you can get out of it, if this is just going to switch from sector to sector, stock to stock, and it could get reckless?\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: Markets are about investors and traders looking at valuations, but also looking at, oh, look at this, there's a 100 percent short on this and the stock is acting well and the group's acting well, I'm going to get on it. And that's what you have seen.\par \par \uc2 And, again, we saw \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2  skyrocket, \uc2 BlackBerry\uc2  skyrocket, some biotechs, all types of industry. Yes, I do believe this is a cottage industry right now, but they all better learn a lesson, because, in the end, no matter what, valuation will matter, and these stocks will go back to where they came from.\par \par \uc2 Tootsie Roll is now back down lower than where it was before they squeezed it for like 90 percent to the upside. So, buyer beware, be very, very careful, because when the music stops, there will not be any chairs left, and they will -- I think \uc2 GameStop\uc2  will go back to $20. So just be very, very careful, best advice I can give.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Gary, thank you very much, Gary Kaltbaum.\par \par \uc2 We will be following all of that.\par \par \uc2 We should alert you too, as well, that governments can sometimes intervene to a severe degree. In South Korea, by the way, they have continued the freeze on all short selling, in other words, all betting on stocks tanking or going down, to control that market. It happens, and it's happened periodically in ours.\par \par \uc2 Be careful what you wish for.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, everything was going just dandy with \uc2 Amazon\uc2  trading today, but late in the day, it seemed like traders were reassessing, wait a minute, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  isn't going to be running this company day to day, because, in the end, though a very rich stock, sliding 2 percent, people are asking, what happens now?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Adjustments could be tough. They are saying that now about \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  after stepping down from running \uc2 Amazon\uc2  day to day. They said the same about what would happen to \uc2 Apple\uc2  after \uc2 Steve Jobs\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  after \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What did they say?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  becoming the first e-tailer to serve 10 million cyber- shoppers. CEO \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  marking the occasion today by hand-delivering himself the lucky customer's order, a set of antique golf clubs.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JEFF BEZOS\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, PRESIDENT & CEO, AMAZON.COM: It's great to be here. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It must have been pretty neat for that guy to have the big cheese bring him the stuff.\par \par \uc2 BEZOS: I think it was more fun for me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes?\par \par \uc2 BEZOS: So, yes, four years ago, I was delivering all the packages to the post office in my Chevy Blazer. So, it's a big change.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BEZOS: We're extremely focused, we're focused on--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Focused? You're -- you're selling everything known to man.\par \par \uc2 BEZOS: We're focused on e-commerce. And what we're trying to do is build a place where people can come to find and discover anything that they might want to buy online.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All these people who short your stock, hoping that it goes down, they have got your head on a platter. They're waiting just to smash you like a bug.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 BEZOS: Well, I think that that's very normal. We're a company that I think has a complicated investment story.\par \par \uc2 We're doing a great job for customers. We really believe in our long-term future. But we have got tons of supporters and we have got tons of critics. And for a pioneering company like us, a company it really is blazing new paths, I think that's the way it should be.\par \par \uc2 But we have always had lots of supporters. And we have always had lots of critics. And the good news is even our critics have been our customers.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Do you use your site to shop--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BEZOS: I -- in fact, some people would accuse me of having kind of an addiction. So, yes, I have got boxes coming every day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A year from now, will you be a profitable company?\par \par \uc2 BEZOS: You know we don't speculate on it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That memorable laugh.\par \par \uc2 I have had the pleasure in this job to talk to a lot of big cheeses and CEOs and the like, and some visionaries that changed history. I would certainly put \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  on the list of a half a handful of favorites that changed the world, changed the way we do things, and with the A-plus attitude to go with it.\par \par \uc2 That laugh. I always loved that laugh. And now he's stepping down from running the company on a day-to-day basis. He's not leaving the company, but it does unnerve a lot of folks, wondering, without the maestro, are you still going to get the same music?\par \par \uc2 John Layfield joins us right now, as does Adam Lashinsky and Danielle DiMartino Booth.\par \par \uc2 Adam, what do you think of that? I mean, we run -- and, of course, you have literally written the book on these technology titans. There are very few like \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  and what he created and what he did. And I'm just wondering. I know -- I know the pioneer eventually leaves it to others to continue that pioneering company, certainly worked for \uc2 Steve Jobs\uc2 , tragically, after his death, and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , for \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  after \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  stepped down to pursue charitable endeavors, and probably here as well.\par \par \uc2 But it is an adjustment, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 ADAM LASHINSKY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And may I just send you I thought that was as great a highlight reel Neil Cavuto as it was of \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  just now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I thought my toupee was better back then, much better.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LASHINSKY: I think one thing you can say for certain is that the company changes under these second-generation leaders.\par \par \uc2 So, if you want to look at the cup half-full, you would say, well, Bezos isn't going we're going anywhere, he's going to be executive chairman. He will be around perhaps as much as he's been around for the past few years, which was a period when he had delegated a lot of day-to-day authority to these other people, including the incoming CEO, \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But that said, as he pays attention to other things and as the company grows and goes into different areas, it becomes a different company, just as \uc2 Apple\uc2  has under \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  and as \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  has under \uc2 Satya Nadella\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Danielle, there are little things I remark on when I talk to the people and all, and all of you, included, by the way, is generally they're very comfortable in their skin, and they don't screen questions ahead of time, anything you want to ask. He was famous for that.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering, in this guarded age, where companies are loath to reveal too much or to tip their hand, whether that kind of stuff ends.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be fair, in recent years, Bezos has been a little less accessible. And that would accompany someone who, for a while, was the world's richest man, now barely number two to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Does that change things? In other words, does company get essentially so big, you can't be the company you were, the DNA itself has changed?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE DIMARTINO BOOTH, FORMER FEDERAL RESERVE ADVISER: Well, so, the bigger you grow to be, the less nimble you are. It is what it is.\par \par \uc2 And even though \uc2 Amazon\uc2  today remains the world's largest e-tailer, and that is their greatest source of revenue, if you look at their cloud computing, their web services, there's a reason that Jassy rose up to the top. It accounts for 60 percent of its operating profits.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's a bigger picture here. When Bezos sent out the statement to the employees, he said, I'm going to be more focused on my role at The Washington Post, which he owns, as well as my charities.\par \par \uc2 There's something to be said for the last few months and years, where antitrust scrutiny has become acute. And so maybe he is looking more so to safeguard his legacy and the 27 years he's spent building this company by becoming more active, even tangentially, through The Washington Post, which is one of the most influential newspapers inside the Beltway.\par \par \uc2 So, he may have to shift his focus to save his company.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, indeed. He bought The Washington Post with his own funds. That was not \uc2 Amazon\uc2  funds, of course, those funds available because of the dramatic rise in \uc2 Amazon\uc2  stock.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 , who is going to take over the company right now. He did run that cloud business, which is all the rage and all the future.\par \par \uc2 And if you think about it, John Layfield, when \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  came out with its numbers, and these other companies reporting numbers, what they're doing up there in the cloud in a lot of their cloud-based businesses, that seems to be the cash generator. And that seems to be the theme for \uc2 Amazon\uc2  going forward as well, for all of these guys.\par \par \uc2 It is curious, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 JOHN LAYFIELD, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 And that has been the cash generator for some time. Very few CEOs would have had the leeway that \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  had. When you asked him in that interview that, are you going to be profitable next year, and he refused to give you any insight into predictions--\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAYFIELD: -- he was not very profitable for a very long time. And people were very upset with him about that.\par \par \uc2 But he was doing what companies should do if you want to take over the world. And that is take in all the profits, building it into infrastructure. But most CEOs don't get the leeway like that.\par \par \uc2 And Jassy, at the same time, who came along in '97, who built this cloud computing from scratch, it was $45 billion last year, up 30 percent year over year. You look at what they have done, and a lot of what's not what's talked about, the undersea cables that are crisscrossing the oceans from the Pacific to the Atlantic to the Mediterranean to the Indian Ocean, that's all part of this cloud computing.\par \par \uc2 And that is the cash cow. As far as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  itself, they have a lot of things that they're getting into as far as health care. They have got to (AUDIO GAP) with on (AUDIO GAP) on geo-resources probably in India -- in India, to get into that market.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think a lot of things change systemically.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will see.\par \par \uc2 Guys, thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 What remains, again, my favorite feature of \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  -- and I sound like I'm a little biased here -- I am, because he is such an impressive human being -- was the passion and the humor he brings to the job, I'm sure almost anything and everything else he will do. He brings that passion.\par \par \uc2 I have seen few like him, and I doubt I will see few like in the future.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a lot more coming up, including \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  now in the vaccination business, but will that prove a shot in the arm or a pain in the butt?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I want to show you something.\par \par \uc2 This is a sign in a -- I think it's a New York \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , talking about the possibility we don't have the vaccine yet available here, but come to think of it, we will, and soon. It's in Jersey. I misspoke.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they're going to have them nationwide. That's the goal that \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  has set right now to be, well, a COVID vaccine supercenter. You need your vaccine, just walk in there, much as you could do now with flu shots and the rest.\par \par \uc2 Rina Shah joins us, Walgreens group vice president of pharmacy operations and services.\par \par \uc2 Rina, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 So, when does this start?\par \par \uc2 RINA SHAH, GROUP VICE PRESIDENT OF SPECIALTY AND RETAIL PHARMACY OPERATIONS, WALGREENS: Thank you so much for having me, and really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We're really excited about the transition we're making from the long-term care program into this next phase. And this next phase actually starts next week, in which there will be limited doses that will be available across pharmacy providers, where we can administer vaccine to patients that are eligible within the state.\par \par \uc2 Again, we are activating our stores where possible, but vaccine is still limited. And so the intent is to ensure that what vaccine we do have available, we will be able to administer as quickly as possible.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How will that work?\par \par \uc2 Various states have different programs set up, Rina. And you and I discussed that last time, but I'm just wondering how it goes then. If you have got your ticket or form that says, all right, I can get the vaccine today, can I just walk into a \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  with that and get it?\par \par \uc2 SHAH: So, the way it's going to work is that it's going to be appointment- based.\par \par \uc2 And so to ensure that patients are able to come in and be in and out as safely and as effectively as possible, all of our patients, all of our customers that are eligible will be able to go online or call one of our stores, schedule an appointment. Again, vaccine is still very limited.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But how do you prove that eligibility, Rina? That's what I'm asking.\par \par \uc2 In other words, if I call you or a local \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  to try to come in, they're going to ask I mean, whether I'm in that group, right, I mean, that can go ahead to \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , right?\par \par \uc2 SHAH: That's right. Yes, you're right.\par \par \uc2 Every state has different eligibility requirements. It's different across each of the jurisdictions. And so, when you go ahead and make that appointment, those are the questions that we will be asking either online or on the phone.\par \par \uc2 And then not only do we ask that to validate that. When you come into the store, we're also attesting -- asking the patient to attest that they have also met those eligibility requirements. So, it's a combination of doing that pre-screen in advance and then coming in store.\par \par \uc2 And, as you mentioned, every state is different. So, we're managing state- by-state requirements to ensure that those eligible individuals are the ones getting vaccines.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I'm wondering, too, how do you determine who's eligible?\par \par \uc2 As you know, there have been people trying to front-run the system and work a way around it, and they're not among those eligible to get it. You hear these horror stories about health care workers who are shunted aside in favor of other groups, the elderly, who sit in long lines and not being given the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 How do you deal with that?\par \par \uc2 SHAH: Yes, it's a very complex situation.\par \par \uc2 The best way, though, is that every state has made it public in which who is the eligible group that's in there? So, for example, in Illinois, it's those that are 65 years of age and older, as well as essential workers.\par \par \uc2 And so those are the individuals that are eligible. That's what the patient would actually attest to when they're online for pre-screening, and then when they come into the store to get their vaccine.\par \par \uc2 And so, in that situation, every state has different eligibility criteria. And when you go onto our Web site, we have that all listed up. And we're updating that on a daily basis as we speak, because, to your point, eligibility is changing on a day-to-day basis.\par \par \uc2 And as more vaccine becomes available, more and more individuals will be able to get vaccine. And so it's really about, as vaccine grows, we will be able to immunize many more patients.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, I believe most of the drug manufacturers that have these vaccines, it's two doses. \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 , I think, is the exception.\par \par \uc2 But how do you handle that? They have to come back in 21 days. Do they make an appointment as soon as they're there, 21 days later, you come back and get that second dose?\par \par \uc2 SHAH: The way it's -- imagine an airline. You book your round-trip, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 SHAH: You pick your first dose, and then you get your second dose.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be scheduling both doses at the same time. And we're asking our patients to go ahead and schedule both. In case they didn't, there's -- maybe they decided they didn't want to do that, they can come into the store and, as they get that first dose, we will go ahead and schedule that second one as well at the same time, because it's extremely important that all of our patients complete their series, both dose one and dose two.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the one thing, you guys make it so intriguing, because, when I had the flu shot done recently, I realized I was right near where they had these big old Reese's Peanut Butter Cups.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And it was like two birds with one stone. And I figured, I would gain five pounds, and I got my vaccine. So, don't make it so tempting. Don't make it so easy.\par \par \uc2 Rina, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 SHAH: We will make sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's a great idea. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAH: Thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just put them a little further away. It's like--\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SHAH: Yes, we will put them further away, and we will have water bottles and vitamin waters and all of the other things, and health bars.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, no. No store will be safe if I break in there.\par \par \uc2 Rina, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have been telling you, meanwhile, about all the fuss going on with this turning away from fossil fuel and all of that. At least, that's what a lot of critics charge of the Biden administration. But they're not all Republican critics.\par \par \uc2 One is a Democratic congressman for the beautiful state of Texas on the right there, who says Rick Perry, when it comes to this, is right; this is going too far -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICK PERRY, FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF ENERGY: If you're going to parrot this line that we have got to do things to help the climate out, for God's sakes, man, get off your private jet, and go travel with the rest of the folks out there, if you're going to move around.\par \par \uc2 And the idea that he can so parsimoniously sit there and say, well, they can go get a job over in the solar panel business, well, most of those folks along that pipeline can't relocate to China, because that's where most of those solar panels are being constructed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Rick Perry, the former energy secretary, as well as the former, better than 10 years, governor of Texas, criticizing \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  for talking about just switching over to solar paying jobs for a lot of these workers who are going to be out of jobs altogether, and the hypocrisy of all that traveling on private jets.\par \par \uc2 Now, Senator Kerry has mentioned since that, because he shuttles back all over the world, he has to take private jets, and it's a lot more seamless doing it this way than it would be going back in commercial. Jump ball, however your view of that. So, he has responded to that.\par \par \uc2 But I did catch up with another Texan, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here's the difference. He is a Democratic congressman from Texas who says that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should be reaching out and talking to more Democrats who share his views, Cuellar's views, that you go after energy like this, you're going after a lot of jobs, like those in his district.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): There are several Democrats that we do want to have a conversation with the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 I'm a supporter of President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I have known him for many years, and I have been a big supporter. On this particular point, we just want to make sure that they take certain things in consideration.\par \par \uc2 And that is, keep in mind that my area or even Texas, there is, though -- we're dependent on oil and gas. I do understand the transition period. I understand that.\par \par \uc2 But keep in mind that, if you look at the best-case scenario, it's going to be years before we can totally go from fossil fuel over to the alternative.\par \par \uc2 And one last thing. And you know, this. Texas is number one in oil and gas in the nation, but we're also number one in wind. So, why can we not work on both issues and bring the energy folks to the table, so we can help address some of those issues?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Congressman, it seems like a battle between two core Democratic constituencies, that is, union types, who obviously treasure those jobs that are about to go because of this presidential directive, and environmentalists within your party, who seem to be winning out.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried that it's going to come back to bite the president, to bite you, to bite Democrats?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: No, I mean, I think this is just a conversation. Just like the Republicans have their different voices, same thing. We have the different voices here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it is more than a conversation, right, because it's actually happening.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these jobs are actually going. That's not a conversation. That's a fact. Thousands are going without jobs now as a result.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Yes, and it's -- and I think what you're referring to is, there was a temporary ban on public lands that does affect, let's say, New Mexico. It affects the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 For example, if you look at the permanent trust fund for the state of Texas, the PUF, the University Fund, most of that money that they get from public lands, from oil and gas goes to higher education or even public education.\par \par \uc2 So, it affects different ones. So, we're hoping that we can have that conversation, so we can find balance. Again, if we don't bring the stakeholders, how are we going to address this issue? You just can't keep the energy out and say, here are the winners, here are the losers.\par \par \uc2 You got to bring people together at the table and try to work things out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  is among those Democrats saying he's all in on all types of energy, but not at the expense of traditional energy.\par \par \uc2 We will see where that fight goes. Right now, that is an uphill climb, to put it mildly.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we're getting some new details as to the fate of \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , for that matter. Republicans are meeting behind closed doors to discuss where each is going.\par \par \uc2 One interesting development, with the Republican leader, McCarthy, saying that Democrats' resolution to strip the congresswoman, Greene, of committee post is a distraction to Congress. McCarthy, in a statement on Greene, makes no mention of any move to reprimand her.\par \par \uc2 This comes at the same time \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is also being discussed at whether, given her vote on impeaching \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which he said was a matter of conscience, which has caused by fiery response by conservative Republicans, including those back in Wyoming who say she let her party and her state down. They don't want her in leadership in Congress. In fact, many are saying, we don't want you in Congress at all.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , voting her conscience, in jeopardy of losing a leadership position, while \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  in jeopardy of losing nothing.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, it sounds like a high minimum wage at the federal level, $15 an hour, but what if I told you there's a possibility that it could save $30 billion in welfare-related programs and costs?\par \par \uc2 Now, to the government, that might sound like a good idea, but it still means that bosses are the ones who are going to have to fork over that higher wage. And I suspect they really don't much care whether that's going to decrease national health care costs.\par \par \uc2 But it is one of the selling points that Democrats are using to push this higher wage.\par \par \uc2 And Douglas Holtz-Eakin joins me right now, the former Congressional Budget Office director, American Action Forum president.\par \par \uc2 Leaving aside who benefits from these benefit savings, it does make sense on paper, I guess, Doug, that, if you're earning more, you don't need as much from the government. The $30 billion, though in just welfare, what did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CONGRESSIONAL BUDGET OFFICE DIRECTOR: Well, I think this is really looking at it the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a proposal that, in normal circumstances, incredibly damaging to the labor market. That \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  estimates that it would kill 1.3 million jobs. And in these circumstances, it's going to be much, much worse. Sixty percent of minimum wage workers are in leisure and hospitality.\par \par \uc2 In December alone, we lost half-a-million jobs in that industry. And the damage that this would do would be just simply dramatic. and that's damage that is inflicted on the least educated, least skilled, least experienced workers in the American economy.\par \par \uc2 And who benefits? People who have jobs. So, we're effectively saying, OK, we're going to take some money and give it to the people who have jobs, and we're going to take it away from people who are trying to get a job in the middle of a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 This really does not hang together as a sensible way to get the economy out of the condition it's in and get Americans back on their feet. Health care, that's great. We have lots of policies we can deal with health care spending. This is about giving people a job and getting them back to having the dignity of their earnings and the chance to get ahead.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and it does stand to reason, obviously, if you move out of poverty, which has been \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's argument here, you move out of poverty, you don't need, qualify or want any of these other programs. I get that.\par \par \uc2 But you just mentioned health care. If better than a million Americans lose jobs, a good many of them lose that health care. So you're back in the saddle for that.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wondering where this goes, right?\par \par \uc2 And I know now the CBO, present-day CBO, is going to explore this in a little bit more detail. What do you think they will come up?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Well, I think it's hard to come up with anything but that this is a really bad idea.\par \par \uc2 When they looked at it prior to the pandemic, it was pretty obviously something that was going to be very damaging. It's going to be worse now. There's no question about it. We have millions of people out of work in exactly the industries that have the minimum wage workers, and you're going to double the minimum wage over the course of four years. That's incredibly rapid.\par \par \uc2 It might make sense in Seattle or something like, of a high-wage metropolitan area, but it's not going to make sense in rural parts of America. And it's really going to hurt the sector that's been damaged the most, small businesses.\par \par \uc2 If you're a small businessman, this is your cost structure, and you have just now made it impossible for many of them to reopen. So, I understand the desire of people to have workers earning a wage that they can be proud of. That makes perfect sense.\par \par \uc2 But you do have to question the timing. This is when we're trying to get a businesses back open, and you're doubling their costs and making it really hard for them to do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, timing is everything here. And whatever you save, of course, for these bosses, I'm sure, if you run a pizza shop or a local restaurant, you might say, touche, saving on benefits and all, but that doesn't affect your bottom line, which will be stretched.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much. Douglas Holtz-Eakin.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 This Republican powwow still going on deciding on the fate right now of two key congresswomen, one who voted her conscience on impeaching the president of the United States. The other said crazy stuff.\par \par \uc2 The one who said crazy stuff looks like she will be OK. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , don't know.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210204eh2300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Former U.S. Energy Secretary Rick Perry\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 1,313 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had promised this day would be coming. But the impact is still pretty serious, huh?\par \par \uc2 LAURIE COX, STROPPEL HOTEL AND MINERAL BATHS: It is very serious, and still very shocking.\par \par \uc2 I guess maybe I was a little naive in going, no, he can`t shut down a project that`s already started. Or how could they shut down a project already started?\par \par \uc2 Our KOA invested a lot of money and getting hookups so that the workers could stay there over the winter. And we have just got a lot of other businesses around this area, that a void has been left in our communities because of this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, normally, when we think of the Keystone pipeline shutting down, that oil workers are hurt, not just oil workers, though. That nice lady runs a very, very popular hotel in South Dakota and a store that catered to the crowd that had those jobs, had them until now.\par \par \uc2 Other businesses are feeling the pinch right now. That could turn out to be the thousands.\par \par \uc2 Rick Perry, the former energy secretary of the United States and Texas governor, with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 As you had been saying in your days, the best thing that ever happened to the industry was getting it going again, and the U.S. being responsible for its own production and fracking and the like, and the Keystone pipeline and the like. And now that`s being taken back bit by bit.\par \par \uc2 I have talked to industry workers, and even those that depend on those workers, who are worried it gets worse. Will it get worse, in your eyes?\par \par \uc2 RICK PERRY, FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF ENERGY: It will get worse.\par \par \uc2 And that`s a sad story for the men and women who were counting on getting COVID over with and kind of getting the economy headed back in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 And I can`t think of anything more unkind, anything more thoughtless than what the Biden administration has done with stopping this. And they`re playing to the Greta Thunbergs of the world, people halfway around the world who stand up and applaud the Biden administration for being so pro- climate.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is, the United States was leading the country in the reduction of emissions because of the American natural gas that was taking the place of a lot of inefficient power plants around the world. And that`s going to be lost.\par \par \uc2 And so political points, but real personal loss for people around the United States. I can`t help but think, having been the former governor of Texas, what the governor of New Mexico is thinking today.\par \par \uc2 I think well over half of the proceeds that come in for their state come from federal lands when it comes to oil and gas. I mean, what do you tell the teachers and the administrators of your public schools in New Mexico, when you campaigned for this fellow that is absolutely sticking a knife in the back of New Mexico?\par \par \uc2 So -- and it`s my understanding that the secretary of the interior is from New Mexico. So, I just don`t know how you square that. I mean, how do you with a straight face...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Then how do you square, Secretary -- what do you think -- you talk about how you square stuff.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  says things like there are plenty of solar panel jobs that are going to be in the millions in the future, how do those oil workers, those Keystone workers, and all those across the country who now could be facing job losses, how do they transition to making, building, putting on homes and businesses solar panels?\par \par \uc2 How do they do that without -- without some preparation, without going back, essentially, to the beginning, to zero? Because all they know is this industry. All they know is oil production and everything that goes with it. That`s quite a bit of knowledge that now they have to just put back.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Well, first off, \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  is a pretty poor spokesperson for the climate business, when he hops on his private jet and leaves a trail of emissions.\par \par \uc2 It`s about 40 times more than what it would be if he flew first-class on a United Airline going somewhere, \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , one of our great airlines. Matter of fact, they probably need to work right now.\par \par \uc2 So, it just doesn`t square good with me and I think tens of thousands of Americans out there and those that care about the climate, that, listen, if you`re going to -- if you`re going to parrot this line that we have got to do things to help the climate out, for God`s sakes, man, get off your private jet, and go travel with the rest of the folks out there, if you`re going to move around.\par \par \uc2 And the idea that he can so parsimoniously sit there and say, well, they can go get a job over in the solar panel business, well, most of those folks along that pipeline can`t relocate to China, because that`s where most of those solar panels are being constructed.\par \par \uc2 So, listen, this is -- this is...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Are you worried, though, Secretary, about another thing he outlines in that statement there, where he says, even if we do everything we should do, of course, the reality is that we`re only a small percentage of the world?\par \par \uc2 Ninety percent of the pollution, everything else borne by countries like China and Brazil and India, that will still be a reality that world officials have to address. So, the double damning indemnity for a lot of these workers is that they`re sacrificing their jobs for something that will not necessarily have tangible results for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Well said, Mr. Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 This is absolutely being sacrificed on the bonfire of the vanities here. This is truly a political move that`s not going to have a bit...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But can`t you do everything, Secretary? I mean, you espoused -- I know, when you were running for president, you were open to wind and solar and all this other stuff, but not at the expense one over the other.\par \par \uc2 Now, clearly, we have said, these are our future technologies, and not oil, fossil fuels, coal, none of that. I thought we would be all in on everything.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: And think about nuclear being a zero-emission energy source. And my bet is the Biden administration is not for that.\par \par \uc2 They are for wind, they are for solar, they`re for whatever else the environmental community tells them to be for, but they`re not for fossil fuels. They`re not for the nuclear industry.\par \par \uc2 I -- and, listen, I mean, the reality is, we`re going to continue to use fossil fuels. We`re going to need energy in this country. The real winners in all of this, China, India, countries that are going to continue to develop their energy sources.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of those energy sources are going to be fossil fuel-driven. And the climate is not going to be protected the way that \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  and his ilk would like to tell you that it`s going to be.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will see.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 In the middle of all of this, to the secretary`s interest here, oil prices were heading up north, mainly on the notion, though, that economic activity is picking up.\par \par \uc2 So, that will be a factor to consider, along with maybe cut production in the United States as a result of this.\par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020210202eh2200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Administration Takes Aim At Our Basic Institution; The Continued Occupation Of Washington, D.C.; Populist Revolt Underway On Wall Street; D.C. Statehood Bill Has 38 Co-Sponsors In Senate; Chicago Mayor Wants To Stop Deporting Criminals; Another Radical Biden Pick, Jalina Porter; Left Ignores The Violent Rhetoric Of Democrat Lawmakers; Keystone Pipeline Was Supposed To Create 11,000 Jobs; Billions In Revenue Lost And Thousands Of Jobs At Risk After Biden's Energy Orders; White House Pauses Gitmo Vaccine Plan After Pressure\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Rick Leventhal, Marc Siegel, Joey Jones \par \uc2 8,183 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 February 2021\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY, FOX NEWS HOST: And I hope that you'll join us.\par \par \uc2 Up next is Tucker Carlson. Tucker, we hope you have a great show tonight.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON, FOX NEWS HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT. Happy Monday.\par \par \uc2 The news is moving with bewildering speed, in case you haven't noticed. We've noticed.\par \par \uc2 No presidential administration has ever changed so many basic institutions and facts of American life, and done it so quickly. We've done our best to bring you nightly updates on what's happening for the week and a half that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been President, but at times, it's overwhelming, there's just too much to cover.\par \par \uc2 The other day, for example, we learned that the Biden administration plans to make the tiny District of Columbia, our 51st state, a more corrupt English speaking version of Monaco, as if lobbyists in Washington didn't have enough control over your life. Now, there will be U.S. senators.\par \par \uc2 But that's not even the biggest news. Just a few hours ago, Biden officials informed us that after almost 65 years of working to build a nuclear weapon, the nation of Iran maybe finally -- and you'll find this amazing, quote: "just weeks away" from getting a bomb. After 65 years of trying. What a coincidence.\par \par \uc2 You already know how that story is going to end, another pointless foreign war, trillions spent many dead, the United States gaining nothing. We've seen that a lot and it's happening again.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just sent American troops to Syria. Well, you didn't know that? Yes. Hours after he became President, American soldiers crossed into the nation of Syria, sending them to Syria was one of the first things \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did.\par