ld have done this if impeachment was so important, let's have the investigation, let's get all the players out. We are literally learning new stuff every day.\par \par \uc2 And there's one thing I can tell you as a brother. The last thing I ever want to see is a speedy trial. Nothing ever good comes from a speedy trial. In my entire life experience, and my forefathers, I'm not for it. I think it's sad.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, you want to support due process. And Emily, it's the statement that we saw him make on this leaked video, it seemed at odds with the thread where he explained, you know, going down this road, banning President Trump sets a very dangerous precedent. Yet on the video, he says the exact opposite. He says this is just the beginning, and we are going to go wholeheartedly after him and many other people for many, many more days.\par \par \uc2 EMILY COMPAGNO, FOX NEWS HOST: That's exactly right. And he's trying to have people fall in line, emperor's new clothes style so that they won't see the hypocrisy and they won't see the contradiction. And when he mentions real-world violence, as if this is the first time that we've seen a real-world violence application stemming from social media abuse, or use. That's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 What about cyber bullying? Three-fourth of students report being cyber- bullied. And those that are, are twice as likely to die by suicide. What about sex trafficking, revenge porn? There's a million things happening online that is perpetuated by social media --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Good point.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: -- and those big tech platforms that up until now, we've not seen the slightest modicum of caring about it. In fact, what about the San Bernardino shootings? Don't you remember how at that time privacy was all of a sudden so important to big tech, that they wouldn't cooperate with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  on it.\par \par \uc2 And yet now, we have what should have been a similar, unalienable right, and yet all of a sudden to them it's super flimsy. For some reason, privacy blows free speech out of the water. I don't understand it. And there's no way that it's defensible.\par \par \uc2 And to your point earlier, unless we do continue pushing back, there is no way that there will ever be accountability held. And by the way, quick final point, other world leaders who don't agree with Trump on his policies or many other things, they have come out speaking against this.\par \par \uc2 German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  said this was problematic. The Mexican President Obrador said it was a bad sign. Even the French junior minister for the E.U. affairs said this is shocking. Everything about this doesn't sit well with the American people and with the world except for these guys who were behind the big tech pulling the puppet strings.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Juan, speak to Emily's point about Silicon Valley protecting the privacy of a dead terrorist, the San Bernardino shooter, but not protecting the first amendment rights of not only the president but many of his supporters.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, I think that you have a situation here, Jesse, where, you know, when there's violence, I don't understand it. I don't -- I didn't understand it in the San Bernardino case. I spoke against it, because I think you have to cooperate in terms of protecting us.\par \par \uc2 But I think what's missing from this conversation is the context that, you know, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  , all these platforms, acted only after, after the violence, the siege at the capitol. You know, we learned today that in some of these indictments that are being filed, that there was an effort not only to capture, but possibly assassinate some of our elected officials in the capitol.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that what you're seeing is that \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  and others are speaking to the notion that their platforms were used in some cases to help organize and plot this seditious act. And that the whole country, it feels that, you know, democracy itself has been threatened, and that our way of life is under threat. And that they have to now understand is going to be some responsibility, some moderation for what took place.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's what we talked about earlier in the week with Parler and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  . Where \uc2 Amazon\uc2  responded to Parler by saying, here are instances of people calling for violence, racial violence, assassination on your web site, no moderation. I think people think, you know, something is wrong there, that something has to be done. It's not just open, you know, an open road. And you can make all kinds of awful, organize awful efforts and violence, and think that no one is going to notice.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I would agree that moderation is critical, but the feeling of many people on the right is that there is only moderation on the right, that the left can run wild and say whatever they want whenever they want it, and there's no accountability there.\par \par \uc2 Dana, before I forget, first of all, Project Veritas is, again, this is another big, big whistleblower video leak that they have obtained. And you know, we'd never know about this if they hadn't gotten that. Speak to your interpretation of \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  , what he said on that video.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, I mean, if you're going to give a video - - if you're going to do a video to 5,400 employees, you have to imagine that it's going to leak. And I think that's why the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  spokesperson said that what he said in the video is what he said in his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 And as I said yesterday, \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2  seems to like working all of this out in his head, kind of on a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  thread like what do you think about this? I don't know. What should we do? He is going to be called up to testify right away. So, we will see that stream of consciousness again play out.\par \par \uc2 You know, you can't be a part-time CEO in this situation. You have to be on top of it at all times. And I would rather -- I would rather everybody just post pictures of their dogs and kids --\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: Or fish.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- and not have any of this vitriol. I don't need to know anybody's political opinion. I think there's -- \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is not that important. There's a small number of people that are on it. It's an important place for the public square, but when the -- Silicon Valley had the ideas for all of these great things, they thought it was going to be Utopia and everyone was going to get along, and people are going to find long-lost friends, and you are going to see the people that you haven't seen before -- seen since high school. And everything is going to be amazing.\par \par \uc2 And there is a lot of good. I get it. That comes from it. I love posting pictures of my dog. But some of the other things that Emily went through are very real as well.\par \par \uc2 Can I mention one other thing? It's not just\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- social media companies. This past week, Politico playbook is a, you know, kind of a D.C. insider publication but a lot of people read it -- they are going through a transition right now. So, they've had guest writers all week.\par \par \uc2 Well, yesterday, \uc2 Ben Shapiro\uc2  was a guest writer. And it upset some of the newsrooms so much that they had to have a 300-person therapy session because \uc2 Ben Shapiro\uc2  , who has actually also been quite critical of President Trump calls balls and strikes for the past, that they had -- that they just went into meltdown. But when Don Lemon have guest hosted and wrote Politico playbook two days before, nobody said a word.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not just in Silicon Valley. And I think that is the anxiety that conservatives feel, that it does feel like it's coming from everywhere, from your college, from your social media, from the newspapers that you read, and you were wondering where it's going to come from next.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, everybody agrees, for the next several days, dogs and kid pictures. That's all we are going to give them. All right?\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Democratic hypocrisy on full display after months of egging on violent Black Lives Matter protesters, Democrats are putting pressure on Pelosi to cancel a Republican congressman just over the words that came out of his mouth.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Welcome back. GOP Congressman \uc2 Madison Cawthorn\uc2  slamming cancel culture after North Carolina Democrat sent a letter to Speaker Pelosi demanding he be expelled from Congress. They claim his, quote, "seditious behavior help incite the chaos at the capitol."\par \par \uc2 Cawthorn spoke at the rally before the mob stormed to the building. And conservatives are now wondering why there wasn't the same outrage when Democrats said stuff like this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXINE WATERS (D-CA): If you anybody from that cabinet in a restaurant, in a department store, in a gasoline station, and you tell them they are not welcome.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): Please, get up in the face of some congresspeople.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2  , FORMER UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: You cannot be civil with a political party that wants to destroy what you stand for, what you care about.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER , FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL OF THE UNITED STATES: When they go low, we kicked.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): You have to be ready to take a punch, and you have to be ready to throw a punch.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Juan, I'd like to start with you. Because to me, it is clear that all of this rhetoric on both sides, it's all aspirational, it's not intentional. And I want to know how this is not outright hypocrisy right now by the far left and also how this is supposed to further unity that they are calling for.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think, one, and on that montage we just played, I think those people were speaking and saying it was necessary to confront people that they thought were acting negatively, but it didn't result in any violence.\par \par \uc2 What we saw though, in the case of \uc2 Madison Cawthorn\uc2 , the congressman from North Carolina is that, you know, at Turning Point USA, he was saying lightly threatened -- I think that's an exact quote, likely threatened members of Congress who don't buy into this claim that the election was stolen. And we all know that's not true.\par \par \uc2 And then after that, I mean, he was saying members of Congress were cowards. You know, when he spoke at the, before President Trump, at the rally in D.C., he was telling the crowd, you know, you guys have fight in you. Go get -- I mean, so I think that's why the Democrats in North Carolina said they considered what he had done to be damaging and dangerous behavior. And they put it online with sedition.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there's a big difference between people saying, you know, stand up and fight and this guy calling out people and saying lightly threatened -- or you are fighting just before a crowd goes and attacks the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Jesse, to Juan's point, I actually don't see a difference. One of the tweets that Congressman Cawthorn set out said specifically the House is on the floor here of Congress, meaning, legally, meaning through procedures. And he said, quote, "not on the streets outside." And we did see violence coming from the left. We saw absolute violence and people dying as well. So, how do you explain this and how is, again, this outright hypocrisy? How is it tolerable?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Juan is too fair of a guy. And I know, Juan, you are a fair guy. You are too fair of a guy to draw a line from using your traditional political euphemism as, you know, we got to fight back, we get -- and drawing the connection between political violence on the street.\par \par \uc2 Because in that case, you can say, when \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  said get in their face, and a guy tackled \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  and broke his ribs, you could draw that and say she is complicit and an accessory to a crime. You could say all the cheerleading that the Democrats and the media did all summer for the Black Lives Matter riots. That led to a lot of fatalities, 30 or more this summer.\par \par \uc2 So, it's irresponsible -- and I know Juan would never do this, because he is such a fair guy -- it's irresponsible to put that on Madison. And Madison is just too handsome to be expelled from Congress. It's not fair. He should have some sort of protection there. And I say that with a strict record of heterosexuality.\par \par \uc2 But they are -- Emily, they are really trying to criminalize political speech. And that is the point here. Political speech is not hate speech. People have been saying, you know, give him hell, get in their face, you know, fight to the finish. They've been saying that since the 18th century. You know? When a Republican says it, it doesn't make them a criminal, it just makes them a politician.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Dana, can you compare what we are seeing now in terms of the actual rhetoric and actions by those in politics, by those, for example, the backlash now to Congressman Cawthorn -- what we are seeing not just the American public, but actual, again, a letter trying to get him expelled, this actual decision. Can you compare this right now with the Bush 43 administration and what you experience at that time? Is there any similarities at all?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I mean, you kind of put me on the spot there to test my memory -- no. I mean, I don't think so. Obviously, you know, there was -- remember when Code Pink came into the hearing room and had fake blood on their hands, and got in Condoleezza Rice's face while she was testifying in front of the Senate? You know, you have seen things like this happen over time.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the problems that we have right now is that we are now nine days since the riot. And we still do not have good information about all that happens. We know that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is arresting a lot of people, OK, we got that, but we don't have a lot more information about where -- all the -- how it started. What happened? What happened at the barricades? What happened to the police officers who were beaten?\par \par \uc2 And as to Tyrus' point in the first block, decisions are being made about what should happen to people and punishments for people, before we even actually have any concrete information as to what happened.\par \par \uc2 That's a real dereliction, I believe, on the part of different law enforcement agencies. I understand that they are doing an investigation, but America needs to have more information.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Tyrus, to Dana's point, isn't this than just an oversimplification too soon without investigation anyway?\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: No, I don't think it's an oversimplification. I think we've, as a country, keep making the same mistake. We are not learning from our mistakes. The first mistake we're making is when these violent things happen, we immediately go into the political cover up.\par \par \uc2 So, if it happened on the left side of the field, and you are a politician on the left, you have to protect the cause and try to justify whatever way you can why they did those things, they are just a few bad actors. But, you know, their heart is in the right place because they have been so attacked by President Trump's tweets.\par \par \uc2 And then on the right, it's the same thing, where they are trying to take something from us -- you understand when these people stepped out, because I would argue most hard-core Trump supporters were working. Now everyone -- especially now during a pandemic --\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: -- we can -- no one is loading up the family to drive to Washington. Now we have bad people. They are not -- they are not anything else, they're not far left to the galaxy, they are bad people who will say, yes, I'm a Democrat, and as soon we start marching for peace, they go loot a footlocker.\par \par \uc2 That's not a Democrat. That's a bad person. And the same thing we were seeing is saying that you are a Trump supporter, no problem, saying wearing a MAGA hat, no problem. Once you walk across and decide to put hands on somebody, you are now a human being committing a crime. You are no longer representing your -- there is no -- that's the problem we are having.\par \par \uc2 And then we end up with the sides where if I'm a Republican and I say these Republican people who were acting in the name of Republicans to commit a crime or to hurt somebody, take that away. You are a person who made a bad choice and hurt another human being.\par \par \uc2 That's what we need to do. We need to stop giving them cover by saying these extreme right-wing guys stormed the capitol. No. These group of bad people stormed the capitol. There's going to be investigations and arrests and we'll tune in and we'll let you know. Investigate and start call -- taking people and give accountability. That's what I want to say. I'm there now, accountability.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Amen. All right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I love it.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Coming up, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  pitching a progressive stimulus plan, but it's still not enough for the far left. Wait until you hear what they're demanding next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: President-elect \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  unveiling his nearly $2 trillion economic recovery plan. It calls for standing $1,400 stimulus checks to Americans. And there's money for state and local government. But it also includes policies progressives have been calling for. I will give you an example, a $15 an hour minimum wage. Senator Bernie Sanders says he likes what he sees.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (D-VT): It's a very good start, I think, very serious start. Biden's plan also understands that the economy is in shambles and that we need emergency relief for working families. So, you know, we are going to have to take a lot of hard look at the details, but it is a very good start in addressing the pandemic crisis and the economic crisis we now face.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But other Democrats are demanding even more. \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2  is accusing Biden of breaking a promise by not proposing a $2,000 stimulus check. And Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  says Americans should get $2,000 per month until the pandemic is over.\par \par \uc2 Dana, I just want to start with you and say, you know, right now, lots of Americans are anxious about getting the vaccine, about how the vaccine is being distributed. So, did you think President-elect Biden's plan to spend more money to develop vaccine and get them distributed was good?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think, yes. And I think that people could agree that the states have not done a great job of following through on the distribution, or what they call it -- when they get the shots into the arms, whatever they're calling that -- administered.\par \par \uc2 That has not gone well. I had Texas Governor Abbott on today. And now they figured -- he said it was slow that first week, but they figured it out, so maybe that will start to pick up. But the way that we get the economy back on track where everybody is making more money, and kids are back in school as if we get this vaccine out and about, so I'm for that.\par \par \uc2 I did think it was interesting that Bernie Sanders didn't decide to go in a different direction. Like, it would have helped Biden more if he had said something like, wow, $15.00 an hour minimum wage. Yes, that is just not going to cut it for me. That is way too conservative. And then the storyline wouldn't have been, wow, the left is pretty happy but, you know, they would like a little bit more, but they weren't disappointed. So, they got to think a little bit before they speak here.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: OK. So, Jesse, the spending -- you know, people say, well, this is a lot of spending. But in many cases, people want stimulus checks, especially with high unemployment rates. We saw unemployment numbers that were higher this week than we've seen any time since August. So, you know, isn't it something the American people actually want that they think the government should act in the midst of a national emergency?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, do we want those checks, Juan? These things are never going away, man. Like, once you do that one or two times, this country is hooked. You're talking about $1,400 or two grand from the Treasury, direct deposit? Are you kidding me? You're never going to be able to take this away from these people.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is -- this is big money we're talking about now. We're all in the Yang gang. This is universal income. It's a very wealthy country, we live in, Juan. We send billions overseas. You can easily make an argument that, you know, people are struggling now with the pandemic, send that money right into their bank accounts and, and let them get it back up off their feet. But I don't see how you end something like this.\par \par \uc2 And if the Republicans even tried to stop it, they're just going to be portrayed as callous and I just don't know if they have the rhetorical talent to explain why checks directly into your bank account each month from the government is a -- is a bad thing.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to just give respect to Bernie Sanders' backdrop right there. Did you see how beautiful that was with the snow and the clock tower? That it was like -- it was like a scene out of -- out of Back to the Future. It was beautiful. I want one of those.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Better than his campaign.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Maybe he's up in Vermont.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: What would you say, Dana?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: There it is. Look at that. It's gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: His campaign backdrops were not great. This is a great backdrop.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, I think -- it looks -- it looks Vermont to me. Yes. So, Tyrus, let's get on to Jesse's point. We do have unemployment and people who are -- you know, the restaurants closed, lots of businesses shut down, especially in the hospitality industry. So, I think -- as I was saying, Jesse, people think that it's appropriate in this moment emergency to offer some help to people who are unemployed or, you know, can't work.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I agree, to a certain extent, we have to help. We -- the government needs to step in. But you -- we need to streamline. We need to focus. The way that they came together to impeach the president in like 48 hours or whatever that was, we need that same vigor and sense of urgency to get the country back on the feet again. And again, we cannot do it with ridiculous bills.\par \par \uc2 You cannot -- and I'm-- I've worked for $57.00 an hour. I get it. I've been there. But you can't raise minimum wage when small businesses can't stay open. If you're going to give the stimulus check, if you're going to give us money so we can get things going again, you hold off on the other stuff. You fix one tire to get the car rolling before -- you don't try to change all the flats at once.\par \par \uc2 And we're seeing the same thing where it's just being dragged out over and over again. We're going to do this, we're going to add this, pork bellies, make sure panda bears are getting enough bamboo. And you need to stop all that ridiculousness. If you're going to give stimulus checks, then you tell the American people, we cannot make wage higher until we get our economy on track.\par \par \uc2 Start telling people the truth. Tell them what we can do and what we can't do. That will be so phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Emily, lots of talk after Biden's speech last night about him being empathetic rather than the showman that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was. What was your reaction to the Biden speech? Did you like it?\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Yes, I think he's always -- he is always a more calm deliver of course. I think this shouldn't be surprising at all. I will say that I think he is wasting a significant amount of time right now capitulating to the far-left talking about a federal minimum wage and arguing into the ridiculous goalpost shifting direct cash payment debate and the lockdown relief bill debate when he should be focusing on opening the economy and recognizing that, Tyrus, to your point, all of these factors are intertwined.\par \par \uc2 The two biggest concerns for CEOs and business owners this year are yes, the Coronavirus and also a recession. Those two are linked and his policies should be focused on maintaining employment for the average American and how everyone will thrive in that capacity.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: All right, thanks, Emily. Ahead, \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend Charles Barkley's outrageous comments on who should get special treatment for the vaccine. The controversy next for you on THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Forget grandma and the at risk. \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend and my favorite golfer, Charles Barkley, says rich athletes should get special treatment when it comes to a vaccine. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BARKLEY, \uc2 NBA\uc2  LEGEND: Give a thousand to some \uc2 NBA\uc2  players.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What about it -- what about \uc2 NFL\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 BARKLEY: The \uc2 NFL\uc2  -- I was going to say, \uc2 NFL\uc2  players, hockey players. Listen, as much taxes as these players pay -- let me repeat that, as much taxes these players pay, they deserve some preferential treatment for life and death. Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Yes. You know, sometimes you know -- and I just like to point out that the entire panel was like, no, this is a -- this is not a good idea. I love Charles Barkley. He's one of my favorite people. Juan, I'm going to go to you first. First of all, I'm glad you're feeling better. Welcome back, all that good stuff.\par \par \uc2 You know, Juan, sometimes somebody says something, and their heart is in the right place, but boy, it just comes out wrong.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes. I mean, he's a terrific commentator. But, you know, that's just so wrong. It's outrageously wrong, Tyrus. And, you know, I mean, the idea that somehow your life is worth more because you pay more taxes, that's just so twisted. I mean, so that means that a rich person's life is worth more than a poor person's life. That's just -- that's wrong as a matter of morality, as a matter of civics. I don't know where it's right, but it isn't right in my mind.\par \par \uc2 I mean, even down in Florida, wealthy members of a nursing home board were offered to get shots. And the governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , the senator, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  , all said that's wrong. Don't do that.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Yes. You know, and \uc2 Adam Silver\uc2  , he -- the commissioner of the \uc2 NBA\uc2  said the same thing. Watters, Juan's brother from another mother, let me ask you this. How long before Charles Barkley is excommunicated by \uc2 Twitter\uc2  ? He's got to be done, right?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No. Chuck is Teflon. He can say whatever he wants. I liked how Kenny tried to save them.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: He did.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It kind of reminded me of Dana on this show when I say stuff like Charles. I mean, I feel like I say stuff like that all the time. I love perks. I think very similarly to the way Charles thinks. Should I -- should I backup Charles? Should I agree with Charles? No, I'm smarter than that.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: You know what, Watters, you're not just another pretty face.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: But I think -- I think there's something there.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Nice job, Watters.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Legendary Dana Perino, first of all, thank you so much for letting me be a part of "THE DAILY BRIEFING." It was awesome experience. Thank you. You are literally, to Watters' point, the most levelheaded being in all of television. Like, if you have a question about anything --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So levelheaded.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: If you were on that panel, what would you have said to Charles right after that comment during the commercial break?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Hold his hand.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I have to say, I thought he was joking, and like, maybe he is. Maybe he is joking around with all of us. Obviously, the \uc2 NBA\uc2  has done a great job in terms of COVID, right. They get to live in a bubble. They get to fly on private airplanes. I would say that their fans are the ones that need the vaccine so that they can get back to work.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: And I -- and I would argue, most \uc2 NBA\uc2  players, that would be their line of thinking. All right --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Emily, you're a die-hard sports fan like myself. And when you -- you know, sometimes you kind of wish when Charles talks, you don't wish he was an \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend. You wish he was like a former boxer and just maybe stayed for one too many last fights every once in a while. You, as an attorney, if you're an agent, what would you say after a show like that? How would that go?\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Oh, man. I mean, I think that there's an entertainment factor here where he doesn't need necessarily an attorney to say, OK, this is how you damage control. I think everyone is used to him sort of being his own person, being a compelling, entertaining personality. And to Jesse's point, you know, everyone loves them.\par \par \uc2 But I think almost like you, Jesse, where you get away with saying some outlandish stuff on this show too, that everyone is like, it's just Jesse. And I think in a way, maybe that comment was so outlandish on his part that we all know he can't be serious, that of course, taxes do count for something but not for health and not for vaccines.\par \par \uc2 And in my opinion, we are already enough in a Hunger Games society that we don't need this sort of fantastical crazy reward like that, right? That's not what our society is based on. And I think that's pretty obvious to everyone else, too.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: Yes, I agree. Change it up, Charles. Change it up, Chuck. All right, "FAN MAIL FRIDAY" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wow, that song brings back great memories. It's "FAN MAIL FRIDAY." Let's get to it. First question. What do you do to take your mind off of politics? What do you do to take your mind off of politics, Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I watch football. And I watch football the entire weekend last weekend wildcard Saturday and I'm looking forward to more football this weekend. And that's basically it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK, football, football, football. Tyrus?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: I guess you'd have to switch the question for me is, what do I do to get myself to think about politics? Because my life phenomenal. I'm busy. I'm raising fish, kids, chasing your keys, chopping wood, building lodges. I got stuff going on all the while socially distancing six feet, wearing masks and getting the damn thing done. So, I have to remind myself on occasion --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: How do you raise a fish?\par \par \uc2 TYRUS: How do you raise a fish? With love and understanding, just like you raise a child, Juan. Do it together.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No, 