ements in FISA applications, particularly if they are material. And to the extent that is a misstatement, that's not one I would consider acceptable.\par \par \uc2 Now, another official references the now infamous \uc2 FBI\uc2  debriefings of Christopher Steele and claims the Intel community never undertook the official process known as verification, but then used Steele's reporting anyway. There's also mention of Steele's sub source, who we learned was himself under investigation by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, tonight, Democrats are pushing back hard. Senator Dianne Feinstein saying, the transcripts released today are valuable for one reason, they confirm that the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's Crossfire hurricane investigation into Russian election interference and Trump campaign ties to Russia was entirely justified.\par \par \uc2 So, now as President Trump prepares to leave office. His supporters say all this is just further proof of his mistreatment, something they and something the president has complained of all along. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Gillian, thank you very much. Senator Lindsey Graham saying today that the subjects of the investigation have their lives turned upside down. Joining me now is one of those people, K.T. McFarland, former Deputy National Security Adviser under President Trump and author of Revolution Trump Washington and We the People. K.T., good to have you back again tonight.\par \par \uc2 You described this the other night, the impact on your life. You went to serve the country, serve the administration, take the experience that you had and national security going all the way back to the Nixon administration and put it to good use. What has this experience been like for people who did that and what does the future look like?\par \par \uc2 KT MCFARLAND, FORMER DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Well, it's probably the most damning thing that's ever happened to myself and a lot of other people, and for a couple of reasons, Martha, it's not just that my career was destroyed, or my reputation was ruined or that I paid a huge financial cost in having to defend myself against crimes I didn't commit.\par \par \uc2 It's the idea that this is my country and that the country you love, and you believe in and you believe all of that stuff you learned as a kid and patriotism and integrity and people who want to be in Washington and serve their nation. And what did we find out? It was just - they were just as corrupt as it could be that there were people in the deep state that didn't like Donald Trump and they didn't want him to be president and they were willing to lie about it. They were willing to commit, I think, the original sin in trying to prevent him from being president. And when that didn't work, try to undermine it and what amounted to a palace coup.\par \par \uc2 And we're now seeing a lot of documentation coming out about it. It just is a betrayal. And so, what so many of us believe in and I do think that this is the reason why so many Americans distrust Washington, and they look at the election of Donald Trump and the presidency of Donald Trump and what Washington, the official establishment tried to do to him, and they say, we've had enough. This is not how it's supposed to be.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, K.T., they were told over and over that the Horowitz report, the inspector general was going to reveal these things and that people would be held accountable for what happened. Then they were told that John Durham's investigation was going to make sure that happen. It was supposed to come out in the summer, never came out in the summer, before the election, never came out before the election.\par \par \uc2 So, they look at that and they say, there is no accountability. Or my other question is, is it the case that the things that were done and the way that the system was manipulated and used didn't really break the law, but certainly through a lot of shade on a lot of people and ruined their