s doesn't happen to normal people, Charlie. The things that we worked on or have - they don't just disappear in enormous chunks conveniently when they're the subject of investigation.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE KIRK, PRESIDENT, TURNING POINT USA: And I think K.T. brought up a great point that this really charted a different direction for the Trump presidency from day one. Imagine where the country would be if all of a sudden you have that 100-day period just to kind of get your administration set and plot out your main objectives. I mean, from day eight to day 15 to day 20 of the Trump administration, it was mired with rumors and leaks and resignations, all stemming from this very investigation.\par \par \uc2 We still don't have full transparency from it. And Victor brings up a phenomenal point, which is why is it just to date, there's a singular indictment with Kevin Clinesmith, with all of that has happened here, it ruined people's lives. And if Carter Page and K.T. McFarland able to make the case, I hope that they're able to bring that to court because it ruined them financially and otherwise.\par \par \uc2 And it's sad to say that this happened in the United States of America and it had more damage than even to President Trump, even more than his administration. It divided the country because still to this day, half the country believes the narrative that President Trump was compromised by Russia. Something has been proven not to be true. And I hope that the declassification can at least help bring some justice out of the situation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Before I let you go, I want to get your quick thought on this other topic, but it's all kind of related in a way, Victor. This was in The Wall Street Journal today talking about \uc2 Jack Dorsey\uc2 's remorse, if you can call it that. His sort of second look at the decision to ban the president from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And it says the CEO maintained that his ban on Mr. Trump was necessary but expressed unease about the power an individual or corporation has over a part of the global public conversation. Quick thought on that before I let you go, Victor.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Very quickly, you can't have three companies all coincidentally go after Parler and then try to deplatform and cancel Donald Trump. But it's part of a Jesse Smollettization or Covington kids change of heart in this hysteria. And I think Donald Trump, he's been impeached and now we're going to break the Constitution, violate it and try to convict him as a private citizen. We're going to destroy his companies. We're going to not let him his companies have loans. We're going to blacklist him from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . We're going to destroy Parler.\par \par \uc2 At some point, people are going to say, what do you want to do, mutilate the political corpse of Donald Trump? And we're going to have a very radical agenda from Joe Biden. I think people are going - there's going to be a backlash at all of this. This isn't the American way at all.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, we will see, and we will cover it closely. Victor Davis Hanson, Charlie Kirk, great to have you both here tonight. Thank you, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the proud owner of an oyster bar in Florida fears that President-elect Biden's minimum wage hike may stimulate him right out of business. He's here tonight and Steve Hilton on what we can expect from Joe Biden's second attempt at turning around the United States economy with enormous mountains of taxpayer dollars, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hard to believe that these images are from the streets of America right now, patrolled by National Guard, trucks and soldiers armed with rifles. There are more troops guarding the United States Capitol and the area around it right now for the inauguration than there are in Afghanistan and Iraq combined. So, that is the climate in which Joe Biden will take on the presidency on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 And he is hoping that one path to a brighter future for the country is a $1.9 trillion COVID relief package that includes $1400 checks for Americans and a $15 federal minimum wage. And you see some of the other elements of his plan there. So, joining me now is John Horne, the owner of Anna-Maria Oyster Bars in Florida. John, thank you very much for being here. Good to have you here tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HORNE, FLORIDA RESTAURANT OWNER: Thanks so much for inviting me, Martha. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. John, thanks. Thanks for being here. So, you heard the plan. There's a lot to it. There's been a ton of sort of passion around helping people get through these final stages. This has lasted a lot longer than any of us thought. What's the impact of the Biden plan on your business in Florida?\par \par \uc2 HORNE: Well, I mean, obviously getting people - the money to people is helping us. But this minimum wage is absolutely going to destroy the hospitality industry in Florida and across the nation. They're trying to paint with a broad brush the entire nation. They're trying to say that what's good in Alexandria, Virginia, is good in Alexandria, Tennessee, and we can't do that.\par \par \uc2 The market's always driven by what we pay people to work, and that's what we need to continue. So, raising it to $15 is just going to increase costs. It's going to increase menu prices; it's going to increase all consumer prices.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, if you were forced to get up to the $15 number per hour for your waiters and waitress and kitchen staff and everybody, what would the impact be? Would you have to - would you hire more people? Would you have to hire fewer people?\par \par \uc2 HORNE: Well, Martha, what most people don't realize is probably two-thirds of the hours in a restaurant are tipped employees, bartenders, servers, and they're already through the tip process. They're already making $30, $25 to $40 an