CALLUM: Yes, every place is different, and every business is different. John Horne, thank you, Anne-Marie Oyster Bar, it looks like a great place behind you there. Hope to check it out someday. I got my start in the hospitality industry myself. So, good luck to you, sir. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 HORNE: Thanks so much. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, also here tonight to look at this whole package, Steve Hilton, host of The Next Revolution. Steve, great to have you here tonight. What goes through your mind as you listen to John and you think about businesses across this country under this new plan?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HILTON, HOST OF THE NEXT REVOLUTION: Well, a number of things, Martha. First of all, the insanity of trying to impose these kinds of policies across a vast continent like America. It's not just stupid and economically illiterate to trying to have one pay policy for the whole country. It's actually really anti-American. It's opposite to everything the founders intended in terms of decentralizing power.\par \par \uc2 So that's the first thing. That also applies to many other aspects of this package that Biden has put forward, which includes a whole host of unrelated expansions of the federal government that are going to be permanent, have nothing to do with the virus. It just sneaking it all the things they've been wanting to do for a long time, regardless of the virus.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it's true that around the country you've got small businesses and families who are desperate for help. But the best thing we can do to help them right now is to open up the economy, protect the vulnerable, increase the vaccinations, focus on those who need that, but open things up because what they really want to do is earn a living free of all these stupid, counterproductive restrictions. That's what we need to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I kept listening for that last night. We covered the speech here on The Story and I kept waiting for 