xtremists say they're changing their plans. The Story investigates with former D.C. Detective Ted Williams, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So while U.S. officials are warning of the potential for nationwide attacks next week and a possible second attack on the United States Capitol or the White House, it's just possible that the arrests of these individuals and the 20 to 40-year prison sentences that they may face along with a dampened ability to communicate may, emphasis on may, change things a bit for this inaugural next week. We will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Since 20,000 National Guards surround the mall from the capitol to Lincoln Memorial and beyond. The fences are wrapped in barb wire. One leader from the far-right group the Proud Boys writes, quote, "we won't sit on our hands for the next four years, but we can pick and choose our battles moving forward."\par \par \uc2 So, joining me now with what we know about these groups, whether or not their plans may be changing and morphing, Ted Williams former D.C. homicide detective and Fox News contributor. Ted, good to see you tonight. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you think about that? Do you think that these arrests and the lengthy sentences that could be attached to them have these rioters and domestic terrorists changing their plans?\par \par \uc2 TED WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, hi, Martha. First, let me say to you, congratulations on your new 3 p.m. Eastern Standard Time show. I watch your show at night, and I love it --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- and I know the viewers are going to follow you --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- from this time slot to the 3 p.m. time slot.\par \par \uc2 What I believe is that the Proud Boys, the Boogaloo boys, QAnon, all of these right-wing groups come from under their rocks and what they are now trying to do is move forward and perhaps start a 