ountry and you really need to watch out for that, right?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: We need to absolutely watch out for that. And also, Martha, we need to look at the fact that the international terrorists, they haven't stopped doing what they want to do to Americans. And that is kill Americans. So, what they are doing is inculcating themselves into these various groups and they are also able to carry out terrorist acts. And so, we are in for a great deal of upheaval I believe in the near future here, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Wise words from someone who has seen a lot, Ted Williams, thank you very much for your insight and thanks for being here. I look forward to seeing you at three o'clock in the future, sir. Many thanks.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, cancel culture strikes Trump connected graduates of \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  and there are lots of them. We are going to talk about that coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, this week, we've been following the effort to blacklist all people who are tied to President Trump in any way and to have an impact on their future ability to make a living.\par \par \uc2 So, at \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  students are now circulating a petition to revoke the diplomas of Trump affiliated graduates including Senator Ted Cruz, White House press secretary Kayleigh McEnany, and Congressman Dan Crenshaw who they call, quote, "violent actors and inciters of insurrection."\par \par \uc2 Harvard Kennedy School\uc2  master student John Hartley joins me now. John, good to have you back on the program. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 I want to start by just showing everybody a bit of this petition because it's pretty incredible. It says, Harvard should be confident in its ability to expose students to diverse viewpoints without empowering the instigators of a violent racist revanchist movement. There should be little concern of a cascading, quote, "slippery slope of degrees revoked unless more alumni choose to encourage armed insurrection in the future."\par \par \uc2 What are your thoughts on all of this tonight, John?\par \par \uc2 JOHN HARTLEY, STUDENT, \uc2 HARVARD UNIVERSITY\uc2 : Well, thanks, Martha, for having me. And let me start by one congratulating you on your new time and show and also just say that, you know, my thoughts and prayers are with the family members of those who tragically died in the attacks last week and that there should be absolutely no room for violence in our society.\par \par \uc2 With respect to rescinding degrees, I think it's a rather very extreme measure and I think it's almost totally unlikely to happen. Universities, you know, they are very bureaucratic institutions, essentially, the university bylaws say that degrees can essentially be only revoked under circumstances like cheating, plagiarism, or lying to admissions.\par \par \uc2 And so, you've seen over the past week a bunch of honorary degrees being rescinded but degrees actually being paid for and studied for are very, very difficult to rescind. I think 10 years ago the petition cited that this Russian KGB officer had their degree rescinded or revoked that's because they lied about their name in admissions.\par \par \uc2 And so, unless there is some sort of evidence of something like that happening, this is, I think totally unlikely to happen. I know there were allegations from I believe Mary Trump, tut the president supposedly hiring somebody to cheat on their SATS, something like that or evidence of something like that is probably more likely to get somebody's degree rescinded than what happened than what's happened recently, I believe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it would be, you know, helpful also to sort of lay out you would think at Harvard students would lay out an evidentiary case for, you know, how they are connecting these individuals to insurrection or incitement for insurrection which I think would be a difficult link to make in these cases, but obviously anybody can write a petition, that's one of the great things about the United States of America. And as you say whether or not it goes anywhere is another.\par \par \uc2 But I'd love to hear your thoughts on sort of the larger picture here of the effort to make sure that individuals that that are linked to the Trump administration in any way can't get jobs are blacklisted.\par \par \uc2 I told a story last night about a Yale educated attorney that I heard about who was dropped by a headhunter because she had been affiliated with the Trump administration. I'm sure she is not alone, far from alone.\par \par \uc2 HARTLEY: Well, you know, I say right now, I think, you know, especially now and especially after the attacks last week, you know, I think now should be a time for unity and I think we should support the nation coming together and seeking justice for those that committed federal crimes last week, you know, violent crimes last week.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I think right now, we've got to think about where we're at. You know, there's a couple thousand people dying of coronavirus every day. We got millions of people who are unemployment. The president-elect put out his new COVID relief plan last night. I think there are a lot of things that Republicans and myself don't like about that but they are going to need 10 Republican senators if they want to get it through without going to reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we need to, you know, come together and actually think about what solutions are going to help us get out of this pandemic and recession.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wise words. I think you're absolutely right, John. Good to see you tonight. John Hartley, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, the ethnic studies curriculum challenging third graders to examine their race, sexuality, power and privilege in third grade at eight years old, that is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: As you know no third grade education is complete without learning how to deconstruct your racial identity and rank yourself and others by race, power, and privilege, right?\par \par \uc2 So that's what a group of 8-year-olds in Cupertino, California had to do and as you might imagine some of the parents thought that their kids were going there to read well and write well. They weren't too happy about this experiment.\par \par \uc2 In moments, Robby Soave we'll talk to him in a second. But first, to correspondent William LaJeunesse with the back story here tonight.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LAJEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Martha, this is part of a larger mandate in California to teach ethnic studies in K-12. History, the states says is top from the point of minorities base on race, class, and sexual orientation.\par \par \uc2 So, this course was taught at Meyerholz Elementary, a highly rated predominantly Asian-American and white public school and a wealthy suburb of San Jose. According to course documents, a teacher asked third graders to examine the racial and sexual identity by ranking themselves according to their power and privilege, such as, being white middle-class, educated Christian, able-bodied or English-speaking.\par \par \uc2 Another side ask students to identify the benefits of being part of a dominant culture. And they had to examine 50 activities to support an anti- racism journey. Some parents aware because of remote learning were incensed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WENYUAN WU, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, CALIFORNIANS FOR EQUAL RIGHTS: They are frustrated, baffled, and even outrage by the level of political indoctrination and brainwashing in this particular divisive curriculum.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAJEUNESSE: Students also had to write an essay describing their power and privilege. Another assignment for third graders explored transgenderism and nonbinary sexuality, quoting, "I used to think everyone is either a boy or a girl but now I know that gender is something you get to choose. It can be the same sex as the doctor assigned you at birth or it can be different."\par \par \uc2 An educator we spoke to said it is entirely appropriate for third graders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOEL BAUM, SENIOR DIRECTOR, GENDER SPECTRUM: We are often asked aren't kids too young to be learning about all this stuff related to gender? And we feel like that's the wrong question because they are already learning a ton about gender.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAJEUNESSE: Now confronted by parents, the school backed off saying to Fox, in November, the district became aware of concerns from parents regarding supplementary instructional materials that have been used in a third-grade class. To clarify, the materials in question, we're not part of the district's adopted curriculum.\par \par \uc2 So that class is dead. But under Governor Newsom and his mandate the state's new model curriculum for K-12 schools will, quote, "elevate the stories and voices of historically marginalized populations." Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. William LaJeunesse, thank you very much. Robby Soave joins me now, senior editor at Reason.com. Robby, great to see you tonight. What do you think about all of this?\par \par \uc2 ROBBY SOAVE, ASSOCIATE EDITOR, REASON.COM: The sort of thing that make you wish maybe schools just shouldn't reopen. Obviously, it's a little bit out there. I'm fine, in fact, I hope that students do learn not to be racist, not to treat people differently based on the color of their skin, for instance. But it looks like this, to me, is telling kids that you are different based on the color of your skin and that you have privilege that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 I also understand that most of these are Asian-American students which actually face one of the last remaining structural examples of racism in the education system which is when they apply for college places like Harvard which are allowed to discriminate against them. So, it seems entirely backward the way they're framing this who has power privilege question.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That's a great point. I went to a Supreme Court case on that. So speaking of college, the college admission application service, the Common App which we all remember and some of us have, you know, seen -- overseen our kids as they worked on it is used by more than 900 colleges and a million students who are applying to college each year.\par \par \uc2 So tonight, it's getting some backlash for a message that was sent out to the high school students following the Capitol Hill siege from the Common App CEO, I'm not sure why this was an important message for her to speak about in the capacity of her job but.\par \par \uc2 So, she said violent white supremacists, insurrectionists stormed the U.S. Capitol in an attempt to undo a fair and legal election. The stark differences between how peaceful black and brown protesters have been treated for years relative to Wednesday's coup again calling attentions to the open wound of systemic racism.\par \par \uc2 Now, her name is Jenny Rickard, she is the CEO of the Common App. And I -- you know, Jenny is welcome to her opinion on any of this. I think that's completely understandable. Buff the question is why it would be sent out in a message and on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to all of these students who are filling out college applications?\par \par \uc2 SOAVE: Yes. I don't know why she would need to weigh in, in that way at all. I also think I disagree with the point. I mean, you hear some people saying this even Kamala Harris sort of said this that well there is a different way they were treated by law enforcement the protesters over the summer and the protesters at the capitol. I think she even said that well, they were just led into the capitol. Of course, we know that's not true. They overran the police.\par \par \uc2 There was a lot of bad stuff in both kinds of protests and there should be more consistent condemning of it from the right but also from Democrats who didn't condemn the rioting and the looting over the summer.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Police officers who lost their lives and a person who was one of the rioters who was shot by a police officer it's a horrific situation.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Robby. Good to have you weigh in tonight. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SOAVE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So more of The Story -- thank you. More of The Story right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Tonight, on this January the 15th, a special thank you to all of you at home who have with us since the first hundred days when we started. Hard to believe it has been four years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Would it ever be proper for an FBI director to make notes of a conversation in that regard and leak them to the press?\par \par \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN, FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL: You know, as a general proposition the Department of Justice we take confidentiality seriously. And so, when we have memoranda about our ongoing matters, we have an obligation to keep that confidential.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Where do you think this is coming from? Why would she make this up?\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, UNITED STATES SUPREME COURT: What I know is the truth, and the truth is I've never sexually assaulted anyone in high school or otherwise. I'm not questioning, and have not questioned that perhaps Dr. Ford at some point in her life was sexually assaulted by someone in some place, but what I know is I've never sexually assaulted anyone.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are you interested in seeing the evidence that Rudy Giuliani has been gathering in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR, UNITED STATES ATTORNEY GENERAL: The Department of Justice is interested in seeing all evidence that could be (Inaudible) up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you ever think well, maybe one term would be enough of this kind of life?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Well, I mean, I want to see how we are doing. I don't think anybody has ever done this much in 100 days. But I've always said it's going to be eight years, not four years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Thanks to all of those great guests that have joined us here over the years and to you for your outreach this week, here's a quote that sums up a lot of what we've heard. Thank you for being a tough but fair journalist and a voice of reason in these challenging times. That is the best compliment anyone in this business can receive, so thank you all.\par \par \uc2 And we will continue to do exactly that as The Story moves to 3 p.m. on Monday, we hope you'll join us there every day. And Bret and I, of course, we'll see you in D.C. next week with our team coverage of the inaugural.\par \par \uc2 Until then, have a great weekend everybody. I look forward to seeing you on Monday on The Story. Good night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210116eh1f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Push Intensifies For Federal $15 Minimum Wage; D.C. on Lockdown Ahead of Inauguration; Biden Lays Out Coronavirus Response Plan; Some Companies Paying Workers to Get Vaccinated; Interview With Former Acting Centers for Disease Control and Prevention Director \uc2 Richard Besser\uc2 ; Interview With Sen. Roger Wicker (R-MS)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Gianno Caldwell, Andy McCarthy, Jeff Flock, Jennifer  Griffin \par \uc2 7,831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 January 2021\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2021 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thank you, Bill, very much. And good luck to you, although I don't think you're going to need it.\par \par \uc2 Right now, though, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  might need a little luck to meet an aggressive timetable.\par \par \uc2 We have been monitoring his remarks, as Bill has told you, in Wilmington, where he's outlining a plan to get more vaccines out to people more quickly. Just a fraction of them have gotten out. And a lot of people are perplexed as to why, in some states, it's working just fine, in other states, it's a slow go.\par \par \uc2 His goal is to get better than 100 million out in his first 100 days. The problem is enough sites across the country where they can do this in very, very large venues, like they're doing in Disneyland right now, where they're giving it to 7,000 people a day, or at least that's the goal, in New York at the Javits Center, to make sure plenty of vaccines are made available, because they're there.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is that last mile. They're trying to address that last mile, from the state of where they're storing them to the people and how soon they can get them.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. Happy Friday to you. I'm Neil Cavuto. And this is "Your World." So much going on so quickly, this aggressive plan right now to deal with a virus that is running out of control.\par \par \uc2 We crossed the two million mark globally in deaths, closing in on the 400,000-death mark here in the United States. In fact, this has been one of the more tragic weeks we have seen for the COVID escalation in both cases and hospitalizations and, I might add, deaths since the start of the pandemic itself.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest read on some of these proposals the president-elect is outlining from \uc2 Dr. Richard Besser\uc2 . The good doctor is a former acting CDC director, Robert Wood Johnson foundation CEO.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, good to have you back with us.\par \par \uc2 DR. RICHARD BESSER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING DIRECTOR, CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL AND PREVENTION: Nice to be here, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you first about the aggressive timeline here to get 100 million vaccines out in 100 days.\par \par \uc2 We're at about 12 million in the United States right now, but that represents a little more than 3 or 4 percent of the U.S. population. So what gives?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Well, a couple of things, Neil.\par \par \uc2 This is an ambitious goal. And I worry at times when public health overpromises and then underdelivers. The intent is where we need to be in terms of rapid vaccination. But it's not just about the numbers.\par \par \uc2 When you when you think about increasing numbers, you can increase numbers by vaccinating those who have the most access, and you may be vaccinating people who are at lower risk of getting severe infection and dying.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that gave me some hope in the -- in what I'm hearing is the intention to focus on those communities that are getting hit the hardest, setting up mass vaccination sites in areas that are easy to get to, mobile vaccine clinics that are reaching people who may be harder to reach.\par \par \uc2 Front-line workers. I'm concerned about those whose risk is the greatest, because, in their day-to-day activities, they're exposed to people and could get infected. But this winter, Neil, I think, if people are thinking that vaccination is what's going to save the lives this winter, they're going to be sorely disappointed, because what's going to save lives this winter is us coming together as a nation, wearing masks, keeping apart, washing our hands, and following those kind of recommendations from public health as a new national approach to tackling this.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, what's complicated things, Doctor -- and you I have gotten into this before -- is this variant of it, and this morphed nature of a virus that was first spotted in Britain, and now in at least half the states in this country.\par \par \uc2 The CDC warning that this new virus variant, whatever you want to call it, could lead to a significant spike in cases. Now, we know at least two of the vaccines work against this variant.\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But why would we still have to see those spikes even with it?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, this variant is now the dominant variant in Great Britain. And while it doesn't cause any more severe disease, it's not -- its symptoms aren't more severe, the fact that it can spread easier means that more people will get infected with it, more will be hospitalized, and, unfortunately, more will die.\par \par \uc2 And what it means is that any lapses in what we do will have a greater impact, because, if you're next to somebody -- you're wearing a mask and you're next to somebody who's not, your mask is not 100 percent protective. And if that person next to you has COVID and doesn't know it, your chances of getting infected will be higher.\par \par \uc2 So, there's an intensity here in terms of time. The CDC thinks that, by March, that could be the dominant strain we see here in the United States. So, we have to get more serious about this.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully, Congress will give people the resources so that, if you're -- if you're a working person in America, and you think you have been exposed, if you don't have money to pay the rent and put food on the table, you're going to go to work whether or not you think you're sick.\par \par \uc2 If they -- if people have the support, have the unemployment insurance, have the medical leave, have the ability to stay home, it will keep everyone safer.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm hoping that Congress steps up and does what needs to be done, because, at the end of the month, the eviction protections expire, the unemployment benefit surpluses, those expire. A lot of the things that are allowing people right now to take care of themselves and their families and their communities are going to go away.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, part of that stimulus plan that the president-elect has outlined is to push that back until at least September. We will get into that a little later in the show.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, I do want to get, though, your thoughts on when we might be rounding the bend on this. I mean, if it -- as you know, the growing talk has been this carries well into the spring, wear your mask well into the spring, into April or May.\par \par \uc2 Where are you on this? I have talked to other doctors who are saying, no, no, no, keep that as mask handy right through next Christmas. Where are you?\par \par \uc2 BESSER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, the winter is the virus' favorite time of year. It stays alive longer. It stays in the air longer. And so, clearly, through this winter, the virus is going to have an advantage.\par \par \uc2 As more and more people get vaccinated, that's going to help, for sure. As more and more people step up and do the right thing in terms of wearing masks, that's going to help. But we still don't know whether someone who's been vaccinated and gets this virus into their bodies, whether they would be able to spread this to other people.\par \par \uc2 So, the recommendation in terms of masks is an unanswered question. And I expect we're going to be wearing masks all year. If we can get people used to that, and it ends up being shorter, that will be terrific. But we need to have the mind-set that we're going to need to change our behavior for quite some time, even as vaccines are rolling out.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Dr. Richard Besser\uc2 , you have been a good guide for us on all of this. I mean, it's a little scary, but we just handle it week by week.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Richard Besser\uc2 , the former acting CDC director.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we are getting some word from companies that are trying to make this easier for their workers to get the vaccines that are currently out there, \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 , Trader Joe's among a growing number of companies that are saying, we will incentivize you, we will pay it to get it?\par \par \uc2 There others who are saying that, for the safety of their work force, after offering incentives, they will force the issue and demand that you get it, again, for the safety of the work force.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy right now, former assistant U.S. attorney, FOX News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Andy, I guess first the carrot, then the stick. What do you make of companies that are now saying, by all means, we urge you to get it, we will even pay you to get it, but we could come down a little harder if you don't?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, the carrots is always better than the stick, Neil, but the stick could end up being important. And it's certainly valid legally. There are certain things that you would have to do in order to have a constitutionally legally viable mandatory inoculation, a mandatory vaccine program as a company.\par \par \uc2 You would have to have an exclusion for religious reasons. You would have to have an exclusion for medical reasons, if people had other medical conditions that, for whatever reason, made having the vaccine dangerous to them. That would have to be respected.\par \par \uc2 But, at the end of the day, while it's preferable to let companies that know their work force better than anyone else does handle it in the best way they see fit, which would be to try to entice people to do it, rather than force them, they can certainly force them.\par \par \uc2 And it seems to me that Congress could help here by giving these businesses immunity, because they have this whole other basket of concerns about how much legal liability they're going to have from operating under circumstances where we have the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 If Congress wants to make these businesses its agent for purposes of doing what president-elect Biden says he wants to do, which is get this vaccination program out as widely and as quickly as possible, I think they could do a lot more to get business cooperation by immunizing them if they conduct their business responsibly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that was not built into the last stimulus measure, the one that Congress approved a few weeks ago, even though I know \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and others were pushing for it. So that didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 And this one, I haven't looked at every detail of the latest Biden plan, a roughly $2 trillion effort, whether it would be into that. I know Republicans will once again make that call.\par \par \uc2 But if you think about it, Andy, companies could be damned if they do, damned if they don't, sued on the basis of forcing their workers to take shots...\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... and -- but then sued by those who get sick because some of those workers did not take shots.\par \par \uc2 So, it's double jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Right.\par \par \uc2 It sure is. And that's why I'm saying, if you want them to do the right thing -- they're going to want to do the right thing, Neil. Nobody wants to put their clients and customers in danger. No one wants to put their work force in danger.\par \par \uc2 But why not make it easier for them to do the right thing and the thing that helps the country the most?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Legally, these are some new areas that we're getting into. The irony of what was the center of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's attacks on the vaccine getting out in fits and starts is, it was left up to the states to handle. The federal government has the vaccines, doled out the vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Then, lo and behold, different governors handled it differently, different stipulations who gets it and when and the order of events. It sounds to me, Andy, that the Biden administration would take a different approach, where the federal government handles the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 And states, it's one size, one approach fits all. I don't know whether that's good or bad. But that's definitely the push he seems to be hinting at.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, I think that's tends to be a bad idea, at least as a default position.\par \par \uc2 And I'd say two things about it. Neil. One is, some states are doing much better than other states, which means we should be leaning on those states' experience, which may be superior to what the federal government has in mind, or at least may be persuasive to them.\par \par \uc2 We have to see, do they do better because of some unique characteristics about the states, or are they simply -- they have a better plan?\par \par \uc2 And then the second thing I'd say is, a lot of the states that are lagging are states that I think are more apt, because they're -- they tend to be blue states, are more apt to be cooperative with the Biden administration than perhaps they were with the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 So, maybe the Biden administration won't need a stick as much as the Trump people have. Maybe they can bring them along, rather than having to try a forced federal one-size-fits-all approach, which has a lot of downside, it seems to me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Very well put.\par \par \uc2 Andy McCarthy, thank you very, very much for this.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about the spikes in cases in this country, particularly in New York and New Jersey, Arizona that's become a hot spot as well, but it's happening abroad as well, so much so that, with the sharp rise in cases in Japan, we're hearing reports that the Shimbun news agency that hopes for the Tokyo Olympics are beginning to decline.\par \par \uc2 Remember, they pushed them back last year because of the pandemic. Renewed cases might push them to the brink even now.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wrapping up that conference a short time ago, talking about his COVID-19 plans, getting 100 million doses out in the first 100 days, says that he feels safe ahead of next week's inauguration, based on some of the intelligence reports as well.\par \par \uc2 In that vein, we're also hearing that more people are going to be attending this, at least among the powers that be, than was earlier thought to be the case. You know that the vice president is. In fact, he phoned \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  today to congratulate her on her victory.\par \par \uc2 Senator Ted Cruz the latest to say he too will attend \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's inauguration, of course, famous for challenging the election or the electoral vote results in Congress, but he will be at that inauguration.\par \par \uc2 It will certainly be a different inauguration. If you don't have a ticket or you're not in the powers that be, you ain't going. That's the order from authorities there.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Griffin right now on how they're locking down for the big day to keep everybody safe -- Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, the \uc2 National Park Service\uc2  has now announced that all of the D.C. National Mall and all D.C. landmarks will be closed from today until the day after the inauguration. And they are limiting the size of any protest to 100 people. So, no more than 100 people can gather together.\par \par \uc2 Any protesters will have to walk through metal detectors. They're very worried about the potential for armed protests. We have also just learned and confirmed that the Pentagon has now authorized up to 25,000 National Guard for the inauguration, an increase due to more requests to help from the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 We just saw the Special Forces, the Special Forces shown here from the Air National Guard, arriving here in the Capitol. They are staging at the \uc2 Library of Congress\uc2 . The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says the online chatter is continuing on the Internet in open forums, even as the large pro-Trump mob stormed -- even after -- excuse me -- the large mob stormed the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVEN D'ANTUONO, ASSISTANT DIRECTOR, FBI WASHINGTON FIELD OFFICE: To those of you who took part in the violence, here is something you should know. Every \uc2 FBI\uc2  field office in the country is looking for you.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of fact, even your friends and family are tipping us off. So, you might want to consider turning yourself in, instead of wondering when we're going to come knocking on your door, because we will.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Secret Service and others are alarmed by the open calls to bring weapons to Washington, D.C., and to state capitols for an armed protest this Sunday at noon.\par \par \uc2 One group hoped for -- to march from the Washington Monument to the White House. That won't be possible anymore. The flyers say: "Refuse to be silenced." Protesters who bring weapons to Washington will be arrested, we're told, just as Proud Boy leader Enrique Tarrio was on January 5.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden team, we have learned, had to -- postponed a rehearsal for the inauguration on Sunday due to those security concerns. All 3,800 D.C. police will be on duty during the inauguration, according to the mayor. And they will also have around town EOD, explosives teams, looking for explosives on any vehicles coming into the city.\par \par \uc2 And most vehicles will not be allowed into the city -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And this goes right through the end of the month, past the inauguration, too. I don't know the same number of troops, but it's not as if, on the 21st, they're out of town. Right?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: That's right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 In fact, we're told that it is somewhat indefinite. It could go into February, but they will stay as long as there is a perceived threat, and as long as they are invited to do so by the mayor, the Capitol Hill police, and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jennifer, thank you very, very much, Jennifer Griffin following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-five thousand troops, that's a lot of troops there.\par \par \uc2 Chris Swecker, former FBI assistant director, what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS SWECKER, FORMER FBI ASSISTANT DIRECTOR: Neil, this inauguration has been in the planning for over a year in terms of security. It's a level one national security event already.\par \par \uc2 And it's headed up by the Secret Service. They have the inner perimeter. Now, with 25,000 National Guard troops, and you will have drones and every -- someone will be on every tall building in the surrounding area, it's going to be buttoned down pretty tight.\par \par \uc2 I don't expect to see any problems on the inner perimeter. My concern is going to be in the soft areas outside that inner perimeter and around the country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it's around the country that worries me the most, Chris. And I know you and I'd gotten into this.\par \par \uc2 But I'm always confused when \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials, including Director Wray, were talking about chatter that talks about planned events, not only in the nation's capital, but elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 What do they pick up, especially when people are well aware who might be planning this kind of stuff that people could be listening in on them might go dark? What do you do?\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, now that some of the more conservative platforms have been silenced -- and 