ich was another issue.\par \par \uc2 At this particular point in time, I do think that the left is going to continue to try to push \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as far to the left as possible. And what he will soon realize is, you need more than just a moderate Democrats and Republican votes. You're going to need those progressives too. And he's not going to be able to make those folks happy, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Kaylee, if you think about it, though, I think Democrats have Republicans in sort of a locked corner here.\par \par \uc2 Who are they to judge aggressive spending? We're coming off a year a $3.5 trillion deficit. That used to be our country's entire debt not too long ago, half-a-trillion dollars in just the last three months of the year.\par \par \uc2 So, they feel they have got ample room to weather a lot of this criticism. And the Ocasio-Cortezes and Bernie Sanders are quick to point that out. How lethal an attack line is that?\par \par \uc2 MCGHEE: Well, it is an attack line.\par \par \uc2 But the thing is, the problem is not the stimulus itself. Most Republicans would agree that the American people need additional relief, whether that's another $1,400, another $2,000 or whatever.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that the proposal right now does not include any sort of check to make sure that that relief is going to only people who need it. It's not targeted. It's not narrow. It's just hundreds of billions of dollars that the government has no control over, and it doesn't really care to have any control over.\par \par \uc2 And that's even what some of the moderate Democrats are saying, is that, if we are going to pass additional relief, it needs to be more targeted, so that we can make sure that it's going to the people who actually need it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Real quickly, Gianno -- and you touched on it -- do you think that $2,000 relief that the president wanted, and that ultimately they whittled down to $600, and now with this $1,400 add-on, ha