placed by driving and honking. Take a look at the pictures. In Chicago and 14 other cities across the country, yes, they want $15 minimum wage. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says he will give it to them.\par \par \uc2 But your last guest, talking about small businesses, says they're going to be killed. Can you imagine raising the minimum wage, doubling it in the midst of a pandemic? Terrible idea.\par \par \uc2 But Biden is going to be caught by his progressive wing, who say not only do we want an increase in the minimum wage; we want it to be at least $15, and we want it right now.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what was said today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD JACKSON, WAREHOUSE WORKERS FOR JUSTICE: Put your money where your mouth is. You said you would do it. We want this done within the first -- let me say this -- the first 100 days.\par \par \uc2 The black and the brown helped put you over the top. And we're not giving up on this $15-plus an hour.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: So, you can imagine Neil, Vice President Biden, soon to be President Biden, caught kind of in the middle of that. Maybe he works out a deal.\par \par \uc2 But, if it's less than $15, and if it comes any sooner -- or any later than the 100 days, maybe people are going to be upset. I don't know if I want to be president these days. You're going to tick some people off...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: ... whichever way you go.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You can't win on that one.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, thank you very much, Jeff Flock.\par \par \uc2 To Stanford School of Business Professor Dave Dodson on this.\par \par \uc2 Dave, where do you think this goes? They will have a friendlier crowd in Washington that, pretty much to a branch, is OK with this idea of a $15 wage.\par \par \uc2 DAVID DODSON, STANFORD UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Well, look, Neil, it could not come at the worst possible time.\par \par \uc2 We have to be mindful of what the stimulus bill is supposed to do, and it's supposed to help certain people who've been affected get back to work, and, most importantly, certain companies that have been deeply affected reopening.\par \par \uc2 And by slamming $15-an-hour minimum wage, this sort of macro policy, on an emergency time, couldn't happen at a worst possible time.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, you don't -- you could get any guest you want to come on the show and cite some report about minimum wage laws. You just have to use common sense and think about it from the standpoint of a business owner.\par \par \uc2 If you're a restaurant owner, one-third of your labor is -- one-third of your cost is labor. And you're deciding whether to open, how many hours to have, how many people did I want to have on the staff, whether I'm going to do overtime, and now all of a sudden my labor costs are up 25 percent?\par \par \uc2 You cannot tell me that that's not going to affect how many people you bring back.\par \par \uc2 Secondly -- and this actually may be the real critical issue -- is that restaurant owners -- and, by the way, this affects -- 50 percent of the people affected are in the restaurant business. Restaurant owners have had a horrendous nine months and a really bad December, and many of them are wondering whether they're going to open at all.\par \par \uc2 And part of the calculus here is that they have got a whole bunch of (AUDIO GAP) that they owe, they have debt that they have, and they have bills that they have to pay. And now we're saying, oh, by the way, when you are most fragile, we're going to kick you in the teeth and raise your labor costs.\par \par \uc2 What's going to happen is, they're just going to say, you know what, I'm just not going to open.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And if you think about it, Professor, there already is a $15 wage or close to it in a lot of cities. So it's different strokes for different folks in markets and all that demand it.\par \par \uc2 A lot of companies have found that, unless they were paying that much, they were just going to be beaten down by someone else who was. So, that's fine.\par \par \uc2 But, I mean, if you're in a rural area or a state where these costs are not nearly as high, there ought to be some flexibility to it, right?\par \par \uc2 DODSON: Neil, that's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this really is, to a large extent, a rural vs. urban issue, right? Because 29 states and the D.C. -- District of Columbia already have minimum wage laws that are higher than the federal minimum wage law. But if you look at where those are located, it's really a blue state/red state issue, where blue states, who are largely not going to be affected by this, are imposing minimum wage on states that don't have one now and can't afford it, because it doesn't cost the same to live in Greenwich, Connecticut, as it as it cost to live in Biloxi, Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 And this kind of notion of macro nanny state economics, well, we're going to decide what the wage should be across the whole United States, without thinking about those regional differences, is insane.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Professor Dave Dodson, strongly spoken, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very, very much, from the Stanford School of Business, Dave Dodson.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, we told you at the beginning of the hour how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants to get more COVID doses out to Americans quickly. What is left out of that is, he's essentially federalizing that effort. A lot of states have had mixed results in getting it out.\par \par \uc2 So, if he federalizes that, what are the impacts of that?\par \par \uc2 We will be exploring that tomorrow morning 10:00 a.m. Eastern on "Cavuto Live," also looking at the security efforts that are building, as are the number of soldiers there, to prepare for a safe and peaceful, well, inauguration. That's the hope.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. 