t vote for those of us who did vote both against what happened in Arizona and Pennsylvania, and that was that they didn't follow their legislative guidelines. The legislature sets the tone. The Constitution, by the way, says this, the U.S. Constitution, since it's legislative bodies that set the rules for elections in federal races for president. In a few of those states went around their legislative bodies, which is, again against the Constitution that used to mean something.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Understood. And we talked about those situations quite a bit here.\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: By the way, in 2005, there were over a 100 Democrats who did not vote to certify Ohio, over 100 Democrats who did not vote to certify Ohio. They weren't called seditionists. They did that. Over 30 of them voted against Ohio specifically and 80 Democrats didn't even vote. They literally took a walk on the vote.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Your point well taken. Before I let you go, I want to play this because this is sort of the pressure that's on a lot of members of the House who took the same votes that you did. And it came across in a way that I know caught your attention because you retweeted this. This is Jake Tapper. Watch this about Congressman \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN HOST: Congressman \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2 , a Republican from Florida who lost his legs, by the way, fighting for democracy abroad, although I don't know what is - I don't know about his commitment to it here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Representative Mast responded to that. He said, I lost two legs for Jake Tapper's right to say whatever the hell he wants. But that free speech also protects Republicans. He's so eager to condemn for asking constitutional questions about the election. And Tapper went back and defended his position with regard to that on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as well. Thought on that before I let you go tonight, sir.\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: Martha, that was a disgraceful comment. Jake Tapper needs to just apologize, admit what he said was wrong. I'm dear friends with \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2 . \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2  and I went to Walter Reed Hospital and worked out with wounded warriors. Brian lost two legs fighting for this country, to question his love for this country and his commitment to this country when he literally lost his two legs and lost some fingers as a bomb squad technician fighting for our freedoms. He owes \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2  an apology. \uc2 Brian Mast\uc2  is the kind of person that makes this country great. He's an American hero. And Jake really deserves - should just apologize and drop it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Representative Steve Scalise, good to have you here tonight, sir. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: Great being with you. Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. Joining me now, Deroy Murdock, National Review Online Contributing Editor, and \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former House Oversight Committee Chairman, both are Fox News Contributor. Gentlemen, thank you. Good to have both of you with us. Trey, you're no longer in office. Representative Scalise is, is this still the Republican Party or is it Trump's party when you look at these numbers and you look at clearly the pressure that was on these members to vote the way they did in both of these cases?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE CHAIR (R): I think they're going to have to figure out a way to synthesize the two. I mean, I think you can have a President Trump's policies without the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account. And I know that there are Republicans. I mean, you've got Tim Scott, Nikki Haley, Tom Cotton, you have others that are authentically conservative. They just have a different manner of expressing themselves. So, I reject the notion that you have to pick between the Republican Party and Trump Party. I think you can have both and do it