 bin Laden\uc2  did not enter U.S. soil on September 11, but it was widely acknowledged that he was responsible for inspiring the attack on our country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you comparing President Trump to \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: I'm comparing the words of an individual who would incite and radicalize somebody, as \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  did, to what President Trump did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, this comes from someone who said that President Trump was a Russian agent. He swore up and down that that was the case without anything to back that up. Now he's comparing him to \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  for something that happened on January 6th, that an investigation has barely begun on in terms of what we know and don't know about how all of that horrible behavior actually came about. Deroy?\par \par \uc2 MURDOCK: This is horrible, reckless, totally immature, terrible words you ought to retract, but bin Laden's responsible for roasting many of my neighbors here in New York City. I watched as the towers burned with my own eyes.\par \par \uc2 Nothing anything like that happened on Tuesday when in fact, last Wednesday, I should say, when the President of the United States said to his supporters, I know that everyone here will soon be marching over to the Capitol building to peacefully and patriotically make your voices heard. That does not sound like the words of \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2 . That's a president calling on his supporters to be peaceful and patriotic.\par \par \uc2 Some of them obviously didn't heed that advice, but that's the advice he gave them. So, I think Swalwell should apologize for that and also make the comparison between \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2 , who was chased to all ends of the country and hounded by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and had to resign, eventually compare his crime, which was talking to a Russian diplomat versus Swalwell who had sexual intercourse with a Chinese spy. And \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's let him be on the intelligence committee, remain there and also be an impeachment manager. The contrast between the two is absolutely stark and horrifying.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, about that last part, about their relationship, I don't think he's confirmed that. I don't think he denied it because he hasn't had an opportunity to do either. But I just want to be on the record with that. Trey, what do you think about what he had to say there?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: I think he's just a reckless buffoon. I think back to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  telling people during \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 's confirmation hearing that she's coming for your rights. That's pretty incendiary to tell people they're coming for your voting rights and your health care rights. I think about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  saying, they want to put you back in chains. There's a lot of really hot rhetoric on both sides. I wish people would stop doing it and just use logic.\par \par \uc2 But Swalwell is a buffoon. So, I don't follow him on social media because he hasn't said anything interesting in a number of years. I hope that people are smart enough to not compare the President of the United States with somebody who murdered thousands of Americans. I hope that they are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Deroy Murdock, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , thank you very much. Gentlemen, good to have both of you here tonight. So, President-elect \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  set to unveil his American rescue plan. He will soon bear the burden of rescuing those businesses that have suffered under the COVID lockdowns. He's talking about 2 trillion more in taxpayer funded relief packages. He's also facing the task of rectifying the painfully slow rollout of these vaccines by state leadership so far. National correspondent Jacqui Heinrich joins us from Wilmington, Delaware, as we await this speech tonight. Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Martha. Good evening to you. Well, Biden is going to unveil the first of two plans. The American rescue plan has a price tag of $1.9 trillion, it will be followed in a couple of months by the American recovery plan. But the big takeaways of what he's unveiling tonight include a $1400 direct payment to Americans that would bring the total relief to $2000 on top of December $600 payments. It increases the current $300 week unemployment benefit to $400 and extends it through September. Plus, it increases the federal minimum wage to $15 an hour, providing subsidies for childcare costs and mandatory paid leave. And it increases the child tax credit from $2000 to $3000 a year, with an additional $600 for kids under the age of six.\par \par \uc2 It also extends the eviction and foreclosure moratorium and gives rental and utility assistance to qualifying people. More than 1 trillion of the total price tags is for direct relief to families and individuals. 440 billion supports businesses and communities, and 400 billion is directly related to tackling the virus itself.\par \par \uc2 Now that includes considerable funding for vaccine development and distribution, including launching high-capacity vaccination sites potentially run by the National Guard and FEMA and also mobile units to reach rural communities. It provides for $50 billion to ramp up testing, which senior officials said is critical to allow schools to reopen--\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President-elect Biden has taken the stage. Jacqui, jump right in the - the plan is being rolled out now. Vice President-elect - President- elect, I should say, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT-ELECT OF THE UNITED STATES: On February, the 6th in 2020, Patricia Dowd took her last breath at home under the California sun in Santa Clara. She was 57 years old, a beloved wife, mother, daughter, and sister. She never knew she had the virus. At the time and most folks never heard about the virus. But just like that, she was gone and almost exactly one year later, nearly 400,000 of our fellow Americans have met the same cruel fate.\par \par \uc2 Countless families and friends left behind with unrelenting grief and guilt, anger and frustration and the emptiness felt by the loss of life is compounded by the loss of our way of life.\par \par \uc2 During this pandemic, millions of Americans, through no fault of their own have lost the dignity and respect that comes with the job and a paycheck. Millions of Americans never thought they'd be out of work. Many of them never even envisioned the idea are facing eviction, waiting for hours in their cars to feed their families as they drive up to a food bank. Millions have kept their jobs but have seen their hours and paychecks reduced, barely hanging on as well.\par \par \uc2 That's happening today in the United States of America, just in the midst of a dark winter of this pandemic as cases, hospitalizations and deaths spike at record levels, there is real pain overwhelming the real economy.\par \par \uc2 One where people rely on paychecks, not their investments, to pay for their bills and their meals and their children's needs, you won't see this pain if your score card is how things are going on Wall Street. But you will see it very clearly if you examine what the twin crises of a pandemic and this sinking economy have laid bare. The growing divide between those few people at the very top who are doing quite well in this economy and the rest of America.\par \par \uc2 Just since this pandemic began, the wealth, the top one percent of the nation has grown roughly $1.5 trillion since the end of last year, four times the amount for the entire bottom 50 percent of American wage earners. Some 18 million Americans are still relying on unemployment insurance, some 400,000 small businesses have permanently closed their doors. And it's not hard to see that we're in the middle of a once in several generations' economic crisis with a once in several generations' public health crisis.\par \par \uc2 A crisis of deep human suffering is in plain sight, and there's no time to waste, we have to act, and we have to act now. This is what economists are telling us. More importantly, it's what the values we hold dear in our hearts as Americans are telling us. A growing chorus of top economists agree that the moment of crisis in this moment of crisis, with interest rates at historic lows, we cannot afford inaction.\par \par \uc2 It's not just a smart fiscal investment, including deficit spending are more urgent than ever, is that the return on these investments in jobs, racial equity will prevent long-term economic damage and the benefits will far out surpass - far surpassed the cost. A growing number of top economists have shown even our debt situation will be more stable, not less stable, if we seize this moment with vision and purpose.\par \par \uc2 And so tonight, I'd like to talk to you about our way forward, a two-step plan of rescue and recovery, a two-step plan to build a bridge to the other side of the crisis we face to a better, stronger, more secure America.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, I'll lay out my first step, the American rescue plan. It will tackle the pandemic and get direct financial assistance and relief to Americans who need it the most. Next month, my first appearance before a joint session of Congress, I will lay out my build back better recovery plan. It'll make historic investments in infrastructure that build back better plan, infrastructure, manufacturing, innovation, research and development, and clean energy, investments in a caregiving economy and skills and training needed by our workers to be able to compete and win in the global economy of the coming years.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  , an independent Wall Street firm, said my approach will create more than 18 million good paying jobs. Our rescue and recovery plan is a path forward with both seriousness of purpose and a clear plan with transparency and accountability, with a call for unity that is equally necessary. And unity is not some pie in the sky dream, it's a practical step to getting the things we have to get done as a country, get done together.\par \par \uc2 As I said, when it passed in December, the bipartisan COVID-19 relief package was a very important first step. I'm grateful for the Democrats, Republicans and independent members of Congress who came together to get it done. But I said at the time, it's just a down payment. We need more action, more bipartisanship, and we need to move quickly. We need to move fast.\par \par \uc2 Our rescue plan starts aggressively in order to speed up our national COVID-19 response. The vaccines offer so much hope and we're grateful to the scientists and researchers and everyone who participated in the clinical trials. We're also grateful for the rigorous review and testing that has led to millions of people around the world already being vaccinated safely. But the vaccine roll out in the United States has been a dismal failure thus far.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, I will lay out our vaccination plan. To correct course and meet our goal of 100 million shots at the end of my first 100 days as president. This would be one of the most challenging operational efforts we have ever undertaken as a nation. We'll have to move heaven and earth to get more people vaccinated, to create more places for them to get vaccinated, to mobilize more medical teams, to get shots in people's arms, to increase vaccine supply and to get it out the door as fast as possible.\par \par \uc2 We'll also do everything we can to keep our educators and students safe - to safely reopen the majority of our K through 8 schools by the end of the first 100 days. We can do this if we give the school districts, the schools themselves, the communities, the states, the clear guidance they need as well as the resources they need that they can't afford right now because of the economic dilemma they're in.\par \par \uc2 That means more testing and transportation, additional cleaning and sanitizing services and other schools, protective equipment and ventilation systems in those schools need to make sure that workers who have COVID-19 symptoms are quarantined and those who need to take care of their family members with COVID-19 symptoms should be able to stay home from work and still get paid.\par \par \uc2 This will reduce the spread of the virus and make sure workers get the support they need to maintain their families. But they need about - we need about 400 billion in funding from Congress to make all of what I just said happened. It's a great deal, but I'm convinced we are ready to get this done.\par \par \uc2 The very health of our nation is at stake. A rescue plan also includes immediate relief to Americans hardest hit and most in need. We will finish the job of getting a total of $2000 and cash relief to people who need it the most. The $600 already appropriated is simply not enough. We just have to choose between paying rent and putting food on the table.\par \par \uc2 Even for those who have kept their jobs, these checks are really important. You see, if you're an American worker making $40,000 a year with less than $400 in savings, maybe you've lost hours or maybe you're doing fewer shifts driving a truck or caring for the kids or the elderly. You're out there putting your life on the line to work during this pandemic and worried every week that you get sick, lose your job or worse. $2000 is going to go a long way to ease that pain.\par \par \uc2 Also provide more peace of mind for struggling families by extending unemployment insurance beyond the end of March for millions of workers. That means an 18 million Americans currently run-on unemployment benefits while they look for work can count on these checks continuing to be there. Plus, it will be a $400 per week supplement, so people can make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 This gets money quickly into the pockets of millions of Americans who will spend it immediately on food and rent and other basic needs, as the economists tell us, that helps the whole economy grow. We'll also tackle the growing hunger crisis in America.\par \par \uc2 As I speak, and the vice president-elect has spoken to this many times, one in seven households in America, more than one in five black and Latino households in America report they don't have enough food to eat. This includes 30 million adults and as many as 12 million children. It's wrong. It's tragic. It's unnecessary. It's unacceptable. So, we're going to extend emergency assistance for 30 -- for 43 million children and their families enrolled in the SNAP program through the rest of this year.\par \par \uc2 We'll help hard-hit restaurants prepare meals for the hungry, to provide food for the families who need it. We will invest $3 billion in making sure mothers and their young children have the nutrition they need. This will not only meet our moral obligation we have to one another but it will also spur our economic growth and get restaurants and workers back on the job.\par \par \uc2 And as we work to keep people from going hungry, we'll also work to keep a roof over their heads to stem the growing housing crisis and evictions that are looming. Proximally, 14 million Americans are falling behind on rent, many at risk of eviction. If we don't act now there will be a wave of evictions and foreclosures in the coming months as the pandemic rages on.\par \par \uc2 This would overwhelm emergency shelters, increase COVID-19 infections, as people have nowhere to go and can't socially distance. Next week we'll take action to extend nationwide restrictions on evictions and foreclosures. This will provide -- this will provide more than 25 million Americans greater stability instead of living on the edge every single month.\par \par \uc2 And I'm asking Congress to do its part by funding rental assistance for 14 million hard-hit families and tenants. It will also be a bridge to economic recovery for countless mom-and-pop landlords. These crises are straining the budget of states and cities and travel communities that are force to consider lay off and service restrictions to the most needed workers.\par \par \uc2 It means that people are putting their lives at risk are the very people that are now at risk of losing their jobs, police officers, firefighters, all first responders, nurses, and educators. You know, over the last year alone, over 600,000 educators have been lost -- have lost their jobs in our cities and towns. Our rescue plan will write emergency funding to keep these essential workers on the job and maintain essential services. We'll ensure that vaccines are administered and schools can reopen.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris and I have been speaking with county officials, mayors, governors on both parties on a regular basis. We are ready to work with them. Help them get the relief they need to.\par \par \uc2 Our rescue plan will also help small businesses that are the engines of our economic growth, our economy at whole -- as a whole, the glue that holds communities together as well. But they are hurting badly. And you realize they account for nearly half of the entire total U.S. workforce.\par \par \uc2 Our rescue plan will provide flexible grants to help those hardest hit small businesses survive the pandemic, and a low-cost capital that will help entrepreneurs of all backgrounds create and maintain jobs plus provide the essential goods and services that communities depend upon.\par \par \uc2 Last week, I laid out how we will make sure that our emergency small business relief is distributed swiftly and equitably unlike the first time around. We're going to focus on small businesses on main street. We will focus on minority owned small businesses, women on small businesses and finally having equal access to the resources they need to reopen and to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 And we will be responsible with taxpayer's dollars in ensuring accountability that reduces waste and fraud and abuse like we did in the Recovery Act that I administered in our administration. Direct cash payments, extended unemployment insurance, rent relief, food assistance, keeping essential frontline workers on the job, aid to small businesses.\par \par \uc2 These are key elements to the American rescue plan that would lift 12 million Americans out of poverty and cut child poverty in half. That's five million children lifted out of poverty if we move. Our plan will reduce poverty in the black community by one-third. We'll reduce poverty in the Hispanic community by almost 40 percent, and it includes much more. Like an increase in the minimum wage to at least $15 an hour.\par \par \uc2 People tell me that's going to be hard to pass, Florida just passed it. As divided as that state is, they just passed it. And the rest of the country is ready to move as well. It's going to be a national minimum wage of $15 an hour. No one working 40 hours a week should live below the poverty line.\par \par \uc2 That's what it means, if you work for less than $15 an hour and work 40 hours a week, you are living in poverty. It includes access to affordable child care and will enable parents, particularly women, to get back to work.\par \par \uc2 I look forward to working with members of Congress on both parties to move quickly to get with the American rescue plan to the American people and then we can move with equal urgency and bipartisanship to my Build Back Better recovery plan that I will call for next month to generate even more economic growth.\par \par \uc2 American manufacturing was the arsenal of democracy in World War II. It will be so again. Imagine the future, made in America, all made in America and all by Americans. When these taxpayers' dollars to rebuild America, we'll buy American products supporting millions of American manufacturing jobs, enhancing our competitor's strength in an increasingly competitive world.\par \par \uc2 Imagine the stark investments in research and development to sharpen America's innovative edge in markets for global leadership is up for grabs. Markets like the battery technology, artificial intelligence, biotechnology, clean energy. Imagine confronting the climate crisis with American jobs and ingenuity leading the world.\par \par \uc2 It's time to stop talking about infrastructure. And to finally start building an infrastructure so we can be more competitive. Millions of good paying jobs to put Americans to work, rebuilding our roads, our bridges, our ports to make the more climate resilient, to make them faster, cheaper and cleaner to transport American-made goods across our country and around the world. That's how we compete.\par \par \uc2 And imagine millions of jobs in a caregiving economy to ease the financial burden of caring for young children and aged loved ones. Let's make sure our caregivers, mostly women and women of color, immigrants, have the same pay and dignity that they deserve so we can do these bold practical things now. Now.\par \par \uc2 I know what I just describe does not come cheaply. But failure to do so will cost as dearly. The consensus among leading economists is we simply cannot afford not to do what I'm proposing. Independent respected institutions around the world from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  have underscored the urgency.\par \par \uc2 Even Wall Street firms have reinforced the logic. If we invest now boldly, smartly, and with unwavering focus on American workers and families we will strengthen our economy, reduce inequity and put our nations long-term finances on the most sustainable course.\par \par \uc2 And where we're making permanent investments, recurring investments as I said in the campaign trail, we will pay for that by making sure that everyone pays their pay share. Not punishing anybody. We can do without punishing a single person by closing tax loopholes for companies that ship jobs overseas.\par \par \uc2 Or to allow American companies, 90 of them are in the top Fortune 500 to pay zero in federal income taxes, asking everyone to pay their fair share at the top so we can make permanent investments to rescue and rebuild America. It's the right thing of our economy, it's the fair thing, it's the decent thing to do.\par \par \uc2 We're not only having an economic imperative to act now. I believe we have a moral obligation. In this pandemic in America we cannot let people go hungry. We cannot let people get evicted. We cannot watch nurses, educators and others lose their jobs. We so badly need them. We must act now and act decisively.\par \par \uc2 My fellow Americans, the decisions we make in the next few weeks and months are going to determine whether we thrive in a way that benefits all Americans, or do we stay stuck in a place where those at the top do great while economic growth from most everyone else is just a spectator sport. And where American prospects are dim and not brighten.\par \par \uc2 These investments will determine whether we reassert American leadership and outcompete our competitors in a global economy. We are better equipped to do this in any nation in the world, or do we watch them catch up and pass us by?\par \par \uc2 Together, I know which path we'll choose and that includes all Americans so we can own the 21st century. But even with all of these bold steps is going to take time to get where we need to be. There will be stumbles, but I will always be honest with you about both the progress we are making and what setbacks we meet. And there will -- and here's the deal. The more people we vaccinate, the faster we do it, the sooner we can save lives and put this pandemic behind us and get back to our lives and our loved ones.\par \par \uc2 And the sooner we can rescue and rebuild the American economy, the biggest and most profitable engine in the world. I know it's been nearly a year. It has tested us beyond measure.\par \par \uc2 For all of you who have lost someone, my heart goes out to you. I know that feeling looking at an empty chair across the table. All of you have fallen on hard times. I know you can never get back what you lost. But as your president, I know that every day matters and every person matters.\par \par \uc2 From the very first to the nearly 400,000 lost American souls and counting to the millions of you just looking for a fighting chance in this economy. I promise you, we will not forget you. We understand what you are going through and we will never, ever give up. And we will come back together. Well, we didn't get into all of this overnight. We won't get out of it overnight.\par \par \uc2 And we can't do it as a separated and divided nation. The only way we can come to do it is come together, to come together as fellow Americans, as neighbors, as the United States of America. And when we do, there's nothing beyond our capacity, I've said this many times, when America acts as one, there's never been a single we've been unable to do no matter how consequential the issue has been.\par \par \uc2 Out of all the peril of this moment I want you to know, I give you my word, I see the promise, I promise as well we've seen clearly what we face now. Now we remain so optimistic about America, as optimistic as I've ever been, we have everything we need but the will must be demonstrated.\par \par \uc2 So, come Wednesday we begin a new chapter. The vice president-elect and I will do our best to meet all the expectations you have for the country. And the expectations we have for it. I'm confident. I'm truly confident together - together we can get this done and come out better off than when we went in to this crisis. God bless you all and may God protect our troops.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right. We were told that he was not going to take any questions, about 26 minutes of speaking there for the president-elect. He laid out his COVID relief plan to stimulate the economy and to fund vaccine distribution. And there he goes.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Guy Benson, Fox News contributor and host of the Guy Benson Show.\par \par \uc2 A lot there actually. And some questions that I have that wasn't there. He did say it wouldn't come cheap the plan that I'm talking about. And I would just point out that we have already spent $12.4 trillion or allocated that much, $12.4 already. This is another $2 trillion, Guy.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to remind everyone something that I always remind everyone often when we have this conversation, almost five trillion of the already allocated hasn't even been spent or distributed yet. So, we've got five trillion in the bucket, two more trillion coming. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, at least, right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, at least.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: This is 1.9 trillion that he talked about here and then he promised another big laundry list at his quasi state of the union address in February of next month. So, we've got another tranche of proposals coming from the president-elect.\par \par \uc2 And here's what's interesting to me, Martha, as a matter not a policy but of politics, two things. Number one, how can Republicans moving forward if they want to object at least to a portion of this, namely the $2,000, the bump of 1,400 per family, right, which is what President Trump has demanded, he hasn't gotten it because it got caught up in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 If we have a Democratic president asking for the same thing with numerous Republicans on the record in support of it, I'm not really sure how the Republicans who will then be in the minority very soon on Capitol Hill, how they can stand up to that and say no? Even though, you know, in my view it's not nearly targeted enough for the people who need it. This is been endorse by the current president and the incoming president. That seems like low-hanging fruit at least politically.\par \par \uc2 The second point, Martha, and I think this is the conversation unfortunately we're going to be having now for a number of years. When the Republicans are going to stand up and call for fiscal restraint which I think is going to be necessary as with the trillions get bigger and bigger, and you know, Biden was talking about, you know, it's going to make the debt not as bad.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Don't worry, we'll create all these jobs. And just beware all those promises. I remember the stimulus promises. I remember Obamacare promises as Republicans try to oppose some of the excesses. And there is good stuff in this proposal and I think it ought to pass, part of it, but if they try to band together and linked arms and say, hang on. These trillions are unnecessary or these trillions aren't related to COVID-19 necessarily, let's debate that separately.\par \par \uc2 How are Republicans going to convincingly communicate to the American people that they have re-found religion on debt and deficits when very few of them seem to be talking about that in any sort of compelling way over the last four years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well the president has not at all over the last four years, and as you say he was in favor of 2,000. A lot of Republicans were not. I would just point out that he said that 18 million Americans are still relying on unemployment. He's going to extend the unemployment benefits through March.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of controversy on that. The thing he didn't talk much about was the fact that he, you know, reopening our businesses, he talked about reopening K through 8, you know, getting them open in the first 100 days, he is going to have to convince a lot of teachers unions that they -- that it's time to get back into the classroom. So, a lot to -- a lot to dig into here.\par \par \uc2 Guy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great to see you tonight. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, as we mentioned at the top of the show from corporate America to academia, and even Hollywood there is an unprecedented effort to make all things Trump be erased across all of these platforms. Nationally syndicated radio talk show host Dennis Prager on that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): So, the effort to completely erase President Trump includes banishment from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , now \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 . New York City terminated all contracts including two ice skating rinks, Central Park carousel, golf course in the Bronx, the PGA, the British Open, all canceling events. Even \uc2 Shopify\uc2  shut down the online web store today for MAGA hat and Trump stuff. The online store for the Trump organization, as I said is not off line.\par \par \uc2 So, businesses, organizations, academia, all distance themselves. And fans and Macaulay Culkin even want to excise this scene from Christmas movie history.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACAULAY CULKIN, ACTOR: Excuse me, where is the lobby?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Down the hall and to the left.\par \par \uc2 CULKIN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Come on. But perhaps most vicious is the concerted effort by corporations and law firms and entities such as Forbes and the Lincoln Project to block any human being, any person who has worked in any area of the Trump administration from the freedom to work. This includes attorneys from the DOJ, the White House Counsel's Office, all of them.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Dennis Prager, president and founder of PragerU and a nationally syndicated radio talk show host. Dennis, good to see you tonight. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 DENNIS PRAGER, FOUNDER, PRAGERU: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, this last part, you know, I spoke to someone today who worked in the administration, attorneys, they've been -- some telling stories about people being dropped from headhunting firms. They say, sorry, we can't represent you if you work for the Trump administration. You're on your own. What do you say about all of this, Dennis?\par \par \uc2 PRAGER: This is the most frightening development in American life since the Civil War. The remarkable thing is the similarity to totalitarian movements of the past, the erasure of the past. It was a joke, a bitter joke told by Soviet dissidents and I spent time in the Soviet Union, that was the Russian Institute of Columbia so that was my field.\par \par \uc2 There was this a joke in the Soviet Union, the future has known it's the past that is always changing. And that's what totalitarian groups to the left in America is virtually identical to the communist party of the Soviet Union. Obviously, they're not running a gulag, at least not yet.\par \par \uc2 But I speak to people who escape from communist countries to come to the country of the Statue of Liberty. And they're depressed. They are -- they're almost clinically depressed seeing America go in the direction of the countries that they fled.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRAGER: This, the other thing, well there are other things, but another thing to note is the left speaks about hate, no one hates like the left hates. They have almost a monopoly of hate and if an honorable American voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , or obviously if they worked for, it's not even a question, but if it becomes known that you voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , what could happen to you in your school, at your place of work.\par \par \uc2 There were 74 million people who voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Please understand that all of this is because we are all, and I am one of them, held in such contempt. We are worthy of suppression. If this doesn't frighten the liberals, and more point, Martha, if I may.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PRAGER: Liberals, liberalism and leftism have nothing in common. Nothing. Liberals believe in free speech, leftist do not. Liberals believe in capitalism, leftists do not. Liberals believe in racial integration, leftists do not. But the liberal is the useful idiots of the left.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PRAGER: They vote for everything they don't stand for. There are few liberals like \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2  who understand the left is the enemy of liberalism but most liberals still think that we conservatives are their enemy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know --\par \par \uc2 PRAGER: They will be devoured like we are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I just want to ask you one more point here before I let you go. You know, I think back to when General Mattis and General Kelly left the White House. And there were some who said, you know, we need you to stay there, right? That you are serving your country, and so the people who did stay in this, in the DOJ, in the counsel's office, all of these people who stayed to serve their country, to serve in the White House, should not be ostracized for doing their job, for staying on their duty and for trying to fulfill their service to the country. Really quick, Dennis, just 15 seconds.\par \par \uc2 PRAGER: Crushing lives, ruining lives --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's what's happening.\par \par \uc2 PRAGER: -- defaming good people is a form of art on the left.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Dennis Prager, thank you very much for your insight tonight. More of The Story right after this. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is The Story of Thursday, January the 14th. We'll see you right back here tomorrow night at seven. Have a great night, everybody. \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020210115eh1e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \