-\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I hate to tell you, we really are.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: --our policies worked. No, our policies worked.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We're going to hell in a handbasket. I hate to be honest, but I'm not going to lie.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Is that going to be your next book title? Hell in a handbasket.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No, no, no--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: My mother used to always say that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: There'd be no - there's no more books. I didn't do - want for 10 years.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, done with that?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm done with the book. I did it to warn America. "Live Free Or Die: America and the World on the Brink." Now, we're on the brink side of things and I was hoping we wouldn't get here. INGRAHAM: That's the fighting spirit, Hannity.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm just telling you. I'm not going to stop fighting. I'm fighting like, hell.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: With everything I got.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Let your heart be troubled. Let not your heart be troubled.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: My heart is troubled. It's aching for my country right now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Well, I'll save it, don't worry.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But you will make us all feel better. Go right ahead.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I will. Hannity, it was a great show. Thanks so much. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is "The Ingraham Angle." We have a big show tonight from Washington. The events of last week have called into serious question, the vision and judgment of those leading the Republican Party. Now, we're going to explain what we mean by that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, call it woke elementary. A California school is forcing third graders to deconstruct their racial and gender and every identity, I guess, rank themselves according to their "power and privilege." Chris Rufo has the documents. He's going to share them with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And the Democratic leaders conspire to destroy American businesses and thus hurt Trump? Well, we show you how. But, first, "Never Trump Takes A Bow," that's the focus of "Tonight's Angle."\par \par \uc2 The folks running "The New York times," "The Washington Post," they're an intolerant bunch. They bend over backwards to give the benefit of the doubt to China, while they endlessly malign President Trump and his followers. And they also hate publishing conservatives - conservative writers.\par \par \uc2 But they'll make an exception for Republicans who trash Trump or otherwise echo Democrat talking points. Enter Rich Lowry whose magazine "National Review," back in February 2016, published this "Against Trump" edition.\par \par \uc2 Well, now in this new political magazine piece, Rich Lowry seems pretty self-satisfied. The siege of the Capitol and Trump's post-election challenges gave Lowry a classic, "See, I told you so" moment. But of course he had had four years to say these things and is only saying them now, because he thinks Trump's finished.\par \par \uc2 He writes an opposition to what Former Trump NSC staffer Michael Anton called the Flight 93 election in his famous essay during the 2016 campaign. Now, Anton's point was that Republicans who maybe didn't like Trump's tone or style, they needed to get on board with him in order to save America from the Democrats and all they were planning to do.\par \par \uc2 Well, history has shown that Anton's basic view was correct. The American Left has proven itself to be dangerous. The GOP establishment was hapless, and we needed a chance - we needed to take a chance on something else. So, Anton was 100 percent, right. The chance we took on Trump brought us four years of time and three good justices on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Now, Lowry simply ignores the fact that we had the strongest economy in the G7 for the last three years of Trump's presidency. Now, do you remember how bright things looked before the damn Wuhan plague hit? I do. And let's not forget that because of Trump, Washington finally challenged China and shifted the national conversation and ended up - ending our attitude of weakness toward that dangerous government.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Trump had flaws and made mistakes. Don't we all? And given the nightmare of last week and that absurd second impeachment vote, his political capital, it may be spent, we don't know yet.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, a recent Axios-Ipsos poll for all of you who say, write Trump off, found that 92 percent of Trump supporters would vote for him again in 2024, as would 46 percent of self-described traditional Republicans. So it's a specious argument to claim Trump made things worse in America. All the problems we have now would have existed without him, except things would be much worse with Hillary's open borders, flat wages and unstoppable China, and of course, sky high taxes.\par \par \uc2 And no one ever said that character and norms don't matter. That was always a straw man argument. But part of character is courage, and part of that includes a willingness to fight for others. And norms that are designed to weaken the voice of the people and make it easier for the Left to rule should be disregarded.\par \par \uc2 Now, George W. Bush is largely respected as a good man and that's across the political spectrum now. But during his presidency, Democrats branded Mr. Nice Guy as a dimwitted frat boy war criminal. His approval rating when he left office - 34 percent. During Bush's second term Republicans lost the House, the Senate and the presidency to Obama.\par \par \uc2 But, presumably, Lowry and the Never Trumpers considered that a resounding success. The bottom line is that Lowry - a nice guy, is like a lot of folks who make money only because corporate bigwigs write checks. Like many of the Never Trumpers his career is grounded in the 1990s. And look, that was the time when Americans were generally pretty optimistic. The world was generally safe and the Left was held in check by really memories of Reagan landslides.\par \par \uc2 He's never been able to accept that the world of the 1990s no longer exists. I don't think if Lowry does he fear China? I don't know. He doesn't seem to fear AOC or the cancel culture. He doesn't seem to fear globalism or open borders or rising crime rates or big tech. He thinks we should maybe oppose all of that stuff. Sure. But in the pleasant, gentle manner of maybe a Mitt Romney warning the kids not to stay out too late.\par \par \uc2 The view seems to be that the Left and China and big tech will prevail. And our culture just going to turn into garbage, our global influence will be lost and our middle-class will be destroyed. But that it's no reason for us to stop being genteel. His real fear, the only thing that truly gets him and the rest of the "National Review" crowd riled up is of the American people, who he plainly regards as dangerous fanatics in need of controlling.\par \par \uc2 He writes as though what happened on January 6th was some sort of dangerous attempt to take down the Republic, instead of a bunch of desperate people who would have been easily handled by a sufficiently staffed Capitol Police.\par \par \uc2 Well, we see how much influence Lowry's got in the party these days. He could only rally 10 House members to impeach Trump. Most of the party, the overwhelming majority in fact, agrees that Anton was right and that we must try harder to save our country.\par \par \uc2 Trump didn't get us all the way there. But back in 1964 Barry Goldwater didn't get us all the way there either. He lost in a landslide, in large part, because he was too angry, too stubborn and too divisive.\par \par \uc2 But the conservatives of that era, including the folks at "National Review," never conceded that nominating Goldwater was a mistake. They took the lessons learned in that campaign and kept building their movement until they saved our country. That's what's going on here now. That's what we're going to do here now as self-indulgent think tanks and snoozy conservative publications continue their slow fade. And that's "The Angle."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is aforementioned - the aforementioned Michael Anton, former Trump National Security Official and Lecturer at Hillsdale College. Michael, it appears that the tenets of your piece were right on the money, looking back on it. The Left is ravenous and the establishment, Right, is ill-equipped to handle them. Your thoughts tonight?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ANTON, LECTURER, HILLSDALE COLLEGE: Yes. Look - well, I would say, first of all, look, I'm not a mind reader. So the things that I predicted in 2016 that the Left is doing now, I discovered in their own writings. All I had to do was read them and listen to them. And they were saying, and we get powered, this is what we want to do. That's why I wrote that warning.\par \par \uc2 So I take no pleasure from having been right. I would much rather have been wrong and have none of this coming to pass. I think, though, it's really instructive. First of all, "National Review" founded by William F. Buckley is allegedly the flagship magazine of the conservative movement, and it's edited by Rich Lowry.\par \par \uc2 Now, do his subscribers and donors, most of whom, I think pretty overwhelmingly support Trump, know that he has essentially made an argument that the Republican Party should have lost and supported Hillary Clinton in 2016. Do they know that?\par \par \uc2 Moreover, how would they. Because he doesn't publish it in his own magazine for his own readers, donors and supporters to see, he publishes it in a journal for the Beltway elite intellectual - well, intellectual, that's a stretch. But the Beltway political and media elite so that he can basically signal to them, don't worry, I'm on your side against the rubes. I'm still one of you. I just find that sad, to coin a phrase.\par \par \uc2 And also it's quite hypocritical because, yes, "National Review" published against Trump in 